@episodes = {2007=>[{"slug"=>"91584-time", "title"=>"Time", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab042407pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91584", "description"=>"<p>Jorge Luis Borges wrote, \"Time is the substance from which I am made. Time is a river which carries me along, but I am the river; it is a tiger that devours me, but I am the tiger; it is a fire that consumes me, but I am the fire.\" And it’s still as close a definition as we have. This hour of Radiolab, we try our hand at unlocking the mysteries of time. We stretch and bend it, wrestle with its subjective nature, and wrap our minds around strategies to standardize it...stopping along the way at a 19<sup>th</sup>-century railroad station in Ohio, a track meet, and a Beethoven concert.</p>", "tease"=>"The strange, subjective nature of time -- from a sped-up spin through childhood, to a really, really slowed-down Beethoven symphony.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1282664868000, "publish-at"=>"2010-08-24T11:47:48", "date-line-ts"=>1180454400, "estimated-duration"=>3466, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Clockwork", "name"=>"photologue/photos/time_.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/512/640/c/80/photologue/photos/time_.jpg", "h"=>640, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Clockwork", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/29233640@N07/3793530899/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/time_.jpg", "w"=>512, "id"=>66010, "credits-name"=>"Robert Couse-Baker"}, "objectID"=>"91584", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Time", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Jorge Luis Borges wrote, \"Time is the substance from which I am made. Time is a river which carries me along, but I am the river; it is a tiger that devours me, but I am the tiger; it is a fire that consumes me, but I am the fire.\" And it’s still as close a definition as we have. This hour of Radiolab, we try our hand at unlocking the mysteries of time. We stretch and bend it, wrestle with its subjective nature, and wrap our minds around strategies to standardize it...stopping along the way at a 19<sup>th</sup>-century railroad station in Ohio, a track meet, and a Beethoven concert.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-08-24T11:47:48", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91539-placebo", "title"=>"Placebo", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab051807pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91539", "description"=>"<p>With new research demonstrating the startling power of the placebo effect, this hour of Radiolab examines the chemical consequences of belief and imagination.</p><p>Could the best medicine be no medicine at all? We take stock of the pharmacy in our brains, consider the symbolic power of the doctor coat, and visit the tent of a self-proclaimed faith healer.</p>", "tease"=>"The healing powers of belief, from the symbolic power of the doctor coat, to the very real stash of opium in your brain.", "publishTime"=>1262018834000, "publish-at"=>"2009-12-28T11:47:14", "date-line-ts"=>1179417600, "estimated-duration"=>3426, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Pill", "name"=>"photologue/photos/placebo.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/515/c/80/photologue/photos/placebo.jpg", "h"=>515, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Pill", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/lucasincas/6584693409/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/placebo.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>67085, "credits-name"=>"Lucas Lucas"}, "objectID"=>"91539", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Placebo", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>With new research demonstrating the startling power of the placebo effect, this hour of Radiolab examines the chemical consequences of belief and imagination.</p><p>Could the best medicine be no medicine at all? We take stock of the pharmacy in our brains, consider the symbolic power of the doctor coat, and visit the tent of a self-proclaimed faith healer.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-12-28T11:47:14", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91749-the-wright-brothers", "title"=>"The Wright Brothers", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab121807pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91749", "description"=>"<p>104 years ago this week, Wilbur and Orville Wright managed to coax their spruce biplane off the North Carolina sand for twelve seconds, and those twelve seconds started a revolution in flight. We examine the human desire to fly, and how getting flight changed us.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">For more information about this episode go <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2007/12/18\">here</a>.</div>", "tease"=>"In 1903, Wilbur and Orville Wright managed to coax their spruce biplane off the North Carolina sand for twelve seconds, and those twelve seconds started a revolution in flight. We exa...", "publishTime"=>1197996229000, "publish-at"=>"2007-12-18T11:43:49", "date-line-ts"=>1197996229, "estimated-duration"=>601, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Wright Brothers' plane", "name"=>"photologue/photos/wright_plane.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/479/c/80/photologue/photos/wright_plane.jpg", "h"=>479, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Wright Brothers' plane", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/tjc/324416163/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/wright_plane.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>27203, "credits-name"=>"TimothyJ"}, "objectID"=>"91749", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Wright Brothers", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>104 years ago this week, Wilbur and Orville Wright managed to coax their spruce biplane off the North Carolina sand for twelve seconds, and those twelve seconds started a revolution in flight. We examine the human desire to fly, and how getting flight changed us.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">For more information about this episode go <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2007/12/18\">here</a>.</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-12-18T11:43:49", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91748-contact", "title"=>"Contact", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab120407pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91748", "description"=>"<p>This week, a look at the different ways that people connect to each other, and how they act once they’re together. NOTE: This episode contains EXPLICIT language about sex.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">For more information about this episode go <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2007/12/04\">here</a>.</div>", "tease"=>"<p>This week, a look at the different ways that people connect to each other, and how they act once they&rsquo;re together. NOTE: This episode contains EXPLICIT language about sex.</p>", "publishTime"=>1196786849000, "publish-at"=>"2007-12-04T11:47:29", "date-line-ts"=>1196786849, "estimated-duration"=>3456, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"high five", "name"=>"photologue/photos/high_five.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/c/80/photologue/photos/high_five.jpg", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"high five", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/nicmcphee/276227701/in/datetaken/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/high_five.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>27188, "credits-name"=>"Unhindered by Talent"}, "objectID"=>"91748", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Contact", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This week, a look at the different ways that people connect to each other, and how they act once they’re together. NOTE: This episode contains EXPLICIT language about sex.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">For more information about this episode go <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2007/12/04\">here</a>.</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-12-04T11:47:29", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91747-space-capsules", "title"=>"Space Capsules", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab112007pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91747", "description"=>"<div>How would you describe life on Earth to an alien? In 1977, the Voyager spacecraft launched into space. And with it, went the Golden Record-- a sort time capsule, a collection of sounds and images that would describe life on Earth to whomever or whatever might find it.</div><p>Imagine trying to sum up existence on Earth into one little record... for an alien or humans of the far-off future. What sounds would you use? What music? What images? We put this charge to a bunch of artists, and asked what they would put into a space capsule. And in this week's podcast, a few of the answers we got back. From Margaret Cho, Philip Glass, Alice Waters, Michael Cunningham, and Neil Gaiman.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"How would you describe life on Earth to an alien? In 1977, the Voyager spacecraft launched into space. And with it, went the Golden Record-- a sort of time capsule, a collection of so...", "publishTime"=>1195577767000, "publish-at"=>"2007-11-20T11:56:07", "date-line-ts"=>1195577767, "estimated-duration"=>1106, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Record needle", "name"=>"photologue/photos/space_capsules.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/429/c/80/photologue/photos/space_capsules.jpg", "h"=>429, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Record needle", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/alexkerhead/2739759537/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/space_capsules.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>65983, "credits-name"=>"alexkerhead"}, "objectID"=>"91747", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Space Capsules", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div>How would you describe life on Earth to an alien? In 1977, the Voyager spacecraft launched into space. And with it, went the Golden Record-- a sort time capsule, a collection of sounds and images that would describe life on Earth to whomever or whatever might find it.</div><p>Imagine trying to sum up existence on Earth into one little record... for an alien or humans of the far-off future. What sounds would you use? What music? What images? We put this charge to a bunch of artists, and asked what they would put into a space capsule. And in this week's podcast, a few of the answers we got back. From Margaret Cho, Philip Glass, Alice Waters, Michael Cunningham, and Neil Gaiman.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-11-20T11:56:07", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91746-making-radio-lab", "title"=>"Making Radiolab", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab110907pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91746", "description"=>"<p>In spring of 2006, Jad and Robert took the stage at the SoHo Apple Store to talk about the making of Radiolab. Jad geeks out on the nitty-gritty of digital sound editing, and Robert discusses the editorial questions raised in creating imaginative soundscapes. And film editor Walter Murch weighs in on the components of storytelling.</p><div class=\"rtimage\"></div>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this short incorrectly stated that Stravinsky’s “Rite of Spring” premiered in 1938. In fact, it premiered in 1913. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><code></code></p>", "tease"=>"In spring of 2006, Jad and Robert took the stage at the SoHo Apple Store to talk about the making of Radiolab. Jad geeks out on digital sound editing, and Robert raises editorial ques...", "publishTime"=>1194627627000, "publish-at"=>"2007-11-09T12:00:27", "date-line-ts"=>1194627627, "estimated-duration"=>2013, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"lights", "name"=>"photologue/photos/ADC_lights.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/429/c/80/photologue/photos/ADC_lights.jpg", "h"=>429, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"lights", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/turbojoe/2618363167/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/ADC_lights.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>27214, "credits-name"=>"turbojoe (away)"}, "objectID"=>"91746", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Making Radiolab", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In spring of 2006, Jad and Robert took the stage at the SoHo Apple Store to talk about the making of Radiolab. Jad geeks out on the nitty-gritty of digital sound editing, and Robert discusses the editorial questions raised in creating imaginative soundscapes. And film editor Walter Murch weighs in on the components of storytelling.</p><div class=\"rtimage\"></div>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this short incorrectly stated that Stravinsky’s “Rite of Spring” premiered in 1938. In fact, it premiered in 1913. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><code></code></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-11-09T12:00:27", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91512-musical-language", "title"=>"Musical Language", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab042106.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91512", "description"=>"<p>In this hour of Radiolab, we examine the line between language and music.</p><p>What is music? Why does it move us? How does the brain process sound, and why are some people better at it than others?</p>\n<p>We re-imagine the disastrous debut of Stravinsky’s Rite of Spring in 1913 through the lens of modern neurology, and we meet a composer who uses computers to capture the musical DNA of dead composers in order to create new work.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this piece incorrectly stated the dates of two performances of “Rite of Spring” and the time that passed between them. The performance that inspired rioting occurred on May 29th, 1913. The second performance that we discussed occurred in April of 1914. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this piece incorrectly stated that the “Rite of Spring” was used in the movie “Fantasia” during the part that featured mushrooms. It was in fact used during the part that featured dinosaurs. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>", "tease"=>"We explore the line between music and language, and turn to physics and biochemistry to ask how sound becomes feeling.", "publishTime"=>1190648803000, "publish-at"=>"2007-09-24T11:46:43", "date-line-ts"=>1190606400, "estimated-duration"=>3532, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Learning to play piano", "name"=>"photologue/photos/musical_language.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/427/c/80/photologue/photos/musical_language.jpg", "h"=>427, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Learning to play piano", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/s-t-r-a-n-g-e/5948998191/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/musical_language.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>67315, "credits-name"=>"Victor Bezrukov"}, "objectID"=>"91512", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Musical Language", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this hour of Radiolab, we examine the line between language and music.</p><p>What is music? Why does it move us? How does the brain process sound, and why are some people better at it than others?</p>\n<p>We re-imagine the disastrous debut of Stravinsky’s Rite of Spring in 1913 through the lens of modern neurology, and we meet a composer who uses computers to capture the musical DNA of dead composers in order to create new work.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this piece incorrectly stated the dates of two performances of “Rite of Spring” and the time that passed between them. The performance that inspired rioting occurred on May 29th, 1913. The second performance that we discussed occurred in April of 1914. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this piece incorrectly stated that the “Rite of Spring” was used in the movie “Fantasia” during the part that featured mushrooms. It was in fact used during the part that featured dinosaurs. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-09-24T11:46:43", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91516-detective-stories", "title"=>"Detective Stories", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab041406.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91516", "description"=>"<p>Forensics, archeology, genealogy, and genetics are devoted to figuring out what really happened. In this hour of Radiolab, digging up the past leads to some very unexpected finds.</p><p>We begin at a trash dump in Egypt, where we find Jesus, Satan, sissies, and porn. Next, a goat on a cow leads us to hundreds of old letters scattered on the side of Route 101. And lastly, a blood-sampling tour of Asia reveals a prolific baby-maker...and potentially a world conqueror.</p>", "tease"=>"Mystery, intrigue, and a goat standing on top of a cow.", "publishTime"=>1189439209000, "publish-at"=>"2007-09-10T11:46:49", "date-line-ts"=>1189396800, "estimated-duration"=>3573, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Man ducking into phone booth", "name"=>"photologue/photos/detective_stories.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/detective_stories.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Man ducking into phone booth", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/53959560@N00/4305570683/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/detective_stories.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>67114, "credits-name"=>"Pure'n'Simple"}, "objectID"=>"91516", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Detective Stories", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Forensics, archeology, genealogy, and genetics are devoted to figuring out what really happened. In this hour of Radiolab, digging up the past leads to some very unexpected finds.</p><p>We begin at a trash dump in Egypt, where we find Jesus, Satan, sissies, and porn. Next, a goat on a cow leads us to hundreds of old letters scattered on the side of Route 101. And lastly, a blood-sampling tour of Asia reveals a prolific baby-maker...and potentially a world conqueror.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-09-10T11:46:49", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91744-this-is-your-brain-on-love", "title"=>"This is Your Brain On Love", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab082807pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91744", "description"=>"<div class=\"rtimage\">Radiolab is given the charge to put on a Singles Night. That's right. 'Jad,' they said, 'stand on a stage and make strangers fall in love! Or, at least, you know, exchange a few phone numbers with each other.' So obviously, we turned to science. Jad consults a few experts on the chemistry of a 'brain on love.'</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>For more information about this episode go <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2007/08/28\">here</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>Jad turns to science to help strangers fall in love...or at least   exchange a few phone numbers...as the host of a Singles Night. And he   gets some advice from a few experts on the chemistry of a 'brain on   love.'</p>", "publishTime"=>1188318527000, "publish-at"=>"2007-08-28T12:28:47", "date-line-ts"=>1188318527, "estimated-duration"=>1449, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Neon brain", "name"=>"photologue/photos/brain_on_love.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/628/640/c/80/photologue/photos/brain_on_love.jpg", "h"=>640, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Neon brain", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/dierkschaefer/2961565820/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/brain_on_love.jpg", "w"=>628, "id"=>67116, "credits-name"=>"dierk schaefer"}, "objectID"=>"91744", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"This is Your Brain On Love", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div class=\"rtimage\">Radiolab is given the charge to put on a Singles Night. That's right. 'Jad,' they said, 'stand on a stage and make strangers fall in love! Or, at least, you know, exchange a few phone numbers with each other.' So obviously, we turned to science. Jad consults a few experts on the chemistry of a 'brain on love.'</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p>For more information about this episode go <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2007/08/28\">here</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-08-28T12:28:47", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91500-emergence", "title"=>"Emergence ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab081407pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91500", "description"=>"<p>What happens when there is no leader? Starlings, bees, and ants manage just fine. In fact, they form staggeringly complicated societies -- all without a Toscanini to conduct them into harmony. This hour of Radiolab, we ask how this happens.</p><p>We gaze down at the bottom-up logic of cities, Google, and even our very own brains with fire-flyologists, ant experts, neurologists, a mathematician, and an economist.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>What happens when there is no leader? We look at the bottom-up logic of cities, Google, and even our brains.</p>", "publishTime"=>1187106394000, "publish-at"=>"2007-08-14T11:46:34", "date-line-ts"=>1187064000, "estimated-duration"=>3467, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Emergence", "name"=>"photologue/photos/emergence.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/emergence.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/modomatic/3588491628/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/emergence.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>75723, "credits-name"=>"modomatic"}, "objectID"=>"91500", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Emergence ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>What happens when there is no leader? Starlings, bees, and ants manage just fine. In fact, they form staggeringly complicated societies -- all without a Toscanini to conduct them into harmony. This hour of Radiolab, we ask how this happens.</p><p>We gaze down at the bottom-up logic of cities, Google, and even our very own brains with fire-flyologists, ant experts, neurologists, a mathematician, and an economist.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-08-14T11:46:34", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91508-morality", "title"=>"Morality", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab042806.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91508", "description"=>"<p>Where does our sense of right and wrong come from?</p><p>We watch chimps at a primate research center sharing blackberries, observe 3-year-olds fighting over toys, and tour Eastern State Penitentiary -- the country's first penitentiary. Plus, a story of land grabbing, indentured servitude, and slumlording in the fourth grade.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>UPDATE: Jad is now a father! If you'd like to know how becoming a parent did or did not change his answer to the thought experiment in the first half of this episode, check out what he has to say, <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/91930-killing-babies-saving-the-world/\">here</a>. </p>", "tease"=>"For thousands of years philosophers have debated the essence of morality. Now, neuroscientists may have answers.", "publishTime"=>1187019998000, "publish-at"=>"2007-08-13T11:46:38", "date-line-ts"=>1187020800, "estimated-duration"=>3389, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Photo of 'Cupid and Pan,' oil on canvas attributed to Federico Zuccaro circa 1600, at the Getty Center ", "name"=>"1/morality_cupid_owns_pan.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/886/c/80/1/morality_cupid_owns_pan.jpg", "h"=>886, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/randysonofrobert/2711063451/in/photolist-58yUHi-7fkqoR-5AzQDh-hmE7E-7fwSy4-At3jo-ebRBh1-fLCp3U-dHcAR5-84vci7-6S1M2X-9KVwgx-8S9sBp-8tYNpa-7fpbFY-4Cbb7e-ceZifw-4LnECB-ySkm3-", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/morality_cupid_owns_pan.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>107368, "credits-name"=>"Randy Robertson"}, "objectID"=>"91508", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Morality", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Where does our sense of right and wrong come from?</p><p>We watch chimps at a primate research center sharing blackberries, observe 3-year-olds fighting over toys, and tour Eastern State Penitentiary -- the country's first penitentiary. Plus, a story of land grabbing, indentured servitude, and slumlording in the fourth grade.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>UPDATE: Jad is now a father! If you'd like to know how becoming a parent did or did not change his answer to the thought experiment in the first half of this episode, check out what he has to say, <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/91930-killing-babies-saving-the-world/\">here</a>. </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-08-13T11:46:38", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91504-beyond-time", "title"=>"Beyond Time", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab030405.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91504", "description"=>"<p>This hour, Radiolab goes to the front lines with men and women who are battling against time -- or at least the common-sense view of time.</p><p>Einstein's Theory of Relativity may have implications on the concept of choice. Namely, that there is none. Do we choose what movie to see tonight? No. (It's already been chosen, some say.) Do we choose to wiggle our finger? No. (Already wiggled.) We'll visit a particle accelerator where scientists recreate the moment just after the beginning of time, and a Dublin artist whose life is a 19th-century time experiment. We end in the Mojave desert, where geologic time flows like a frozen hourglass.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>Fighting the inevitable march of time -- or at least the common sense view of it.</p>", "publishTime"=>1185291996000, "publish-at"=>"2007-07-24T11:46:36", "date-line-ts"=>1185249600, "estimated-duration"=>3468, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Stopwatch", "name"=>"photologue/photos/beyond_time.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/c/80/photologue/photos/beyond_time.jpg", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Stopwatch", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/wwarby/3297205226/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/beyond_time.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>65981, "credits-name"=>"wwarby"}, "objectID"=>"91504", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Beyond Time", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This hour, Radiolab goes to the front lines with men and women who are battling against time -- or at least the common-sense view of time.</p><p>Einstein's Theory of Relativity may have implications on the concept of choice. Namely, that there is none. Do we choose what movie to see tonight? No. (It's already been chosen, some say.) Do we choose to wiggle our finger? No. (Already wiggled.) We'll visit a particle accelerator where scientists recreate the moment just after the beginning of time, and a Dublin artist whose life is a 19th-century time experiment. We end in the Mojave desert, where geologic time flows like a frozen hourglass.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-07-24T11:46:36", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91562-mortality", "title"=>"Mortality", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab061507pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91562", "description"=>"<p>This hour of Radiolab: is death a disease that can be cured?</p>\n<p>We filter the modern search for the fountain of youth through personal stories of witnessing death -- the death of a cell, the death of a loved one...and the aging of a society.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>The modern search for the fountain of youth, and personal stories of witnessing death.</p>", "publishTime"=>1181836048000, "publish-at"=>"2007-06-14T11:47:28", "date-line-ts"=>1181793600, "estimated-duration"=>3414, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/79725.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/200/150/c/80/photologue/photos/79725.jpg", "h"=>150, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/eqqman/19633458/in/set-287661/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/79725.jpg", "w"=>200, "id"=>26440, "credits-name"=>"eqqman"}, "objectID"=>"91562", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Mortality", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This hour of Radiolab: is death a disease that can be cured?</p>\n<p>We filter the modern search for the fountain of youth through personal stories of witnessing death -- the death of a cell, the death of a loved one...and the aging of a society.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-06-14T11:47:28", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91569-memory-and-forgetting", "title"=>"Memory and Forgetting", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab060807pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91569", "description"=>"<p>This hour of Radiolab, a look behind the curtain of how memories are made...and forgotten.</p><p>Remembering is an unstable and profoundly unreliable process--it’s easy come, easy go as we learn how true memories can be obliterated, and false ones added. And Oliver Sacks joins us to tell the story of an amnesiac whose love for his wife and music transcend his 7-second memory.</p>", "tease"=>"Remembering is a tricky, unstable business. This hour: a look behind the curtain of how memories are made...and forgotten.", "publishTime"=>1181231257000, "publish-at"=>"2007-06-07T11:47:37", "date-line-ts"=>1181232000, "estimated-duration"=>3426, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Faded photos", "name"=>"photologue/photos/memory_and_forgetting.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/memory_and_forgetting.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Faded photos", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/ben124/6090133418/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/memory_and_forgetting.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>67118, "credits-name"=>"ben124"}, "objectID"=>"91569", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Memory and Forgetting", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This hour of Radiolab, a look behind the curtain of how memories are made...and forgotten.</p><p>Remembering is an unstable and profoundly unreliable process--it’s easy come, easy go as we learn how true memories can be obliterated, and false ones added. And Oliver Sacks joins us to tell the story of an amnesiac whose love for his wife and music transcend his 7-second memory.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-06-07T11:47:37", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91552-zoos", "title"=>"Zoos", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab060107pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91552", "description"=>"<p>In a cruel trick of evolution, humans can stand just three feet from a ferocious animal and still be perfectly safe. This hour, Radiolab goes to the zoo.</p><p>What's with our need to get close to \"wildness\"? We examine where we stand in this paradox--starting with the Romans, and ending in the wilds of Belize, staring into the eyes of a wild jaguar.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>Blood-thirsty Romans, frozen carcasses, wild jaguars, and a question: how do you build a better cage?</p>", "publishTime"=>1180972041000, "publish-at"=>"2007-06-04T11:47:21", "date-line-ts"=>1180929600, "estimated-duration"=>3409, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"tiger paw", "name"=>"photologue/photos/79092.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/200/150/c/80/photologue/photos/79092.jpg", "h"=>150, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/oskarn/122070530/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/79092.jpg", "w"=>200, "id"=>26436, "credits-name"=>"oskarn"}, "objectID"=>"91552", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Zoos", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In a cruel trick of evolution, humans can stand just three feet from a ferocious animal and still be perfectly safe. This hour, Radiolab goes to the zoo.</p><p>What's with our need to get close to \"wildness\"? We examine where we stand in this paradox--starting with the Romans, and ending in the wilds of Belize, staring into the eyes of a wild jaguar.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-06-04T11:47:21", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91528-sleep", "title"=>"Sleep", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab052507pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91528", "description"=>"<p>Birds do it, bees do it...yet science still can't answer the basic question: why do we sleep?</p><p>Every creature on the planet sleeps--from giant humpback whales to teeny fruit flies. What does it do for us, and what happens when we go without? We take a peek at iguanas sleeping with one eye open, get in bed with a pair of sleep-deprived new parents, and eavesdrop on the uneasy dreams of rats.</p>", "tease"=>"Birds do it, bees do it...yet science still can't answer the basic question: why do we sleep?", "publishTime"=>1180021628000, "publish-at"=>"2007-05-24T11:47:08", "date-line-ts"=>1180022400, "estimated-duration"=>3359, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Sleeping lamb", "name"=>"photologue/photos/sleep.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/313/c/80/photologue/photos/sleep.jpg", "h"=>313, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Sleeping lamb", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/davehamster/386521306/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/sleep.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>65980, "credits-name"=>"Dave Hamster"}, "objectID"=>"91528", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Sleep", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Birds do it, bees do it...yet science still can't answer the basic question: why do we sleep?</p><p>Every creature on the planet sleeps--from giant humpback whales to teeny fruit flies. What does it do for us, and what happens when we go without? We take a peek at iguanas sleeping with one eye open, get in bed with a pair of sleep-deprived new parents, and eavesdrop on the uneasy dreams of rats.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-05-24T11:47:08", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91496-who-am-i", "title"=>"Who Am I?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab050807pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91496", "description"=>"<p>The \"mind\" and \"self\" were formerly the domain of philosophers and priests. But in this hour of Radiolab, neurologists lead the charge on profound questions like \"How does the brain make me?\"</p><p>We stare into the mirror with Dr. Julian Keenan, reflect on the illusion of selfhood with British neurologist Paul Broks, and contemplate the evolution of consciousness with Dr. V. S. Ramachandran. Also: the story of woman who one day woke up as a completely different person.</p>", "tease"=>"The \"mind\" and \"self\" were formerly the domain of philosophers &amp; priests. But this hour, neurologists lead the charge.", "publishTime"=>1178552791000, "publish-at"=>"2007-05-07T11:46:31", "date-line-ts"=>1178553600, "estimated-duration"=>3427, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Neon brain", "name"=>"photologue/photos/brain_on_love.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/628/640/c/80/photologue/photos/brain_on_love.jpg", "h"=>640, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Neon brain", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/dierkschaefer/2961565820/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/brain_on_love.jpg", "w"=>628, "id"=>67116, "credits-name"=>"dierk schaefer"}, "objectID"=>"91496", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Who Am I?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>The \"mind\" and \"self\" were formerly the domain of philosophers and priests. But in this hour of Radiolab, neurologists lead the charge on profound questions like \"How does the brain make me?\"</p><p>We stare into the mirror with Dr. Julian Keenan, reflect on the illusion of selfhood with British neurologist Paul Broks, and contemplate the evolution of consciousness with Dr. V. S. Ramachandran. Also: the story of woman who one day woke up as a completely different person.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-05-07T11:46:31", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91580-stress", "title"=>"Stress ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab041007pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91580", "description"=>"<p>Stress may save your life if you're being chased by a tiger. But if you're stuck in traffic, it may be more likely to make you sick. This hour, a long hard look at the body's system for getting out of trouble.</p><p>Stanford University neurologist (and part-time \"baboonologist\") Dr. Robert Sapolsky takes us through what happens on our insides when we stand in the wrong line at the supermarket, and offers a few coping strategies: gnawing on wood, beating the crap out of somebody, and having friends. Plus: the story of a singer who lost her voice, and an author stuck in a body that never grew up.</p>", "tease"=>"Stories of stress -- from a singer who loses her voice, to an author caught in a body that never grew up.", "publishTime"=>1176133666000, "publish-at"=>"2007-04-09T11:47:46", "date-line-ts"=>1176134400, "estimated-duration"=>3437, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Hand squeezing stress ball", "name"=>"photologue/photos/stress_ball.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/stress_ball.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/22964099@N05/2204059683/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/stress_ball.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>76133, "credits-name"=>"bottled_void"}, "objectID"=>"91580", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Stress ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Stress may save your life if you're being chased by a tiger. But if you're stuck in traffic, it may be more likely to make you sick. This hour, a long hard look at the body's system for getting out of trouble.</p><p>Stanford University neurologist (and part-time \"baboonologist\") Dr. Robert Sapolsky takes us through what happens on our insides when we stand in the wrong line at the supermarket, and offers a few coping strategies: gnawing on wood, beating the crap out of somebody, and having friends. Plus: the story of a singer who lost her voice, and an author stuck in a body that never grew up.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-04-09T11:47:46", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2008=>[{"slug"=>"91662-diagnosis", "title"=>"Diagnosis", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab120508.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91662", "description"=>"<p>Humans love to solve problems. In this hour of Radiolab, diagnosis--our attempt to find out what's wrong, and give it a label.</p><p>In this day and age, we have astonishing technology--chemicals and computers and machines that can pinpoint things imperceptible to our senses. But humans aren't obsolete--intuition and creativity still lead the way both in discovering the nature of the problem, and in dealing with that knowledge.</p>", "tease"=>"Humans love to solve problems. This hour, Diagnosis -- our attempt to find out what's wrong, and give it a label.", "publishTime"=>1230569345000, "publish-at"=>"2008-12-29T11:49:05", "date-line-ts"=>1230570000, "estimated-duration"=>3400, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/113752.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/200/255/c/80/photologue/photos/113752.jpg", "h"=>255, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d3/IF-THEN-ELSE-END_flowchart.png", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/113752.jpg", "w"=>200, "id"=>26483, "credits-name"=>"WikimediaCommons"}, "objectID"=>"91662", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Diagnosis", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Humans love to solve problems. In this hour of Radiolab, diagnosis--our attempt to find out what's wrong, and give it a label.</p><p>In this day and age, we have astonishing technology--chemicals and computers and machines that can pinpoint things imperceptible to our senses. But humans aren't obsolete--intuition and creativity still lead the way both in discovering the nature of the problem, and in dealing with that knowledge.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-12-29T11:49:05", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91653-race", "title"=>"Race", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab112808.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91653", "description"=>"<p>This hour of Radiolab, a look at race. </p><p>When the human genome was first fully mapped in 2000, Bill Clinton, Craig Venter, and Francis Collins took the stage and pronounced that \"The concept of race has no genetic or scientific basis.\" Great words spoken with great intentions. But what do they really mean, and where do they leave us? Our genes are nearly all the same, but that hasn't made race meaningless, or wiped out our evolving conversation about it.</p>", "tease"=>"Radiolab asks what race is, and whether it's fixed or fluid, genes or culture?", "publishTime"=>1229359738000, "publish-at"=>"2008-12-15T11:48:58", "date-line-ts"=>1229360400, "estimated-duration"=>3413, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Race", "name"=>"photologue/photos/Race_FINAL_shea_walsh_web.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/h/80/photologue/photos/Race_FINAL_shea_walsh_web.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"Race", "credits-url"=>"http://www.amoebicexpansion.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/Race_FINAL_shea_walsh_web.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>71873, "credits-name"=>"Shea Walsh"}, "objectID"=>"91653", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Race", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This hour of Radiolab, a look at race. </p><p>When the human genome was first fully mapped in 2000, Bill Clinton, Craig Venter, and Francis Collins took the stage and pronounced that \"The concept of race has no genetic or scientific basis.\" Great words spoken with great intentions. But what do they really mean, and where do they leave us? Our genes are nearly all the same, but that hasn't made race meaningless, or wiped out our evolving conversation about it.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-12-15T11:48:58", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91646-sperm", "title"=>"Sperm", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab112108.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91646", "description"=>"<p>Sperm carry half the genes needed for human life. In this hour of Radiolab, some basic questions and profound thoughts about reproduction.</p><p>To begin: why so many sperm? We turn to the animal kingdom for answers, which lands us on a tour of sperm battles in ducks, flying pig sperm, and promiscuous whippoorwills. Next, we ponder fatherhood, and wonder...in a world where sperm can be frozen and kept for all eternity...what the future holds for men. We end quietly, in a stark sonic space with a widow struggling to keep some essence of her husband alive.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>We examine our beginnings, take a tour of the animal kingdom, and ponder a world where frozen sperm can last for all eternity.</p>", "publishTime"=>1228150131000, "publish-at"=>"2008-12-01T11:48:51", "date-line-ts"=>1228107600, "estimated-duration"=>3411, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/115268.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/200/157/c/80/photologue/photos/115268.jpg", "h"=>157, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"illustration courtesy of: <a href=\"http://www.mattbuchanan.co.uk\">Matt Buchanan</a>", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/115268.jpg", "w"=>200, "id"=>26475, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"91646", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Sperm", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Sperm carry half the genes needed for human life. In this hour of Radiolab, some basic questions and profound thoughts about reproduction.</p><p>To begin: why so many sperm? We turn to the animal kingdom for answers, which lands us on a tour of sperm battles in ducks, flying pig sperm, and promiscuous whippoorwills. Next, we ponder fatherhood, and wonder...in a world where sperm can be frozen and kept for all eternity...what the future holds for men. We end quietly, in a stark sonic space with a widow struggling to keep some essence of her husband alive.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-12-01T11:48:51", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91640-choice", "title"=>"Choice", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab111408.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91640", "description"=>"<p>Logic and emotion aren't the only forces that guide our decisions. This hour of Radiolab, we turn up the volume on the voices in our heads, and try to make sense of the babble. Forget free will, some important decisions could come down to a steaming cup of coffee.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>UPDATE: </em><em>The Williams &amp; Bargh Yale coffee study \"Experiencing Physical Warmth Promotes Interpersonal Warmth\" was replicated in 2014 by researchers at three different universities, Kenyon College, Michigan State University, and University of Manchester. They did not observe the same results as in the original study. They conclude that the difference between the original and the replications may have been due to some issues with the methods of the original study (\"The effect observed by Williams and Bargh may have been due, in part, to unconscious cues given by the researcher\") or may simply have been due to chance. They are very careful in their language to not discredit the original study but they advise that future researchers be more cautious \"when considering whether exposure to hot or cold temperatures impacts prosocial behavior.\" In sum: the original Yale study mostly still stands, but researchers now look the methods and results with slight skepticism (not outright disbelief though). You can check out the replications here:  <a href=\"http://econtent.hogrefe.com/doi/full/10.1027/1864-9335/a000187\">http://econtent.hogrefe.com/doi/full/10.1027/1864-9335/a000187</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>", "tease"=>"We turn up the volume on the voices in our heads, and try to get to the bottom of what really steers our decisions.", "publishTime"=>1226940523000, "publish-at"=>"2008-11-17T11:48:43", "date-line-ts"=>1226898000, "estimated-duration"=>3415, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Shelves of candy jars", "name"=>"1/Radiolab_choice_candy_jars.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/999/c/80/1/Radiolab_choice_candy_jars.jpg", "h"=>999, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/8136496@N05/5402526966/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Radiolab_choice_candy_jars.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>95724, "credits-name"=>"terren in Virginia"}, "objectID"=>"91640", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Choice", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Logic and emotion aren't the only forces that guide our decisions. This hour of Radiolab, we turn up the volume on the voices in our heads, and try to make sense of the babble. Forget free will, some important decisions could come down to a steaming cup of coffee.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>UPDATE: </em><em>The Williams &amp; Bargh Yale coffee study \"Experiencing Physical Warmth Promotes Interpersonal Warmth\" was replicated in 2014 by researchers at three different universities, Kenyon College, Michigan State University, and University of Manchester. They did not observe the same results as in the original study. They conclude that the difference between the original and the replications may have been due to some issues with the methods of the original study (\"The effect observed by Williams and Bargh may have been due, in part, to unconscious cues given by the researcher\") or may simply have been due to chance. They are very careful in their language to not discredit the original study but they advise that future researchers be more cautious \"when considering whether exposure to hot or cold temperatures impacts prosocial behavior.\" In sum: the original Yale study mostly still stands, but researchers now look the methods and results with slight skepticism (not outright disbelief though). You can check out the replications here:  <a href=\"http://econtent.hogrefe.com/doi/full/10.1027/1864-9335/a000187\">http://econtent.hogrefe.com/doi/full/10.1027/1864-9335/a000187</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-11-17T11:48:43", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91872-chris-and-lisa", "title"=>"Chris And Lisa", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast102108.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91872", "description"=>"<p>Chris had a crush on Lisa. But how to woo her?</p><p>He met her on a park bench in Chicago, handed her a stack of CD's, and sent her off on an extremely specific mission.  Did it work? Find out on this week's podcast.</p>\n<p>And hey! Chicago peeps, get your tix to our <a href=\"http://blogs.wnyc.org/radiolab/2008/10/08/get-yer-tickets-war-of-the-worlds-live-in-chicago/\">LIVE EVENT</a></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"<p>Chris had a crush on Lisa. But how to woo her? He met her on a park bench in Chicago, handed her a stack of CD's, and sent her off on an extremely specific mission. Did it work? Find out on this week's podcast.</p>", "publishTime"=>1224566836000, "publish-at"=>"2008-10-21T01:27:16", "date-line-ts"=>1224566836, "estimated-duration"=>1154, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Couple on a bench", "name"=>"photologue/photos/chris_lisa.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/501/c/80/photologue/photos/chris_lisa.jpg", "h"=>501, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Couple on a bench", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/nurpax/3893098280/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/chris_lisa.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>67305, "credits-name"=>"nurpax"}, "objectID"=>"91872", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Chris And Lisa", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Chris had a crush on Lisa. But how to woo her?</p><p>He met her on a park bench in Chicago, handed her a stack of CD's, and sent her off on an extremely specific mission.  Did it work? Find out on this week's podcast.</p>\n<p>And hey! Chicago peeps, get your tix to our <a href=\"http://blogs.wnyc.org/radiolab/2008/10/08/get-yer-tickets-war-of-the-worlds-live-in-chicago/\">LIVE EVENT</a></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-10-21T01:27:16", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91868-sperm-tales", "title"=>"Sperm Tales", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast100708.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91868", "description"=>"<p>In today’s podcast, a teaser for our hour-long <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/2008/dec/01/\">Sperm</a> show. If you think you learned all there is to know from that junior high school filmstrip, think again.</p>\n<p> </p><p>We give you two short pieces on sperm that hint at the new ideas and amazing stories we came across once we started following the trail of this wriggly little cell. First, in a twisted tale of twisted tails, fertility specialist <a href=\"http://www.crb.wsu.edu/3FacultyPages/Ellington.html\">Joanna Ellington</a>, cofounder of <a href=\"http://www.preseed.com/TheCompany/Who_We_Are.php\">ING Fertility</a>, gives Robert a guided tour of all the sperm that are doomed to fail. Then <a href=\"http://www.shef.ac.uk/aps/staff/acadstaff/birkhead.html\">Tim Birkhead</a>, a biologist at the University of Sheffield, tells Jad and Robert how a dead wood mouse completely upended the idea that it’s “every sperm for himself.”</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"<p>Two short pieces on sperm that hint at the new ideas and amazing stories we came across once we started following the trail of this wriggly little cell.</p>", "publishTime"=>1223358865000, "publish-at"=>"2008-10-07T01:54:25", "date-line-ts"=>1223358865, "estimated-duration"=>526, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Sperm drawing", "name"=>"photologue/photos/sperm_drawing.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/c/80/photologue/photos/sperm_drawing.jpg", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Sperm drawing", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/rollertrain/3856763327/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/sperm_drawing.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>27175, "credits-name"=>"libby lynn"}, "objectID"=>"91868", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Sperm Tales", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In today’s podcast, a teaser for our hour-long <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/2008/dec/01/\">Sperm</a> show. If you think you learned all there is to know from that junior high school filmstrip, think again.</p>\n<p> </p><p>We give you two short pieces on sperm that hint at the new ideas and amazing stories we came across once we started following the trail of this wriggly little cell. First, in a twisted tale of twisted tails, fertility specialist <a href=\"http://www.crb.wsu.edu/3FacultyPages/Ellington.html\">Joanna Ellington</a>, cofounder of <a href=\"http://www.preseed.com/TheCompany/Who_We_Are.php\">ING Fertility</a>, gives Robert a guided tour of all the sperm that are doomed to fail. Then <a href=\"http://www.shef.ac.uk/aps/staff/acadstaff/birkhead.html\">Tim Birkhead</a>, a biologist at the University of Sheffield, tells Jad and Robert how a dead wood mouse completely upended the idea that it’s “every sperm for himself.”</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-10-07T01:54:25", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91867-chasing-bugs", "title"=>"Chasing Bugs", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast092308.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91867", "description"=>"<p>Remember the first time you ever saw an ant hill? That parade of black insects pouring in and out of a small sand mound...most of us stopped, looked and then moved on to other parts of the playground. <a href=\"http://www.eowilson.org/\">E. O. Wilson</a> is the kid who never took his eyes off the mound.</p><p>He grew up to revolutionize the fields of entomology, sociobiology and conservationist thought. E. O. (E is for Edward, O is for Osborne) got a nod from Time Magazine on their list of the 25 Most Influential People in America and picked up a few Pulitzers along the way. But before all that he was just an eight-year-old boy in the South whose nickname was 'Bugs.'</p>\n<p>Ed and Robert Krulwich spoke a few years ago at the <a href=\"http://www.92y.org/\">92nd Street Y</a> in Manhattan about Ed's early insect-philia and how it blossomed. Ed tells Robert about the time he figured out how to make hundreds of ants trace his name and the time he convinced an ant colony one of their ants was <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=grbSQ6O6kbs&amp;feature=related\">dead</a> when it was anything but.</p>\n<p>If you like this conversation, stay tuned for Season 5. We are working on a whole show devoted to people falling in (and out of) love with science. Can't wait? Bugs crawling on your skin now? Re-visit Ed and other ant enthusiasts in our <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2005/02/18\">Emergence episode</a>.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Remember the first time you ever saw an ant hill? That parade of black insects pouring in and out of a small sand mound...most of us stopped, looked, and then moved on to other parts ...", "publishTime"=>1222208223000, "publish-at"=>"2008-09-23T18:17:03", "date-line-ts"=>1222208223, "estimated-duration"=>1229, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Ant", "name"=>"photologue/photos/ant.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/c/80/photologue/photos/ant.jpg", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Ant", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/itsgreg/26686026/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/ant.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>27176, "credits-name"=>"It'sGreg"}, "objectID"=>"91867", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Chasing Bugs", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Remember the first time you ever saw an ant hill? That parade of black insects pouring in and out of a small sand mound...most of us stopped, looked and then moved on to other parts of the playground. <a href=\"http://www.eowilson.org/\">E. O. Wilson</a> is the kid who never took his eyes off the mound.</p><p>He grew up to revolutionize the fields of entomology, sociobiology and conservationist thought. E. O. (E is for Edward, O is for Osborne) got a nod from Time Magazine on their list of the 25 Most Influential People in America and picked up a few Pulitzers along the way. But before all that he was just an eight-year-old boy in the South whose nickname was 'Bugs.'</p>\n<p>Ed and Robert Krulwich spoke a few years ago at the <a href=\"http://www.92y.org/\">92nd Street Y</a> in Manhattan about Ed's early insect-philia and how it blossomed. Ed tells Robert about the time he figured out how to make hundreds of ants trace his name and the time he convinced an ant colony one of their ants was <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=grbSQ6O6kbs&amp;feature=related\">dead</a> when it was anything but.</p>\n<p>If you like this conversation, stay tuned for Season 5. We are working on a whole show devoted to people falling in (and out of) love with science. Can't wait? Bugs crawling on your skin now? Re-visit Ed and other ant enthusiasts in our <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2005/02/18\">Emergence episode</a>.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-09-23T18:17:03", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91863-making-the-hippo-dance", "title"=>"Making the Hippo Dance", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast090908.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91863", "description"=>"<p>We play some never-released tape from the vault, and reveal a bit about what techniques we used to try and make it sing.</p>\n<p> </p><p>Earlier this year, Jad and Robert visited the <a href=\"http://www.koshland-science-museum.org/\">Koshland Science Museum</a> in Washington D.C. to give listeners a behind-the-scenes look at Radiolab. The question here is just how far can you go in the name of making an idea clear? What's allowed? Is music allowed? Are sound effects allowed? What helps? What hurts?</p>\n<p>Also, if you enjoyed this conversation, you may want to check out the other Radiolab process talks, like this one at <a href=\"http://blogs.wnyc.org/radiolab/2008/05/06/jad-and-robert-the-early-days/\">Oberlin College</a> in the spring and another last fall at the <a href=\"http://blogs.wnyc.org/radiolab/2007/11/09/making-radio-lab/\">Apple store</a> in New York. And if you didn't enjoy...don't worry--new Radiolab shows are coming soon!</p>", "tease"=>"<p>We play some never-released tape from the vault, and reveal a bit about what techniques we used to try and make it sing.</p>", "publishTime"=>1220937057000, "publish-at"=>"2008-09-09T01:10:57", "date-line-ts"=>1220937057, "estimated-duration"=>1747, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Hippos", "name"=>"photologue/photos/making_hippo_dance.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/c/80/photologue/photos/making_hippo_dance.jpg", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Hippos", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/wwarby/4915167765/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/making_hippo_dance.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>65993, "credits-name"=>"wwarby"}, "objectID"=>"91863", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Making the Hippo Dance", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>We play some never-released tape from the vault, and reveal a bit about what techniques we used to try and make it sing.</p>\n<p> </p><p>Earlier this year, Jad and Robert visited the <a href=\"http://www.koshland-science-museum.org/\">Koshland Science Museum</a> in Washington D.C. to give listeners a behind-the-scenes look at Radiolab. The question here is just how far can you go in the name of making an idea clear? What's allowed? Is music allowed? Are sound effects allowed? What helps? What hurts?</p>\n<p>Also, if you enjoyed this conversation, you may want to check out the other Radiolab process talks, like this one at <a href=\"http://blogs.wnyc.org/radiolab/2008/05/06/jad-and-robert-the-early-days/\">Oberlin College</a> in the spring and another last fall at the <a href=\"http://blogs.wnyc.org/radiolab/2007/11/09/making-radio-lab/\">Apple store</a> in New York. And if you didn't enjoy...don't worry--new Radiolab shows are coming soon!</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-09-09T01:10:57", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91862-quantum-cello", "title"=>"Quantum Cello", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast082608.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91862", "description"=>"<p>Jad and cellist Zoe Keating discuss the physics (if not metaphysics) of looping sound and how to use a 17th century instrument to make avant-garde electronic music.</p><p><span class=\"small\"> </span>Zoe Keating is the cellist from our live show, <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2008/03/07\">War of the Worlds</a>. She used to play with the band Rasputina and now solos and records music for films, such as horror flick, 'The Devil's Chair' and a PBS documentary on Lincoln's assassination. Her music process reminded us a bit of ours (looping and layering sound) so she and Jad sat down together in San Francisco to talk shop and listen to some unreleased stuff off her new album (as of yet untitled).</p>\n<p>You can see her on <a href=\"http://www.zoekeating.com/perform.html\">tour</a> with Amanda Palmer of the Dresden Dolls in September and October. You can also check out her album,<br> <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/B000CAKQ0M/wnycorg-20\" target=\"_blank\">One Cello X 16: Natoma</a>. Read more about her <a href=\"http://www.zoekeating.com\">here</a>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><br></span></p>", "tease"=>"<p>Jad and cellist Zoe Keating discuss the physics (if not metaphysics) of looping sound, and how to use a 17th century instrument to make avant-garde electronic music.</p>", "publishTime"=>1219701643000, "publish-at"=>"2008-08-25T18:00:43", "date-line-ts"=>1219701643, "estimated-duration"=>2127, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Zoe Keating", "name"=>"photologue/photos/zoe_for_blog.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/360/240/c/80/photologue/photos/zoe_for_blog.jpg", "h"=>240, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Zoe Keating", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/zoe_for_blog.jpg", "w"=>360, "id"=>27178, "credits-name"=>"Lane Hartwell"}, "objectID"=>"91862", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Quantum Cello", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Jad and cellist Zoe Keating discuss the physics (if not metaphysics) of looping sound and how to use a 17th century instrument to make avant-garde electronic music.</p><p><span class=\"small\"> </span>Zoe Keating is the cellist from our live show, <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2008/03/07\">War of the Worlds</a>. She used to play with the band Rasputina and now solos and records music for films, such as horror flick, 'The Devil's Chair' and a PBS documentary on Lincoln's assassination. Her music process reminded us a bit of ours (looping and layering sound) so she and Jad sat down together in San Francisco to talk shop and listen to some unreleased stuff off her new album (as of yet untitled).</p>\n<p>You can see her on <a href=\"http://www.zoekeating.com/perform.html\">tour</a> with Amanda Palmer of the Dresden Dolls in September and October. You can also check out her album,<br> <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/B000CAKQ0M/wnycorg-20\" target=\"_blank\">One Cello X 16: Natoma</a>. Read more about her <a href=\"http://www.zoekeating.com\">here</a>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><br></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-08-25T18:00:43", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91859-the-multi-universes", "title"=>"The (Multi) Universe(s)", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast081208.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91859", "description"=>"<p>Robert and Brian Greene discuss what's beyond the horizon of our universe, what you might wear in infinite universes with finite pairs of designer shoes, and why the Universe and swiss cheese have more in common than you think.</p><p><span class=\"small\"> </span>Have you wondered if there is another you out there? Somewhere? Sitting in the same chair, reading the same blog post, wearing the same clothes and thinking the same thoughts? Well, Brian Greene says there must be one. Or two. Or lots and lots and lots and lots and...  Why? You ask, well listen to Greene's argument in this week's podcast.</p>\n<p>We are still furiously working on Season 5, so while you wait we bring you today's podcast of a conversation between Robert Krulwich and <a href=\"http://www.columbia.edu/cu/physics/fac-bios/Greene/faculty.html\">Brian Greene</a>, physics and mathematics professor and director of the Institute of Strings, Cosmology, and Astroparticle Physics at Columbia University. The interview is part of a series called '<a href=\"http://www.92y.org/shop/event_detail.asp?productid=T%2DLC5PF02\">Giants of Science</a>' hosted by venerable New York institution, the 92nd St Y.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>PLEASE NOTE: Our apologies, there's some noise at the end of the recording, please don't be alarmed! It's us, not you.</p>\n<p>You can see a video of Brian talking about string theory <a href=\"http://www.ted.com/index.php/talks/brian_greene_on_string_theory.html\">here</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"Robert and Brian Greene discuss what's beyond the horizon of our universe, what you might wear in infinite universes with finite pairs of designer shoes, and why the Universe and swis...", "publishTime"=>1218517417000, "publish-at"=>"2008-08-12T01:03:37", "date-line-ts"=>1218517417, "estimated-duration"=>3126, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Multi tees", "name"=>"photologue/photos/multi_tees.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/427/c/80/photologue/photos/multi_tees.jpg", "h"=>427, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Multi tees", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/cayusa/384187184/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/multi_tees.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>27179, "credits-name"=>"Cayusa"}, "objectID"=>"91859", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The (Multi) Universe(s)", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Robert and Brian Greene discuss what's beyond the horizon of our universe, what you might wear in infinite universes with finite pairs of designer shoes, and why the Universe and swiss cheese have more in common than you think.</p><p><span class=\"small\"> </span>Have you wondered if there is another you out there? Somewhere? Sitting in the same chair, reading the same blog post, wearing the same clothes and thinking the same thoughts? Well, Brian Greene says there must be one. Or two. Or lots and lots and lots and lots and...  Why? You ask, well listen to Greene's argument in this week's podcast.</p>\n<p>We are still furiously working on Season 5, so while you wait we bring you today's podcast of a conversation between Robert Krulwich and <a href=\"http://www.columbia.edu/cu/physics/fac-bios/Greene/faculty.html\">Brian Greene</a>, physics and mathematics professor and director of the Institute of Strings, Cosmology, and Astroparticle Physics at Columbia University. The interview is part of a series called '<a href=\"http://www.92y.org/shop/event_detail.asp?productid=T%2DLC5PF02\">Giants of Science</a>' hosted by venerable New York institution, the 92nd St Y.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>PLEASE NOTE: Our apologies, there's some noise at the end of the recording, please don't be alarmed! It's us, not you.</p>\n<p>You can see a video of Brian talking about string theory <a href=\"http://www.ted.com/index.php/talks/brian_greene_on_string_theory.html\">here</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-08-12T01:03:37", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91852-tell-me-a-story", "title"=>"Tell Me A Story", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast072908.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91852", "description"=>"<p>Robert Krulwich's commencement speech at <a href=\"http://pr.caltech.edu/commencement/\">California Institute of Technology</a> gets at the heart of what we do here at Radiolab.</p><p>It's a treat to hear his passion. We enjoyed it. And we thought you might too.</p>", "tease"=>"Robert Krulwich's commencement speech at California Institute of Technology gets at the heart of what we do here at Radiolab. It's a treat to hear his passion. We enjoyed it. And we t...", "publishTime"=>1217308000000, "publish-at"=>"2008-07-29T01:06:40", "date-line-ts"=>1217308000, "estimated-duration"=>1597, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Robert Krulwich", "name"=>"photologue/photos/krulwichgrad.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/350/234/c/80/photologue/photos/krulwichgrad.jpg", "h"=>234, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Robert Krulwich", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/krulwichgrad.jpg", "w"=>350, "id"=>27180, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"91852", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Tell Me A Story", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Robert Krulwich's commencement speech at <a href=\"http://pr.caltech.edu/commencement/\">California Institute of Technology</a> gets at the heart of what we do here at Radiolab.</p><p>It's a treat to hear his passion. We enjoyed it. And we thought you might too.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-07-29T01:06:40", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91841-city-x", "title"=>"City X", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast070108.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91841", "description"=>"<p>This week, a piece from one of our favorite radio-makers, <a href=\"http://www.prx.org/user/Jonathan/pieces\">Jonathan Mitchell</a>. 'City X' is a history of the modern shopping mall through perspectives of people living in a real, yet unnamed, city.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">Using a sound rich audio mosaic of observations and ruminations, all scored to Muzak, the universal mall experience comes to life, for better or for worse.</div>\n<p>City X was commissioned by <a href=\"http://hearingvoices.com/\">Hearing Voices</a> with funding from the Corporation for Public Broadcasting.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"<p>This week, a piece from one of our favorite radio-makers, Jonathan Mitchell. 'City X' is a history of the modern shopping mall through perspectives of people living in a real, yet unnamed, city.</p>", "publishTime"=>1214888697000, "publish-at"=>"2008-07-01T01:04:57", "date-line-ts"=>1214888697, "estimated-duration"=>1476, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Mall", "name"=>"photologue/photos/shopping_mall.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/c/80/photologue/photos/shopping_mall.jpg", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Mall", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/barbietron/105031209/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/shopping_mall.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>27181, "credits-name"=>"SWANclothing"}, "objectID"=>"91841", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"City X", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This week, a piece from one of our favorite radio-makers, <a href=\"http://www.prx.org/user/Jonathan/pieces\">Jonathan Mitchell</a>. 'City X' is a history of the modern shopping mall through perspectives of people living in a real, yet unnamed, city.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">Using a sound rich audio mosaic of observations and ruminations, all scored to Muzak, the universal mall experience comes to life, for better or for worse.</div>\n<p>City X was commissioned by <a href=\"http://hearingvoices.com/\">Hearing Voices</a> with funding from the Corporation for Public Broadcasting.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-07-01T01:04:57", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91838-earworms", "title"=>"Earworms", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast061708.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91838", "description"=>"<p>First, we asked you to tell us <a href=\"http://blogs.wnyc.org/radiolab/2008/02/11/the-worlds-stickiest-song/\">what song gets stuck in your head</a>. Then, we asked you<a href=\"http://blogs.wnyc.org/radiolab/2008/02/09/how-to-unstick-a-song-stuck-in-your-head/\"> how you got it out</a>. Finally, we made a podcast. Thank you to everyone who called in, shared their secret techniques, and sang without shame.</p><p>Your suggestions ranged from the hilarious (Darth Vader breathing) to the malicious (give it to some one else) to the oddly-aligned (multiple people called in suggesting 'Girl from Ipanema' as a cure-all earworm). And now, we release your wisdom to the masses. We hope that this will be of help to <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2008/03/21\">earworm-sufferers</a>, but be forewarned, it might just plague you with Journey.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"First, we asked you to tell us what song gets stuck in your head. Then, we asked you how you got it out. Finally, we made a podcast. Thank you to everyone who called in, shared their ...", "publishTime"=>1213592492000, "publish-at"=>"2008-06-16T01:01:32", "date-line-ts"=>1213678892, "estimated-duration"=>476, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Worm plus ear", "name"=>"photologue/photos/ear_worms.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/", "name"=>"Wikimedia Commons"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/271/h/80/photologue/photos/ear_worms.jpg", "h"=>271, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"Worm plus ear", "credits-url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ATidens_naturl%C3%A6re_fig40.png", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/ear_worms.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>73308, "credits-name"=>"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons"}, "objectID"=>"91838", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Earworms", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>First, we asked you to tell us <a href=\"http://blogs.wnyc.org/radiolab/2008/02/11/the-worlds-stickiest-song/\">what song gets stuck in your head</a>. Then, we asked you<a href=\"http://blogs.wnyc.org/radiolab/2008/02/09/how-to-unstick-a-song-stuck-in-your-head/\"> how you got it out</a>. Finally, we made a podcast. Thank you to everyone who called in, shared their secret techniques, and sang without shame.</p><p>Your suggestions ranged from the hilarious (Darth Vader breathing) to the malicious (give it to some one else) to the oddly-aligned (multiple people called in suggesting 'Girl from Ipanema' as a cure-all earworm). And now, we release your wisdom to the masses. We hope that this will be of help to <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2008/03/21\">earworm-sufferers</a>, but be forewarned, it might just plague you with Journey.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-06-16T01:01:32", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91836-wordless-music", "title"=>"Wordless Music", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast060308.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91836", "description"=>"<p>On this week's podcast, we share an excerpt from <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/music/articles/100215\">Wordless Music on WNYC</a>, a 4-part music program hosted by Jad, exploring the boundaries between classical and pop music.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">The series pairs rock and electronic musicians with more traditional chamber and new music performers, to create an entirely new concert experience. On this week's selection, Jad waxes googly-eyed fan when he gets to talk about one of his favorite bands, <a href=\"http://www.myspace.com/starsofthelid\">Stars of the Lid</a>.</div>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"An excerpt from Wordless Music on WNYC, a 4-part music program hosted by Jad, exploring the boundaries between classical and pop music. Jad waxes googly-eyed fan when he gets to talk ...", "publishTime"=>1212516037000, "publish-at"=>"2008-06-03T14:00:37", "date-line-ts"=>1212516037, "estimated-duration"=>1401, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Wordless Music", "name"=>"photologue/photos/100220-6.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/211/211/c/80/photologue/photos/100220-6.jpg", "h"=>211, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Lure Design", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/100220-6.jpg", "w"=>211, "id"=>315, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"91836", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Wordless Music", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>On this week's podcast, we share an excerpt from <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/music/articles/100215\">Wordless Music on WNYC</a>, a 4-part music program hosted by Jad, exploring the boundaries between classical and pop music.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">The series pairs rock and electronic musicians with more traditional chamber and new music performers, to create an entirely new concert experience. On this week's selection, Jad waxes googly-eyed fan when he gets to talk about one of his favorite bands, <a href=\"http://www.myspace.com/starsofthelid\">Stars of the Lid</a>.</div>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-06-03T14:00:37", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91831-open-outcry", "title"=>"Open Outcry", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast052008.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91831", "description"=>"<p>Jad presents a piece by one of his favorite producers: Ben Rubin. Rubin created this audio portrait called 'Open Outcry' as a part of a sound installation called <a href=\"http://www.creativetime.org/programs/archive/2002/SonicGarden/sonicgarden/a_rubin.html\">Sonic Garden</a> commissioned to celebrate the reopening of the Winter Garden, an atrium space within the World Financial Center, after 9/11.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">The trading floor of the <a href=\"http://www.nymex.com\">New York Mercantile Exchange</a> may look and sound chaotic to the uninitiated, with circles of hundreds of traders shouting unintelligible phonic abbreviations and numbers back and forth. But it's a complex and sophisticated human system in flux and since 1872, the mosh pit full of traders has driven the prices of energy, metals, livestock and other commodities through this open outcry trading. The trading floor of the NYMEX was destroyed in the attacks of September 11, 2001 when the building that houses it, the World Financial Center, was seriously damaged.</div>\n<p>Want to learn more about this piece? Ben did an interesting <a href=\"http://www.thirdcoastfestival.org/behind_scenes_rubin.asp\">interview</a> for the Third Coast International Audio Festival and you can learn more about this piece and his approach <a href=\"http://www.thirdcoastfestival.org/behind_scenes_rubin.asp\">here</a>.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Jad presents a piece by one of his favorite producers: Ben Rubin. This audio portrait, called 'Open Outcry,' visits the trading floor of the New York Mercantile Exchange, with its hun...", "publishTime"=>1211260906000, "publish-at"=>"2008-05-20T01:21:46", "date-line-ts"=>1211260906, "estimated-duration"=>594, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Traders on the floor of the New York Mercantile Exchange on August 4, 2010 in New York City", "name"=>"photologue/photos/market_economy.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1000/667/c/80/photologue/photos/market_economy.jpg", "h"=>667, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Traders on the floor of the New York Mercantile Exchange on August 4, 2010 in New York City", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/market_economy.jpg", "w"=>1000, "id"=>25753, "credits-name"=>"Getty Images"}, "objectID"=>"91831", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Open Outcry", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Jad presents a piece by one of his favorite producers: Ben Rubin. Rubin created this audio portrait called 'Open Outcry' as a part of a sound installation called <a href=\"http://www.creativetime.org/programs/archive/2002/SonicGarden/sonicgarden/a_rubin.html\">Sonic Garden</a> commissioned to celebrate the reopening of the Winter Garden, an atrium space within the World Financial Center, after 9/11.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">The trading floor of the <a href=\"http://www.nymex.com\">New York Mercantile Exchange</a> may look and sound chaotic to the uninitiated, with circles of hundreds of traders shouting unintelligible phonic abbreviations and numbers back and forth. But it's a complex and sophisticated human system in flux and since 1872, the mosh pit full of traders has driven the prices of energy, metals, livestock and other commodities through this open outcry trading. The trading floor of the NYMEX was destroyed in the attacks of September 11, 2001 when the building that houses it, the World Financial Center, was seriously damaged.</div>\n<p>Want to learn more about this piece? Ben did an interesting <a href=\"http://www.thirdcoastfestival.org/behind_scenes_rubin.asp\">interview</a> for the Third Coast International Audio Festival and you can learn more about this piece and his approach <a href=\"http://www.thirdcoastfestival.org/behind_scenes_rubin.asp\">here</a>.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-05-20T01:21:46", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91820-jad-and-robert-the-early-years", "title"=>"Jad and Robert: The Early Years", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast050608.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91820", "description"=>"<p>Ever wonder how Jad and Robert met?</p><p>Well it all began with an everyday encounter where they discovered they both went to the same small liberal arts college in Ohio. For this week's podcast, the guys go on stage at Oberlin College to tell the tale of their meeting and how they started tinkering around with tape to come up with the <em>Radiolab </em> you know today.</p>\n<p><strong>Vintage Radiolab alert!</strong> You'll hear the very first piece Jad and Robert made together. It's an audio-experiment called 'Flag Day' that they submitted to <em><a href=\"http://www.thisamericanlife.org/\">This American Life</a></em>. <em>TAL</em>'s Ira Glass and Julie Snyder phone in to share what they thought of it.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"<p>Ever wonder how Jad and Robert met? They tell their tale on stage at Oberlin College, and talk about how they started tinkering around with tape to come up with the Radiolab you know today.</p>", "publishTime"=>1210052597000, "publish-at"=>"2008-05-06T01:43:17", "date-line-ts"=>1210052597, "estimated-duration"=>0, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Robert Krulwich and Jad Abumrad", "name"=>"photologue/photos/jad_and_robert_breakfast.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/350/244/c/80/photologue/photos/jad_and_robert_breakfast.jpg", "h"=>244, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Robert Krulwich and Jad Abumrad", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/jad_and_robert_breakfast.jpg", "w"=>350, "id"=>27201, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"91820", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Jad and Robert: The Early Years", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Ever wonder how Jad and Robert met?</p><p>Well it all began with an everyday encounter where they discovered they both went to the same small liberal arts college in Ohio. For this week's podcast, the guys go on stage at Oberlin College to tell the tale of their meeting and how they started tinkering around with tape to come up with the <em>Radiolab </em> you know today.</p>\n<p><strong>Vintage Radiolab alert!</strong> You'll hear the very first piece Jad and Robert made together. It's an audio-experiment called 'Flag Day' that they submitted to <em><a href=\"http://www.thisamericanlife.org/\">This American Life</a></em>. <em>TAL</em>'s Ira Glass and Julie Snyder phone in to share what they thought of it.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-05-06T01:43:17", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91596-so-called-life", "title"=>"(So-Called) Life", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab031408.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91596", "description"=>"<p>In a world where biology and engineering intersect, how do you decide what's \"natural\"?</p><p>Biotechnology is making it easier and easier to create new forms of life, but what are the consequences when humans play with life? We travel back to the first billion years of life on Earth, take a look at how modern engineers tinker with living things, and meet a woman who could have been two people.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"<p>The uneasy marriage of biology and engineering raises big questions about the nature of life.</p>", "publishTime"=>1207583282000, "publish-at"=>"2008-04-07T11:48:02", "date-line-ts"=>1207540800, "estimated-duration"=>3413, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"DNA Double Helix", "name"=>"photologue/photos/92802.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/187/200/c/80/photologue/photos/92802.png", "h"=>200, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DNA_double_helix_45.PNG", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/92802.png", "w"=>187, "id"=>26455, "credits-name"=>"WikimediaCommons"}, "objectID"=>"91596", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"(So-Called) Life", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In a world where biology and engineering intersect, how do you decide what's \"natural\"?</p><p>Biotechnology is making it easier and easier to create new forms of life, but what are the consequences when humans play with life? We travel back to the first billion years of life on Earth, take a look at how modern engineers tinker with living things, and meet a woman who could have been two people.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-04-07T11:48:02", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91629-pop-music", "title"=>"Pop Music", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab032108.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91629", "description"=>"<p>This hour of Radiolab: pop music's pull.</p><p>Some songs have the nefarious power to stick mercilessly in our heads, and some songs have the transcendent allure to overcome cultural differences. We ask how songwriters create these songs seemingly out of the ether, listen in on the music a deaf man hears, and examine the timeless appeal of the Elvis of Afghanistan.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>For transcripts, see individual segment pages.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Nightmarish stories of musical hallucinations, songs with the power to transcend language,&nbsp;and the triumphant return of the Elvis of Afghanistan.", "publishTime"=>1206114511000, "publish-at"=>"2008-03-21T11:48:31", "date-line-ts"=>1208793600, "estimated-duration"=>3416, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"vinyl record", "name"=>"photologue/photos/92804.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/200/150/c/80/photologue/photos/92804.jpg", "h"=>150, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/dongraft/309172490", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/92804.jpg", "w"=>200, "id"=>26467, "credits-name"=>"dongraft"}, "objectID"=>"91629", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Pop Music", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This hour of Radiolab: pop music's pull.</p><p>Some songs have the nefarious power to stick mercilessly in our heads, and some songs have the transcendent allure to overcome cultural differences. We ask how songwriters create these songs seemingly out of the ether, listen in on the music a deaf man hears, and examine the timeless appeal of the Elvis of Afghanistan.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>For transcripts, see individual segment pages.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-03-21T11:48:31", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91588-laughter", "title"=>"Laughter", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab022208.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91588", "description"=>"<p>We all laugh. This hour of Radiolab asks why.</p><p>If you look closely, you'll find that humor has very little to do with it. We ask what makes us laugh, and how it affects us. Along the way, we tickle some rats, listen in on a baby's first laugh, talk to a group of professional laughers, and travel to Tanzania to investigate an outbreak of contagious laughter.</p>", "tease"=>"We all laugh. This hour of Radiolab asks why.", "publishTime"=>1203958073000, "publish-at"=>"2008-02-25T11:47:53", "date-line-ts"=>1203958800, "estimated-duration"=>3415, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/92796.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/200/133/c/80/photologue/photos/92796.jpg", "h"=>133, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/summerluu/2596493753/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/92796.jpg", "w"=>200, "id"=>26451, "credits-name"=>"summerluu"}, "objectID"=>"91588", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Laughter", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>We all laugh. This hour of Radiolab asks why.</p><p>If you look closely, you'll find that humor has very little to do with it. We ask what makes us laugh, and how it affects us. Along the way, we tickle some rats, listen in on a baby's first laugh, talk to a group of professional laughers, and travel to Tanzania to investigate an outbreak of contagious laughter.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-02-25T11:47:53", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91763-our-podcast-comes-in-all-shapes-and-sizes", "title"=>"Our Podcast comes in all shapes and sizes", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast021108.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91763", "description"=>"<p>Jad plays one of his favorite pieces of all time, 'IF' by <a href=\"http://www.sounddesign.unimelb.edu.au/web/biogs/P000334b.htm\">Sherre DeLys.</a></p><div class=\"rtimage\"></div>\n<p>We've been getting some emails from some of you who are confused about the varying lengths of our podcasts: some are long, some are short. Fear not! There's nothing wrong with your download. That's the way it should be. Sometimes we podcast an entire hour-long episode. Sometimes we podcast a shorter piece that may only be 8 minutes or so. That's just how we roll.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"<p>Jad plays one of his favorite pieces of all time, 'IF' by Sherre DeLys.</p>", "publishTime"=>1202772192000, "publish-at"=>"2008-02-11T18:23:12", "date-line-ts"=>1202772192, "estimated-duration"=>502, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Eggs of different sizes", "name"=>"photologue/photos/podcast_shapes_sizes.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/540/640/c/80/photologue/photos/podcast_shapes_sizes.jpg", "h"=>640, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Eggs of different sizes", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/66176388@N00/442106088/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/podcast_shapes_sizes.jpg", "w"=>540, "id"=>65989, "credits-name"=>"me'nthedogs"}, "objectID"=>"91763", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Our Podcast comes in all shapes and sizes", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Jad plays one of his favorite pieces of all time, 'IF' by <a href=\"http://www.sounddesign.unimelb.edu.au/web/biogs/P000334b.htm\">Sherre DeLys.</a></p><div class=\"rtimage\"></div>\n<p>We've been getting some emails from some of you who are confused about the varying lengths of our podcasts: some are long, some are short. Fear not! There's nothing wrong with your download. That's the way it should be. Sometimes we podcast an entire hour-long episode. Sometimes we podcast a shorter piece that may only be 8 minutes or so. That's just how we roll.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-02-11T18:23:12", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91753-salle-des-departs", "title"=>"Salle Des Departs", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab012908pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91753", "description"=>"<p>Imagine that you're a composer. Imagine getting the commission to write a song that will allow family members to face the death of a loved one.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">Well, composer <a href=\"http://www.bangonacan.org/about_us/david_lang\">David Lang</a> had to do just that when a hospital in Garches, France, asked him to write music for their morgue, or <a href=\"http://www.bbc.co.uk/radio4/science/lastgoodbye.shtml\">'Salle Des Departs.'</a> What do you do? What should death sound like? Producer <a href=\"http://www.strangemusic.com/JGonzales.htm\">Jocelyn Gonzales</a> brings us this piece about David Lang and his commission for the “Salle Des Departs.”</div>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Imagine that you're a composer. Imagine getting the commission to write a song that will allow family members to face the death of a loved one. David Lang had to do just that when a h...", "publishTime"=>1201629022000, "publish-at"=>"2008-01-29T12:50:22", "date-line-ts"=>1201629022, "estimated-duration"=>635, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Stained glass window", "name"=>"photologue/photos/salle.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/450/c/80/photologue/photos/salle.jpg", "h"=>450, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Stained glass window", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/hisgett/2810485049/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/salle.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>65987, "credits-name"=>"ahisgett"}, "objectID"=>"91753", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Salle Des Departs", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Imagine that you're a composer. Imagine getting the commission to write a song that will allow family members to face the death of a loved one.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">Well, composer <a href=\"http://www.bangonacan.org/about_us/david_lang\">David Lang</a> had to do just that when a hospital in Garches, France, asked him to write music for their morgue, or <a href=\"http://www.bbc.co.uk/radio4/science/lastgoodbye.shtml\">'Salle Des Departs.'</a> What do you do? What should death sound like? Producer <a href=\"http://www.strangemusic.com/JGonzales.htm\">Jocelyn Gonzales</a> brings us this piece about David Lang and his commission for the “Salle Des Departs.”</div>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2008-01-29T12:50:22", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91750-the-ring-and-i", "title"=>"The Ring and I", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab010108pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91750", "description"=>"<p>On this Radiolab/WNYC Special, we explore the impact and influence of Wagner's Ring Cycle on the Metropolitan Opera's 2004 Presentation.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">It might seem hyperbole to claim, as many Wagnerites do, that The Ring Cycle is 'The Greatest Work of Art Ever.' But the grandeur and power of this monumental work have permeated our culture from Star Wars to Bugs Bunny to J.R.R. Tolkien.</div>\n<p>This piece includes the voices of Howard Shore, Oscar-winning composer of The Lord of the Rings, Playwrite Tony Kushner, Joe Clark, technical director for the Metropolitan Opera, Alex Ross, music critic of The New Yorker magazine, Jungian Psychologist Laurie Layton Shapira, Seattle Opera director Speight Jenkins, Guitarist Gary Lucas, Fred Plotkin, Food &amp; Opera Writer; Will Berger, author of Wagner without Fear, and John Rockwell, cultural correspondent for The New York Times.</p>\n<p>For more information about this episode go <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/articles/music/2004/mar/02/the-ring-and-i-the-passion-the-myth-the-mania/\">here</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"It might seem hyperbole to claim, as many Wagnerites do, that The Ring Cycle is 'The Greatest Work of Art Ever.' But it's permeated our culture from Star Wars to Bugs Bunny to J.R.R. ...", "publishTime"=>1199120893000, "publish-at"=>"2007-12-31T12:08:13", "date-line-ts"=>1199202549, "estimated-duration"=>3539, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"'The Rhine's fair children, / Bewailing their lost gold, weep.'", "name"=>"photologue/photos/ring_and_i.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/", "name"=>"Wikimedia Commons"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/868/c/80/photologue/photos/ring_and_i.jpg", "h"=>868, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"\"The Rhine's fair children, / Bewailing their lost gold, weep.\"", "credits-url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhinegold_and_the_Valkyries_p_072.jpg", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/ring_and_i.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>67285, "credits-name"=>"Arthur Rackham"}, "objectID"=>"91750", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Ring and I", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>On this Radiolab/WNYC Special, we explore the impact and influence of Wagner's Ring Cycle on the Metropolitan Opera's 2004 Presentation.</p><div class=\"rtimage\">It might seem hyperbole to claim, as many Wagnerites do, that The Ring Cycle is 'The Greatest Work of Art Ever.' But the grandeur and power of this monumental work have permeated our culture from Star Wars to Bugs Bunny to J.R.R. Tolkien.</div>\n<p>This piece includes the voices of Howard Shore, Oscar-winning composer of The Lord of the Rings, Playwrite Tony Kushner, Joe Clark, technical director for the Metropolitan Opera, Alex Ross, music critic of The New Yorker magazine, Jungian Psychologist Laurie Layton Shapira, Seattle Opera director Speight Jenkins, Guitarist Gary Lucas, Fred Plotkin, Food &amp; Opera Writer; Will Berger, author of Wagner without Fear, and John Rockwell, cultural correspondent for The New York Times.</p>\n<p>For more information about this episode go <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/articles/music/2004/mar/02/the-ring-and-i-the-passion-the-myth-the-mania/\">here</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2007-12-31T12:08:13", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2009=>[{"slug"=>"91934-in-c", "title"=>"In C", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast09inc.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91934", "description"=>"<p>Ok, so last podcast you heard counting babies.  Here’s a new spin...</p><p>Not too long ago, Jad was invited to contribute to <a href=\"http://www.in-c-remixed.com/\">In C Remixed</a>, a compilation of remixed versions of the 1964 Terry Riley piece that quietly changed the world of classical music (and eventually pop music too).  In this podcast, Jad talks to musicians <a href=\"http://www.earspasm.com/\">Michael Lowenstern</a> and <a href=\"http://www.zoekeating.com/\">Zoe Keating</a> about their remixes, what they did and why.  Then Jad plays Robert his own kaleidoscopic remix of In C: minimalism as seen through the lenses of fatherhood and Radiolab. For his version, Jad threw a few counting babies into the musical mix (actually, only one of the babies can count ... the one that isn’t his).</p>\n<p>Special thanks to Amanda Aronczyk and her daughter Mina (the baby who actually counts), to Bill Ryan and the <a href=\"http://newmusicensemble.org/\">Grand Valley State New Music Ensemble</a>, and to Silas Brown and Jennifer Munson (for their engineering expertise).</p>\n<p>Zoe Keeting’s new album, “Into the Trees” will be available shortly.  We can’t wait.</p>\n<p>And Michael Lowenstern, in all his tweaker glory, can be found <a href=\"http://www.earspasm.com\">here</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>Jad talks to musicians Michael Lowenstern and Zoe Keating about their remixes of Terry Riley's In C.</p>", "publishTime"=>1260842429000, "publish-at"=>"2009-12-14T21:00:29", "date-line-ts"=>1260842429, "estimated-duration"=>1140, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"The letter C", "name"=>"photologue/photos/in_c.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/503/640/c/80/photologue/photos/in_c.jpg", "h"=>640, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"The letter C", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/brenda-starr/4459034284/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/in_c.jpg", "w"=>503, "id"=>67087, "credits-name"=>"~Brenda Starr~"}, "objectID"=>"91934", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"In C", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Ok, so last podcast you heard counting babies.  Here’s a new spin...</p><p>Not too long ago, Jad was invited to contribute to <a href=\"http://www.in-c-remixed.com/\">In C Remixed</a>, a compilation of remixed versions of the 1964 Terry Riley piece that quietly changed the world of classical music (and eventually pop music too).  In this podcast, Jad talks to musicians <a href=\"http://www.earspasm.com/\">Michael Lowenstern</a> and <a href=\"http://www.zoekeating.com/\">Zoe Keating</a> about their remixes, what they did and why.  Then Jad plays Robert his own kaleidoscopic remix of In C: minimalism as seen through the lenses of fatherhood and Radiolab. For his version, Jad threw a few counting babies into the musical mix (actually, only one of the babies can count ... the one that isn’t his).</p>\n<p>Special thanks to Amanda Aronczyk and her daughter Mina (the baby who actually counts), to Bill Ryan and the <a href=\"http://newmusicensemble.org/\">Grand Valley State New Music Ensemble</a>, and to Silas Brown and Jennifer Munson (for their engineering expertise).</p>\n<p>Zoe Keeting’s new album, “Into the Trees” will be available shortly.  We can’t wait.</p>\n<p>And Michael Lowenstern, in all his tweaker glory, can be found <a href=\"http://www.earspasm.com\">here</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-12-14T21:00:29", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91697-numbers", "title"=>"Numbers", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab100909.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91697", "description"=>"<p>Whether you love 'em or hate 'em, chances are you rely on numbers every day of your life. Where do they come from, and what do they really do for us? This hour: stories of how numbers confuse us, connect us, and even reveal secrets about us.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Transcripts are on individual segment pages.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Love 'em or hate 'em, you rely on numbers every day. We ask how they confuse us, connect us, &amp; even reveal secrets about us.", "publishTime"=>1259599789000, "publish-at"=>"2009-11-30T11:49:49", "date-line-ts"=>1259600400, "estimated-duration"=>3413, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Numbers", "name"=>"photologue/photos/numbers_.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/numbers_.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Numbers", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/kvh/3366308421/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/numbers_.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>66017, "credits-name"=>"kvanhorn"}, "objectID"=>"91697", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Numbers", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Whether you love 'em or hate 'em, chances are you rely on numbers every day of your life. Where do they come from, and what do they really do for us? This hour: stories of how numbers confuse us, connect us, and even reveal secrets about us.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Transcripts are on individual segment pages.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-11-30T11:49:49", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91930-killing-babies-saving-the-world", "title"=>"Killing Babies, Saving the World", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast09joshgreene.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91930", "description"=>"<p>To get this podcast started, Robert ambushes Jad with a question...a question we've all been dying to ask him since June 10th, 2009, when Amil Abumrad came into the world.</p><p>But fear not, we didn't do a whole podcast just to give the new dad a hard time. Robert talks to <a href=\"http://www.wjh.harvard.edu/~jgreene/\">Josh Greene</a>, the Harvard professor we had on our <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2006/04/28\">Morality</a> show. They revisit some ideas from that show in the context of the big, complicated problems of today (think global warming and nuclear war).  Josh argues that to deal with those problems, we're going to have to learn how to make better use of that tiny part of our brain that handles abstract thinking. Not a simple proposition, but, despite the odds, Josh has hope.</p>", "tease"=>"Robert ambushes Jad with a question we've all been dying to ask him since he became a father. And we revisit some other ideas from our Morality show to think about a few really big mo...", "publishTime"=>1258425045000, "publish-at"=>"2009-11-16T21:30:45", "date-line-ts"=>1258425045, "estimated-duration"=>1126, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Shadows of children", "name"=>"photologue/photos/killing_babies.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/425/c/80/photologue/photos/killing_babies.jpg", "h"=>425, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Shadows of children", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/familymwr/4922472602/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/killing_babies.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>67289, "credits-name"=>"familymwr"}, "objectID"=>"91930", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Killing Babies, Saving the World", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>To get this podcast started, Robert ambushes Jad with a question...a question we've all been dying to ask him since June 10th, 2009, when Amil Abumrad came into the world.</p><p>But fear not, we didn't do a whole podcast just to give the new dad a hard time. Robert talks to <a href=\"http://www.wjh.harvard.edu/~jgreene/\">Josh Greene</a>, the Harvard professor we had on our <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2006/04/28\">Morality</a> show. They revisit some ideas from that show in the context of the big, complicated problems of today (think global warming and nuclear war).  Josh argues that to deal with those problems, we're going to have to learn how to make better use of that tiny part of our brain that handles abstract thinking. Not a simple proposition, but, despite the odds, Josh has hope.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-11-16T21:30:45", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91929-helicopter-boy", "title"=>"Helicopter Boy", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast09helicopter.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91929", "description"=>"<p>In this podcast, a story about a mom, a boy, and a home-made helicopter.</p><p>(And no! This has nothing to do with the Balloon Boy incident.) Instead, it's about how public radio...literally saved a boy's life. Well, not quite. But sorta. Kinda. It's a story about why we do what we do: we're trying to tell stories that move you and make you feel different about the world, even just a little bit.</p>\n<p>Please <strong><a href=\"https://www.wnyc.org/epledge/rl\">support us</a></strong> in that mission.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>A story about a boy, a mom, and a homemade helicopter--and how radio can move you to feel a little bit different about the world.</p>", "publishTime"=>1257226534000, "publish-at"=>"2009-11-03T00:35:34", "date-line-ts"=>1257226534, "estimated-duration"=>945, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"helicopter boy", "name"=>"photologue/photos/helicopterboy-225x300.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/225/300/c/80/photologue/photos/helicopterboy-225x300.jpg", "h"=>300, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>nil, "caption"=>"helicopter boy", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/helicopterboy-225x300.jpg", "w"=>225, "id"=>27079, "credits-name"=>"Photo courtesy of Jennifer Babb"}, "objectID"=>"91929", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Helicopter Boy", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this podcast, a story about a mom, a boy, and a home-made helicopter.</p><p>(And no! This has nothing to do with the Balloon Boy incident.) Instead, it's about how public radio...literally saved a boy's life. Well, not quite. But sorta. Kinda. It's a story about why we do what we do: we're trying to tell stories that move you and make you feel different about the world, even just a little bit.</p>\n<p>Please <strong><a href=\"https://www.wnyc.org/epledge/rl\">support us</a></strong> in that mission.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-11-03T00:35:34", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91693-new-normal", "title"=>"New Normal?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab100209.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91693", "description"=>"<p>In this hour of Radiolab: reframing our ideas about normalcy.  </p>\n<p> </p><p>Evolution results from the ability of organisms to change. But how do you tell the difference between a sea change and a ripple in the water? Is a peacenik baboon, a man in a dress, or a cuddly fox a sign of things to come? Or just a flukey outlier from the norm? And is there ever really a norm?</p>", "tease"=>"Peacenik baboons, a man in a dress, and cuddly tame foxes. Stories of adaptation, and reframing ideas about normalcy.", "publishTime"=>1255967379000, "publish-at"=>"2009-10-19T11:49:39", "date-line-ts"=>1255968000, "estimated-duration"=>3413, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Candies in a pea shell", "name"=>"photologue/images/0b/new_normal_101812.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/342/c/80/photologue/images/0b/new_normal_101812.jpg", "h"=>342, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/kkoshy/4334413228/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/images/0b/new_normal_101812.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>82456, "credits-name"=>"Koshyk"}, "objectID"=>"91693", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"New Normal?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this hour of Radiolab: reframing our ideas about normalcy.  </p>\n<p> </p><p>Evolution results from the ability of organisms to change. But how do you tell the difference between a sea change and a ripple in the water? Is a peacenik baboon, a man in a dress, or a cuddly fox a sign of things to come? Or just a flukey outlier from the norm? And is there ever really a norm?</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-10-19T11:49:39", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91925-blink", "title"=>"Blink", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast09blink.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91925", "description"=>"<p>We ask a question we thought was a no-brainer in this podcast: why do we blink?</p><p>Film editor <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0004555/\">Walter Murch</a> tells us about a strange discovery he made years ago while working on <em>The Conversation</em> - could something as small as a blink actually be the trick of his trade?   We also talk to Japanese researchers Tamami Nakano and Shigeru Kitazawa about the experiment they conducted to understand how we see the world, when we choose not to, and why.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>We tackle a question we thought was a no-brainer: why do we blink?</p>", "publishTime"=>1254799117000, "publish-at"=>"2009-10-05T23:18:37", "date-line-ts"=>1254799117, "estimated-duration"=>872, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"eye", "name"=>"photologue/photos/867883420_772d65a85d_z.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/c/80/photologue/photos/867883420_772d65a85d_z.jpg", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/raylopez/867883420/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/867883420_772d65a85d_z.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>27080, "credits-name"=>"Down Town Pictures"}, "objectID"=>"91925", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Blink", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>We ask a question we thought was a no-brainer in this podcast: why do we blink?</p><p>Film editor <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0004555/\">Walter Murch</a> tells us about a strange discovery he made years ago while working on <em>The Conversation</em> - could something as small as a blink actually be the trick of his trade?   We also talk to Japanese researchers Tamami Nakano and Shigeru Kitazawa about the experiment they conducted to understand how we see the world, when we choose not to, and why.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-10-05T23:18:37", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91924-it-might-be-science", "title"=>"It Might Be Science", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast09mightbe.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91924", "description"=>"<p><a href=\"http://www.theymightbegiants.com/\">They Might Be Giants</a> just came out with a new album, 'Here Comes Science.' So we invited them to come play with us at our season launch party last week at the Water Taxi Beach in Queens. And then we ambushed them with annoying little questions about science and about the tricky business of turning science into entertainment ... because of that whole, you know, 'getting the facts right' thing.</p><p>On this podcast, we decided to share this magical evening with those of you who weren't able to join us live. Hope you enjoy the music, pesky science teachers, and miasmas of plasma.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>They Might Be Giants celebrate at our season launch party with a live concert, and a conversation about the tricky business of combining science and entertainment.</p>", "publishTime"=>1253577603000, "publish-at"=>"2009-09-21T20:00:03", "date-line-ts"=>1253577603, "estimated-duration"=>2209, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Radiolab hosts Jad Abumrad and Robert Krulwich with John Flansburg of They Might Be Giants", "name"=>"photologue/photos/WaterTaxiBeach.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/500/333/l/80/photologue/photos/WaterTaxiBeach.jpg", "h"=>333, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Radiolab hosts Jad Abumrad and Robert Krulwich with John Flansburg of They Might Be Giants", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/pabo76/3927378535/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/WaterTaxiBeach.jpg", "w"=>500, "id"=>215, "credits-name"=>"pabo76"}, "objectID"=>"91924", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"It Might Be Science", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><a href=\"http://www.theymightbegiants.com/\">They Might Be Giants</a> just came out with a new album, 'Here Comes Science.' So we invited them to come play with us at our season launch party last week at the Water Taxi Beach in Queens. And then we ambushed them with annoying little questions about science and about the tricky business of turning science into entertainment ... because of that whole, you know, 'getting the facts right' thing.</p><p>On this podcast, we decided to share this magical evening with those of you who weren't able to join us live. Hope you enjoy the music, pesky science teachers, and miasmas of plasma.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-09-21T20:00:03", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91689-parasites", "title"=>"Parasites", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab092509.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91689", "description"=>"<p>What's gotten into you? In this hour, Radiolab uncovers a world full of parasites.</p><p>Could parasites be the shadowy hands that pull the strings of life? We explore nature's moochers, with tales of lethargic farmers, zombie cockroaches, and even mind-controlled humans (kinda, maybe). And we examine claims that some parasites may actually be good for you.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>For transcripts, see individual segment pages.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Tales of lethargic farmers, zombie cockroaches, and even mind-controlled humans (kinda, maybe).", "publishTime"=>1252338574000, "publish-at"=>"2009-09-07T11:49:34", "date-line-ts"=>1252339200, "estimated-duration"=>3455, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Schistosome parasite", "name"=>"photologue/photos/Parasite_Schistosome_SEM.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Wikipedia Commons"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/828/1024/c/80/photologue/photos/Parasite_Schistosome_SEM.jpg", "h"=>1024, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Schistosome parasite", "credits-url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schistosome_Parasite_SEM.jpg", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/Parasite_Schistosome_SEM.jpg", "w"=>828, "id"=>67089, "credits-name"=>"Bruce Wetzel and Harry Schaefer"}, "objectID"=>"91689", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Parasites", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>What's gotten into you? In this hour, Radiolab uncovers a world full of parasites.</p><p>Could parasites be the shadowy hands that pull the strings of life? We explore nature's moochers, with tales of lethargic farmers, zombie cockroaches, and even mind-controlled humans (kinda, maybe). And we examine claims that some parasites may actually be good for you.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>For transcripts, see individual segment pages.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-09-07T11:49:34", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91919-after-birth", "title"=>"After Birth", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcastafterbirth.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91919", "description"=>"<p>Pardon the graphic pun, but hey! For this podcast, Jad--a brand new father--wonders what's going on inside the head of his baby Amil.</p><p>(And don't worry, you don't need kids to enjoy this podcast.) The questions here are big: what is it like to be so brand new to the world? None of us have memories from this time, so how could we possibly ever know? Is it just chaos? Or, is there something more, some understanding from the very beginning?  Jad found a development psychologist named <a href=\"http://www.charlesfernyhough.com/\">Charles Fernyhough</a> to explore some of his questions.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Further reading from Charles Fernyhough:</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Thousand-Days-Wonder-Scientists-Developing/dp/B002IKLO7S/ref=sr_1_1?ie=UTF8&amp;s=books&amp;qid=1251148505&amp;sr=8-1\">A Thousand Days of Wonder</a><br> <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Baby-Mirror-Childs-World-Birth/dp/1847080073/ref=pd_sim_b_4\">Baby In The Mirror</a></p>", "tease"=>"<p>Jad--a brand new father--wonders what's going on inside the head of his baby Amil. Is it just chaos? Or is there something more, some understanding from the very beginning?</p>", "publishTime"=>1251172525000, "publish-at"=>"2009-08-24T23:55:25", "date-line-ts"=>1251172525, "estimated-duration"=>605, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/Amil2.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1395/1860/c/80/photologue/photos/Amil2.jpg", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/Amil2.jpg", "w"=>1395, "id"=>27073, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"91919", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"After Birth", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Pardon the graphic pun, but hey! For this podcast, Jad--a brand new father--wonders what's going on inside the head of his baby Amil.</p><p>(And don't worry, you don't need kids to enjoy this podcast.) The questions here are big: what is it like to be so brand new to the world? None of us have memories from this time, so how could we possibly ever know? Is it just chaos? Or, is there something more, some understanding from the very beginning?  Jad found a development psychologist named <a href=\"http://www.charlesfernyhough.com/\">Charles Fernyhough</a> to explore some of his questions.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Further reading from Charles Fernyhough:</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Thousand-Days-Wonder-Scientists-Developing/dp/B002IKLO7S/ref=sr_1_1?ie=UTF8&amp;s=books&amp;qid=1251148505&amp;sr=8-1\">A Thousand Days of Wonder</a><br> <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Baby-Mirror-Childs-World-Birth/dp/1847080073/ref=pd_sim_b_4\">Baby In The Mirror</a></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-08-24T23:55:25", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91918-16-moments", "title"=>"16: Moments", "audio"=>nil, "description"=>"<p>After hearing our show about moments of death, filmmaker <a href=\"http://www.anyoneeverything.com/\">Will Hoffman</a> went out in search of moments of life. What follows is what he found.</p>", "tease"=>"After hearing our show about moments of death, filmmaker Will Hoffman went out in search of moments of life. What follows is what he found.", "publishTime"=>1250294454000, "publish-at"=>"2009-08-14T20:00:54", "date-line-ts"=>1250294454, "estimated-duration"=>0, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"girl smells flowers", "name"=>"photologue/photos/girl_flowers.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/497/c/80/photologue/photos/girl_flowers.jpg", "h"=>497, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/girl_flowers.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>27081, "credits-name"=>"William Hoffman"}, "objectID"=>"91918", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"16: Moments", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>After hearing our show about moments of death, filmmaker <a href=\"http://www.anyoneeverything.com/\">Will Hoffman</a> went out in search of moments of life. What follows is what he found.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-08-14T20:00:54", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91917-15-sum", "title"=>"15: Sum", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15sum.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91917", "description"=>"<p>For meditation number fifteen we have a reading from <a href=\"http://www.davideagleman.com/\">David Eagleman's</a> book <a href=\"http://www.davideagleman.com/SUM.html\">Sum</a>. It's a vision of the after life that's both playful and... horrifying.  Sum is read by actor <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0001787/\">Jeffrey Tambor</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"For meditation number fifteen we have a reading from David Eagleman's book Sum. It's a vision of the after life that's both playful and... horrifying.  Sum is read by actor Jeffrey Ta...", "publishTime"=>1250208052000, "publish-at"=>"2009-08-13T20:00:52", "date-line-ts"=>1250208052, "estimated-duration"=>307, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"LEGO afterlife", "name"=>"photologue/photos/2213926196_6a810bca1a_z.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/427/h/80/photologue/photos/2213926196_6a810bca1a_z.jpg", "h"=>427, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/john/2213926196/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/2213926196_6a810bca1a_z.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>136, "credits-name"=>"fd"}, "objectID"=>"91917", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"15: Sum", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>For meditation number fifteen we have a reading from <a href=\"http://www.davideagleman.com/\">David Eagleman's</a> book <a href=\"http://www.davideagleman.com/SUM.html\">Sum</a>. It's a vision of the after life that's both playful and... horrifying.  Sum is read by actor <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0001787/\">Jeffrey Tambor</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-08-13T20:00:52", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91916-14-the-four-groans", "title"=>"14: The Four Groans", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14groans.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91916", "description"=>"<p>Another meditation on what happens after the moment of death, this time as Shakespeare envisions it. </p><p>Ron Rosenbaum, author of <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Shakespeare-Wars-Clashing-Scholars-Fiascoes/dp/0375503390\">The Shakespeare Wars</a>, tells us about a very small variation in the text of Hamlet that makes a huge difference about how Shakespeare envisioned Hamlet’s dying moment. Then we pay a visit to Tony Award-winning actor<a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TU9iCgGDjRI\"> Mark Rylance</a> to get his take on encountering the edge of consciousness.</p>", "tease"=>"Another meditation on what happens after the moment of death, this time as Shakespeare envisions it. ", "publishTime"=>1250121626000, "publish-at"=>"2009-08-12T20:00:26", "date-line-ts"=>1250121626, "estimated-duration"=>477, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Window", "name"=>"photologue/photos/window_sky_.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/window_sky_.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Window", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/geezaweezer/6211860987/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/window_sky_.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>63044, "credits-name"=>"geezaweezer"}, "objectID"=>"91916", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"14: The Four Groans", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Another meditation on what happens after the moment of death, this time as Shakespeare envisions it. </p><p>Ron Rosenbaum, author of <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Shakespeare-Wars-Clashing-Scholars-Fiascoes/dp/0375503390\">The Shakespeare Wars</a>, tells us about a very small variation in the text of Hamlet that makes a huge difference about how Shakespeare envisioned Hamlet’s dying moment. Then we pay a visit to Tony Award-winning actor<a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TU9iCgGDjRI\"> Mark Rylance</a> to get his take on encountering the edge of consciousness.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-08-12T20:00:26", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91915-13-gone", "title"=>"13: Gone", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13gone.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91915", "description"=>"<p>We continue our meditations on death with a reading from poet and writer,  <a href=\"http://www.markdoty.org/\">Mark Doty</a>.  This is an excerpt from Doty's 1996 memoir <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Heavens-Coast-Memoir-Mark-Doty/dp/0060928050\">Heaven's Coast</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"We continue our meditations on death with a reading from poet and writer,  Mark Doty.  This is an excerpt from Doty's 1996 memoir Heaven's Coast.", "publishTime"=>1250035258000, "publish-at"=>"2009-08-11T20:00:58", "date-line-ts"=>1250035258, "estimated-duration"=>405, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/1438355294_b07a88d17d_z.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/426/l/80/photologue/photos/1438355294_b07a88d17d_z.jpg", "h"=>426, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/vardhana/1438355294/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/1438355294_b07a88d17d_z.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>137, "credits-name"=>"vardhana"}, "objectID"=>"91915", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"13: Gone", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>We continue our meditations on death with a reading from poet and writer,  <a href=\"http://www.markdoty.org/\">Mark Doty</a>.  This is an excerpt from Doty's 1996 memoir <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Heavens-Coast-Memoir-Mark-Doty/dp/0060928050\">Heaven's Coast</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-08-11T20:00:58", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91914-12-proof", "title"=>"12: Proof", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10proof.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91914", "description"=>"<p>This week on the podcast, we continue our meditations on death. Our <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/2009/jul/27/\">After Life</a> episode had eleven meditations, and now we’re gonna throw a new one at you each day, all week long, culminating in a very special treat at the end of the week. </p><p>To get things started, Jad talks to <a href=\"http://www.maryroach.net/\">Mary Roach</a> about a bold claim she made in an article for the New Scientist. Read more about science's attempts to understand the afterlife in Mary's book, <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Spook-Science-Afterlife-Mary-Roach/dp/0393059626\"><em>Spook</em></a>. By the way that beautiful song, 'Old Fashion Morphine' is by <a href=\"http://www.jolieholland.com/\">Jolie Holland</a>, off her album Escondida.</p>", "tease"=>"This week on the podcast, we continue our meditations on death (our After Life episode had eleven). We'll throw a new one at you each day, all week long, culminating in a very special...", "publishTime"=>1249948859000, "publish-at"=>"2009-08-10T20:00:59", "date-line-ts"=>1249948859, "estimated-duration"=>459, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Rotary phone", "name"=>"photologue/photos/proof.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/617/c/80/photologue/photos/proof.jpg", "h"=>617, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Rotary phone", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/robbn1/3448476781/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/proof.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>67092, "credits-name"=>"Robb North"}, "objectID"=>"91914", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"12: Proof", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This week on the podcast, we continue our meditations on death. Our <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/2009/jul/27/\">After Life</a> episode had eleven meditations, and now we’re gonna throw a new one at you each day, all week long, culminating in a very special treat at the end of the week. </p><p>To get things started, Jad talks to <a href=\"http://www.maryroach.net/\">Mary Roach</a> about a bold claim she made in an article for the New Scientist. Read more about science's attempts to understand the afterlife in Mary's book, <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Spook-Science-Afterlife-Mary-Roach/dp/0393059626\"><em>Spook</em></a>. By the way that beautiful song, 'Old Fashion Morphine' is by <a href=\"http://www.jolieholland.com/\">Jolie Holland</a>, off her album Escondida.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-08-10T20:00:59", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91680-after-life", "title"=>"After Life", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab091809.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91680", "description"=>"<p>This hour: Radiolab stares down the very moment of passing, and speculates about what may lie beyond.</p><p>What happens at the moment when we slip from life...to the other side? Is it a moment? If it is, when exactly does it happen? And what happens afterward? It's a show of questions that don't have easy answers. So, in a slight departure from our regular format, we bring you eleven meditations on how, when, and even if we die.</p>", "tease"=>"Eleven meditations on how, when, and even if we die.", "publishTime"=>1248709759000, "publish-at"=>"2009-07-27T11:49:19", "date-line-ts"=>1248710400, "estimated-duration"=>3472, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Foggy road", "name"=>"photologue/photos/after_life.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/after_life.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Foggy road", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/eioua/3068749696/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/after_life.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>67096, "credits-name"=>"eioua"}, "objectID"=>"91680", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"After Life", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This hour: Radiolab stares down the very moment of passing, and speculates about what may lie beyond.</p><p>What happens at the moment when we slip from life...to the other side? Is it a moment? If it is, when exactly does it happen? And what happens afterward? It's a show of questions that don't have easy answers. So, in a slight departure from our regular format, we bring you eleven meditations on how, when, and even if we die.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-07-27T11:49:19", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91912-in-defense-of-darwin", "title"=>"In Defense of Darwin?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast09indefenseofdarwin.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91912", "description"=>"<p>When evolutionary biologist <a href=\"http://richarddawkins.net/\">Richard Dawkins'</a> daughter was six years old, he told her that flowers are not here for beauty, not here for the bees, but instead merely to copy their own DNA. Sigh, what a Dad. So is Richard Dawkins always so gloomy and reductionist about the world? Well yes, but he would say that his vision of the world is anything but gloomy, he even calls it romantic. In this conversation from the 92nd St Y, Robert challenges Dawkins on this and a number of other sticky spots on the topic of biological evolution.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>Robert challenges Richard Dawkins on a number of sticky spots on the subject of biological evolution.</p>", "publishTime"=>1247531428000, "publish-at"=>"2009-07-13T20:30:28", "date-line-ts"=>1247531428, "estimated-duration"=>1093, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Richard Dawkins", "name"=>"photologue/photos/defense_of_darwin.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/425/c/80/photologue/photos/defense_of_darwin.jpg", "h"=>425, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Richard Dawkins", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/sentxd/4439740065/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/defense_of_darwin.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>67103, "credits-name"=>"sentxd"}, "objectID"=>"91912", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"In Defense of Darwin?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>When evolutionary biologist <a href=\"http://richarddawkins.net/\">Richard Dawkins'</a> daughter was six years old, he told her that flowers are not here for beauty, not here for the bees, but instead merely to copy their own DNA. Sigh, what a Dad. So is Richard Dawkins always so gloomy and reductionist about the world? Well yes, but he would say that his vision of the world is anything but gloomy, he even calls it romantic. In this conversation from the 92nd St Y, Robert challenges Dawkins on this and a number of other sticky spots on the topic of biological evolution.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-07-13T20:30:28", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91911-are-we-coins", "title"=>"Are We Coins?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast08arewecoins.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91911", "description"=>"<p>After we released our show about <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/2009/jun/15/\">Stochasticity</a>, we received a lot of comments about the idea humans can be just as predictable as coins. In that show, Jonah Lehrer was telling us about a study on the 82-83 76ers, and he was saying that even when a basketball player is supposedly hot – really on a streak – he is no more likely to make his next shot that any other time. Basketball players are slaves to their averages. Well, it turns out this isn't the whole story.</p><p>In fact, right before we released the show, Jad got a call from <a href=\"http://www.stevenstrogatz.com/\">Steve Strogatz</a>, a mathematician from Cornell University.</p>\n<p>After talking to Steve, we turn to neuroscientist <a href=\"http://www.cns.nyu.edu/~glimcher/\">Paul Glimcher</a>, as he and Gregory Warner explore whether the little choices we make every day are predictable or not.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>We follow up on our <a href=\"/2009/jun/15/\">Stochasticity</a> show with an exploration pf whether the little choices we make every day are predictable or not.</p>", "publishTime"=>1246325457000, "publish-at"=>"2009-06-29T21:30:57", "date-line-ts"=>1246325457, "estimated-duration"=>1084, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"coin toss", "name"=>"photologue/photos/3635981474_e017b5e5b2_z.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/506/640/h/80/photologue/photos/3635981474_e017b5e5b2_z.jpg", "h"=>640, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/icma/3635981474/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/3635981474_e017b5e5b2_z.jpg", "w"=>506, "id"=>126, "credits-name"=>"icma"}, "objectID"=>"91911", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Are We Coins?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>After we released our show about <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/2009/jun/15/\">Stochasticity</a>, we received a lot of comments about the idea humans can be just as predictable as coins. In that show, Jonah Lehrer was telling us about a study on the 82-83 76ers, and he was saying that even when a basketball player is supposedly hot – really on a streak – he is no more likely to make his next shot that any other time. Basketball players are slaves to their averages. Well, it turns out this isn't the whole story.</p><p>In fact, right before we released the show, Jad got a call from <a href=\"http://www.stevenstrogatz.com/\">Steve Strogatz</a>, a mathematician from Cornell University.</p>\n<p>After talking to Steve, we turn to neuroscientist <a href=\"http://www.cns.nyu.edu/~glimcher/\">Paul Glimcher</a>, as he and Gregory Warner explore whether the little choices we make every day are predictable or not.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-06-29T21:30:57", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91909-stochasticity-bonus-video", "title"=>"Stochasticity Bonus Video!", "audio"=>nil, "description"=>"<p>We have a special bonus this week to accompany our Stochasticity episode.  We asked our friends, <a href=\"http://highermammals.com/\">Higher Mammals</a> to produce a song and video for our Stochasticity show.  We hope you find it completely Random!</p><p>Higher Mammals features Josh Kurz and Shane Winter, with additional vocals from Jason Major, Kendra May, and Wendy Roderweiss.</p>", "tease"=>"We have a special bonus this week to accompany our Stochasticity episode.  We asked our friends, Higher Mammals to produce a song and video for our Stochasticity show.  We hope you fi...", "publishTime"=>1245124740000, "publish-at"=>"2009-06-15T23:59:00", "date-line-ts"=>1245110421, "estimated-duration"=>0, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Stochasticity", "name"=>"photologue/photos/stochasticity.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/500/515/c/80/photologue/photos/stochasticity.jpg", "h"=>515, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/stochasticity.jpg", "w"=>500, "id"=>127, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"91909", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Stochasticity Bonus Video!", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>We have a special bonus this week to accompany our Stochasticity episode.  We asked our friends, <a href=\"http://highermammals.com/\">Higher Mammals</a> to produce a song and video for our Stochasticity show.  We hope you find it completely Random!</p><p>Higher Mammals features Josh Kurz and Shane Winter, with additional vocals from Jason Major, Kendra May, and Wendy Roderweiss.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-06-15T23:59:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91684-stochasticity", "title"=>"Stochasticity", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab091109.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91684", "description"=>"<p>Stochasticity (a wonderfully slippery and smarty-pants word for randomness), may be at the very foundation of our lives. To understand how big a role it plays, we look at chance and patterns in sports, lottery tickets, and even the cells in our own body.</p><p>Along the way, we talk to a woman suddenly consumed by a frenzied gambling addiction, meet two friends whose meeting seems to defy pure chance, and take a close look at some very noisy bacteria.</p>", "tease"=>"How stochasticity -- a wonderfully smarty-pants word for randomness -- drives our lives, and the patterns we see around us.", "publishTime"=>1245080966000, "publish-at"=>"2009-06-15T11:49:26", "date-line-ts"=>1245081600, "estimated-duration"=>3413, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Blobs of light", "name"=>"1/lens_flares_stochasticity.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1018/1024/c/80/1/lens_flares_stochasticity.jpg", "h"=>1024, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Blobs of light", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/hardchessesandyou/6023506097/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/lens_flares_stochasticity.jpg", "w"=>1018, "id"=>67107, "credits-name"=>"M I T C H Ǝ L L"}, "objectID"=>"91684", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Stochasticity", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Stochasticity (a wonderfully slippery and smarty-pants word for randomness), may be at the very foundation of our lives. To understand how big a role it plays, we look at chance and patterns in sports, lottery tickets, and even the cells in our own body.</p><p>Along the way, we talk to a woman suddenly consumed by a frenzied gambling addiction, meet two friends whose meeting seems to defy pure chance, and take a close look at some very noisy bacteria.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-06-15T11:49:26", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91908-stayin-alive", "title"=>"Stayin' Alive", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast07stayinalive.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91908", "description"=>"<p>This week on the podcast we take a look at four unconventional ways to stay alive. We talk to geneticist <a href=\"http://arep.med.harvard.edu/gmc/\">George Church</a>, who originally appeared in our <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2008/03/14\">So Called Life Show</a>, biologist <a href=\"http://www.uvm.edu/~biology/Faculty/Heinrich/Heinrich.html\">Bernd Heinrich</a>, neuroscientist <a href=\"http://www.davideagleman.com/\">David Eagleman</a>, and finally, we visit a CPR class.</p>", "tease"=>"A look at four unconventional ways to stay alive.", "publishTime"=>1243975231000, "publish-at"=>"2009-06-02T16:40:31", "date-line-ts"=>1243975231, "estimated-duration"=>910, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"silver action figure doing dance from Saturday Night Fever", "name"=>"photologue/photos/167753869_77625e3d50_o.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/566/c/80/photologue/photos/167753869_77625e3d50_o.jpg", "h"=>566, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"silver action figure doing dance from Saturday Night Fever", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/lameazoid/167753869/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/167753869_77625e3d50_o.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>128, "credits-name"=>"Josh Miller"}, "objectID"=>"91908", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Stayin' Alive", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This week on the podcast we take a look at four unconventional ways to stay alive. We talk to geneticist <a href=\"http://arep.med.harvard.edu/gmc/\">George Church</a>, who originally appeared in our <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2008/03/14\">So Called Life Show</a>, biologist <a href=\"http://www.uvm.edu/~biology/Faculty/Heinrich/Heinrich.html\">Bernd Heinrich</a>, neuroscientist <a href=\"http://www.davideagleman.com/\">David Eagleman</a>, and finally, we visit a CPR class.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-06-02T16:40:31", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91905-av-smackdown-the-podcast", "title"=>"AV Smackdown . . .   The Podcast", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast06avsmackdown.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91905", "description"=>"<p>On May 6th, at WNYC's new Jerome L. Greene Performance Space, we opened up an age old can of worms. Jad and Robert faced off over which medium is superior -- television or radio. This American Life's Ira Glass was the referee. There were stunning jabs, wicked uppercuts, and even the occasional low blow.</p><p>In TV’s corner, Robert \"The Krusher\" Krulwich hit hard with stunning video images, but audio-savant Jad \"Boom Boom\" Abumrad pounded his opponent with the power of sound.  The bout went five hard rounds and had to go to the cards for a decision.  Tears were shed, and after a short intermission Jad and Robert sat down with Ira to discuss the challenges of working in both TV and Radio.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"<p>We open up an age old can of worms at WNYC's Jerome L. Greene Performance Space: which medium is superior -- television or radio? Jad and Robert face off, with This American Life's Ira Glass as referee.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>", "publishTime"=>1242696651000, "publish-at"=>"2009-05-18T21:30:51", "date-line-ts"=>1242696651, "estimated-duration"=>1410, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"radiolab10.jpg", "name"=>"photologue/photos/radiolab10_.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/630/416/c/80/photologue/photos/radiolab10_.jpg", "h"=>416, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Jad \"Boom Boom\" Abumrad in the Red Corner", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/radiolab10_.jpg", "w"=>630, "id"=>26539, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"91905", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"AV Smackdown . . .   The Podcast", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>On May 6th, at WNYC's new Jerome L. Greene Performance Space, we opened up an age old can of worms. Jad and Robert faced off over which medium is superior -- television or radio. This American Life's Ira Glass was the referee. There were stunning jabs, wicked uppercuts, and even the occasional low blow.</p><p>In TV’s corner, Robert \"The Krusher\" Krulwich hit hard with stunning video images, but audio-savant Jad \"Boom Boom\" Abumrad pounded his opponent with the power of sound.  The bout went five hard rounds and had to go to the cards for a decision.  Tears were shed, and after a short intermission Jad and Robert sat down with Ira to discuss the challenges of working in both TV and Radio.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-05-18T21:30:51", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91903-juana-molina", "title"=>"Juana Molina", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast05juana.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91903", "description"=>"<p>Sometimes on the podcast, we like to talk about musicians and the music they make. Today we introduce you to Juana Molina. Last season we used some of her of music in the breaks for the <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2008/11/21\">Sperm</a> show. We received an outpouring of email asking about her music, so this podcast is for those curious listeners who wrote in and for those who haven't heard about her ... until now.</p>", "tease"=>"Sometimes on the podcast, we like to talk about musicians and the music they make. Today we introduce you to Juana Molina. Last season we used some of her of music in the breaks for t...", "publishTime"=>1241483410000, "publish-at"=>"2009-05-04T20:30:10", "date-line-ts"=>1241483410, "estimated-duration"=>896, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Juana Molina on stage", "name"=>"photologue/photos/juana_molina_1.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/428/c/80/photologue/photos/juana_molina_1.jpg", "h"=>428, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Juana Molina on stage", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/blmurch/4305193445/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/juana_molina_1.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>68875, "credits-name"=>"blmurch"}, "objectID"=>"91903", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Juana Molina", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Sometimes on the podcast, we like to talk about musicians and the music they make. Today we introduce you to Juana Molina. Last season we used some of her of music in the breaks for the <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2008/11/21\">Sperm</a> show. We received an outpouring of email asking about her music, so this podcast is for those curious listeners who wrote in and for those who haven't heard about her ... until now.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-05-04T20:30:10", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91898-in-silence", "title"=>"In Silence", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast04insilence.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91898", "description"=>"<p>Here at Radiolab we explore big ideas and ask big questions to see how the world works.</p><p>To do that, we often talk to scientists who are trying to answer those questions by doing experiments and gathering data. But there are some questions that don't give in to experiments and data.  And with Easter and Passover around the corner, we decided to take on one of those questions, not through science, but through a story.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"<p>There are some questions that just don't give in to experiments and data. We take on one of those questions.</p>", "publishTime"=>1239139812000, "publish-at"=>"2009-04-07T17:30:12", "date-line-ts"=>1239139812, "estimated-duration"=>1590, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Rembrandt's Sacrifice of Issac", "name"=>"photologue/photos/in_silence.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/436/640/c/80/photologue/photos/in_silence.jpg", "h"=>640, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Rembrandt's Sacrifice of Issac", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/8545333@N07/2236201781/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/in_silence.jpg", "w"=>436, "id"=>67110, "credits-name"=>"carulmare"}, "objectID"=>"91898", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"In Silence", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Here at Radiolab we explore big ideas and ask big questions to see how the world works.</p><p>To do that, we often talk to scientists who are trying to answer those questions by doing experiments and gathering data. But there are some questions that don't give in to experiments and data.  And with Easter and Passover around the corner, we decided to take on one of those questions, not through science, but through a story.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-04-07T17:30:12", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91896-diy-universe", "title"=>"DIY Universe", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast03blackholes.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91896", "description"=>"<p>Can you make your own universe? We usually think of the universe as 'everything that exists,' so how could you make another one?</p><p>Well, physicists have been speculating about the existence of multiple universes for some time now. And for Robert, the obvious next question was: 'Can we make one?' So he invited physicist <a href=\"http://www.columbia.edu/cu/physics/fac-bios/Greene/faculty.html\">Brian Greene</a> to his kitchen to speculate about just that. And it turns out, it's not such a far-fetched idea. There are scientists right now trying to figure out whether it's possible and what it would take. According to Brian, it would require a tiny black hole, a dash of reverse-gravity, and a lot of luck. But the laws of physics don't rule it out.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"<p>Can you make your own universe? We usually think of the universe as 'everything that exists,' so how could you make another one?</p>", "publishTime"=>1238026513000, "publish-at"=>"2009-03-25T20:15:13", "date-line-ts"=>1238026513, "estimated-duration"=>991, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Starry sky", "name"=>"photologue/photos/starry_sky.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/512/c/80/photologue/photos/starry_sky.jpg", "h"=>512, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Starry sky", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/33403047@N00/3749825872/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/starry_sky.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>27172, "credits-name"=>"gainesp2003"}, "objectID"=>"91896", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"DIY Universe", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Can you make your own universe? We usually think of the universe as 'everything that exists,' so how could you make another one?</p><p>Well, physicists have been speculating about the existence of multiple universes for some time now. And for Robert, the obvious next question was: 'Can we make one?' So he invited physicist <a href=\"http://www.columbia.edu/cu/physics/fac-bios/Greene/faculty.html\">Brian Greene</a> to his kitchen to speculate about just that. And it turns out, it's not such a far-fetched idea. There are scientists right now trying to figure out whether it's possible and what it would take. According to Brian, it would require a tiny black hole, a dash of reverse-gravity, and a lot of luck. But the laws of physics don't rule it out.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-03-25T20:15:13", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91894-mischels-marshmallows", "title"=>"Mischel’s Marshmallows", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast02marshmallows.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91894", "description"=>"<p>How are your New Year's resolutions holding out? This might at least help you feel better about them.</p><p>Psychologist <a href=\"http://www.columbia.edu/cu/psychology/indiv_pages/mischel.html\">Walter Mischel</a> explains how one little test involving a marshmallow might tell you a frightening amount about what kind of person you are. And Radiolab favorite <a href=\"http://www.jonahlehrer.com/\">Jonah Lehrer</a> helps us make sense of the results. This one's all about our will power (or lack thereof).</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this piece incorrectly stated that the kids who performed better on the marshmallow test had higher GPAs in high school and went to better colleges. Those elements were not a part of Mischel’s original study. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Psychologist Walter Mischel explains how one little test involving a marshmallow might tell you a frightening amount about what kind of person you are.", "publishTime"=>1236492268000, "publish-at"=>"2009-03-08T01:04:28", "date-line-ts"=>1236575068, "estimated-duration"=>890, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/Marshmallows.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/c/80/photologue/photos/Marshmallows.jpg", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/flattop341/260207117/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/Marshmallows.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>27171, "credits-name"=>"Flattop341"}, "objectID"=>"91894", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Mischel’s Marshmallows", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>How are your New Year's resolutions holding out? This might at least help you feel better about them.</p><p>Psychologist <a href=\"http://www.columbia.edu/cu/psychology/indiv_pages/mischel.html\">Walter Mischel</a> explains how one little test involving a marshmallow might tell you a frightening amount about what kind of person you are. And Radiolab favorite <a href=\"http://www.jonahlehrer.com/\">Jonah Lehrer</a> helps us make sense of the results. This one's all about our will power (or lack thereof).</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this piece incorrectly stated that the kids who performed better on the marshmallow test had higher GPAs in high school and went to better colleges. Those elements were not a part of Mischel’s original study. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-03-08T01:04:28", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91893-darwinvaganza", "title"=>"Darwinvaganza", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast01darwin.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91893", "description"=>"<p>For this week's podcast, Radiolab throws a birthday party for Charles Darwin!</p><p>Robert Krulwich invites three experts to toast the birthday boy.  <a href=\"http://www.wwnorton.com/catalog/spring07/032995.htm\">David Quammen</a> tells us it takes a village to raise a theory of evolution;  <a href=\"http://www.deborahheiligman.com/charlesandemma.html\">Deborah Heiligman</a> shows why love delayed the Origin of Species more than two decades; and <a href=\"http://features.csmonitor.com/books/2009/02/11/angels-and-ages/\">Adam Gopnik</a> explains why most of the planet still has problems with Darwin's idea. It's going to be a paaa-tay!</p>", "tease"=>"<p>Radiolab throws a birthday party for Charles Darwin! Robert Krulwich invites three experts to toast the birthday boy.</p>", "publishTime"=>1235455206000, "publish-at"=>"2009-02-24T01:00:06", "date-line-ts"=>1235455206, "estimated-duration"=>1543, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Hello my name is Charles Darwin", "name"=>"photologue/photos/darwinvaganza.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/darwinvaganza.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Hello my name is Charles Darwin", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/benetd/4731308627/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/darwinvaganza.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>67124, "credits-name"=>"benet2006"}, "objectID"=>"91893", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Darwinvaganza", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>For this week's podcast, Radiolab throws a birthday party for Charles Darwin!</p><p>Robert Krulwich invites three experts to toast the birthday boy.  <a href=\"http://www.wwnorton.com/catalog/spring07/032995.htm\">David Quammen</a> tells us it takes a village to raise a theory of evolution;  <a href=\"http://www.deborahheiligman.com/charlesandemma.html\">Deborah Heiligman</a> shows why love delayed the Origin of Species more than two decades; and <a href=\"http://features.csmonitor.com/books/2009/02/11/angels-and-ages/\">Adam Gopnik</a> explains why most of the planet still has problems with Darwin's idea. It's going to be a paaa-tay!</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-02-24T01:00:06", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91886-the-obama-effect-perhaps", "title"=>"The Obama Effect, Perhaps.", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast012709.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91886", "description"=>"<p>When Jad and Robert saw <a href=\"http://scienceblogs.com/cortex/2009/01/obama_and_stereotype_threat.php\" target=\"_blank\">this article</a> about a study that found a link between President Obama's election, and the test scores of African Americans, it made them think about an earlier study by <a href=\"http://www-psych.stanford.edu/~steele/\">Claude Steele</a>,about a psychological effect called \"stereotype threat.\"</p><p>On this podcast, they'll tell us about that and answer some listener mail (in the aggregate) answering questions about <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2008/12/05/segments/114867\">Patient X</a> and questionable Jad's use of the term 'bitches' in the <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2008/11/14/segments/113274\">Choice episode</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Also, dear all, the piano piece you've been asking about from this podcast is: “Vladimir’s Blues” by Max Richter.</strong></p>", "tease"=>"<p>A study that finds a link between President Obama's election and the test scores of African Americans gets Jad and Robert thinking about an earlier study on a psychological effect called \"stereotype threat.\"</p>", "publishTime"=>1233103639000, "publish-at"=>"2009-01-27T19:47:19", "date-line-ts"=>1233103639, "estimated-duration"=>1023, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Obama button", "name"=>"photologue/photos/obama_button.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/c/80/photologue/photos/obama_button.jpg", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Obama button", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/queen_of_subtle/3212271049/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/obama_button.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>27173, "credits-name"=>"the queen of subtle"}, "objectID"=>"91886", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Obama Effect, Perhaps.", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>When Jad and Robert saw <a href=\"http://scienceblogs.com/cortex/2009/01/obama_and_stereotype_threat.php\" target=\"_blank\">this article</a> about a study that found a link between President Obama's election, and the test scores of African Americans, it made them think about an earlier study by <a href=\"http://www-psych.stanford.edu/~steele/\">Claude Steele</a>,about a psychological effect called \"stereotype threat.\"</p><p>On this podcast, they'll tell us about that and answer some listener mail (in the aggregate) answering questions about <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2008/12/05/segments/114867\">Patient X</a> and questionable Jad's use of the term 'bitches' in the <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolab/episodes/2008/11/14/segments/113274\">Choice episode</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Also, dear all, the piano piece you've been asking about from this podcast is: “Vladimir’s Blues” by Max Richter.</strong></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-01-27T19:47:19", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91884-parabolas-etc", "title"=>"Parabolas (etc.)", "audio"=>nil, "description"=>"<p>Special bonus of the week! A video inspired by the mathematician, Steve Strogatz. At the age of thirteen, Steve was astonished to find that pendulums and water fountains had a strange relationship that had previously been completely hidden from him.</p><p>Directed by <a href=\"http://www.anyoneeverything.com\">Will Hoffman</a> with Director of Photography <a href=\"http://www.dpboyle.com\">Derek Paul Boyle</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"Special bonus of the week! A video inspired by the mathematician, Steve Strogatz. At the age of thirteen, Steve was astonished to find that pendulums and water fountains had a strange...", "publishTime"=>1231903492000, "publish-at"=>"2009-01-13T22:24:52", "date-line-ts"=>1231903492, "estimated-duration"=>0, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"parabolic sculpture", "name"=>"photologue/photos/2986937389_684caf0fd9_o.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/493/458/c/80/photologue/photos/2986937389_684caf0fd9_o.jpg", "h"=>458, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/nikonvscanon/2986937389/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/2986937389_684caf0fd9_o.jpg", "w"=>493, "id"=>274, "credits-name"=>"nikonvscanon"}, "objectID"=>"91884", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Parabolas (etc.)", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Special bonus of the week! A video inspired by the mathematician, Steve Strogatz. At the age of thirteen, Steve was astonished to find that pendulums and water fountains had a strange relationship that had previously been completely hidden from him.</p><p>Directed by <a href=\"http://www.anyoneeverything.com\">Will Hoffman</a> with Director of Photography <a href=\"http://www.dpboyle.com\">Derek Paul Boyle</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-01-13T22:24:52", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91672-yellow-fluff-and-other-curious-encounters", "title"=>"Yellow Fluff and Other Curious Encounters", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab121208.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91672", "description"=>"<p>The quest for scientific knowledge is one of the great and noble pursuits of humankind. It's also one of the most dangerous, frustrating, ego-driven, transcendent, dirty, sublime, tedious, demoralizing, inspiring...you get the idea. This hour, stories of love and loss in the name of science.</p>", "tease"=>"Stories of love and loss in the name of science.", "publishTime"=>1231778952000, "publish-at"=>"2009-01-12T11:49:12", "date-line-ts"=>1231736400, "estimated-duration"=>3466, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"'City of Ferrara' Airship. The original picture, by Carlo Burzagli, is dated 1914.", "name"=>"photologue/photos/yellow_fluff_03.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/", "name"=>"Wikimedia Commons"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/415/c/80/photologue/photos/yellow_fluff_03.jpg", "h"=>415, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"\"City of Ferrara\" Airship. The original picture, by Carlo Burzagli, is dated 1914.", "credits-url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirigibile_citt%C3%A0_di_Ferrara3.jpg", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/yellow_fluff_03.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>67298, "credits-name"=>"Carlo Burzagli (Private Archive of Burzagli Family.)"}, "objectID"=>"91672", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Yellow Fluff and Other Curious Encounters", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>The quest for scientific knowledge is one of the great and noble pursuits of humankind. It's also one of the most dangerous, frustrating, ego-driven, transcendent, dirty, sublime, tedious, demoralizing, inspiring...you get the idea. This hour, stories of love and loss in the name of science.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2009-01-12T11:49:12", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2010=>[{"slug"=>"105440-blood-buddies", "title"=>"Blood Buddies", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10bats.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=105440", "description"=>"<p>In this new short, a tree full of blood-sucking bats lends a startling twist to our understanding of altruism and natural selection.</p><p>In our recent episode <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/2010/dec/14/\">The Good Show</a>, we grappled with a troubling question: how can goodness and self-sacrifice thrive in a world that Darwin tells us ought to favor selfishness? We follow up on that idea with <strong><a href=\"http://www.life.umd.edu/faculty/wilkinson/\" target=\"_blank\">Jerry Wilkinson</a></strong>, chair of biology at the University of Maryland-College Park, who describes an amazing discovery he made in 1977 that revealed an entirely new way of explaining selflessness.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"In this new short, a tree full of blood-sucking bats lends a startling twist to our understanding of altruism and natural selection.", "publishTime"=>1293581091000, "publish-at"=>"2010-12-28T19:04:51", "date-line-ts"=>1293557387, "estimated-duration"=>868, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"bat", "name"=>"photologue/photos/bat.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/381/476/h/80/photologue/photos/bat.jpg", "h"=>476, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"bat", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/cephalopoddreams/1357845384/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/bat.jpg", "w"=>381, "id"=>38281, "credits-name"=>"leiapico_art"}, "objectID"=>"105440", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Blood Buddies", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this new short, a tree full of blood-sucking bats lends a startling twist to our understanding of altruism and natural selection.</p><p>In our recent episode <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/2010/dec/14/\">The Good Show</a>, we grappled with a troubling question: how can goodness and self-sacrifice thrive in a world that Darwin tells us ought to favor selfishness? We follow up on that idea with <strong><a href=\"http://www.life.umd.edu/faculty/wilkinson/\" target=\"_blank\">Jerry Wilkinson</a></strong>, chair of biology at the University of Maryland-College Park, who describes an amazing discovery he made in 1977 that revealed an entirely new way of explaining selflessness.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-12-28T19:04:51", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"103951-the-good-show", "title"=>"The Good Show", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab121410.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=103951", "description"=>"<p>In this episode, a question that haunted Charles Darwin: if natural selection boils down to survival of the fittest, how do you explain why one creature might stick its neck out for another?</p><p>The standard view of evolution is that living things are shaped by cold-hearted competition. And there is no doubt that today's plants and animals carry the genetic legacy of ancestors who fought fiercely to survive and reproduce. But in this hour, we wonder whether there might also be a logic behind sharing, niceness, kindness ... or even, self-sacrifice. Is altruism an aberration, or just an elaborate guise for sneaky self-interest? Do we really live in a selfish, dog-eat-dog world? Or has evolution carved out a hidden code that rewards genuine cooperation?</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>For transcripts, see individual segment pages.</em></p>", "tease"=>"If natural selection boils down to survival of the fittest, how do you explain why one creature might stick its neck out for another?", "publishTime"=>1292371200000, "publish-at"=>"2010-12-14T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1292346000, "estimated-duration"=>3674, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"rock heart", "name"=>"photologue/photos/rock_heart.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/c/80/photologue/photos/rock_heart.jpg", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"rock heart", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/studio-d/2741744888/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/rock_heart.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>37517, "credits-name"=>"studio-d"}, "objectID"=>"103951", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Good Show", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this episode, a question that haunted Charles Darwin: if natural selection boils down to survival of the fittest, how do you explain why one creature might stick its neck out for another?</p><p>The standard view of evolution is that living things are shaped by cold-hearted competition. And there is no doubt that today's plants and animals carry the genetic legacy of ancestors who fought fiercely to survive and reproduce. But in this hour, we wonder whether there might also be a logic behind sharing, niceness, kindness ... or even, self-sacrifice. Is altruism an aberration, or just an elaborate guise for sneaky self-interest? Do we really live in a selfish, dog-eat-dog world? Or has evolution carved out a hidden code that rewards genuine cooperation?</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>For transcripts, see individual segment pages.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-12-14T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"102525-vertigo", "title"=>"Gravitational Anarchy", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10vertigo.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=102525", "description"=>"<p>A mysterious case of the topsy turvies and a return to the question of what felines feel when they fall.</p><p>In this podcast, we revist some ideas from our recent epsiode on <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2010/sep/20/\" target=\"_blank\">Falling</a>. We begin with a story excerpted from an essay by Berton Roueché, which first appeared in the New Yorker in 1958 and was later published by Dutton in a book called \"<a href=\"http://us.penguingroup.com/nf/Book/BookDisplay/0,,9780452265882,00.html?The_Medical_Detectives_Berton_Roueche\" target=\"_blank\">The Medical Detectives</a>.\" Read for us by the actress <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0204706/\" target=\"_blank\">Hope Davis</a>, it tells the true tale of a woman named Rosemary Morton, who had a little, um, trouble with gravity. After that, we return to a segment from the Falling episode that has troubled some of our listeners: the mystery of <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2010/sep/20/taking-plunge/\" target=\"_blank\">falling cats</a>. Neil deGrasse Tyson, astrophysicist extraordinaire and Director of the Hayden Planetarium in New York, takes us to task for using \"bad data.\" We call on science writer David Quammen to help us fight back and, in the end, we wonder how we can ever know the mind of a falling cat. </p>", "tease"=>"A mysterious case of the topsy turvies and a return to the question of what felines feel when they fall.", "publishTime"=>1291172400000, "publish-at"=>"2010-11-30T22:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1291051431, "estimated-duration"=>1378, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Spiral staircase", "name"=>"photologue/photos/gravitational_anarchy.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/gravitational_anarchy.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Spiral staircase", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/hinkelstone/2765597758/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/gravitational_anarchy.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>66005, "credits-name"=>"quapan"}, "objectID"=>"102525", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Gravitational Anarchy", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>A mysterious case of the topsy turvies and a return to the question of what felines feel when they fall.</p><p>In this podcast, we revist some ideas from our recent epsiode on <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2010/sep/20/\" target=\"_blank\">Falling</a>. We begin with a story excerpted from an essay by Berton Roueché, which first appeared in the New Yorker in 1958 and was later published by Dutton in a book called \"<a href=\"http://us.penguingroup.com/nf/Book/BookDisplay/0,,9780452265882,00.html?The_Medical_Detectives_Berton_Roueche\" target=\"_blank\">The Medical Detectives</a>.\" Read for us by the actress <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/name/nm0204706/\" target=\"_blank\">Hope Davis</a>, it tells the true tale of a woman named Rosemary Morton, who had a little, um, trouble with gravity. After that, we return to a segment from the Falling episode that has troubled some of our listeners: the mystery of <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2010/sep/20/taking-plunge/\" target=\"_blank\">falling cats</a>. Neil deGrasse Tyson, astrophysicist extraordinaire and Director of the Hayden Planetarium in New York, takes us to task for using \"bad data.\" We call on science writer David Quammen to help us fight back and, in the end, we wonder how we can ever know the mind of a falling cat. </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-11-30T22:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"101024-idea-time-come", "title"=>"What Does Technology Want?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10tech.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=101024", "description"=>"<p>Are new ideas and new inventions inevitable? Are they driven by us or by a larger force of nature?</p><p>In this conversation recorded as part of the New York Public Library series <a href=\"http://www.nypl.org/events/live-nypl\" target=\"_blank\">LIVE from the NYPL</a>, Steven Johnson (author of <em>Where Good Ideas Come From</em>) and Kevin Kelly (author of <em>What Technology Wants</em>) try to convince Robert that the things we make—from spoons to microwaves to computers—are an extension of the same evolutionary processes that made us. And we may need to adapt to the idea that our technology could someday truly have a mind of its own.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Are new ideas and new inventions inevitable? Are they driven by us or by a larger force of nature?", "publishTime"=>1289962800000, "publish-at"=>"2010-11-16T22:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1289925860, "estimated-duration"=>1449, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Lightbulbs", "name"=>"photologue/photos/what_technology_want.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/what_technology_want.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Lightbulbs", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/grafixer/3180236074/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/what_technology_want.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>66007, "credits-name"=>"faith goble"}, "objectID"=>"101024", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"What Does Technology Want?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Are new ideas and new inventions inevitable? Are they driven by us or by a larger force of nature?</p><p>In this conversation recorded as part of the New York Public Library series <a href=\"http://www.nypl.org/events/live-nypl\" target=\"_blank\">LIVE from the NYPL</a>, Steven Johnson (author of <em>Where Good Ideas Come From</em>) and Kevin Kelly (author of <em>What Technology Wants</em>) try to convince Robert that the things we make—from spoons to microwaves to computers—are an extension of the same evolutionary processes that made us. And we may need to adapt to the idea that our technology could someday truly have a mind of its own.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-11-16T22:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91732-cities", "title"=>"Cities", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab100810.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91732", "description"=>"<p>In this hour of Radiolab, we take to the street to ask what makes cities tick.</p><p>There's no scientific metric for measuring a city's personality. But step out on the sidewalk, and you can see and feel it. Two physicists explain one tidy mathematical formula that they believe holds the key to what drives a city. Yet math can't explain most of the human-scale details that make urban life unique. So we head out in search of what the numbers miss, and meet a reluctant city dweller, a man who's walked 700 feet below Manhattan, and a once-thriving community that's slipping away.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><span><span>CORRECTION: An earlier version of this program indicated that the Dow Jones Industrial Average originated in the 1920’s.  In fact, it originated in the 1890’s; during the 1920’s it was expanded to include 30 companies, the number it includes today.  The audio has been adjusted in consideration of this fact.</span></span></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>In the first segment of this episode, we listed a German census as one of West and Bettencourt’s sources. This was incorrect, a German census did not appear in their data set, and the audio has been updated to reflect this correction. </em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>In the second segment, we used the term “watts” incorrectly. The audio has been updated to recognize this error. </em><br><span></span></p>", "tease"=>"One tidy mathematical formula may hold the key to how cities work. We take to the streets to test the numbers, &amp; ask what really makes cities tick.", "publishTime"=>1288751003000, "publish-at"=>"2010-11-02T22:23:23", "date-line-ts"=>1286553600, "estimated-duration"=>3414, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Cityscape", "name"=>"photologue/photos/cities.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/cities.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Cityscape", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/dexxus/358919966/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/cities.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>67079, "credits-name"=>"paul (dex)"}, "objectID"=>"91732", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Cities", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this hour of Radiolab, we take to the street to ask what makes cities tick.</p><p>There's no scientific metric for measuring a city's personality. But step out on the sidewalk, and you can see and feel it. Two physicists explain one tidy mathematical formula that they believe holds the key to what drives a city. Yet math can't explain most of the human-scale details that make urban life unique. So we head out in search of what the numbers miss, and meet a reluctant city dweller, a man who's walked 700 feet below Manhattan, and a once-thriving community that's slipping away.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><span><span>CORRECTION: An earlier version of this program indicated that the Dow Jones Industrial Average originated in the 1920’s.  In fact, it originated in the 1890’s; during the 1920’s it was expanded to include 30 companies, the number it includes today.  The audio has been adjusted in consideration of this fact.</span></span></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>In the first segment of this episode, we listed a German census as one of West and Bettencourt’s sources. This was incorrect, a German census did not appear in their data set, and the audio has been updated to reflect this correction. </em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>In the second segment, we used the term “watts” incorrectly. The audio has been updated to recognize this error. </em><br><span></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-11-02T22:23:23", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"98611-wild-talk", "title"=>"Wild Talk", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10prairie.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=98611", "description"=>"<p>In today's podcast, we get a tantalizing taste of words in the wild, from the jungles to the prairie.</p><p>Reporter <a href=\"http://www.aridanielshapiro.com\" target=\"_blank\">Ari Daniel Shapiro</a> tells us about <a href=\"http://www.st-andrews.ac.uk/~www_sp/people/lect/kz3.shtml\" target=\"_blank\">Klaus Zuberbuhler's</a> work in the Tai Forest of West Africa. When Klaus first came to the forest, he hit a wall of sound. But he slowly started making sense of that sonic chaos by scaring a particular monkey called the Diana Monkey. Turns out, the Diana Monkey is making more than just noise. Then we jump from the jungle to the prairie, where <a href=\"http://jan.ucc.nau.edu/~cns3/\" target=\"_blank\">Con Slobodchikoff</a> has discovered what he calls a grammar of color, shapes, and sizes embedded in prairie dog chirps. His discovery leaves Jad and Robert wondering whether we could ever understand the language of a different species. Back in the jungle, Klaus is wondering the same thing, and tells us about one day when the cacophony of monkey calls distilled into a life-saving warning.</p>", "tease"=>"In today's podcast, we get a tantalizing taste of words in the wild, from the jungles to the prairie.", "publishTime"=>1287540000000, "publish-at"=>"2010-10-19T22:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1287436974, "estimated-duration"=>1228, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Prairie dog", "name"=>"photologue/photos/wild_talk.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/wild_talk.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Prairie dog", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/larry1732/6019728478/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/wild_talk.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>66009, "credits-name"=>"Larry1372"}, "objectID"=>"98611", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Wild Talk", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In today's podcast, we get a tantalizing taste of words in the wild, from the jungles to the prairie.</p><p>Reporter <a href=\"http://www.aridanielshapiro.com\" target=\"_blank\">Ari Daniel Shapiro</a> tells us about <a href=\"http://www.st-andrews.ac.uk/~www_sp/people/lect/kz3.shtml\" target=\"_blank\">Klaus Zuberbuhler's</a> work in the Tai Forest of West Africa. When Klaus first came to the forest, he hit a wall of sound. But he slowly started making sense of that sonic chaos by scaring a particular monkey called the Diana Monkey. Turns out, the Diana Monkey is making more than just noise. Then we jump from the jungle to the prairie, where <a href=\"http://jan.ucc.nau.edu/~cns3/\" target=\"_blank\">Con Slobodchikoff</a> has discovered what he calls a grammar of color, shapes, and sizes embedded in prairie dog chirps. His discovery leaves Jad and Robert wondering whether we could ever understand the language of a different species. Back in the jungle, Klaus is wondering the same thing, and tells us about one day when the cacophony of monkey calls distilled into a life-saving warning.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-10-19T22:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"96854-walls-jericho", "title"=>"The Walls of Jericho", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10jericho.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=96854", "description"=>"<p>Jad and Robert pit physics against a bible story with this simple question: could a team of trumpeters really bring down the walls of Jericho?</p><p>Sure, sound can be a powerful - an alarm clock can get you out of bed and headed for the shower, a pop song can send you back to Junior Prom. But what about sound as a physical force? In the bible, the walls of Jericho fall to the sound of seven shofar players (shofars are basically trumpets made of rams' horns). In this podcast, Jad and Robert talk to engineer and sound expert David Lubman to find out how many shofars it would actually take to level a Bronze Age wall. To get a sense of the power of the shofar, we pay a visit to Cantor <a href=\"http://home.rr.com/danpincus\" target=\"_blank\">Daniel Pincus</a> to hear him and his students blow some horns. Then, we talk to inventor <a href=\"http://woodynorris.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Woody Norris</a> for a modern approach to this biblical challenge.</p>\n<p>Many thanks to the 2010 Shofar All-Stars who played for us: David Liebowitz, Daniela Drakhler, Miriam Frank, Adam Hametz-Berner, Rachel Kelk, Ed Kerson, Anna Levy, Richard Scheiner, and Robert Wine.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>In this podcast, Jad and Robert throw some physics at a bible story. We find out just how many trumpeters you'd actually need to blow down the walls of Jericho.</p>", "publishTime"=>1286244000000, "publish-at"=>"2010-10-04T22:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1286221015, "estimated-duration"=>896, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/MudBrickWall.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/427/c/80/photologue/photos/MudBrickWall.jpg", "h"=>427, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/eflon/4505793366/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/MudBrickWall.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>31741, "credits-name"=>"eflon"}, "objectID"=>"96854", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Walls of Jericho", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Jad and Robert pit physics against a bible story with this simple question: could a team of trumpeters really bring down the walls of Jericho?</p><p>Sure, sound can be a powerful - an alarm clock can get you out of bed and headed for the shower, a pop song can send you back to Junior Prom. But what about sound as a physical force? In the bible, the walls of Jericho fall to the sound of seven shofar players (shofars are basically trumpets made of rams' horns). In this podcast, Jad and Robert talk to engineer and sound expert David Lubman to find out how many shofars it would actually take to level a Bronze Age wall. To get a sense of the power of the shofar, we pay a visit to Cantor <a href=\"http://home.rr.com/danpincus\" target=\"_blank\">Daniel Pincus</a> to hear him and his students blow some horns. Then, we talk to inventor <a href=\"http://woodynorris.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Woody Norris</a> for a modern approach to this biblical challenge.</p>\n<p>Many thanks to the 2010 Shofar All-Stars who played for us: David Liebowitz, Daniela Drakhler, Miriam Frank, Adam Hametz-Berner, Rachel Kelk, Ed Kerson, Anna Levy, Richard Scheiner, and Robert Wine.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-10-04T22:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"93554-voices-in-your-head", "title"=>"Voices in Your Head", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10voices.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=93554", "description"=>"<p>Jad talks to Charles Fernyhough about the connection between thought, inner speech, and the voice in our heads.</p><p>In our last episode, <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2010/aug/09/\">Words</a>, we got into a debate about whether kids can think before they have words. For this podcast, Jad revisits that question with Charles Fernyhough, who tells Jad about a theory developed by a Russian psychologist name Lev Vygotsky. Vygotsky’s theory describes how words and thoughts move from speech outside our heads to speech inside our heads. We also talk to someone who hears other people's voices in his head, and ask Fernyhough about whether Vygotsky's ideas could shed light on this surprisingly common phenomena.</p>\n<p>If you want to read more about voice-hearing and schizophrenia, check out the <a href=\"http://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736%2898%2905288-X/fulltext\" target=\"_blank\">study</a> that Fernyhough mentioned in this podcast.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>In this podcast, Jad talks to Charles Fernyhough about the connection between thought and the voice in your head. How did it get there? And what's happening when people hear someone else's voice in their head?</p>", "publishTime"=>1283910910000, "publish-at"=>"2010-09-07T21:55:10", "date-line-ts"=>1283910856, "estimated-duration"=>807, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/4006709_1fb1633c41_o.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/580/435/c/80/photologue/photos/4006709_1fb1633c41_o.jpg", "h"=>435, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/a_mason/4006709/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/4006709_1fb1633c41_o.jpg", "w"=>580, "id"=>27512, "credits-name"=>"Andrew Mason"}, "objectID"=>"93554", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Voices in Your Head", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Jad talks to Charles Fernyhough about the connection between thought, inner speech, and the voice in our heads.</p><p>In our last episode, <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2010/aug/09/\">Words</a>, we got into a debate about whether kids can think before they have words. For this podcast, Jad revisits that question with Charles Fernyhough, who tells Jad about a theory developed by a Russian psychologist name Lev Vygotsky. Vygotsky’s theory describes how words and thoughts move from speech outside our heads to speech inside our heads. We also talk to someone who hears other people's voices in his head, and ask Fernyhough about whether Vygotsky's ideas could shed light on this surprisingly common phenomena.</p>\n<p>If you want to read more about voice-hearing and schizophrenia, check out the <a href=\"http://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736%2898%2905288-X/fulltext\" target=\"_blank\">study</a> that Fernyhough mentioned in this podcast.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-09-07T21:55:10", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91974-bonus-video-words", "title"=>"Bonus Video: Words", "audio"=>nil, "description"=>"<p>Words have the power to shape the way we think and feel. In this stunning video, filmmakers <a href=\"http://www.everynone.com/\">Will Hoffman and Daniel Mercadante</a> bandy visual wordplay into a moving exploration of language set to an original score by <a href=\"http://www.unseen-music.com\">Keith Kenniff</a>.</p><p>Listen to our <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/2010/aug/09\">Words</a> show.</p>", "tease"=>"Words have the power to shape the way we think and feel. In this stunning video (made to accompany our Words episode), filmmakers Will Hoffman and Daniel Mercadante bandy visual wordp...", "publishTime"=>1281409215000, "publish-at"=>"2010-08-09T23:00:15", "date-line-ts"=>1281409215, "estimated-duration"=>0, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Still from 'Words'", "name"=>"1/words_video_still.png", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.youtube.com/", "name"=>"Youtube"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1281/693/l/80/1/words_video_still.png", "h"=>693, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=j0HfwkArpvU", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/words_video_still.png", "w"=>1281, "id"=>121238, "credits-name"=>"Everynone"}, "objectID"=>"91974", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Bonus Video: Words", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Words have the power to shape the way we think and feel. In this stunning video, filmmakers <a href=\"http://www.everynone.com/\">Will Hoffman and Daniel Mercadante</a> bandy visual wordplay into a moving exploration of language set to an original score by <a href=\"http://www.unseen-music.com\">Keith Kenniff</a>.</p><p>Listen to our <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/2010/aug/09\">Words</a> show.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-08-09T23:00:15", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91725-words", "title"=>"Words", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab091010.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91725", "description"=>"<p>It’s almost impossible to imagine a world without words. But this hour, we try to do just that.</p><p>We meet a woman who taught a 27-year-old man the first words of his life, hear a firsthand account of what it feels like to have the language center of your brain wiped out by a stroke, and retrace the birth of a brand new language 30 years ago.</p>", "tease"=>"It&rsquo;s almost impossible to imagine a world without words. But in this hour, we try to do just that.", "publishTime"=>1281369047000, "publish-at"=>"2010-08-09T11:50:47", "date-line-ts"=>1281326400, "estimated-duration"=>3430, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/writing_.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/397/397/c/80/photologue/photos/writing_.jpg", "h"=>397, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/manojvasanth/3779872335/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/writing_.jpg", "w"=>397, "id"=>27072, "credits-name"=>"ManojVasanth"}, "objectID"=>"91725", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Words", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>It’s almost impossible to imagine a world without words. But this hour, we try to do just that.</p><p>We meet a woman who taught a 27-year-old man the first words of his life, hear a firsthand account of what it feels like to have the language center of your brain wiped out by a stroke, and retrace the birth of a brand new language 30 years ago.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-08-09T11:50:47", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91971-secrets-of-success", "title"=>"Secrets of Success", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10success.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91971", "description"=>"<p><a href=\"http://www.gladwell.com/\">Malcolm Gladwell </a>doesn't like Gifted and Talented Education Programs. And he doesn't believe that innate ability can fully explain superstar hockey players or billionaire software giants. In this podcast, we listen in on a conversation between Robert and Malcolm recorded at the <a href=\"http://www.92y.org/\">92nd St Y</a>.</p><p>Robert asks Malcolm if he's a 'genius denier,' and Malcolm asks Robert if he's uncomfortable with the power of love, as they duke it out over questions of luck, talent, passion, and success.</p>", "tease"=>"Robert and Malcolm Gladwell duke it out over questions of luck, talent, passion, and success.", "publishTime"=>1280196958000, "publish-at"=>"2010-07-26T22:15:58", "date-line-ts"=>1280196958, "estimated-duration"=>1472, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Dice by rocketship", "name"=>"photologue/photos/dice.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/c/80/photologue/photos/dice.jpg", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/inkytwist/2654900768/sizes/z/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/dice.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>26953, "credits-name"=>"rocketship"}, "objectID"=>"91971", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Secrets of Success", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><a href=\"http://www.gladwell.com/\">Malcolm Gladwell </a>doesn't like Gifted and Talented Education Programs. And he doesn't believe that innate ability can fully explain superstar hockey players or billionaire software giants. In this podcast, we listen in on a conversation between Robert and Malcolm recorded at the <a href=\"http://www.92y.org/\">92nd St Y</a>.</p><p>Robert asks Malcolm if he's a 'genius denier,' and Malcolm asks Robert if he's uncomfortable with the power of love, as they duke it out over questions of luck, talent, passion, and success.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-07-26T22:15:58", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91969-the-luckiest-lobster", "title"=>"The Luckiest Lobster", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10lobster.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91969", "description"=>"<p>One place you absolutely, positively do not want to be if you're a healthy, middle-aged American lobster: trapped in a suburban grocery store in western Pennsylvania. But that's where this week's podcast begins.</p><p>It doesn't stay there long, though. <strong>Bonnie Hazen</strong> and <strong>Toni Leone</strong> take us on an adventure that carries us by car, by plane, and by boat toward a deeper understanding of those mysterious protective feelings that sometimes sweep over us -- well, some of us -- when we encounter our fellow animals -- um, okay, some of them. <strong><a href=\"http://www.trevorcorson.com\">Trevor Corson</a></strong>, author of the bestselling <a href=\"http://www.trevorcorson.com/lobster-book.html\"><em>The Secret Life of Lobsters</em></a>, assists.</p>", "tease"=>"An unlikely escape story begins in a supermarket, and ends in a boat off the coast of Maine.", "publishTime"=>1278978715000, "publish-at"=>"2010-07-12T19:51:55", "date-line-ts"=>1278978715, "estimated-duration"=>792, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"American Lobster", "name"=>"photologue/photos/lobster.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/494/640/c/80/photologue/photos/lobster.jpg", "h"=>640, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/billyh/4683661513/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/lobster.jpg", "w"=>494, "id"=>26975, "credits-name"=>"Chicken Billy"}, "objectID"=>"91969", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Luckiest Lobster", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>One place you absolutely, positively do not want to be if you're a healthy, middle-aged American lobster: trapped in a suburban grocery store in western Pennsylvania. But that's where this week's podcast begins.</p><p>It doesn't stay there long, though. <strong>Bonnie Hazen</strong> and <strong>Toni Leone</strong> take us on an adventure that carries us by car, by plane, and by boat toward a deeper understanding of those mysterious protective feelings that sometimes sweep over us -- well, some of us -- when we encounter our fellow animals -- um, okay, some of them. <strong><a href=\"http://www.trevorcorson.com\">Trevor Corson</a></strong>, author of the bestselling <a href=\"http://www.trevorcorson.com/lobster-book.html\"><em>The Secret Life of Lobsters</em></a>, assists.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-07-12T19:51:55", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91721-oops", "title"=>"Oops", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab090310.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91721", "description"=>"<p>Oops. In this hour of Radiolab, stories of unintended consequences.</p><p>You come up with a great idea. You devise a plan. You control for every imaginable variable. And once everything’s in place, the train hops your carefully laid tracks. In this episode, one psychologist's zeal to safeguard national security may have created a terrorist, while one community's efforts to protect an endangered bird had deadly consequences. And against all odds, a toxic lake spawns new life.</p>", "tease"=>"Stories of unintended consequences -- from a psychologist who may have helped create a terrorist, to a toxic lake that spawned new life.", "publishTime"=>1277740241000, "publish-at"=>"2010-06-28T11:50:41", "date-line-ts"=>1277740800, "estimated-duration"=>3441, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/156624.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/211/140/c/80/photologue/photos/156624.jpg", "h"=>140, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"<a href=\"http://www.flickr.com/photos/estreya/4084232019/\">Photo: flickr/The Real Estreya</a>", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/156624.jpg", "w"=>211, "id"=>26530, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"91721", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Oops", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Oops. In this hour of Radiolab, stories of unintended consequences.</p><p>You come up with a great idea. You devise a plan. You control for every imaginable variable. And once everything’s in place, the train hops your carefully laid tracks. In this episode, one psychologist's zeal to safeguard national security may have created a terrorist, while one community's efforts to protect an endangered bird had deadly consequences. And against all odds, a toxic lake spawns new life.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-06-28T11:50:41", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91967-strangers-in-the-mirror", "title"=>"Strangers in the Mirror", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10strangers.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91967", "description"=>"<p><a href=\"http://www.oliversacks.com/\">Oliver Sacks</a>, the famous neuroscientist and author, can't recognize faces. Neither can <a href=\"http://www.chuckclose.coe.uh.edu/\">Chuck Close</a>, the great artist known for his enormous paintings of ... that's right, faces.</p><p>Oliver and Chuck--both born with the condition known as Face Blindness--have spent their lives decoding who is saying hello to them. You can sit down with either man, talk to him for an hour, and if he sees you again just fifteen minutes later, he will have no idea who you are. (Unless you have a very squeaky voice or happen to be wearing the same odd purple hat.)</p>\n<p>In this podcast, we listen in on a conversation Robert had with Chuck and Oliver at Hunter College in New York City as part of the <a href=\"http://www.worldsciencefestival.com\">World Science Festival</a>. Chuck and Oliver tell Robert what it's like to live with Face Blindness and describe two very different ways of coping with this condition, which may be more common than we think.</p>\n<p>The World Science Festival is an annual festival in New York City that pays tribute to imagination, ingenuity, and inventiveness. It's pretty much an all-star line up of fascinating talks and performances. This year's festival just concluded, but you can still catch full broadcasts of the 2010 programs, follow their blog, and sign up for email updates <a href=\"http://www.worldsciencefestival.com/\">HERE</a>. In the meantime, we hope you enjoy this particular morsel.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>Oliver Sacks, the famous neuroscientist and author, can't recognize faces. Neither can Chuck Close--the great artist known for his enormous paintings of ... that's right, faces.</p>", "publishTime"=>1276660767000, "publish-at"=>"2010-06-15T23:59:27", "date-line-ts"=>1276660767, "estimated-duration"=>1527, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Blank by Jeremy Brooks", "name"=>"photologue/photos/stranger.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/426/640/c/80/photologue/photos/stranger.jpg", "h"=>640, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/jeremybrooks/3615928914/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/stranger.jpg", "w"=>426, "id"=>26976, "credits-name"=>"Jeremy Brooks"}, "objectID"=>"91967", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Strangers in the Mirror", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><a href=\"http://www.oliversacks.com/\">Oliver Sacks</a>, the famous neuroscientist and author, can't recognize faces. Neither can <a href=\"http://www.chuckclose.coe.uh.edu/\">Chuck Close</a>, the great artist known for his enormous paintings of ... that's right, faces.</p><p>Oliver and Chuck--both born with the condition known as Face Blindness--have spent their lives decoding who is saying hello to them. You can sit down with either man, talk to him for an hour, and if he sees you again just fifteen minutes later, he will have no idea who you are. (Unless you have a very squeaky voice or happen to be wearing the same odd purple hat.)</p>\n<p>In this podcast, we listen in on a conversation Robert had with Chuck and Oliver at Hunter College in New York City as part of the <a href=\"http://www.worldsciencefestival.com\">World Science Festival</a>. Chuck and Oliver tell Robert what it's like to live with Face Blindness and describe two very different ways of coping with this condition, which may be more common than we think.</p>\n<p>The World Science Festival is an annual festival in New York City that pays tribute to imagination, ingenuity, and inventiveness. It's pretty much an all-star line up of fascinating talks and performances. This year's festival just concluded, but you can still catch full broadcasts of the 2010 programs, follow their blog, and sign up for email updates <a href=\"http://www.worldsciencefestival.com/\">HERE</a>. In the meantime, we hope you enjoy this particular morsel.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-06-15T23:59:27", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91713-famous-tumors", "title"=>"Famous Tumors", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab050710.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91713", "description"=>"<p>In this hour of Radiolab: an unflinching look at the good, bad, and ugly side of tumors.</p><p>Say hello to the growth that killed Ulysses S. Grant, meet Tasmanian Devils battling contagious tumors, and get to know the woman whose cancer cells changed modern medicine.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>Say hello to the growth that killed Ulysses S. Grant, &amp; get to know the woman whose cancer cells changed modern medicine.</p>", "publishTime"=>1274111428000, "publish-at"=>"2010-05-17T11:50:28", "date-line-ts"=>1274068800, "estimated-duration"=>3440, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"HeLa cells treated with a microtubule stabilizing drug, paclitaxel. Stained for DNA (blue), tubulin (green) and actin (red).", "name"=>"photologue/photos/famous_tumors.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/490/c/80/photologue/photos/famous_tumors.jpg", "h"=>490, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"HeLa cells treated with a microtubule stabilizing drug, paclitaxel. Stained for DNA (blue), tubulin (green) and actin (red).", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/gehealthcare/4254334450/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/famous_tumors.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>66012, "credits-name"=>"Anne Martinez, CEA Grenoble/ iRTSV, France / GE Healthcare"}, "objectID"=>"91713", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Famous Tumors", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this hour of Radiolab: an unflinching look at the good, bad, and ugly side of tumors.</p><p>Say hello to the growth that killed Ulysses S. Grant, meet Tasmanian Devils battling contagious tumors, and get to know the woman whose cancer cells changed modern medicine.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-05-17T11:50:28", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91960-vanishing-words", "title"=>"Vanishing Words", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10nuns.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91960", "description"=>"<p>Agatha Christie's clever detective novels may reveal more about the inner workings of the human mind than she intended. In this podcast, a look at what scientists uncover when they treat words like data.</p><p>According to <a href=\"http://www.chass.utoronto.ca/~ian/\">Dr. Ian Lancashire</a> at the University of Toronto, the Queen of Crime left behind hidden clues to the real-life mysteries of human aging in her writing. Meanwhile, <a href=\"http://www.cmrr.umn.edu/facultystaff/kelvin.shtml\">Dr. Kelvin Lim</a> and <a href=\"http://www.pharmacy.umn.edu/faculty/pakhomov_sergey/home.html\">Dr. Serguei Pakhomov</a> from the University of Minnesota add to the intrigue with the story of an unexpected find in a convent archive that could someday help pinpoint very early warning signs for Alzheimer's disease and dementia. <strong>Sister Alberta Sheridan</strong>, a 94-year-old<a href=\"http://www.healthstudies.umn.edu/nunstudy/\"> Nun Study</a> participant, reads an essay she wrote more than 70 years ago.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>When scientists treat words like data, clues to the real-life mysteries of human aging are found in the writings of Agatha Christie and 678 nuns.</p>", "publishTime"=>1273042835000, "publish-at"=>"2010-05-05T03:00:35", "date-line-ts"=>1273042835, "estimated-duration"=>944, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/typewriter-keys-300x224.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/300/224/c/80/photologue/photos/typewriter-keys-300x224.jpg", "h"=>224, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/76283671@N00/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/typewriter-keys-300x224.jpg", "w"=>300, "id"=>26982, "credits-name"=>"Laineys Repertoire"}, "objectID"=>"91960", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Vanishing Words", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Agatha Christie's clever detective novels may reveal more about the inner workings of the human mind than she intended. In this podcast, a look at what scientists uncover when they treat words like data.</p><p>According to <a href=\"http://www.chass.utoronto.ca/~ian/\">Dr. Ian Lancashire</a> at the University of Toronto, the Queen of Crime left behind hidden clues to the real-life mysteries of human aging in her writing. Meanwhile, <a href=\"http://www.cmrr.umn.edu/facultystaff/kelvin.shtml\">Dr. Kelvin Lim</a> and <a href=\"http://www.pharmacy.umn.edu/faculty/pakhomov_sergey/home.html\">Dr. Serguei Pakhomov</a> from the University of Minnesota add to the intrigue with the story of an unexpected find in a convent archive that could someday help pinpoint very early warning signs for Alzheimer's disease and dementia. <strong>Sister Alberta Sheridan</strong>, a 94-year-old<a href=\"http://www.healthstudies.umn.edu/nunstudy/\"> Nun Study</a> participant, reads an essay she wrote more than 70 years ago.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-05-05T03:00:35", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91959-the-loudest-miniature-fuzz", "title"=>"The Loudest Miniature Fuzz", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10buke.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91959", "description"=>"<p>Music duo Buke and Gass play for us, attempt to describe their genre-bending sound, and talk a bit about what's it like to play out what you don't say in this podcast.</p><p><a href=\"http://bukeandgass.com/\"><strong>Buke and Gass</strong></a> (pronounced 'Buke and Gase') produce weird and wonderful, twangy and chaotic sounds with their homemade instruments. Though they sound like a whole rip-roaring party of bodies, the band is in fact only comprised of two people: Arone Dyer and Aron Sanchez.</p>\n<p>Also, for those of you wondering about AWE-MAGEDDON, our live event series, here's an excerpt from our first show!</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://webscript.princeton.edu/~icouzin/website/iain-couzin-cv/\">Iain Couzin</a> is an assistant professor in the Department of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology at Princeton University, where he studies collective behaviors within animal groups.</p>\n<p>Check out more <strong><a href=\"http://www.thegreenespace.org/thegreenespace/\">EVENTS HAPPENING AT WNYC..</a>.</strong></p>", "tease"=>"<p>Music duo Buke and Gass talk to Jad about coaxing delightfully twangy sounds from their homemade instruments.</p>", "publishTime"=>1271818569000, "publish-at"=>"2010-04-20T22:56:09", "date-line-ts"=>1271818569, "estimated-duration"=>870, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Buke and Gass", "name"=>"photologue/photos/3-300x240.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/300/240/c/80/photologue/photos/3-300x240.jpg", "h"=>240, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Buke and Gass", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/3-300x240.jpg", "w"=>300, "id"=>26984, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"91959", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Loudest Miniature Fuzz", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Music duo Buke and Gass play for us, attempt to describe their genre-bending sound, and talk a bit about what's it like to play out what you don't say in this podcast.</p><p><a href=\"http://bukeandgass.com/\"><strong>Buke and Gass</strong></a> (pronounced 'Buke and Gase') produce weird and wonderful, twangy and chaotic sounds with their homemade instruments. Though they sound like a whole rip-roaring party of bodies, the band is in fact only comprised of two people: Arone Dyer and Aron Sanchez.</p>\n<p>Also, for those of you wondering about AWE-MAGEDDON, our live event series, here's an excerpt from our first show!</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://webscript.princeton.edu/~icouzin/website/iain-couzin-cv/\">Iain Couzin</a> is an assistant professor in the Department of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology at Princeton University, where he studies collective behaviors within animal groups.</p>\n<p>Check out more <strong><a href=\"http://www.thegreenespace.org/thegreenespace/\">EVENTS HAPPENING AT WNYC..</a>.</strong></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-04-20T22:56:09", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91709-limits", "title"=>"Limits", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab041610.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91709", "description"=>"<p>On this hour of Radiolab: a journey to the edge of human limits.</p><p>How much can you jam into a human brain? How far can you push yourself past feelings of exhaustion? We test physical endurance with a bike race that makes the Tour de France look like child’s play, and mental capacity with a mind-stretching memory competition. And we ask if robots--for better or worse--may be forging beyond the limits of human understanding.</p>\n<p>We'd also like to honor the passing of one of our guests in this episode, Jure Robic. Lulu Miller, who interviewed Jure for this story, wrote a remembrance of Jure, and included some audio not featured in the episode. You can find that <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/articles/96512-goodbye-jure\">here. </a></p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this piece stated in error that Mr. S. remembered what his editor had assigned all the reporters at the newspaper. In A.R. Luria’s book, there is mention only of Mr. S. remembering his own assignments. We also inaccurately stated the rate at which Mr. S. could recall numbers. The actual rate was 50 numbers in 2.5-3 minutes. We also incorrectly stated that Mr. S. memorized Dante's “Inferno.” In fact, Mr. S. memorized only the first several stanzas. In addition, we depicted details of Mr. S.’s mnemonic performances without making clear that they were based in part on supposition. The audio has been adjusted to correct these facts and clarify our suppositions.</em></p>", "tease"=>"A journey to the edge of human limits -- from a bike race that makes the Tour de France look like child&rsquo;s play, to a mind-stretching memory competition.", "publishTime"=>1270482621000, "publish-at"=>"2010-04-05T11:50:21", "date-line-ts"=>1270483200, "estimated-duration"=>3598, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/150430.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/211/211/c/80/photologue/photos/150430.jpg", "h"=>211, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"<a href=http://www.flickr.com/photos/marceltuit/4250296563/> Photo: flickr/MarcelT1981 </a>", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/marceltuit/4250296563/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/150430.jpg", "w"=>211, "id"=>26518, "credits-name"=>"MarcelT1981"}, "objectID"=>"91709", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Limits", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>On this hour of Radiolab: a journey to the edge of human limits.</p><p>How much can you jam into a human brain? How far can you push yourself past feelings of exhaustion? We test physical endurance with a bike race that makes the Tour de France look like child’s play, and mental capacity with a mind-stretching memory competition. And we ask if robots--for better or worse--may be forging beyond the limits of human understanding.</p>\n<p>We'd also like to honor the passing of one of our guests in this episode, Jure Robic. Lulu Miller, who interviewed Jure for this story, wrote a remembrance of Jure, and included some audio not featured in the episode. You can find that <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/articles/96512-goodbye-jure\">here. </a></p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this piece stated in error that Mr. S. remembered what his editor had assigned all the reporters at the newspaper. In A.R. Luria’s book, there is mention only of Mr. S. remembering his own assignments. We also inaccurately stated the rate at which Mr. S. could recall numbers. The actual rate was 50 numbers in 2.5-3 minutes. We also incorrectly stated that Mr. S. memorized Dante's “Inferno.” In fact, Mr. S. memorized only the first several stanzas. In addition, we depicted details of Mr. S.’s mnemonic performances without making clear that they were based in part on supposition. The audio has been adjusted to correct these facts and clarify our suppositions.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-04-05T11:50:21", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91948-the-bus-stop", "title"=>"The Bus Stop", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10busstop.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91948", "description"=>"<p>There’s a common problem faced by Alzheimer's and Dementia patients all over the world: lost in their memories, they sometimes get disoriented, and wander off. In this podcast, Lulu Miller talks to a nursing home in Düsseldorf, Germany that came up with a novel solution.</p><p>When an Alzheimer's or Dementia patient wanders, they can end up too far from home, frightened, or hurt. So what are you supposed to do if your loved one--a parent, a grandparent--begins to wander in this way? Often times the only solution is to lock them up. Which just feels cruel. But what else are you supposed to do if you want to keep them safe?</p>\n<p>Well, the <a href=\"http://www.handelsblatt.com/magazin/nachrichten/phantomhaltestelle-hilft-demenzkranken;1402368\">Benrath Senior Center</a>, came up with a new idea. An idea so simple you almost think it couldn't work. Producer Lulu Miller talks to Richard Neureither and Regine Hauch about what they've done in Düsseldorf.</p>", "tease"=>"Lulu Miller talks to a nursing home in D&uuml;sseldorf, Germany that came up with a novel approach to caring for Alzheimer's and Dementia patients.", "publishTime"=>1269386142000, "publish-at"=>"2010-03-23T19:15:42", "date-line-ts"=>1269386142, "estimated-duration"=>806, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Bus stop", "name"=>"photologue/photos/bus_stop.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/503/464/c/80/photologue/photos/bus_stop.jpg", "h"=>464, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Bus stop", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/vittis/877730611/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/bus_stop.jpg", "w"=>503, "id"=>67081, "credits-name"=>"Vittis from Lithuania"}, "objectID"=>"91948", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Bus Stop", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>There’s a common problem faced by Alzheimer's and Dementia patients all over the world: lost in their memories, they sometimes get disoriented, and wander off. In this podcast, Lulu Miller talks to a nursing home in Düsseldorf, Germany that came up with a novel solution.</p><p>When an Alzheimer's or Dementia patient wanders, they can end up too far from home, frightened, or hurt. So what are you supposed to do if your loved one--a parent, a grandparent--begins to wander in this way? Often times the only solution is to lock them up. Which just feels cruel. But what else are you supposed to do if you want to keep them safe?</p>\n<p>Well, the <a href=\"http://www.handelsblatt.com/magazin/nachrichten/phantomhaltestelle-hilft-demenzkranken;1402368\">Benrath Senior Center</a>, came up with a new idea. An idea so simple you almost think it couldn't work. Producer Lulu Miller talks to Richard Neureither and Regine Hauch about what they've done in Düsseldorf.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-03-23T19:15:42", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91947-do-i-know-you", "title"=>"Do I Know You?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10capgras.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91947", "description"=>"<p>How do you know your mother is really your mother? It's simple, right? You look at her, you recognize her, enough said. Well, in this podcast...it may not be that simple.</p><p>It turns out that recognizing people, even the people we know the best, is more about how they make us feel than what we see in front of our eyes. And when your feelings about someone get jumbled, it can be disorienting, even traumatic. In this the podcast we talk to <a href=\"http://www.carolwberman.com/\">Dr. Carol Berman</a> and <a href=\"http://www.edge.org/3rd_culture/bios/ramachandran.html\">Dr. V.S. Ramachandran</a> to explore the psychology and neurology behind a rare but disturbing delusional disorder called Capgras.</p>", "tease"=>"A rare and haunting disorder called Capgras turns loved ones into imposters--and reveals that recognizing people, even the people we know the best, is more about how they make us feel...", "publishTime"=>1268092322000, "publish-at"=>"2010-03-08T18:52:02", "date-line-ts"=>1268092322, "estimated-duration"=>528, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/316991959_93fc574e02-200x300.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/200/300/l/80/photologue/photos/316991959_93fc574e02-200x300.jpg", "h"=>300, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/sklathill/316991959/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/316991959_93fc574e02-200x300.jpg", "w"=>200, "id"=>27009, "credits-name"=>"sklathill"}, "objectID"=>"91947", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Do I Know You?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>How do you know your mother is really your mother? It's simple, right? You look at her, you recognize her, enough said. Well, in this podcast...it may not be that simple.</p><p>It turns out that recognizing people, even the people we know the best, is more about how they make us feel than what we see in front of our eyes. And when your feelings about someone get jumbled, it can be disorienting, even traumatic. In this the podcast we talk to <a href=\"http://www.carolwberman.com/\">Dr. Carol Berman</a> and <a href=\"http://www.edge.org/3rd_culture/bios/ramachandran.html\">Dr. V.S. Ramachandran</a> to explore the psychology and neurology behind a rare but disturbing delusional disorder called Capgras.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-03-08T18:52:02", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91705-lucy", "title"=>"Lucy", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab040910.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91705", "description"=>"<p>Chimps. Bonobos. Humans. We're all great apes, but that doesn’t mean we’re one happy family. This hour of Radiolab: stories of trying to live together.</p><p>Is this kind of cross-species co-habitation an utterly stupid idea? Or might it be our one last hope as more and more humans fill up the planet? A chimp named Lucy teaches us the ups and downs of growing up human, and a visit to The Great Ape Trust in Des Moines, Iowa highlights some of the basics of bonobo culture (be careful, they bite).</p>\n<p>Check out this slideshow of Lucy's life, including the photo snapped as Janis and Lucy hugged in Gambia:</p>\n<p><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm140274470179808ef691c28-f71d-4d28-a4f1-aeb69c6a3a3f\"><iframe src=\"https://player.vimeo.com/video/9377513?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;h=5f7d534357&amp;showinfo=0&amp;app_id=122963&amp;enablejsapi=1\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" title=\"Lucy\" id=\"a5138536407857181480\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://vimeo.com/9377513\"></iframe></div></div>  </p>\n<p>Slideshow produced by Sharon Shattuck.</p>", "tease"=>"Chimps. Bonobos. Humans. We're all great apes, but that doesn&rsquo;t mean we&rsquo;re one happy family.", "publishTime"=>1266598206000, "publish-at"=>"2010-02-19T11:50:06", "date-line-ts"=>1266598800, "estimated-duration"=>3413, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/150362.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/211/211/c/80/photologue/photos/150362.jpg", "h"=>211, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/150362.jpg", "w"=>211, "id"=>26515, "credits-name"=>"Courtesy of <a href=http://www.sbbks.com/> Science and Behavior Books, Inc. </a>"}, "objectID"=>"91705", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Lucy", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Chimps. Bonobos. Humans. We're all great apes, but that doesn’t mean we’re one happy family. This hour of Radiolab: stories of trying to live together.</p><p>Is this kind of cross-species co-habitation an utterly stupid idea? Or might it be our one last hope as more and more humans fill up the planet? A chimp named Lucy teaches us the ups and downs of growing up human, and a visit to The Great Ape Trust in Des Moines, Iowa highlights some of the basics of bonobo culture (be careful, they bite).</p>\n<p>Check out this slideshow of Lucy's life, including the photo snapped as Janis and Lucy hugged in Gambia:</p>\n<p><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm140274470179808ef691c28-f71d-4d28-a4f1-aeb69c6a3a3f\"><iframe src=\"https://player.vimeo.com/video/9377513?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;h=5f7d534357&amp;showinfo=0&amp;app_id=122963&amp;enablejsapi=1\" width=\"400\" height=\"300\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" title=\"Lucy\" id=\"a5138536407857181480\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://vimeo.com/9377513\"></iframe></div></div>  </p>\n<p>Slideshow produced by Sharon Shattuck.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-02-19T11:50:06", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91942-the-shy-baboon", "title"=>"The Shy Baboon ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10rainhut.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91942", "description"=>"<p>In this podcast, a biopsychologist attempts to find an elusive bit of shared space across species lines.</p><p><a href=\"http://www.lsa.umich.edu/psych/people/directory/profiles/faculty/?uniquename=bsmuts\">Barbara Smuts</a>, a professor at the University of Michigan, tells the story of trying to gain the trust of a troop of baboons in a remote area of Kenya in the 1970s.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>Biopsychologist Barbara Smuts takes us to a remote area of Kenya, where she tried to gain the trust of a troop of baboons in the 1970s.</p>", "publishTime"=>1265672328000, "publish-at"=>"2010-02-08T18:38:48", "date-line-ts"=>1265672328, "estimated-duration"=>591, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"photologue/photos/BabsUnderTree.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/450/307/c/80/photologue/photos/BabsUnderTree.jpg", "h"=>307, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/BabsUnderTree.jpg", "w"=>450, "id"=>27012, "credits-name"=>"Barbara Smuts"}, "objectID"=>"91942", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Shy Baboon ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this podcast, a biopsychologist attempts to find an elusive bit of shared space across species lines.</p><p><a href=\"http://www.lsa.umich.edu/psych/people/directory/profiles/faculty/?uniquename=bsmuts\">Barbara Smuts</a>, a professor at the University of Michigan, tells the story of trying to gain the trust of a troop of baboons in a remote area of Kenya in the 1970s.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-02-08T18:38:48", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91939-fu-manchu", "title"=>"Fu Manchu", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast10fumanchu.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91939", "description"=>"<p>In our episode <a href=\"http://beta.radiolab.org/2010/apr/02/\">Animal Minds</a>, we asked whether it was possible for one animal to know what was going on in another animal's mind. For us, it was a really about whether we, as humans, can really share a meaningful moment with an animal. In this podcast, we take that question another step further.</p><p>Can an animal know what's in our heads so well that they can manipulate and deceive us? To answer that question, reporter Ben Calhoun takes us back to the 1960s to tell the story of a showdown between zookeeper Jerry Stones and a wily orangutan named Fu Manchu. Then, to help us get a grip on the science behind animals and deception, Ben talks to primatologist and orangutan expert <a href=\"http://www.indyzoo.com/\">Rob Shumaker</a> of the <a href=\"http://www.indyzoo.com/\">Indianapolis Zoo</a>.</p>\n<p>Sorry photo is not actually Fu Manchu.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>A showdown between a zookeeper and an orangutan named Fu Manchu raises a question: can an animal know what's in your head well enough to manipulate and deceive you?</p>", "publishTime"=>1264471239000, "publish-at"=>"2010-01-25T21:00:39", "date-line-ts"=>1264471239, "estimated-duration"=>738, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Orangutan", "name"=>"photologue/photos/fu_manchu.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/414/c/80/photologue/photos/fu_manchu.jpg", "h"=>414, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Orangutan", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/frank-wouters/3843033833/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/fu_manchu.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>66014, "credits-name"=>"belgianchocolate"}, "objectID"=>"91939", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Fu Manchu", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In our episode <a href=\"http://beta.radiolab.org/2010/apr/02/\">Animal Minds</a>, we asked whether it was possible for one animal to know what was going on in another animal's mind. For us, it was a really about whether we, as humans, can really share a meaningful moment with an animal. In this podcast, we take that question another step further.</p><p>Can an animal know what's in our heads so well that they can manipulate and deceive us? To answer that question, reporter Ben Calhoun takes us back to the 1960s to tell the story of a showdown between zookeeper Jerry Stones and a wily orangutan named Fu Manchu. Then, to help us get a grip on the science behind animals and deception, Ben talks to primatologist and orangutan expert <a href=\"http://www.indyzoo.com/\">Rob Shumaker</a> of the <a href=\"http://www.indyzoo.com/\">Indianapolis Zoo</a>.</p>\n<p>Sorry photo is not actually Fu Manchu.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-01-25T21:00:39", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91701-animal-minds", "title"=>"Animal Minds", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab040210.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91701", "description"=>"<p>In this hour of Radiolab, stories of cross-species communication.</p><p>When we gaze into the eyes of a wild animal, or even a beloved pet, can we ever really know what they might be thinking? Is it naive to assume they're experiencing something close to human emotions? Or is it ridiculous to assume that they AREN'T feeling something like that? We get the story of a rescued whale that may have found a way to say thanks, ask whether dogs feel guilt, and wonder if a successful predator may have fallen in love with a photographer.</p>", "tease"=>"Communicating across species -- from bringing pets to church, to a rescued whale that may have found a way to say thanks.", "publishTime"=>1263228597000, "publish-at"=>"2010-01-11T11:49:57", "date-line-ts"=>1263229200, "estimated-duration"=>3422, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Dog and man", "name"=>"photologue/photos/animal_minds_.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/400/412/c/80/photologue/photos/animal_minds_.jpg", "h"=>412, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Dog and man", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/philhawksworth/2664707739/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/animal_minds_.jpg", "w"=>400, "id"=>67082, "credits-name"=>"Phil Hawksworth"}, "objectID"=>"91701", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Animal Minds", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this hour of Radiolab, stories of cross-species communication.</p><p>When we gaze into the eyes of a wild animal, or even a beloved pet, can we ever really know what they might be thinking? Is it naive to assume they're experiencing something close to human emotions? Or is it ridiculous to assume that they AREN'T feeling something like that? We get the story of a rescued whale that may have found a way to say thanks, ask whether dogs feel guilt, and wonder if a successful predator may have fallen in love with a photographer.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2010-01-11T11:49:57", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2011=>[{"slug"=>"177199-mutant-rights", "title"=>"Mutant Rights", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11xmen.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=177199", "description"=>"<p>In this podcast short, a strange twist of legal taxonomy causes a dispute over whether X-MEN  action figures are toys or dolls and sparks a court case about what it means  to be human.</p><p>Reporter <strong>Ike Sriskandarajah </strong>tells Jad and Robert a story about two international trade lawyers, <strong>Sherry Singer </strong>and<strong> Indie Singh</strong>, who noticed something interesting while looking at a book of tariff classifications. \"Dolls,\" which represent human beings, are taxed at almost twice the rate of \"toys,\" which represent something not human - such as robots, monsters, or demons. As soon as they read that, Sherry and Indie saw dollar signs. it just so happened that one of their clients, Marvel Comics, was importing its action figures as dolls. And one set of action figures really piqued Sherry and Indie's interest: The XMEN, normal humans who, at around puberty, start to change in ways that give them strange powers.</p>\n<p>So Sherry and Indie went down to the customs office with a bag of XMEN action figures to convince the US government that these mutants are NOT human. That argument eventually became a court case that went on for years.<strong> </strong><strong> Joe Liebman, </strong>former international trade attorney for the US Department of Justice,<strong> </strong>helps us understand the government's side. And <strong>Ike, </strong>with help from director and producer <strong>Bryan Singer,</strong> reflects on the story of the XMEN, and tells us why this case is so poignant for anyone who’s fought to be different without being cast as an outsider.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"In this podcast short, a strange twist of legal taxonomy causes a dispute over whether X-MEN  action figures are toys or dolls and sparks a court case about what it means  to be human.", "publishTime"=>1324944000000, "publish-at"=>"2011-12-26T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1324568542, "estimated-duration"=>944, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Radiolab presents all-new actionable figures", "name"=>"photologue/photos/XMEN_620.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"WNYC"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/XMEN_620.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Radiolab presents all-new actionable figures", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/XMEN_620.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>66592, "credits-name"=>"Adam Cole"}, "objectID"=>"177199", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Mutant Rights", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this podcast short, a strange twist of legal taxonomy causes a dispute over whether X-MEN  action figures are toys or dolls and sparks a court case about what it means  to be human.</p><p>Reporter <strong>Ike Sriskandarajah </strong>tells Jad and Robert a story about two international trade lawyers, <strong>Sherry Singer </strong>and<strong> Indie Singh</strong>, who noticed something interesting while looking at a book of tariff classifications. \"Dolls,\" which represent human beings, are taxed at almost twice the rate of \"toys,\" which represent something not human - such as robots, monsters, or demons. As soon as they read that, Sherry and Indie saw dollar signs. it just so happened that one of their clients, Marvel Comics, was importing its action figures as dolls. And one set of action figures really piqued Sherry and Indie's interest: The XMEN, normal humans who, at around puberty, start to change in ways that give them strange powers.</p>\n<p>So Sherry and Indie went down to the customs office with a bag of XMEN action figures to convince the US government that these mutants are NOT human. That argument eventually became a court case that went on for years.<strong> </strong><strong> Joe Liebman, </strong>former international trade attorney for the US Department of Justice,<strong> </strong>helps us understand the government's side. And <strong>Ike, </strong>with help from director and producer <strong>Bryan Singer,</strong> reflects on the story of the XMEN, and tells us why this case is so poignant for anyone who’s fought to be different without being cast as an outsider.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-12-26T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"175276-radiolab-presents-99-invisible", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: 99% Invisible", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11invisible.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=175276", "description"=>"<p>Roman Mars loves to spotlight the seams and joints that make up the world around us. He's the host of an irresistible podcast called 99% Invisible--a series of tiny radio stories that provoke enormous questions. Roman joins Jad and Robert to play a few favorites, and to chat about the hidden language of design that shapes our lives--from sound effects to stuff that’s more ... concrete.</p><p>In this short, Jad and Robert invite Roman into the studio to feature three of his episodes and answer some questions.</p>\n<p>We start with the art of making modern electronic devices \"sound right\" with carefully designed beeps and clicks. Then, a complicated portrait emerges from decades-old graffiti etched into the sidewalks of San Francisco. And dense infographics become a new kind of diary for a guy who charts his time on Earth as a series of <a href=\"http://feltron.com/\" target=\"_blank\">annual reports</a>. Roman's podcast is full of little shake-up moments--discoveries that make you stop and re-examine the objects you see and touch every day.</p>", "tease"=>"Roman Mars loves to spotlight the seams and joints that make up the world around us. He's the host of an irresistible podcast called 99% Invisible--a series of tiny radio stories that...", "publishTime"=>1323734400000, "publish-at"=>"2011-12-12T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1323734400, "estimated-duration"=>1525, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"From the 2009 Feltron Annual Report ", "name"=>"photologue/photos/feltron_ar09_03.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/raw/photologue/photos/feltron_ar09_03.jpg", "h"=>0, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"From the 2009 Feltron Annual Report ", "credits-url"=>"http://feltron.com/ar09_03.html", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/feltron_ar09_03.jpg", "w"=>0, "id"=>65913, "credits-name"=>"Courtesy of Nicholas Felton"}, "objectID"=>"175276", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: 99% Invisible", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Roman Mars loves to spotlight the seams and joints that make up the world around us. He's the host of an irresistible podcast called 99% Invisible--a series of tiny radio stories that provoke enormous questions. Roman joins Jad and Robert to play a few favorites, and to chat about the hidden language of design that shapes our lives--from sound effects to stuff that’s more ... concrete.</p><p>In this short, Jad and Robert invite Roman into the studio to feature three of his episodes and answer some questions.</p>\n<p>We start with the art of making modern electronic devices \"sound right\" with carefully designed beeps and clicks. Then, a complicated portrait emerges from decades-old graffiti etched into the sidewalks of San Francisco. And dense infographics become a new kind of diary for a guy who charts his time on Earth as a series of <a href=\"http://feltron.com/\" target=\"_blank\">annual reports</a>. Roman's podcast is full of little shake-up moments--discoveries that make you stop and re-examine the objects you see and touch every day.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-12-12T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"172693-death-mask", "title"=>"Death Mask", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11deathmask.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=172693", "description"=>"<p>Near the end of the 19th century, a mysterious young woman with a beguiling smile turned up in Paris. She became a huge sensation. She also happened to be dead. You'd probably recognize her face yourself. You might have even touched it.</p><p>Radiolabber <strong><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/people/sean-cole/\" target=\"_blank\">Sean Cole</a></strong> explains that, long ago, death masks were a common way of preserving the faces of famous people--clay moulds were made of Napoleon, Beethoven, Lincoln, and so on. But one of the most famous death masks was of a woman who wasn't famous at all when she was alive. BBC producer <strong>Jeremy Grange</strong> tells the story of this face, known simply as \"L'inconnue de la Seine\" (the unknown woman of the Seine), and how it found its way into living rooms across Europe.</p>\n<p>Fast forward to 1960. An Austrian doctor named Peter Safar was developing the basics of CPR, and he needed a way for people to practice his new method. He tracked down a toy maker in Norway, Asmund Laerdal, who had constructed prosthetic wounds for use in military training. Little did Safar know that Laerdal had a compelling, personal reason for getting involved. Ultimately, Laerdal decided the best way to learn artificial resuscitation would to practice on a dummy. All he needed was the perfect face. </p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/photos/Asmund_Laerdal_with_the_Resusci_Anne.jpg\" alt=\"Asmund Laerdal with Resusci Anne\" width=\"620\" height=\"372\"></p>\n<p><em>Asmund Laerdal with the CPR doll Resusci Anne. </em></p>\n<p>Sean tells Jad &amp; Robert how the dummy got her face, and about the real woman behind the mask.</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/photos/inconnue_resusci_anne.jpg\" alt=\"L'inconnue &amp; Resusci Anne\"></p>\n<p><em>L'inconnue (<a href=\"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inconnue.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">wiki commons</a>) and Resusci Anne (<a href=\"http://www.flickr.com/photos/45131642@N00/4392146993/\" target=\"_blank\">Phil_Parker/flickrCC-BY-2.0</a>)</em></p>", "tease"=>"Radiolabber&nbsp;Sean Cole&nbsp;explains that, long ago, death masks were a common way of preserving the faces of famous people--clay moulds were made of Napoleon, Beethoven, Lincoln, and so on.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1322552211000, "publish-at"=>"2011-11-29T02:36:51", "date-line-ts"=>1322466108, "estimated-duration"=>1008, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"L'inconnue", "name"=>"photologue/photos/linconnueAC.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"WNYC"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/500/500/c/80/photologue/photos/linconnueAC.jpg", "h"=>500, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"L'inconnue", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/linconnueAC.jpg", "w"=>500, "id"=>64947, "credits-name"=>"Adam Cole"}, "objectID"=>"172693", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Death Mask", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Near the end of the 19th century, a mysterious young woman with a beguiling smile turned up in Paris. She became a huge sensation. She also happened to be dead. You'd probably recognize her face yourself. You might have even touched it.</p><p>Radiolabber <strong><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/people/sean-cole/\" target=\"_blank\">Sean Cole</a></strong> explains that, long ago, death masks were a common way of preserving the faces of famous people--clay moulds were made of Napoleon, Beethoven, Lincoln, and so on. But one of the most famous death masks was of a woman who wasn't famous at all when she was alive. BBC producer <strong>Jeremy Grange</strong> tells the story of this face, known simply as \"L'inconnue de la Seine\" (the unknown woman of the Seine), and how it found its way into living rooms across Europe.</p>\n<p>Fast forward to 1960. An Austrian doctor named Peter Safar was developing the basics of CPR, and he needed a way for people to practice his new method. He tracked down a toy maker in Norway, Asmund Laerdal, who had constructed prosthetic wounds for use in military training. Little did Safar know that Laerdal had a compelling, personal reason for getting involved. Ultimately, Laerdal decided the best way to learn artificial resuscitation would to practice on a dummy. All he needed was the perfect face. </p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/photos/Asmund_Laerdal_with_the_Resusci_Anne.jpg\" alt=\"Asmund Laerdal with Resusci Anne\" width=\"620\" height=\"372\"></p>\n<p><em>Asmund Laerdal with the CPR doll Resusci Anne. </em></p>\n<p>Sean tells Jad &amp; Robert how the dummy got her face, and about the real woman behind the mask.</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/photos/inconnue_resusci_anne.jpg\" alt=\"L'inconnue &amp; Resusci Anne\"></p>\n<p><em>L'inconnue (<a href=\"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inconnue.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">wiki commons</a>) and Resusci Anne (<a href=\"http://www.flickr.com/photos/45131642@N00/4392146993/\" target=\"_blank\">Phil_Parker/flickrCC-BY-2.0</a>)</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-11-29T02:36:51", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"169879-patient-zero", "title"=>"Patient Zero", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab111411.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=169879", "description"=>"<p>The greatest mysteries have a shadowy figure at the center—someone who sets things in motion and holds the key to how the story unfolds. In epidemiology, this central character is known as Patient Zero—the case at the heart of an outbreak. This hour, Radiolab hunts for Patient Zeroes from all over the map.</p><p>We start with the story of perhaps the most iconic Patient Zero of all time: Typhoid Mary. Then, we dive into a molecular detective story to pinpoint the beginning of the AIDS, and we re-imagine the moment the virus that caused the global pandemic sprang to life. After that, we're left wondering if you can trace the spread of an idea the way you can trace the spread of a disease. In the end, we find ourselves faced with a choice between competing claims about the origin of the high five. And we come to a perfectly sensible, thoroughly disturbing conclusion about the nature of the universe ... all by way of the cowboy hat.</p>", "tease"=>"We hunt for Patient Zeroes from all over the map.", "publishTime"=>1321348646000, "publish-at"=>"2011-11-15T04:17:26", "date-line-ts"=>1321290000, "estimated-duration"=>4424, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Mexico City train passengers wearing surgical masks", "name"=>"1/patient_zero.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/605/c/80/1/patient_zero.jpg", "h"=>605, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/eneas/3471986083/in/photolist-6hNQv2-7XGJf-8N7N9j-4mnXV9-cWtQv1-bokECF-dcqdHb-7T9doc-bWqDgR-e43Q7M-e4P5iR-ceBZ8Q-bD4fLt-4ZsQND-bqLWNZ-cemECf-cdUcmS-e4kY4G-n6p7r-8xuYpW-8xu", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/patient_zero.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>102360, "credits-name"=>"Eneas De Troya"}, "objectID"=>"169879", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Patient Zero", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>The greatest mysteries have a shadowy figure at the center—someone who sets things in motion and holds the key to how the story unfolds. In epidemiology, this central character is known as Patient Zero—the case at the heart of an outbreak. This hour, Radiolab hunts for Patient Zeroes from all over the map.</p><p>We start with the story of perhaps the most iconic Patient Zero of all time: Typhoid Mary. Then, we dive into a molecular detective story to pinpoint the beginning of the AIDS, and we re-imagine the moment the virus that caused the global pandemic sprang to life. After that, we're left wondering if you can trace the spread of an idea the way you can trace the spread of a disease. In the end, we find ourselves faced with a choice between competing claims about the origin of the high five. And we come to a perfectly sensible, thoroughly disturbing conclusion about the nature of the universe ... all by way of the cowboy hat.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-11-15T04:17:26", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"167837-sleepless-south-sudan", "title"=>"Sleepless in South Sudan", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11sleepless.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=167837", "description"=>"<p>Carl Zimmer is one of our go-to guys when we need help untangling a complicated scientific idea. But in this short, he unravels something much more personal.</p><p>One night 14 years ago, <strong><a href=\"http://carlzimmer.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Carl Zimmer</a></strong> woke up in the middle of the night in a panic. He was in South Sudan to research a collection of terrible, deadly diseases--like sleeping sickness and a particularly virulent strain of malaria--that plagued the region. As he looked up at the moon, he noticed several holes in his mosquito netting, and his brain began to spin through the grim possibilities.</p>\n<p>Without giving too much away, Carl goes on to tell the story of how he found himself in that tent with the torn netting in the first place. And he explains how what happened that night changed his life.</p>\n<p>Carl's story was recorded in a basement in Brooklyn for <a href=\"http://storycollider.org/\" target=\"_blank\">The Story Collider</a>, a live event series, podcast, and soon-to-be magazine focusing on stories of how people from all backgrounds experience science in their personal lives.</p>\n<p>Read more from Carl Zimmer (or check out a full list of <a href=\"http://carlzimmer.com/books/books.html\" target=\"_blank\">his many books on his web site</a>):</p>\n<p><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/074320011X/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Parasite Rex: Inside the Bizarre World of Nature's Most Dangerous Creatures</em></a></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0061138401/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Evolution: The Triumph of an Idea</em></a></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1402783604/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Science Ink: Tattoos of the Science Obsessed</em></a></span></p>", "tease"=>"Carl Zimmer is one of our go-to guys when we need help untangling a complicated scientific idea. But in this short, he unravels something much more personal.", "publishTime"=>1320102000000, "publish-at"=>"2011-10-31T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1320102000, "estimated-duration"=>1395, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Carl Zimmer", "name"=>"photologue/photos/zimmer_sleepless.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/350/c/80/photologue/photos/zimmer_sleepless.jpg", "h"=>350, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Carl Zimmer", "credits-url"=>"http://storycollider.org/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/zimmer_sleepless.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>62945, "credits-name"=>"The Story Collider"}, "objectID"=>"167837", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Sleepless in South Sudan", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Carl Zimmer is one of our go-to guys when we need help untangling a complicated scientific idea. But in this short, he unravels something much more personal.</p><p>One night 14 years ago, <strong><a href=\"http://carlzimmer.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Carl Zimmer</a></strong> woke up in the middle of the night in a panic. He was in South Sudan to research a collection of terrible, deadly diseases--like sleeping sickness and a particularly virulent strain of malaria--that plagued the region. As he looked up at the moon, he noticed several holes in his mosquito netting, and his brain began to spin through the grim possibilities.</p>\n<p>Without giving too much away, Carl goes on to tell the story of how he found himself in that tent with the torn netting in the first place. And he explains how what happened that night changed his life.</p>\n<p>Carl's story was recorded in a basement in Brooklyn for <a href=\"http://storycollider.org/\" target=\"_blank\">The Story Collider</a>, a live event series, podcast, and soon-to-be magazine focusing on stories of how people from all backgrounds experience science in their personal lives.</p>\n<p>Read more from Carl Zimmer (or check out a full list of <a href=\"http://carlzimmer.com/books/books.html\" target=\"_blank\">his many books on his web site</a>):</p>\n<p><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/074320011X/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Parasite Rex: Inside the Bizarre World of Nature's Most Dangerous Creatures</em></a></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0061138401/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Evolution: The Triumph of an Idea</em></a></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1402783604/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Science Ink: Tattoos of the Science Obsessed</em></a></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-10-31T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"165190-slow", "title"=>"Slow", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11slow.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=165190", "description"=>"<p>Kohn Ashmore’s voice is arresting. It stopped his friend <strong><a href=\"http://andymillsmedia.tumblr.com/\">Andy Mills</a></strong> in his tracks the first time they met. But in this short about the power of friendship and familiarity, Andy explains that Kohn’s voice isn't the most striking thing about him at all.</p><p>When Andy first met Kohn, he saw a college freshman in a wheelchair who moved slow and talked slow. But it only took one conversation for Andy to realize that Kohn was also witty and observant. They clicked so effortlessly over lunch one day that Andy went ahead and asked an audacious question: why was Kohn so slow? Kohn told him that when he was 8-years-old, he was hit by a car. He was in a coma for five months, and when he finally woke up, he everything about him was slowed down ... except for his mind.</p>\n<p>That lunch quickly led to deep discussions and lots of late nights spent joking around and playing music. But when Andy decided to interview Kohn on tape last summer, Kohn told him another story about himself that caught Andy completely off guard--and made Andy question what it means to be truly familiar with something ... like the sound of your own voice, or that of a friend.</p>\n<p>Neurologist <strong><a href=\"http://epilepsy.med.nyu.edu/orrin-devinsky-md\" target=\"_blank\">Orrin Devinsky</a></strong> joins us to answer some questions raised by Andy and Kohn’s story, and the band <a href=\"http://www.dogsontour.org/contributors/hudsonbranch\" target=\"_blank\">Hudson Branch</a> helps us hear, and feel, the world through Kohn’s ears.</p>\n<p>Hudson Branch is: Matthew and Jacob Boll, Corey and Cobey Bienert, and Enoch Kim. Becky Beighley, Andy Mills, and Kohn Ashmore join them to sing an adaptation of Damien Rice's \"Grey Room.\"</p>", "tease"=>"When Andy first met Kohn, he saw a college freshman in a wheelchair who moved and talked slow. Getting to know Kohn&nbsp;had him&nbsp;questioning what it means to be truly familiar with something.", "publishTime"=>1318978800000, "publish-at"=>"2011-10-18T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1318978800, "estimated-duration"=>1158, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Kohn Ashmore", "name"=>"photologue/photos/KOHN_RLPHOTO.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/962/c/80/photologue/photos/KOHN_RLPHOTO.jpg", "h"=>962, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Kohn Ashmore", "credits-url"=>"http://www.jacobbollphoto.blogspot.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/KOHN_RLPHOTO.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>61857, "credits-name"=>"Jacob Boll"}, "objectID"=>"165190", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Slow", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Kohn Ashmore’s voice is arresting. It stopped his friend <strong><a href=\"http://andymillsmedia.tumblr.com/\">Andy Mills</a></strong> in his tracks the first time they met. But in this short about the power of friendship and familiarity, Andy explains that Kohn’s voice isn't the most striking thing about him at all.</p><p>When Andy first met Kohn, he saw a college freshman in a wheelchair who moved slow and talked slow. But it only took one conversation for Andy to realize that Kohn was also witty and observant. They clicked so effortlessly over lunch one day that Andy went ahead and asked an audacious question: why was Kohn so slow? Kohn told him that when he was 8-years-old, he was hit by a car. He was in a coma for five months, and when he finally woke up, he everything about him was slowed down ... except for his mind.</p>\n<p>That lunch quickly led to deep discussions and lots of late nights spent joking around and playing music. But when Andy decided to interview Kohn on tape last summer, Kohn told him another story about himself that caught Andy completely off guard--and made Andy question what it means to be truly familiar with something ... like the sound of your own voice, or that of a friend.</p>\n<p>Neurologist <strong><a href=\"http://epilepsy.med.nyu.edu/orrin-devinsky-md\" target=\"_blank\">Orrin Devinsky</a></strong> joins us to answer some questions raised by Andy and Kohn’s story, and the band <a href=\"http://www.dogsontour.org/contributors/hudsonbranch\" target=\"_blank\">Hudson Branch</a> helps us hear, and feel, the world through Kohn’s ears.</p>\n<p>Hudson Branch is: Matthew and Jacob Boll, Corey and Cobey Bienert, and Enoch Kim. Becky Beighley, Andy Mills, and Kohn Ashmore join them to sing an adaptation of Damien Rice's \"Grey Room.\"</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-10-18T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"159748-loop-loop", "title"=>"Loop the Loop", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11beachey.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=159748", "description"=>"<p>For most of human history, flight was an impossible dream. In this short, the dizzying rise and fall of a pilot whose aeronautic feats changed aviation forever and turned chancy stunts into acrobatic mastery.</p><p>Lincoln Beachey is one of the most famous men you’ve never heard of. Born in 1887 in San Francisco, Beachy was lonely, chubby kid who, as authors <strong><a href=\"http://samkean.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Sam Kean</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"http://www.frankmarrero.com/Beachey/Welcome.html\" target=\"_blank\">Frank Marrero</a></strong> tell us, nobody would have suspected of becoming a hero. But he was fearless. By the age of 10 he was hurling himself down San Francisco’s stomach-churning <a href=\"http://content.cdlib.org/ark:/13030/tf2d5nb2wf/?NAAN=13030&amp;docId=tf2d5nb2wf&amp;&amp;layout=printable-details\" target=\"_blank\">Fillmore Hill</a> on a bicycle with no brakes. </p>\n<p>But what Beachy really wanted to do was fly airplanes. Then one day, while working as a mechanic at an airshow in Los Angeles, he got his big break: a star pilot got hurt, and Beachey leaped in to take his place. He shot upwards, 3,000 feet into the air…and his motor failed. He went into a nose-diving spin that no pilot had ever survived. And he did what no pilot had ever done: he turned into the spin, regained control, and landed safe and sound.</p>\n<p>After that, Beachey became a superstar. At a time when the entire population of the US was 90 million people, 17 million came out to see him fly in just one year. He invented figure 8s and the vertical drop, and was the first pilot to achieve terminal velocity by flying straight toward the ground. In fact, what Beachey did was so extraordinary, and so dangerous, that a wave of pilots died trying to imitate him. After the death of a dear friend of his, Beachey finally vowed to retire. And he did. For three months. Until he finally buckled…and strapped himself back in a cockpit to master the trick of all tricks: the loop the loop. Lincoln perfected it—looping so effortlessly he seemed to own the sky. Until his final flight in 1915, where he stunned the crowds—a quarter of a million fans at the World’s Fair in San Francisco—one last time.</p>\n<p>Related reads:</p>\n<p>Sam Kean's <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0316051632/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>The Disappearing Spoo</em>n</a></span></p>\n<p>Frank Marrero's <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lincoln-Beachey-Man-Who-Owned/dp/0967326532/ref=sr_1_1_twi_pap_3?s=books&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1508418524&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=lincoln+beachey:+the+man+who+owned+the+sky\">Lincoln Beachey: The Man Who Owned the Sky</a></p>\n<p><em>Thanks to the talented jump-ropers at CS 200 in Harlem, and singers at LaGuardia School of the Arts!</em></p>", "tease"=>"Lincoln Beachey is one of the most famous men you&rsquo;ve never heard of.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1316560200000, "publish-at"=>"2011-09-20T19:10:00", "date-line-ts"=>1316559600, "estimated-duration"=>958, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Lincoln Beachey", "name"=>"photologue/photos/Lincoln_Beachey.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.loc.gov/", "name"=>"loc.gov"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/Lincoln_Beachey.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Lincoln Beachey", "credits-url"=>"http://www.loc.gov/pictures/resource/cph.3b03026/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/Lincoln_Beachey.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>59915, "credits-name"=>"Library of Congress, Prints & Photographs Division, LC-USZ62-55137"}, "objectID"=>"159748", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Loop the Loop", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>For most of human history, flight was an impossible dream. In this short, the dizzying rise and fall of a pilot whose aeronautic feats changed aviation forever and turned chancy stunts into acrobatic mastery.</p><p>Lincoln Beachey is one of the most famous men you’ve never heard of. Born in 1887 in San Francisco, Beachy was lonely, chubby kid who, as authors <strong><a href=\"http://samkean.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Sam Kean</a></strong> and <strong><a href=\"http://www.frankmarrero.com/Beachey/Welcome.html\" target=\"_blank\">Frank Marrero</a></strong> tell us, nobody would have suspected of becoming a hero. But he was fearless. By the age of 10 he was hurling himself down San Francisco’s stomach-churning <a href=\"http://content.cdlib.org/ark:/13030/tf2d5nb2wf/?NAAN=13030&amp;docId=tf2d5nb2wf&amp;&amp;layout=printable-details\" target=\"_blank\">Fillmore Hill</a> on a bicycle with no brakes. </p>\n<p>But what Beachy really wanted to do was fly airplanes. Then one day, while working as a mechanic at an airshow in Los Angeles, he got his big break: a star pilot got hurt, and Beachey leaped in to take his place. He shot upwards, 3,000 feet into the air…and his motor failed. He went into a nose-diving spin that no pilot had ever survived. And he did what no pilot had ever done: he turned into the spin, regained control, and landed safe and sound.</p>\n<p>After that, Beachey became a superstar. At a time when the entire population of the US was 90 million people, 17 million came out to see him fly in just one year. He invented figure 8s and the vertical drop, and was the first pilot to achieve terminal velocity by flying straight toward the ground. In fact, what Beachey did was so extraordinary, and so dangerous, that a wave of pilots died trying to imitate him. After the death of a dear friend of his, Beachey finally vowed to retire. And he did. For three months. Until he finally buckled…and strapped himself back in a cockpit to master the trick of all tricks: the loop the loop. Lincoln perfected it—looping so effortlessly he seemed to own the sky. Until his final flight in 1915, where he stunned the crowds—a quarter of a million fans at the World’s Fair in San Francisco—one last time.</p>\n<p>Related reads:</p>\n<p>Sam Kean's <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0316051632/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>The Disappearing Spoo</em>n</a></span></p>\n<p>Frank Marrero's <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lincoln-Beachey-Man-Who-Owned/dp/0967326532/ref=sr_1_1_twi_pap_3?s=books&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1508418524&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=lincoln+beachey:+the+man+who+owned+the+sky\">Lincoln Beachey: The Man Who Owned the Sky</a></p>\n<p><em>Thanks to the talented jump-ropers at CS 200 in Harlem, and singers at LaGuardia School of the Arts!</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-09-20T19:10:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"156900-three-row", "title"=>"Mapping Tic Tac Toe-dom", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11tictac.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=156900", "description"=>"<p>Writer Ian Frazier made a startling discovery several years ago in eastern Siberia: no one he met there had ever heard of tic tac toe. In this short, Jad and Robert wonder how a game that seems carved into childhood DNA could be completely unknown in some parts of the world.</p><p>When Ian Frazier was a kid, he (like most 6-year-olds) mastered the art of tic tac toe. Pretty soon, every match was a draw, and the game lost its magic.</p>\n<p>Then one day, many years later, he met a 6-year-old named Igor who lived deep in Siberia. On a whim, Ian drew a tic tac toe board in his notebook and showed it to Igor...who had no idea what it was. Ian taught him how to play, then joyfully went about clobbering his new opponent.</p>\n<p>Then, Ian started asking around...and it turned out <em>none</em> of the Russians he talked to had heard of tic tac toe. Could it be that huge swaths of the globe just didn't play? Jad and Robert wanted to find out just how widespread this seemingly universal game really is--so they rallied listeners from all over the world to help them do some legwork.</p>\n<p>Ian Frazier's most recent book is <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0312610602/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Travels in Siberia</em>.</a></span></p>", "tease"=>"Writer Ian Frazier made a startling discovery several years ago in eastern Siberia: no one he met there had ever heard of tic tac toe. In this short, Jad and Robert wonder how a game ...", "publishTime"=>1315350000000, "publish-at"=>"2011-09-06T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1315350000, "estimated-duration"=>864, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Tic Tac Toe", "name"=>"photologue/photos/tictactoered.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/tictactoered.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Tic Tac Toe", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/smaedli/4728934121/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/tictactoered.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>58645, "credits-name"=>"smaedli"}, "objectID"=>"156900", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Mapping Tic Tac Toe-dom", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Writer Ian Frazier made a startling discovery several years ago in eastern Siberia: no one he met there had ever heard of tic tac toe. In this short, Jad and Robert wonder how a game that seems carved into childhood DNA could be completely unknown in some parts of the world.</p><p>When Ian Frazier was a kid, he (like most 6-year-olds) mastered the art of tic tac toe. Pretty soon, every match was a draw, and the game lost its magic.</p>\n<p>Then one day, many years later, he met a 6-year-old named Igor who lived deep in Siberia. On a whim, Ian drew a tic tac toe board in his notebook and showed it to Igor...who had no idea what it was. Ian taught him how to play, then joyfully went about clobbering his new opponent.</p>\n<p>Then, Ian started asking around...and it turned out <em>none</em> of the Russians he talked to had heard of tic tac toe. Could it be that huge swaths of the globe just didn't play? Jad and Robert wanted to find out just how widespread this seemingly universal game really is--so they rallied listeners from all over the world to help them do some legwork.</p>\n<p>Ian Frazier's most recent book is <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0312610602/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Travels in Siberia</em>.</a></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-09-06T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"153799-games", "title"=>"Games", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab082311.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=153799", "description"=>"<p>A good game--whether it's a pro football playoff, or a family showdown on the kitchen table--can make you feel, at least for a little while, like your whole life hangs in the balance. This hour of Radiolab, Jad and Robert wonder why we get so invested in something so trivial. What is it about games that make them feel so pivotal?</p><p>We hear how a recurring dream about football turned into a real-life  lesson for Stephen Dubner, we watch a chessboard turn into a playground  where by-the-book moves give way to totally unpredictable possibilities, and we relive a moment where  rooting for the underdog makes us rethink what a truly happy ending is.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>Winners, losers, underdogs -- what can games tell us about who we really are?</p>", "publishTime"=>1314140400000, "publish-at"=>"2011-08-23T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1314072000, "estimated-duration"=>4567, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Basketball hoop", "name"=>"photologue/photos/basketball_hoop.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/384/c/80/photologue/photos/basketball_hoop.jpg", "h"=>384, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Basketball hoop", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/seanj/384418110/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/basketball_hoop.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>57503, "credits-name"=>"*sean"}, "objectID"=>"153799", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Games", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>A good game--whether it's a pro football playoff, or a family showdown on the kitchen table--can make you feel, at least for a little while, like your whole life hangs in the balance. This hour of Radiolab, Jad and Robert wonder why we get so invested in something so trivial. What is it about games that make them feel so pivotal?</p><p>We hear how a recurring dream about football turned into a real-life  lesson for Stephen Dubner, we watch a chessboard turn into a playground  where by-the-book moves give way to totally unpredictable possibilities, and we relive a moment where  rooting for the underdog makes us rethink what a truly happy ending is.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-08-23T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"151639-damn-it-basal-ganglia", "title"=>"Damn It, Basal Ganglia", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11basal.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=151639", "description"=>"<p>The basal ganglia is a core part of the brain, deep inside your skull, that helps control movement. Unless something upsets the chain of command. In this short, Jad and Robert meet a young researcher who was studying what happens when the basal ganglia gets short-circuited in mice...until one fateful day, when things got really, really weird.</p><p>After graduating from college in 2009, <strong>Liza Shoenfeld </strong>got a job as a research associate in a <a href=\"http://keck.ucsf.edu/neurograd/faculty/kreitzer.html\" target=\"_blank\">lab at the University of California, San Francisco</a>. She was just starting her career in neuroscience, and though she was kind of at the bottom of the totem pole, she got to be a part of some really cutting-edge research. As Liza explains, the lab was zeroing in on how the basal ganglia worked by experimenting on mice. And more specifically, her lab figured out a way to essentially switch different parts of the basal ganglia on and off, by shining a special laser into their little mouse brains.</p>\n<p>You can watch a <a href=\"http://www.nature.com/nature/journal/v466/n7306/extref/nature09159-s3.mov\" target=\"_blank\">video of the mice here</a> (it was posted to <em>Nature</em>, along with a <a href=\"http://www.nature.com/nature/journal/v466/n7306/abs/nature09159.html\" target=\"_blank\">paper on basal ganglia pathways</a>).</p>\n<p>So, armed with her lab skills and an interest in the basal ganglia, Liza started applying to grad schools where she could turn her experience with the mice into research questions of her own.</p>\n<p>And this is where things get strange. When Liza got to her final round of interviews, everything got turned on its head. And Liza got much closer to her subject then she'd ever intended.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"The basal ganglia is a core part of the brain, deep inside your skull, that helps control movement. Unless something upsets the chain of command. In this short, Jad and Robert meet a ...", "publishTime"=>1312933800000, "publish-at"=>"2011-08-09T19:50:00", "date-line-ts"=>1312933800, "estimated-duration"=>746, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Shadow puppet", "name"=>"photologue/photos/shadow_puppet.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/384/c/80/photologue/photos/shadow_puppet.jpg", "h"=>384, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Shadow puppet", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/antara365/823844897/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/shadow_puppet.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>56916, "credits-name"=>"Only Sequel"}, "objectID"=>"151639", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Damn It, Basal Ganglia", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>The basal ganglia is a core part of the brain, deep inside your skull, that helps control movement. Unless something upsets the chain of command. In this short, Jad and Robert meet a young researcher who was studying what happens when the basal ganglia gets short-circuited in mice...until one fateful day, when things got really, really weird.</p><p>After graduating from college in 2009, <strong>Liza Shoenfeld </strong>got a job as a research associate in a <a href=\"http://keck.ucsf.edu/neurograd/faculty/kreitzer.html\" target=\"_blank\">lab at the University of California, San Francisco</a>. She was just starting her career in neuroscience, and though she was kind of at the bottom of the totem pole, she got to be a part of some really cutting-edge research. As Liza explains, the lab was zeroing in on how the basal ganglia worked by experimenting on mice. And more specifically, her lab figured out a way to essentially switch different parts of the basal ganglia on and off, by shining a special laser into their little mouse brains.</p>\n<p>You can watch a <a href=\"http://www.nature.com/nature/journal/v466/n7306/extref/nature09159-s3.mov\" target=\"_blank\">video of the mice here</a> (it was posted to <em>Nature</em>, along with a <a href=\"http://www.nature.com/nature/journal/v466/n7306/abs/nature09159.html\" target=\"_blank\">paper on basal ganglia pathways</a>).</p>\n<p>So, armed with her lab skills and an interest in the basal ganglia, Liza started applying to grad schools where she could turn her experience with the mice into research questions of her own.</p>\n<p>And this is where things get strange. When Liza got to her final round of interviews, everything got turned on its head. And Liza got much closer to her subject then she'd ever intended.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-08-09T19:50:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"148670-4-track-mind", "title"=>"A 4-Track Mind", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11milne.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=148670", "description"=>"<p>In this short, a neurologist issues a dare to a ragtime piano player and a famous conductor. When the two men face off in an fMRI machine, the challenge is so unimaginably difficult that one man instantly gives up. But the other achieves a musical feat that ought to be impossible.</p><p>Reporter<strong> Jessica Benko </strong>went to Michigan to visit <strong>Bob Milne</strong>, one of the best ragtime piano players in the world, and a preternaturally talented musician. Usually, Bob sticks to playing piano for small groups of ragtime enthusiasts, but he recently caught the attention of Penn State neuroscientist <strong>Kerstin Betterman</strong>, who had heard that Bob had a rare talent: He can play technically challenging pieces of music on demand while carrying on a conversation and cracking jokes. According to Kerstin, our brains just aren't wired for that. So she decided to investigate Bob's brain, and along the way she discovered that Bob has an even more amazing ability ... one that we could hardly believe and science can't explain.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>" In this short, a neurologist issues a dare to a ragtime piano player and a famous conductor. One of them achieves a musical feat that ought to be impossible.", "publishTime"=>1311721200000, "publish-at"=>"2011-07-26T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1311721200, "estimated-duration"=>1165, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Old piano keys", "name"=>"photologue/photos/old_piano_keys.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/2.0/deed.en", "name"=>"CC-BY-NC-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/383/c/80/photologue/photos/old_piano_keys.jpg", "h"=>383, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Old piano keys", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/umami88/5594794334/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/old_piano_keys.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>56208, "credits-name"=>"u m a m i"}, "objectID"=>"148670", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"A 4-Track Mind", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this short, a neurologist issues a dare to a ragtime piano player and a famous conductor. When the two men face off in an fMRI machine, the challenge is so unimaginably difficult that one man instantly gives up. But the other achieves a musical feat that ought to be impossible.</p><p>Reporter<strong> Jessica Benko </strong>went to Michigan to visit <strong>Bob Milne</strong>, one of the best ragtime piano players in the world, and a preternaturally talented musician. Usually, Bob sticks to playing piano for small groups of ragtime enthusiasts, but he recently caught the attention of Penn State neuroscientist <strong>Kerstin Betterman</strong>, who had heard that Bob had a rare talent: He can play technically challenging pieces of music on demand while carrying on a conversation and cracking jokes. According to Kerstin, our brains just aren't wired for that. So she decided to investigate Bob's brain, and along the way she discovered that Bob has an even more amazing ability ... one that we could hardly believe and science can't explain.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-07-26T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"145638-rebroadcast-detective-stories", "title"=>"REBROADCAST: Detective Stories", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab071111.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=145638", "description"=>"<p>We're celebrating summer with a classic episode of Radiolab--full of mystery, intrigue...and a goat standing on a cow. We haven't actually tried listening to it around a campfire, but we're betting it would totally work. See you in two weeks with a new short!</p><p>In the meantime, we go sleuthing to dig up the past in some very unusual places: an   ancient trash dump in Egypt, the side of the highway in California, and   in the blood of 16 million men in Central Asia. Read more (from guest names to listener comments) on the <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2007/sep/10/\">Detective Stories episode page. </a></p>", "tease"=>"We're celebrating summer with a classic episode of Radiolab--full of mystery, intrigue...and a goat standing on a cow. We haven't actually tried listening to it around a campfire, but...", "publishTime"=>1310425200000, "publish-at"=>"2011-07-11T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1310425200, "estimated-duration"=>3517, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Man ducking into phone booth", "name"=>"photologue/photos/detective_stories.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/detective_stories.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Man ducking into phone booth", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/53959560@N00/4305570683/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/detective_stories.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>67114, "credits-name"=>"Pure'n'Simple"}, "objectID"=>"145638", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"REBROADCAST: Detective Stories", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>We're celebrating summer with a classic episode of Radiolab--full of mystery, intrigue...and a goat standing on a cow. We haven't actually tried listening to it around a campfire, but we're betting it would totally work. See you in two weeks with a new short!</p><p>In the meantime, we go sleuthing to dig up the past in some very unusual places: an   ancient trash dump in Egypt, the side of the highway in California, and   in the blood of 16 million men in Central Asia. Read more (from guest names to listener comments) on the <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2007/sep/10/\">Detective Stories episode page. </a></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-07-11T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"143084-curious-sounds", "title"=>"Curious Sounds: A Radiolab Concert", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11curiousjune.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=143084", "description"=>"<p>In this short, Jad presents the electrifying sounds of three mind-bending musical acts: Brooklyn duo Buke &amp; Gass, drummer Glenn Kotche of Wilco, and the one-and-only Reggie Watts. Their performances were recorded live at our Curious Sounds concert earlier this month in NYC.</p><p>Radiolab is a deeply musical show--from Jad's scoring and sound design, to the way we tell stories. So we decided to devote a night to three musical acts who all share a sense of musical adventure and curiosity--and the ability to rock the rafters of any stage they step on.</p>\n<p>Genre-bending duo <a href=\"http://www.bukeandgass.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Buke &amp; Gass</strong></a> kick the podcast off with a live performance of “Your Face Left Before You”...a raucous song featuring Arone Dyer's fierce vocals, bass ukulele, and \"toe-bourine\" (check out the picture below), and Aron Sanchez's driving, homemade guitar-bass. </p>\n<p>Up next, percussionist<strong> </strong><a href=\"http://glennkotche.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Glenn Kotche</strong></a> of Wilco performs “Monkey Chant,” his retelling of the ancient Hindu epic the Ramayana--using different instruments in his drum kit to convey different characters. And after explaining how he once wrote a string quartet on the drums, he plays<strong> </strong>one more composition: “Projections of What Might.”</p>\n<p>And finally, <a href=\"http://www.reggiewatts.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Reggie Watts</strong></a>...musician, comedian, stand-up-surrealist-beat-boxer-force-of-nature...takes command of the stage.</p>\n<h3>More from Buke &amp; Gass, Glenn Kotche, and Reggie Watts:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Buke &amp; Gass's latest album <a href=\"http://click.linksynergy.com/fs-bin/click?id=EVE6a7Rvokc&amp;offerid=146261.390316878&amp;type=2&amp;subid=0\">Riposte</a><img src=\"https://ad.linksynergy.com/fs-bin/show?id=EVE6a7Rvokc&amp;bids=146261.390316878&amp;type=2&amp;subid=0\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"> on iTunes</li>\n<li>Glenn's album <a href=\"http://click.linksynergy.com/fs-bin/click?id=EVE6a7Rvokc&amp;offerid=146261.127969528&amp;type=2&amp;subid=0\">Mobile</a><img src=\"https://ad.linksynergy.com/fs-bin/show?id=EVE6a7Rvokc&amp;bids=146261.127969528&amp;type=2&amp;subid=0\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"> on iTunes</li>\n<li>Reggie's first solo album, <a href=\"http://click.linksynergy.com/fs-bin/click?id=EVE6a7Rvokc&amp;offerid=146261.4214715&amp;type=2&amp;subid=0\">Simplified</a><img src=\"https://ad.linksynergy.com/fs-bin/show?id=EVE6a7Rvokc&amp;bids=146261.4214715&amp;type=2&amp;subid=0\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\">, on iTunes</li>\n<li>Reggie's latest album, <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B003MCRSXA/ref=as_li_tf_tl?ie=UTF8&amp;tag=radiolab-20&amp;linkCode=as2&amp;camp=217145&amp;creative=399373&amp;creativeASIN=B003MCRSXA\"> Why S*** So Crazy? [Deluxe Edition] [Explicit] [+video]</a>, on Amazon</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Free downloads:</h3>\n<p><div class=\"inline_audioplayer_wrapper inline_audioplayer_embedded_wrapper\"><h3>Buke & Gass, Your Face Left Before You</h3><iframe frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" height=\"54\" width=\"100%\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/widgets/ondemand_player/wnyc/#file=http://audio.wnyc.org/rl_extras/rl_extras060111bukegass.mp3&share=1\"></iframe></div></p>\n<p><div class=\"inline_audioplayer_wrapper inline_audioplayer_embedded_wrapper\"><h3>Reggie Watts, Every Day is Like Sunday</h3><iframe frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" height=\"54\" width=\"100%\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/widgets/ondemand_player/wnyc/#file=http://audio.wnyc.org/rl_extras/rl_extras062211reggiewatts.mp3&share=1\"></iframe></div></p>", "tease"=>"In this short, Jad presents the electrifying sounds of three mind-bending musical acts: Brooklyn duo Buke & Gass, drummer Glenn Kotche of Wilco, and the one-and-only Reggie Watts. The...", "publishTime"=>1309222800000, "publish-at"=>"2011-06-27T21:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1309222800, "estimated-duration"=>2774, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Group hug: Jad Abumrad, Buke & Gass, Glenn Kotche, and Reggie Watts", "name"=>"photologue/photos/group_hug_live_radiolab_.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/384/c/80/photologue/photos/group_hug_live_radiolab_.jpg", "h"=>384, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Group hug: Jad Abumrad, Buke & Gass, Glenn Kotche, and Reggie Watts", "credits-url"=>"http://www.de-vices.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/group_hug_live_radiolab_.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>54645, "credits-name"=>"Jeff Tan"}, "objectID"=>"143084", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Curious Sounds: A Radiolab Concert", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this short, Jad presents the electrifying sounds of three mind-bending musical acts: Brooklyn duo Buke &amp; Gass, drummer Glenn Kotche of Wilco, and the one-and-only Reggie Watts. Their performances were recorded live at our Curious Sounds concert earlier this month in NYC.</p><p>Radiolab is a deeply musical show--from Jad's scoring and sound design, to the way we tell stories. So we decided to devote a night to three musical acts who all share a sense of musical adventure and curiosity--and the ability to rock the rafters of any stage they step on.</p>\n<p>Genre-bending duo <a href=\"http://www.bukeandgass.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Buke &amp; Gass</strong></a> kick the podcast off with a live performance of “Your Face Left Before You”...a raucous song featuring Arone Dyer's fierce vocals, bass ukulele, and \"toe-bourine\" (check out the picture below), and Aron Sanchez's driving, homemade guitar-bass. </p>\n<p>Up next, percussionist<strong> </strong><a href=\"http://glennkotche.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Glenn Kotche</strong></a> of Wilco performs “Monkey Chant,” his retelling of the ancient Hindu epic the Ramayana--using different instruments in his drum kit to convey different characters. And after explaining how he once wrote a string quartet on the drums, he plays<strong> </strong>one more composition: “Projections of What Might.”</p>\n<p>And finally, <a href=\"http://www.reggiewatts.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Reggie Watts</strong></a>...musician, comedian, stand-up-surrealist-beat-boxer-force-of-nature...takes command of the stage.</p>\n<h3>More from Buke &amp; Gass, Glenn Kotche, and Reggie Watts:</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Buke &amp; Gass's latest album <a href=\"http://click.linksynergy.com/fs-bin/click?id=EVE6a7Rvokc&amp;offerid=146261.390316878&amp;type=2&amp;subid=0\">Riposte</a><img src=\"https://ad.linksynergy.com/fs-bin/show?id=EVE6a7Rvokc&amp;bids=146261.390316878&amp;type=2&amp;subid=0\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"> on iTunes</li>\n<li>Glenn's album <a href=\"http://click.linksynergy.com/fs-bin/click?id=EVE6a7Rvokc&amp;offerid=146261.127969528&amp;type=2&amp;subid=0\">Mobile</a><img src=\"https://ad.linksynergy.com/fs-bin/show?id=EVE6a7Rvokc&amp;bids=146261.127969528&amp;type=2&amp;subid=0\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\"> on iTunes</li>\n<li>Reggie's first solo album, <a href=\"http://click.linksynergy.com/fs-bin/click?id=EVE6a7Rvokc&amp;offerid=146261.4214715&amp;type=2&amp;subid=0\">Simplified</a><img src=\"https://ad.linksynergy.com/fs-bin/show?id=EVE6a7Rvokc&amp;bids=146261.4214715&amp;type=2&amp;subid=0\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\">, on iTunes</li>\n<li>Reggie's latest album, <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B003MCRSXA/ref=as_li_tf_tl?ie=UTF8&amp;tag=radiolab-20&amp;linkCode=as2&amp;camp=217145&amp;creative=399373&amp;creativeASIN=B003MCRSXA\"> Why S*** So Crazy? [Deluxe Edition] [Explicit] [+video]</a>, on Amazon</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Free downloads:</h3>\n<p><div class=\"inline_audioplayer_wrapper inline_audioplayer_embedded_wrapper\"><h3>Buke & Gass, Your Face Left Before You</h3><iframe frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" height=\"54\" width=\"100%\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/widgets/ondemand_player/wnyc/#file=http://audio.wnyc.org/rl_extras/rl_extras060111bukegass.mp3&share=1\"></iframe></div></p>\n<p><div class=\"inline_audioplayer_wrapper inline_audioplayer_embedded_wrapper\"><h3>Reggie Watts, Every Day is Like Sunday</h3><iframe frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" height=\"54\" width=\"100%\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/widgets/ondemand_player/wnyc/#file=http://audio.wnyc.org/rl_extras/rl_extras062211reggiewatts.mp3&share=1\"></iframe></div></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-06-27T21:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"137407-talking-to-machines", "title"=>"Talking to Machines", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab053111.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=137407", "description"=>"<p>This hour of Radiolab, Jad and Robert meet humans and robots who are trying to connect, and blur the line.</p><p>We begin with a love story--from a man who unwittingly fell in love with a chatbot on an online dating site. Then, we encounter a robot therapist whose inventor became so unnerved by its success that he pulled the plug. And we talk to the man who coded Cleverbot, a software program that learns from every new line of conversation it receives...and that's chatting with more than 3 million humans each month. Then, five intrepid kids help us test a hypothesis about a toy designed to push our buttons, and play on our human empathy. And we meet a robot built to be so sentient that its creators hope it will one day have a consciousness, and a life, all its own. </p>", "tease"=>"<p>What can machines tell us about being human?</p>", "publishTime"=>1306913426000, "publish-at"=>"2011-06-01T03:30:26", "date-line-ts"=>1306814400, "estimated-duration"=>3834, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Wire head", "name"=>"photologue/photos/wire_head.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/384/c/80/photologue/photos/wire_head.jpg", "h"=>384, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Wire head", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/paulmoody/314897848/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/wire_head.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>52868, "credits-name"=>"paul+photos=moody"}, "objectID"=>"137407", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Talking to Machines", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This hour of Radiolab, Jad and Robert meet humans and robots who are trying to connect, and blur the line.</p><p>We begin with a love story--from a man who unwittingly fell in love with a chatbot on an online dating site. Then, we encounter a robot therapist whose inventor became so unnerved by its success that he pulled the plug. And we talk to the man who coded Cleverbot, a software program that learns from every new line of conversation it receives...and that's chatting with more than 3 million humans each month. Then, five intrepid kids help us test a hypothesis about a toy designed to push our buttons, and play on our human empathy. And we meet a robot built to be so sentient that its creators hope it will one day have a consciousness, and a life, all its own. </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-06-01T03:30:26", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"135401-dogs-gone-wild", "title"=>"Dogs Gone Wild", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11charliewild.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=135401", "description"=>"<p>In this short, a family dog disappears into the woods...and the mystery of what happened to him raises a big question about what it means to be wild.</p><p>In our <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2009/oct/19/new-nice/\" target=\"_blank\">New Normal</a> episode, we talked to evolutionary biologist <a href=\"http://www.dibs.duke.edu/research/profiles/88-brian-hare\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Brian Hare</strong></a> about what happens to animals when they get domesticated. In this podcast, we turn that question around and wonder about the remnants of wildness in our household pets.</p>\n<p>When <strong>Lulu Miller </strong>first heard the call of coyotes as a teenager at her family’s cabin in Cape Cod, she loved the sound—it was a thrilling taste of a world that hadn’t been tamed. But one night, she and her family came back to the cabin to find that their much loved, and very domesticated, terrier Charlie was missing. When they called him, they heard a loud yelp from the forest, followed by a chorus of howls ... and never saw Charlie again.</p>\n<p>Lulu and our producer <strong>Soren Wheeler</strong> talk to Brian Hare about what he thinks might've happened to Charlie and ask him whether a domestic animal can ever really return to the wild. To explain, he tells them the strange tale of the New Guinea Singing Dogs. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"In this short, a family dog disappears into the woods...and the mystery of what happened to him raises a big question about what it means to be wild.", "publishTime"=>1305682380000, "publish-at"=>"2011-05-17T21:33:00", "date-line-ts"=>1305680400, "estimated-duration"=>1138, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Howling statue", "name"=>"photologue/photos/howlstatue_.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/raw/photologue/photos/howlstatue_.jpg", "h"=>0, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Howling statue", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/pguyton/4476145247/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/howlstatue_.jpg", "w"=>0, "id"=>52243, "credits-name"=>"pjguyton2002"}, "objectID"=>"135401", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dogs Gone Wild", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this short, a family dog disappears into the woods...and the mystery of what happened to him raises a big question about what it means to be wild.</p><p>In our <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2009/oct/19/new-nice/\" target=\"_blank\">New Normal</a> episode, we talked to evolutionary biologist <a href=\"http://www.dibs.duke.edu/research/profiles/88-brian-hare\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Brian Hare</strong></a> about what happens to animals when they get domesticated. In this podcast, we turn that question around and wonder about the remnants of wildness in our household pets.</p>\n<p>When <strong>Lulu Miller </strong>first heard the call of coyotes as a teenager at her family’s cabin in Cape Cod, she loved the sound—it was a thrilling taste of a world that hadn’t been tamed. But one night, she and her family came back to the cabin to find that their much loved, and very domesticated, terrier Charlie was missing. When they called him, they heard a loud yelp from the forest, followed by a chorus of howls ... and never saw Charlie again.</p>\n<p>Lulu and our producer <strong>Soren Wheeler</strong> talk to Brian Hare about what he thinks might've happened to Charlie and ask him whether a domestic animal can ever really return to the wild. To explain, he tells them the strange tale of the New Guinea Singing Dogs. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-05-17T21:33:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"127732-cosmic-habituation", "title"=>"Cosmic Habituation", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11schooler.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=127732", "description"=>"<p>In this short, Jonathan Schooler tells us about a discovery that launched his career and led to a puzzle that has haunted him ever since.</p><p>In the late 1980s, when Jonathan Schooler was a graduate student in psychology, he did a little study that became a big deal. Schooler asked a group of people to watch a video of a man robbing a bank. After watching the video, he had half of them jot down a description of the robber. And, wait for it ... turns out that the people who took notes were significantly LESS likely to recognize the robber later.</p>\n<p>Not surprisingly, this weird effect -- called \"verbal overshadowing\" -- got a lot of attention. (In fact, we talked with Malcolm Gladwell about verbal overshadowing in our show about <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2008/nov/17/\" target=\"_blank\">Choice</a>). But just as Schooler's big discovery was making it into newspapers and Psych 101 textbooks…something started happening to his data. Each time he repeated the study, the exact same study, his once attention-grabbing effect got smaller and smaller.</p>\n<p>In this podcast, Schooler tells Jad and Robert about his journey to figure out what had happened to him, and why it was happening to other scientists too. After considering all the reasonable explanations (statistical quirks and procedural stumbles), Schooler found himself thinking that maybe, just maybe, the laws of nature are less solid than they seem.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this short incorrectly stated that Jonathan Schooler saw the effect size of his study fall by 30% on two different occasions. In fact, he saw it fall by that amount the first time he repeated the study and saw a general downward trend thereafter. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this short incorrectly attributed a statement to Jonathan Schooler’s advisor. The statement was actually made by his colleague. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>", "tease"=>"In this short, Jonathan Schooler tells us about a discovery that launched his career and led to a puzzle that has haunted him ever since.", "publishTime"=>1304464800000, "publish-at"=>"2011-05-03T19:20:00", "date-line-ts"=>1304464800, "estimated-duration"=>956, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Magnifying glass", "name"=>"photologue/photos/magnifyingglass_.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/564/c/80/photologue/photos/magnifyingglass_.jpg", "h"=>564, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Magnifying glass", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/26341587@N04/4280203413/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/magnifyingglass_.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>49706, "credits-name"=>"Jazzyblue TR"}, "objectID"=>"127732", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Cosmic Habituation", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this short, Jonathan Schooler tells us about a discovery that launched his career and led to a puzzle that has haunted him ever since.</p><p>In the late 1980s, when Jonathan Schooler was a graduate student in psychology, he did a little study that became a big deal. Schooler asked a group of people to watch a video of a man robbing a bank. After watching the video, he had half of them jot down a description of the robber. And, wait for it ... turns out that the people who took notes were significantly LESS likely to recognize the robber later.</p>\n<p>Not surprisingly, this weird effect -- called \"verbal overshadowing\" -- got a lot of attention. (In fact, we talked with Malcolm Gladwell about verbal overshadowing in our show about <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2008/nov/17/\" target=\"_blank\">Choice</a>). But just as Schooler's big discovery was making it into newspapers and Psych 101 textbooks…something started happening to his data. Each time he repeated the study, the exact same study, his once attention-grabbing effect got smaller and smaller.</p>\n<p>In this podcast, Schooler tells Jad and Robert about his journey to figure out what had happened to him, and why it was happening to other scientists too. After considering all the reasonable explanations (statistical quirks and procedural stumbles), Schooler found himself thinking that maybe, just maybe, the laws of nature are less solid than they seem.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this short incorrectly stated that Jonathan Schooler saw the effect size of his study fall by 30% on two different occasions. In fact, he saw it fall by that amount the first time he repeated the study and saw a general downward trend thereafter. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this short incorrectly attributed a statement to Jonathan Schooler’s advisor. The statement was actually made by his colleague. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-05-03T19:20:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"124920-radiolab-video-symmetry", "title"=>"Radiolab Video: Symmetry", "audio"=>nil, "description"=>"<p>Is the world full of deep symmetries and ordered pairs? Or do we live in a lopsided universe? This striking video by <a href=\"http://www.everynone.com/\">Everynone</a> plays with our yearning for balance, and reveals how beautiful imperfect matches can be. The video was inspired by our episode <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/2011/apr/18/\">Desperately Seeking Symmetry</a>.</p><p>If you enjoy Symmetry, check out our other Radiolab videos made in collaboration with Everynone (filmmakers Will Hoffman, Daniel Mercadante, and Julius Metoyer III): <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blog/2010/aug/09/bonus-video-words/\">Words</a>, <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blog/2009/aug/14/16-moments/\">Moments</a>, and <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blog/2009/jan/13/parabolas-etc/\">Parabolas (etc.)</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"Is the world full of deep symmetries and ordered pairs? Or do we live in a lopsided universe? This striking video by Everynone plays with our yearning for balance, and reveals how bea...", "publishTime"=>1303172607000, "publish-at"=>"2011-04-18T20:23:27", "date-line-ts"=>1303172481, "estimated-duration"=>0, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Symmetry, A Radiolab Video Made by Everynone", "name"=>"photologue/photos/symmetryvidposter.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/360/c/80/photologue/photos/symmetryvidposter.jpg", "h"=>360, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Symmetry, A Radiolab Video Made by Everynone", "credits-url"=>"http://everynone.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/symmetryvidposter.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>48867, "credits-name"=>"Everynone"}, "objectID"=>"124920", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Video: Symmetry", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Is the world full of deep symmetries and ordered pairs? Or do we live in a lopsided universe? This striking video by <a href=\"http://www.everynone.com/\">Everynone</a> plays with our yearning for balance, and reveals how beautiful imperfect matches can be. The video was inspired by our episode <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/2011/apr/18/\">Desperately Seeking Symmetry</a>.</p><p>If you enjoy Symmetry, check out our other Radiolab videos made in collaboration with Everynone (filmmakers Will Hoffman, Daniel Mercadante, and Julius Metoyer III): <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blog/2010/aug/09/bonus-video-words/\">Words</a>, <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blog/2009/aug/14/16-moments/\">Moments</a>, and <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blog/2009/jan/13/parabolas-etc/\">Parabolas (etc.)</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-04-18T20:23:27", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"122382-desperately-seeking-symmetry", "title"=>"Desperately Seeking Symmetry", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab041811.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=122382", "description"=>"<p>This hour of Radiolab, Jad and Robert set out in search of order and  balance in the world around us, and ask how symmetry shapes our very  existence -- from the origins of the universe, to what we see when we  look in the mirror.</p><p>Along the way, we look for love in ancient Greece, head to modern-day Princeton to peer inside our brains, and turn up an unlikely headline from the Oval Office circa 1979.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>From hair parts to the origin of the universe, how symmetry shapes our existence.</p>", "publishTime"=>1303167735000, "publish-at"=>"2011-04-18T19:02:15", "date-line-ts"=>1303099200, "estimated-duration"=>3410, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Jad Abumrad and Robert Krulwich on stage", "name"=>"photologue/photos/JadRobertSymmetry.JPG", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/600/400/c/80/photologue/photos/JadRobertSymmetry.JPG", "h"=>400, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Jad Abumrad and Robert Krulwich on stage", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/JadRobertSymmetry.JPG", "w"=>600, "id"=>48061, "credits-name"=>"Steve Silberman"}, "objectID"=>"122382", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Desperately Seeking Symmetry", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This hour of Radiolab, Jad and Robert set out in search of order and  balance in the world around us, and ask how symmetry shapes our very  existence -- from the origins of the universe, to what we see when we  look in the mirror.</p><p>Along the way, we look for love in ancient Greece, head to modern-day Princeton to peer inside our brains, and turn up an unlikely headline from the Oval Office circa 1979.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-04-18T19:02:15", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"119040-pass-science", "title"=>"Pass the Science", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11holmes.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=119040", "description"=>"<p>Richard Holmes went to Cambridge University intending to study the lives of poets. Until a dueling mathematician, and a dinner conversation composed entirely of gestures, changed his mind.</p><p>In this short, Robert asks Richard how he came to write <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Age-Wonder-Romantic-Generation-Discovery/dp/1400031877/ref=ntt_at_ep_dpt_1\" target=\"_blank\"><em>The Age of Wonder</em></a>, a rollicking book full of adventure and discovery about the rise of modern science in the late 1700s and early 1800s. Richard tells us the poignant story of mathematician Évariste Galois--and how dropping his name at the High Table at Cambridge University led to a wordless demonstration of cubic equations by a cutlery-wielding Russian mathematician (who spoke no English). In the end, Richard was so taken with the lengths scientists will go to in order to explain their work (even when they fail), that he decided to give it a go himself. We get that.</p>", "tease"=>"Richard Holmes went to Cambridge University intending to study the lives of poets. Until a dueling mathematician, and a dinner conversation composed entirely of gestures, changed his ...", "publishTime"=>1300834800000, "publish-at"=>"2011-03-22T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1300834800, "estimated-duration"=>856, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Place setting", "name"=>"photologue/photos/pass_the_science.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/480/640/c/80/photologue/photos/pass_the_science.jpg", "h"=>640, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Place setting", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/jenny-pics/3948548508/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/pass_the_science.jpg", "w"=>480, "id"=>65997, "credits-name"=>"jenny downing"}, "objectID"=>"119040", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Pass the Science", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Richard Holmes went to Cambridge University intending to study the lives of poets. Until a dueling mathematician, and a dinner conversation composed entirely of gestures, changed his mind.</p><p>In this short, Robert asks Richard how he came to write <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Age-Wonder-Romantic-Generation-Discovery/dp/1400031877/ref=ntt_at_ep_dpt_1\" target=\"_blank\"><em>The Age of Wonder</em></a>, a rollicking book full of adventure and discovery about the rise of modern science in the late 1700s and early 1800s. Richard tells us the poignant story of mathematician Évariste Galois--and how dropping his name at the High Table at Cambridge University led to a wordless demonstration of cubic equations by a cutlery-wielding Russian mathematician (who spoke no English). In the end, Richard was so taken with the lengths scientists will go to in order to explain their work (even when they fail), that he decided to give it a go himself. We get that.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-03-22T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"117165-help", "title"=>"Help!", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab030811.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=117165", "description"=>"<p>What do you do when your own worst enemy is...you? This hour, Radiolab looks for ways to gain the upper hand over those forces inside us--from unhealthy urges, to creative insights--that seem to have a mind of their own.</p><p>We meet a Cold War negotiator who, in order to quit smoking, backs himself into a tactical corner, and we visit a clinic in Russia where patients turn to a radical treatment to help fight their demons. Plus, Elizabeth Gilbert lays out strategies for doing battle with your muse.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>What do you do when your own worst enemy is...you?</p>", "publishTime"=>1299628800000, "publish-at"=>"2011-03-08T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1299560400, "estimated-duration"=>3282, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Greek Silver Dekadrachm of Athens (Attica)", "name"=>"photologue/photos/help.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/309/c/80/photologue/photos/help.jpg", "h"=>309, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Greek Silver Dekadrachm of Athens (Attica)", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/antiquitiesproject/6407587089/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/help.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>65995, "credits-name"=>"Ancient Art"}, "objectID"=>"117165", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Help!", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>What do you do when your own worst enemy is...you? This hour, Radiolab looks for ways to gain the upper hand over those forces inside us--from unhealthy urges, to creative insights--that seem to have a mind of their own.</p><p>We meet a Cold War negotiator who, in order to quit smoking, backs himself into a tactical corner, and we visit a clinic in Russia where patients turn to a radical treatment to help fight their demons. Plus, Elizabeth Gilbert lays out strategies for doing battle with your muse.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-03-08T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"115544-flock-two", "title"=>"A Flock of Two", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11eggers.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=115544", "description"=>"<p>In today's short, we get to know a man who struggles, and mostly fails, to contain his violent  outbursts...until he meets a bird who can keep him in check.</p><p>Animals rescue people all the time, but not like this. Jim Eggers is a 44-year-old man who suffers from a problem that not only puts his life at risk--it jeopardizes the safety of everybody around him. But with the help of Sadie, his pet African Grey Parrot, Jim found an unlikely (and seemingly successful) way to manage his anger. African Grey Parrot expert Irene Pepperberg helps us understand how this could work, and shares some insights from her work with a parrot named Alex.</p>\n<p>And one quick note from our producer <strong>Pat Walters</strong>: Jim considers Sadie to be a “service animal,” a designation under the Americans with Disabilities Act that protects the rights of individuals with disabilities to bring certain animals into public places. The federal government recently redefined the term service animal to include only dogs and miniature horses. And while Jim disagrees with the change, he says he hasn’t run into any problems yet—in fact, the local bus company has already told him they’ll make an exception for Sadie. </p>", "tease"=>"In today's short, we get to know a man who struggles, and mostly fails, to contain his violent  outbursts...until he meets a bird who can keep him in check.", "publishTime"=>1298419200000, "publish-at"=>"2011-02-22T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1298419200, "estimated-duration"=>1039, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Jim Eggers and his parrot Sadie", "name"=>"photologue/photos/jim&sadie.JPG", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"WNYC"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/388/263/c/80/photologue/photos/jim&sadie.JPG", "h"=>263, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Jim Eggers and his parrot Sadie", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/jim&sadie.JPG", "w"=>388, "id"=>45491, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"115544", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"A Flock of Two", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In today's short, we get to know a man who struggles, and mostly fails, to contain his violent  outbursts...until he meets a bird who can keep him in check.</p><p>Animals rescue people all the time, but not like this. Jim Eggers is a 44-year-old man who suffers from a problem that not only puts his life at risk--it jeopardizes the safety of everybody around him. But with the help of Sadie, his pet African Grey Parrot, Jim found an unlikely (and seemingly successful) way to manage his anger. African Grey Parrot expert Irene Pepperberg helps us understand how this could work, and shares some insights from her work with a parrot named Alex.</p>\n<p>And one quick note from our producer <strong>Pat Walters</strong>: Jim considers Sadie to be a “service animal,” a designation under the Americans with Disabilities Act that protects the rights of individuals with disabilities to bring certain animals into public places. The federal government recently redefined the term service animal to include only dogs and miniature horses. And while Jim disagrees with the change, he says he hasn’t run into any problems yet—in fact, the local bus company has already told him they’ll make an exception for Sadie. </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-02-22T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"113579-radiolab-presents-loneliness-goalkeeper", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: The Loneliness of the Goalkeeper", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11goalkeeper.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=113579", "description"=>"<p>This week on the podcast, football! No, it's not a Super Bowl recap. Jad and Robert present a piece from across the pond--a piece about soccer they fell in love with when they heard it at the Third Coast festival in Chicago.</p><p>Back in October, Jad and Robert hosted the awards ceremony at the <a href=\"http://www.thirdcoastfestival.org/happenings/conference\">Third Coast International Audio Festival.</a> And one piece, well...kinda blew their minds. Partly because it's beautiful (it won one of the big awards), and partly because it has a lot to say about symmetry--a topic we'll spend a full hour on in an upcoming episode. (By the way: Jad and Robert will be performing the symmetry show live in New York, Los Angeles, and Seattle in March, <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blog/2011/feb/07/get-tickets-radiolab-live-symmetry/\" target=\"_blank\">get more info and tickets here</a>!)</p>\n<p>So, consider this an appetizer for the symmetry shmorgishborg to come. \"The Loneliness of the Goalkeeper,\" presented by writer, broadcaster, and former goalkeeper Hardeep Singh Kohli, and produced by Adam Fowler, is a Ladbroke Radio production, and was originally broadcast on BBC Radio 4. Enjoy!</p>", "tease"=>"This week on the podcast, football! No, it's not a Super Bowl recap. Jad and Robert present a piece from across the pond--a piece about soccer they fell in love with when they heard i...", "publishTime"=>1297209600000, "publish-at"=>"2011-02-08T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1297209600, "estimated-duration"=>1272, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Goalie", "name"=>"photologue/photos/goalie.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/428/c/80/photologue/photos/goalie.jpg", "h"=>428, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Goalie", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/landerholm/4982331985/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/goalie.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>44549, "credits-name"=>"k.landerholm"}, "objectID"=>"113579", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: The Loneliness of the Goalkeeper", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This week on the podcast, football! No, it's not a Super Bowl recap. Jad and Robert present a piece from across the pond--a piece about soccer they fell in love with when they heard it at the Third Coast festival in Chicago.</p><p>Back in October, Jad and Robert hosted the awards ceremony at the <a href=\"http://www.thirdcoastfestival.org/happenings/conference\">Third Coast International Audio Festival.</a> And one piece, well...kinda blew their minds. Partly because it's beautiful (it won one of the big awards), and partly because it has a lot to say about symmetry--a topic we'll spend a full hour on in an upcoming episode. (By the way: Jad and Robert will be performing the symmetry show live in New York, Los Angeles, and Seattle in March, <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blog/2011/feb/07/get-tickets-radiolab-live-symmetry/\" target=\"_blank\">get more info and tickets here</a>!)</p>\n<p>So, consider this an appetizer for the symmetry shmorgishborg to come. \"The Loneliness of the Goalkeeper,\" presented by writer, broadcaster, and former goalkeeper Hardeep Singh Kohli, and produced by Adam Fowler, is a Ladbroke Radio production, and was originally broadcast on BBC Radio 4. Enjoy!</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-02-08T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"110079-lost-found", "title"=>"Lost & Found", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab012511.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=110079", "description"=>"<p>In this episode, we steer our way through a series of stories about getting lost, and ask how our brains, and our hearts, help us find our way back home. </p><p>After hearing about a little girl who gets lost in front of her own house, Jad and Robert wonder how we find our way in the world. We meet a woman who has spent her entire life getting lost, and find out how our brains make maps of the world around us. We go to a military base in New Jersey to learn about some amazing feats of navigational wizardry, and are introduced to a group of people in Australia with impeccable orientation. Finally, we turn to a very different kind of lost and found: a love story about running into a terrifying, and unexpected, fork in the road.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>For transcripts, see individual segment pages.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Stories about getting lost, and how our brains, and our hearts, help us find our way back home.", "publishTime"=>1296000000000, "publish-at"=>"2011-01-25T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1295974800, "estimated-duration"=>3422, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"You Are Here", "name"=>"photologue/photos/you_are_here.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/633/409/c/80/photologue/photos/you_are_here.jpg", "h"=>409, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"You Are Here", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/quasimondo/138297060/in/pool-36521979000@N01", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/you_are_here.jpg", "w"=>633, "id"=>41027, "credits-name"=>"Quasimondo"}, "objectID"=>"110079", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Lost & Found", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this episode, we steer our way through a series of stories about getting lost, and ask how our brains, and our hearts, help us find our way back home. </p><p>After hearing about a little girl who gets lost in front of her own house, Jad and Robert wonder how we find our way in the world. We meet a woman who has spent her entire life getting lost, and find out how our brains make maps of the world around us. We go to a military base in New Jersey to learn about some amazing feats of navigational wizardry, and are introduced to a group of people in Australia with impeccable orientation. Finally, we turn to a very different kind of lost and found: a love story about running into a terrifying, and unexpected, fork in the road.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>For transcripts, see individual segment pages.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-01-25T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"108770-universe-knows-my-name", "title"=>"The Universe Knows My Name", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast11myname.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=108770", "description"=>"<p>In this new short, we explore luck and fate, both good and bad, with an author and a cartoon character.</p><p>Questions of fate and free will come up all the time on Radiolab, whether we're telling a story or talking to a scientist. And in this short, we decided to take a playful approach to the subject. <strong>Paul Auster</strong> tells a couple good yarns (true ones) that make Jad and Robert wonder whether the universe is playing puppet master. Then Pat Walters and Lulu Miller talk to <strong>Michael Barrier</strong> (he's the guy you call if you have a big profound question about Looney Tunes). Along the way, they answer a question that has been bugging Lulu for a long, long time.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"In this new short, we explore luck and fate, both good and bad, with an author and a cartoon character.", "publishTime"=>1294802730000, "publish-at"=>"2011-01-11T22:25:30", "date-line-ts"=>1294777562, "estimated-duration"=>1003, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Crystal ball", "name"=>"photologue/photos/universe_knows.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/410/c/80/photologue/photos/universe_knows.jpg", "h"=>410, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Crystal ball", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/frogman2212/3970181993/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/universe_knows.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>66000, "credits-name"=>"Frogman!"}, "objectID"=>"108770", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Universe Knows My Name", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this new short, we explore luck and fate, both good and bad, with an author and a cartoon character.</p><p>Questions of fate and free will come up all the time on Radiolab, whether we're telling a story or talking to a scientist. And in this short, we decided to take a playful approach to the subject. <strong>Paul Auster</strong> tells a couple good yarns (true ones) that make Jad and Robert wonder whether the universe is playing puppet master. Then Pat Walters and Lulu Miller talk to <strong>Michael Barrier</strong> (he's the guy you call if you have a big profound question about Looney Tunes). Along the way, they answer a question that has been bugging Lulu for a long, long time.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-01-11T22:25:30", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2012=>[{"slug"=>"257189-bliss", "title"=>"Bliss", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab121712.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=257189", "description"=>"<p>Moments of total, world-shaking bliss are not easy to come by. Maybe that's what makes them feel so life-altering when they strike. And so worth chasing. This hour: stories of striving, grasping, tripping, and falling for happiness, perfection, and ideals.</p>", "tease"=>"Stories of striving, grasping, tripping, and falling for happiness, perfection, and Bliss.", "publishTime"=>1355792400000, "publish-at"=>"2012-12-17T20:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1355763600, "estimated-duration"=>4307, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Illustration of a ladder to the moon", "name"=>"photologue/images/11/Radiolab_moon_620x372.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/614/h/80/photologue/images/11/Radiolab_moon_620x372.jpg", "h"=>614, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://daniel-horowitz.com/category/illustration/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/images/11/Radiolab_moon_620x372.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>85070, "credits-name"=>"Daniel Horowitz"}, "objectID"=>"257189", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Bliss", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Moments of total, world-shaking bliss are not easy to come by. Maybe that's what makes them feel so life-altering when they strike. And so worth chasing. This hour: stories of striving, grasping, tripping, and falling for happiness, perfection, and ideals.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-12-17T20:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"254840-operation-migration", "title"=>"Raising Crane", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12cranes.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=254840", "description"=>"<p>In this short, costumed scientists create a carefully choreographed childhood for a flock of whooping cranes to save them from extinction. It's the ultimate feel-good story, but it also raises some troubling questions about what it takes to get a species back to being wild.</p><p><img style=\"float: left; margin: 10px;\" src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/df/op_mig_whooping_crane.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"200\">Perched in a duck blind in a wildlife research center outside of Washington, D.C., <a class=\"guestlink\" href=\"/people//\"><strong>Andrea Seabrook</strong></a> gets a glimpse of her first whooping crane: a tall, snowy-white bird on stalky legs with a bright red-crested head. It's no surprise she had to drive to Maryland and hide in a tiny shack to see one -- less than a hundred years ago, there were just 16 whooping cranes left, and only 4 breeding females. </p>\n<p>Now scientists like <a class=\"guestlink\" href=\"/people//\"><strong>John B. French</strong></a> are taking these cranes under their wings to keep them from dying out. It's no easy task -- the scientists go to ludicrous lengths to teach these cranes how to survive in the wild, while making sure the cranes never know they're interacting with humans. Chicks nestle with taxidermied moms, are taught the basics of crane life by puppet-wielding researchers wearing white cloth suits, and are led across the country by ultralight planes (piloted, of course, by a crane-suited human).</p>\n<p>Crazily enough, it seems to be working -- there are now somewhere around 500 whooping cranes in the wild, 30 times more than there were in the 1930s.</p>\n<p>But that's not quite the whole story. Andrea tells us about a sad mystery in the middle of this monumental effort -- one very crucial moment where things go terribly wrong. She and John French explain the strange behavior that has crane champions worried, and what might be causing it.</p>\n<p>*<em>Image of whooping crane at Patokah River National Wildlife Refuge in Indiana on its migration south. <a href=\"http://www.flickr.com/photos/usfwsmidwest/5220592829/\" target=\"_blank\">Steve Gifford/USFWS/flickrCC-BY-2.0.</a></em></p>\n<p>For more on whooping cranes, check out <a href=\"http://www.operationmigration.org/\" target=\"_blank\">Operation Migration's site for tons of amazing photos and info</a>. And take a look at their <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/user/OperationMigration?feature=watch\" target=\"_blank\">YouTube channel</a>, where we found this video of a friendly puppet teaching a whooping crane chick how to eat and drink:</p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"465\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/sevdFNOFCh8\" width=\"620\"></iframe></p>\n<p>Flip through this slideshow of whooping crane photos from the US Fish and Wildlife Service Headquarters:</p>", "tease"=>"Perched in a duck blind in a wildlife research center outside of Washington, D.C.,&nbsp;Andrea Seabrook&nbsp;gets a glimpse of her first whooping crane", "publishTime"=>1354579200000, "publish-at"=>"2012-12-03T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1354579200, "estimated-duration"=>1265, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Whooping cranes learning their fall migration route from an ultralight aircraft piloted by a human wearing a crane suit. ", "name"=>"photologue/images/42/op_mig_flying_1.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/h/80/photologue/images/42/op_mig_flying_1.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"Whooping cranes learning their fall migration route from an ultralight aircraft piloted by a human wearing a crane suit. ", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/usfwshq/6777513684/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/images/42/op_mig_flying_1.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>84597, "credits-name"=>"USFWS"}, "objectID"=>"254840", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Raising Crane", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this short, costumed scientists create a carefully choreographed childhood for a flock of whooping cranes to save them from extinction. It's the ultimate feel-good story, but it also raises some troubling questions about what it takes to get a species back to being wild.</p><p><img style=\"float: left; margin: 10px;\" src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/df/op_mig_whooping_crane.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"200\">Perched in a duck blind in a wildlife research center outside of Washington, D.C., <a class=\"guestlink\" href=\"/people//\"><strong>Andrea Seabrook</strong></a> gets a glimpse of her first whooping crane: a tall, snowy-white bird on stalky legs with a bright red-crested head. It's no surprise she had to drive to Maryland and hide in a tiny shack to see one -- less than a hundred years ago, there were just 16 whooping cranes left, and only 4 breeding females. </p>\n<p>Now scientists like <a class=\"guestlink\" href=\"/people//\"><strong>John B. French</strong></a> are taking these cranes under their wings to keep them from dying out. It's no easy task -- the scientists go to ludicrous lengths to teach these cranes how to survive in the wild, while making sure the cranes never know they're interacting with humans. Chicks nestle with taxidermied moms, are taught the basics of crane life by puppet-wielding researchers wearing white cloth suits, and are led across the country by ultralight planes (piloted, of course, by a crane-suited human).</p>\n<p>Crazily enough, it seems to be working -- there are now somewhere around 500 whooping cranes in the wild, 30 times more than there were in the 1930s.</p>\n<p>But that's not quite the whole story. Andrea tells us about a sad mystery in the middle of this monumental effort -- one very crucial moment where things go terribly wrong. She and John French explain the strange behavior that has crane champions worried, and what might be causing it.</p>\n<p>*<em>Image of whooping crane at Patokah River National Wildlife Refuge in Indiana on its migration south. <a href=\"http://www.flickr.com/photos/usfwsmidwest/5220592829/\" target=\"_blank\">Steve Gifford/USFWS/flickrCC-BY-2.0.</a></em></p>\n<p>For more on whooping cranes, check out <a href=\"http://www.operationmigration.org/\" target=\"_blank\">Operation Migration's site for tons of amazing photos and info</a>. And take a look at their <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/user/OperationMigration?feature=watch\" target=\"_blank\">YouTube channel</a>, where we found this video of a friendly puppet teaching a whooping crane chick how to eat and drink:</p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"465\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/sevdFNOFCh8\" width=\"620\"></iframe></p>\n<p>Flip through this slideshow of whooping crane photos from the US Fish and Wildlife Service Headquarters:</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-12-03T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"251876-inheritance", "title"=>"Inheritance", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab111912.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=251876", "description"=>"<p>Once a kid is born, their genetic fate is pretty much sealed. Or is it? This hour, we put nature and nurture on a collision course and discover how outside forces can find a way inside us, shaping not just our hearts and minds, but the basic biological blueprint that we pass on to future generations.</p>", "tease"=>"<p><span>Stories of nature and nurture slamming into each other, &amp; <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2012/nov/19/\">shaping our biological blueprints</a>.</span></p>", "publishTime"=>1353380400000, "publish-at"=>"2012-11-19T22:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1353301200, "estimated-duration"=>3791, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Illustration of a crystal ball revealing DNA", "name"=>"photologue/images/b6/genetic_crystal_ball.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/600/450/c/80/photologue/images/b6/genetic_crystal_ball.jpg", "h"=>450, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://daniel-horowitz.com/category/illustration/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/images/b6/genetic_crystal_ball.jpg", "w"=>600, "id"=>84143, "credits-name"=>"Daniel Horowitz"}, "objectID"=>"251876", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Inheritance", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Once a kid is born, their genetic fate is pretty much sealed. Or is it? This hour, we put nature and nurture on a collision course and discover how outside forces can find a way inside us, shaping not just our hearts and minds, but the basic biological blueprint that we pass on to future generations.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-11-19T22:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"248590-blanc", "title"=>"What's Up, Doc?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12blanc.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=248590", "description"=>"<p>Mel Blanc was known as \"the man of 1,000 voices,\" but the actual number may have been closer to 1,500. Bugs Bunny, Porky Pig, Tweety, Barney Rubble -- all Mel. His characters made him one of the most beloved men in America. And in 1961, when a car crash left him in a coma, these characters may have saved him.</p><p>Mel Blanc wasn't just a voice man. He created entire personalities, each with its own nuances and hilarious quirks. His son <strong>Noel Blanc </strong>says his dad invested so much into Bugs, Porky, Daffy, Tweety et al that Mel's face and body would transform with every cartoon animal that spoke through him. This summer, our producer <strong>Sean Cole</strong> interviewed Noel at the Blanc family house on Big Bear Lake outside of LA. Sean had heard a crazy story about Mel nearly dying in a crash on Dead Man's Curve on Hollywood Boulevard -- and about the moment two weeks later when Bugs Bunny emerged from Mel's coma before Mel did. In fact, according to neurosurgeon <strong>Louis Conway</strong> who attended to Mel at the time, it seemed as though Bugs Bunny was trying to save his life.</p>\n<p>Sean, Noel, Dr. Conway and NYU brain scientist Orrin Devinsky weigh over what it might mean to be rescued by a figment of your own imagination, and whether one self can win out over another in a moment of crisis.</p>\n<p><em>\"Dead's Man Curve,\" which Jan and Dean immortalized in song, is just north of UCLA's Drake Stadium on Sunset Boulevard. According to Mel Blanc's autobiography \"That's Not All Folks,\" age-old plans to straighten the curve were finally approved after his accident. <br></em></p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"350\" marginwidth=\"0\" marginheight=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" src=\"https://maps.google.com/maps?f=q&amp;source=s_q&amp;hl=en&amp;geocode=&amp;q=drake+stadium+sunset+boulevard+los+angeles,+CA&amp;aq=&amp;sll=37.0625,-95.677068&amp;sspn=74.440576,131.308594&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;hq=drake+stadium&amp;hnear=Sunset+Blvd,+Los+Angeles,+California+90026&amp;t=m&amp;ll=34.072142,-118.448281&amp;spn=0.012442,0.026608&amp;z=15&amp;iwloc=A&amp;output=embed\" width=\"620\"></iframe><br><small><a style=\"color: #0000ff; text-align: left;\" href=\"https://maps.google.com/maps?f=q&amp;source=embed&amp;hl=en&amp;geocode=&amp;q=drake+stadium+sunset+boulevard+los+angeles,+CA&amp;aq=&amp;sll=37.0625,-95.677068&amp;sspn=74.440576,131.308594&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;hq=drake+stadium&amp;hnear=Sunset+Blvd,+Los+Angeles,+California+90026&amp;t=m&amp;ll=34.072142,-118.448281&amp;spn=0.012442,0.026608&amp;z=15&amp;iwloc=A\">View Larger Map</a></small></p>", "tease"=>"Mel Blanc wasn't just a voice man. He created entire personalities, each with its own nuances and hilarious quirks.", "publishTime"=>1352235600000, "publish-at"=>"2012-11-06T16:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1352235600, "estimated-duration"=>1141, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Illustration of a coma-zapping bunny", "name"=>"photologue/images/9d/bunny_coma_web_1.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/900/540/l/80/photologue/images/9d/bunny_coma_web_1.jpg", "h"=>540, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://daniel-horowitz.com/category/illustration/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/images/9d/bunny_coma_web_1.jpg", "w"=>900, "id"=>83291, "credits-name"=>"Daniel Horowitz"}, "objectID"=>"248590", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"What's Up, Doc?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Mel Blanc was known as \"the man of 1,000 voices,\" but the actual number may have been closer to 1,500. Bugs Bunny, Porky Pig, Tweety, Barney Rubble -- all Mel. His characters made him one of the most beloved men in America. And in 1961, when a car crash left him in a coma, these characters may have saved him.</p><p>Mel Blanc wasn't just a voice man. He created entire personalities, each with its own nuances and hilarious quirks. His son <strong>Noel Blanc </strong>says his dad invested so much into Bugs, Porky, Daffy, Tweety et al that Mel's face and body would transform with every cartoon animal that spoke through him. This summer, our producer <strong>Sean Cole</strong> interviewed Noel at the Blanc family house on Big Bear Lake outside of LA. Sean had heard a crazy story about Mel nearly dying in a crash on Dead Man's Curve on Hollywood Boulevard -- and about the moment two weeks later when Bugs Bunny emerged from Mel's coma before Mel did. In fact, according to neurosurgeon <strong>Louis Conway</strong> who attended to Mel at the time, it seemed as though Bugs Bunny was trying to save his life.</p>\n<p>Sean, Noel, Dr. Conway and NYU brain scientist Orrin Devinsky weigh over what it might mean to be rescued by a figment of your own imagination, and whether one self can win out over another in a moment of crisis.</p>\n<p><em>\"Dead's Man Curve,\" which Jan and Dean immortalized in song, is just north of UCLA's Drake Stadium on Sunset Boulevard. According to Mel Blanc's autobiography \"That's Not All Folks,\" age-old plans to straighten the curve were finally approved after his accident. <br></em></p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"350\" marginwidth=\"0\" marginheight=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" src=\"https://maps.google.com/maps?f=q&amp;source=s_q&amp;hl=en&amp;geocode=&amp;q=drake+stadium+sunset+boulevard+los+angeles,+CA&amp;aq=&amp;sll=37.0625,-95.677068&amp;sspn=74.440576,131.308594&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;hq=drake+stadium&amp;hnear=Sunset+Blvd,+Los+Angeles,+California+90026&amp;t=m&amp;ll=34.072142,-118.448281&amp;spn=0.012442,0.026608&amp;z=15&amp;iwloc=A&amp;output=embed\" width=\"620\"></iframe><br><small><a style=\"color: #0000ff; text-align: left;\" href=\"https://maps.google.com/maps?f=q&amp;source=embed&amp;hl=en&amp;geocode=&amp;q=drake+stadium+sunset+boulevard+los+angeles,+CA&amp;aq=&amp;sll=37.0625,-95.677068&amp;sspn=74.440576,131.308594&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;hq=drake+stadium&amp;hnear=Sunset+Blvd,+Los+Angeles,+California+90026&amp;t=m&amp;ll=34.072142,-118.448281&amp;spn=0.012442,0.026608&amp;z=15&amp;iwloc=A\">View Larger Map</a></small></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-11-06T16:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"245482-seeing-dark", "title"=>"Seeing in the Dark", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_extras/radiolab_extras102212blindtruth.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=245482", "description"=>"<p>John and Zoltan are both blind, but they deal with the world in completely different ways -- one paints vivid pictures in his mind, while the other refuses to picture anything at all. In this short, they argue about the truth of a world they can't see.</p><p>When John Hull, a theology professor in England, lost his sight he became convinced that the images in his mind -- like his memories of his wife's face when she was younger -- no longer matched the reality in which he lived. He didn't want to live in a world of fantasy, so decided to stop picturing the world altogether. Zoltan Torey, on the other hand, simply couldn't stand living in a world without images, so he resolved to visualize everything. He constantly creates a world of pictures inside his head that (he says) matches up with the world as it really is.</p>\n<p>Because they settled on diametrically opposed ways of living without sight, we wondered what would happen if we got them on the phone together to duke it out. So we patched them through our studio, and recorded their conversation for our live show <em>In the Dark</em>.</p>\n<p><img style=\"float: left; margin-right: 10px;\" src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/cd/demetri_martin.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"150\">And! Before we go, a quick reminder that we need your support to keep this podcast going strong, to keep giving you stories like this one. If you like what we do, help us keep it coming -- it takes just a few minutes. For a <a href=\"http://wny.cc/TVl09O\">$75 contribution, you can pick up a copy of \"Demetri Martin: Standup Comedian\" as a thank you gift</a>. Or join our new <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/labpartners/\">digital membership program, Lab Partners, and we'll thank you with new downloads each month like Radiolab ringtones, digital posters, and video extras</a> -- in November, Lab Partners will get exclusive access to a video of Demetri performing during our live show <em>In the Dark</em>. Thanks in advance, and thanks so much to everyone who's already donated!</p>\n<p><em>Read more:</em></p>\n<p>Zoltan Torey, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/026251284X/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>The Crucible of Consciousness: An Integrated Theory of Mind and Brain</em></a></span></p>\n<p>John Hull, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1851681418/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>On Sight and Insight: A Journey into the World of Blindness</em></a></span></p>", "tease"=>"While John finds truth in darkness, Zoltan sees an emotional void. And as they argue, they reveal some very powerful truths about how we connect to one another.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1350946800000, "publish-at"=>"2012-10-22T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1350946800, "estimated-duration"=>1508, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Child running in the dark", "name"=>"photologue/images/3b/102212_rl_darkness.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/600/600/c/80/photologue/images/3b/102212_rl_darkness.jpg", "h"=>600, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/vinothchandar/5997445354/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/images/3b/102212_rl_darkness.jpg", "w"=>600, "id"=>82619, "credits-name"=>"Vinoth Chandar"}, "objectID"=>"245482", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Seeing in the Dark", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>John and Zoltan are both blind, but they deal with the world in completely different ways -- one paints vivid pictures in his mind, while the other refuses to picture anything at all. In this short, they argue about the truth of a world they can't see.</p><p>When John Hull, a theology professor in England, lost his sight he became convinced that the images in his mind -- like his memories of his wife's face when she was younger -- no longer matched the reality in which he lived. He didn't want to live in a world of fantasy, so decided to stop picturing the world altogether. Zoltan Torey, on the other hand, simply couldn't stand living in a world without images, so he resolved to visualize everything. He constantly creates a world of pictures inside his head that (he says) matches up with the world as it really is.</p>\n<p>Because they settled on diametrically opposed ways of living without sight, we wondered what would happen if we got them on the phone together to duke it out. So we patched them through our studio, and recorded their conversation for our live show <em>In the Dark</em>.</p>\n<p><img style=\"float: left; margin-right: 10px;\" src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/cd/demetri_martin.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"150\">And! Before we go, a quick reminder that we need your support to keep this podcast going strong, to keep giving you stories like this one. If you like what we do, help us keep it coming -- it takes just a few minutes. For a <a href=\"http://wny.cc/TVl09O\">$75 contribution, you can pick up a copy of \"Demetri Martin: Standup Comedian\" as a thank you gift</a>. Or join our new <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/labpartners/\">digital membership program, Lab Partners, and we'll thank you with new downloads each month like Radiolab ringtones, digital posters, and video extras</a> -- in November, Lab Partners will get exclusive access to a video of Demetri performing during our live show <em>In the Dark</em>. Thanks in advance, and thanks so much to everyone who's already donated!</p>\n<p><em>Read more:</em></p>\n<p>Zoltan Torey, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/026251284X/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>The Crucible of Consciousness: An Integrated Theory of Mind and Brain</em></a></span></p>\n<p>John Hull, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1851681418/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>On Sight and Insight: A Journey into the World of Blindness</em></a></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-10-22T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"242184-dark-side-earth", "title"=>"Dark Side of the Earth", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12astronaut.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=242184", "description"=>"<p>200 miles above Earth's surface, astronaut Dave Wolf -- rocketing through the blackness of Earth's shadow at 5 miles a second -- floated out of the Mir Space Station on his very first spacewalk. In this short, he describes the extremes of light and dark in space, relives a heart-pounding close call, and shares one of the most tranquil moments of his life.</p><p>When we were putting together our live show <em>In the Dark</em>, Jad and Robert called up Dave Wolf to ask him if he had any stories about darkness. And boy, did he. Dave told us two stories that  became the finale of our show.</p>\n<p><img style=\"float: right; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 5px;\" src=\"https://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/imagery/photos/sts-86/86p-036.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\"><img style=\"float: right; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 5px;\" src=\"https://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/imagery/photos/sts-86/86p-027.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\">Back in late 1997, Dave Wolf was on his first spacewalk, to perform work on the Mir (the photo to the right was taken during that mission, courtesy of <a href=\"http://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/sts86/photo/sts-86-photo-36.htm\" target=\"_blank\">NASA.</a>). Dave wasn't alone -- with him was veteran Russian cosmonaut Anatoly Solovyev. (That's a picture of Dave giving Anatoly a hug on board the Mir, also courtesy of <a href=\"http://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/sts86/photo/sts-86-photo-27.htm\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a>).</p>\n<p>Out in blackness of space, the contrast between light and dark is almost unimaginably extreme -- every 45 minutes, you plunge between absolute darkness on the night-side of Earth, and blazing light as the sun screams into view. Dave and Anatoly were tethered to the spacecraft, traveling 5 miles per second. That's 16 times faster than we travel on Earth's surface as it rotates -- so as they orbited, they experienced 16 nights and 16 days for every Earth day.</p>\n<p>Dave's description of his first spacewalk was all we could've asked for, and more. But what happened next ... well, it's just one of those stories that you always hope an astronaut will tell. Dave and Anatoly were ready to call it a job and head back into the Mir when something went wrong   with the airlock. They couldn't get it to re-pressurize. In other words, they   were locked out. After hours of trying to fix the airlock, they were running out of the resources that kept them alive in their space   suits and facing a grisly death. So, they unhooked their tethers, and tried one last desperate move .</p>\n<p>In the end, they made it through, and Dave went on to perform dozens more spacewalks in the years to come, but he never again experienced anything like those harrowing minutes trying to improvise his way back into the Mir.</p>\n<p>After that terrifying tale, Dave told us about another moment he and Anatoly shared, floating  high above Earth, staring out into the universe ... a moment so beautiful, and peaceful, we decided  to use the audience recreate it, as best we could, for the final act of our live show.</p>\n<p>Pilobolus creates a shadow astronaut during Dave Wolf's story on stage (photo by Lars Topelmann):</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/bb/itd_astro_shadow_MG_9001.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p>The audience turns Portland's Keller auditorium into a view of outer space with thousands of LED lights (photo by Lars Topelmann):</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/29/itd_leds_MG_0717.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p>Here's Dave Wolf in the dark darkness of space, performing a spacewalk in 2009 (courtesy of <a href=\"http://spaceflight.nasa.gov/gallery/images/shuttle/sts-127/html/s127e007210.html\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a>):</p>\n<p><em><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/3e/s127e007096.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></em></p>\n<p>To give you an idea of what it looks like during the brightness of day, here's another photo taken in 2009 -- more than a decade after the adventure described in our podcast -- this time of astronaut Tom Marshburn (Dave Wolf is with him, out of frame, photo courtesy of <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/multimedia/imagegallery/image_feature_1425.html\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a>):</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/56/Astro_TM_372055main_image_1425_1024_768.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p>We had such an amazing time touring around the country with <em>Radiolab Live: In the Dark. </em>Here's a slideshow of some favorite action shots:</p>", "tease"=>"200 miles above Earth's surface, astronaut Dave Wolf -- rocketing through the blackness of Earth's shadow at 5 miles a second -- floated out of the Mir Space Station on his very first...", "publishTime"=>1349737200000, "publish-at"=>"2012-10-08T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1349737200, "estimated-duration"=>1169, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Astronaut Dave Wolf performing a spacewalk on the Endeavour in July, 2009.", "name"=>"photologue/images/ca/s127e007210_1.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/900/540/h/80/photologue/images/ca/s127e007210_1.jpg", "h"=>540, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"Astronaut Dave Wolf performing a spacewalk on the Endeavour in July, 2009.", "credits-url"=>"http://spaceflight.nasa.gov/gallery/images/shuttle/sts-127/html/s127e007210.html", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/images/ca/s127e007210_1.jpg", "w"=>900, "id"=>81784, "credits-name"=>"NASA"}, "objectID"=>"242184", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dark Side of the Earth", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>200 miles above Earth's surface, astronaut Dave Wolf -- rocketing through the blackness of Earth's shadow at 5 miles a second -- floated out of the Mir Space Station on his very first spacewalk. In this short, he describes the extremes of light and dark in space, relives a heart-pounding close call, and shares one of the most tranquil moments of his life.</p><p>When we were putting together our live show <em>In the Dark</em>, Jad and Robert called up Dave Wolf to ask him if he had any stories about darkness. And boy, did he. Dave told us two stories that  became the finale of our show.</p>\n<p><img style=\"float: right; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 5px;\" src=\"https://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/imagery/photos/sts-86/86p-036.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\"><img style=\"float: right; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; margin-top: 5px; margin-bottom: 5px;\" src=\"https://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/imagery/photos/sts-86/86p-027.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\">Back in late 1997, Dave Wolf was on his first spacewalk, to perform work on the Mir (the photo to the right was taken during that mission, courtesy of <a href=\"http://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/sts86/photo/sts-86-photo-36.htm\" target=\"_blank\">NASA.</a>). Dave wasn't alone -- with him was veteran Russian cosmonaut Anatoly Solovyev. (That's a picture of Dave giving Anatoly a hug on board the Mir, also courtesy of <a href=\"http://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/sts86/photo/sts-86-photo-27.htm\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a>).</p>\n<p>Out in blackness of space, the contrast between light and dark is almost unimaginably extreme -- every 45 minutes, you plunge between absolute darkness on the night-side of Earth, and blazing light as the sun screams into view. Dave and Anatoly were tethered to the spacecraft, traveling 5 miles per second. That's 16 times faster than we travel on Earth's surface as it rotates -- so as they orbited, they experienced 16 nights and 16 days for every Earth day.</p>\n<p>Dave's description of his first spacewalk was all we could've asked for, and more. But what happened next ... well, it's just one of those stories that you always hope an astronaut will tell. Dave and Anatoly were ready to call it a job and head back into the Mir when something went wrong   with the airlock. They couldn't get it to re-pressurize. In other words, they   were locked out. After hours of trying to fix the airlock, they were running out of the resources that kept them alive in their space   suits and facing a grisly death. So, they unhooked their tethers, and tried one last desperate move .</p>\n<p>In the end, they made it through, and Dave went on to perform dozens more spacewalks in the years to come, but he never again experienced anything like those harrowing minutes trying to improvise his way back into the Mir.</p>\n<p>After that terrifying tale, Dave told us about another moment he and Anatoly shared, floating  high above Earth, staring out into the universe ... a moment so beautiful, and peaceful, we decided  to use the audience recreate it, as best we could, for the final act of our live show.</p>\n<p>Pilobolus creates a shadow astronaut during Dave Wolf's story on stage (photo by Lars Topelmann):</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/bb/itd_astro_shadow_MG_9001.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p>The audience turns Portland's Keller auditorium into a view of outer space with thousands of LED lights (photo by Lars Topelmann):</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/29/itd_leds_MG_0717.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p>Here's Dave Wolf in the dark darkness of space, performing a spacewalk in 2009 (courtesy of <a href=\"http://spaceflight.nasa.gov/gallery/images/shuttle/sts-127/html/s127e007210.html\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a>):</p>\n<p><em><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/3e/s127e007096.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></em></p>\n<p>To give you an idea of what it looks like during the brightness of day, here's another photo taken in 2009 -- more than a decade after the adventure described in our podcast -- this time of astronaut Tom Marshburn (Dave Wolf is with him, out of frame, photo courtesy of <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/multimedia/imagegallery/image_feature_1425.html\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a>):</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/56/Astro_TM_372055main_image_1425_1024_768.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p>We had such an amazing time touring around the country with <em>Radiolab Live: In the Dark. </em>Here's a slideshow of some favorite action shots:</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-10-08T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"239470-the-fact-of-the-matter", "title"=>"The Fact of the Matter", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab092412.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=239470", "description"=>"<p>Getting a firm hold on the truth is never as simple as nailing down the facts of a situation. This hour, we go after a series of seemingly simple facts -- facts that offer surprising insight, facts that inspire deeply different stories, and facts that, in the end, might not matter at all.</p><p>We start with the story of Errol Morris' obsessive search for a straightforward answer about a photograph. He travels the globe to get to the bottom of things, and ends up with an odd peek into a slice of time 150 years ago. Then, one of the strangest stories to come out of the Cold War hinges on evidence that turns out to be deeply entangled with a little-reported tragedy, and a history-shaping accusation. And we end with a story about friendship, and a moving case for how truly knowing someone often requires seeing beyond the details.</p>\n<p><em>Editor's Note: this episode, which was originally podcast on September 24th, 2012, was amended on October 5th, 2012.</em></p>\n<p>Read More:</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blogland/2012/sep/30/robert-krulwich-yellow-rain/\">Robert responds to concerns about the \"Yellow Rain\" segment from this hour.</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blogland/2012/sep/26/yellow-rain/\" target=\"_blank\">Jad offers more context on this segment.</a></p>\n<p><em><br></em></p>", "tease"=>"Getting a firm hold on the truth is never as simple as nailing down the facts of a situation. This hour, we go after a series of seemingly simple facts -- facts that offer surprising ...", "publishTime"=>1348527600000, "publish-at"=>"2012-09-24T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1348459200, "estimated-duration"=>3619, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Illustration: a forest of questions", "name"=>"photologue/images/2e/factmatter.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/900/540/h/80/photologue/images/2e/factmatter.jpg", "h"=>540, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://daniel-horowitz.com/category/illustration/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/images/2e/factmatter.jpg", "w"=>900, "id"=>81198, "credits-name"=>"Daniel Horowitz"}, "objectID"=>"239470", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Fact of the Matter", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Getting a firm hold on the truth is never as simple as nailing down the facts of a situation. This hour, we go after a series of seemingly simple facts -- facts that offer surprising insight, facts that inspire deeply different stories, and facts that, in the end, might not matter at all.</p><p>We start with the story of Errol Morris' obsessive search for a straightforward answer about a photograph. He travels the globe to get to the bottom of things, and ends up with an odd peek into a slice of time 150 years ago. Then, one of the strangest stories to come out of the Cold War hinges on evidence that turns out to be deeply entangled with a little-reported tragedy, and a history-shaping accusation. And we end with a story about friendship, and a moving case for how truly knowing someone often requires seeing beyond the details.</p>\n<p><em>Editor's Note: this episode, which was originally podcast on September 24th, 2012, was amended on October 5th, 2012.</em></p>\n<p>Read More:</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blogland/2012/sep/30/robert-krulwich-yellow-rain/\">Robert responds to concerns about the \"Yellow Rain\" segment from this hour.</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blogland/2012/sep/26/yellow-rain/\" target=\"_blank\">Jad offers more context on this segment.</a></p>\n<p><em><br></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-09-24T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"236351-what-slinky-knows", "title"=>"What a Slinky Knows", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12slinky.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=236351", "description"=>"<p>\"Hey kids,\" said physicist Tadashi Tokieda, \"Wanna see a magic trick?\" He pulled out a Slinky and did something that amazed the kids, &amp; their dad Steve Strogatz. Steve, along with Neil deGrasse Tyson, explains what the gravity-defying Slinky trick reveals about the nature of all things great and small (including us).</p><p>If you've played with a Slinky for more than five minutes, you've probably mastered all the classic moves. But it turns out those humble coils have a surprise up their sleeves. Do this:</p>\n<p>1) Dangle a Slinky above the ground as though you were holding a fish by the tip of its tail.</p>\n<p>2) Let it extend to its full length.</p>\n<p>3) Let go.</p>\n<p>For a fraction of a second, something amazing happens: the bottom of slinky hovers in midair, seeming to defy the laws of physics, while the top collapses toward it.</p>\n<p>The reason the bottom just floats there, according to <a href=\"http://www.stevenstrogatz.com/\"><strong>Steve Strogatz</strong>, </a>is simple: it hasn't gotten the memo yet. We're not just being cute here. Information flows have a lot to do with how our physical world works. And this little experiment, when extrapolated out, might reveal some pretty surprising truths about... well... everything. From anti-tank weaponry, to what would happen if the sun suddenly disappeared, Steve and and our astronomical friend <a href=\"http://www.haydenplanetarium.org/tyson/\"><strong>Neil deGrasse Tyson</strong></a> help us unravel the implications of the \"wonderful toy.\" The bottom of the Slinky, friends, is us!</p>\n<p>Also check out:</p>\n<p>Steve Strogatz's new book <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0547517653/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>The Joy of X</em></a></span></p>\n<p>And this slow-mo video of a falling Slinky...</p>", "tease"=>"If you've played with a Slinky for more than five minutes, you've probably mastered all the classic moves", "publishTime"=>1347329520000, "publish-at"=>"2012-09-10T22:12:00", "date-line-ts"=>1347318000, "estimated-duration"=>781, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Looking through a slinky at the sky", "name"=>"photologue/images/a1/slinky_2.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/487/c/80/photologue/images/a1/slinky_2.jpg", "h"=>487, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/sashawolff/3249699728/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/images/a1/slinky_2.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>80662, "credits-name"=>"SashaW"}, "objectID"=>"236351", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"What a Slinky Knows", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>\"Hey kids,\" said physicist Tadashi Tokieda, \"Wanna see a magic trick?\" He pulled out a Slinky and did something that amazed the kids, &amp; their dad Steve Strogatz. Steve, along with Neil deGrasse Tyson, explains what the gravity-defying Slinky trick reveals about the nature of all things great and small (including us).</p><p>If you've played with a Slinky for more than five minutes, you've probably mastered all the classic moves. But it turns out those humble coils have a surprise up their sleeves. Do this:</p>\n<p>1) Dangle a Slinky above the ground as though you were holding a fish by the tip of its tail.</p>\n<p>2) Let it extend to its full length.</p>\n<p>3) Let go.</p>\n<p>For a fraction of a second, something amazing happens: the bottom of slinky hovers in midair, seeming to defy the laws of physics, while the top collapses toward it.</p>\n<p>The reason the bottom just floats there, according to <a href=\"http://www.stevenstrogatz.com/\"><strong>Steve Strogatz</strong>, </a>is simple: it hasn't gotten the memo yet. We're not just being cute here. Information flows have a lot to do with how our physical world works. And this little experiment, when extrapolated out, might reveal some pretty surprising truths about... well... everything. From anti-tank weaponry, to what would happen if the sun suddenly disappeared, Steve and and our astronomical friend <a href=\"http://www.haydenplanetarium.org/tyson/\"><strong>Neil deGrasse Tyson</strong></a> help us unravel the implications of the \"wonderful toy.\" The bottom of the Slinky, friends, is us!</p>\n<p>Also check out:</p>\n<p>Steve Strogatz's new book <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0547517653/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>The Joy of X</em></a></span></p>\n<p>And this slow-mo video of a falling Slinky...</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-09-10T22:12:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"233143-pain-scale", "title"=>"Inside \"Ouch!\"", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12painscale.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=233143", "description"=>"<p>Pain is a fundamental part of life, and often a very lonely part. Doctors want to understand their patients' pain, and we all want to understand the suffering of our friends, relatives, or spouses. But pinning down another person's hurt is a slippery business. </p><p>Is your relentless lower back pain more or less unbearable than my crushing headache? Problem is, pain is maddeningly subjective. In this short, producer <strong>Tim Howard</strong> introduces us to three attempts to put a number on pain in the hopes that we can truly understand the suffering of another.</p>\n<p>We begin with entomologist <strong>Justin Schmidt's</strong> globe-trotting adventure to plot the relative nastiness of insect bites and stings. Then, <strong><a href=\"http://clas.uiowa.edu/history/people/paula-michaels\" target=\"_blank\">Paula Michaels</a></strong>, a professor in the History of Medicine at the University of Iowa, brings us back to 1948, to a well-intentioned but ultimately misguided attempt to demystify the pain of childbirth. And we end with a very modern, very personal struggle for understanding as non-fiction writer <strong><a href=\"http://eulabiss.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Eula Biss</a></strong> tries to rate her own chronic pain.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><em>NOTE: Pain Scale image remixed from CC By-SA 3.0 images from <a href=\"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Safety_pin2.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Haragayato</a>; <a href=\"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brass_thumbtack.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">John Dalton</a>; <a href=\"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houseaux.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Isabelle Grosjean</a>; <a href=\"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiodi_quadri.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Luigi Chiesa</a>; and <a href=\"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Railroad_spikes.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">Sean Lamb</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Pain is a fundamental part of life, but pinning down another person's hurt is a slippery business. ", "publishTime"=>1346197856000, "publish-at"=>"2012-08-28T19:50:56", "date-line-ts"=>1346108400, "estimated-duration"=>1546, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"illustration: pain scale", "name"=>"photologue/photos/PAIN_SCALE_web.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/", "name"=>"Wikimedia Commons"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/h/80/photologue/photos/PAIN_SCALE_web.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"Illustration: pain scale", "credits-url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Main_Page", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/PAIN_SCALE_web.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>80048, "credits-name"=>"Remixed from CC By-SA 3.0 images from Haragayato; John Dalton; Isabelle Grosjean; Luigi Chiesa; and Sean Lamb"}, "objectID"=>"233143", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Inside \"Ouch!\"", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Pain is a fundamental part of life, and often a very lonely part. Doctors want to understand their patients' pain, and we all want to understand the suffering of our friends, relatives, or spouses. But pinning down another person's hurt is a slippery business. </p><p>Is your relentless lower back pain more or less unbearable than my crushing headache? Problem is, pain is maddeningly subjective. In this short, producer <strong>Tim Howard</strong> introduces us to three attempts to put a number on pain in the hopes that we can truly understand the suffering of another.</p>\n<p>We begin with entomologist <strong>Justin Schmidt's</strong> globe-trotting adventure to plot the relative nastiness of insect bites and stings. Then, <strong><a href=\"http://clas.uiowa.edu/history/people/paula-michaels\" target=\"_blank\">Paula Michaels</a></strong>, a professor in the History of Medicine at the University of Iowa, brings us back to 1948, to a well-intentioned but ultimately misguided attempt to demystify the pain of childbirth. And we end with a very modern, very personal struggle for understanding as non-fiction writer <strong><a href=\"http://eulabiss.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Eula Biss</a></strong> tries to rate her own chronic pain.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><em>NOTE: Pain Scale image remixed from CC By-SA 3.0 images from <a href=\"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Safety_pin2.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Haragayato</a>; <a href=\"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brass_thumbtack.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">John Dalton</a>; <a href=\"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houseaux.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Isabelle Grosjean</a>; <a href=\"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chiodi_quadri.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">Luigi Chiesa</a>; and <a href=\"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Railroad_spikes.JPG\" target=\"_blank\">Sean Lamb</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-08-28T19:50:56", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"231536-rebroadcast-space", "title"=>"REBROADCAST: Space", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab051206.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=231536", "description"=>"<p>Celebrate the 35th anniversary of the launch of Voyager 2 (it rocketed off Earth on 8/20/77 carrying a copy of the Golden Record), and tip your hat to the Mars rover Curiosity as it kicks off its third week on the red planet, with a rebroadcast of one our favorite episodes: <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2007/oct/22/\">Space</a>.</p><p>We've been space-crazy the past few weeks here at Radiolab -- from cheering on the scientifically <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/msl/index.html\" target=\"_blank\">epic landing of the Mars rover</a> earlier this month, to staying up late to watch the <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blogland/2012/aug/09/deluxe-meteor-shower/\" target=\"_blank\">Perseid meteor showers</a>, to reliving a stomach-churning spacewalk with an astronaut in <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/series/in-the-dark/\" target=\"_blank\">our live show In the Dark.</a> We've been happily turning our thoughts and gazes skyward all summer long. So before the nights get too chilly, grab a blanket, cue up <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2007/oct/22/\">Space</a>, and stare into that vast, glittering, perspective-shaking darkness with us.</p>", "tease"=>"Celebrate the 35th anniversary of the launch of Voyager 2 (it rocketed off Earth on 8/20/77 carrying a copy of the Golden Record), and tip your hat to the Mars rover Curiosity as it k...", "publishTime"=>1345516740000, "publish-at"=>"2012-08-20T22:39:00", "date-line-ts"=>1345516800, "estimated-duration"=>3437, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Ed White performs first US spacewalk", "name"=>"photologue/photos/Ed_White_performs_first_US_web.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/900/688/c/80/photologue/photos/Ed_White_performs_first_US_web.jpg", "h"=>688, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/Ed_White_performs_first_US_web.jpg", "w"=>900, "id"=>79687, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"231536", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"REBROADCAST: Space", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Celebrate the 35th anniversary of the launch of Voyager 2 (it rocketed off Earth on 8/20/77 carrying a copy of the Golden Record), and tip your hat to the Mars rover Curiosity as it kicks off its third week on the red planet, with a rebroadcast of one our favorite episodes: <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2007/oct/22/\">Space</a>.</p><p>We've been space-crazy the past few weeks here at Radiolab -- from cheering on the scientifically <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/mission_pages/msl/index.html\" target=\"_blank\">epic landing of the Mars rover</a> earlier this month, to staying up late to watch the <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blogland/2012/aug/09/deluxe-meteor-shower/\" target=\"_blank\">Perseid meteor showers</a>, to reliving a stomach-churning spacewalk with an astronaut in <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/series/in-the-dark/\" target=\"_blank\">our live show In the Dark.</a> We've been happily turning our thoughts and gazes skyward all summer long. So before the nights get too chilly, grab a blanket, cue up <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2007/oct/22/\">Space</a>, and stare into that vast, glittering, perspective-shaking darkness with us.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-08-20T22:39:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"226523-ants", "title"=>"Argentine Invasion", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12ants.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=226523", "description"=>"<p>From a suburban sidewalk in southern California, Jad and Robert witness the carnage of a gruesome turf war. Though the tiny warriors doing battle clock in at just a fraction of an inch, they have evolved a surprising, successful, and rather unsettling strategy of ironclad loyalty, absolute intolerance, and brutal violence.</p><p><strong>David Holway</strong>, an ecologist and evolutionary biologist from UC San Diego, takes us to a driveway in Escondido, California where a grisly battle rages. In this quiet suburban spot, two groups of ants are putting on a chilling display of dismemberment and death. According to David, this battle line marks the edge of an enormous super-colony of Argentine ants. Think of that anthill in your backyard, and stretch it out across five continents.</p>\n<p>Argentine ants are not good neighbors. When they meet ants from another colony, any other colony, they fight to the death, and tear the other ants to pieces. While other kinds of ants sometimes take slaves or even have sex with ants from different colonies, the Argentine ants don’t fool around. If you’re not part of the colony, you’re dead.</p>\n<p>According to evolutionary biologist <strong>Neil Tsutsui </strong>and ecologist <strong>Mark Moffett</strong>, the flood plains of northern Argentina offer a clue as to how these ants came to dominate the planet. Because of the frequent flooding, the homeland of <em>Linepithema humile</em> is basically a bootcamp for badass ants. One day, a couple ants from one of these families of Argentine ants made their way onto a boat and landed in New Orleans in the late 1800s. Over the last century, these Argentine ants wreaked havoc across the southern U.S. and a significant chunk of coastal California.</p>\n<p>In fact,<strong> Melissa Thomas</strong>, an Australian entomologist, reveals that these Argentine ants are even more well-heeled than we expected - they've made to every continent except Antarctica. No matter how many thousands of miles separate individual ants, when researchers place two of them together - whether they're plucked from Australia, Japan, Hawaii ... even Easter Island - they recognize each other as belonging to the same super-colony.</p>\n<p>But the really mind-blowing thing about these little guys is the surprising success of their us-versus-them death-dealing. Jad and Robert wrestle with what to make of this ant regime, whether it will last, and what, if anything, it might mean for other warlike organisms with global ambitions.</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p>Mark Moffett, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0520271289/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Adventures among Ants: A Global Safari with a Cast of Trillions</em></a></span></p>", "tease"=>"From a suburban sidewalk in southern California, Jad and Robert witness the carnage of a gruesome turf war. Though the tiny warriors doing battle clock in at just a fraction of an inc...", "publishTime"=>1343700000000, "publish-at"=>"2012-07-30T22:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1343700000, "estimated-duration"=>1147, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Drawing of an Argentinte Ant", "name"=>"photologue/photos/AntsTout.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"WNYC"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/275/h/80/photologue/photos/AntsTout.jpg", "h"=>275, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/AntsTout.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>78709, "credits-name"=>"Adam Cole"}, "objectID"=>"226523", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Argentine Invasion", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>From a suburban sidewalk in southern California, Jad and Robert witness the carnage of a gruesome turf war. Though the tiny warriors doing battle clock in at just a fraction of an inch, they have evolved a surprising, successful, and rather unsettling strategy of ironclad loyalty, absolute intolerance, and brutal violence.</p><p><strong>David Holway</strong>, an ecologist and evolutionary biologist from UC San Diego, takes us to a driveway in Escondido, California where a grisly battle rages. In this quiet suburban spot, two groups of ants are putting on a chilling display of dismemberment and death. According to David, this battle line marks the edge of an enormous super-colony of Argentine ants. Think of that anthill in your backyard, and stretch it out across five continents.</p>\n<p>Argentine ants are not good neighbors. When they meet ants from another colony, any other colony, they fight to the death, and tear the other ants to pieces. While other kinds of ants sometimes take slaves or even have sex with ants from different colonies, the Argentine ants don’t fool around. If you’re not part of the colony, you’re dead.</p>\n<p>According to evolutionary biologist <strong>Neil Tsutsui </strong>and ecologist <strong>Mark Moffett</strong>, the flood plains of northern Argentina offer a clue as to how these ants came to dominate the planet. Because of the frequent flooding, the homeland of <em>Linepithema humile</em> is basically a bootcamp for badass ants. One day, a couple ants from one of these families of Argentine ants made their way onto a boat and landed in New Orleans in the late 1800s. Over the last century, these Argentine ants wreaked havoc across the southern U.S. and a significant chunk of coastal California.</p>\n<p>In fact,<strong> Melissa Thomas</strong>, an Australian entomologist, reveals that these Argentine ants are even more well-heeled than we expected - they've made to every continent except Antarctica. No matter how many thousands of miles separate individual ants, when researchers place two of them together - whether they're plucked from Australia, Japan, Hawaii ... even Easter Island - they recognize each other as belonging to the same super-colony.</p>\n<p>But the really mind-blowing thing about these little guys is the surprising success of their us-versus-them death-dealing. Jad and Robert wrestle with what to make of this ant regime, whether it will last, and what, if anything, it might mean for other warlike organisms with global ambitions.</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p>Mark Moffett, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0520271289/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Adventures among Ants: A Global Safari with a Cast of Trillions</em></a></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-07-30T22:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"223276-double-blasted", "title"=>"Double Blasted", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12gamma.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=223276", "description"=>"<p>In early August of 1945, Tsutomu Yamaguchi had a run of the worst luck imaginable. A double blast of radiation left his future, and the future of his descendants, in doubt. In this short: an utterly amazing survival story that spans ... well, 4 billion years when you get down to it.</p><p>On the morning of August 6th, 1945, Tsutomu Yamaguchi was in Hiroshima on a work trip. He was walking to the office when the first atomic bomb was dropped about a mile away. He survived, and eventually managed to get himself onto a train back to his hometown ... Nagasaki. The very next morning, as he tried to convince his boss that a single bomb could destroy a whole city, the second bomb dropped. Sam Kean, whose latest book <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0316182311/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>The Violinist's Thumb</em></a></span> scrutinizes the mysteries of our genetic code, tells Jad and Robert the incredible story of what happened to Tsutomu, explains how gamma rays shred DNA, and helps us understand how Tsutomu sidestepped a thousand year curse.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"In early August of 1945, Tsutomu Yamaguchi had a run of the worst luck imaginable. A double blast of radiation left his future, and the future of his descendants, in doubt. In this sh...", "publishTime"=>1342479600000, "publish-at"=>"2012-07-16T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1342479600, "estimated-duration"=>1166, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Illustration: a special enzyme encircles the double helix to repair a broken strand of DNA. ", "name"=>"photologue/photos/DNA_double_trouble.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://images.nigms.nih.gov/index.cfm", "name"=>"NIGMS Image Gallery"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/500/642/c/80/photologue/photos/DNA_double_trouble.jpg", "h"=>642, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Illustration: a special enzyme encircles the double helix to repair a broken strand of DNA. ", "credits-url"=>"http://images.nigms.nih.gov/index.cfm?event=viewDetail&imageID=2330", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/DNA_double_trouble.jpg", "w"=>500, "id"=>77991, "credits-name"=>"Tom Ellenberger, Washington University School of Medicine"}, "objectID"=>"223276", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Double Blasted", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In early August of 1945, Tsutomu Yamaguchi had a run of the worst luck imaginable. A double blast of radiation left his future, and the future of his descendants, in doubt. In this short: an utterly amazing survival story that spans ... well, 4 billion years when you get down to it.</p><p>On the morning of August 6th, 1945, Tsutomu Yamaguchi was in Hiroshima on a work trip. He was walking to the office when the first atomic bomb was dropped about a mile away. He survived, and eventually managed to get himself onto a train back to his hometown ... Nagasaki. The very next morning, as he tried to convince his boss that a single bomb could destroy a whole city, the second bomb dropped. Sam Kean, whose latest book <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0316182311/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>The Violinist's Thumb</em></a></span> scrutinizes the mysteries of our genetic code, tells Jad and Robert the incredible story of what happened to Tsutomu, explains how gamma rays shred DNA, and helps us understand how Tsutomu sidestepped a thousand year curse.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-07-16T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"220080-radiolab-remixed", "title"=>"Radiolab Remixed", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12remix.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=220080", "description"=>"<p>Turning ideas into radio is one of the most exciting, frustrating, rewarding, and insanely fun things there is. Which got us thinking--why not ask <em>you</em> to join in on the fun? So we teamed up with <a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Indaba</a> for our first-ever remix competition. And now we get to play the winners.</p>\n<p> </p><p>A few weeks ago, <a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/opportunities/wnyc-radiolab-remix-contest\" target=\"_blank\">we put the raw materials from a few favorite pieces up on Indaba</a>, and asked listeners to have at it in a no-holds-barred audio throwdown. 136 brave contenders jumped into the mix, and made some really exciting, really impressive, new stuff. (You might notice that our sonic ID--the voices and noises at the beginning of every show--was tweaked a little bit. That's the work of one of our remixers, <strong><a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/people/943214957\" target=\"_blank\">aleatorictelevision</a></strong>.)</p>\n<p>In this podcast, Jad and Robert share a couple of their favorites. First up: grand prize winner <strong><a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/people/davidminnick\" target=\"_blank\">David Minnick</a></strong> zeroes in on the pitch patterns of Jeffrey Tambor's reading of \"Sum\" (from our short<strong> </strong><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blog/2009/aug/13/15-sum/\" target=\"_blank\">15: Sum</a>) to create a musical reckoning of what life after death could look like. Then Jad admits his love of techno and annoys Robert with a remix by <strong><a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/people/sonicemblem\" target=\"_blank\">Sonic Emblem</a></strong>.</p>\n<p>Then, one of our favorite runners up--<a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/people/JeffBarr\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Jeff Barr</strong></a>, editor of a podcast called <a href=\"http://risk-show.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Risk</a>--mines the show for unexpected moments...like coins flipping and voices falling...that morph into a melody of their own.</p>\n<p>And finally, we end with a director's choice, from <strong><a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/people/sketchlightly\" target=\"_blank\">sketch lightly &amp; daimyo</a></strong>, that completely re-imagines a story we did on <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blog/2010/nov/29/vertigo/\">vertigo</a>--by turning our telling of a real-life medical mystery into a deeply personal meditation on love.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Turning ideas into radio is one of the most exciting, frustrating, rewarding, and insanely fun things there is. Which got us thinking--why not ask you to join in on the fun? So we tea...", "publishTime"=>1341270000000, "publish-at"=>"2012-07-02T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1341270000, "estimated-duration"=>1505, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Spinning record", "name"=>"photologue/photos/remix_record.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/h/80/photologue/photos/remix_record.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/ivanzuber/2776100984/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/remix_record.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>77328, "credits-name"=>"Ivan Zuber"}, "objectID"=>"220080", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Remixed", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Turning ideas into radio is one of the most exciting, frustrating, rewarding, and insanely fun things there is. Which got us thinking--why not ask <em>you</em> to join in on the fun? So we teamed up with <a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Indaba</a> for our first-ever remix competition. And now we get to play the winners.</p>\n<p> </p><p>A few weeks ago, <a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/opportunities/wnyc-radiolab-remix-contest\" target=\"_blank\">we put the raw materials from a few favorite pieces up on Indaba</a>, and asked listeners to have at it in a no-holds-barred audio throwdown. 136 brave contenders jumped into the mix, and made some really exciting, really impressive, new stuff. (You might notice that our sonic ID--the voices and noises at the beginning of every show--was tweaked a little bit. That's the work of one of our remixers, <strong><a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/people/943214957\" target=\"_blank\">aleatorictelevision</a></strong>.)</p>\n<p>In this podcast, Jad and Robert share a couple of their favorites. First up: grand prize winner <strong><a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/people/davidminnick\" target=\"_blank\">David Minnick</a></strong> zeroes in on the pitch patterns of Jeffrey Tambor's reading of \"Sum\" (from our short<strong> </strong><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blog/2009/aug/13/15-sum/\" target=\"_blank\">15: Sum</a>) to create a musical reckoning of what life after death could look like. Then Jad admits his love of techno and annoys Robert with a remix by <strong><a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/people/sonicemblem\" target=\"_blank\">Sonic Emblem</a></strong>.</p>\n<p>Then, one of our favorite runners up--<a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/people/JeffBarr\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Jeff Barr</strong></a>, editor of a podcast called <a href=\"http://risk-show.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Risk</a>--mines the show for unexpected moments...like coins flipping and voices falling...that morph into a melody of their own.</p>\n<p>And finally, we end with a director's choice, from <strong><a href=\"http://www.indabamusic.com/people/sketchlightly\" target=\"_blank\">sketch lightly &amp; daimyo</a></strong>, that completely re-imagines a story we did on <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/blogs/radiolab-blog/2010/nov/29/vertigo/\">vertigo</a>--by turning our telling of a real-life medical mystery into a deeply personal meditation on love.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-07-02T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"214366-grumpy-old-terrorists", "title"=>"Grumpy Old Terrorists", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12threat.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=214366", "description"=>"<p>While working on <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2012/jan/09/\">The Bad Show</a>,  producer <strong><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/people/pat-walters/\">Pat Walters</a></strong> ran across some recordings that spooked  him--partly because they seemed like they had to be a big joke ... and  partly because, at the same time, they sounded so deadly serious. In this  short, Jad &amp; Robert try to decide how to feel.</p><p>Things can get really murky when you try to fix a clear line between empty threats and concrete criminal plans. And that uncertainty is precisely what makes this story feel so unnerving on the one hand, and weirdly ridiculous on the other.</p>\n<p>We begin with <strong>Tom Junod</strong>, a writer for <em>Esquire</em>, who tells us about a headline story that caught his eye back in November, 2011. As Tom explains, four men had been caught on tape trying to buy explosives to blow up federal office buildings in Atlanta, Georgia. But what struck Tom most wasn't what the men were plotting--it was something unusual about the men themselves: they were senior citizens, all over 60, and they'd been caught after meeting (among other places) at neighborhood chains like Waffle House and Shoney's.</p>\n<p>We're left wondering how seriously to take these guys--are they really would-be terrorists, or just trash-talking senior citizens? US Attorney <strong>Sally Yates</strong> weighs in, and <strong>Dina Temple-Raston</strong>, counterterrorism correspondent for NPR, tries to help us get our bearings, but in the end, we're left with an unsettling question: does catching men like this really make us feel any safer?</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p>Tom Junod's <em>Esquire</em> article \"<a href=\"http://www.esquire.com/features/waffle-house-terrorists-0212\" target=\"_blank\">Counter-Terrorism Is Getting Complicated</a>\"</p>", "tease"=>"While working on The Bad Show,  producer Pat Walters ran across some recordings that spooked  him--partly because they seemed like they had to be a big joke ... and  partly because, a...", "publishTime"=>1338850800000, "publish-at"=>"2012-06-04T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1338850800, "estimated-duration"=>1155, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Man drinking coffee in diner", "name"=>"photologue/photos/coffee_man.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/500/332/c/80/photologue/photos/coffee_man.jpg", "h"=>332, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Man drinking coffee in diner", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/ashleycampbellphotography/5336910533/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/coffee_man.jpg", "w"=>500, "id"=>75935, "credits-name"=>"Adapted from photo by Ashley Campbell Photography"}, "objectID"=>"214366", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Grumpy Old Terrorists", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>While working on <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2012/jan/09/\">The Bad Show</a>,  producer <strong><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/people/pat-walters/\">Pat Walters</a></strong> ran across some recordings that spooked  him--partly because they seemed like they had to be a big joke ... and  partly because, at the same time, they sounded so deadly serious. In this  short, Jad &amp; Robert try to decide how to feel.</p><p>Things can get really murky when you try to fix a clear line between empty threats and concrete criminal plans. And that uncertainty is precisely what makes this story feel so unnerving on the one hand, and weirdly ridiculous on the other.</p>\n<p>We begin with <strong>Tom Junod</strong>, a writer for <em>Esquire</em>, who tells us about a headline story that caught his eye back in November, 2011. As Tom explains, four men had been caught on tape trying to buy explosives to blow up federal office buildings in Atlanta, Georgia. But what struck Tom most wasn't what the men were plotting--it was something unusual about the men themselves: they were senior citizens, all over 60, and they'd been caught after meeting (among other places) at neighborhood chains like Waffle House and Shoney's.</p>\n<p>We're left wondering how seriously to take these guys--are they really would-be terrorists, or just trash-talking senior citizens? US Attorney <strong>Sally Yates</strong> weighs in, and <strong>Dina Temple-Raston</strong>, counterterrorism correspondent for NPR, tries to help us get our bearings, but in the end, we're left with an unsettling question: does catching men like this really make us feel any safer?</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p>Tom Junod's <em>Esquire</em> article \"<a href=\"http://www.esquire.com/features/waffle-house-terrorists-0212\" target=\"_blank\">Counter-Terrorism Is Getting Complicated</a>\"</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-06-04T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"211119-colors", "title"=>"Colors", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab052112.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=211119", "description"=>"<p>Our world is saturated in color, from soft hues to violent stains. How does something so intangible pack such a visceral punch? This hour, in the name of science <em>and </em>poetry, Jad and Robert tear the rainbow to pieces.</p>\n<p> </p><p>To what extent is color a physical thing in the physical world, and to what extent is it created in our minds? We start with Sir Isaac Newton, who was so eager to solve this very mystery, he stuck a knife in his eye to pinpoint the answer. Then, we meet a sea creature that sees a rainbow way beyond anything humans can experience, and we track down a woman who we're pretty sure can see thousands (maybe even millions) more colors than the rest of us. And we end with an age-old question, that, it turns out, never even occurred to most humans until very recently: why is the sky blue?</p>", "tease"=>"Radiolab rips the rainbow a new one.", "publishTime"=>1337641200000, "publish-at"=>"2012-05-21T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1337616000, "estimated-duration"=>3951, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Dark side of the eye", "name"=>"photologue/photos/RL_Colors_620_no_title.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"WNYC"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/227/l/80/photologue/photos/RL_Colors_620_no_title.jpg", "h"=>227, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/RL_Colors_620_no_title.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>75125, "credits-name"=>"Adam Cole"}, "objectID"=>"211119", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Colors", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Our world is saturated in color, from soft hues to violent stains. How does something so intangible pack such a visceral punch? This hour, in the name of science <em>and </em>poetry, Jad and Robert tear the rainbow to pieces.</p>\n<p> </p><p>To what extent is color a physical thing in the physical world, and to what extent is it created in our minds? We start with Sir Isaac Newton, who was so eager to solve this very mystery, he stuck a knife in his eye to pinpoint the answer. Then, we meet a sea creature that sees a rainbow way beyond anything humans can experience, and we track down a woman who we're pretty sure can see thousands (maybe even millions) more colors than the rest of us. And we end with an age-old question, that, it turns out, never even occurred to most humans until very recently: why is the sky blue?</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-05-21T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"209271-colors-sneak-peek", "title"=>"Colors Sneak Peek", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12fromla.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=209271", "description"=>"<p>Just before the curtain went up on our live show in Los Angeles, Jad and Robert carved out a little stage time for a sneak peek at next week's Colors episode.</p><p>We're so excited about our upcoming Colors show--an hour devoted to the mysteries of rainbows, wavelengths, and perception--that we wanted to do something a little unusual. Something we've never done before. We decided to take on a musical experiment on a much bigger scale than anything we've ever tried.</p>\n<p>So we picked out a big batch of songs about colors (from Blue Monday, to Pink Moon, to Yellow Submarine), then we asked a passel of adventurous artists (from all over the spectrum--seriously, we've got indie rock, blue grass, funk, gamelan...) to record some technicolor covers.</p>\n<p>What came back has us seeing stars (in a really, really good way). So here's a taste--including a wonderfully trippy live version of Bein' Green by <strong>Reggie Watts</strong>. Stay tuned for Colors next week, and if you want to <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/labpartners/\">help support Radiolab in the meantime <em>and</em> get access to mp3 downloads of some of these covers</a>, check out our new digital membership program Lab Partners. Thanks everybody!</p>", "tease"=>"Just before the curtain went up on our live show in Los Angeles, Jad and Robert carved out a little stage time for a sneak peek at next week's Colors episode.", "publishTime"=>1337036400000, "publish-at"=>"2012-05-14T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1337036400, "estimated-duration"=>596, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Jad and Robert get ready to take the stage", "name"=>"photologue/photos/radiolab_berkeley-6349_JRwings_620x372.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/radiolab_berkeley-6349_JRwings_620x372.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/radiolab_berkeley-6349_JRwings_620x372.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>74745, "credits-name"=>"Jared Kelly"}, "objectID"=>"209271", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Colors Sneak Peek", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Just before the curtain went up on our live show in Los Angeles, Jad and Robert carved out a little stage time for a sneak peek at next week's Colors episode.</p><p>We're so excited about our upcoming Colors show--an hour devoted to the mysteries of rainbows, wavelengths, and perception--that we wanted to do something a little unusual. Something we've never done before. We decided to take on a musical experiment on a much bigger scale than anything we've ever tried.</p>\n<p>So we picked out a big batch of songs about colors (from Blue Monday, to Pink Moon, to Yellow Submarine), then we asked a passel of adventurous artists (from all over the spectrum--seriously, we've got indie rock, blue grass, funk, gamelan...) to record some technicolor covers.</p>\n<p>What came back has us seeing stars (in a really, really good way). So here's a taste--including a wonderfully trippy live version of Bein' Green by <strong>Reggie Watts</strong>. Stay tuned for Colors next week, and if you want to <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/labpartners/\">help support Radiolab in the meantime <em>and</em> get access to mp3 downloads of some of these covers</a>, check out our new digital membership program Lab Partners. Thanks everybody!</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-05-14T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"205334-fetal-consequences", "title"=>"Fetal Consequences", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12fetal.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=205334", "description"=>"<p>Mother's day is nigh. Sort of. Anyway, without knowing it, you might have already given your mom a pretty lasting gift. But whether it helps or hurts her, or both, is still an open question. In this Radiolab short, Robert updates us on the science of fetal cells -- one of the first topics he <a href=\"http://www.npr.org/templates/story/story.php?storyId=5195551\" target=\"_parent\">covered</a> as an NPR science correspondent.</p><p>Six years ago, wearing his NPR science-correspondent hat, Robert presented listeners with this question: what if we told you that legions of fetal cells hang out inside a mother for decades after she gives birth -- and might even help heal her when she's sick or hurt? Back then he described this as a \"too dangerously beautiful idea\" for the scientists researching fetal cells. They wanted to believe it, but the evidence wasn't there yet. One of those scientists was Kirby Johnson at Tufts University, who explained that the cells might also <em>hurt</em> the mother. He wasn't sure which. \"I think that that's something that we're going to see within the next five years or less,\" Kirby said. So, Robert thought it was high-time to call Kirby for an update, and to ask once again about Kirby's personal stake in the work he's doing.</p>", "tease"=>"Mother's day is nigh. Sort of. Anyway, without knowing it, you might have already given your mom a pretty lasting gift. But whether it helps or hurts her, or both, is still an open qu...", "publishTime"=>1335834000000, "publish-at"=>"2012-04-30T21:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1335834000, "estimated-duration"=>1132, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Infant hand", "name"=>"photologue/photos/kirby_1.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/439/372/l/80/photologue/photos/kirby_1.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/danielledeleon/2923176790/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/kirby_1.jpg", "w"=>439, "id"=>73719, "credits-name"=>"Dannerzz"}, "objectID"=>"205334", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Fetal Consequences", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Mother's day is nigh. Sort of. Anyway, without knowing it, you might have already given your mom a pretty lasting gift. But whether it helps or hurts her, or both, is still an open question. In this Radiolab short, Robert updates us on the science of fetal cells -- one of the first topics he <a href=\"http://www.npr.org/templates/story/story.php?storyId=5195551\" target=\"_parent\">covered</a> as an NPR science correspondent.</p><p>Six years ago, wearing his NPR science-correspondent hat, Robert presented listeners with this question: what if we told you that legions of fetal cells hang out inside a mother for decades after she gives birth -- and might even help heal her when she's sick or hurt? Back then he described this as a \"too dangerously beautiful idea\" for the scientists researching fetal cells. They wanted to believe it, but the evidence wasn't there yet. One of those scientists was Kirby Johnson at Tufts University, who explained that the cells might also <em>hurt</em> the mother. He wasn't sure which. \"I think that that's something that we're going to see within the next five years or less,\" Kirby said. So, Robert thought it was high-time to call Kirby for an update, and to ask once again about Kirby's personal stake in the work he's doing.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-04-30T21:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"199705-crossroads", "title"=>"Crossroads", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12crossroads.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=199705", "description"=>"<p>In this short, we go looking for the devil, and find ourselves tangled in a web of details surrounding one of the most haunting figures in music--a legendary guitarist whose shadowy life spawned a legend so powerful, it's still being repeated...even by fans who don't believe a word of it.</p><p>For years and years, Jad's been fascinated by the myth of what happened to Robert Johnson at the crossroads in Clarksdale, Mississippi. The story goes like this: back in the 1920s, Robert Johnson wanted to play the blues. But he really sucked. He sucked so much, that everyone who heard him told him to get lost. So he did. He disappeared for a little while, and when he came back, he was <em>different.</em> His music was startling--and musicians who'd laughed at him before now wanted to know how he did it. And according to the now-famous legend, Johnson had a simple answer: he went out to the crossroads just before midnight, and when the devil offered to tune his guitar in exchange for his soul, he took the deal.</p>\n<p>Producer Pat Walters bravely escorts Jad to the scene of the supposed crime, in the middle of the night in the Mississippi Delta, to try to track down some shred of truth to all this. Not because they really thought something spooky would actually happen, but because deep down, there's a part of this story that--as much as the facts fall apart--still feels kind of true. </p>\n<p>To help us get close to the real human behind the tall tales, we talk to Robert Johnson experts <strong>Tom Graves</strong>, <strong>Elijah Wald</strong>, <strong>David Evans</strong>, and <strong>Robert “Mack” McCormick</strong>. And we hear, posthumously, from <strong>Ledell Johnson</strong>...a man of no relation to Robert, who unintentionally helped the world fall for a blues-imbued ghost story. </p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p>Tom Graves, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0981600212/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Crossroads: The Life and Afterlife of Blues Legend Robert Johnson</em></a></span></p>\n<p>Elijah Wald, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0060524235/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Escaping the Delta: Robert Johnson and the Invention of the Blues</em></a></span></p>\n<p>David Evans, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0289701511/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Tommy Johnson</em></a></span></p>\n<p>Peter Guralnick, <em><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0452279496/radiolabbooks-20/\">Searching for Robert Johnson<br></a></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"In this short, we go looking for the devil, and find ourselves tangled in a web of details surrounding one of the most haunting figures in music--a legendary guitarist whose shadowy l...", "publishTime"=>1334617200000, "publish-at"=>"2012-04-16T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1334617200, "estimated-duration"=>1738, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Crossroad at night", "name"=>"photologue/photos/crossroads_1.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/276/h/80/photologue/photos/crossroads_1.jpg", "h"=>276, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"Crossroad at night", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/eioua/4382335688/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/crossroads_1.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>72963, "credits-name"=>"eioua"}, "objectID"=>"199705", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Crossroads", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this short, we go looking for the devil, and find ourselves tangled in a web of details surrounding one of the most haunting figures in music--a legendary guitarist whose shadowy life spawned a legend so powerful, it's still being repeated...even by fans who don't believe a word of it.</p><p>For years and years, Jad's been fascinated by the myth of what happened to Robert Johnson at the crossroads in Clarksdale, Mississippi. The story goes like this: back in the 1920s, Robert Johnson wanted to play the blues. But he really sucked. He sucked so much, that everyone who heard him told him to get lost. So he did. He disappeared for a little while, and when he came back, he was <em>different.</em> His music was startling--and musicians who'd laughed at him before now wanted to know how he did it. And according to the now-famous legend, Johnson had a simple answer: he went out to the crossroads just before midnight, and when the devil offered to tune his guitar in exchange for his soul, he took the deal.</p>\n<p>Producer Pat Walters bravely escorts Jad to the scene of the supposed crime, in the middle of the night in the Mississippi Delta, to try to track down some shred of truth to all this. Not because they really thought something spooky would actually happen, but because deep down, there's a part of this story that--as much as the facts fall apart--still feels kind of true. </p>\n<p>To help us get close to the real human behind the tall tales, we talk to Robert Johnson experts <strong>Tom Graves</strong>, <strong>Elijah Wald</strong>, <strong>David Evans</strong>, and <strong>Robert “Mack” McCormick</strong>. And we hear, posthumously, from <strong>Ledell Johnson</strong>...a man of no relation to Robert, who unintentionally helped the world fall for a blues-imbued ghost story. </p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p>Tom Graves, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0981600212/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Crossroads: The Life and Afterlife of Blues Legend Robert Johnson</em></a></span></p>\n<p>Elijah Wald, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0060524235/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Escaping the Delta: Robert Johnson and the Invention of the Blues</em></a></span></p>\n<p>David Evans, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0289701511/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Tommy Johnson</em></a></span></p>\n<p>Peter Guralnick, <em><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0452279496/radiolabbooks-20/\">Searching for Robert Johnson<br></a></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-04-16T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"197112-guts", "title"=>"Guts", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab040212.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=197112", "description"=>"<p>This hour, we dive into the messy mystery in the middle of us. What's going on down there? And what can the rumblings deep in our bellies tell us about ourselves?</p><p>We stick our hand in a cow stomach, get a window into our core (thanks to a hunter who became a walking science experiment in the 1800s), and listen in on the surprising back-and-forth between our gut and our brain. And we talk to a man who kind of went out of his mind when a medical procedure left him (for a little while) gutless.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this piece implied that subjects in the human probiotics study were given a larger dose of probiotics than they actually were. We also compared the results of the study to the effect of giving people anti-anxiety medication. The subjects did experience a significant positive mood change, but the researchers did not compare that result to the effect of anti-anxiety medication. The audio has been adjusted to reflect these facts.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>For transcripts, see individual segment pages.</em></p>", "tease"=>"A look at the messy mystery in our middles, and what the rumblings deep in our bellies can tell us about ourselves.", "publishTime"=>1333407600000, "publish-at"=>"2012-04-02T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1333382400, "estimated-duration"=>3306, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Guts", "name"=>"photologue/photos/guts_620_web.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/227/h/80/photologue/photos/guts_620_web.jpg", "h"=>227, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"Guts", "credits-url"=>"http://www.subverbo.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/guts_620_web.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>72177, "credits-name"=>"Charles Michelet"}, "objectID"=>"197112", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Guts", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This hour, we dive into the messy mystery in the middle of us. What's going on down there? And what can the rumblings deep in our bellies tell us about ourselves?</p><p>We stick our hand in a cow stomach, get a window into our core (thanks to a hunter who became a walking science experiment in the 1800s), and listen in on the surprising back-and-forth between our gut and our brain. And we talk to a man who kind of went out of his mind when a medical procedure left him (for a little while) gutless.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this piece implied that subjects in the human probiotics study were given a larger dose of probiotics than they actually were. We also compared the results of the study to the effect of giving people anti-anxiety medication. The subjects did experience a significant positive mood change, but the researchers did not compare that result to the effect of anti-anxiety medication. The audio has been adjusted to reflect these facts.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>For transcripts, see individual segment pages.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-04-02T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"193037-turing-problem", "title"=>"The Turing Problem ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12turing.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=193037", "description"=>"<p>100 years ago this year, the man who first conceived of the computer age was born. His name was Alan Turing. He was also a math genius, a hero of World War II and he is widely considered to be the father of artificial intelligence. But the world wasn't kind to Alan Turing. In 1952, he was arrested and convicted under a British law that prohibited \"acts of gross indecency between men, in public or private.\"</p>\n<p> </p><p>In 1936, a young Alan Turing devised a machine that would ultimately change the world. You're staring at it right now--except Turing's \"universal machine\" was much, much simpler and totally imaginary. Nonetheless, he proved that with just a few simple ingredients, the machine could compute any mathematical problem that a human could compute.</p>\n<p>You: So?</p>\n<p>Us: So this was back when the only \"computers\" were <em>people</em> doing math by hand. It was also back when machines were single-function. \"Reprogramming\" required a screwdriver. To think the kinds of thoughts Turing was thinking, you had to be either a genius or a psychic.</p>\n<p>Turing lived his whole life with machines. He built the machine that deciphered the German \"Engima Code\" during World War II. He imagined a day when machines would flirt with us, joke with us, listen to our problems and, above all, think for themselves. He even thought up a way to test whether machines had become indistinguishable from humans (for more on the Turing Test, check out our episode <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2011/may/31/\">Talking to Machines</a>).</p>\n<p>The idea that machines would become our equals was unsettling for many of Turing's peers. (Frankly, it still unsettles a lot of folks today, just ask Robert!) But to Turing, it was just the natural extension of his fundamental belief that we, all of us, are machines ourselves in a way. And he worried that society might judge the computers of the future as harshly as it judged him.</p>\n<p>Alan Turing was arrested and convicted in 1952 for activities that are no longer illegal in England. <strong>Janna Levin</strong> and <strong>David Leavitt</strong> help explain how Alan Turing's personal life may have shaped his relationship to machines. And <strong>James Gleick</strong> muses about how profound Turing's contributions to math and computing really were.</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p>Janna Levin, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1400032407/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>A Madman Dreams of Turing Machines</em></a></span></p>\n<p>David Leavitt, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0739471953/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>The Man Who Knew Too Much: Alan Turing and the Invention of the Computer</em></a></span></p>\n<p>James Gleick, <span class=\"book\"><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1400096235/wnycorg-20/\"><em>The Information: A History, A Theory, A Flood</em></a></span></span></p>", "tease"=>"Alan Turing's mental leaps about machines and computers were some of the  most innovative ideas of the 20th century. But the world wasn't kind to  him. In this short, Robert wonders h...", "publishTime"=>1332198000000, "publish-at"=>"2012-03-19T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1332198000, "estimated-duration"=>1380, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Alan Turing, sculpted by Stephen Kettle", "name"=>"photologue/photos/turing.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/481/373/c/80/photologue/photos/turing.jpg", "h"=>373, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Alan Turing, sculpted by Stephen Kettle", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/ell-r-brown/5118274331/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/turing.jpg", "w"=>481, "id"=>71480, "credits-name"=>"ell brown"}, "objectID"=>"193037", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Turing Problem ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>100 years ago this year, the man who first conceived of the computer age was born. His name was Alan Turing. He was also a math genius, a hero of World War II and he is widely considered to be the father of artificial intelligence. But the world wasn't kind to Alan Turing. In 1952, he was arrested and convicted under a British law that prohibited \"acts of gross indecency between men, in public or private.\"</p>\n<p> </p><p>In 1936, a young Alan Turing devised a machine that would ultimately change the world. You're staring at it right now--except Turing's \"universal machine\" was much, much simpler and totally imaginary. Nonetheless, he proved that with just a few simple ingredients, the machine could compute any mathematical problem that a human could compute.</p>\n<p>You: So?</p>\n<p>Us: So this was back when the only \"computers\" were <em>people</em> doing math by hand. It was also back when machines were single-function. \"Reprogramming\" required a screwdriver. To think the kinds of thoughts Turing was thinking, you had to be either a genius or a psychic.</p>\n<p>Turing lived his whole life with machines. He built the machine that deciphered the German \"Engima Code\" during World War II. He imagined a day when machines would flirt with us, joke with us, listen to our problems and, above all, think for themselves. He even thought up a way to test whether machines had become indistinguishable from humans (for more on the Turing Test, check out our episode <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2011/may/31/\">Talking to Machines</a>).</p>\n<p>The idea that machines would become our equals was unsettling for many of Turing's peers. (Frankly, it still unsettles a lot of folks today, just ask Robert!) But to Turing, it was just the natural extension of his fundamental belief that we, all of us, are machines ourselves in a way. And he worried that society might judge the computers of the future as harshly as it judged him.</p>\n<p>Alan Turing was arrested and convicted in 1952 for activities that are no longer illegal in England. <strong>Janna Levin</strong> and <strong>David Leavitt</strong> help explain how Alan Turing's personal life may have shaped his relationship to machines. And <strong>James Gleick</strong> muses about how profound Turing's contributions to math and computing really were.</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p>Janna Levin, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1400032407/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>A Madman Dreams of Turing Machines</em></a></span></p>\n<p>David Leavitt, <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0739471953/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>The Man Who Knew Too Much: Alan Turing and the Invention of the Computer</em></a></span></p>\n<p>James Gleick, <span class=\"book\"><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/1400096235/wnycorg-20/\"><em>The Information: A History, A Theory, A Flood</em></a></span></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-03-19T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"190284-war-we-need", "title"=>"A War We Need", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12cocco.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=190284", "description"=>"<p>Every day, every moment, an epic battle is raging across the globe. It's happening in the ocean. And the evidence is both highly visible and totally hidden, depending on your perspective. In this short, the tale of an arms race involving trillions of sea creatures--and why their struggle is vital to our survival.</p><p>Reporter <strong>Ari Daniel</strong> visits with <a href=\"http://www.bigelow.org/research/srs/william_h_wilson/\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Willie Wilson</strong></a>, who studies phytoplankton--aka microscopic plant-like creatures--at Bigelow Laboratory in Maine. There's a war in Willie's test tubes. A certain sort of phytoplankton known as coccolithophores are engaged in a surprisingly complicated arms race with deadly viruses. A virus is problematic enough when you're a human. Now imagine being a single-cell plant and mixing it up with the hugest virus you've ever seen. The coccos (as we've taken to calling them) are outgunned, but they won't go down with out a serious fight.</p>\n<p>Ari and Willie explain how our itsy-bitsy heroes take arms against (literally) a sea of troubles--and how this battle, and others like it, make life on Earth possible.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Every day, every moment, an epic battle is raging across the globe. It's happening in the ocean. And the evidence is both highly visible and totally hidden, depending on your perspect...", "publishTime"=>1330992000000, "publish-at"=>"2012-03-05T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1330992000, "estimated-duration"=>657, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Coccolithophore bloom off Brittany, France", "name"=>"photologue/photos/cocco.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.nasa.gov/", "name"=>"NASA"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/372/c/80/photologue/photos/cocco.jpg", "h"=>372, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Coccolithophore bloom off Brittany, France", "credits-url"=>"http://visibleearth.nasa.gov/view.php?id=71344", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/cocco.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>70698, "credits-name"=>"Jacques Descloitres, MODIS Rapid Response Team, NASA/GSFC"}, "objectID"=>"190284", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"A War We Need", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Every day, every moment, an epic battle is raging across the globe. It's happening in the ocean. And the evidence is both highly visible and totally hidden, depending on your perspective. In this short, the tale of an arms race involving trillions of sea creatures--and why their struggle is vital to our survival.</p><p>Reporter <strong>Ari Daniel</strong> visits with <a href=\"http://www.bigelow.org/research/srs/william_h_wilson/\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Willie Wilson</strong></a>, who studies phytoplankton--aka microscopic plant-like creatures--at Bigelow Laboratory in Maine. There's a war in Willie's test tubes. A certain sort of phytoplankton known as coccolithophores are engaged in a surprisingly complicated arms race with deadly viruses. A virus is problematic enough when you're a human. Now imagine being a single-cell plant and mixing it up with the hugest virus you've ever seen. The coccos (as we've taken to calling them) are outgunned, but they won't go down with out a serious fight.</p>\n<p>Ari and Willie explain how our itsy-bitsy heroes take arms against (literally) a sea of troubles--and how this battle, and others like it, make life on Earth possible.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-03-05T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"187571-escape", "title"=>"Escape!", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab022012.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=187571", "description"=>"<p>The walls are closing in, you've got no way out... and then, suddenly, you escape! This hour, stories about traps, getaways, perpetual cycles, and staggering breakthroughs.</p><p>We kick things off with a true escape artist--a man who’s broken out of jail more times than anyone alive. We try to figure out why he keeps running... and whether he will ever stop. Then, the ingeniously simple question that led Isaac Newton to an enormous intellectual breakthrough: why doesn’t the moon fall out of the sky? In the wake of Newton's new idea, we find ourselves in a strange space at the edge of the solar system, about to cross a boundary beyond which we know nothing. Finally, we hear the story of a blind kid who freed himself from an unhappy childhood by climbing into the telephone system, and bending it to his will.</p>", "tease"=>"Stories about traps and getaways ... about getting stuck, and breaking free.", "publishTime"=>1329782400000, "publish-at"=>"2012-02-20T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1329757200, "estimated-duration"=>3940, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Open bird cage", "name"=>"photologue/photos/escape_episode.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/620/c/80/photologue/photos/escape_episode.jpg", "h"=>620, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Open bird cage", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/jimmybrown/4528629903/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/escape_episode.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>70060, "credits-name"=>"jumpinjimmyjava"}, "objectID"=>"187571", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Escape!", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>The walls are closing in, you've got no way out... and then, suddenly, you escape! This hour, stories about traps, getaways, perpetual cycles, and staggering breakthroughs.</p><p>We kick things off with a true escape artist--a man who’s broken out of jail more times than anyone alive. We try to figure out why he keeps running... and whether he will ever stop. Then, the ingeniously simple question that led Isaac Newton to an enormous intellectual breakthrough: why doesn’t the moon fall out of the sky? In the wake of Newton's new idea, we find ourselves in a strange space at the edge of the solar system, about to cross a boundary beyond which we know nothing. Finally, we hear the story of a blind kid who freed himself from an unhappy childhood by climbing into the telephone system, and bending it to his will.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-02-20T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"185551-killer-empathy", "title"=>"Killer Empathy", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12empathy.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=185551", "description"=>"<p>Sometimes being a good scientist requires putting aside your emotions. But what happens when objectivity isn't enough to make sense of a seemingly senseless act of violence? In this short, Jad and Robert talk to an entomologist about the risks, and the rewards, of trying to see the world through someone else's eyes. </p><p><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/people/lulu-miller/\"><strong>Lulu Miller</strong></a> introduces us to <strong><a href=\"http://www.uwyo.edu/philosophy/faculty/lockwood.html\" target=\"_blank\">Jeff Lockwood</a></strong>, a professor at the University of Wyoming, who spent a part of his career studying a particularly ferocious set of insects: Gryllacrididae. Or, as Jeff describes them, \"crickets on steroids.\" They have crushingly strong, serrated jaws, and they launch all-out attacks on anyone who gets in their way--whether it's another cricket, or the guy trying to take them out of their cages.</p>\n<p>In order to work with the gryllacridids, Jeff had to figure out how to out-maneuver them. And as he devised ways to keep from getting slashed and bitten, he felt like he was getting to know them. Maybe they weren't just mindless brutes ... but their own creatures, each with their own sense of self. And that got him wondering: what could their fierceness tell him about the nature of violence? How well could he understand the minds of these insects, and what drove them to be so bloody?</p>\n<p>That's when the alarm bells went off. Jeff would picture his mentor, Dr. LaFage, lecturing him back in college--warning him not to slip into a muddled, empathic mood ... not to let his emotions sideswipe his objectivity. And that would usually do the trick--Jeff would think of LaFage, and rein himself back in.</p>\n<p>But then one night, something happened that gave Dr. LaFage's advice a terrible new kind of significance. <strong>Tamra Carboni </strong>tells us this part of the story, and challenges Jeff's belief that there's a way to understand it.</p>", "tease"=>"Killer Empathy", "publishTime"=>1328517660000, "publish-at"=>"2012-02-06T03:41:00", "date-line-ts"=>1328580000, "estimated-duration"=>1098, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Broken spectacles", "name"=>"photologue/photos/crickets.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/398/c/80/photologue/photos/crickets.jpg", "h"=>398, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Broken spectacles", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/josierichards/5592564151/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/crickets.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>69168, "credits-name"=>"josie lynn richards"}, "objectID"=>"185551", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Killer Empathy", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Sometimes being a good scientist requires putting aside your emotions. But what happens when objectivity isn't enough to make sense of a seemingly senseless act of violence? In this short, Jad and Robert talk to an entomologist about the risks, and the rewards, of trying to see the world through someone else's eyes. </p><p><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/people/lulu-miller/\"><strong>Lulu Miller</strong></a> introduces us to <strong><a href=\"http://www.uwyo.edu/philosophy/faculty/lockwood.html\" target=\"_blank\">Jeff Lockwood</a></strong>, a professor at the University of Wyoming, who spent a part of his career studying a particularly ferocious set of insects: Gryllacrididae. Or, as Jeff describes them, \"crickets on steroids.\" They have crushingly strong, serrated jaws, and they launch all-out attacks on anyone who gets in their way--whether it's another cricket, or the guy trying to take them out of their cages.</p>\n<p>In order to work with the gryllacridids, Jeff had to figure out how to out-maneuver them. And as he devised ways to keep from getting slashed and bitten, he felt like he was getting to know them. Maybe they weren't just mindless brutes ... but their own creatures, each with their own sense of self. And that got him wondering: what could their fierceness tell him about the nature of violence? How well could he understand the minds of these insects, and what drove them to be so bloody?</p>\n<p>That's when the alarm bells went off. Jeff would picture his mentor, Dr. LaFage, lecturing him back in college--warning him not to slip into a muddled, empathic mood ... not to let his emotions sideswipe his objectivity. And that would usually do the trick--Jeff would think of LaFage, and rein himself back in.</p>\n<p>But then one night, something happened that gave Dr. LaFage's advice a terrible new kind of significance. <strong>Tamra Carboni </strong>tells us this part of the story, and challenges Jeff's belief that there's a way to understand it.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-02-06T03:41:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"182747-wake-up-dream", "title"=>"Wake Up and Dream", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast12nightmare.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=182747", "description"=>"<p>In today's short, a man confronts a bully, and frees himself from a recurring nightmare that's terrorized him for more than 20 years.</p><p><strong>Matt Kielty</strong> introduces us to <strong><a href=\"http://stevevolk.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Steve Volk</a></strong>, a city reporter in Philadelphia who--for decades--was plagued by a recurring nightmare. It popped up whenever Steve was going through a stressful time, and it always played out exactly the same way. But no matter how self-aware Steve was about his most current set of anxieties, and no matter how hard he tried to rationalize and explain away the dream...he couldn't make it stop.</p>\n<p>Then one year, Steve started working on a book about topics at the edge of science, and along the way he stumbled into lucid dreaming. Pretty soon, Steve was reading through old sleep studies conducted by a scientist named Stephen LaBerge, and he was starting to wonder if lucid dreaming might not be so fringe-y after all. So he called up LaBerge's assistant and began training himself on a set of techniques that would eventually help him put his inner demon to bed.</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0061857718/radiolabbooks-20/\">Fringe-ology</a></span>, by Steve Volk</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Exploring-World-Dreaming-Stephen-LaBerge/dp/034537410X\" target=\"_blank\">Exploring the World of Lucid Dreaming</a>, by Stephen LaBerge</p>", "tease"=>"In today's short, a man confronts a bully, and frees himself from a recurring nightmare that's terrorized him for more than 20 years.", "publishTime"=>1327366800000, "publish-at"=>"2012-01-23T20:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1327366800, "estimated-duration"=>1010, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Man lurking at window", "name"=>"photologue/photos/nightmare.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/raw/photologue/photos/nightmare.jpg", "h"=>0, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Man lurking at window", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/cusegoyle/224037760/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/nightmare.jpg", "w"=>0, "id"=>68246, "credits-name"=>"mnapoleon"}, "objectID"=>"182747", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Wake Up and Dream", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In today's short, a man confronts a bully, and frees himself from a recurring nightmare that's terrorized him for more than 20 years.</p><p><strong>Matt Kielty</strong> introduces us to <strong><a href=\"http://stevevolk.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Steve Volk</a></strong>, a city reporter in Philadelphia who--for decades--was plagued by a recurring nightmare. It popped up whenever Steve was going through a stressful time, and it always played out exactly the same way. But no matter how self-aware Steve was about his most current set of anxieties, and no matter how hard he tried to rationalize and explain away the dream...he couldn't make it stop.</p>\n<p>Then one year, Steve started working on a book about topics at the edge of science, and along the way he stumbled into lucid dreaming. Pretty soon, Steve was reading through old sleep studies conducted by a scientist named Stephen LaBerge, and he was starting to wonder if lucid dreaming might not be so fringe-y after all. So he called up LaBerge's assistant and began training himself on a set of techniques that would eventually help him put his inner demon to bed.</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0061857718/radiolabbooks-20/\">Fringe-ology</a></span>, by Steve Volk</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Exploring-World-Dreaming-Stephen-LaBerge/dp/034537410X\" target=\"_blank\">Exploring the World of Lucid Dreaming</a>, by Stephen LaBerge</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2012-01-23T20:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"180092-the-bad-show", "title"=>"The Bad Show", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab010912.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=180092", "description"=>"<p>Cruelty, violence, badness... This episode of Radiolab, we wrestle with the dark side of human nature, and ask whether it's something we can ever really understand, or fully escape.</p>\n<p> </p><p>We begin with a chilling statistic: 91% of men, and 84% of women, have fantasized about killing someone. We take a look at one particular fantasy lurking behind these numbers, and wonder what this shadow world might tell us about ourselves and our neighbors. Then, we reconsider what Stanley Milgram's famous experiment really revealed about human nature (it's both better and worse than we thought). Next, we meet a man who scrambles our notions of good and evil: chemist Fritz Haber, who won a Nobel Prize in 1918...around the same time officials in the US were calling him a war criminal. And we end with the story of a man who chased one of the most prolific serial killers in US history, then got a chance to ask him the question that had haunted him for years: why?</p>", "tease"=>"We wrestle with the dark side of human nature, and ask whether it's something we can ever really understand, or fully escape.", "publishTime"=>1318514188000, "publish-at"=>"2011-10-13T09:56:28", "date-line-ts"=>1326128400, "estimated-duration"=>3919, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"The Bad Show", "name"=>"photologue/photos/badshow_reupload.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"WNYC"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/277/c/80/photologue/photos/badshow_reupload.jpg", "h"=>277, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/badshow_reupload.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>67638, "credits-name"=>"Adam Cole"}, "objectID"=>"180092", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Bad Show", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Cruelty, violence, badness... This episode of Radiolab, we wrestle with the dark side of human nature, and ask whether it's something we can ever really understand, or fully escape.</p>\n<p> </p><p>We begin with a chilling statistic: 91% of men, and 84% of women, have fantasized about killing someone. We take a look at one particular fantasy lurking behind these numbers, and wonder what this shadow world might tell us about ourselves and our neighbors. Then, we reconsider what Stanley Milgram's famous experiment really revealed about human nature (it's both better and worse than we thought). Next, we meet a man who scrambles our notions of good and evil: chemist Fritz Haber, who won a Nobel Prize in 1918...around the same time officials in the US were calling him a war criminal. And we end with the story of a man who chased one of the most prolific serial killers in US history, then got a chance to ask him the question that had haunted him for years: why?</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2011-10-13T09:56:28", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2013=>[{"slug"=>"times-they-are-changin", "title"=>"The Times They Are a-Changin'", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13timesachangin.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=339013", "description"=>"<p>At the start of this new year we crack open some fossils, peer back into ancient seas, and look up at lunar skies to find that a year is not quite as fixed as we thought it was.</p>\n<p> </p><p>With the help of paleontologist Neil Shubin, reporter Emily Graslie and the Field Museum's Paul Mayer we discover that our world is full of ancient coral calendars. Each one of these sea skeletons reveals that once upon a very-long-time-ago, years were shorter by over forty days. And astrophysicist Chis Impey helps us comprehend how the change is all to be blamed on a celestial slow dance with the moon. </p>\n<p>Plus, Robert indulges his curiosity about stopping time and counteracting the spinning of the spheres by taking astrophysicist Neil deGrasse Tyson on a (theoretical) trip to Venus with a rooster and sprinter Usain Bolt.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"With the help of paleontologist Neil Shubin, reporter Emily Graslie and the Field Museum's Paul Mayer we discover that our world is full of ancient coral calendars", "publishTime"=>1388442642000, "publish-at"=>"2013-12-30T17:30:42", "date-line-ts"=>1388442641, "estimated-duration"=>1215, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Radiolab_MoonCoral_V3.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/467/l/80/1/Radiolab_MoonCoral_V3.jpg", "h"=>467, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"coral moon by WNYC's Sahar Baharloo                          ", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Radiolab_MoonCoral_V3.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>109757, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"339013", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Times They Are a-Changin'", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>At the start of this new year we crack open some fossils, peer back into ancient seas, and look up at lunar skies to find that a year is not quite as fixed as we thought it was.</p>\n<p> </p><p>With the help of paleontologist Neil Shubin, reporter Emily Graslie and the Field Museum's Paul Mayer we discover that our world is full of ancient coral calendars. Each one of these sea skeletons reveals that once upon a very-long-time-ago, years were shorter by over forty days. And astrophysicist Chis Impey helps us comprehend how the change is all to be blamed on a celestial slow dance with the moon. </p>\n<p>Plus, Robert indulges his curiosity about stopping time and counteracting the spinning of the spheres by taking astrophysicist Neil deGrasse Tyson on a (theoretical) trip to Venus with a rooster and sprinter Usain Bolt.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-12-30T17:30:42", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"sex-ducks-and-founding-feud", "title"=>"Sex, Ducks, and The Founding Feud", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast121913sexducksfoundingfeud.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=337502", "description"=>"<p>Jilted lovers and disrupted duck hunts provide a very odd look into the soul of the US Constitution.</p><p>What does a jilted lover’s revenge have to do with an international chemical weapons treaty? More than you’d think. From poison and duck hunts to our feuding fathers, we step into a very odd tug of war between local and federal law.</p>\n<p>When Carol Anne Bond found out her husband had impregnated her best friend, she took revenge. Carol's particular flavor of revenge led to a US Supreme Court case that puts into question a part of the US treaty power. </p>\n<p>Producer Kelsey Padgett drags Jad and Robert into Carol's poisonous web, which starts them on a journey from the birth of the US Constitution, to a duck hunt in 1918, and back to the present day … it’s all about an ongoing argument that might actually be the very heart and soul of our system of government</p>\n<p><em>UPDATE: The Supreme Court made a decision in the Carol Anne Bond case during the summer of 2014. If you've listened to the piece (or don't mind a spoiler) <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/decision-kind/\">check out what our producer Kelsey Padgett had to say about the verdict</a>.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Jilted lovers and disrupted duck hunts provide a very odd look into the soul of the US Constitution.", "publishTime"=>1387492302000, "publish-at"=>"2013-12-19T17:31:42", "date-line-ts"=>1387492301, "estimated-duration"=>1313, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/duck_hunt.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/823/454/l/80/1/duck_hunt.jpg", "h"=>454, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/duck_hunt.jpg", "w"=>823, "id"=>109467, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"337502", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Sex, Ducks, and The Founding Feud", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Jilted lovers and disrupted duck hunts provide a very odd look into the soul of the US Constitution.</p><p>What does a jilted lover’s revenge have to do with an international chemical weapons treaty? More than you’d think. From poison and duck hunts to our feuding fathers, we step into a very odd tug of war between local and federal law.</p>\n<p>When Carol Anne Bond found out her husband had impregnated her best friend, she took revenge. Carol's particular flavor of revenge led to a US Supreme Court case that puts into question a part of the US treaty power. </p>\n<p>Producer Kelsey Padgett drags Jad and Robert into Carol's poisonous web, which starts them on a journey from the birth of the US Constitution, to a duck hunt in 1918, and back to the present day … it’s all about an ongoing argument that might actually be the very heart and soul of our system of government</p>\n<p><em>UPDATE: The Supreme Court made a decision in the Carol Anne Bond case during the summer of 2014. If you've listened to the piece (or don't mind a spoiler) <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/decision-kind/\">check out what our producer Kelsey Padgett had to say about the verdict</a>.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-12-19T17:31:42", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"video-radiolab-live-apocalyptical-sneak-peek", "title"=>"VIDEO: Radiolab Live Apocalyptical Sneak Peek", "audio"=>nil, "description"=>"<p>A preview of Radiolab's live show Apocalyptical: dinosaurs, death, destruction... plus cinematic live scoring and comedic mayhem from Reggie Watts and Kurt Braunohler. Feast your eyes on more video -- including a cut of the full show! -- at <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/live/\">radiolab.org/live</a>.</p><p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"349\" src=\"http://www.youtube.com/embed/Fp1JjldKCQg\" width=\"620\"></iframe></p>\n<p>Recorded live at Seattle's <span>Paramount Theatre on November 21st and 22nd.</span></p>", "tease"=>"A preview of Radiolab's live show Apocalyptical: dinosaurs, death, destruction... plus cinematic live scoring and comedic mayhem from Reggie Watts and Kurt Braunohler.", "publishTime"=>1386626700000, "publish-at"=>"2013-12-09T17:05:00", "date-line-ts"=>1386626700, "estimated-duration"=>0, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Stage shot from Radiolab Live Apocalyptical", "name"=>"1/Radiolab_apocalyptical_gun.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1280/708/h/80/1/Radiolab_apocalyptical_gun.png", "h"=>708, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Radiolab_apocalyptical_gun.png", "w"=>1280, "id"=>108816, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"335410", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"VIDEO: Radiolab Live Apocalyptical Sneak Peek", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>A preview of Radiolab's live show Apocalyptical: dinosaurs, death, destruction... plus cinematic live scoring and comedic mayhem from Reggie Watts and Kurt Braunohler. Feast your eyes on more video -- including a cut of the full show! -- at <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/live/\">radiolab.org/live</a>.</p><p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"349\" src=\"http://www.youtube.com/embed/Fp1JjldKCQg\" width=\"620\"></iframe></p>\n<p>Recorded live at Seattle's <span>Paramount Theatre on November 21st and 22nd.</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-12-09T17:05:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"ice-cold-case", "title"=>"An Ice-Cold Case", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13otzi.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=331933", "description"=>"<p><span>Scientists' obsession with one particular man - and with the tiny scraps of evidence left in the wake of his death - gives us a surprisingly intimate peek into the life of someone who should've been lost to the ages.</span></p><p>A little over 20 years ago, a perfectly preserved corpse was found buried in the ice, high up in the Alps. And after decades of investigating, cutting-edge forensics have revealed not only a murder mystery, but a startling story about one man's final days.<br><br>When hikers first found Ötzi (the nickname given to the body discovered in 1991), everyone assumed they'd stumbled upon an unfortunate mountaineering accident. But as the body was pulled from the ice, authorities started to suspect this wasn't a modern-day adventure gone wrong. It was, as producer Andy Mills explains, an OLD body. Really, really old. <br><br>Botanist <a href=\"http://www.universitystory.gla.ac.uk/biography/?id=WH1659&amp;type=P\">Jim Dickson</a>, graphic artist <a href=\"http://www.aaronbirk.com/\">Aaron Birk</a>, and <a href=\"http://www.eurac.edu/staff/AZink/default.html\">Albert Zinc</a>, head of the Institute for Mummies and the Iceman, describe how scientific advances and modern forensic breakthroughs have uncovered an ancient tale of violence and humanity.</p>", "tease"=>"A little over 20 years ago, a perfectly preserved corpse was found buried in the ice, high up in the Alps.", "publishTime"=>1384892424000, "publish-at"=>"2013-11-19T15:20:24", "date-line-ts"=>1384892423, "estimated-duration"=>1293, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/otzi_sm_1.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/768/c/80/1/otzi_sm_1.jpg", "h"=>768, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.danicanovgorodoff.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/otzi_sm_1.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>107743, "credits-name"=>"Danica Novgorodoff"}, "objectID"=>"331933", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"An Ice-Cold Case", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Scientists' obsession with one particular man - and with the tiny scraps of evidence left in the wake of his death - gives us a surprisingly intimate peek into the life of someone who should've been lost to the ages.</span></p><p>A little over 20 years ago, a perfectly preserved corpse was found buried in the ice, high up in the Alps. And after decades of investigating, cutting-edge forensics have revealed not only a murder mystery, but a startling story about one man's final days.<br><br>When hikers first found Ötzi (the nickname given to the body discovered in 1991), everyone assumed they'd stumbled upon an unfortunate mountaineering accident. But as the body was pulled from the ice, authorities started to suspect this wasn't a modern-day adventure gone wrong. It was, as producer Andy Mills explains, an OLD body. Really, really old. <br><br>Botanist <a href=\"http://www.universitystory.gla.ac.uk/biography/?id=WH1659&amp;type=P\">Jim Dickson</a>, graphic artist <a href=\"http://www.aaronbirk.com/\">Aaron Birk</a>, and <a href=\"http://www.eurac.edu/staff/AZink/default.html\">Albert Zinc</a>, head of the Institute for Mummies and the Iceman, describe how scientific advances and modern forensic breakthroughs have uncovered an ancient tale of violence and humanity.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-11-19T15:20:24", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"runners", "title"=>"Cut and Run", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13runners.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=328316", "description"=>"<p>Legions of athletes, sports gurus, and scientists have tried to figure out why Kenyans dominate long-distance running. In this short, we stumble across a surprising, and sort of terrifying, explanation.</p><p>At the 1968 summer Olympics in Mexico City, Kipchoge Keino overcame a gall bladder infection to win gold in the 1500 meter race. Since then, one particular group of Kenyans - the Kalenjin - has produced an astonishing number of great long-distance runners. <a href=\"http://www.npr.org/people/172020165/gregory-warner\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Gregory Warner</strong></a> - NPR's East Africa correspondent - takes Jad and Robert down a rabbit hole of theories about what exactly is going on in Kalenjin country. <strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>David Epstein</strong> and <strong>John Manners</strong> help Greg untangle a web of potential factors - from something in the cornmeal to simple economics. And, after talking to a young Kalenjin runner named <strong>Elly <span><span>Kipgogei</span></span>, </strong>Greg discovers a somewhat disturbing explanation for Kalenjin running prowess that actually makes him want to get on the treadmill and push himself just a little harder. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Check out a video of Kipchoge Keino's 1968 Olympic 1500m run:</p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"465\" src=\"http://www.youtube.com/embed/P6Lo7A9y9pU\" width=\"620\"></iframe></p>", "tease"=>"Cut and run", "publishTime"=>1383339600000, "publish-at"=>"2013-11-01T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1383339600, "estimated-duration"=>1507, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Kenyan runners", "name"=>"1/kenyan_runners.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1448/972/c/80/1/kenyan_runners.jpg", "h"=>972, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/tryingyouth/145971109/in/photolist-dU98g-6QjoS3-9fFek6-8MYhkG-8MPih5-8MPi3s-8MPhXG-8MPhTU-8MPhFh-8MPho7-8MPh4L-8MPh1m-8MPgSQ-8MPgLd-8MPgu5-8MPgfb-8MPfWL-8MPffG-8MPfcS-8MPe", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/kenyan_runners.jpg", "w"=>1448, "id"=>106757, "credits-name"=>"a trying youth"}, "objectID"=>"328316", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Cut and Run", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Legions of athletes, sports gurus, and scientists have tried to figure out why Kenyans dominate long-distance running. In this short, we stumble across a surprising, and sort of terrifying, explanation.</p><p>At the 1968 summer Olympics in Mexico City, Kipchoge Keino overcame a gall bladder infection to win gold in the 1500 meter race. Since then, one particular group of Kenyans - the Kalenjin - has produced an astonishing number of great long-distance runners. <a href=\"http://www.npr.org/people/172020165/gregory-warner\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Gregory Warner</strong></a> - NPR's East Africa correspondent - takes Jad and Robert down a rabbit hole of theories about what exactly is going on in Kalenjin country. <strong> </strong></p>\n<p><strong>David Epstein</strong> and <strong>John Manners</strong> help Greg untangle a web of potential factors - from something in the cornmeal to simple economics. And, after talking to a young Kalenjin runner named <strong>Elly <span><span>Kipgogei</span></span>, </strong>Greg discovers a somewhat disturbing explanation for Kalenjin running prowess that actually makes him want to get on the treadmill and push himself just a little harder. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Check out a video of Kipchoge Keino's 1968 Olympic 1500m run:</p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"465\" src=\"http://www.youtube.com/embed/P6Lo7A9y9pU\" width=\"620\"></iframe></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-11-01T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"update-famous-tumors", "title"=>"UPDATE: Famous Tumors", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13updatetumors.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=325668", "description"=>"<p>When we first released Famous Tumors, Rebecca Skloot's book about the life and legacy of Henrietta Lacks (and her famous cells) had just hit the shelves. Since then, some interesting things have happened to both Henrietta's cells and her family. So, 4 years later, we have a newly updated show!</p><p>This hour, we poke and prod at the good, bad, and ugly sides of tumors -- from the growth that killed Ulysses S. Grant, to mushy lumps leaping from the faces of infected Tasmanian Devils, to a mass that awakened a new (though pretty strange) kind of euphoria for one man. Plus, the updated story of one woman's medically miraculous cancer cells, and how they changed modern science and, eventually, her family's understanding of itself.</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p>Rebecca Skloot's <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/B00338QENI/radiolabbooks20/\"><em>The Immortal Life of Henrietta Lacks</em></a></span></p>", "tease"=>"When we first released Famous Tumors, Rebecca Skloot's book about the life and legacy of Henrietta Lacks (and her famous cells) had just hit the shelves. Since then, some interesting ...", "publishTime"=>1382481120000, "publish-at"=>"2013-10-22T18:32:00", "date-line-ts"=>1382481120, "estimated-duration"=>4036, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"HeLa S3 cells", "name"=>"1/hela_cells_green_web.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/605/h/80/1/hela_cells_green_web.jpg", "h"=>605, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"HeLa S3 cells", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/8674020@N04/5420346423/in/photolist-9fYG9F-dYxbuo-dZVM8G", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/hela_cells_green_web.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>105921, "credits-name"=>"opiado"}, "objectID"=>"325668", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"UPDATE: Famous Tumors", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>When we first released Famous Tumors, Rebecca Skloot's book about the life and legacy of Henrietta Lacks (and her famous cells) had just hit the shelves. Since then, some interesting things have happened to both Henrietta's cells and her family. So, 4 years later, we have a newly updated show!</p><p>This hour, we poke and prod at the good, bad, and ugly sides of tumors -- from the growth that killed Ulysses S. Grant, to mushy lumps leaping from the faces of infected Tasmanian Devils, to a mass that awakened a new (though pretty strange) kind of euphoria for one man. Plus, the updated story of one woman's medically miraculous cancer cells, and how they changed modern science and, eventually, her family's understanding of itself.</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p>Rebecca Skloot's <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/B00338QENI/radiolabbooks20/\"><em>The Immortal Life of Henrietta Lacks</em></a></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-10-22T18:32:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"quicksand", "title"=>"Quicksaaaand!", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13quicksand.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=323373", "description"=>"<p>For many of us, quicksand was once a real fear -- it held a vise-grip on our imaginations, from childish sandbox games to grown-up anxieties about venturing into unknown lands. But these days, quicksand can't even scare an 8-year-old. In this short, we try to find out why. </p><p>Producer Soren Wheeler introduces us to Dan Engber, writer and columnist for <em>Slate</em>, who ran across a strange fact: kids are no longer afraid of quicksand. To figure out what happened to quicksand, Dan immersed himself in research, compiled mountains of data, and met with quicksand fetishists. Dan tells Soren and Robert about his journey, and shares his theory about why the terror of his childhood seems to have lost its menacing allure. And Carlton Cuse, best-known as writer and executive producer of <em>Lost</em>, weighs in on whether giant pits of hero-swallowing mud might one day creep back into the spotlight.</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/quicksand_movies_graph_620.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\" height=\"299\"></p>\n<p><em>Dan Engber's data on the percentage of movies released that feature quicksand.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Producer Soren Wheeler introduces us to Dan Engber, writer and columnist for&nbsp;Slate, who ran across a strange fact: kids are no longer afraid of quicksand.", "publishTime"=>1381442400000, "publish-at"=>"2013-10-10T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1381442400, "estimated-duration"=>959, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Quicksand sign", "name"=>"1/drijfzand.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/768/l/80/1/drijfzand.jpg", "h"=>768, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/rs-foto/1242024959/in/photolist-2TKGKr-5d6yWs-d6vfwC-dLaM4t-fQsv2s-5w6iGp-5s2mHR-65cdLU-dU6FTL-65ce4y-8F7Esf-e4vKEA-buR5Ff-9qHLLT-bHSRSP-dLTfuQ-dLTesh-3TNdiG-ezTx6h-ezTwPA", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/drijfzand.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>105441, "credits-name"=>"rs-foto"}, "objectID"=>"323373", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Quicksaaaand!", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>For many of us, quicksand was once a real fear -- it held a vise-grip on our imaginations, from childish sandbox games to grown-up anxieties about venturing into unknown lands. But these days, quicksand can't even scare an 8-year-old. In this short, we try to find out why. </p><p>Producer Soren Wheeler introduces us to Dan Engber, writer and columnist for <em>Slate</em>, who ran across a strange fact: kids are no longer afraid of quicksand. To figure out what happened to quicksand, Dan immersed himself in research, compiled mountains of data, and met with quicksand fetishists. Dan tells Soren and Robert about his journey, and shares his theory about why the terror of his childhood seems to have lost its menacing allure. And Carlton Cuse, best-known as writer and executive producer of <em>Lost</em>, weighs in on whether giant pits of hero-swallowing mud might one day creep back into the spotlight.</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/quicksand_movies_graph_620.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\" height=\"299\"></p>\n<p><em>Dan Engber's data on the percentage of movies released that feature quicksand.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-10-10T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"poop-train", "title"=>"Poop Train", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13pooptrain.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=319978", "description"=>"<p>You may not give a second thought (or backward glance) to what the toilet whisks away after you do your business. But we got wondering -- where would we wind up if we thought of flushing as the start, and not the end, of a journey? In this short, we head out to trace the trail of sludge...from Manhattan, to wherever poop leads us.</p><p>This all started back when we were working on our <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/197112-guts/\">Guts</a> show, and author Frederick Kaufman told us about getting sucked in to the mystery of what happens to poop in New York City. Robert and producer Pat Walters decided to take Fred's advice and pay a visit to the <a href=\"http://www.nyc.gov/html/dep/html/dep_projects/cp_north_river_plant.shtml\" target=\"_blank\">North River Wastewater Treatment Plant</a>... which turned out to be just the beginning of a surprisingly far-ranging quest.</p>\n<p>Want some more sewer fun?</p>\n<p>Read: As Robert and Pat report, some of that sewer sludge made it out into the ocean. <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/sludge-bottom-sea/\" target=\"_blank\">Wonder what happened to it?</a></p>\n<p>Play: Try out our Poop Quiz:</p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"785\" scrolling=\"no\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/datanewswidget/poop-quiz/\" width=\"100%\"></iframe></p>", "tease"=>"We decided to pay a visit to the North River Wastewater Treatment Plant&nbsp;to solve the mystery of what happens to poop in New York City.", "publishTime"=>1380056400000, "publish-at"=>"2013-09-24T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1380056400, "estimated-duration"=>1374, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"NYC sewer manhole cover", "name"=>"1/NYC_sewer_cover_1.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1000/667/c/80/1/NYC_sewer_cover_1.jpg", "h"=>667, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/NYC_sewer_cover_1.jpg", "w"=>1000, "id"=>104240, "credits-name"=>"Michael Katzif / WNYC"}, "objectID"=>"319978", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Poop Train", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>You may not give a second thought (or backward glance) to what the toilet whisks away after you do your business. But we got wondering -- where would we wind up if we thought of flushing as the start, and not the end, of a journey? In this short, we head out to trace the trail of sludge...from Manhattan, to wherever poop leads us.</p><p>This all started back when we were working on our <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/197112-guts/\">Guts</a> show, and author Frederick Kaufman told us about getting sucked in to the mystery of what happens to poop in New York City. Robert and producer Pat Walters decided to take Fred's advice and pay a visit to the <a href=\"http://www.nyc.gov/html/dep/html/dep_projects/cp_north_river_plant.shtml\" target=\"_blank\">North River Wastewater Treatment Plant</a>... which turned out to be just the beginning of a surprisingly far-ranging quest.</p>\n<p>Want some more sewer fun?</p>\n<p>Read: As Robert and Pat report, some of that sewer sludge made it out into the ocean. <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/sludge-bottom-sea/\" target=\"_blank\">Wonder what happened to it?</a></p>\n<p>Play: Try out our Poop Quiz:</p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"785\" scrolling=\"no\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/datanewswidget/poop-quiz/\" width=\"100%\"></iframe></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-09-24T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"317421-blame", "title"=>"Blame", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab091213.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=317421", "description"=>"<p>We've all felt it, that irresistible urge to point the finger. But new technologies are complicating age-old moral conundrums about accountability. This hour, we ask what blame does for us -- why do we need it, when isn't it enough, and what happens when we try to push past it with forgiveness and mercy?</p>", "tease"=>"We've all felt it, that irresistible urge to point the finger. But why do we need blame and how is new technology complicating accountability?&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1379024280000, "publish-at"=>"2013-09-12T18:18:00", "date-line-ts"=>1379001600, "estimated-duration"=>3740, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Making their points", "name"=>"1/point_fingers.png", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1000/535/c/80/1/point_fingers.png", "h"=>535, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/19251296@N00/2255468498/in/photolist-4riScU-eGiLyC-5dorYC-9tHFVK-bzsdeU-4Y4SCS-4LjsGa-75S19L-bNmSbr-anvH5K-6HSWSN-ax9sif-dGmsXr-ceGsJL-dLJSi-5uVQz-w1oiY-9CZbWb-97hcz9-4whn", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/point_fingers.png", "w"=>1000, "id"=>103567, "credits-name"=>"Susan Sermoneta"}, "objectID"=>"317421", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Blame", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>We've all felt it, that irresistible urge to point the finger. But new technologies are complicating age-old moral conundrums about accountability. This hour, we ask what blame does for us -- why do we need it, when isn't it enough, and what happens when we try to push past it with forgiveness and mercy?</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-09-12T18:18:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"313542-dawn-midi", "title"=>"Dawn of Midi", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13dawnmidi.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=313542", "description"=>"<p>In this short, Jad puts on his music hat and shares his love of Dawn of Midi, a band that he recently started using on the show.</p><p>Midi, for those of you who don't know, is sort of like a computer language for music. But the band Dawn of Midi seems to exist at the intersection between acoustic and electronic sounds. Jad talks to Aakaash Israni, Amino Belyamani, and Qasim Naqvi about how they met and how they ended up creating their latest album, Dysnomia, an album filled with heavily layered rhythms that feel both mechanistic and deeply human at the same time.</p>", "tease"=>"Midi, for those of you who don't know, is sort of like a computer language for music.", "publishTime"=>1377813600000, "publish-at"=>"2013-08-29T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1377813600, "estimated-duration"=>835, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Dawn of Midi. L-R: Qasim Naqvi, Amino Belyamani, Aakaash Israni", "name"=>"1/DawnofMidi.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/885/c/80/1/DawnofMidi.jpg", "h"=>885, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Dawn of Midi. L-R: Qasim Naqvi, Amino Belyamani, Aakaash Israni", "credits-url"=>"http://bighassle.com/publicity/dawn-of-midi", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/DawnofMidi.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>102315, "credits-name"=>"Falkwyn de Goyeneche"}, "objectID"=>"313542", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dawn of Midi", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this short, Jad puts on his music hat and shares his love of Dawn of Midi, a band that he recently started using on the show.</p><p>Midi, for those of you who don't know, is sort of like a computer language for music. But the band Dawn of Midi seems to exist at the intersection between acoustic and electronic sounds. Jad talks to Aakaash Israni, Amino Belyamani, and Qasim Naqvi about how they met and how they ended up creating their latest album, Dysnomia, an album filled with heavily layered rhythms that feel both mechanistic and deeply human at the same time.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-08-29T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"312245-rodney-versus-death", "title"=>"Rodney Versus Death", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13rodneyvdeath.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=312245", "description"=>"<p>What do you do in the face of a monstrous disease with a 100% fatality rate? In this short, a Milwaukee doctor tries to knock death incarnate off its throne.</p><p>In the fall of 2004, Jeanna Giese checked into the Children's Hospital of Wisconsin with a set of puzzling symptoms ... and her condition was deteriorating fast. By the time Dr. Rodney Willoughby saw her, he only knew one thing for sure: if Jeanna's disturbing breakdown turned out to be rabies, she was doomed to die.</p>\n<p>What happened next seemed like a medical impossibility. Producer <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/people/tim-howard/\"><strong>Tim Howard</strong></a> tells Jeanna's story and talks to authors Monica Murphy and Bill Wasik, and scientists Amy Gilbert and Sergio Recuenco, while trying to unravel the mystery of an unusual patient, and a doctor who dared to take on certain death.</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0143123572/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Rabid: A Cultural History of the World's Most Diabolical Virus</em></a></span>, by Bill Wasik and Monica Murphy</p>\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.wired.com/wiredscience/2012/07/ff_rabies/all/\" target=\"_blank\">Undead: The Rabies Virus Remains a Medical Mystery</a>,\" <em>Wired</em> article by Monica Murphy and Bill Wasik</p>\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.wired.com/wiredscience/2012/08/rabies-survivor-mystery-deepens/\" target=\"_blank\">Bats Incredible: The Mystery of Rabies Survivorship Deepens</a>,\" <em>Wired</em> article by Monica Murphy and Bill Wasik</p>\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.cdc.gov/media/releases/2012/dpk0801-rabies.html\" target=\"_blank\">Study Detects Rabies Immune Response in Amazon Populations</a>,\" the CDC's page on Amy Gilbert and Sergio Recuenco's work (inc. photos from Peru)</p>\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.cdc.gov/rabies/resources/news/mp-haiti-lg.html\" target=\"_blank\">Selection Criteria for Milwaukee Protocol</a>,\" when to try the Milwaukee Protocol</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://site.jeannagiese.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Jeanna Giese's website</a></p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"785\" scrolling=\"no\" src=\"https://project.wnyc.org/rabies-quiz/\" width=\"100%\"></iframe></p>", "tease"=>"In the fall of 2004, Jeanna Giese checked into the Children's Hospital of Wisconsin with a set of puzzling symptoms ...&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1376427600000, "publish-at"=>"2013-08-13T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1376427600, "estimated-duration"=>1841, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Main illustration from the short story 'The Opener of the Way.' Internal Illustration from the pulp magazine Weird Tales (October 1936, vol. 28, no. 3, page 277).", "name"=>"1/WeirdTalesv28n3pg277_illustration.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/", "name"=>"Wikimedia Commons"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1012/1002/l/80/1/WeirdTalesv28n3pg277_illustration.jpg", "h"=>1002, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AWeirdTalesv28n3pg277_Opener_of_the_Way.png", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/WeirdTalesv28n3pg277_illustration.jpg", "w"=>1012, "id"=>101939, "credits-name"=>"Virgil Finlay [Public domain]"}, "objectID"=>"312245", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Rodney Versus Death", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>What do you do in the face of a monstrous disease with a 100% fatality rate? In this short, a Milwaukee doctor tries to knock death incarnate off its throne.</p><p>In the fall of 2004, Jeanna Giese checked into the Children's Hospital of Wisconsin with a set of puzzling symptoms ... and her condition was deteriorating fast. By the time Dr. Rodney Willoughby saw her, he only knew one thing for sure: if Jeanna's disturbing breakdown turned out to be rabies, she was doomed to die.</p>\n<p>What happened next seemed like a medical impossibility. Producer <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/people/tim-howard/\"><strong>Tim Howard</strong></a> tells Jeanna's story and talks to authors Monica Murphy and Bill Wasik, and scientists Amy Gilbert and Sergio Recuenco, while trying to unravel the mystery of an unusual patient, and a doctor who dared to take on certain death.</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p><span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0143123572/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Rabid: A Cultural History of the World's Most Diabolical Virus</em></a></span>, by Bill Wasik and Monica Murphy</p>\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.wired.com/wiredscience/2012/07/ff_rabies/all/\" target=\"_blank\">Undead: The Rabies Virus Remains a Medical Mystery</a>,\" <em>Wired</em> article by Monica Murphy and Bill Wasik</p>\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.wired.com/wiredscience/2012/08/rabies-survivor-mystery-deepens/\" target=\"_blank\">Bats Incredible: The Mystery of Rabies Survivorship Deepens</a>,\" <em>Wired</em> article by Monica Murphy and Bill Wasik</p>\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.cdc.gov/media/releases/2012/dpk0801-rabies.html\" target=\"_blank\">Study Detects Rabies Immune Response in Amazon Populations</a>,\" the CDC's page on Amy Gilbert and Sergio Recuenco's work (inc. photos from Peru)</p>\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.cdc.gov/rabies/resources/news/mp-haiti-lg.html\" target=\"_blank\">Selection Criteria for Milwaukee Protocol</a>,\" when to try the Milwaukee Protocol</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://site.jeannagiese.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Jeanna Giese's website</a></p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"785\" scrolling=\"no\" src=\"https://project.wnyc.org/rabies-quiz/\" width=\"100%\"></iframe></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-08-13T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"308403-blood", "title"=>"Blood", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab073113.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=308403", "description"=>"<p>From medicine to the movies, the horrifying to the holy, and history to the present day -- we're kinda obsessed with blood. This hour, we consider the power and magic of the red liquid that runs through our veins.</p>", "tease"=>"The metaphor, magic, and money coursing through our veins...", "publishTime"=>1375304400000, "publish-at"=>"2013-07-31T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1375286400, "estimated-duration"=>3820, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Illustration of a hand holding a vial of blood", "name"=>"1/Blood_final.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/900/c/80/1/Blood_final.jpg", "h"=>900, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://jrosen.org/index.html", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Blood_final.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>100898, "credits-name"=>"Jonathon Rosen"}, "objectID"=>"308403", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Blood", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>From medicine to the movies, the horrifying to the holy, and history to the present day -- we're kinda obsessed with blood. This hour, we consider the power and magic of the red liquid that runs through our veins.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-07-31T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"305470-happy-birthday-good-doctor-sacks", "title"=>"Happy Birthday, Good Dr. Sacks", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13oliver.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=305470", "description"=>"<p>One of our favorite human beings turns 80 this week. To celebrate, Robert asks Oliver Sacks to look back on his career, and explain how thousands of worms and a motorbike accident led to a brilliant writing career.</p><p>More Oliver:</p>\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/07/opinion/sunday/the-joy-of-old-age-no-kidding.html\" target=\"_blank\">The Joy of Old Age. (No Kidding.)</a>\" <em>Oliver Sacks reflects on his life, and turning 80, in the New York Times</em></p>\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/title/tt0099077/\" target=\"_blank\">Awakenings</a>\" <em>The 1990 movie based on Oliver's book of the same name.</em></p>\n<p>\n<script type=\"text/javascript\">// <![CDATA[\r\nvar amzn_wdgt={widget:'Carousel'};\r\namzn_wdgt.tag='radiolabbooks-20';\r\namzn_wdgt.widgetType='ASINList';\r\namzn_wdgt.title='10 Books by Oliver Sacks';\r\namzn_wdgt.width='620';\r\namzn_wdgt.ASIN='0684853949,0375704051,0679756973,0375700730,037570406X,0307473023,0307957241,1400033535,0375704043,0375704078';\r\namzn_wdgt.width='620';\r\namzn_wdgt.height='200';\r\n// ]]></script>\n</p>", "tease"=>"Radiolab wishes Oliver Sacks a happy birthday.", "publishTime"=>1373403600000, "publish-at"=>"2013-07-09T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1373403600, "estimated-duration"=>1364, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Oliver Sacks", "name"=>"1/oliver_sacks.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/612/612/l/80/1/oliver_sacks.jpg", "h"=>612, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Oliver Sacks", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/oliver_sacks.jpg", "w"=>612, "id"=>100190, "credits-name"=>"Andy Mills"}, "objectID"=>"305470", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Happy Birthday, Good Dr. Sacks", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>One of our favorite human beings turns 80 this week. To celebrate, Robert asks Oliver Sacks to look back on his career, and explain how thousands of worms and a motorbike accident led to a brilliant writing career.</p><p>More Oliver:</p>\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2013/07/07/opinion/sunday/the-joy-of-old-age-no-kidding.html\" target=\"_blank\">The Joy of Old Age. (No Kidding.)</a>\" <em>Oliver Sacks reflects on his life, and turning 80, in the New York Times</em></p>\n<p>\"<a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/title/tt0099077/\" target=\"_blank\">Awakenings</a>\" <em>The 1990 movie based on Oliver's book of the same name.</em></p>\n<p>\n<script type=\"text/javascript\">// <![CDATA[\r\nvar amzn_wdgt={widget:'Carousel'};\r\namzn_wdgt.tag='radiolabbooks-20';\r\namzn_wdgt.widgetType='ASINList';\r\namzn_wdgt.title='10 Books by Oliver Sacks';\r\namzn_wdgt.width='620';\r\namzn_wdgt.ASIN='0684853949,0375704051,0679756973,0375700730,037570406X,0307473023,0307957241,1400033535,0375704043,0375704078';\r\namzn_wdgt.width='620';\r\namzn_wdgt.height='200';\r\n// ]]></script>\n</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-07-09T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"304341-allys-choice", "title"=>"Ally's Choice", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13allyschoice.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=304341", "description"=>"<p>Producer Lu Olkowski brings us the story of a tightly-knit family caught on opposite sides of a very big divide. If you ask Ally Manning's mom and sister, they'll tell you there's no question: they're black. But as a teenager, Ally decided that what was true for them didn't make sense for her.</p><p>Lu explains how the complex racial history of two towns in Ohio leads members of the same family to disagree strongly about whether they're black or white. And Ally, along with her mom Clarice and her sister Carlotta, wrestles with what it means to choose a different identity from her closest relations.</p>\n<p>Ally Manning, Clarice Shreck’s daughter, takes a moment before heading work to read a poem she wrote about her mom:</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/Ally_Lloyd_Cederstrand_web.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p><em><a href=\"http://www.flickr.com/photos/stateofthereunion/8206838920/in/photostream/\" target=\"_blank\">Photo: Lloyd Cederdstrand</a>.</em></p>\n<p>Carlotta Hixon and her mom Clarice Shreck:</p>\n<p><em><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/Carlotta_Clarice_Lloyd_Cederstrand_web_1.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"><br></em></p>\n<p><em><a href=\"http://www.flickr.com/photos/stateofthereunion/8205771705/in/photostream/\" target=\"_blank\">Photo: Lloyd Cederstrand</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em><img style=\"float: left; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px;\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/OHC_Logo_w_txt_BLK.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"75\">A version of this story, “As Black As We Wish to Be,” was sponsored by <a href=\"http://ohiohillcountry.org/\" target=\"_blank\">Ohio’s Hill Country Heritage Area</a> and made possible with funding from the <a href=\"http://www.ohiohumanities.org\" target=\"_blank\">Ohio Humanities Council</a>. It originally aired on <a href=\"http://stateofthereunion.com/pike-county-oh-as-black-as-we-wish-to-be/\" target=\"_blank\">State of the Re:Union</a>.\"</em></p>", "tease"=>"The complex racial history of two towns in Ohio leads members of the same family to disagree strongly about whether they're black or white.", "publishTime"=>1372806000000, "publish-at"=>"2013-07-02T19:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1372806000, "estimated-duration"=>1071, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Clarice holds up her birth certificate", "name"=>"1/Clarice_Lloyd_Cederstrand_web.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/886/l/80/1/Clarice_Lloyd_Cederstrand_web.jpg", "h"=>886, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/stateofthereunion/8205779079/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Clarice_Lloyd_Cederstrand_web.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>99979, "credits-name"=>"Lloyd Cederstrand"}, "objectID"=>"304341", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Ally's Choice", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Producer Lu Olkowski brings us the story of a tightly-knit family caught on opposite sides of a very big divide. If you ask Ally Manning's mom and sister, they'll tell you there's no question: they're black. But as a teenager, Ally decided that what was true for them didn't make sense for her.</p><p>Lu explains how the complex racial history of two towns in Ohio leads members of the same family to disagree strongly about whether they're black or white. And Ally, along with her mom Clarice and her sister Carlotta, wrestles with what it means to choose a different identity from her closest relations.</p>\n<p>Ally Manning, Clarice Shreck’s daughter, takes a moment before heading work to read a poem she wrote about her mom:</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/Ally_Lloyd_Cederstrand_web.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p><em><a href=\"http://www.flickr.com/photos/stateofthereunion/8206838920/in/photostream/\" target=\"_blank\">Photo: Lloyd Cederdstrand</a>.</em></p>\n<p>Carlotta Hixon and her mom Clarice Shreck:</p>\n<p><em><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/Carlotta_Clarice_Lloyd_Cederstrand_web_1.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"><br></em></p>\n<p><em><a href=\"http://www.flickr.com/photos/stateofthereunion/8205771705/in/photostream/\" target=\"_blank\">Photo: Lloyd Cederstrand</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em><img style=\"float: left; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px;\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/OHC_Logo_w_txt_BLK.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"75\">A version of this story, “As Black As We Wish to Be,” was sponsored by <a href=\"http://ohiohillcountry.org/\" target=\"_blank\">Ohio’s Hill Country Heritage Area</a> and made possible with funding from the <a href=\"http://www.ohiohumanities.org\" target=\"_blank\">Ohio Humanities Council</a>. It originally aired on <a href=\"http://stateofthereunion.com/pike-county-oh-as-black-as-we-wish-to-be/\" target=\"_blank\">State of the Re:Union</a>.\"</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-07-02T19:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"303638-curious-sounds-solid-sound-festival", "title"=>"Curious Sounds from the Solid Sound Festival ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/rl_extras/rl_extras062713solid.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=303638", "description"=>"<p>This fall, we're hitting the road with our brand-new live show. We're stopping in 20 cities across the US (plus 1 stop in Canada), and we have some exciting news about the special musical guests who are joining us for the tour. Listen to a quick sneak peek, and <a href=\"http://radiolab.org/live\">grab your tix now</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"This fall, we're hitting the road with our brand-new live show. We're stopping in 20 cities across the US (plus 1 stop in Canada), and we have some exciting news about the special mus...", "publishTime"=>1372366800000, "publish-at"=>"2013-06-27T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1372366800, "estimated-duration"=>554, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Radiolab performs live at the Solid Sound Festival", "name"=>"1/Radiolab_Solid_Sound_June_2013_RLP_1910_web.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/908/580/h/80/1/Radiolab_Solid_Sound_June_2013_RLP_1910_web.jpg", "h"=>580, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.rlphoto.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Radiolab_Solid_Sound_June_2013_RLP_1910_web.jpg", "w"=>908, "id"=>99814, "credits-name"=>"Rick Levison, RLP Photo, Inc"}, "objectID"=>"303638", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Curious Sounds from the Solid Sound Festival ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This fall, we're hitting the road with our brand-new live show. We're stopping in 20 cities across the US (plus 1 stop in Canada), and we have some exciting news about the special musical guests who are joining us for the tour. Listen to a quick sneak peek, and <a href=\"http://radiolab.org/live\">grab your tix now</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-06-27T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"298146-trouble-everything", "title"=>"The Trouble with Everything", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13everything.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=298146", "description"=>"<p>The desire to trace your way back to the very beginning, to understand everything -- whether it's the mysteries of love or the mechanics of the universe -- is deeply human. It might also be deeply flawed.</p><p>In this short, Jad and Robert talk to a writer and two physicists who are all grappling with versions of the same enormous question: is it possible to understand everything, or are we chasing an impossible dream... one built on questions that always lead to more questions?</p>\n<p><strong>Jenny Hollowell</strong> kicks things off with her gorgeous short story \"A History of Everything, Including You.\" It's a powerful tale with a sweeping scope -- the history not just of one couple, but everything that led to them -- distilled into a poetic crush of just a few pages. The piece was born out of a sense of frustration Jenny felt about trying to account for \"everything\" in order to understand her life. And in many ways, her solution speaks to an eerily similar moment of uncertainty in physics. Inspired by an essay written by physicist and novelist <strong>Alan Lightman</strong>, Robert pays a visit to <strong>Brian Greene</strong> to ask if the latest developments in theoretical physics spell a crisis for science -- where we find we've reached the limit of what we can see and test, and are left with mathematical equations that can't be verified by experiments or observation.</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Jenny Hollowell's \"A History of Everything, Including You\" can be found in the anthology <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0393328015/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>New Sudden Fiction: Short-Short Stories from America and Beyond</em></a></span></li>\n<li>Alan Lightman's essay for Harper's Magazine \"<a href=\"http://harpers.org/archive/2011/12/the-accidental-universe/\" target=\"_blank\">The Accidental Universe: Science’s crisis of faith</a>\"</li>\n</ul>", "tease"=>"Is it possible to understand everything, or are we chasing an impossible dream... one built on questions that always lead to more questions?", "publishTime"=>1371160140000, "publish-at"=>"2013-06-13T17:49:00", "date-line-ts"=>1371160140, "estimated-duration"=>1995, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"New stars being born in the Elephant's Trunk Nebula", "name"=>"1/NASA_elephants_trunk_nebula_PIA04935_crop.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/1480/c/80/1/NASA_elephants_trunk_nebula_PIA04935_crop.jpg", "h"=>1480, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"New stars being born in the Elephant's Trunk Nebula", "credits-url"=>"http://www.jpl.nasa.gov/spaceimages/details.php?id=PIA04935", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/NASA_elephants_trunk_nebula_PIA04935_crop.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>98535, "credits-name"=>"NASA/JPL/Caltech "}, "objectID"=>"298146", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Trouble with Everything", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>The desire to trace your way back to the very beginning, to understand everything -- whether it's the mysteries of love or the mechanics of the universe -- is deeply human. It might also be deeply flawed.</p><p>In this short, Jad and Robert talk to a writer and two physicists who are all grappling with versions of the same enormous question: is it possible to understand everything, or are we chasing an impossible dream... one built on questions that always lead to more questions?</p>\n<p><strong>Jenny Hollowell</strong> kicks things off with her gorgeous short story \"A History of Everything, Including You.\" It's a powerful tale with a sweeping scope -- the history not just of one couple, but everything that led to them -- distilled into a poetic crush of just a few pages. The piece was born out of a sense of frustration Jenny felt about trying to account for \"everything\" in order to understand her life. And in many ways, her solution speaks to an eerily similar moment of uncertainty in physics. Inspired by an essay written by physicist and novelist <strong>Alan Lightman</strong>, Robert pays a visit to <strong>Brian Greene</strong> to ask if the latest developments in theoretical physics spell a crisis for science -- where we find we've reached the limit of what we can see and test, and are left with mathematical equations that can't be verified by experiments or observation.</p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Jenny Hollowell's \"A History of Everything, Including You\" can be found in the anthology <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0393328015/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>New Sudden Fiction: Short-Short Stories from America and Beyond</em></a></span></li>\n<li>Alan Lightman's essay for Harper's Magazine \"<a href=\"http://harpers.org/archive/2011/12/the-accidental-universe/\" target=\"_blank\">The Accidental Universe: Science’s crisis of faith</a>\"</li>\n</ul>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-06-13T17:49:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"295210-adoptive-couple-v-baby-girl", "title"=>"Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13babygirl.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=295210", "description"=>"<p>This is the story of a three-year-old girl and the highest court in the land. The Supreme Court case <em>Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl</em> is a legal battle that has entangled a biological father, a heart-broken couple, and the tragic history of Native American children taken from their families.</p><p>When producer <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/people/tim-howard/\">Tim Howard</a> first read about this case, it struck him as a sad but seemingly straightforward custody dispute. But, as he started talking to lawyers and historians and the families involved in the case, it became clear that it was much more than that. Because <em>Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl </em>challenges parts of the 1978 Indian Child Welfare Act, this case puts one little girl at the center of a storm of legal intricacies, Native American tribal culture, and heart-wrenching personal stakes.</p>\n<p><strong>UPDATE: The Supreme Court has made its ruling in <em>Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl</em>. Hear our update at the end of this episode.<br></strong></p>\n<h3>Background and Reporting from a range of different perspectives</h3>\n<p>\"<strong><a href=\"http://www.postandcourier.com/article/20120101/PC1602/301019972/1282/couple-forced-to-give-up-daughter\" target=\"_blank\">Couple forced to give up daughter</a></strong>\"<br> <em>An introductory article by Allyson Bird, for the Charleston, SC Post and Courier</em></p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"http://www.postandcourier.com/section/PC160444\" target=\"_blank\">Comprehensive coverage of the case by the Charleston <em>Post and Courier</em></a></strong></p>\n<p>\"<strong><a href=\"http://indiancountrytodaymedianetwork.com/2013/04/16/supreme-court-takes-indian-child-welfare-act-baby-veronica-case-148855\" target=\"_blank\">Supreme Court Takes on Indian Child Welfare Act in Baby Veronica Case</a></strong>\"<em> </em><br> <em>A report for Indian Country Today by Suzette Brewer, who has also written a <a href=\"http://indiancountrytodaymedianetwork.com/2013/05/06/fight-baby-veronica-part-i-149219\" target=\"_blank\">two-part series</a> on the case.</em></p>\n<p>\"<strong><a href=\"http://www.tulsaworld.com/site/printerfriendlystory.aspx?articleid=20130415_11_A1_ULNSut262929\" target=\"_blank\">Supreme Court hears Indian child custody case</a></strong>\"<br><em>Tulsa World</em> article by Michael Overall which includes Dusten Brown's account of his break-up with Veronica's mother, and his understanding about his custodial rights. Plus photos of Dusten, Veronica, and Dusten's wife Robin in their Oklahoma home<em>.</em></p>\n<p>Randi Kaye's report for CNN on the background of the case, and interviews with Melanie and Matt Capobianco: \"<strong><a href=\"http://ac360.blogs.cnn.com/2012/02/22/video-adoption-custody-battle-for-veronica/?hpt=ac_bn1\" target=\"_blank\">Video: Adoption custody battle for Veronica</a>\"</strong><br> <object data=\"http://i.cdn.turner.com/cnn/.element/apps/cvp/3.0/swf/cnn_embed_2x_container.swf?site=cnn&amp;profile=desktop&amp;context=embed&amp;videoId=bestoftv/2012/02/22/ac-kaye-veronica-custody-appeal.cnn&amp;contentId=bestoftv/2012/02/22/ac-kaye-veronica-custody-appeal.cnn\" type=\"application/x-shockwave-flash\" width=\"620\" height=\"349\">\n<param value=\"#000000\">\n<param name=\"bgcolor\" value=\"#000000\">\n<param name=\"src\" value=\"http://i.cdn.turner.com/cnn/.element/apps/cvp/3.0/swf/cnn_embed_2x_container.swf?site=cnn&amp;profile=desktop&amp;context=embed&amp;videoId=bestoftv/2012/02/22/ac-kaye-veronica-custody-appeal.cnn&amp;contentId=bestoftv/2012/02/22/ac-kaye-veronica-custody-appeal.cnn\">\n<param name=\"wmode\" value=\"transparent\">\n<param name=\"allowfullscreen\" value=\"true\"></object></p>\n<p>Nina Totenberg’s report for NPR: \"<strong><a href=\"http://www.npr.org/2013/04/16/177327391/adoption-case-brings-rare-family-law-dispute-to-high-court?ft=1&amp;f=1070\" target=\"_blank\">Adoption Case Brings Rare Family Law Dispute To High Court</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p>Reporting by NPR's Laura Sullivan and Amy Walters on <strong><a href=\"http://www.npr.org/2013/02/06/171310945/south-dakota-tribes-accuse-state-of-violating-indian-welfare-act\" target=\"_blank\">current ICWA violations in South Dakota</a></strong>.</p>\n<p>Dr. Phil's coverage: \"<strong><a href=\"http://www.drphil.com/shows/show/1895\" target=\"_blank\">Adoption Controversy: Battle over Baby Veronica\"</a></strong></p>\n<h3>Analysis and Editorials</h3>\n<p>Op-ed by Veronica's birth mom, Christy Maldonado, in the <em>Washington Post</em>: \"<span><a href=\"http://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/baby-veronicas-birth-mother-girl-belongs-with-adoptive-parents/2013/07/12/40d38a12-e995-11e2-a301-ea5a8116d211_story.html\" target=\"_blank\">Baby Veronica belongs with her adoptive parents</a>\"</span></p>\n<p><em>Colorlines </em>report \"<strong><a href=\"http://colorlines.com/archives/2013/04/the_cherokee_nations_baby_girl_goes_on_trial.html\">The Cherokee Nation’s Baby Girl Goes on Trial</a></strong>:\"</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Americans remain dangerously uninformed about the basics of tribal sovereignty, and what it means for the relationship between the United States and Native tribes and nations.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><em>The Weekly Standard's</em> Ethan Epstein argues that ICWA is \"being used to tear [families] apart]: \"<strong><a href=\"http://www.weeklystandard.com/articles/mistreating-native-american-children_649719.html\" target=\"_blank\">Mistreating Native American Children</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p>Andrew Cohen considers the trickier legal aspects of the case for the <em>Atlantic</em> in \"<strong><a href=\"http://www.theatlantic.com/national/archive/2013/04/indian-affairs-adoption-and-race-the-baby-veronica-case-comes-to-washington/274758/\" target=\"_blank\">Indian Affairs, Adoption, and Race: The Baby Veronica Case Comes to Washington</a></strong>:\"</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A little girl is at the heart of a big case at the Supreme Court next week, a racially-tinged fight over Native American rights and state custody laws.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Marcia Zug's breakdown of the case (Marica Zug is an associate professor of law at the University of South Carolina School of Law who she specializes in family and American Indian law) \"<strong><a href=\"http://www.slate.com/articles/double_x/doublex/2012/08/baby_veronica_returns_to_her_biological_father_affirming_icwa_south_carolina_s_supreme_court_made_the_right_decision_.html\" target=\"_blank\">Doing What’s Best for the Tribe</a></strong>\" for <em>Slate</em>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Two-year-old “Baby Veronica” was ripped from the only home she’s known. The court made the right decision.  </p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Marcia Zug for the Michigan Law Review: \"<strong><a href=\"http://www.michiganlawreview.org/articles/em-adoptive-couple-v-baby-girl-em-two-and-a-half-ways-to-destroy-indian-law\" target=\"_blank\">Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl: Two-and-a-Half WAys To Destroy Indian Law</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p>From Walter Olson, a senior fellow at the Cato Institute’s Center for Constitutional Studies: \"<strong><a href=\"http://reason.com/archives/2013/04/22/the-constitutional-flaws-of-the-indian-c\" target=\"_blank\">The Constitutional Flaws of the Indian Child Welfare Act</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p><em>Rapid City Journal</em> columnist David Rooks poses a set of tough questions about ICWA: \"<strong><a href=\"http://rapidcityjournal.com/news/opinion/rooks-questions-unasked-unanswered/article_efa51d54-a5d9-56dc-8f45-4455206d356c.html\" target=\"_blank\">ROOKS: Questions unasked, unanswered</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p>From Johnston Moore, an adoptive father of six children, three of whom are part Indian. (Moore is director and co-founder of Home Forever, and a founding member of the Coalition for the Protection of Indian Children &amp; Families. NewsOK): \"<strong><a href=\"http://newsok.com/some-different-talking-points-about-indian-child-welfare-act/article/3695665/?page=1\" target=\"_blank\">Some different talking points about Indian Child Welfare Act</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p>Editorial coverage from <em>The New York Times</em>:</p>\n<p>\"<strong><a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/16/opinion/a-wrenching-adoption-under-the-indian-child-welfare-act.html\" target=\"_blank\">A Wrenching Adoption Case</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p>\"<strong><a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/roomfordebate/2013/01/24/adoptive-parents-vs-tribal-rights\" target=\"_blank\">Adoptive Parents vs. Tribal Rights</a></strong>\"</p>\n<h3>Contemporary, Historic, and Legal Source Materials</h3>\n<p>Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl on the <strong><a href=\"http://www.scotusblog.com/case-files/cases/adoptive-couple-v-baby-girl/\" target=\"_blank\">SCOTUS (Supreme Court of the United States) Blog</a></strong></p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"http://www.oyez.org/cases/2010-2019/2012/2012_12_399\" target=\"_blank\">Audio from the oral arguments in the Supreme Court</a></strong></p>\n<p><object id=\"oyez_movie\" data=\"http://www.oyez.org/sites/default/files/OyezPlayer-mini.swf?media_url=/transcripts/2012/12-399_20130416-argument_1.xml&amp;base_url=http://www.oyez.org/sites/default/files/&amp;nobutton=1\" type=\"application/x-shockwave-flash\" width=\"620\" height=\"250\">\n<param name=\"quality\" value=\"high\">\n<param name=\"bgcolor\" value=\"#ffffff\">\n<param name=\"allowScriptAccess\" value=\"always\">\n<param name=\"src\" value=\"http://www.oyez.org/sites/default/files/OyezPlayer-mini.swf?media_url=/transcripts/2012/12-399_20130416-argument_1.xml&amp;base_url=http://www.oyez.org/sites/default/files/&amp;nobutton=1\">\n<param name=\"name\" value=\"oyez_movie\"></object></p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"http://www.icwlc.org/icwaoutline.html\" target=\"_blank\">Official website for ICWA</a></strong> (the federal Indian Child Welfare Act)</p>\n<p>1974 Hearings Before the Subcommitee on Indian Affairs \"<strong><a href=\"http://narf.org/icwa/federal/lh/hear040874/hear040874.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">on problems that American Indian families face in raising their children and how these problems are affected by federal action or inaction</a></strong>.\" PDF</p>\n<p>The <strong><a href=\"http://nicwa.org/\" target=\"_blank\">National Indian Child Welfare Association</a></strong></p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"http://www.saveveronica.org/\" target=\"_blank\">SaveVeronica.org</a></strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The <strong><a href=\"http://www.wearecominghome.com/\" target=\"_blank\">First Nations Repatriation Institute</a></strong>, which works with and does advocacy for adoptees</p>", "tease"=>"This case puts one little girl at the center of a storm of legal intricacies, Native American tribal culture, and heart-wrenching personal stakes.", "publishTime"=>1369947600000, "publish-at"=>"2013-05-30T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1369947600, "estimated-duration"=>2575, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Veronica, the little girl at the heart of Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl", "name"=>"1/Veronica_Duck_Pen_web.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/1223/c/80/1/Veronica_Duck_Pen_web.jpg", "h"=>1223, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Veronica, the little girl at the heart of Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Veronica_Duck_Pen_web.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>97982, "credits-name"=>"Courtesy of John Nichols"}, "objectID"=>"295210", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This is the story of a three-year-old girl and the highest court in the land. The Supreme Court case <em>Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl</em> is a legal battle that has entangled a biological father, a heart-broken couple, and the tragic history of Native American children taken from their families.</p><p>When producer <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/people/tim-howard/\">Tim Howard</a> first read about this case, it struck him as a sad but seemingly straightforward custody dispute. But, as he started talking to lawyers and historians and the families involved in the case, it became clear that it was much more than that. Because <em>Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl </em>challenges parts of the 1978 Indian Child Welfare Act, this case puts one little girl at the center of a storm of legal intricacies, Native American tribal culture, and heart-wrenching personal stakes.</p>\n<p><strong>UPDATE: The Supreme Court has made its ruling in <em>Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl</em>. Hear our update at the end of this episode.<br></strong></p>\n<h3>Background and Reporting from a range of different perspectives</h3>\n<p>\"<strong><a href=\"http://www.postandcourier.com/article/20120101/PC1602/301019972/1282/couple-forced-to-give-up-daughter\" target=\"_blank\">Couple forced to give up daughter</a></strong>\"<br> <em>An introductory article by Allyson Bird, for the Charleston, SC Post and Courier</em></p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"http://www.postandcourier.com/section/PC160444\" target=\"_blank\">Comprehensive coverage of the case by the Charleston <em>Post and Courier</em></a></strong></p>\n<p>\"<strong><a href=\"http://indiancountrytodaymedianetwork.com/2013/04/16/supreme-court-takes-indian-child-welfare-act-baby-veronica-case-148855\" target=\"_blank\">Supreme Court Takes on Indian Child Welfare Act in Baby Veronica Case</a></strong>\"<em> </em><br> <em>A report for Indian Country Today by Suzette Brewer, who has also written a <a href=\"http://indiancountrytodaymedianetwork.com/2013/05/06/fight-baby-veronica-part-i-149219\" target=\"_blank\">two-part series</a> on the case.</em></p>\n<p>\"<strong><a href=\"http://www.tulsaworld.com/site/printerfriendlystory.aspx?articleid=20130415_11_A1_ULNSut262929\" target=\"_blank\">Supreme Court hears Indian child custody case</a></strong>\"<br><em>Tulsa World</em> article by Michael Overall which includes Dusten Brown's account of his break-up with Veronica's mother, and his understanding about his custodial rights. Plus photos of Dusten, Veronica, and Dusten's wife Robin in their Oklahoma home<em>.</em></p>\n<p>Randi Kaye's report for CNN on the background of the case, and interviews with Melanie and Matt Capobianco: \"<strong><a href=\"http://ac360.blogs.cnn.com/2012/02/22/video-adoption-custody-battle-for-veronica/?hpt=ac_bn1\" target=\"_blank\">Video: Adoption custody battle for Veronica</a>\"</strong><br> <object data=\"http://i.cdn.turner.com/cnn/.element/apps/cvp/3.0/swf/cnn_embed_2x_container.swf?site=cnn&amp;profile=desktop&amp;context=embed&amp;videoId=bestoftv/2012/02/22/ac-kaye-veronica-custody-appeal.cnn&amp;contentId=bestoftv/2012/02/22/ac-kaye-veronica-custody-appeal.cnn\" type=\"application/x-shockwave-flash\" width=\"620\" height=\"349\">\n<param value=\"#000000\">\n<param name=\"bgcolor\" value=\"#000000\">\n<param name=\"src\" value=\"http://i.cdn.turner.com/cnn/.element/apps/cvp/3.0/swf/cnn_embed_2x_container.swf?site=cnn&amp;profile=desktop&amp;context=embed&amp;videoId=bestoftv/2012/02/22/ac-kaye-veronica-custody-appeal.cnn&amp;contentId=bestoftv/2012/02/22/ac-kaye-veronica-custody-appeal.cnn\">\n<param name=\"wmode\" value=\"transparent\">\n<param name=\"allowfullscreen\" value=\"true\"></object></p>\n<p>Nina Totenberg’s report for NPR: \"<strong><a href=\"http://www.npr.org/2013/04/16/177327391/adoption-case-brings-rare-family-law-dispute-to-high-court?ft=1&amp;f=1070\" target=\"_blank\">Adoption Case Brings Rare Family Law Dispute To High Court</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p>Reporting by NPR's Laura Sullivan and Amy Walters on <strong><a href=\"http://www.npr.org/2013/02/06/171310945/south-dakota-tribes-accuse-state-of-violating-indian-welfare-act\" target=\"_blank\">current ICWA violations in South Dakota</a></strong>.</p>\n<p>Dr. Phil's coverage: \"<strong><a href=\"http://www.drphil.com/shows/show/1895\" target=\"_blank\">Adoption Controversy: Battle over Baby Veronica\"</a></strong></p>\n<h3>Analysis and Editorials</h3>\n<p>Op-ed by Veronica's birth mom, Christy Maldonado, in the <em>Washington Post</em>: \"<span><a href=\"http://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/baby-veronicas-birth-mother-girl-belongs-with-adoptive-parents/2013/07/12/40d38a12-e995-11e2-a301-ea5a8116d211_story.html\" target=\"_blank\">Baby Veronica belongs with her adoptive parents</a>\"</span></p>\n<p><em>Colorlines </em>report \"<strong><a href=\"http://colorlines.com/archives/2013/04/the_cherokee_nations_baby_girl_goes_on_trial.html\">The Cherokee Nation’s Baby Girl Goes on Trial</a></strong>:\"</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Americans remain dangerously uninformed about the basics of tribal sovereignty, and what it means for the relationship between the United States and Native tribes and nations.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><em>The Weekly Standard's</em> Ethan Epstein argues that ICWA is \"being used to tear [families] apart]: \"<strong><a href=\"http://www.weeklystandard.com/articles/mistreating-native-american-children_649719.html\" target=\"_blank\">Mistreating Native American Children</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p>Andrew Cohen considers the trickier legal aspects of the case for the <em>Atlantic</em> in \"<strong><a href=\"http://www.theatlantic.com/national/archive/2013/04/indian-affairs-adoption-and-race-the-baby-veronica-case-comes-to-washington/274758/\" target=\"_blank\">Indian Affairs, Adoption, and Race: The Baby Veronica Case Comes to Washington</a></strong>:\"</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A little girl is at the heart of a big case at the Supreme Court next week, a racially-tinged fight over Native American rights and state custody laws.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Marcia Zug's breakdown of the case (Marica Zug is an associate professor of law at the University of South Carolina School of Law who she specializes in family and American Indian law) \"<strong><a href=\"http://www.slate.com/articles/double_x/doublex/2012/08/baby_veronica_returns_to_her_biological_father_affirming_icwa_south_carolina_s_supreme_court_made_the_right_decision_.html\" target=\"_blank\">Doing What’s Best for the Tribe</a></strong>\" for <em>Slate</em>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Two-year-old “Baby Veronica” was ripped from the only home she’s known. The court made the right decision.  </p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Marcia Zug for the Michigan Law Review: \"<strong><a href=\"http://www.michiganlawreview.org/articles/em-adoptive-couple-v-baby-girl-em-two-and-a-half-ways-to-destroy-indian-law\" target=\"_blank\">Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl: Two-and-a-Half WAys To Destroy Indian Law</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p>From Walter Olson, a senior fellow at the Cato Institute’s Center for Constitutional Studies: \"<strong><a href=\"http://reason.com/archives/2013/04/22/the-constitutional-flaws-of-the-indian-c\" target=\"_blank\">The Constitutional Flaws of the Indian Child Welfare Act</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p><em>Rapid City Journal</em> columnist David Rooks poses a set of tough questions about ICWA: \"<strong><a href=\"http://rapidcityjournal.com/news/opinion/rooks-questions-unasked-unanswered/article_efa51d54-a5d9-56dc-8f45-4455206d356c.html\" target=\"_blank\">ROOKS: Questions unasked, unanswered</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p>From Johnston Moore, an adoptive father of six children, three of whom are part Indian. (Moore is director and co-founder of Home Forever, and a founding member of the Coalition for the Protection of Indian Children &amp; Families. NewsOK): \"<strong><a href=\"http://newsok.com/some-different-talking-points-about-indian-child-welfare-act/article/3695665/?page=1\" target=\"_blank\">Some different talking points about Indian Child Welfare Act</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p>Editorial coverage from <em>The New York Times</em>:</p>\n<p>\"<strong><a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/16/opinion/a-wrenching-adoption-under-the-indian-child-welfare-act.html\" target=\"_blank\">A Wrenching Adoption Case</a></strong>\"</p>\n<p>\"<strong><a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/roomfordebate/2013/01/24/adoptive-parents-vs-tribal-rights\" target=\"_blank\">Adoptive Parents vs. Tribal Rights</a></strong>\"</p>\n<h3>Contemporary, Historic, and Legal Source Materials</h3>\n<p>Adoptive Couple v. Baby Girl on the <strong><a href=\"http://www.scotusblog.com/case-files/cases/adoptive-couple-v-baby-girl/\" target=\"_blank\">SCOTUS (Supreme Court of the United States) Blog</a></strong></p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"http://www.oyez.org/cases/2010-2019/2012/2012_12_399\" target=\"_blank\">Audio from the oral arguments in the Supreme Court</a></strong></p>\n<p><object id=\"oyez_movie\" data=\"http://www.oyez.org/sites/default/files/OyezPlayer-mini.swf?media_url=/transcripts/2012/12-399_20130416-argument_1.xml&amp;base_url=http://www.oyez.org/sites/default/files/&amp;nobutton=1\" type=\"application/x-shockwave-flash\" width=\"620\" height=\"250\">\n<param name=\"quality\" value=\"high\">\n<param name=\"bgcolor\" value=\"#ffffff\">\n<param name=\"allowScriptAccess\" value=\"always\">\n<param name=\"src\" value=\"http://www.oyez.org/sites/default/files/OyezPlayer-mini.swf?media_url=/transcripts/2012/12-399_20130416-argument_1.xml&amp;base_url=http://www.oyez.org/sites/default/files/&amp;nobutton=1\">\n<param name=\"name\" value=\"oyez_movie\"></object></p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"http://www.icwlc.org/icwaoutline.html\" target=\"_blank\">Official website for ICWA</a></strong> (the federal Indian Child Welfare Act)</p>\n<p>1974 Hearings Before the Subcommitee on Indian Affairs \"<strong><a href=\"http://narf.org/icwa/federal/lh/hear040874/hear040874.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">on problems that American Indian families face in raising their children and how these problems are affected by federal action or inaction</a></strong>.\" PDF</p>\n<p>The <strong><a href=\"http://nicwa.org/\" target=\"_blank\">National Indian Child Welfare Association</a></strong></p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"http://www.saveveronica.org/\" target=\"_blank\">SaveVeronica.org</a></strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The <strong><a href=\"http://www.wearecominghome.com/\" target=\"_blank\">First Nations Repatriation Institute</a></strong>, which works with and does advocacy for adoptees</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-05-30T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"294289-video-radiolab-behind-scenes", "title"=>"VIDEO: Radiolab Behind the Scenes", "audio"=>nil, "description"=>"<p>If you've ever wondered how the podcast comes together, or what it's like to work at Radiolab, here's a peek into our process.</p><p>If you're a fan of the show, help us make more Radiolab by <strong><a title=\"Donate to Radiolab\" href=\"http://radiolab.org/donate\" target=\"_blank\">making a contribution</a></strong>. Thanks so much, Radiolab couldn't exist without support from listeners like you!</p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://player.vimeo.com/video/66583939?title=0&amp;byline=0&amp;portrait=0&amp;color=ff9933\" width=\"620\"></iframe></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to David Fine and American Hipster. Their complete Radiolab video can be seen at <a href=\"http://americanhipsterpresents.com\" target=\"_blank\">americanhipsterpresents.com</a></em></p>", "tease"=>"If you've ever wondered how the podcast comes together, or what it's like to work at Radiolab, here's a peek into our process.", "publishTime"=>1369083600000, "publish-at"=>"2013-05-20T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1369083600, "estimated-duration"=>0, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Jad Abumrad and Robert Krulwich in studio", "name"=>"1/still_jad_robert_studio_web.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/366/c/80/1/still_jad_robert_studio_web.jpg", "h"=>366, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/still_jad_robert_studio_web.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>97566, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"294289", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"VIDEO: Radiolab Behind the Scenes", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>If you've ever wondered how the podcast comes together, or what it's like to work at Radiolab, here's a peek into our process.</p><p>If you're a fan of the show, help us make more Radiolab by <strong><a title=\"Donate to Radiolab\" href=\"http://radiolab.org/donate\" target=\"_blank\">making a contribution</a></strong>. Thanks so much, Radiolab couldn't exist without support from listeners like you!</p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://player.vimeo.com/video/66583939?title=0&amp;byline=0&amp;portrait=0&amp;color=ff9933\" width=\"620\"></iframe></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to David Fine and American Hipster. Their complete Radiolab video can be seen at <a href=\"http://americanhipsterpresents.com\" target=\"_blank\">americanhipsterpresents.com</a></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-05-20T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"282571-distance-of-moon", "title"=>"The Distance of the Moon", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13calvino.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=282571", "description"=>"<p>What if the moon were just a jump away? In this short, a beautiful answer to that question from Italo Calvino, read live by Liev Schreiber. </p><p>According to one theory, the moon formed when a Mars-sized chunk of rock collided with Earth. After the moon coalesced out of the debris from that impact, it was much closer to Earth than it is today. This idea is taken to it's fanciful limit in Italo Calvino's story \"The Distance of the Moon\" (from his collection <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0156226006/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Cosmicomics</em></a></span>, translated by William Weaver). The story, narrated by a character with the impossible-to-pronounce name Qfwfq, tells of a strange crew who jump between Earth and moon, and sometimes hover in the nether reaches of gravity between the two.</p>\n<p>This reading was part of a live event hosted by Radiolab and Selected Shorts, and it originally aired on WNYC’s and PRI’s SELECTED SHORTS, paired with a Ray Bradbury classic, “All Summer in a Day,” read by musical theater star Michael Cerveris. Hosted by BD Wong, you can <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/shorts/2013/apr/07/\" target=\"_blank\">listen to the full show here</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"According to one theory, the moon formed when a Mars-sized chunk of rock collided with Earth.", "publishTime"=>1366146000000, "publish-at"=>"2013-04-16T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1366146000, "estimated-duration"=>2431, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Liev Schreiber", "name"=>"1/LievS_Headshot_web_cr_Ben_Watts_Corbis_Online.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1490/1500/c/80/1/LievS_Headshot_web_cr_Ben_Watts_Corbis_Online.jpg", "h"=>1500, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Liev Schreiber", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/LievS_Headshot_web_cr_Ben_Watts_Corbis_Online.jpg", "w"=>1490, "id"=>90583, "credits-name"=>"Photo by Ben Watts -- Corbis Online"}, "objectID"=>"282571", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Distance of the Moon", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>What if the moon were just a jump away? In this short, a beautiful answer to that question from Italo Calvino, read live by Liev Schreiber. </p><p>According to one theory, the moon formed when a Mars-sized chunk of rock collided with Earth. After the moon coalesced out of the debris from that impact, it was much closer to Earth than it is today. This idea is taken to it's fanciful limit in Italo Calvino's story \"The Distance of the Moon\" (from his collection <span class=\"book\"><a title=\"buy this book at Amazon\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0156226006/radiolabbooks-20/\"><em>Cosmicomics</em></a></span>, translated by William Weaver). The story, narrated by a character with the impossible-to-pronounce name Qfwfq, tells of a strange crew who jump between Earth and moon, and sometimes hover in the nether reaches of gravity between the two.</p>\n<p>This reading was part of a live event hosted by Radiolab and Selected Shorts, and it originally aired on WNYC’s and PRI’s SELECTED SHORTS, paired with a Ray Bradbury classic, “All Summer in a Day,” read by musical theater star Michael Cerveris. Hosted by BD Wong, you can <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/shorts/2013/apr/07/\" target=\"_blank\">listen to the full show here</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-04-16T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"279566-radiolab-presents-tj-dave", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: TJ & Dave", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13tjdave.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=279566", "description"=>"<p>Improv comedy puts uncertainty on center stage -- performers usually start by asking the audience for a prompt, then they make up the details as they go. But two actors in Chicago are taking this idea to its absolute limit, and finding ways to navigate the unknown.</p><p>When<a href=\"http://tjanddave.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><strong> TJ Jagodowski</strong> and <strong>Dave Pasquesi</strong> </a>get on stage they introduce themselves, work the crowd a bit ... and then, the lights go off. And when the lights come back on, they're just standing there, staring at each other. The audience is waiting, wondering what's going to happen. And so are TJ and Dave. There are no audience suggestions to kick off the show, there’s no plan -- TJ and Dave begin each night as a complete blank, without even a glimmer of an <em>idea</em> about who they're going to be for the next hour, where they'll find themselves, or what might happen with all those eyes on them. And yet, so far without fail, an elaborate, operatic, two-person play filled with incredibly rich characters and situations emerges. In this short, Robert Krulwich and producer Sean Cole talk to TJ and Dave about stepping into the unknown, take a peek into one of their performances, and discover a very unusual strategy for dealing with the stress of having no idea what's going to happen: just assume it’s been happening all along. </p>", "tease"=>"When&nbsp;TJ Jagodowski&nbsp;and&nbsp;Dave Pasquesi&nbsp;get on stage they introduce themselves, work the crowd a bit ... and then, the lights go off.", "publishTime"=>1364938020000, "publish-at"=>"2013-04-02T17:27:00", "date-line-ts"=>1364936400, "estimated-duration"=>942, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"TJ & Dave", "name"=>"1/tjd_stage_0718.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/852/709/c/80/1/tjd_stage_0718.jpg", "h"=>709, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/31646672@N06/7556016608/sizes/o/in/set-72157630543287192/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/tjd_stage_0718.jpg", "w"=>852, "id"=>90058, "credits-name"=>"Jerry Schulman, used with permission (all rights reserved)"}, "objectID"=>"279566", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: TJ & Dave", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Improv comedy puts uncertainty on center stage -- performers usually start by asking the audience for a prompt, then they make up the details as they go. But two actors in Chicago are taking this idea to its absolute limit, and finding ways to navigate the unknown.</p><p>When<a href=\"http://tjanddave.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><strong> TJ Jagodowski</strong> and <strong>Dave Pasquesi</strong> </a>get on stage they introduce themselves, work the crowd a bit ... and then, the lights go off. And when the lights come back on, they're just standing there, staring at each other. The audience is waiting, wondering what's going to happen. And so are TJ and Dave. There are no audience suggestions to kick off the show, there’s no plan -- TJ and Dave begin each night as a complete blank, without even a glimmer of an <em>idea</em> about who they're going to be for the next hour, where they'll find themselves, or what might happen with all those eyes on them. And yet, so far without fail, an elaborate, operatic, two-person play filled with incredibly rich characters and situations emerges. In this short, Robert Krulwich and producer Sean Cole talk to TJ and Dave about stepping into the unknown, take a peek into one of their performances, and discover a very unusual strategy for dealing with the stress of having no idea what's going to happen: just assume it’s been happening all along. </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-04-02T17:27:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"278075-are-you-sure", "title"=>"Are You Sure?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab032613.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=278075", "description"=>"<p>This hour, we walk the tightrope between doubt and certainty, and wonder if there's a way to make yourself at home on that razor's edge between definitely...and not so sure.</p><p>We meet a geologist whose life is rocked by a crisis of faith, talk to a gambler who's made a name (and millions) by embracing what she can't know, and we relive a series of decisions and convictions that turn one woman's certainty into a deeply troubling question about just how certain is certain enough.</p>\n<p><em>NOTE: This episode contains graphic descriptions of sexual assault and violence.</em></p>", "tease"=>"<p>Stories about walking the tightrope between doubt and certainty.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>", "publishTime"=>1364335200000, "publish-at"=>"2013-03-26T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1364270400, "estimated-duration"=>4096, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"A narrow rope bridge", "name"=>"1/rope_bridge.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/680/c/80/1/rope_bridge.jpg", "h"=>680, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/42447934@N05/3916764179/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/rope_bridge.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>89781, "credits-name"=>"Deniz Sarac"}, "objectID"=>"278075", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Are You Sure?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This hour, we walk the tightrope between doubt and certainty, and wonder if there's a way to make yourself at home on that razor's edge between definitely...and not so sure.</p><p>We meet a geologist whose life is rocked by a crisis of faith, talk to a gambler who's made a name (and millions) by embracing what she can't know, and we relive a series of decisions and convictions that turn one woman's certainty into a deeply troubling question about just how certain is certain enough.</p>\n<p><em>NOTE: This episode contains graphic descriptions of sexual assault and violence.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-03-26T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"276577-rebroadcast-emergence", "title"=>"REBROADCAST: Emergence", "audio"=>"https://www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/http://audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab081407pod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=276577", "description"=>"<p>This spring, parts of the East Coast will turn squishy and crunchy -- the return of the 17-year cicadas means surfaces in certain locations (in patches from VA to CT) will once again be coated in bugs buzzing at 7 kilohertz. In their honor, we're rebroadcasting one of our favorite episodes: Emergence.</p><p>In this classic hour, we take a look at the bottom-up logic of cities, Google, and even our very own brains...  with fire-flyologists, ant experts, neurologists, a mathematician, and  an economist.</p>\n<p>We'll be back next week with a brand-new hour. In the meantime, enjoy <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2007/aug/14/\" target=\"_blank\">Emergence</a>, and consider rolling up your sleeves for some DIY bug science: help us <a href=\"http://project.wnyc.org/cicadas/\" target=\"_blank\">track the return of the </a><em><a href=\"http://project.wnyc.org/cicadas/\" target=\"_blank\">Magicicada Brood II</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"This spring, parts of the East Coast will turn squishy and crunchy -- the return of the 17-year cicadas means surfaces in certain locations (in patches from VA to CT) will once again ...", "publishTime"=>1363726800000, "publish-at"=>"2013-03-19T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1363726800, "estimated-duration"=>3467, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"17-Year Periodical Cicada - Brood XIII (Magicicada sp.)", "name"=>"1/emergence_rebroadcast_magicicada_brood_xiii.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/c/80/1/emergence_rebroadcast_magicicada_brood_xiii.jpg", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"17-Year Periodical Cicada - Brood XIII (Magicicada sp.)", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/50352333@N06/4647642398/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/emergence_rebroadcast_magicicada_brood_xiii.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>89342, "credits-name"=>"Jason Sturner 72"}, "objectID"=>"276577", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"REBROADCAST: Emergence", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This spring, parts of the East Coast will turn squishy and crunchy -- the return of the 17-year cicadas means surfaces in certain locations (in patches from VA to CT) will once again be coated in bugs buzzing at 7 kilohertz. In their honor, we're rebroadcasting one of our favorite episodes: Emergence.</p><p>In this classic hour, we take a look at the bottom-up logic of cities, Google, and even our very own brains...  with fire-flyologists, ant experts, neurologists, a mathematician, and  an economist.</p>\n<p>We'll be back next week with a brand-new hour. In the meantime, enjoy <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/2007/aug/14/\" target=\"_blank\">Emergence</a>, and consider rolling up your sleeves for some DIY bug science: help us <a href=\"http://project.wnyc.org/cicadas/\" target=\"_blank\">track the return of the </a><em><a href=\"http://project.wnyc.org/cicadas/\" target=\"_blank\">Magicicada Brood II</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-03-19T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"273532-heimlich", "title"=>"The Man Behind the Maneuver", "audio"=>"https://www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/http://audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13heimlich.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=273532", "description"=>"<p>In the 1970s, choking became national news: thousands were choking to death, leading to more accidental deaths than guns. Nobody knew what to do. Until a man named Henry Heimlich came along with a big idea. Since then, thousands and thousands -- maybe even millions -- have been rescued by the Heimlich maneuver. Yet the story of the man who invented it may not have such a happy ending.</p><p>Producer Pat Walters wouldn't be here without the Heimlich maneuver -- it saved his life when he was just 11 years old. And one day he started wondering -- who was Heimlich, anyway? And how did he come up with his choking remedy? Pat had always kinda assumed Heimlich died in the mid-1800s. Not so. The man is very much alive: he's 93 years old, and calls Cincinnati, Ohio home.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Producer’s note:</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>We made some minor changes to this story that do not alter the substance.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>First, we removed the audio of Peter Heimlich, Henry Heimlich’s son, from the version now on the site. When we approached Henry’s other son Phil to arrange an interview with his father, one of Phil’s conditions was that we not air audio of Peter. We thought he’d waived that provision in a subsequent conversation but he contends he did not. So we are honoring the original request.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Second, we originally reported that Henry Heimlich was involved in a train wreck when he was 19 years old and on the way to summer camp. In fact the wreck happened on the way back to New York City from summer camp, in the summer of 1941, making Heimlich 21 years old. We also stated that a woman whose husband attempted to perform an emergency tracheotomy on her bled to death, when in fact she choked to death. The audio has been adjusted to correct these facts.</em></p>", "tease"=>"In the 1970s, choking became national news: thousands were choking to death, leading to more accidental deaths than guns.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1362520800000, "publish-at"=>"2013-03-05T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1362520800, "estimated-duration"=>1487, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Two figures performing the Heimlich Maneuver", "name"=>"1/Heimlich-web_tout.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/526/598/c/80/1/Heimlich-web_tout.jpg", "h"=>598, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Heimlich-web_tout.jpg", "w"=>526, "id"=>88653, "credits-name"=>"Larry Buchanan"}, "objectID"=>"273532", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Man Behind the Maneuver", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In the 1970s, choking became national news: thousands were choking to death, leading to more accidental deaths than guns. Nobody knew what to do. Until a man named Henry Heimlich came along with a big idea. Since then, thousands and thousands -- maybe even millions -- have been rescued by the Heimlich maneuver. Yet the story of the man who invented it may not have such a happy ending.</p><p>Producer Pat Walters wouldn't be here without the Heimlich maneuver -- it saved his life when he was just 11 years old. And one day he started wondering -- who was Heimlich, anyway? And how did he come up with his choking remedy? Pat had always kinda assumed Heimlich died in the mid-1800s. Not so. The man is very much alive: he's 93 years old, and calls Cincinnati, Ohio home.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Producer’s note:</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>We made some minor changes to this story that do not alter the substance.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>First, we removed the audio of Peter Heimlich, Henry Heimlich’s son, from the version now on the site. When we approached Henry’s other son Phil to arrange an interview with his father, one of Phil’s conditions was that we not air audio of Peter. We thought he’d waived that provision in a subsequent conversation but he contends he did not. So we are honoring the original request.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Second, we originally reported that Henry Heimlich was involved in a train wreck when he was 19 years old and on the way to summer camp. In fact the wreck happened on the way back to New York City from summer camp, in the summer of 1941, making Heimlich 21 years old. We also stated that a woman whose husband attempted to perform an emergency tracheotomy on her bled to death, when in fact she choked to death. The audio has been adjusted to correct these facts.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-03-05T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"267124-speed", "title"=>"Speed", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab020513.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=267124", "description"=>"<p>We live our lives at human speed, we experience and interact with the world on a human time scale. But this hour, we put ourselves through the paces, peek inside a microsecond, and master the fastest thing in the universe.</p>", "tease"=>"<p>The inhumanly fast world of high-speed trading, an excruciatingly slow experiment, and a physicist plays Zeus.</p>", "publishTime"=>1360103700000, "publish-at"=>"2013-02-05T17:35:00", "date-line-ts"=>1360040400, "estimated-duration"=>3412, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Illustration of person jumping over the hands of stopwatch", "name"=>"1/Speed_episode.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/614/c/80/1/Speed_episode.jpg", "h"=>614, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://daniel-horowitz.com/category/illustration/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Speed_episode.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>87264, "credits-name"=>"Daniel Horowitz"}, "objectID"=>"267124", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Speed", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>We live our lives at human speed, we experience and interact with the world on a human time scale. But this hour, we put ourselves through the paces, peek inside a microsecond, and master the fastest thing in the universe.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-02-05T17:35:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"262588-bitter-end", "title"=>"The Bitter End", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast13doctors.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=262588", "description"=>"<p>We turn to doctors to save our lives -- to heal us, repair us, and keep us healthy. But when it comes to the critical question of what to do when death is at hand, there seems to be a gap between what we want doctors to do for us, and what doctors want done for themselves.</p><p>Producer <strong><a class=\"guestlink\" href=\"/people/sean-cole/\">Sean Cole</a></strong> introduces us to <strong><a class=\"guestlink\" href=\"/people/joseph-gallo/\">Joseph Gallo</a></strong>, a doctor and professor at Johns Hopkins University who discovered something striking about what doctors were <em>not </em>willing to do to save their own lives. As part of the decades-long <a href=\"http://www.jhu.edu/jhumag/0601web/study.html\" target=\"_blank\">Johns Hopkins Precursors Study</a>, Gallo found himself asking the study's aging doctor-subjects questions about death. Their answers, it turns out, don't sync up with the answers most of us give.</p>\n<p><strong><a class=\"guestlink\" href=\"/people/ken-murray/\">Ken Murray</a></strong>, a doctor who's written several articles about how doctors think about death, explains that there's a huge gap between what patients expect from life-saving interventions (such as CPR, ventilation, and feeding tubes), and what doctors think of these very same procedures.</p>\n<p>Jad attempts to bridge the gap with a difficult conversation -- he asks his father, a doctor, why he's made the decisions he has about his own end-of-life care... and whether it was different when he had to answer the same questions for his father and mother.</p>\n<p>A chart of doctor responses from the Precursors Study:</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/Gallo-figure-2.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p><em>Preferences of physician-participants for treatment given a scenario of irreversible brain injury without terminal illness. Percentage of physicians shown on the vertical axis. For cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), surgery, and invasive diagnostic testing, no choice for a trial of treatment was given. Data from the Johns Hopkins Precursors Study, 1998. Courtesy of Joseph Gallo, \"Life-Sustaining Treatments: What Do Physicians Want and Do They Express Their Wishes to Others?\"</em></p>", "tease"=>"Producer Sean Cole introduces us to Joseph Gallo, a doctor and professor at Johns Hopkins University.", "publishTime"=>1358287200000, "publish-at"=>"2013-01-15T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1358287200, "estimated-duration"=>1258, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Doctor holding stethoscope", "name"=>"1/doctor_stethoscope.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/414/c/80/1/doctor_stethoscope.jpg", "h"=>414, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/proimos/6869336880/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/doctor_stethoscope.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>86302, "credits-name"=>"Alex E. Proimos"}, "objectID"=>"262588", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Bitter End", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>We turn to doctors to save our lives -- to heal us, repair us, and keep us healthy. But when it comes to the critical question of what to do when death is at hand, there seems to be a gap between what we want doctors to do for us, and what doctors want done for themselves.</p><p>Producer <strong><a class=\"guestlink\" href=\"/people/sean-cole/\">Sean Cole</a></strong> introduces us to <strong><a class=\"guestlink\" href=\"/people/joseph-gallo/\">Joseph Gallo</a></strong>, a doctor and professor at Johns Hopkins University who discovered something striking about what doctors were <em>not </em>willing to do to save their own lives. As part of the decades-long <a href=\"http://www.jhu.edu/jhumag/0601web/study.html\" target=\"_blank\">Johns Hopkins Precursors Study</a>, Gallo found himself asking the study's aging doctor-subjects questions about death. Their answers, it turns out, don't sync up with the answers most of us give.</p>\n<p><strong><a class=\"guestlink\" href=\"/people/ken-murray/\">Ken Murray</a></strong>, a doctor who's written several articles about how doctors think about death, explains that there's a huge gap between what patients expect from life-saving interventions (such as CPR, ventilation, and feeding tubes), and what doctors think of these very same procedures.</p>\n<p>Jad attempts to bridge the gap with a difficult conversation -- he asks his father, a doctor, why he's made the decisions he has about his own end-of-life care... and whether it was different when he had to answer the same questions for his father and mother.</p>\n<p>A chart of doctor responses from the Precursors Study:</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/Gallo-figure-2.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p><em>Preferences of physician-participants for treatment given a scenario of irreversible brain injury without terminal illness. Percentage of physicians shown on the vertical axis. For cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), surgery, and invasive diagnostic testing, no choice for a trial of treatment was given. Data from the Johns Hopkins Precursors Study, 1998. Courtesy of Joseph Gallo, \"Life-Sustaining Treatments: What Do Physicians Want and Do They Express Their Wishes to Others?\"</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2013-01-15T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2014=>[{"slug"=>"worth", "title"=>"Worth", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab122314.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=420943", "description"=>"<p><span>This episode, we make three earnest, possibly foolhardy, attempts to put a price on the priceless. We figure out the dollar value for an accidental death, another day of life, and the work of bats and bees as we try to keep our careful calculations from falling apart in the face of the realities of life, and love, and loss.</span></p>", "tease"=>"We&rsquo;ve gathered a handful of stories that show how every time we think we&rsquo;ve settled on a price for something, it slips out of our grasp.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1418862945000, "publish-at"=>"2014-12-17T19:35:45", "date-line-ts"=>1419354000, "estimated-duration"=>4292, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Paresh_Gajria.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/997/l/80/1/Paresh_Gajria.jpg", "h"=>997, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Paresh_Gajria.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>129999, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Paresh Gadjria"}, "objectID"=>"420943", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Worth", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This episode, we make three earnest, possibly foolhardy, attempts to put a price on the priceless. We figure out the dollar value for an accidental death, another day of life, and the work of bats and bees as we try to keep our careful calculations from falling apart in the face of the realities of life, and love, and loss.</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-12-17T19:35:45", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"buttons-not-buttons", "title"=>"Buttons Not Buttons", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14buttonsnotbuttons.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=419346", "description"=>"<p>Buttons are usually small and unimportant. But not always. Sometimes they are a portal to power, freedom, and destruction. Today we thread together tales of taking charge of the little things in life, of fortunes made and lost, and of the ease with which the world can end. </p>\n<p>Confused? Push the button marked Play.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Special thanks for the music of <a href=\"http://ghosttrainorchestra.com/\">Brian Carpenter's Ghost Train Orchestra</a></em></p>", "tease"=>"A quartet of buttons that may just leave you stuck, rich, ugly, or dead.&nbsp;Confused? Push the button marked &ldquo;Play&rdquo;.", "publishTime"=>1418414863000, "publish-at"=>"2014-12-12T15:07:43", "date-line-ts"=>1418414863, "estimated-duration"=>1569, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/3085157011_f67824fabb_o.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/1000/l/80/1/3085157011_f67824fabb_o.jpg", "h"=>1000, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/3085157011_f67824fabb_o.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>129663, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Włodi"}, "objectID"=>"419346", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Buttons Not Buttons", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Buttons are usually small and unimportant. But not always. Sometimes they are a portal to power, freedom, and destruction. Today we thread together tales of taking charge of the little things in life, of fortunes made and lost, and of the ease with which the world can end. </p>\n<p>Confused? Push the button marked Play.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Special thanks for the music of <a href=\"http://ghosttrainorchestra.com/\">Brian Carpenter's Ghost Train Orchestra</a></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-12-12T15:07:43", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"outside-westgate", "title"=>"Outside Westgate", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14outsidewestgate.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=416794", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">In the wake of public tragedy there is a space between the official narrative and the stories of the people who experienced it. Today, we crawl inside that space and question the role of journalists in helping us move on from a traumatic event. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">NPR's East Africa correspondent Gregory Warner takes us back to the 2013 terrorist attacks on the Westgate Mall in Nairobi, Kenya. Warner reported on the attack as it happened, listening to eyewitness accounts, sorting out the facts, establishing the truth. But he's been been wrestling with it ever since as his friends and neighbors try not only to put their lives back together, but also try to piece together what really happened that day.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks to Jason Straziuso, Heidi Vogt, Robert Alai, Didi Schanche and Edith Chapin.</em></p>", "tease"=>"After&nbsp;a public tragedy, a&nbsp;reporter looks at&nbsp;the&nbsp;space between the stories of the people who experienced it and the&nbsp;official narrative.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1417288933000, "publish-at"=>"2014-11-29T14:22:13", "date-line-ts"=>1417288932, "estimated-duration"=>2185, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/181529155.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Getty"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/594/395/l/80/1/181529155.jpg", "h"=>395, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/181529155.jpg", "w"=>594, "id"=>128916, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Simon Maina"}, "objectID"=>"416794", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Outside Westgate", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">In the wake of public tragedy there is a space between the official narrative and the stories of the people who experienced it. Today, we crawl inside that space and question the role of journalists in helping us move on from a traumatic event. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">NPR's East Africa correspondent Gregory Warner takes us back to the 2013 terrorist attacks on the Westgate Mall in Nairobi, Kenya. Warner reported on the attack as it happened, listening to eyewitness accounts, sorting out the facts, establishing the truth. But he's been been wrestling with it ever since as his friends and neighbors try not only to put their lives back together, but also try to piece together what really happened that day.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks to Jason Straziuso, Heidi Vogt, Robert Alai, Didi Schanche and Edith Chapin.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-11-29T14:22:13", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"patient-zero-updated", "title"=>"Patient Zero - Updated", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab111314.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=413714", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">The greatest mysteries have a shadowy figure at the center—someone who sets things in motion and holds the key to how the story unfolds—Patient Zero. This hour, Radiolab hunts for Patient Zeroes of all kinds and considers the course of an ongoing outbreak.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">We start with the story of perhaps the most iconic Patient Zero of all time: Typhoid Mary. Then, we dive into a molecular detective story to pinpoint the beginning of the AIDS epidemic, and we re-imagine the moment the virus that caused the global pandemic sprang to life. After that, we update the show with a quick look at the very current Ebola outbreak in west Africa. In the end, we're left wondering if you can trace the spread of an idea the way you can trace the spread of a disease and find ourselves faced with competing claims about the origin of the high five.</p>", "tease"=>"The greatest mysteries have a shadowy figure at the center, Patient Zero.&nbsp;We hunt for Patient Zeroes from all over the map. &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1415917532000, "publish-at"=>"2014-11-13T17:25:32", "date-line-ts"=>1415898000, "estimated-duration"=>4086, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Mexico City train passengers wearing surgical masks", "name"=>"1/patient_zero.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/605/c/80/1/patient_zero.jpg", "h"=>605, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/eneas/3471986083/in/photolist-6hNQv2-7XGJf-8N7N9j-4mnXV9-cWtQv1-bokECF-dcqdHb-7T9doc-bWqDgR-e43Q7M-e4P5iR-ceBZ8Q-bD4fLt-4ZsQND-bqLWNZ-cemECf-cdUcmS-e4kY4G-n6p7r-8xuYpW-8xu", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/patient_zero.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>102360, "credits-name"=>"Eneas De Troya"}, "objectID"=>"413714", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Patient Zero - Updated", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">The greatest mysteries have a shadowy figure at the center—someone who sets things in motion and holds the key to how the story unfolds—Patient Zero. This hour, Radiolab hunts for Patient Zeroes of all kinds and considers the course of an ongoing outbreak.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">We start with the story of perhaps the most iconic Patient Zero of all time: Typhoid Mary. Then, we dive into a molecular detective story to pinpoint the beginning of the AIDS epidemic, and we re-imagine the moment the virus that caused the global pandemic sprang to life. After that, we update the show with a quick look at the very current Ebola outbreak in west Africa. In the end, we're left wondering if you can trace the spread of an idea the way you can trace the spread of a disease and find ourselves faced with competing claims about the origin of the high five.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-11-13T17:25:32", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"haunted", "title"=>"Haunted", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14haunted.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=410732", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Dennis Conrow was stuck. After a brief stint at college, he’d passed most of his 20’s back home with his parents, sleeping in his childhood room. And just when he finally struck out on his own, fate intervened. He lost both his parents to cancer. So Dennis was left, back in the house, alone. Until one night when a group of paranormal investigators showed up at his door and made him realize what it really means for a house, or a man, to be haunted.</p>", "tease"=>"How a group of paranormal investigators made one man realize what it really means for a house, or a man, to be haunted.", "publishTime"=>1414685155000, "publish-at"=>"2014-10-30T12:05:55", "date-line-ts"=>1414702800, "estimated-duration"=>1812, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/DennisHouse.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/1125/l/80/1/DennisHouse.png", "h"=>1125, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/DennisHouse.png", "w"=>1500, "id"=>127386, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Dennis Conrow"}, "objectID"=>"410732", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Haunted", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Dennis Conrow was stuck. After a brief stint at college, he’d passed most of his 20’s back home with his parents, sleeping in his childhood room. And just when he finally struck out on his own, fate intervened. He lost both his parents to cancer. So Dennis was left, back in the house, alone. Until one night when a group of paranormal investigators showed up at his door and made him realize what it really means for a house, or a man, to be haunted.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-10-30T12:05:55", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"john-luther-adams", "title"=>"John Luther Adams", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14johnlutheradams.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=404188", "description"=>"<p>What's the soundtrack for the end of the world? We go looking for an answer.</p>\n<p>When Jad started to compose music for <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/live/\">our live show Apocalyptical</a>, he immediately thought of John Luther Adams. <a href=\"http://cantaloupemusic.com/albums/become-ocean\">Adams' symphony “Become Ocean</a>,” rooted in the sounds of nature, is elemental, tectonic, and unstoppable. It seemed a natural fit for our consideration of the (spoiler alert) extinction of the dinosaurs.</p>\n<p>In this piece, Jad introduces Robert to a special on Adams from a podcast called <a href=\"http://www.wqxr.org/programs/meet-composer/\">Meet the Composer</a>. Through interviews and snippets of his music, it captures all the forces at play in Adam's work and reveals the dark majesty of Adams' take on the apocalypse.</p>", "tease"=>"What's the soundtrack for the end of the world? We go looking for an answer.", "publishTime"=>1412368042000, "publish-at"=>"2014-10-03T16:27:22", "date-line-ts"=>1412368041, "estimated-duration"=>1510, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Become_Ocean.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/844/c/80/1/Become_Ocean.jpg", "h"=>844, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Become_Ocean.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>125843, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Paul Chesley"}, "objectID"=>"404188", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"John Luther Adams", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>What's the soundtrack for the end of the world? We go looking for an answer.</p>\n<p>When Jad started to compose music for <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/live/\">our live show Apocalyptical</a>, he immediately thought of John Luther Adams. <a href=\"http://cantaloupemusic.com/albums/become-ocean\">Adams' symphony “Become Ocean</a>,” rooted in the sounds of nature, is elemental, tectonic, and unstoppable. It seemed a natural fit for our consideration of the (spoiler alert) extinction of the dinosaurs.</p>\n<p>In this piece, Jad introduces Robert to a special on Adams from a podcast called <a href=\"http://www.wqxr.org/programs/meet-composer/\">Meet the Composer</a>. Through interviews and snippets of his music, it captures all the forces at play in Adam's work and reveals the dark majesty of Adams' take on the apocalypse.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-10-03T16:27:22", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"juicervose", "title"=>"Juicervose", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14juicervose.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=401355", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Ron and Cornelia Suskind had two healthy young sons, promising careers, and a brand new home when their youngest son Owen started to disappear. </p>\n<p class=\"p2\">3 months later a specialist sat Ron and Cornelia down and said the word that changed everything for them: Autism. </p>\n<p class=\"p2\">In this episode, the Suskind family finds an unlikely way to access their silent son's world. We set off to figure out what their story can tell us about Autism, a disorder with a wide spectrum of symptoms and severity. Along the way, we speak to specialists, therapists, and advocates including Simon Baron-Cohen, Barry and Raun Kaufmann, Dave Royko, Geraldine Dawson, Temple Grandin, and Gil Tippy.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Produced by Kelsey Padgett.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Ron and Cornelia Suskind had two healthy young sons, thriving&nbsp;careers and a brand new home when their youngest,&nbsp;Owen, started to disappear.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1411072550000, "publish-at"=>"2014-09-18T16:35:50", "date-line-ts"=>1411072549, "estimated-duration"=>2619, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Suskind_Tighter.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/491/l/80/1/Suskind_Tighter.jpg", "h"=>491, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Ron, Owen and Cornelia Suskind", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Suskind_Tighter.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>124865, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Ron Suskind"}, "objectID"=>"401355", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Juicervose", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Ron and Cornelia Suskind had two healthy young sons, promising careers, and a brand new home when their youngest son Owen started to disappear. </p>\n<p class=\"p2\">3 months later a specialist sat Ron and Cornelia down and said the word that changed everything for them: Autism. </p>\n<p class=\"p2\">In this episode, the Suskind family finds an unlikely way to access their silent son's world. We set off to figure out what their story can tell us about Autism, a disorder with a wide spectrum of symptoms and severity. Along the way, we speak to specialists, therapists, and advocates including Simon Baron-Cohen, Barry and Raun Kaufmann, Dave Royko, Geraldine Dawson, Temple Grandin, and Gil Tippy.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Produced by Kelsey Padgett.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-09-18T16:35:50", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dust-planet", "title"=>"In The Dust Of This Planet", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14dustofthisplanet.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=399432", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Horror, fashion, and the end of the world … things get weird as we explore the undercurrents of thought that link nihilists, beard-stroking philosophers, Jay-Z, and True Detective.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Today on Radiolab, a puzzle. Jad’s brother-in-law <a href=\"http://www.zero-books.net/books/in-the-dust-of-this-planet\">wrote a book called 'In The Dust of This Planet'.</a></p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/400/620/c/80/1/Cover2.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\n<p class=\"p1\">It’s an academic treatise about the horror humanity feels as we realize that we are nothing but a speck in the universe. For a few years nobody read it. But then …</p>\n<p> </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/620/348/l/80/1/True_Detective.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\"><a href=\"http://blogs.wsj.com/speakeasy/2014/02/02/writer-nic-pizzolatto-on-thomas-ligotti-and-the-weird-secrets-of-true-detective/\">It seemed to show up on True Detective.</a></div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/594/867/l/80/1/Lilly_Collins.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Then in a fashion magazine.</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/620/348/l/80/1/JayZShot.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">And then on Jay-Z's back. How?</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">We talk nihilism with Eugene Thacker &amp; Simon Critchley, leather jackets with June Ambrose, climate change with David Victor, and hope with the father of Transcendental Black Metal - Hunter Hunt Hendrix of the band <a href=\"http://www.thrilljockey.com/thrill/Liturgy/Aesthethica#.VA9NM7ywK68\">Liturgy. Special thanks to Thrill Jockey</a> for use of the Liturgy song 'Generation'. <a href=\"http://www.thrilljockey.com/thrill/Liturgy/Aesthethica\">It's from their album Aesthetica, out now, which is highly recommended listening for the end times.</a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><a href=\"http://www.zero-books.net/books/in-the-dust-of-this-planet\">You can find Eugene Thacker's 'In The Dust Of the Planet' at Zero Books</a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Correction: An earlier version of this piece mistakenly identified Nic Pizzolatto as the director of True Detective, when he is in fact the creator, writer, and executive producer of the series. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact. Cary Fukunaga (brilliantly) directed season one of True Detective. </em></p>", "tease"=>"Horror, fashion, and the end of the world ... the undercurrents&nbsp;of thought that link nihilists, philosophers, Jay-Z and True Detective.", "publishTime"=>1410204877000, "publish-at"=>"2014-09-08T15:34:37", "date-line-ts"=>1410204876, "estimated-duration"=>2494, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/afterlight.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/394/394/h/80/1/afterlight.jpeg", "h"=>394, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/afterlight.jpeg", "w"=>394, "id"=>124207, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Zero Books"}, "objectID"=>"399432", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"In The Dust Of This Planet", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Horror, fashion, and the end of the world … things get weird as we explore the undercurrents of thought that link nihilists, beard-stroking philosophers, Jay-Z, and True Detective.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Today on Radiolab, a puzzle. Jad’s brother-in-law <a href=\"http://www.zero-books.net/books/in-the-dust-of-this-planet\">wrote a book called 'In The Dust of This Planet'.</a></p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/400/620/c/80/1/Cover2.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\n<p class=\"p1\">It’s an academic treatise about the horror humanity feels as we realize that we are nothing but a speck in the universe. For a few years nobody read it. But then …</p>\n<p> </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/620/348/l/80/1/True_Detective.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\"><a href=\"http://blogs.wsj.com/speakeasy/2014/02/02/writer-nic-pizzolatto-on-thomas-ligotti-and-the-weird-secrets-of-true-detective/\">It seemed to show up on True Detective.</a></div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/594/867/l/80/1/Lilly_Collins.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Then in a fashion magazine.</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/620/348/l/80/1/JayZShot.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">And then on Jay-Z's back. How?</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">We talk nihilism with Eugene Thacker &amp; Simon Critchley, leather jackets with June Ambrose, climate change with David Victor, and hope with the father of Transcendental Black Metal - Hunter Hunt Hendrix of the band <a href=\"http://www.thrilljockey.com/thrill/Liturgy/Aesthethica#.VA9NM7ywK68\">Liturgy. Special thanks to Thrill Jockey</a> for use of the Liturgy song 'Generation'. <a href=\"http://www.thrilljockey.com/thrill/Liturgy/Aesthethica\">It's from their album Aesthetica, out now, which is highly recommended listening for the end times.</a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><a href=\"http://www.zero-books.net/books/in-the-dust-of-this-planet\">You can find Eugene Thacker's 'In The Dust Of the Planet' at Zero Books</a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Correction: An earlier version of this piece mistakenly identified Nic Pizzolatto as the director of True Detective, when he is in fact the creator, writer, and executive producer of the series. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact. Cary Fukunaga (brilliantly) directed season one of True Detective. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-09-08T15:34:37", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"hello", "title"=>"Hello", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab082114.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=396440", "description"=>"<p>It's hard to start a conversation with a stranger—especially when that stranger is, well, different. He doesn't share your customs, celebrate your holidays, watch your TV shows, or even speak your language. Plus he has a blowhole.</p>\n<p>In this episode, we try to make contact with some of the strangest strangers on our little planet: dolphins. Producer Lynn Levy eavesdrops on some human-dolphin conversations, from a studio apartment in the Virgin Islands to a research vessel in the Bermuda Triangle.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Special thanks for the music of <a href=\"http://ghosttrainorchestra.com/\">Brian Carpenter's Ghost Train Orchestra</a></em></p>", "tease"=>"It's tough to make small talk with a stranger&mdash;especially when that stranger doesn't speak your language. (And&nbsp;he has a blowhole.)", "publishTime"=>1408655125000, "publish-at"=>"2014-08-21T17:05:25", "date-line-ts"=>1408636800, "estimated-duration"=>2748, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Silhouette of a child reaching up for a dolphin swimming in the deep blue water of an aquarium.", "name"=>"1/5400274929_0755d38b1d_o.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1239/l/80/1/5400274929_0755d38b1d_o.jpg", "h"=>1239, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/5400274929_0755d38b1d_o.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>123391, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: きうこ"}, "objectID"=>"396440", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Hello", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>It's hard to start a conversation with a stranger—especially when that stranger is, well, different. He doesn't share your customs, celebrate your holidays, watch your TV shows, or even speak your language. Plus he has a blowhole.</p>\n<p>In this episode, we try to make contact with some of the strangest strangers on our little planet: dolphins. Producer Lynn Levy eavesdrops on some human-dolphin conversations, from a studio apartment in the Virgin Islands to a research vessel in the Bermuda Triangle.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Special thanks for the music of <a href=\"http://ghosttrainorchestra.com/\">Brian Carpenter's Ghost Train Orchestra</a></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-08-21T17:05:25", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"happy-birthday-bobby-k", "title"=>"Happy Birthday Bobby K", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14happybirthdaybobby.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=393702", "description"=>"<p>It’s Robert’s birthday! (Or it was, anyway, a couple days back.) So today we celebrate with some classic Krulwich radio and a backwards peek into the spirit and sensibility that, in many ways, drives our show.</p>\n<p>For his birthday surprise we all listened to some old NPR pieces that Robert did in the 70s, 80s and early 90s — a news piece on the dawn of the ATM, a fake opera on interest rates, and the story of a family business splintered into relatives fighting to be first in the phone book. Along the way, we hear some incredible stories from Robert’s life … </p>\n<p>And, just to celebrate the man whose infectious curiosity draws so many people (including us) to his side … we share with you the kind of gonzo, full-throated Krulwich story we usually can’t include in the show … an epic of secret zoos, sewing machines, an alligator farm, a marching band, and a bus full of French tourists that save the day.</p>", "tease"=>"For&nbsp;Robert&rsquo;s birthday we celebrate with some classic Krulwich and a peek into the spirit and sensibility that, in many ways, drives our show.", "publishTime"=>1407445085000, "publish-at"=>"2014-08-07T16:58:05", "date-line-ts"=>1407445084, "estimated-duration"=>1770, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Krulwich.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/433/566/c/80/1/Krulwich.jpg", "h"=>566, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Krulwich.jpg", "w"=>433, "id"=>122612, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"393702", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Happy Birthday Bobby K", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>It’s Robert’s birthday! (Or it was, anyway, a couple days back.) So today we celebrate with some classic Krulwich radio and a backwards peek into the spirit and sensibility that, in many ways, drives our show.</p>\n<p>For his birthday surprise we all listened to some old NPR pieces that Robert did in the 70s, 80s and early 90s — a news piece on the dawn of the ATM, a fake opera on interest rates, and the story of a family business splintered into relatives fighting to be first in the phone book. Along the way, we hear some incredible stories from Robert’s life … </p>\n<p>And, just to celebrate the man whose infectious curiosity draws so many people (including us) to his side … we share with you the kind of gonzo, full-throated Krulwich story we usually can’t include in the show … an epic of secret zoos, sewing machines, an alligator farm, a marching band, and a bus full of French tourists that save the day.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-08-07T16:58:05", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"birds", "title"=>"For the Birds", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14forthebirds.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=390309", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Today, a lady with a bird in her backyard upends our whole sense of what we may have to give up to keep a wild creature wild.</p><p class=\"p1\">When the conservationists showed up at Clarice Gibbs’ door and asked her to take down her bird feeders down for the sake of an endangered bird, she said no. Everybody just figured she was a crazy bird lady. But writer Jon Mooallem went to see her and discovered there was much more to this story. Mrs. Gibbs tells us her surprising side of the tale, and together with Joe Duff, we struggle with the realization that keeping things wild in today's world will be harder than we ever would’ve thought.</p>", "tease"=>"When the conservationists showed up at Clarice Gibbs&rsquo; door and asked her to take down her bird feeders down for the sake of an endangered bird, she said no.", "publishTime"=>1406241990000, "publish-at"=>"2014-07-24T18:46:30", "date-line-ts"=>1406241990, "estimated-duration"=>870, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/6885949371_12315f9647_o.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/1000/l/80/1/6885949371_12315f9647_o.jpg", "h"=>1000, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/6885949371_12315f9647_o.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>122425, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: US Department of Agriculture"}, "objectID"=>"390309", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"For the Birds", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Today, a lady with a bird in her backyard upends our whole sense of what we may have to give up to keep a wild creature wild.</p><p class=\"p1\">When the conservationists showed up at Clarice Gibbs’ door and asked her to take down her bird feeders down for the sake of an endangered bird, she said no. Everybody just figured she was a crazy bird lady. But writer Jon Mooallem went to see her and discovered there was much more to this story. Mrs. Gibbs tells us her surprising side of the tale, and together with Joe Duff, we struggle with the realization that keeping things wild in today's world will be harder than we ever would’ve thought.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-07-24T18:46:30", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"galapagos", "title"=>"Galapagos", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab071814.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=388850", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Today, the strange story of a small group of islands that raise a big question: is it inevitable that even our most sacred natural landscapes will eventually get swallowed up by humans? And just how far are we willing to go to stop that from happening?</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">We are dedicating a whole hour to the Galapagos archipelago, the place that inspired Darwin’s theory of evolution and natural selection. 179 years later, the Galapagos are undergoing rapid changes that continue to pose -- and possibly answer -- critical questions about the fragility and resilience of life on Earth.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>For transcripts, see individual segment pages.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Is it inevitable that even our most sacred natural landscapes will eventually get swallowed up by humans?&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1405634289000, "publish-at"=>"2014-07-17T17:58:09", "date-line-ts"=>1405612800, "estimated-duration"=>3771, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/011941-01.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1200/801/c/80/1/011941-01.jpg", "h"=>801, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.lanting.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/011941-01.jpg", "w"=>1200, "id"=>121545, "credits-name"=>"Frans Lanting / www.lanting.com"}, "objectID"=>"388850", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Galapagos", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Today, the strange story of a small group of islands that raise a big question: is it inevitable that even our most sacred natural landscapes will eventually get swallowed up by humans? And just how far are we willing to go to stop that from happening?</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">We are dedicating a whole hour to the Galapagos archipelago, the place that inspired Darwin’s theory of evolution and natural selection. 179 years later, the Galapagos are undergoing rapid changes that continue to pose -- and possibly answer -- critical questions about the fragility and resilience of life on Earth.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>For transcripts, see individual segment pages.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-07-17T17:58:09", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"9-volt-nirvana", "title"=>"9-Volt Nirvana", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14ninevoltnirvana.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=382924", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Learn a new language faster than ever! Leave doubt in the dust! Be a better sniper! Could you do all that and more with just a zap to the noggin? Maybe.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Sally Adee, an editor at <em>New Scientist</em>, was at a conference for DARPA - The Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency - when she heard about a way to speed up learning with something called trans-cranial direct current stimulation (tDCS). A couple years later, Sally found herself weilding an M4 assualt rifle, picking off enemy combatants with a battery wired to her temple. Of course, it was a simulation, but Sally's sniper skills made producer Soren Wheeler wonder what we should think of the world of brain stimulation. </p>\n<p class=\"p2\">In the last couple years, tDCS has been all over the news. Researchers claim that juicing the brain with just 2 milliamps (think 9-volt battery) can help with everything from learning languages, to quitting smoking, to overcoming depression. We bring Michael Weisend, neuroscientist at Wright State Research Institute, into the studio to tell us how it works (Bonus: you get to hear Jad get his brain zapped). Peter Reiner and Nick Fitz of the University of British Columbia help us think through the consequences of a world where anyone with 20 dollars and access to Radioshack can make their own brain zapper. And finally, Sally tells us about the unexpected after-effects of a day of super-charged sniper training and makes us wonder about world where you can order up a state of mind.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Special thanks for the music of <a href=\"http://ghosttrainorchestra.com/\">Brian Carpenter's Ghost Train Orchestra</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"> </p>", "tease"=>"Learn a new language faster than ever! Leave doubt in the dust! Could you do all that and more with just a zap to the noggin? Maybe.", "publishTime"=>1403810231000, "publish-at"=>"2014-06-26T15:17:11", "date-line-ts"=>1403810231, "estimated-duration"=>1467, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/sallygun.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/422/254/c/80/1/sallygun.jpeg", "h"=>254, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/sallygun.jpeg", "w"=>422, "id"=>120242, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"382924", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"9-Volt Nirvana", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Learn a new language faster than ever! Leave doubt in the dust! Be a better sniper! Could you do all that and more with just a zap to the noggin? Maybe.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Sally Adee, an editor at <em>New Scientist</em>, was at a conference for DARPA - The Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency - when she heard about a way to speed up learning with something called trans-cranial direct current stimulation (tDCS). A couple years later, Sally found herself weilding an M4 assualt rifle, picking off enemy combatants with a battery wired to her temple. Of course, it was a simulation, but Sally's sniper skills made producer Soren Wheeler wonder what we should think of the world of brain stimulation. </p>\n<p class=\"p2\">In the last couple years, tDCS has been all over the news. Researchers claim that juicing the brain with just 2 milliamps (think 9-volt battery) can help with everything from learning languages, to quitting smoking, to overcoming depression. We bring Michael Weisend, neuroscientist at Wright State Research Institute, into the studio to tell us how it works (Bonus: you get to hear Jad get his brain zapped). Peter Reiner and Nick Fitz of the University of British Columbia help us think through the consequences of a world where anyone with 20 dollars and access to Radioshack can make their own brain zapper. And finally, Sally tells us about the unexpected after-effects of a day of super-charged sniper training and makes us wonder about world where you can order up a state of mind.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Special thanks for the music of <a href=\"http://ghosttrainorchestra.com/\">Brian Carpenter's Ghost Train Orchestra</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-06-26T15:17:11", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"kg", "title"=>"≤ kg", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14_kg.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=378952", "description"=>"<p>A plum-sized lump of metal takes us from the French Revolution to an underground bunker in Maryland as we try to weigh the way we weigh the world around us.</p><p>In this short, we meet a very special cylinder. It's the gold standard (or, in this case, the platinum-iridium standard) for measuring mass. For decades it's been coddled and cared for and treated like a tiny king. But, as we learn from writer <a href=\"https://twitter.com/andrewmarantz\">Andrew Marantz</a>, things change—even things that were specifically designed to stay the same.</p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Ken Alder, Ari Adland, Eric Perlmutter, Terry Quinn and Richard Davis.</em></p>", "tease"=>"In this short, we meet a very special cylinder. It's the gold standard (or, in this case, the platinum-iridium standard) for measuring mass.", "publishTime"=>1402689600000, "publish-at"=>"2014-06-13T16:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1402673077, "estimated-duration"=>1234, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/MassStandards_024.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/752/599/c/80/1/MassStandards_024.jpg", "h"=>599, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/MassStandards_024.jpg", "w"=>752, "id"=>119414, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"378952", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"≤ kg", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>A plum-sized lump of metal takes us from the French Revolution to an underground bunker in Maryland as we try to weigh the way we weigh the world around us.</p><p>In this short, we meet a very special cylinder. It's the gold standard (or, in this case, the platinum-iridium standard) for measuring mass. For decades it's been coddled and cared for and treated like a tiny king. But, as we learn from writer <a href=\"https://twitter.com/andrewmarantz\">Andrew Marantz</a>, things change—even things that were specifically designed to stay the same.</p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Ken Alder, Ari Adland, Eric Perlmutter, Terry Quinn and Richard Davis.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-06-13T16:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"taung-child", "title"=>"The Skull", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14theskull.mp3?awparams=postroll%3Afalse&awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=371229", "description"=>"<p> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span>Today, the story of one little thing that has radically changed what we know about humanity’s humble beginnings and the kinds of creatures that were out to get us way back when.</span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span> </span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span>Wits University Professor Lee Berger</span><span> </span><span>and Dr. Chris Stringer from London’s Natural History Museum explain how a child’s skull, found in an ancient cave, eventually helped answer one of our oldest questions: Where do we come from? Then Lee takes us on a journey to answer a somewhat smaller question: how did that child die? Along the way, we visit Dr. Bernhard Zipfel at Wits University in Johannesburg to actually hold the skull itself.</span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span> </span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span>We wanted to give you a chance to hold the skull, too. So we did a little experiment: we made a 3D scan of it. If you visit </span><a style=\"text-decoration: none;\" href=\"http://www.thingiverse.com/thing:332463\"><span>our page on Thingiverse</span></a><span>, you’ll see the results. Anyone with access to a 3D printer can print their own copy of the skull. (We printed a bunch, with help from our friends at </span><a style=\"text-decoration: none;\" href=\"https://www.makerbot.com/\"><span>MakerBot</span></a><span>—there’s even a purple one with sparkles.)</span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span> </span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span>We also collaborated with the folks at </span><a style=\"text-decoration: none;\" href=\"http://mmuseumm.com/\"><span>Mmuseumm</span></a><span>, a tiny (really tiny, it’s in an elevator shaft) museum in Manhattan. You can visit them to see the 3D printed skull, along with the other wonderful things in their collection: mosquitoes swatted mid-bite, toothpaste tubes from around the world, and much more.</span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span> </span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><em>Thanks to JP Brown, Emily Graslie and Robert Martin at the Field Museum in Chicago for scanning the skull. Thanks to Curtis Schmitt and <a href=\"https://www.shootdigital.com\">s<span>hootdigital</span></a> for refining the scan. Thanks to Bre Pettis and Jenifer Howard at MakerBot for guiding us through the world of 3D printing. </em></p>\n<div><span> </span></div>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Today, the story of one little thing that has radically changed what we know about humanity&rsquo;s humble beginnings and the kinds of creatures that were out to get us way back when...", "publishTime"=>1400183897000, "publish-at"=>"2014-05-15T15:58:17", "date-line-ts"=>1400183896, "estimated-duration"=>1212, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Taung_SKULL_011BT-V1.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/1500/c/80/1/Taung_SKULL_011BT-V1.jpeg", "h"=>1500, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.shootdigital.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Taung_SKULL_011BT-V1.jpeg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>117798, "credits-name"=>"Thanks to shootdigital"}, "objectID"=>"371229", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Skull", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span>Today, the story of one little thing that has radically changed what we know about humanity’s humble beginnings and the kinds of creatures that were out to get us way back when.</span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span> </span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span>Wits University Professor Lee Berger</span><span> </span><span>and Dr. Chris Stringer from London’s Natural History Museum explain how a child’s skull, found in an ancient cave, eventually helped answer one of our oldest questions: Where do we come from? Then Lee takes us on a journey to answer a somewhat smaller question: how did that child die? Along the way, we visit Dr. Bernhard Zipfel at Wits University in Johannesburg to actually hold the skull itself.</span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span> </span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span>We wanted to give you a chance to hold the skull, too. So we did a little experiment: we made a 3D scan of it. If you visit </span><a style=\"text-decoration: none;\" href=\"http://www.thingiverse.com/thing:332463\"><span>our page on Thingiverse</span></a><span>, you’ll see the results. Anyone with access to a 3D printer can print their own copy of the skull. (We printed a bunch, with help from our friends at </span><a style=\"text-decoration: none;\" href=\"https://www.makerbot.com/\"><span>MakerBot</span></a><span>—there’s even a purple one with sparkles.)</span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span> </span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span>We also collaborated with the folks at </span><a style=\"text-decoration: none;\" href=\"http://mmuseumm.com/\"><span>Mmuseumm</span></a><span>, a tiny (really tiny, it’s in an elevator shaft) museum in Manhattan. You can visit them to see the 3D printed skull, along with the other wonderful things in their collection: mosquitoes swatted mid-bite, toothpaste tubes from around the world, and much more.</span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span> </span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><em>Thanks to JP Brown, Emily Graslie and Robert Martin at the Field Museum in Chicago for scanning the skull. Thanks to Curtis Schmitt and <a href=\"https://www.shootdigital.com\">s<span>hootdigital</span></a> for refining the scan. Thanks to Bre Pettis and Jenifer Howard at MakerBot for guiding us through the world of 3D printing. </em></p>\n<div><span> </span></div>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-05-15T15:58:17", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"love-numbers", "title"=>"For the Love of Numbers", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14fortheloveofnumbers.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=367903", "description"=>"<p>It’s hard to think of anything more rational, more logical and impersonal than a number. But what if we’re all, universally, also deeply attuned to how numbers … feel? Why 2 is warm, 7 is strong and 11 is downright mystical.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p><p>In this short, writer Alex Bellos tells Robert how, from the very first time humans ever used numbers, we couldn’t help but give them human-like qualities. From favorite numbers to numbers that we’re suspicious of, from 501 jeans to Oxy 10, our feelings for these digits may all come down to some serious, subconscious inner-math….a deeply human arithmetic buried in our heart.</p>", "tease"=>"In this short, writer Alex Bellos tells Robert how, from the very first time humans ever used numbers, we couldn&rsquo;t help but give them human-like qualities.", "publishTime"=>1399058142000, "publish-at"=>"2014-05-02T15:15:42", "date-line-ts"=>1399058141, "estimated-duration"=>1177, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Numbers.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/1111/l/80/1/Numbers.png", "h"=>1111, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Image by Henry Reich", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Numbers.png", "w"=>1500, "id"=>116910, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"367903", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"For the Love of Numbers", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>It’s hard to think of anything more rational, more logical and impersonal than a number. But what if we’re all, universally, also deeply attuned to how numbers … feel? Why 2 is warm, 7 is strong and 11 is downright mystical.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p><p>In this short, writer Alex Bellos tells Robert how, from the very first time humans ever used numbers, we couldn’t help but give them human-like qualities. From favorite numbers to numbers that we’re suspicious of, from 501 jeans to Oxy 10, our feelings for these digits may all come down to some serious, subconscious inner-math….a deeply human arithmetic buried in our heart.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-05-02T15:15:42", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"straight-outta-chevy-chase", "title"=>"Straight Outta Chevy Chase", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14hiphop.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=361623", "description"=>"<p>From boom bap to EDM, we look at the line between hip-hop and not, and meet a defender of the genre that makes you question... who's in and who's out.</p><p>Over the past 40 years, hip-hop music has gone from underground phenomenon to global commodity. But as <em>The New Yorker's</em> Andrew Marantz explains, massive commercial success is a tightrope walk for any genre of popular music, and especially one built on authenticity and “realness.”  Hip-hop constantly runs the risk of becoming a watered-down imitation of its former self - just, you know, <em>pop music</em>.</p>\n<p>Andrew introduces us to Peter Rosenberg, a guy who takes this doomsday scenario very seriously. Peter is a DJ at Hot 97, New York City’s iconic hip-hop station, and a vocal booster of what he calls “real” hip-hop. But as a Jewish fellow from suburban Maryland, he's also the first to admit that he's an unlikely arbiter for what is and what isn't hip-hop.</p>\n<p>With the help of Ali Shaheed Muhammad of A Tribe Called Quest and NPR's Frannie Kelley, we explore the strange ways that hip-hop deals with that age-old question: are you in or are you out?</p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to The New Yorker who let us do a radiophonic version of their piece. If you've got a New Yorker subscription check out Andrew Marantz's stellar written version <a href=\"http://www.newyorker.com/reporting/2014/04/07/140407fa_fact_marantz?mbid=social_retweet?mbid=social_retweet\">here</a>. If you don't, well you should get one, but you can also watch Rosenberg crate digging and spinning records <a href=\"http://www.newyorker.com/online/blogs/culture/2014/03/video-peter-rosenbergs-45s.html\">here</a>. </em></p>", "tease"=>"Over the past 40 years, hip-hop music has gone from underground phenomenon to global commodity.", "publishTime"=>1396396790000, "publish-at"=>"2014-04-01T19:59:50", "date-line-ts"=>1396396789, "estimated-duration"=>2076, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/rosenberg1.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/683/h/80/1/rosenberg1.jpg", "h"=>683, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"Peter Rosenberg", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/rosenberg1.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>115054, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"361623", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Straight Outta Chevy Chase", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>From boom bap to EDM, we look at the line between hip-hop and not, and meet a defender of the genre that makes you question... who's in and who's out.</p><p>Over the past 40 years, hip-hop music has gone from underground phenomenon to global commodity. But as <em>The New Yorker's</em> Andrew Marantz explains, massive commercial success is a tightrope walk for any genre of popular music, and especially one built on authenticity and “realness.”  Hip-hop constantly runs the risk of becoming a watered-down imitation of its former self - just, you know, <em>pop music</em>.</p>\n<p>Andrew introduces us to Peter Rosenberg, a guy who takes this doomsday scenario very seriously. Peter is a DJ at Hot 97, New York City’s iconic hip-hop station, and a vocal booster of what he calls “real” hip-hop. But as a Jewish fellow from suburban Maryland, he's also the first to admit that he's an unlikely arbiter for what is and what isn't hip-hop.</p>\n<p>With the help of Ali Shaheed Muhammad of A Tribe Called Quest and NPR's Frannie Kelley, we explore the strange ways that hip-hop deals with that age-old question: are you in or are you out?</p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to The New Yorker who let us do a radiophonic version of their piece. If you've got a New Yorker subscription check out Andrew Marantz's stellar written version <a href=\"http://www.newyorker.com/reporting/2014/04/07/140407fa_fact_marantz?mbid=social_retweet?mbid=social_retweet\">here</a>. If you don't, well you should get one, but you can also watch Rosenberg crate digging and spinning records <a href=\"http://www.newyorker.com/online/blogs/culture/2014/03/video-peter-rosenbergs-45s.html\">here</a>. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-04-01T19:59:50", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"kill-em-all", "title"=>"KILL 'EM ALL", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast14killemall.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=359851", "description"=>"<p>They buzz. They bite. And they have killed more people than cancer, war, or heart disease. Here’s the question: If you could wipe mosquitoes off the face of the planet, would you?</p><p>Ever since there have been humans, mosquitoes have been biting us, and we’ve been trying to kill them. And, for the most part, the mosquitoes have been winning. Today there are over 3000 species on pretty much every corner of Earth. Mosquito-borne diseases kill around 1 million people a year (most of them children) and make more than 500 million people sick. But thanks to Hadyn Perry and his team of scientists, that might be about to change. Producer Andy Mills talks with author Sonia Shah about the difficulties of sharing a planet with mosquitoes and with science writer David Quammen about the risks of getting rid of them. </p>\n<p>Oh, and we visit a mosquito factory in eastern Brazil.</p>\n<p>And after listening, read this, from Radiolab producer Andy Mills: <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/what-if-we-dont-kill-em-all/\" target=\"_blank\">what if we don't kill 'em all?</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.davidbakeronline.com/\"><em>Special thanks to reporter David Baker</em></a></p>", "tease"=>"KILL 'EM ALL", "publishTime"=>1395788383000, "publish-at"=>"2014-03-25T18:59:43", "date-line-ts"=>1395788381, "estimated-duration"=>1288, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/mosquito1.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/365/l/80/1/mosquito1.jpeg", "h"=>365, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/mosquito1.jpeg", "w"=>620, "id"=>114550, "credits-name"=>"US National Library of Medicine"}, "objectID"=>"359851", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"KILL 'EM ALL", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>They buzz. They bite. And they have killed more people than cancer, war, or heart disease. Here’s the question: If you could wipe mosquitoes off the face of the planet, would you?</p><p>Ever since there have been humans, mosquitoes have been biting us, and we’ve been trying to kill them. And, for the most part, the mosquitoes have been winning. Today there are over 3000 species on pretty much every corner of Earth. Mosquito-borne diseases kill around 1 million people a year (most of them children) and make more than 500 million people sick. But thanks to Hadyn Perry and his team of scientists, that might be about to change. Producer Andy Mills talks with author Sonia Shah about the difficulties of sharing a planet with mosquitoes and with science writer David Quammen about the risks of getting rid of them. </p>\n<p>Oh, and we visit a mosquito factory in eastern Brazil.</p>\n<p>And after listening, read this, from Radiolab producer Andy Mills: <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/what-if-we-dont-kill-em-all/\" target=\"_blank\">what if we don't kill 'em all?</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.davidbakeronline.com/\"><em>Special thanks to reporter David Baker</em></a></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2014-03-25T18:59:43", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2015=>[{"slug"=>"cathedral", "title"=>"The Cathedral", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16cathedral.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=562054", "description"=>"<div>\n<p>Ryan and Amy Green were facing the unfaceable: their youngest son, Joel was diagnosed with terminal cancer after his first birthday. Producer Sruthi Pinnamaneni tells the story of how Ryan and Amy stumble onto an unlikely way of processing their experience fighting alongside Joel: they decide to turn it into a video game. In the end, they find themselves facing what might be, for a game designer or a parent, the hardest design problem ever.</p>\n<p><em>Correction: In the original audio we stated that the survival rate of childhood AT/RT cancer is 50% over five years. But studies suggest the survival rate is 50% over two years. </em><em>The audio has been updated to reflect this change.</em></p>\n<p>For an extended version of this story and a bunch more incredible stories, go check out <a href=\"https://gimletmedia.com/show/reply-all/\">Reply All</a>.</p>\n<p>Special thanks to Eilis O’ Neill, Jon Hillman, and Josh Larson. This episode included audio from “Thank You For Playing,” a documentary film about the creation of<a href=\"http://thatdragoncancer.com\"> That Dragon, Cancer </a>by David Osit &amp; Malika Zouhali-Worrall. You can learn more about the film and where you can see it, at <a href=\"http://www.thankyouforplayingfilm.com/\">thankyouforplayingfilm.com</a>. For more, we suggest reading Wired's <a href=\"http://www.wired.com/2016/01/that-dragon-cancer/\">\"Playing For Time.\"</a></p>\n</div>\n\n", "tease"=>"On this podcast, we present a story from an amazing, staff favorite podcast, Reply All.", "publishTime"=>1452207900000, "publish-at"=>"2016-01-07T18:05:00", "date-line-ts"=>1451332494, "estimated-duration"=>1884, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/2014_12_17_CathedralAtrium3.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/720/450/l/80/1/2014_12_17_CathedralAtrium3.png", "h"=>450, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/2014_12_17_CathedralAtrium3.png", "w"=>720, "id"=>147703, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Ryan and Amy Green"}, "objectID"=>"562054", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Cathedral", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div>\n<p>Ryan and Amy Green were facing the unfaceable: their youngest son, Joel was diagnosed with terminal cancer after his first birthday. Producer Sruthi Pinnamaneni tells the story of how Ryan and Amy stumble onto an unlikely way of processing their experience fighting alongside Joel: they decide to turn it into a video game. In the end, they find themselves facing what might be, for a game designer or a parent, the hardest design problem ever.</p>\n<p><em>Correction: In the original audio we stated that the survival rate of childhood AT/RT cancer is 50% over five years. But studies suggest the survival rate is 50% over two years. </em><em>The audio has been updated to reflect this change.</em></p>\n<p>For an extended version of this story and a bunch more incredible stories, go check out <a href=\"https://gimletmedia.com/show/reply-all/\">Reply All</a>.</p>\n<p>Special thanks to Eilis O’ Neill, Jon Hillman, and Josh Larson. This episode included audio from “Thank You For Playing,” a documentary film about the creation of<a href=\"http://thatdragoncancer.com\"> That Dragon, Cancer </a>by David Osit &amp; Malika Zouhali-Worrall. You can learn more about the film and where you can see it, at <a href=\"http://www.thankyouforplayingfilm.com/\">thankyouforplayingfilm.com</a>. For more, we suggest reading Wired's <a href=\"http://www.wired.com/2016/01/that-dragon-cancer/\">\"Playing For Time.\"</a></p>\n</div>\n\n", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-01-07T18:05:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"addiction", "title"=>"The Fix", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15thefix.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=560061", "description"=>"<div><span><span><span><span></span></span></span></span>\n<p>This episode we take a sober look at the throbbing, aching, craving desire states that return people (again and again) to the object of their addiction … and the pills that just might set them free.</p>\n<span><span><span><span></span></span></span></span>\n<p>Reporter Amy O’Leary was fed up with her ex-boyfriend’s hard-drinking, when she discovered a French doctor’s memoir titled <em>The End of My Addiction</em>.  The fix that he proposed seemed too good to be true.  But her phone call with the doctor left her, and us, even more intrigued. Could this malady – so often seen as moral and spiritual - really be beaten back with a pill?</p>\n<span><span><span><span></span></span></span></span>\n<p>We talk to addiction researcher Dr. Anna Rose Childress, a<span>ddiction psychologist</span> Dr. Mark Willenbring, journalist Gabrielle Glaser, The National Institute of Health’s Dr. Nora Volkow, and scores of people dealing with substance abuse as we try to figure out whether we're in the midst of a sea change in how we think about addiction.</p>\n</div>\n<p><em>Produced by Andy Mills with Simon Adler</em></p>\n<p><em>If you are someone looking for help with a substance abuse problem and want to find health care services in your area, <a href=\"https://findtreatment.samhsa.gov/\">check out this map</a> from the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Administration.</em></p>\n<p>For more on Dr. Mark Willenbring and the Alltyr Clinic visit <a href=\"http://alltyr.com/\">their website</a>.</p>\n<p>If you’d like to hear more from Nora Volkow you can watch her speech from <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1AEvkWxbLE\">this summer’s American Psychiatric Association’s annual meeting</a>.</p>\n<p>Or watch her and other top addiction researchers <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p0lL1MN2yCs\">at last year’s World Science Fair</a><em><span><span> </span></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><span><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm140444319622000b666e5fc-02e7-4c36-8277-6155e4e0c7a8\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/p0lL1MN2yCs?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a-7650873955495496299\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p0lL1MN2yCs\"></iframe></div></div>  </span></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Addiction &hellip; and the pills that just might set free those who suffer from it.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1450388947000, "publish-at"=>"2015-12-17T16:49:07", "date-line-ts"=>1450475347, "estimated-duration"=>2432, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Jonathan_Cohen.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/l/80/1/Jonathan_Cohen.jpg", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Jonathan_Cohen.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>147435, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Jonathan Cohen"}, "objectID"=>"560061", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Fix", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div><span><span><span><span></span></span></span></span>\n<p>This episode we take a sober look at the throbbing, aching, craving desire states that return people (again and again) to the object of their addiction … and the pills that just might set them free.</p>\n<span><span><span><span></span></span></span></span>\n<p>Reporter Amy O’Leary was fed up with her ex-boyfriend’s hard-drinking, when she discovered a French doctor’s memoir titled <em>The End of My Addiction</em>.  The fix that he proposed seemed too good to be true.  But her phone call with the doctor left her, and us, even more intrigued. Could this malady – so often seen as moral and spiritual - really be beaten back with a pill?</p>\n<span><span><span><span></span></span></span></span>\n<p>We talk to addiction researcher Dr. Anna Rose Childress, a<span>ddiction psychologist</span> Dr. Mark Willenbring, journalist Gabrielle Glaser, The National Institute of Health’s Dr. Nora Volkow, and scores of people dealing with substance abuse as we try to figure out whether we're in the midst of a sea change in how we think about addiction.</p>\n</div>\n<p><em>Produced by Andy Mills with Simon Adler</em></p>\n<p><em>If you are someone looking for help with a substance abuse problem and want to find health care services in your area, <a href=\"https://findtreatment.samhsa.gov/\">check out this map</a> from the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Administration.</em></p>\n<p>For more on Dr. Mark Willenbring and the Alltyr Clinic visit <a href=\"http://alltyr.com/\">their website</a>.</p>\n<p>If you’d like to hear more from Nora Volkow you can watch her speech from <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X1AEvkWxbLE\">this summer’s American Psychiatric Association’s annual meeting</a>.</p>\n<p>Or watch her and other top addiction researchers <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p0lL1MN2yCs\">at last year’s World Science Fair</a><em><span><span> </span></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><span><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm140444319622000b666e5fc-02e7-4c36-8277-6155e4e0c7a8\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/p0lL1MN2yCs?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a-7650873955495496299\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p0lL1MN2yCs\"></iframe></div></div>  </span></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-12-17T16:49:07", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"cold-war", "title"=>"The Cold War", "audio"=>nil, "description"=>"<p><em>Editor's Note:</em></p>\n<p><em>In our podcast, The Cold War, we failed to correctly credit David Wolman and Julian Smith, who wrote and reported the article on which it was based. At the time we published this podcast, we had not properly determined the extent of their role in finding and developing this story. As a result, we have removed the episode from the Radiolab archive.  We did not feel a correction could rectify the problem and Radiolab honors its relationships with contributors too much to let the error remain.</em></p>\n<p><em>We apologize to David and Julian.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Today we discover the rules of war, negotiation, and conflict resolution in a most unlikely place - deep in the heart and soul of that tasty frozen treat we all scream for.", "publishTime"=>1448956800000, "publish-at"=>"2015-12-01T03:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1448901352, "estimated-duration"=>1735, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/bombpop.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1623/c/80/1/bombpop.jpg", "h"=>1623, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/bombpop.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>146637, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Frankie Leon"}, "objectID"=>"555669", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Cold War", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><em>Editor's Note:</em></p>\n<p><em>In our podcast, The Cold War, we failed to correctly credit David Wolman and Julian Smith, who wrote and reported the article on which it was based. At the time we published this podcast, we had not properly determined the extent of their role in finding and developing this story. As a result, we have removed the episode from the Radiolab archive.  We did not feel a correction could rectify the problem and Radiolab honors its relationships with contributors too much to let the error remain.</em></p>\n<p><em>We apologize to David and Julian.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-12-01T03:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"best-medicine", "title"=>"Staph Retreat", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15staphretreat.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=543737", "description"=>"<p>What happens when you combine an axe-wielding microbiologist and a disease-obsessed historian? A strange brew that's hard to resist, even for a modern day microbe.</p>\n<p>In the war on devilish microbes, our weapons are starting to fail us.  The antibiotics we once wielded like miraculous flaming swords seem more like lukewarm butter knives.</p>\n<p>But today we follow an odd couple to a storied land of elves and dragons. There, they uncover a 1000-year-old secret that makes us reconsider our most basic assumptions about human progress and wonder: What if the only way forward is backward?</p>\n<p><em>Reported by Latif Nasser. Produced by Matt Kielty and Soren Wheeler.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Steve Diggle, <span>Professor Roberta Frank, </span>Alexandra Reider and Justin Park (our Old English readers), Gene Murrow from Gotham Early Music Scene, Marcia Young for her performance on the medieval harp and </em><em>Collin Monro of Tadcaster and the rest of the Barony of Iron Bog.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"In the war on devilish microbes, our weapons are starting to fail us.&nbsp;What if the only way forward is backward?", "publishTime"=>1446515050000, "publish-at"=>"2015-11-02T20:44:10", "date-line-ts"=>1446515050, "estimated-duration"=>1790, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/olde1.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/945/745/l/80/1/olde1.jpg", "h"=>745, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Bald's Leechbook", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/olde1.jpg", "w"=>945, "id"=>145480, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: British Museum"}, "objectID"=>"543737", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Staph Retreat", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>What happens when you combine an axe-wielding microbiologist and a disease-obsessed historian? A strange brew that's hard to resist, even for a modern day microbe.</p>\n<p>In the war on devilish microbes, our weapons are starting to fail us.  The antibiotics we once wielded like miraculous flaming swords seem more like lukewarm butter knives.</p>\n<p>But today we follow an odd couple to a storied land of elves and dragons. There, they uncover a 1000-year-old secret that makes us reconsider our most basic assumptions about human progress and wonder: What if the only way forward is backward?</p>\n<p><em>Reported by Latif Nasser. Produced by Matt Kielty and Soren Wheeler.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Steve Diggle, <span>Professor Roberta Frank, </span>Alexandra Reider and Justin Park (our Old English readers), Gene Murrow from Gotham Early Music Scene, Marcia Young for her performance on the medieval harp and </em><em>Collin Monro of Tadcaster and the rest of the Barony of Iron Bog.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-11-02T20:44:10", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"update-new-normal", "title"=>"Update: New Normal?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab101915.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=540050", "description"=>"<p><span>An update: we revisit our episode about normalcy. Evolution results from the ability of organisms to change. But how do you tell the difference between a sea change and a ripple in the water? Is a peacenik baboon, a man in a dress, or a cuddly fox a sign of things to come? Or just a flukey outlier from the norm? And is there ever really a norm? This episode we return to two stories where choice has challenged destiny to see what's changed and what has become deeply normal. </span></p>\n<p><em>Produced by Soren Wheeler</em></p>", "tease"=>"In this hour of Radiolab: reframing our ideas about normalcy. Three stories where choice challenges destiny.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1445303037000, "publish-at"=>"2015-10-19T21:03:57", "date-line-ts"=>1445227200, "estimated-duration"=>4139, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/4334413228_3a7a656628_o.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1286/854/l/80/1/4334413228_3a7a656628_o.jpg", "h"=>854, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.flickr.com/photos/kkoshy/4334413228/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/4334413228_3a7a656628_o.jpg", "w"=>1286, "id"=>144786, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Koshy Koshy"}, "objectID"=>"540050", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Update: New Normal?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>An update: we revisit our episode about normalcy. Evolution results from the ability of organisms to change. But how do you tell the difference between a sea change and a ripple in the water? Is a peacenik baboon, a man in a dress, or a cuddly fox a sign of things to come? Or just a flukey outlier from the norm? And is there ever really a norm? This episode we return to two stories where choice has challenged destiny to see what's changed and what has become deeply normal. </span></p>\n<p><em>Produced by Soren Wheeler</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-10-19T21:03:57", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"darkode", "title"=>"Darkode", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15darkode.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=532787", "description"=>"<p>It would seem that hackers today can do just about anything they want - from turning on the cellphone in your pocket to holding your life's work hostage. Cyber criminals today have more sophisticated tools, have learned to work collaboratively around the world and have found innovative ways to remain deep undercover in the internet's shadows. This episode, we shine a light into those shadows to see the world from the perspectives of both cybercrime victims and perpetrators.</p>\n<p>First we meet mother-daughter duo Alina and Inna Simone, who tell us about being held hostage by criminals who have burrowed into their lives from half a world away. Along the way we learn about the legally sticky spot that unwitting accomplices like Will Wheeler find themselves in.</p>\n<p>Then reporter and author Joseph Menn tells us about the surprisingly lucrative professional hacker structure in places throughout the former Soviet Union. Finally, the co-creator of one of the most notorious online marketplaces to ever exist speaks to us and NPR cyber-crime expert Dina Temple-Raston about how a young suburban Boy Scout can turn into a world renowned black hat hacker.</p>\n<p><em>Produced by Kelsey Padgett and Andy Mills. </em></p>", "tease"=>"We shine a light into the dark&nbsp;corners of the internet to see the world from the perspective&nbsp;of both cyber crime victims and perpetrators.", "publishTime"=>1442884585000, "publish-at"=>"2015-09-21T21:16:25", "date-line-ts"=>1442884585, "estimated-duration"=>2267, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/20012126753_a7af44d995_o.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1045/l/80/1/20012126753_a7af44d995_o.jpg", "h"=>1045, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/20012126753_a7af44d995_o.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>143321, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Christiaan Colen"}, "objectID"=>"532787", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Darkode", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>It would seem that hackers today can do just about anything they want - from turning on the cellphone in your pocket to holding your life's work hostage. Cyber criminals today have more sophisticated tools, have learned to work collaboratively around the world and have found innovative ways to remain deep undercover in the internet's shadows. This episode, we shine a light into those shadows to see the world from the perspectives of both cybercrime victims and perpetrators.</p>\n<p>First we meet mother-daughter duo Alina and Inna Simone, who tell us about being held hostage by criminals who have burrowed into their lives from half a world away. Along the way we learn about the legally sticky spot that unwitting accomplices like Will Wheeler find themselves in.</p>\n<p>Then reporter and author Joseph Menn tells us about the surprisingly lucrative professional hacker structure in places throughout the former Soviet Union. Finally, the co-creator of one of the most notorious online marketplaces to ever exist speaks to us and NPR cyber-crime expert Dina Temple-Raston about how a young suburban Boy Scout can turn into a world renowned black hat hacker.</p>\n<p><em>Produced by Kelsey Padgett and Andy Mills. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-09-21T21:16:25", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"remembering-oliver-sacks", "title"=>"Remembering Oliver Sacks", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/rl_extras/rl_extras15rememberingoliver.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=527964", "description"=>"<p>In memory of one of our dear friends and one of the truest inspirations for Radiolab, a re-release of the last conversation we had with Dr. Oliver Sacks. </p>\n<p>When Radiolab was just starting out, Robert asked <a href=\"http://www.oliversacks.com/\">Dr. Oliver Sacks</a> if he could help us, maybe send us a few story ideas. Over the years he has shared with us stories of chemistry, music, neurology, hallucinations and more, so much more. Because Oliver notices the world and the people around him with scientific rigor, with insight, and most importantly, with deep empathy. When he announced a few months ago that he had terminal cancer and wasn't going to do any more interviews, we asked him if he'd talk with us one last time. He said yes. So Robert went, as he has done for 30 some years now, to his apartment with a microphone, this time to ask him about the forces that have driven him in his work, in his unique relationships with his patients, and in his own life.</p>\n<p><em>This performance was scored live by the incomparable Sarah Lipstate/Noveller. <a href=\"http://www.sarahlipstate.com/\">Her new album Fantastic Planet is out now. </a></em></p>\n<p><em>The lullabies you hear in \"Dr. Sacks Looks Back\" are sung by Carrie Erving, whose current project is <a href=\"http://www.ponyhofmusic.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Ponyhof</a>. </em></p>\n<p><em>Though it probably goes without saying, <a href=\"http://www.oliversacks.com/books-by-oliver-sacks/on-the-move/\">we highly recommend Dr. Sacks' new autobiorgraphy, On The Move. </a></em></p>", "tease"=>"When Dr. Sacks announced a few months ago that he had terminal cancer and wouldn't do any more interviews, we asked him if he'd talk with us one last time.&nbsp;This is that conversation.", "publishTime"=>1440975442000, "publish-at"=>"2015-08-30T18:57:22", "date-line-ts"=>1440975442, "estimated-duration"=>1550, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Sacks2.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/832/864/l/80/1/Sacks2.jpg", "h"=>864, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Dr. Oliver Sacks", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Sacks2.jpg", "w"=>832, "id"=>137321, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Oliver Sacks"}, "objectID"=>"527964", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Remembering Oliver Sacks", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In memory of one of our dear friends and one of the truest inspirations for Radiolab, a re-release of the last conversation we had with Dr. Oliver Sacks. </p>\n<p>When Radiolab was just starting out, Robert asked <a href=\"http://www.oliversacks.com/\">Dr. Oliver Sacks</a> if he could help us, maybe send us a few story ideas. Over the years he has shared with us stories of chemistry, music, neurology, hallucinations and more, so much more. Because Oliver notices the world and the people around him with scientific rigor, with insight, and most importantly, with deep empathy. When he announced a few months ago that he had terminal cancer and wasn't going to do any more interviews, we asked him if he'd talk with us one last time. He said yes. So Robert went, as he has done for 30 some years now, to his apartment with a microphone, this time to ask him about the forces that have driven him in his work, in his unique relationships with his patients, and in his own life.</p>\n<p><em>This performance was scored live by the incomparable Sarah Lipstate/Noveller. <a href=\"http://www.sarahlipstate.com/\">Her new album Fantastic Planet is out now. </a></em></p>\n<p><em>The lullabies you hear in \"Dr. Sacks Looks Back\" are sung by Carrie Erving, whose current project is <a href=\"http://www.ponyhofmusic.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Ponyhof</a>. </em></p>\n<p><em>Though it probably goes without saying, <a href=\"http://www.oliversacks.com/books-by-oliver-sacks/on-the-move/\">we highly recommend Dr. Sacks' new autobiorgraphy, On The Move. </a></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-08-30T18:57:22", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"archives-oliver-sacks-table-elements", "title"=>"From the Archives: Oliver Sacks' Table of Elements", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15sackselements.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=521722", "description"=>"<p><span>As we're busy working on our next episode, with stories inspired by the Periodic Table of Elements, we thought we'd bring you one of its chief inspirations.  As a young boy, neurologist, author and Radiolab favorite </span><a class=\"external-link\" href=\"http://www.oliversacks.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Oliver Sacks</a> <span>pored over the pages of the Handbook of Physics and Chemistry, fantasizing about the day that he, like the shy gas Xenon, would find a companion with whom to connect and share. That companion turned out to be the Periodic Table of the Elements itself, a relationship he's never outgrown. He introduces us to the elements that he's known and loved. </span></p>", "tease"=>"From the archives: a visit with Oliver Sacks and the elements he's known and loved.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1438901487000, "publish-at"=>"2015-08-06T18:51:27", "date-line-ts"=>1438901487, "estimated-duration"=>960, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Mendeleev2.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/360/203/l/80/1/Mendeleev2.jpg", "h"=>203, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Dmitiri Mendeleev", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Mendeleev2.jpg", "w"=>360, "id"=>141412, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: RIA Novosti"}, "objectID"=>"521722", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"From the Archives: Oliver Sacks' Table of Elements", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>As we're busy working on our next episode, with stories inspired by the Periodic Table of Elements, we thought we'd bring you one of its chief inspirations.  As a young boy, neurologist, author and Radiolab favorite </span><a class=\"external-link\" href=\"http://www.oliversacks.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Oliver Sacks</a> <span>pored over the pages of the Handbook of Physics and Chemistry, fantasizing about the day that he, like the shy gas Xenon, would find a companion with whom to connect and share. That companion turned out to be the Periodic Table of the Elements itself, a relationship he's never outgrown. He introduces us to the elements that he's known and loved. </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-08-06T18:51:27", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"shrink", "title"=>"Shrink", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15shrink.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=520604", "description"=>"<div><span>The definition of life is in flux, complexity is overrated, and humans are shrinking.</span><br>\n<p>Viruses are supposed to be sleek, pared-down, dead-eyed machines. But when one microbiologist stumbled upon a GIANT virus, hundreds of times bigger than any seen before, all that went out the window.  The discovery opened the door not only to a new cast of microscopic characters with names like Mimivirus, Mamavirus, and Megavirus, but also to basic questions: How did we miss these until now? Have they been around since the beginning? What if evolution could go … backwards?</p>\n<div>Join Jad and Robert as they grill Radiolab regular Carl Zimmer on these paradoxical viruses – they’re so big that they can get their own viruses! - and what they can tell us about the nature of life. </div>\n\n\n</div>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"The definition of life is in flux, complexity is overrated, and humans are shrinking.", "publishTime"=>1438304058000, "publish-at"=>"2015-07-30T20:54:18", "date-line-ts"=>1438304058, "estimated-duration"=>2598, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/virus.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/315/c/80/1/virus.jpg", "h"=>315, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Megavirus", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/virus.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>141075, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Wikimedia"}, "objectID"=>"520604", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Shrink", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div><span>The definition of life is in flux, complexity is overrated, and humans are shrinking.</span><br>\n<p>Viruses are supposed to be sleek, pared-down, dead-eyed machines. But when one microbiologist stumbled upon a GIANT virus, hundreds of times bigger than any seen before, all that went out the window.  The discovery opened the door not only to a new cast of microscopic characters with names like Mimivirus, Mamavirus, and Megavirus, but also to basic questions: How did we miss these until now? Have they been around since the beginning? What if evolution could go … backwards?</p>\n<div>Join Jad and Robert as they grill Radiolab regular Carl Zimmer on these paradoxical viruses – they’re so big that they can get their own viruses! - and what they can tell us about the nature of life. </div>\n\n\n</div>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-07-30T20:54:18", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"grays-donation", "title"=>"Gray's Donation", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15graysdonation.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=518120", "description"=>"<div>A donation leads <a href=\"https://www.harpercollins.com/9780062438225/a-life-everlasting\">Sarah and Ross Gray to places we rarely get a chance to see</a>. In this surprising journey, they gain a view of science that is redemptive, fussy facts that are tender, and parts of a loved one that add up to something unexpected.</div>\n<p>Before he was even born, Sarah and Ross knew that their son Thomas wouldn’t live long. But as they let go of him, they made a decision that reverberated through a world that they never bothered to think about. Years later, after a couple awkward phone calls and an unexpected family road trip, they managed to meet the people and places for whom Thomas’ short life was an altogether different kind of gift.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Since we first aired this segment, some exciting things have happened in the Gray's world. Our producer Tracie Hunte sat down with Sarah Gray to get the low-down on what's new. Check it out here: </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"130\" scrolling=\"no\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/widgets/ondemand_player/radiolab/#file=https://www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_blog/radiolab_blog111617_sarahgray_update.mp3&amp;share=0\" width=\"100%\"></iframe></p>", "tease"=>"How a&nbsp;donation leads Sarah and Ross Gray to places we rarely get a chance to see.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1437083553000, "publish-at"=>"2015-07-16T17:52:33", "date-line-ts"=>1437083553, "estimated-duration"=>1518, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/gray.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1235/l/80/1/gray.jpeg", "h"=>1235, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Thomas Gray", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/gray.jpeg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>140391, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Mark Walpole"}, "objectID"=>"518120", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Gray's Donation", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div>A donation leads <a href=\"https://www.harpercollins.com/9780062438225/a-life-everlasting\">Sarah and Ross Gray to places we rarely get a chance to see</a>. In this surprising journey, they gain a view of science that is redemptive, fussy facts that are tender, and parts of a loved one that add up to something unexpected.</div>\n<p>Before he was even born, Sarah and Ross knew that their son Thomas wouldn’t live long. But as they let go of him, they made a decision that reverberated through a world that they never bothered to think about. Years later, after a couple awkward phone calls and an unexpected family road trip, they managed to meet the people and places for whom Thomas’ short life was an altogether different kind of gift.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Since we first aired this segment, some exciting things have happened in the Gray's world. Our producer Tracie Hunte sat down with Sarah Gray to get the low-down on what's new. Check it out here: </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"130\" scrolling=\"no\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/widgets/ondemand_player/radiolab/#file=https://www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_blog/radiolab_blog111617_sarahgray_update.mp3&amp;share=0\" width=\"100%\"></iframe></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-07-16T17:52:33", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"mau-mau", "title"=>"Mau Mau ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15maumau.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=515660", "description"=>"<p>This is the story of a few documents that tumbled out of the secret archives of the biggest empire the world has ever known, offering a glimpse of histories waiting to be rewritten.</p><p>Just down the road from a pub in rural Hanslope Park, England is a massive building — the secret archives of the biggest empire the world has ever known. This is the story of a few documents that tumbled out and offered a glimpse of histories waiting to be rewritten.</p>\n<p>When professor Caroline Elkins came across a stray document left by the British colonial government in Nairobi, Kenya, she opened the door to a new reckoning with the history of one of Britain's colonial crown jewels, and the fearsome group of rebels known as the Mau Mau. We talk to historians, archivists, journalists and send our producer Jamie York to visit the Mau Mau. As the new history of Kenya is concealed and revealed, document by document, we wonder what else lies in wait among the miles of records hidden away in Hanslope Park.</p>\n<p>Produced by Matt Kielty with reporting from Jamie York</p>\n<p>Special thanks to:</p>\n<p>Mattathias Schwartz for first bringing us this story. Martin Mavenjina and Faith Alubbe of the <a href=\"http://www.khrc.or.ke/\">Kenyan Human Rights Commission</a>. </p>\n<p>Nyakinyua Kenda for the use of their music, Rose Mutiso and Anne Moko for translation help, and Sruthi Pinnamaneni for production support.</p>\n<div id=\"divSelDisplay\">\n<div id=\"divExpSubHdr\">\n<div id=\"divSubFs\">\n<div id=\"divFs\">\n<div class=\"divTx\"> </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"divBdy\" class=\"bdyItmPrt\" _fallwcm=\"1\">\n<div><span><span><em>Correction: An earlier version of this episode contained two errors, which we have corrected. </em></span></span></div>\n<p><span><span><em>The first was our mention of Israel as a former British colony where official documents were purged. In fact, Israel</em></span></span><span><span><em> was a successor to the British mandated territory of Palestine, which we also listed, and so we removed the redundancy. </em></span></span></p>\n<p><em>The second was that we qualified our statement about Kikuyu support for the Mau Mau. Some listeners misinterpreted our claim that support for the Mau Mau cut across all demographics among the Kikuyu to mean that all Kikuyu supported the Mau Mau, which is untrue. We tempered the language in that spot.</em></p>\n<div>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"This is the story of a few documents that tumbled out of&nbsp;the secret archives of the biggest empire the world has ever known, offering a glimpse of histories waiting to be rewritten.", "publishTime"=>1435898621000, "publish-at"=>"2015-07-03T00:43:41", "date-line-ts"=>1435898621, "estimated-duration"=>2605, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Mau_Mau_Hhe7utz.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Getty"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/700/479/l/80/1/Mau_Mau_Hhe7utz.jpg", "h"=>479, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Mau Mau detainees", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Mau_Mau_Hhe7utz.jpg", "w"=>700, "id"=>139814, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Getty Images"}, "objectID"=>"515660", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Mau Mau ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This is the story of a few documents that tumbled out of the secret archives of the biggest empire the world has ever known, offering a glimpse of histories waiting to be rewritten.</p><p>Just down the road from a pub in rural Hanslope Park, England is a massive building — the secret archives of the biggest empire the world has ever known. This is the story of a few documents that tumbled out and offered a glimpse of histories waiting to be rewritten.</p>\n<p>When professor Caroline Elkins came across a stray document left by the British colonial government in Nairobi, Kenya, she opened the door to a new reckoning with the history of one of Britain's colonial crown jewels, and the fearsome group of rebels known as the Mau Mau. We talk to historians, archivists, journalists and send our producer Jamie York to visit the Mau Mau. As the new history of Kenya is concealed and revealed, document by document, we wonder what else lies in wait among the miles of records hidden away in Hanslope Park.</p>\n<p>Produced by Matt Kielty with reporting from Jamie York</p>\n<p>Special thanks to:</p>\n<p>Mattathias Schwartz for first bringing us this story. Martin Mavenjina and Faith Alubbe of the <a href=\"http://www.khrc.or.ke/\">Kenyan Human Rights Commission</a>. </p>\n<p>Nyakinyua Kenda for the use of their music, Rose Mutiso and Anne Moko for translation help, and Sruthi Pinnamaneni for production support.</p>\n<div id=\"divSelDisplay\">\n<div id=\"divExpSubHdr\">\n<div id=\"divSubFs\">\n<div id=\"divFs\">\n<div class=\"divTx\"> </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"divBdy\" class=\"bdyItmPrt\" _fallwcm=\"1\">\n<div><span><span><em>Correction: An earlier version of this episode contained two errors, which we have corrected. </em></span></span></div>\n<p><span><span><em>The first was our mention of Israel as a former British colony where official documents were purged. In fact, Israel</em></span></span><span><span><em> was a successor to the British mandated territory of Palestine, which we also listed, and so we removed the redundancy. </em></span></span></p>\n<p><em>The second was that we qualified our statement about Kikuyu support for the Mau Mau. Some listeners misinterpreted our claim that support for the Mau Mau cut across all demographics among the Kikuyu to mean that all Kikuyu supported the Mau Mau, which is untrue. We tempered the language in that spot.</em></p>\n<div>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-07-03T00:43:41", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"eye-sky", "title"=>"Eye in the Sky", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16eyeinthesky.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=511930", "description"=>"<p>Ross McNutt has a superpower — he can zoom in on everyday life, then rewind and fast-forward to solve crimes in a shutter-flash. But should he?</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>In 2004, when casualties in Iraq were rising due to roadside bombs, Ross McNutt and his team came up with an idea. With a small plane and a 44 mega-pixel camera, they figured out how to watch an entire city all at once, all day long. Whenever a bomb detonated, they could zoom onto that spot and then, because this eye in the sky had been there all along, they could scroll back in time and see - literally see - who planted it. After the war, Ross McNutt retired from the airforce, and brought this technology back home with him. Manoush Zomorodi and Alex Goldmark from the podcast “Note to Self” give us the low-down on Ross’s unique brand of persistent surveillance, from Juarez, Mexico to Dayton, Ohio. Then, once we realize what we can do, we wonder whether we should.</span></p>\n<p>Special thanks to Dan Tucker and George Schulz.</p>\n<p>If you're looking for the updated version of this show, you can check it out <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/update-eye-sky\">here</a>.</p>\n<p>More info:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span>Listen to Note to Self's episode on </span><a style=\"line-height: 1.5em; font-size: 9.5pt;\" href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/story/stingray-conspiracy-theory-daniel-rigmaiden-radiolab/\">surveillance coverage</a><span>.</span></li>\n<li><a style=\"line-height: 1.5em; font-size: 9.5pt;\" href=\"http://www.washingtonpost.com/business/technology/new-surveillance-technology-can-track-everyone-in-an-area-for-several-hours-at-a-time/2014/02/05/82f1556e-876f-11e3-a5bd-844629433ba3_story.html\">\"New surveillance technology can track everyone in an area for several hours at a time,\"</a><span> from the Washington Post</span></li>\n<li><a style=\"line-height: 1.5em; font-size: 9.5pt;\" href=\"http://cironline.org/reports/hollywood-style-surveillance-technology-inches-closer-reality-6228\">\"Hollywood-style surveillance technology inches closer to reality,\"</a><span> from the Center of Investigative Reporting</span></li>\n<li><span>Ross McNutt's company </span><a style=\"line-height: 1.5em; font-size: 9.5pt;\" href=\"http://www.pss-1.com/\">Persistent Surveillance Systems</a></li>\n</ul>", "tease"=>"Ross McNutt has a superpower &mdash; he can zoom in on everyday life, then rewind and fast-forward to solve crimes in a shutter-flash. But should he?", "publishTime"=>1434664800000, "publish-at"=>"2015-06-18T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1434664800, "estimated-duration"=>1711, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/IMG_4917.JPG", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/928/684/l/80/1/IMG_4917.JPG", "h"=>684, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.pss-1.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/IMG_4917.JPG", "w"=>928, "id"=>139096, "credits-name"=>"Persistent Survelliance Systems"}, "objectID"=>"511930", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Eye in the Sky", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Ross McNutt has a superpower — he can zoom in on everyday life, then rewind and fast-forward to solve crimes in a shutter-flash. But should he?</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>In 2004, when casualties in Iraq were rising due to roadside bombs, Ross McNutt and his team came up with an idea. With a small plane and a 44 mega-pixel camera, they figured out how to watch an entire city all at once, all day long. Whenever a bomb detonated, they could zoom onto that spot and then, because this eye in the sky had been there all along, they could scroll back in time and see - literally see - who planted it. After the war, Ross McNutt retired from the airforce, and brought this technology back home with him. Manoush Zomorodi and Alex Goldmark from the podcast “Note to Self” give us the low-down on Ross’s unique brand of persistent surveillance, from Juarez, Mexico to Dayton, Ohio. Then, once we realize what we can do, we wonder whether we should.</span></p>\n<p>Special thanks to Dan Tucker and George Schulz.</p>\n<p>If you're looking for the updated version of this show, you can check it out <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/update-eye-sky\">here</a>.</p>\n<p>More info:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span>Listen to Note to Self's episode on </span><a style=\"line-height: 1.5em; font-size: 9.5pt;\" href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/story/stingray-conspiracy-theory-daniel-rigmaiden-radiolab/\">surveillance coverage</a><span>.</span></li>\n<li><a style=\"line-height: 1.5em; font-size: 9.5pt;\" href=\"http://www.washingtonpost.com/business/technology/new-surveillance-technology-can-track-everyone-in-an-area-for-several-hours-at-a-time/2014/02/05/82f1556e-876f-11e3-a5bd-844629433ba3_story.html\">\"New surveillance technology can track everyone in an area for several hours at a time,\"</a><span> from the Washington Post</span></li>\n<li><a style=\"line-height: 1.5em; font-size: 9.5pt;\" href=\"http://cironline.org/reports/hollywood-style-surveillance-technology-inches-closer-reality-6228\">\"Hollywood-style surveillance technology inches closer to reality,\"</a><span> from the Center of Investigative Reporting</span></li>\n<li><span>Ross McNutt's company </span><a style=\"line-height: 1.5em; font-size: 9.5pt;\" href=\"http://www.pss-1.com/\">Persistent Surveillance Systems</a></li>\n</ul>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-06-18T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"antibodies-part-1-crispr", "title"=>"Antibodies Part 1: CRISPR", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15crispr.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=510201", "description"=>"<div><span>Hidden inside some of the world’s smallest organisms is one of the most powerful tools scientists have ever stumbled across. It's a defense system that has existed in bacteria for millions of years and it may some day let us change the course of human evolution. </span></div>\n<p><span>Out drinking with a few biologists, Jad finds out about something called CRISPR. No, it’s not a robot or the latest dating app, it’s a method for genetic manipulation that is rewriting the way we change DNA. Scientists say they’ll someday be able to use CRISPR to fight cancer and maybe even bring animals back from the dead. Or, pretty much do whatever you want. Jad and Robert delve into how CRISPR does what it does, and consider whether we should be worried about a future full of flying pigs, or the simple fact that scientists have now used CRISPR to tweak the genes of human embryos.</span></p>\n<p><em><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/update-crispr/?token=1959941d4b6e2e86b0014a8a180cda5f&amp;content_type_id=26&amp;object_id=738864&amp;_=c72629fa\">As of February 24th, 2017 we've updated this story.</a><br></em></p>", "tease"=>"In 2012, scientists had a realization: hidden inside one of the world&rsquo;s smallest organisms, was one of the world&rsquo;s most powerful tools.", "publishTime"=>1433579916000, "publish-at"=>"2015-06-06T04:38:36", "date-line-ts"=>1433579916, "estimated-duration"=>1845, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/5927204872_5a6d669faf_o.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/611/c/80/1/5927204872_5a6d669faf_o.jpg", "h"=>611, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/5927204872_5a6d669faf_o.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>138497, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: NIH/NIAID"}, "objectID"=>"510201", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Antibodies Part 1: CRISPR", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div><span>Hidden inside some of the world’s smallest organisms is one of the most powerful tools scientists have ever stumbled across. It's a defense system that has existed in bacteria for millions of years and it may some day let us change the course of human evolution. </span></div>\n<p><span>Out drinking with a few biologists, Jad finds out about something called CRISPR. No, it’s not a robot or the latest dating app, it’s a method for genetic manipulation that is rewriting the way we change DNA. Scientists say they’ll someday be able to use CRISPR to fight cancer and maybe even bring animals back from the dead. Or, pretty much do whatever you want. Jad and Robert delve into how CRISPR does what it does, and consider whether we should be worried about a future full of flying pigs, or the simple fact that scientists have now used CRISPR to tweak the genes of human embryos.</span></p>\n<p><em><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/update-crispr/?token=1959941d4b6e2e86b0014a8a180cda5f&amp;content_type_id=26&amp;object_id=738864&amp;_=c72629fa\">As of February 24th, 2017 we've updated this story.</a><br></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-06-06T04:38:36", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"nazi-summer-camp", "title"=>"Nazi Summer Camp", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15nazicamp.mp3?awparams=morgan%3Afalse&awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=455649", "description"=>"<p><span>Reporter Karen Duffin and her father were talking one day when, just as an aside, he mentioned the Nazi prisoners of war that worked on his Idaho farm when he was a kid. Karen was shocked ... and then immediately obsessed. So she spoke with historians, dug through the National Archives and oral histories, and uncovered the astonishing story of a small town in Alabama overwhelmed by thousands of German prisoners of war.  Along the way, she discovered that a very fundamental question  - one that we are struggling with today  -  was playing out seventy years ago in hundreds of towns across America: When your enemy is at your mercy, how should you treat them? Karen helps Jad and Robert try to figure out why we did what we did then, and why we are doing things so differently now.</span></p>\n<p><em>Produced by Kelsey Padgett. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>CORRECTION: A previous version of this podcast stated that the Nuremberg Laws and the Mississippi Black Code could be viewed side by side at a museum in Nuremberg. We were unable to confirm the existence of such an exhibit. We were also unable to confirm that the Nuremberg Laws were literally copied from the Mississippi Black Codes. The audio has been corrected to reflect this.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>We've gathered more photos of Camp Aliceville <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/more-photos-camp-aliceville-and-german-pows/?token=819dd12dc4ccff728efd3f3102273400&amp;content_type_id=26&amp;object_id=455652&amp;_=21281027\">here</a></em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to:</em></p>\n<div>Mary Bess Paluzzi, founding director of the Aliceville Museum </div>\n<div><span>John Gillum, current Director of the Aliceville Museum</span></div>\n<div>Sam Love, <a href=\"http://www.samlove.net/\">a filmmaker who gathered the oral histories</a></div>\n<div>Ruth Beaumont Cook, who wrote a great <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Guests-Behind-Barbed-Wire-Beaumont/dp/1575872609\" target=\"_blank\">book about Aliceville</a></div>", "tease"=>"The incredible, little-known story of the Nazi prisoners of war kept on American soil during World War II.", "publishTime"=>1432335093000, "publish-at"=>"2015-05-22T18:51:33", "date-line-ts"=>1432335093, "estimated-duration"=>1800, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/potter.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/819/1125/l/80/1/potter.png", "h"=>1125, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"A German POW throws a pot at Camp Aliceville", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/potter.png", "w"=>819, "id"=>137909, "credits-name"=>"Photo Courtesy of the Aliceville Museum, Inc., Aliceville, AL"}, "objectID"=>"455649", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Nazi Summer Camp", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Reporter Karen Duffin and her father were talking one day when, just as an aside, he mentioned the Nazi prisoners of war that worked on his Idaho farm when he was a kid. Karen was shocked ... and then immediately obsessed. So she spoke with historians, dug through the National Archives and oral histories, and uncovered the astonishing story of a small town in Alabama overwhelmed by thousands of German prisoners of war.  Along the way, she discovered that a very fundamental question  - one that we are struggling with today  -  was playing out seventy years ago in hundreds of towns across America: When your enemy is at your mercy, how should you treat them? Karen helps Jad and Robert try to figure out why we did what we did then, and why we are doing things so differently now.</span></p>\n<p><em>Produced by Kelsey Padgett. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>CORRECTION: A previous version of this podcast stated that the Nuremberg Laws and the Mississippi Black Code could be viewed side by side at a museum in Nuremberg. We were unable to confirm the existence of such an exhibit. We were also unable to confirm that the Nuremberg Laws were literally copied from the Mississippi Black Codes. The audio has been corrected to reflect this.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>We've gathered more photos of Camp Aliceville <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/more-photos-camp-aliceville-and-german-pows/?token=819dd12dc4ccff728efd3f3102273400&amp;content_type_id=26&amp;object_id=455652&amp;_=21281027\">here</a></em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to:</em></p>\n<div>Mary Bess Paluzzi, founding director of the Aliceville Museum </div>\n<div><span>John Gillum, current Director of the Aliceville Museum</span></div>\n<div>Sam Love, <a href=\"http://www.samlove.net/\">a filmmaker who gathered the oral histories</a></div>\n<div>Ruth Beaumont Cook, who wrote a great <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Guests-Behind-Barbed-Wire-Beaumont/dp/1575872609\" target=\"_blank\">book about Aliceville</a></div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-05-22T18:51:33", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-live-telltale-hearts-featuring-oliver-sacks", "title"=>"Radiolab Live: Tell-Tale Hearts featuring Oliver Sacks", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab051315.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=453314", "description"=>"<p>A few days ago Radiolab performed a live show and this episode we're bringing you a few of the highlights. They were stories of what motivates us, our drives, our loves and losses. Producer Molly Webster tells us the story of life, near-death and what happens when your heart starts to work against you. And we visit with Dr. Oliver Sacks one last time to reflect on his life, his loves and his endless sense of wonder.</p>\n<p>Special thanks to our musical guests, <a href=\"http://sopercussion.com/\">SO Percussion</a> and <a href=\"http://www.sarahlipstate.com/\">Sarah Lipstate</a></p>", "tease"=>"Highlights from a live Radiolab performance about hearts, driving forces, and the people we love - including a final conversation with Dr. Oliver Sacks.", "publishTime"=>1431451486000, "publish-at"=>"2015-05-12T13:24:46", "date-line-ts"=>1431403200, "estimated-duration"=>3490, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/BAM.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/955/636/c/80/1/BAM.jpg", "h"=>636, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Jad & Robert on stage at BAM", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/BAM.jpg", "w"=>955, "id"=>137317, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Matthew Septimus"}, "objectID"=>"453314", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Live: Tell-Tale Hearts featuring Oliver Sacks", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>A few days ago Radiolab performed a live show and this episode we're bringing you a few of the highlights. They were stories of what motivates us, our drives, our loves and losses. Producer Molly Webster tells us the story of life, near-death and what happens when your heart starts to work against you. And we visit with Dr. Oliver Sacks one last time to reflect on his life, his loves and his endless sense of wonder.</p>\n<p>Special thanks to our musical guests, <a href=\"http://sopercussion.com/\">SO Percussion</a> and <a href=\"http://www.sarahlipstate.com/\">Sarah Lipstate</a></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-05-12T13:24:46", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"living-room", "title"=>"The Living Room", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15thelivingroom.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=443884", "description"=>"<p>We're thrilled to present a piece from one of our favorite podcasts, Love + Radio (Nick van der Kolk and Brendan Baker). </p>\n<p>Producer Briana Breen brings us the story: Diane’s new neighbors across the way never shut their curtains, and that was the beginning of an intimate, but very one-sided relationship.</p>\n<p>Please listen to <a href=\"http://loveandradio.org/\">as much of Love + Radio as you can</a>. </p>", "tease"=>"Producer Briana Breen and the podcast Love + Radio bring us a story about a very&nbsp;eventful year in the life of an accidental voyeur.", "publishTime"=>1428607082000, "publish-at"=>"2015-04-09T15:18:02", "date-line-ts"=>1428607082, "estimated-duration"=>1698, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/351123047_b91fe7e6ff_o.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/683/l/80/1/351123047_b91fe7e6ff_o.jpg", "h"=>683, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/351123047_b91fe7e6ff_o.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>135488, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Bernhard Suter"}, "objectID"=>"443884", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Living Room", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>We're thrilled to present a piece from one of our favorite podcasts, Love + Radio (Nick van der Kolk and Brendan Baker). </p>\n<p>Producer Briana Breen brings us the story: Diane’s new neighbors across the way never shut their curtains, and that was the beginning of an intimate, but very one-sided relationship.</p>\n<p>Please listen to <a href=\"http://loveandradio.org/\">as much of Love + Radio as you can</a>. </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-04-09T15:18:02", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"video-radiolab-presents-radio-ambulante", "title"=>"VIDEO: Radiolab Presents: Radio Ambulante", "audio"=>nil, "description"=>"<p>Our story <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/los-frikis/\">Los Frikis</a> was a collaboration with <a href=\"http://radioambulante.org/en/\">Radio Ambulante</a>, who produced a story of their own about two of the last surviving frikis, Yohandra and Gerson. They've also made a translated video of their Spanish-language piece and we're thrilled to share it with you.</p>\n<p><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm139682526303648cfa01d48-358b-402c-9d6b-f663d4757167\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/o7QdPoEsNOk?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a7765580067766576220\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o7QdPoEsNOk&amp;feature=youtu.be\"></iframe></div></div>  </p>\n<p>Reporter <a href=\"http://radioambulante.org/en/?s=luis+trelles&amp;lang=en\">Luis Trelles</a> went to visit Yohandra and Gerson in the sanitarium where they still reside, still punks and still alive, though all their fellow frikis have died.  </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/620/375/l/80/1/Los_portada.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Yohandra and Gerson at the sanitarium in Pinar del Rio</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(Photo Credit: Luis Trelles)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"As a follow-up to our story Los Frikis, we're bringing you a translated version&nbsp;of Radio Ambulante's story on the same subject.", "publishTime"=>1427996291000, "publish-at"=>"2015-04-02T13:38:11", "date-line-ts"=>1427996291, "estimated-duration"=>0, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Yohandra_Gerson.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/750/498/l/80/1/Yohandra_Gerson.jpg", "h"=>498, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Yohandra and Gerson", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Yohandra_Gerson.jpg", "w"=>750, "id"=>135162, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Josu Tueba Leiva"}, "objectID"=>"441891", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"VIDEO: Radiolab Presents: Radio Ambulante", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Our story <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/los-frikis/\">Los Frikis</a> was a collaboration with <a href=\"http://radioambulante.org/en/\">Radio Ambulante</a>, who produced a story of their own about two of the last surviving frikis, Yohandra and Gerson. They've also made a translated video of their Spanish-language piece and we're thrilled to share it with you.</p>\n<p><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm139682526303648cfa01d48-358b-402c-9d6b-f663d4757167\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/o7QdPoEsNOk?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a7765580067766576220\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o7QdPoEsNOk&amp;feature=youtu.be\"></iframe></div></div>  </p>\n<p>Reporter <a href=\"http://radioambulante.org/en/?s=luis+trelles&amp;lang=en\">Luis Trelles</a> went to visit Yohandra and Gerson in the sanitarium where they still reside, still punks and still alive, though all their fellow frikis have died.  </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/620/375/l/80/1/Los_portada.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Yohandra and Gerson at the sanitarium in Pinar del Rio</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(Photo Credit: Luis Trelles)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-04-02T13:38:11", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"los-frikis", "title"=>"Los Frikis", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15losfrikis.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=439393", "description"=>"<p>How a group of 80’s Cuban misfits found rock-and-roll and created a revolution within a revolution, going into exile without ever leaving home. In a collaboration with <a href=\"http://radioambulante.org/en/\">Radio Ambulante</a>, reporter <a href=\"http://radioambulante.org/en/?s=luis+trelles&amp;lang=en\">Luis Trelles</a> bring us the story of punk rock’s arrival in Cuba and a small band of outsiders who sentenced themselves to death and set themselves free.</p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/620/411/l/80/1/18JTL12122010018.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Gerson Govea</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(Photo Credit: Josu Tueba Leiva)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><em>Produced by Tim Howard &amp; Matt Kielty. With production help from Andy Mills. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to VIH, Eskoria, Metamorfosis and Alio Die &amp; Mariolina Zitta for the use of their music. </em></p>", "tease"=>"The story of how punk rock&rsquo;s arrival in Cuba allowed a small band of outsiders to&nbsp;sentence themselves to death and set themselves free.", "publishTime"=>1427225645000, "publish-at"=>"2015-03-24T15:34:05", "date-line-ts"=>1427225645, "estimated-duration"=>1904, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/set_list.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1395/1860/l/80/1/set_list.jpg", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"set list for Gerson Govea's Chispa e Tren ", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/set_list.jpg", "w"=>1395, "id"=>134642, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Luis Trelles"}, "objectID"=>"439393", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Los Frikis", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>How a group of 80’s Cuban misfits found rock-and-roll and created a revolution within a revolution, going into exile without ever leaving home. In a collaboration with <a href=\"http://radioambulante.org/en/\">Radio Ambulante</a>, reporter <a href=\"http://radioambulante.org/en/?s=luis+trelles&amp;lang=en\">Luis Trelles</a> bring us the story of punk rock’s arrival in Cuba and a small band of outsiders who sentenced themselves to death and set themselves free.</p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/620/411/l/80/1/18JTL12122010018.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Gerson Govea</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(Photo Credit: Josu Tueba Leiva)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><em>Produced by Tim Howard &amp; Matt Kielty. With production help from Andy Mills. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to VIH, Eskoria, Metamorfosis and Alio Die &amp; Mariolina Zitta for the use of their music. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-03-24T15:34:05", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"la-mancha-screwjob", "title"=>"La Mancha Screwjob", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab022315.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=433231", "description"=>"<p><span>All the world’s a stage. So we push through the fourth wall, pierce the spandex-ed heart of professional wrestling, and travel 400 years into the past to unmask our obsession with authenticity and our desire to walk the line between reality and fantasy.</span></p>\n<p>Thanks to Nick Hakim for the use of <a href=\"https://soundcloud.com/enhakim\">his song \"The Light\". </a></p>", "tease"=>"This episode we pierce the spandex-ed heart of professional wrestling, and travel 400 years into the past to walk the line between reality and fantasy.", "publishTime"=>1424757010000, "publish-at"=>"2015-02-24T00:50:10", "date-line-ts"=>1424754000, "estimated-duration"=>3097, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/REALITY-CHECK-FINAL-SHOW-IMG.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/350/c/80/1/REALITY-CHECK-FINAL-SHOW-IMG.jpg", "h"=>350, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/REALITY-CHECK-FINAL-SHOW-IMG.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>133150, "credits-name"=>"Matt Kielty, Latif Nasser"}, "objectID"=>"433231", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"La Mancha Screwjob", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>All the world’s a stage. So we push through the fourth wall, pierce the spandex-ed heart of professional wrestling, and travel 400 years into the past to unmask our obsession with authenticity and our desire to walk the line between reality and fantasy.</span></p>\n<p>Thanks to Nick Hakim for the use of <a href=\"https://soundcloud.com/enhakim\">his song \"The Light\". </a></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-02-24T00:50:10", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"trust-engineers", "title"=>"The Trust Engineers", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15trustengineers.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=430674", "description"=>"<p>When we talk online, things can go south fast. But they don’t have to. Today, we meet a group of social engineers who are convinced that tiny changes in wording can make the online world a kinder, gentler place. So long as we agree to be their lab rats.</p>\n<p>Ok, yeah, we’re talking about Facebook. Because Facebook, or something like it, is more and more the way we share and like, and gossip and gripe. And because it's so big, Facebook has a created a laboratory of human behavior the likes of which we’ve never seen. We peek into the work of Arturo Bejar and a team of researchers who are tweaking our online experience, bit by bit, to try to make the world a better place. And along the way we can’t help but wonder whether that’s possible, or even a good idea.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"How a tiny group of social engineers are making our online&nbsp;relationships kindler and gentler, whether we like it or not.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1423530090000, "publish-at"=>"2015-02-09T20:01:30", "date-line-ts"=>1423530090, "estimated-duration"=>1836, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Kielty.facebookcrop.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1459/1035/c/80/1/Kielty.facebookcrop.jpg", "h"=>1035, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Kielty.facebookcrop.jpg", "w"=>1459, "id"=>132461, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Amy Pearl"}, "objectID"=>"430674", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Trust Engineers", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>When we talk online, things can go south fast. But they don’t have to. Today, we meet a group of social engineers who are convinced that tiny changes in wording can make the online world a kinder, gentler place. So long as we agree to be their lab rats.</p>\n<p>Ok, yeah, we’re talking about Facebook. Because Facebook, or something like it, is more and more the way we share and like, and gossip and gripe. And because it's so big, Facebook has a created a laboratory of human behavior the likes of which we’ve never seen. We peek into the work of Arturo Bejar and a team of researchers who are tweaking our online experience, bit by bit, to try to make the world a better place. And along the way we can’t help but wonder whether that’s possible, or even a good idea.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-02-09T20:01:30", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"invisibilia", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: Invisibilia", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast15invisibilia.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=424772", "description"=>"<div><span><em>Producers' Note: A correction has been made to this audio to reflect the wishes of the subject of this story, Paige Abendroth. NPR's Invisibilia's originally included Paige's birth name in this piece due to a miscommunication between Invisibilia's reporter, Alix Spiegel and Paige. We have not been in contact with Paige directly, but NPR has issued t</em></span><em>he following statement from Anne Gudenkauf, senior supervising editor of NPR's science desk: \"We would never have violated Paige’s wishes in this story; it’s an unfortunate misunderstanding.  Invisibilia's upcoming episode on Paige will be edited to remove references to the name she no longer recognizes. Also the upcoming episode, which focuses on how categories affect us all, will explore in more depth the changes in Paige's life over the two years that she and Alix have spoken and will do that, as always, with attention to bi-gender and transgender reporting guidelines.\"</em><span></span></div>\n\n<div><span>Former Radiolab producer Lulu Miller and NPR reporter Alix Spiegel come to the studio to give us a sneak peak of their new show, <a href=\"http://www.npr.org/programs/invisibilia/\">Invisibilia</a>.</span></div>\n\n\n\n<p>Invisibilia has an upcoming episode about categories, so Alix tells us a story about two very basic categories: boy and girl. We've heard lots of stories about the sometimes blurry boundaries between boy and girl, but Alix introduces us to someone who experiences those categories in a way that was totally, completely new to us.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em> </em></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"The&nbsp;lines between&nbsp;boy and girl can be blurry but NPR's Invisibilia&nbsp;introduces us to someone&nbsp;with a&nbsp;very&nbsp;new idea of how blurry they can be.", "publishTime"=>1420842377000, "publish-at"=>"2015-01-09T17:26:17", "date-line-ts"=>1420842377, "estimated-duration"=>1892, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/invisibiliasquare.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/1500/c/80/1/invisibiliasquare.jpg", "h"=>1500, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/invisibiliasquare.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>130867, "credits-name"=>"Invisibilia"}, "objectID"=>"424772", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: Invisibilia", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div><span><em>Producers' Note: A correction has been made to this audio to reflect the wishes of the subject of this story, Paige Abendroth. NPR's Invisibilia's originally included Paige's birth name in this piece due to a miscommunication between Invisibilia's reporter, Alix Spiegel and Paige. We have not been in contact with Paige directly, but NPR has issued t</em></span><em>he following statement from Anne Gudenkauf, senior supervising editor of NPR's science desk: \"We would never have violated Paige’s wishes in this story; it’s an unfortunate misunderstanding.  Invisibilia's upcoming episode on Paige will be edited to remove references to the name she no longer recognizes. Also the upcoming episode, which focuses on how categories affect us all, will explore in more depth the changes in Paige's life over the two years that she and Alix have spoken and will do that, as always, with attention to bi-gender and transgender reporting guidelines.\"</em><span></span></div>\n\n<div><span>Former Radiolab producer Lulu Miller and NPR reporter Alix Spiegel come to the studio to give us a sneak peak of their new show, <a href=\"http://www.npr.org/programs/invisibilia/\">Invisibilia</a>.</span></div>\n\n\n\n<p>Invisibilia has an upcoming episode about categories, so Alix tells us a story about two very basic categories: boy and girl. We've heard lots of stories about the sometimes blurry boundaries between boy and girl, but Alix introduces us to someone who experiences those categories in a way that was totally, completely new to us.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em> </em></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-01-09T17:26:17", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"football", "title"=>"American Football", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab012915.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=424416", "description"=>"<div>Today, we tackle football. It’s the most popular sport in the US, shining a sometimes harsh light on so much of what we have been, what we are, and what we hope to be. Savage, creative, brutal and balletic, whether you love it or loathe it … it’s a touchstone of the American identity.</div>\n<p><span>Along with conflicted parents and players and coaches who aren’t sure if the game will survive, we take a deep dive into the surprising history of how the game came to be. At the end of the 19th century, football is a nascent and nasty sport.</span> The sons of the most powerful men in the country are literally knocking themselves out to win these gladiatorial battles. But then the Carlisle Indian School, formed in 1879 to assimilate the children and grandchildren of the Native American men who fought the final Plains Wars, fields the most American team of all. The kids at Carlisle took the field to face off against a new world that was destroying theirs, and along the way, they changed the fundamentals of football forever. </p>\n<div><em>Correction: An earlier version of this episode included a few errors that we have corrected. We've also added one new piece of information. </em></div>\n\n<div><em>The piece originally stated that British football had no referees.  While this was true in the earliest days of British football, they were eventually added. We stated that referees were added to American football in response to Pop Warner. American referees existed prior to Pop Warner, in order to address brutality as well as the kind of rule-bending that Pop Warner specialized in.</em></div>\n\n<div><em>Chuck Klosterman said that the three most popular sports in the US are football, college football and major league baseball. In fact, baseball actually ranks 2nd, college football is third.</em></div>\n\n<div><em>Monet Edwards stated that 33 members of her family were players in the NFL. That number is actually 13. </em></div>\n\n<div><em>We also added one new fact: over 200 students at The Carlisle Indian School died of malnutrition, poor health or distress from homesickness. </em><br><br></div>\n<div><em>The audio has been adjusted to reflect these corrections.</em></div>", "tease"=>"The most popular sport in the US is savage, creative, brutal &amp; balletic. Love it or loathe it, it&rsquo;s a touchstone of the American identity. &nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1420737839000, "publish-at"=>"2015-01-08T12:23:59", "date-line-ts"=>1422550800, "estimated-duration"=>4440, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/battered_football.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/480/l/80/1/battered_football.png", "h"=>480, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/battered_football.png", "w"=>640, "id"=>132081, "credits-name"=>"flickr: Dewayne Neely/ CC BY-NC-ND 2.0"}, "objectID"=>"424416", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"American Football", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div>Today, we tackle football. It’s the most popular sport in the US, shining a sometimes harsh light on so much of what we have been, what we are, and what we hope to be. Savage, creative, brutal and balletic, whether you love it or loathe it … it’s a touchstone of the American identity.</div>\n<p><span>Along with conflicted parents and players and coaches who aren’t sure if the game will survive, we take a deep dive into the surprising history of how the game came to be. At the end of the 19th century, football is a nascent and nasty sport.</span> The sons of the most powerful men in the country are literally knocking themselves out to win these gladiatorial battles. But then the Carlisle Indian School, formed in 1879 to assimilate the children and grandchildren of the Native American men who fought the final Plains Wars, fields the most American team of all. The kids at Carlisle took the field to face off against a new world that was destroying theirs, and along the way, they changed the fundamentals of football forever. </p>\n<div><em>Correction: An earlier version of this episode included a few errors that we have corrected. We've also added one new piece of information. </em></div>\n\n<div><em>The piece originally stated that British football had no referees.  While this was true in the earliest days of British football, they were eventually added. We stated that referees were added to American football in response to Pop Warner. American referees existed prior to Pop Warner, in order to address brutality as well as the kind of rule-bending that Pop Warner specialized in.</em></div>\n\n<div><em>Chuck Klosterman said that the three most popular sports in the US are football, college football and major league baseball. In fact, baseball actually ranks 2nd, college football is third.</em></div>\n\n<div><em>Monet Edwards stated that 33 members of her family were players in the NFL. That number is actually 13. </em></div>\n\n<div><em>We also added one new fact: over 200 students at The Carlisle Indian School died of malnutrition, poor health or distress from homesickness. </em><br><br></div>\n<div><em>The audio has been adjusted to reflect these corrections.</em></div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2015-01-08T12:23:59", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2016=>[{"slug"=>"lose-lose", "title"=>"Lose Lose", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16loselose.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=726626", "description"=>"<div>No matter what sport you play, the object of the game is to win. And that’s hard enough to do. But we found a match where four top athletes had to do the opposite in one of the most high profile matches of their careers. Thanks to a quirk in the tournament rules, their best shot at winning was … to lose. </div>\n<div>\n<p>This episode, we scrutinize the most paradoxical and upside down badminton match of all time, a match that dumbfounded spectators, officials, and even the players themselves. And it got us to wondering …  what would sports look like if everyone played to lose?</p>\n</div>\n<div>\n<p><em>Reported by Latif Nasser. Produced by Matt Kielty and Annie McEwen and Latif Nasser.</em></p>\n</div>\n<div>\n<p><em>Special thanks to <span>Aparna Nancherla, Mark Phelan, Yuni Kartika, Greysia Polii, Joy Le Li, Mikyoung Kim, Stan Bischof, Vincent Liew, Kota Morikowa, Christ de Roij and Haeryun Kang.</span></em></p>\n<p><span></span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</p>\n</div>", "tease"=>"This episode we look at a high profile sporting event where,&nbsp;thanks to a quirk in the tournament rules, the best shot at winning was &hellip; to lose.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1483088400000, "publish-at"=>"2016-12-30T04:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1483088400, "estimated-duration"=>1518, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/mathieum_m.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1239/c/80/1/mathieum_m.jpg", "h"=>1239, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/mathieum_m.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>160274, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Mathieum_M"}, "objectID"=>"726626", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Lose Lose", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div>No matter what sport you play, the object of the game is to win. And that’s hard enough to do. But we found a match where four top athletes had to do the opposite in one of the most high profile matches of their careers. Thanks to a quirk in the tournament rules, their best shot at winning was … to lose. </div>\n<div>\n<p>This episode, we scrutinize the most paradoxical and upside down badminton match of all time, a match that dumbfounded spectators, officials, and even the players themselves. And it got us to wondering …  what would sports look like if everyone played to lose?</p>\n</div>\n<div>\n<p><em>Reported by Latif Nasser. Produced by Matt Kielty and Annie McEwen and Latif Nasser.</em></p>\n</div>\n<div>\n<p><em>Special thanks to <span>Aparna Nancherla, Mark Phelan, Yuni Kartika, Greysia Polii, Joy Le Li, Mikyoung Kim, Stan Bischof, Vincent Liew, Kota Morikowa, Christ de Roij and Haeryun Kang.</span></em></p>\n<p><span></span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</p>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-12-30T04:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"its-not-us-its-you", "title"=>"It's Not Us, It's You", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/rl_extras/rl_extras16itsnotus.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=693214", "description"=>"<div>It’s the end of the year, and the entire Radiolab team is starting to take stock and come up for air. We're excited about how much ground we've covered - stories about college debaters and figure skaters, meat allergies and salmon-eating trees, deathwatch beetles mating and Kpop stars dating - we're excited for what 2017 holds, and grateful because you have made all these things possible with your support. </div>\n<div>\n<p>But before 2016 comes to an end, we wanted to do something a little different. We wanted to swivel our attention back to you, our listeners, reconnect with some old friends to see how they are doing, and thank everyone for what they've shared with us.</p>\n</div>\n<div>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>\n</div>", "tease"=>"This episode we&nbsp;swivel our attention back to you, our listeners, reconnect with some old friends to see how they are doing, and thank everyone for what they've shared with us.", "publishTime"=>1481864400000, "publish-at"=>"2016-12-16T00:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1481868000, "estimated-duration"=>3558, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/noluck.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"Flickr-creativecommons"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/683/c/80/1/noluck.jpg", "h"=>683, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/noluck.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>159917, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: noluck"}, "objectID"=>"693214", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"It's Not Us, It's You", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div>It’s the end of the year, and the entire Radiolab team is starting to take stock and come up for air. We're excited about how much ground we've covered - stories about college debaters and figure skaters, meat allergies and salmon-eating trees, deathwatch beetles mating and Kpop stars dating - we're excited for what 2017 holds, and grateful because you have made all these things possible with your support. </div>\n<div>\n<p>But before 2016 comes to an end, we wanted to do something a little different. We wanted to swivel our attention back to you, our listeners, reconnect with some old friends to see how they are doing, and thank everyone for what they've shared with us.</p>\n</div>\n<div>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-12-16T00:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-presents-more-perfect-object-anyway", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - Object Anyway", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16mpobjectanyway.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=685691", "description"=>"<div><span><span><span>At the trial of James Batson in 1982, the prosecution eliminated all the black jurors from the jury pool. Batson objected, setting off a complicated discussion about jury selection that would make its way all the way up to the Supreme Court. On this episode of More Perfect, the Supreme Court ruling that was supposed to prevent race-based jury selection, but may have only made the problem worse.</span></span></span></div>\n\n<div><span><span><span> Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </span></span></span></div>\n\n\n<div>\n<p><span><span> </span></span></p>\n</div>\n<div><span><span><span> </span></span></span></div>", "tease"=>"On this episode of More Perfect, the Supreme Court ruling that was supposed to prevent race-based jury selection, but may have only made the problem worse. &nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1479853370000, "publish-at"=>"2016-11-22T17:22:50", "date-line-ts"=>1479855600, "estimated-duration"=>2993, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/05-batson-LARGE_iC4SxAB.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1239/c/80/1/05-batson-LARGE_iC4SxAB.jpg", "h"=>1239, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/05-batson-LARGE_iC4SxAB.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>159244, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"685691", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - Object Anyway", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div><span><span><span>At the trial of James Batson in 1982, the prosecution eliminated all the black jurors from the jury pool. Batson objected, setting off a complicated discussion about jury selection that would make its way all the way up to the Supreme Court. On this episode of More Perfect, the Supreme Court ruling that was supposed to prevent race-based jury selection, but may have only made the problem worse.</span></span></span></div>\n\n<div><span><span><span> Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </span></span></span></div>\n\n\n<div>\n<p><span><span> </span></span></p>\n</div>\n<div><span><span><span> </span></span></span></div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-11-22T17:22:50", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"one-vote", "title"=>"One Vote", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16onevote.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=680738", "description"=>"<div>Come election season, it's easy to get cynical. Why cast a ballot if your single measly vote can't possibly change anything?</div>\n<p>In our first-ever election special, we set off to find a single vote that made a difference. We venture from the biggest election on the planet - where polling officials must brave a lion-inhabited forest to collect the vote of an ascetic temple priest - to the smallest election on the planet - where there are no polling officials, only kitty cats wearing nametags. Along the way, we meet a too-trusting advice columnist, a Texan Emperor, and a passive-aggressive mom who helped change American democracy forever. </p>\n<p><em>Reported by Latif Nasser with help from Tracie Hunte. Produced by Simon Adler, Tracie Hunte, Matt Kielty, Annie McEwen and Latif Nasser. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to The Plymouth Fife and Drum Corps and their director Jim Predhomme. Special thanks also to Professors Timothy Harris, Krista Kesselring, Charles Somerwine, Jim Lehring, Isabel DiVanna, Sara Bronin, Wanda Sobieski, Paula F. Casey, Andrea Mansker, and Jenny Diamond Cheng. Thanks to the Tennessee Archive of Moving Image and Sound. And thanks as well to Cindy Horswell, Robin Melvin, Ken Herman, Laura Harrington and Mel Marvin. </em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>", "tease"=>"In our first-ever election special, we set off to find a single vote that made a difference.", "publishTime"=>1478505600000, "publish-at"=>"2016-11-07T03:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1478505600, "estimated-duration"=>2911, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/vote.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/554/380/c/80/1/vote.jpg", "h"=>380, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/vote.jpg", "w"=>554, "id"=>158637, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Dean Terry"}, "objectID"=>"680738", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"One Vote", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div>Come election season, it's easy to get cynical. Why cast a ballot if your single measly vote can't possibly change anything?</div>\n<p>In our first-ever election special, we set off to find a single vote that made a difference. We venture from the biggest election on the planet - where polling officials must brave a lion-inhabited forest to collect the vote of an ascetic temple priest - to the smallest election on the planet - where there are no polling officials, only kitty cats wearing nametags. Along the way, we meet a too-trusting advice columnist, a Texan Emperor, and a passive-aggressive mom who helped change American democracy forever. </p>\n<p><em>Reported by Latif Nasser with help from Tracie Hunte. Produced by Simon Adler, Tracie Hunte, Matt Kielty, Annie McEwen and Latif Nasser. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to The Plymouth Fife and Drum Corps and their director Jim Predhomme. Special thanks also to Professors Timothy Harris, Krista Kesselring, Charles Somerwine, Jim Lehring, Isabel DiVanna, Sara Bronin, Wanda Sobieski, Paula F. Casey, Andrea Mansker, and Jenny Diamond Cheng. Thanks to the Tennessee Archive of Moving Image and Sound. And thanks as well to Cindy Horswell, Robin Melvin, Ken Herman, Laura Harrington and Mel Marvin. </em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-11-07T03:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"alpha-gal", "title"=>"Alpha Gal", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16alphagal.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=677542", "description"=>"<div>Tuck your napkin under your chin.  We’re about to serve up a tale of love, loss, and lamb chops. </div>\n\n<div>\n<p>For as long as she can remember, Amy Pearl has loved meat in all its glorious cuts and marbled flavors. And then one day, for seemingly no reason, her body wouldn’t tolerate it.  No steaks. No brisket. No weenies.  It made no sense to her or to her doctor: why couldn’t she eat something that she had routinely enjoyed for decades? Something our evolutionary forebears have eaten since time immemorial?  The answer involves mysterious maps, interpretive dance, and a collision of three different species.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div>\n<p><em>Produced by Annie McEwen &amp; Matt Kielty with reporting help from Latif Nasser</em></p>\n</div>\n\n<div>\n<p><em>Thanks to our friends at <a href=\"http://www.sporkful.com/\">The Sporkful</a>, we encourage you to listen to them if you aren't already. </em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>\n</div>", "tease"=>"Tuck your napkin under your chin. &nbsp;We&rsquo;re about to serve up a tale of love, loss, and lamb chops.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1477603992000, "publish-at"=>"2016-10-27T17:33:12", "date-line-ts"=>1477603992, "estimated-duration"=>2042, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Pearl2.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/754/692/c/80/1/Pearl2.jpg", "h"=>692, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Pearl2.jpg", "w"=>754, "id"=>158334, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Amy Pearl"}, "objectID"=>"677542", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Alpha Gal", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div>Tuck your napkin under your chin.  We’re about to serve up a tale of love, loss, and lamb chops. </div>\n\n<div>\n<p>For as long as she can remember, Amy Pearl has loved meat in all its glorious cuts and marbled flavors. And then one day, for seemingly no reason, her body wouldn’t tolerate it.  No steaks. No brisket. No weenies.  It made no sense to her or to her doctor: why couldn’t she eat something that she had routinely enjoyed for decades? Something our evolutionary forebears have eaten since time immemorial?  The answer involves mysterious maps, interpretive dance, and a collision of three different species.</p>\n</div>\n\n<div>\n<p><em>Produced by Annie McEwen &amp; Matt Kielty with reporting help from Latif Nasser</em></p>\n</div>\n\n<div>\n<p><em>Thanks to our friends at <a href=\"http://www.sporkful.com/\">The Sporkful</a>, we encourage you to listen to them if you aren't already. </em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-10-27T17:33:12", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"seneca-nebraska", "title"=>"Seneca, Nebraska", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16senecanebraska.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=671528", "description"=>"<div dir=\"ltr\">Back in 2014 the town of Seneca, Nebraska was deeply divided. How divided? They were so fed up with each other that some citizens began circulating a petition that proposed a radical solution. If a majority wanted to they'd self-destruct, end the town and wipe their community off the map. </div>\n<p>Producer Simon Adler goes to Seneca to knock on doors and sit down with residents for a series of kitchen table conversations. Along the way, we try to piece together what happened in this tiny town and what its fracture says about America.</p>\n<p><em>Produced and Reported by Simon Adler. <span>Special Thanks to Matthew Hansen of the Omaha World Herald.</span></em></p>\n<p><span> Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </span></p>", "tease"=>"In 2014 the town of Seneca, Nebraska was so deeply divided that they weighed their own self-destruction. &nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1476252000000, "publish-at"=>"2016-10-12T02:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1476252000, "estimated-duration"=>1777, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Seneca_2.JPG", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/1/Seneca_2.JPG", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Seneca_2.JPG", "w"=>1860, "id"=>157816, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Simon Adler"}, "objectID"=>"671528", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Seneca, Nebraska", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div dir=\"ltr\">Back in 2014 the town of Seneca, Nebraska was deeply divided. How divided? They were so fed up with each other that some citizens began circulating a petition that proposed a radical solution. If a majority wanted to they'd self-destruct, end the town and wipe their community off the map. </div>\n<p>Producer Simon Adler goes to Seneca to knock on doors and sit down with residents for a series of kitchen table conversations. Along the way, we try to piece together what happened in this tiny town and what its fracture says about America.</p>\n<p><em>Produced and Reported by Simon Adler. <span>Special Thanks to Matthew Hansen of the Omaha World Herald.</span></em></p>\n<p><span> Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-10-12T02:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"primitive-streak", "title"=>"The Primitive Streak", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16primitivestreak.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=665637", "description"=>"<p>Last May, two research groups announced a breakthrough: they each grew human embryos, in the lab, longer than ever before. In doing so, they witnessed a period of human development no one had ever seen. But in the process, they crashed up against something called the '14-day rule,' a guideline set over 30 years ago that dictates what we do, and possibly how we feel, about human embryos in the lab.</p>\n<p>On this episode, join producer Molly Webster as she peers down at our very own origins, and wonders: what do we do now?</p>\n<p><em>This piece was produced by Molly Webster and Annie McEwen, with help from Matt Kielty.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks goes to the Bioethics Research Library at Georgetown University; Omar Sultan Haque, Kevin Fitzgerald, SJ, and Josephine Johnston; Charlie McCarthy; Elizabeth Lockett, Mark Hill, and Robert Cork; plus, Eric Boodman, Lauren Morello, and Martin Pera.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Producer's note about the image:</strong></p>\n<p>Check out the super cool picture that's running with this piece. Scientist Gist Croft sent it to me a couple of weeks after my visit to the Rockefeller lab: it’s an image of the very embryo I looked at under the microscope - a twelve-day old human embryo - but with all the detail highlighted using fluorescent dye. (When I looked in person, we were using a light microscope that showed everything in black and white, with not nearly that precision.) The neon green bits are what's called the epiblast, the clump of cells from which the entire human body develops. See how it looks like it's pulling apart in to two? The scientists don’t know for sure, but they think this embryo might have been on it's way to becoming TWO embryos. Twinning! In action!</p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "tease"=>"In a recent breakthrough, researchers grew human embryos longer than ever before, witnessing&nbsp;a mysterious part of human development, and crashing into a decades-old ethical dilemma.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1474610400000, "publish-at"=>"2016-09-23T02:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1474610400, "estimated-duration"=>1782, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/IMG_3571.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/1024/c/80/1/IMG_3571.jpg", "h"=>1024, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Day12 human embryo, attached in vitro, showing epiblast (green) and structural proteins actin (pink) and tubulin (blue).", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/IMG_3571.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>157221, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Gist Croft, Cecilia Pelligrini, Ali H. Brivanlou, The Rockefeller University."}, "objectID"=>"665637", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Primitive Streak", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Last May, two research groups announced a breakthrough: they each grew human embryos, in the lab, longer than ever before. In doing so, they witnessed a period of human development no one had ever seen. But in the process, they crashed up against something called the '14-day rule,' a guideline set over 30 years ago that dictates what we do, and possibly how we feel, about human embryos in the lab.</p>\n<p>On this episode, join producer Molly Webster as she peers down at our very own origins, and wonders: what do we do now?</p>\n<p><em>This piece was produced by Molly Webster and Annie McEwen, with help from Matt Kielty.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks goes to the Bioethics Research Library at Georgetown University; Omar Sultan Haque, Kevin Fitzgerald, SJ, and Josephine Johnston; Charlie McCarthy; Elizabeth Lockett, Mark Hill, and Robert Cork; plus, Eric Boodman, Lauren Morello, and Martin Pera.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Producer's note about the image:</strong></p>\n<p>Check out the super cool picture that's running with this piece. Scientist Gist Croft sent it to me a couple of weeks after my visit to the Rockefeller lab: it’s an image of the very embryo I looked at under the microscope - a twelve-day old human embryo - but with all the detail highlighted using fluorescent dye. (When I looked in person, we were using a light microscope that showed everything in black and white, with not nearly that precision.) The neon green bits are what's called the epiblast, the clump of cells from which the entire human body develops. See how it looks like it's pulling apart in to two? The scientists don’t know for sure, but they think this embryo might have been on it's way to becoming TWO embryos. Twinning! In action!</p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-09-23T02:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"update-eye-sky", "title"=>"Update: Eye In the Sky", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16eyeinskyupdate.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=662727", "description"=>"<p>An update on Ross McNutt and his superpower — he can zoom in on everyday life, then rewind and fast-forward to solve crimes in a shutter-flash. But should he?</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>In 2004, when casualties in Iraq were rising due to roadside bombs, Ross McNutt and his team came up with an idea. With a small plane and a 44 mega-pixel camera, they figured out how to watch an entire city all at once, all day long. Whenever a bomb detonated, they could zoom onto that spot and then, because this eye in the sky had been there all along, they could scroll back in time and see - literally see - who planted it. After the war, Ross McNutt retired from the Air Force, and brought this technology back home with him. Manoush Zomorodi and Alex Goldmark from the podcast “Note to Self” give us the lowdown on Ross’s unique brand of persistent surveillance, from Juarez, Mexico to Dayton, Ohio. Then, once we realize what we can do, we wonder whether we should.</span></p>\n<p><em>Produced by Andy Mills. Special thanks to Dan Tucker and George Schulz.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"Ross McNutt has a superpower &mdash; he can zoom in on everyday life, then rewind and fast-forward to solve crimes in a shutter-flash. But should he?", "publishTime"=>1473730319000, "publish-at"=>"2016-09-12T21:31:59", "date-line-ts"=>1473730319, "estimated-duration"=>2190, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/IMG_4917.JPG", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/928/684/l/80/1/IMG_4917.JPG", "h"=>684, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.pss-1.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/IMG_4917.JPG", "w"=>928, "id"=>139096, "credits-name"=>"Persistent Survelliance Systems"}, "objectID"=>"662727", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Update: Eye In the Sky", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>An update on Ross McNutt and his superpower — he can zoom in on everyday life, then rewind and fast-forward to solve crimes in a shutter-flash. But should he?</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>In 2004, when casualties in Iraq were rising due to roadside bombs, Ross McNutt and his team came up with an idea. With a small plane and a 44 mega-pixel camera, they figured out how to watch an entire city all at once, all day long. Whenever a bomb detonated, they could zoom onto that spot and then, because this eye in the sky had been there all along, they could scroll back in time and see - literally see - who planted it. After the war, Ross McNutt retired from the Air Force, and brought this technology back home with him. Manoush Zomorodi and Alex Goldmark from the podcast “Note to Self” give us the lowdown on Ross’s unique brand of persistent surveillance, from Juarez, Mexico to Dayton, Ohio. Then, once we realize what we can do, we wonder whether we should.</span></p>\n<p><em>Produced by Andy Mills. Special thanks to Dan Tucker and George Schulz.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-09-12T21:31:59", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"invisible-girl", "title"=>"The Girl Who Doesn't Exist", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16doesntexist.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=658215", "description"=>"<p>In today’s episode, we meet a young woman from Texas, born and raised, who can’t prove that she exists.</p>\n<p>Alecia Faith Pennington was born at home, homeschooled, and never visited a dentist or a hospital. By both chance and design she is completely invisible in the eyes of the state. We follow Faith as she struggles to free herself from one restrictive world only to find that she is trapped in another. In her journey to prove her American citizenship she attempts to answer the age-old question: who am I?</p>\n<p><em>Reported and produced by Alexandra Leigh Young. Produced by Andy Mills and Brenna Farrell. Special thanks to Savannah Escobar, Nick Reed, Chris Van Deusen, David Glenn, Zen Allegra, Russell Whelan, Rachel Coleman and Lake Travis Zipline Adventures.</em></p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this episode's web copy incorrectly stated that Faith Pennington was born on a farm. Pennington was born at home in Houston, TX, then she and her family moved to a farm in Kerrville, TX, where she was raised. </em></p>\n<p><em>Faith’s original Youtube video is posted here: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CPtpKNyaO0U\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CPtpKNyaO0U</a></em></p>\n<p><em>For updates on Faith’s journey, visit her Facebook page Help Me Prove It: <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/Help-Me-Prove-It-882732628415890/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.facebook.com/Help-Me-Prove-It-882732628415890/</a></em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "tease"=>"In today&rsquo;s episode, we meet a young woman from Texas, born and raised, who can&rsquo;t prove that she exists.", "publishTime"=>1472454000000, "publish-at"=>"2016-08-29T03:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1472457600, "estimated-duration"=>2024, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Jeff_Moore.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"Flickr-CC"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/683/h/80/1/Jeff_Moore.jpg", "h"=>683, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Jeff_Moore.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>156231, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Jeff Moore"}, "objectID"=>"658215", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Girl Who Doesn't Exist", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In today’s episode, we meet a young woman from Texas, born and raised, who can’t prove that she exists.</p>\n<p>Alecia Faith Pennington was born at home, homeschooled, and never visited a dentist or a hospital. By both chance and design she is completely invisible in the eyes of the state. We follow Faith as she struggles to free herself from one restrictive world only to find that she is trapped in another. In her journey to prove her American citizenship she attempts to answer the age-old question: who am I?</p>\n<p><em>Reported and produced by Alexandra Leigh Young. Produced by Andy Mills and Brenna Farrell. Special thanks to Savannah Escobar, Nick Reed, Chris Van Deusen, David Glenn, Zen Allegra, Russell Whelan, Rachel Coleman and Lake Travis Zipline Adventures.</em></p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this episode's web copy incorrectly stated that Faith Pennington was born on a farm. Pennington was born at home in Houston, TX, then she and her family moved to a farm in Kerrville, TX, where she was raised. </em></p>\n<p><em>Faith’s original Youtube video is posted here: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CPtpKNyaO0U\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CPtpKNyaO0U</a></em></p>\n<p><em>For updates on Faith’s journey, visit her Facebook page Help Me Prove It: <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/Help-Me-Prove-It-882732628415890/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.facebook.com/Help-Me-Prove-It-882732628415890/</a></em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-08-29T03:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"playing-god", "title"=>"Playing God", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16playinggod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=655987", "description"=>"<p>When people are dying and you can only save some, how do you choose? Maybe you save the youngest. Or the sickest. Maybe you even just put all the names in a hat and pick at random. Would your answer change if a sick person was standing right in front of you?</p>\n<p>In this episode, we follow <em>New York Times</em> reporter Sheri Fink as she searches for the answer. In a warzone, a hurricane, a church basement, and an earthquake, the question remains the same. What happens, what should happen, when humans are forced to play god?</p>\n<p><em>Produced by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen. Reported by Sheri Fink. </em></p>\n<p>In the book that inspired this episode you can find more about what transpired at Memorial Hospital during Hurricane Katrina, <a href=\"http://www.sherifink.net\">Sheri Fink’s</a> exhaustively reported <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Five-Days-Memorial-Storm-Ravaged-Hospital/dp/0307718972/ref=sr_1_1?ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1471783956&amp;sr=8-1&amp;keywords=five+days+at+memorial\">Five Days at Memorial</a></p>\n<p>You can find more about the work going on in Maryland at: <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/triage\">www.nytimes.com/triage</a></p>\n<p>Very special thanks to Lilly Sullivan. </p>\n<p>Special thanks also to: Pat Walters and Jim McCutcheon and Todd Menesses from WWL in New Orleans, <span>the researchers for the allocation of scarce resources project in Maryland - Dr. Lee Daugherty Biddison from Johns </span><span>Hopkins University School of Medicine, Howie Gwon from the Johns Hopkins Medicine Office of Emergency Management, Alan Regenberg </span><span>of the Berman Institute of Bioethics and Dr. Eric Toner of the UPMC Center </span><span>for Health Security.</span></p>\n<p><span> Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </span></p>", "tease"=>"When people are dying and you can only save some, how do you choose?&nbsp;What happens, what should happen, when humans are forced to play god?", "publishTime"=>1471827600000, "publish-at"=>"2016-08-21T21:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1471827600, "estimated-duration"=>3689, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Twm.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1280/960/l/80/1/Twm.jpg", "h"=>960, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Twm.jpg", "w"=>1280, "id"=>156051, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Twm"}, "objectID"=>"655987", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Playing God", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>When people are dying and you can only save some, how do you choose? Maybe you save the youngest. Or the sickest. Maybe you even just put all the names in a hat and pick at random. Would your answer change if a sick person was standing right in front of you?</p>\n<p>In this episode, we follow <em>New York Times</em> reporter Sheri Fink as she searches for the answer. In a warzone, a hurricane, a church basement, and an earthquake, the question remains the same. What happens, what should happen, when humans are forced to play god?</p>\n<p><em>Produced by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen. Reported by Sheri Fink. </em></p>\n<p>In the book that inspired this episode you can find more about what transpired at Memorial Hospital during Hurricane Katrina, <a href=\"http://www.sherifink.net\">Sheri Fink’s</a> exhaustively reported <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Five-Days-Memorial-Storm-Ravaged-Hospital/dp/0307718972/ref=sr_1_1?ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1471783956&amp;sr=8-1&amp;keywords=five+days+at+memorial\">Five Days at Memorial</a></p>\n<p>You can find more about the work going on in Maryland at: <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/triage\">www.nytimes.com/triage</a></p>\n<p>Very special thanks to Lilly Sullivan. </p>\n<p>Special thanks also to: Pat Walters and Jim McCutcheon and Todd Menesses from WWL in New Orleans, <span>the researchers for the allocation of scarce resources project in Maryland - Dr. Lee Daugherty Biddison from Johns </span><span>Hopkins University School of Medicine, Howie Gwon from the Johns Hopkins Medicine Office of Emergency Management, Alan Regenberg </span><span>of the Berman Institute of Bioethics and Dr. Eric Toner of the UPMC Center </span><span>for Health Security.</span></p>\n<p><span> Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-08-21T21:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"from-tree-to-shining-tree", "title"=>"From Tree to Shining Tree", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16trees.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=648425", "description"=>"<p>A forest can feel like a place of great stillness and quiet. But if you dig a little deeper, there’s a hidden world beneath your feet as busy and complicated as a city at rush hour.</p>\n<p>In this story, a dog introduces us to a strange creature that burrows beneath forests, building an underground network where deals are made and lives are saved (and lost) in a complex web of friendships, rivalries, and business relations. It’s a network that scientists are only just beginning to untangle and map, and it’s not only turning our understanding of forests upside down, it’s leading some researchers to rethink what it means to be intelligent. </p>\n<p><em>Produced by Annie McEwen and Brenna Farrell. Special Thanks to Latif Nasser, Stephanie Tam, Teresa Ryan, Marc Guttman, and Professor Nicholas P. Money at Miami University. </em></p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this story misidentified naturalist David Attenborough as his late brother, actor Richard Attenborough. In addition, it dated the earliest scientific studies of fungi to the late 19th century, whereas naturalists have studied fungi since the 17th century. Lastly, we mistakenly stated that the oxygen that a plant respires comes from CO2, when in reality it comes from water. The audio has been adjusted to correct these facts.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>", "tease"=>"Forests&nbsp;feel&nbsp;like a place of great stillness but dig deeper and there&rsquo;s a hidden world beneath your feet as busy and complicated as a city.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1469869620000, "publish-at"=>"2016-07-30T05:07:00", "date-line-ts"=>1469869560, "estimated-duration"=>1924, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/7650980706_652e5ab178_k.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1236/l/80/1/7650980706_652e5ab178_k.jpg", "h"=>1236, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/7650980706_652e5ab178_k.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>155365, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Flickred!"}, "objectID"=>"648425", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"From Tree to Shining Tree", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>A forest can feel like a place of great stillness and quiet. But if you dig a little deeper, there’s a hidden world beneath your feet as busy and complicated as a city at rush hour.</p>\n<p>In this story, a dog introduces us to a strange creature that burrows beneath forests, building an underground network where deals are made and lives are saved (and lost) in a complex web of friendships, rivalries, and business relations. It’s a network that scientists are only just beginning to untangle and map, and it’s not only turning our understanding of forests upside down, it’s leading some researchers to rethink what it means to be intelligent. </p>\n<p><em>Produced by Annie McEwen and Brenna Farrell. Special Thanks to Latif Nasser, Stephanie Tam, Teresa Ryan, Marc Guttman, and Professor Nicholas P. Money at Miami University. </em></p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this story misidentified naturalist David Attenborough as his late brother, actor Richard Attenborough. In addition, it dated the earliest scientific studies of fungi to the late 19th century, whereas naturalists have studied fungi since the 17th century. Lastly, we mistakenly stated that the oxygen that a plant respires comes from CO2, when in reality it comes from water. The audio has been adjusted to correct these facts.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-07-30T05:07:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"david-and-wire", "title"=>"David and the Wire", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16davidandthewire.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=639551", "description"=>"<p>David Weinberg was stuck. He had been kicked out of college, was cleaning toilets by day, delivering pizzas by night and spending his weekends in jail. Then one night he heard a story on the radio and got it in his head that maybe he too could make a great radio story. He’d cast himself as the main character in a great documentary and he’d travel and <em>live</em> and steer his way out of his rut.</p>\n<p>So he bought a recorder and began to secretly record every last meaningful and mundane minute of his life and he found his great idea transformed into a troubling obsession. The very thing that gave him hope and purpose was also distancing him from those he loved the most. What if he’d created an archive of his life that had become his life?</p>\n<p><span><br><em>Produced by Andy Mills.</em></span></p>\n<p>David Weinberg is an award winning reporter and producer for KCRW. His most recent project is Below The Ten (<a href=\"http://www.belowtheten.com/\">www.belowtheten.com</a>)</p>\n<p>The iTunes page for the series: <a href=\"https://itunes.apple.com/us/podcast/kcrws-below-ten-life-in-south/id1030625802\">https://itunes.apple.com/us/podcast/kcrws-below-ten-life-in-south/id1030625802</a></p>\n<p>He has taken lots of those old recordings (and lots of new ones too) and put them together in a collection called Random Tape <a href=\"http://randomtape.com/\">http://randomtape.com/</a></p>\n<p>David explored some of this story on the late, great CBC show Wiretap: <a href=\"https://beta.prx.org/stories/82541\">https://beta.prx.org/stories/82541</a></p>\n<p>David isn't alone in being inspired by Scott Carrier. You can listen to his This American Life stories here: <a href=\"http://www.thisamericanlife.org/search?keys=scott%20carrier\">http://www.thisamericanlife.org/search?keys=scott%20carrier</a></p>\n<p>And we highly recommend his podcast Home of the Brave: <a href=\"http://homebrave.com/\">http://homebrave.com/</a></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "tease"=>"David Weinberg was stuck.&nbsp;Until he started recording every waking minute of his life.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1468300243000, "publish-at"=>"2016-07-12T01:10:43", "date-line-ts"=>1468300243, "estimated-duration"=>1878, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/rae_allen.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1273/l/80/1/rae_allen.jpg", "h"=>1273, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/rae_allen.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>154723, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Rae Allen"}, "objectID"=>"639551", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"David and the Wire", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>David Weinberg was stuck. He had been kicked out of college, was cleaning toilets by day, delivering pizzas by night and spending his weekends in jail. Then one night he heard a story on the radio and got it in his head that maybe he too could make a great radio story. He’d cast himself as the main character in a great documentary and he’d travel and <em>live</em> and steer his way out of his rut.</p>\n<p>So he bought a recorder and began to secretly record every last meaningful and mundane minute of his life and he found his great idea transformed into a troubling obsession. The very thing that gave him hope and purpose was also distancing him from those he loved the most. What if he’d created an archive of his life that had become his life?</p>\n<p><span><br><em>Produced by Andy Mills.</em></span></p>\n<p>David Weinberg is an award winning reporter and producer for KCRW. His most recent project is Below The Ten (<a href=\"http://www.belowtheten.com/\">www.belowtheten.com</a>)</p>\n<p>The iTunes page for the series: <a href=\"https://itunes.apple.com/us/podcast/kcrws-below-ten-life-in-south/id1030625802\">https://itunes.apple.com/us/podcast/kcrws-below-ten-life-in-south/id1030625802</a></p>\n<p>He has taken lots of those old recordings (and lots of new ones too) and put them together in a collection called Random Tape <a href=\"http://randomtape.com/\">http://randomtape.com/</a></p>\n<p>David explored some of this story on the late, great CBC show Wiretap: <a href=\"https://beta.prx.org/stories/82541\">https://beta.prx.org/stories/82541</a></p>\n<p>David isn't alone in being inspired by Scott Carrier. You can listen to his This American Life stories here: <a href=\"http://www.thisamericanlife.org/search?keys=scott%20carrier\">http://www.thisamericanlife.org/search?keys=scott%20carrier</a></p>\n<p>And we highly recommend his podcast Home of the Brave: <a href=\"http://homebrave.com/\">http://homebrave.com/</a></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-07-12T01:10:43", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"more-perfect-plaintiffs", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - The Imperfect Plaintiffs", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab062916_moreperfect.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=634398", "description"=>"<p>Last week, the court decided one of this term’s blockbuster cases — a case that could affect the future of affirmative action in this country. The plaintiff was Abigail Fisher, a white woman, who said she was rejected from the University of Texas because the university unfairly considered race as one of many factors when evaluating applicants. And while Fisher’s claims were the focus of the case, the story behind how she ended up in front of the Supreme Court is a lot more complicated.</p>\n<center>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/304/423/l/80/1/imageedit_1_5483384188.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Edward Blum is the director of the Project on Fair Representation</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(AEI) </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</center>\n<p>On this episode, we visit Edward Blum, a 64-year-old “legal entrepreneur” and former stockbroker who has become something of a Supreme Court matchmaker — He takes an issue, finds the perfect plaintiff, matches them with lawyers, and works his way to the highest court in the land. He’s had remarkable success, with 6 cases heard before the Supreme Court, including that of Abigail Fisher. We also head to Houston, Texas, where in 1998, an unusual 911 call led to one of the most important LGBT rights decisions in the Supreme Court’s history.</p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/646/486/l/80/1/tyronlawrence2.JPG\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">John Lawrence (L) and Tyron Garner (R) at the 2004 Pride Parade in Houston</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(J.D. Doyle/Houston LGBT History)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"></div>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/787/391/l/80/1/lawrencetyron3.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Mitchell Katine (L) introduces Tyron Garner (Middle) and John Lawrence (R) at a rally celebrating the court's decision</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(J.D. Doyle/Houston LGBT History)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>The key links:</strong></p>\n<p>- The <a href=\"http://harvardnotfair.org/\">website</a> Edward Blum is using to find plaintiffs for a case he is building against Harvard University<br>- Susan Carle's <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lawyers-Ethics-Pursuit-Social-Justice/dp/0814716407?ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1466011809&amp;ref_=la_B00E7ROGKC_1_1&amp;s=books&amp;sr=1-1\">book</a> on the history of legal ethics<br>- Ari Berman's <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Give-Us-Ballot-Struggle-America/dp/0374158274/ref=sr_1_1?s=books&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1466106368&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=ari+berman\">book</a> on voting rights in America<br>- An <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2006/09/14/obituaries/14garner.html\">obituary</a> for Tyron Garner when he died in 2006<br>- An <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/24/us/john-lawrence-plaintiff-in-lawrence-v-texas-dies-at-68.html\">obituary</a> for John Lawrence when he died in 2011<br>- Dale Carpenter's <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Flagrant-Conduct-Story-Lawrence-Texas/dp/0393345122\">book</a> on the history of Lawrence v. Texas<br>- A <a href=\"http://www.lambdalegal.org/in-court/cases/lawrence-v-texas\">Lambda Legal documentary</a> on the story of Lawrence v. Texas</p>\n<p><strong>The key cases</strong>:</p>\n<p>- 1896: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1850-1900/163us537\">Plessy v. Ferguson</a><br>- 1917: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1900-1940/245us60\">Buchanan v. Warley</a><br>- 1962: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1962/5\">National Association for the Advancement of Colored People v. Button</a><br>- 1986: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1985/85-140\">Bowers v. Hardwick</a><br>- 1996: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1995/94-805\">Bush v. Vera</a><br>- 2003: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2002/02-102\">Lawrence v. Texas</a><br>- 2009: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2008/08-322\">Northwest Austin Municipal Utility District Number One v. Holder</a><br>- 2013: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2012/12-96\">Shelby County v. Holder</a><br>- 2013: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2012/11-345\">Fisher v. University of Texas (1)</a><br>- 2016: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2015/14-940\">Evenwel v. Abbott</a><br>- 2016: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2015/14-981\">Fisher v. University of Texas (2)</a></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Ari Berman. His book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Give-Us-Ballot-Struggle-America/dp/0374158274/ref=sr_1_1?s=books&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1466106368&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=ari+berman\">Give Us the Ballot</a>, and his reporting for <a href=\"https://www.thenation.com/article/conservatives-who-gutted-voting-rights-act-are-now-challenging-one-person-one-vote/\">The Nation</a>, were hugely helpful in reporting this episode.  </em></p>\n<p><em>More Perfect is funded in part by The William and Flora Hewlett Foundation, The Charles Evans Hughes Memorial Foundation, and the Joyce Foundation.</em></p>\n<p><em>Supreme Court archival audio comes from <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\">Oyez®</a>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</em></p>", "tease"=>"On this episode of&nbsp;More Perfect, we'll talk about the figurative side door to the Supreme Court that has allowed individuals to influence policy for the many.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1467151200000, "publish-at"=>"2016-06-28T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1467151200, "estimated-duration"=>3906, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/03-perfect-plaintiff-SMALL.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/533/c/80/1/03-perfect-plaintiff-SMALL.jpg", "h"=>533, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"The scale of justice is not always balanced", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/03-perfect-plaintiff-SMALL.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>153984, "credits-name"=>"Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"634398", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - The Imperfect Plaintiffs", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Last week, the court decided one of this term’s blockbuster cases — a case that could affect the future of affirmative action in this country. The plaintiff was Abigail Fisher, a white woman, who said she was rejected from the University of Texas because the university unfairly considered race as one of many factors when evaluating applicants. And while Fisher’s claims were the focus of the case, the story behind how she ended up in front of the Supreme Court is a lot more complicated.</p>\n<center>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/304/423/l/80/1/imageedit_1_5483384188.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Edward Blum is the director of the Project on Fair Representation</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(AEI) </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</center>\n<p>On this episode, we visit Edward Blum, a 64-year-old “legal entrepreneur” and former stockbroker who has become something of a Supreme Court matchmaker — He takes an issue, finds the perfect plaintiff, matches them with lawyers, and works his way to the highest court in the land. He’s had remarkable success, with 6 cases heard before the Supreme Court, including that of Abigail Fisher. We also head to Houston, Texas, where in 1998, an unusual 911 call led to one of the most important LGBT rights decisions in the Supreme Court’s history.</p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/646/486/l/80/1/tyronlawrence2.JPG\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">John Lawrence (L) and Tyron Garner (R) at the 2004 Pride Parade in Houston</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(J.D. Doyle/Houston LGBT History)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"></div>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/787/391/l/80/1/lawrencetyron3.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Mitchell Katine (L) introduces Tyron Garner (Middle) and John Lawrence (R) at a rally celebrating the court's decision</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(J.D. Doyle/Houston LGBT History)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>The key links:</strong></p>\n<p>- The <a href=\"http://harvardnotfair.org/\">website</a> Edward Blum is using to find plaintiffs for a case he is building against Harvard University<br>- Susan Carle's <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lawyers-Ethics-Pursuit-Social-Justice/dp/0814716407?ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1466011809&amp;ref_=la_B00E7ROGKC_1_1&amp;s=books&amp;sr=1-1\">book</a> on the history of legal ethics<br>- Ari Berman's <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Give-Us-Ballot-Struggle-America/dp/0374158274/ref=sr_1_1?s=books&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1466106368&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=ari+berman\">book</a> on voting rights in America<br>- An <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2006/09/14/obituaries/14garner.html\">obituary</a> for Tyron Garner when he died in 2006<br>- An <a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/2011/12/24/us/john-lawrence-plaintiff-in-lawrence-v-texas-dies-at-68.html\">obituary</a> for John Lawrence when he died in 2011<br>- Dale Carpenter's <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Flagrant-Conduct-Story-Lawrence-Texas/dp/0393345122\">book</a> on the history of Lawrence v. Texas<br>- A <a href=\"http://www.lambdalegal.org/in-court/cases/lawrence-v-texas\">Lambda Legal documentary</a> on the story of Lawrence v. Texas</p>\n<p><strong>The key cases</strong>:</p>\n<p>- 1896: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1850-1900/163us537\">Plessy v. Ferguson</a><br>- 1917: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1900-1940/245us60\">Buchanan v. Warley</a><br>- 1962: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1962/5\">National Association for the Advancement of Colored People v. Button</a><br>- 1986: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1985/85-140\">Bowers v. Hardwick</a><br>- 1996: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1995/94-805\">Bush v. Vera</a><br>- 2003: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2002/02-102\">Lawrence v. Texas</a><br>- 2009: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2008/08-322\">Northwest Austin Municipal Utility District Number One v. Holder</a><br>- 2013: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2012/12-96\">Shelby County v. Holder</a><br>- 2013: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2012/11-345\">Fisher v. University of Texas (1)</a><br>- 2016: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2015/14-940\">Evenwel v. Abbott</a><br>- 2016: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2015/14-981\">Fisher v. University of Texas (2)</a></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Ari Berman. His book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Give-Us-Ballot-Struggle-America/dp/0374158274/ref=sr_1_1?s=books&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1466106368&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=ari+berman\">Give Us the Ballot</a>, and his reporting for <a href=\"https://www.thenation.com/article/conservatives-who-gutted-voting-rights-act-are-now-challenging-one-person-one-vote/\">The Nation</a>, were hugely helpful in reporting this episode.  </em></p>\n<p><em>More Perfect is funded in part by The William and Flora Hewlett Foundation, The Charles Evans Hughes Memorial Foundation, and the Joyce Foundation.</em></p>\n<p><em>Supreme Court archival audio comes from <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\">Oyez®</a>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-06-28T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"the_political_thicket", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - The Political Thicket", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab061016_moreperfect_episode2.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=627326", "description"=>"<p><em>This story comes from Radiolab's first ever spin-off podcast, More Perfect. To hear more, subscribe <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolabmoreperfect/episodes/\">here</a>.</em></p>\n<p>When Chief Justice Earl Warren was asked at the end of his career, “What was the most important case of your tenure?”, there were a lot of answers he could have given. After all, he had presided over some of the most important decisions in the court’s history — cases that dealt with segregation in schools, the right to an attorney, the right to remain silent, just to name a few. But his answer was a surprise: He said, “Baker v. Carr,” a 1962 redistricting case. </p>\n<p>On this episode of <em>More Perfect</em>, we talk about why this case was so important; important enough, in fact, that it pushed one Supreme Court justice to a nervous breakdown, brought a boiling feud to a head, put one justice in the hospital, and changed the course of the Supreme Court — and the nation — forever.</p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/800/800/l/80/1/pjimage_b6uICRg.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Associate Justice William O. Douglas (L) and Associate Justice Felix Frankfurter (R)</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(Harris &amp; Ewing Photography/Library of Congress)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/600/383/l/80/1/warrencourt.jpg.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\"><span>Top Row (left-right): </span><span>Charles E. Whittaker</span><span>, </span><span>John M. Harlan</span><span>,</span><span>William J. Brennan, Jr.</span><span>, </span><span>Potter Stewart</span><span>. Bottom Row (left-right): </span><span>William O. Douglas</span><span>, </span><span>Hugo L. Black</span><span>, </span><span>Earl Warren</span><span>, </span><span>Felix Frankfurter</span><span>, </span><span>Tom C. Clark</span><span>.</span></div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(Library of Congress)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/800/515/l/80/1/whittaker.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Associate Supreme Court Justice Charles Evans Whittaker at his desk in his chambers.</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(Heywood Davis)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Music in this episode by <a href=\"http://gyanriley.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Gyan Riley</a>, <a href=\"http://www.wqxr.org/people/alexander-overington/\" target=\"_blank\">Alex Overington</a>, <a href=\"http://www.squirrelthing.com/\" target=\"_blank\">David Herman</a>, <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/people/tobin-low/\" target=\"_blank\">Tobin Low</a> and <a href=\"http://www.jadabumrad.com/music.html\" target=\"_blank\">Jad Abumrad</a>. </strong></p>\n<p><em>More Perfect is funded in part by The William and Flora Hewlett Foundation, The Charles Evans Hughes Memorial Foundation, and the Joyce Foundation.</em></p>\n<p><em>Supreme Court archival audio comes from <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\">Oyez®</a>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</em></p>\n<p><em>Archival interviews with Justice William O. Douglas come from the Department of Rare Books and Special Collections at Princeton University Library.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Whittaker's clerks: Heywood Davis, Jerry Libin and James Adler. Also big thanks to Jerry Goldman at Oyez.</em></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"On this episode, the case that pushed one Supreme Court justice to a nervous breakdown, brought a boiling feud to a head, and changed the course of the Supreme Court forever.", "publishTime"=>1465503548000, "publish-at"=>"2016-06-09T16:19:08", "date-line-ts"=>1465542000, "estimated-duration"=>2638, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/whittaker-SMALL.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/533/c/80/1/whittaker-SMALL.jpg", "h"=>533, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Justice Charles Evans Whittaker.", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/whittaker-SMALL.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>153730, "credits-name"=>"Illustration credit: Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"627326", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - The Political Thicket", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><em>This story comes from Radiolab's first ever spin-off podcast, More Perfect. To hear more, subscribe <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolabmoreperfect/episodes/\">here</a>.</em></p>\n<p>When Chief Justice Earl Warren was asked at the end of his career, “What was the most important case of your tenure?”, there were a lot of answers he could have given. After all, he had presided over some of the most important decisions in the court’s history — cases that dealt with segregation in schools, the right to an attorney, the right to remain silent, just to name a few. But his answer was a surprise: He said, “Baker v. Carr,” a 1962 redistricting case. </p>\n<p>On this episode of <em>More Perfect</em>, we talk about why this case was so important; important enough, in fact, that it pushed one Supreme Court justice to a nervous breakdown, brought a boiling feud to a head, put one justice in the hospital, and changed the course of the Supreme Court — and the nation — forever.</p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/800/800/l/80/1/pjimage_b6uICRg.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Associate Justice William O. Douglas (L) and Associate Justice Felix Frankfurter (R)</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(Harris &amp; Ewing Photography/Library of Congress)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/600/383/l/80/1/warrencourt.jpg.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\"><span>Top Row (left-right): </span><span>Charles E. Whittaker</span><span>, </span><span>John M. Harlan</span><span>,</span><span>William J. Brennan, Jr.</span><span>, </span><span>Potter Stewart</span><span>. Bottom Row (left-right): </span><span>William O. Douglas</span><span>, </span><span>Hugo L. Black</span><span>, </span><span>Earl Warren</span><span>, </span><span>Felix Frankfurter</span><span>, </span><span>Tom C. Clark</span><span>.</span></div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(Library of Congress)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/800/515/l/80/1/whittaker.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Associate Supreme Court Justice Charles Evans Whittaker at his desk in his chambers.</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(Heywood Davis)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Music in this episode by <a href=\"http://gyanriley.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Gyan Riley</a>, <a href=\"http://www.wqxr.org/people/alexander-overington/\" target=\"_blank\">Alex Overington</a>, <a href=\"http://www.squirrelthing.com/\" target=\"_blank\">David Herman</a>, <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/people/tobin-low/\" target=\"_blank\">Tobin Low</a> and <a href=\"http://www.jadabumrad.com/music.html\" target=\"_blank\">Jad Abumrad</a>. </strong></p>\n<p><em>More Perfect is funded in part by The William and Flora Hewlett Foundation, The Charles Evans Hughes Memorial Foundation, and the Joyce Foundation.</em></p>\n<p><em>Supreme Court archival audio comes from <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\">Oyez®</a>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</em></p>\n<p><em>Archival interviews with Justice William O. Douglas come from the Department of Rare Books and Special Collections at Princeton University Library.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Whittaker's clerks: Heywood Davis, Jerry Libin and James Adler. Also big thanks to Jerry Goldman at Oyez.</em></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-06-09T16:19:08", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"the_buried_bodies_case", "title"=>"The Buried Bodies Case", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16buriedbodies.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=624449", "description"=>"<div><span><span>In 1973, a massive manhunt in New York's Adirondack Mountains ended when police captured a man named Robert Garrow.  And that’s when this story really gets started.</span></span></div>\n<div><span><span> </span></span></div>\n<div><span><span><span>This episode we consider a string of barbaric crimes by a hated man, and the attorney who, when called to defend him, also wound up defending a core principle of our legal system.  When Frank Armani learned his client’s most gruesome secrets, he made a morally startling decision that stunned the world and goes to the heart of what it means to be a defense attorney - how far should lawyers go to provide the best defense to the worst people?</span></span></span></div>\n<div>\n<p><em>NOTE: This episode contains graphic descriptions of sexual assault and violence.</em></p>\n<p><em>Produced by Matt Kielty and Brenna Farrell. Reported by Brenna Farrell.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Tom Alibrandi, author of Privileged Information, with Frank Armani, Laurence Gooley, author of Terror in the Adirondacks: The True Story of Serial Killer Robert F. Garrow, Charl Bader and the students in her Criminal Defense Clinic at Fordham University, Leslie Levin and the students in her Legal Profession class at The University of Connecticut School of Law, Clark D. Cunningham at Georgia State University College of Law, Debra Armani, Mary Armani, <span>Lohr McKinstry, Tom Scozzafava, </span>Stephanie Jenkins, Brian Farrell, Jennifer Brumback and Nick Capodice. </em></p>\n</div>\n\n<div><br><span></span>\n\n<span></span>\n\n<span></span></div>\n<div><span><span> </span></span></div>\n", "tease"=>"How far should lawyers&nbsp;go to provide the&nbsp;best defense to the worst people?", "publishTime"=>1464987600000, "publish-at"=>"2016-06-03T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1464987600, "estimated-duration"=>2878, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/10007400976_7b0194488e_k.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1237/c/80/1/10007400976_7b0194488e_k.jpg", "h"=>1237, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/10007400976_7b0194488e_k.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>153503, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Chuck Miller"}, "objectID"=>"624449", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Buried Bodies Case", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div><span><span>In 1973, a massive manhunt in New York's Adirondack Mountains ended when police captured a man named Robert Garrow.  And that’s when this story really gets started.</span></span></div>\n<div><span><span> </span></span></div>\n<div><span><span><span>This episode we consider a string of barbaric crimes by a hated man, and the attorney who, when called to defend him, also wound up defending a core principle of our legal system.  When Frank Armani learned his client’s most gruesome secrets, he made a morally startling decision that stunned the world and goes to the heart of what it means to be a defense attorney - how far should lawyers go to provide the best defense to the worst people?</span></span></span></div>\n<div>\n<p><em>NOTE: This episode contains graphic descriptions of sexual assault and violence.</em></p>\n<p><em>Produced by Matt Kielty and Brenna Farrell. Reported by Brenna Farrell.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Tom Alibrandi, author of Privileged Information, with Frank Armani, Laurence Gooley, author of Terror in the Adirondacks: The True Story of Serial Killer Robert F. Garrow, Charl Bader and the students in her Criminal Defense Clinic at Fordham University, Leslie Levin and the students in her Legal Profession class at The University of Connecticut School of Law, Clark D. Cunningham at Georgia State University College of Law, Debra Armani, Mary Armani, <span>Lohr McKinstry, Tom Scozzafava, </span>Stephanie Jenkins, Brian Farrell, Jennifer Brumback and Nick Capodice. </em></p>\n</div>\n\n<div><br><span></span>\n\n<span></span>\n\n<span></span></div>\n<div><span><span> </span></span></div>\n", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-06-03T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"coming-soon-more-perfect", "title"=>"Coming Soon: More Perfect", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/moreperfect/moreperfect052416_episode-zero.mp3?aisGetOriginalStream=true&awCollectionId=600817&awEpisodeId=622543", "description"=>"<p>How does an elite group of nine people shape everything from marriage and money, to safety and sex for an entire nation? From the producers of Radiolab, More Perfect dives into the rarefied world of the Supreme Court to explain how cases deliberated inside hallowed halls affect lives far away from the bench.</p>", "tease"=>"From the producers of Radiolab, More Perfect dives into the rarefied world of the Supreme Court. Season one starts&nbsp;June 2, 2016.", "publishTime"=>1464127200000, "publish-at"=>"2016-05-24T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1464105600, "estimated-duration"=>62, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/SupremeCourt_MP_Episode.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1626/1084/l/80/1/SupremeCourt_MP_Episode.png", "h"=>1084, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"An original illustration of the Supreme Court Building in Washington, D.C. by Mitch Boyer", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/SupremeCourt_MP_Episode.png", "w"=>1626, "id"=>153401, "credits-name"=>"Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"622543", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Coming Soon: More Perfect", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>How does an elite group of nine people shape everything from marriage and money, to safety and sex for an entire nation? From the producers of Radiolab, More Perfect dives into the rarefied world of the Supreme Court to explain how cases deliberated inside hallowed halls affect lives far away from the bench.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-05-24T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"bigger-bacon", "title"=>"Bigger Than Bacon", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16biggerthanbacon.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=603688", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Today's story is a mystery, shockingly hot, and vanishingly tiny. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>It starts with a sound, rising like a mist from the marsh, around a dock in South Carolina. But where it goes next - from submarines to superheroes (and yes, Keanu Reeves!); from the surface of the sun to the middle of the brain - is far from expected. Producer Molly Webster brings her family along for the ride. Enjoy the adventure, before it...implodes. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Produced by Molly Webster and Annie McEwen. Reported by Molly Webster. Guest sound designer, Jeremy Bloom.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to </em><em>Kullervo Hynynen, </em><em>James Bird, and </em><em>Lawrence Crum. </em></p>\n<p>After you listen to the episode (spoiler alerts):</p>\n<p>Wanna see the shrimp bubble in super slowmo? Check it out<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Zg10Et8FEWc\"> here</a> (and note, of the 1,400 views on this video, producer Molly Webster probably comprises 752).<span><span></span></span></p>\n<p>If you want to see cavitation bubbles form, and think you might enjoy watching it happen in French, <a href=\"https://youtu.be/RR6J-yOyT48?t=3m44s\">check this out</a> - the high frame rate makes these shots divine. </p>\n<p><strong>Bigger Better Bubbles </strong></p>\n<p>Before Dave Stein, soap bubbles were round, smallish, and collapsed with a pop. Now, they are anything but. </p>\n<p>Today we explore the story of one man, who - in an instant, changed the art of bubble blowing and what it means to be a bubble forever. </p>\n<p><em>Produced by Simon Adler</em></p>\n<div><em>Special thanks to Megan Colby Parker, Gary Pearlman, David Erk, Rick Findley and everyone who came out to blow giant bubbles with us in Brooklyn's Prospect Park. </em></div>\n<p><em>You can hear <a href=\"https://soundcloud.com/jadabumrad/bubble-dance-party\">Jad's bubble dance party song here</a></em></p>\n<p><strong><span><br></span><span></span></strong></p>\n\n<p> </p>\n\n<p><strong><span><br></span><span><br></span><span></span></strong></p>\n\n\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Today's story is a mystery, shockingly hot, and vanishingly tiny.", "publishTime"=>1462850520000, "publish-at"=>"2016-05-09T23:22:00", "date-line-ts"=>1462849200, "estimated-duration"=>2225, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/bb97965994_2.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://libraries.ucsd.edu/collections/sca/", "name"=>"UC San Diego Library"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/768/690/l/80/1/bb97965994_2.jpg", "h"=>690, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"University of California Division of War Research illustration of underwater sounds, including snapping shrimp, interfering with listening with an underwater acoustic listening device. 1944", "credits-url"=>"http://library.ucsd.edu/dc/object/bb97965994", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/bb97965994_2.jpg", "w"=>768, "id"=>152798, "credits-name"=>"Scripps Institution of Oceanography Photographs "}, "objectID"=>"603688", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Bigger Than Bacon", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Today's story is a mystery, shockingly hot, and vanishingly tiny. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>It starts with a sound, rising like a mist from the marsh, around a dock in South Carolina. But where it goes next - from submarines to superheroes (and yes, Keanu Reeves!); from the surface of the sun to the middle of the brain - is far from expected. Producer Molly Webster brings her family along for the ride. Enjoy the adventure, before it...implodes. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Produced by Molly Webster and Annie McEwen. Reported by Molly Webster. Guest sound designer, Jeremy Bloom.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to </em><em>Kullervo Hynynen, </em><em>James Bird, and </em><em>Lawrence Crum. </em></p>\n<p>After you listen to the episode (spoiler alerts):</p>\n<p>Wanna see the shrimp bubble in super slowmo? Check it out<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Zg10Et8FEWc\"> here</a> (and note, of the 1,400 views on this video, producer Molly Webster probably comprises 752).<span><span></span></span></p>\n<p>If you want to see cavitation bubbles form, and think you might enjoy watching it happen in French, <a href=\"https://youtu.be/RR6J-yOyT48?t=3m44s\">check this out</a> - the high frame rate makes these shots divine. </p>\n<p><strong>Bigger Better Bubbles </strong></p>\n<p>Before Dave Stein, soap bubbles were round, smallish, and collapsed with a pop. Now, they are anything but. </p>\n<p>Today we explore the story of one man, who - in an instant, changed the art of bubble blowing and what it means to be a bubble forever. </p>\n<p><em>Produced by Simon Adler</em></p>\n<div><em>Special thanks to Megan Colby Parker, Gary Pearlman, David Erk, Rick Findley and everyone who came out to blow giant bubbles with us in Brooklyn's Prospect Park. </em></div>\n<p><em>You can hear <a href=\"https://soundcloud.com/jadabumrad/bubble-dance-party\">Jad's bubble dance party song here</a></em></p>\n<p><strong><span><br></span><span></span></strong></p>\n\n<p> </p>\n\n<p><strong><span><br></span><span><br></span><span></span></strong></p>\n\n\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-05-09T23:22:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"edge", "title"=>"On the Edge", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16ontheedge.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=597959", "description"=>"<p>At the 1998 Olympics in Nagano, Japan, one athlete pulled a move that, so far as we know, no one else had ever done in all of human history.</p>\n<p>Surya Bonaly was not your typical figure skater.  She was black. She was athletic. And she didn’t seem to care about artistry.  Her performances – punctuated by triple-triple jumps and other power moves – thrilled audiences around the world.  Yet, commentators claimed she couldn’t skate, and judges never gave her the high marks she felt she deserved.  But Surya didn’t accept that criticism.  Unlike her competitors – ice princesses who hid behind demure smiles – Surya made her feelings known.  And, at her final Olympic performance, she attempted one jump that flew in the face of the establishment, and marked her for life as a rebel. </p>\n<p>This week, we lace up our skates and tell a story about loving a sport that doesn’t love you back, and being judged in front of the world according to rules you don’t understand. </p>\n<p><em>Produced by Matt Kielty with help from Tracie Hunte. Reported by Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to the Sky Rink at Chelsea Piers, the Schwan Super Rink, Richmond Training Center, Simon Bowers of Bowers Audio Service, Vanessa Gusmeroli, Phil Hersh, Allison Manley, Randy Harvey, Rob Bailey and Lynn Plage, Michael Rosenberg, and Linda Lewis</em></p>\n<p>If you heard \"On the Edge\" and you're looking to fall in love with figure skating all over again, start here: <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/here-are-skating-routines-we-cant-stop-watching/\">http://www.radiolab.org/story/here-are-skating-routines-we-cant-stop-watching/</a></p>\n<p>You can take the survey we mentioned at the beginning of this episode here: <a href=\"https://www.research.net/r/wnyclistener\" target=\"_blank\"><span>https://www.research.net/r/wnyclistener</span></a><span> </span><span> </span>Thank you!</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"This week, we lace up our skates and tell a story about loving a sport that doesn&rsquo;t love you back, and being judged in front of the world according to rules you don&rsquo;t understand.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1461286800000, "publish-at"=>"2016-04-21T21:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1461286800, "estimated-duration"=>2579, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/GettyImages-51542600.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Getty"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/828/c/80/1/GettyImages-51542600.jpg", "h"=>828, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Surya Bonaly", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/GettyImages-51542600.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>152009, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Getty Images"}, "objectID"=>"597959", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"On the Edge", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>At the 1998 Olympics in Nagano, Japan, one athlete pulled a move that, so far as we know, no one else had ever done in all of human history.</p>\n<p>Surya Bonaly was not your typical figure skater.  She was black. She was athletic. And she didn’t seem to care about artistry.  Her performances – punctuated by triple-triple jumps and other power moves – thrilled audiences around the world.  Yet, commentators claimed she couldn’t skate, and judges never gave her the high marks she felt she deserved.  But Surya didn’t accept that criticism.  Unlike her competitors – ice princesses who hid behind demure smiles – Surya made her feelings known.  And, at her final Olympic performance, she attempted one jump that flew in the face of the establishment, and marked her for life as a rebel. </p>\n<p>This week, we lace up our skates and tell a story about loving a sport that doesn’t love you back, and being judged in front of the world according to rules you don’t understand. </p>\n<p><em>Produced by Matt Kielty with help from Tracie Hunte. Reported by Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to the Sky Rink at Chelsea Piers, the Schwan Super Rink, Richmond Training Center, Simon Bowers of Bowers Audio Service, Vanessa Gusmeroli, Phil Hersh, Allison Manley, Randy Harvey, Rob Bailey and Lynn Plage, Michael Rosenberg, and Linda Lewis</em></p>\n<p>If you heard \"On the Edge\" and you're looking to fall in love with figure skating all over again, start here: <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/here-are-skating-routines-we-cant-stop-watching/\">http://www.radiolab.org/story/here-are-skating-routines-we-cant-stop-watching/</a></p>\n<p>You can take the survey we mentioned at the beginning of this episode here: <a href=\"https://www.research.net/r/wnyclistener\" target=\"_blank\"><span>https://www.research.net/r/wnyclistener</span></a><span> </span><span> </span>Thank you!</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-04-21T21:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"cellmates", "title"=>"Cellmates", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16cellmates.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=593638", "description"=>"<p>There’s a black hole in the middle of the history of life: how did we go from tiny bags of chemicals to the vast menagerie of creatures we see around us? </p>\n<p>Today, we explore one of the most underrated mysteries of all time, and present one possible answer that takes us from an unexpected houseguest to a tiny bolt of lightning to every critter you hold dear. It’s the story of one cosmic oops moment that changed the game of life forever.  </p>\n<p><em>Production help from Matt Kielty and Annie McEwen. Reporting help from Latif Nasser. Special thanks to Eric Steinbrook, Scott Dawson, Ahna Skop &amp; Rachel Whittaker</em></p>\n", "tease"=>"There&rsquo;s a black hole in the middle of the history of life: how did we go from tiny bags of chemicals to the vast menagerie of creatures we see around us?&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1459901604000, "publish-at"=>"2016-04-05T20:13:24", "date-line-ts"=>1459922400, "estimated-duration"=>1806, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Tom_VanNortwick.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/533/h/80/1/Tom_VanNortwick.jpg", "h"=>533, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Tom_VanNortwick.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>151440, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Tom VanNortwick"}, "objectID"=>"593638", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Cellmates", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>There’s a black hole in the middle of the history of life: how did we go from tiny bags of chemicals to the vast menagerie of creatures we see around us? </p>\n<p>Today, we explore one of the most underrated mysteries of all time, and present one possible answer that takes us from an unexpected houseguest to a tiny bolt of lightning to every critter you hold dear. It’s the story of one cosmic oops moment that changed the game of life forever.  </p>\n<p><em>Production help from Matt Kielty and Annie McEwen. Reporting help from Latif Nasser. Special thanks to Eric Steinbrook, Scott Dawson, Ahna Skop &amp; Rachel Whittaker</em></p>\n", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-04-05T20:13:24", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"update-23-weeks-6-days", "title"=>"Update: 23 Weeks 6 Days", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16update23weeks.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=586996", "description"=>"<p>An update on Juniper French, a tiny baby, born at 23 Weeks and 6 days -- roughly halfway to full term. And a whole universe of medical and moral questions.</p>\n<p><span>Technology has had a profound effect on how we get pregnant, give birth, and think about life and death. The decision to become parents was not an easy one for Kelley and Tom. Even after they sorted out their relationship issues and hopes for the future, getting pregnant wasn't easy. But, thanks to a lot of technology, they found a way to a baby. Then, about halfway through the pregnancy, the trouble began. Neonatal nurse practitioner Diane Loisel describes helping Kelley and Tom make the most important decision of their lives. And Nita Farahany helps Jad and Robert understand the significance of viability, and how technology has influenced its meaning...making a difficult idea even harder to pin down.</span></p>\n<p><span>Kelley and Tom had hoped that meeting their daughter would be the happiest moment of their life. But when she came early -- at just 23 weeks and 6 days, that moment was full of terror and an impossibly difficult decision. And when the time came to face it, Tom and Kelley turned to their baby for help. Seeing their daughter for the first time, they looked for her to \"declare herself.\" That's a phrase that comes up again and again to help guide decisions in Neonatal Intensive Care Units. But parents and medical professionals have very different ideas about what the phrase really means. Nurse Tracy Hullet and Neonatologist Keith Barrington describe the difficulty of interpreting the fuzzy boundary between a baby's strength of will, and simple physiology. Meanwhile Kelley and Tom are left to wonder, and wait.</span></p>\n<p><span>The Neonatal Intensive Care Unit, or NICU, is a land of emotional and medical limbo. Kelley, Tom, and their daughter Juniper got stranded in this limbo for months, fighting to survive, and finally get to the next chapter of their lives. Their doctor, Fauzia Shakeel, describes the moment when Juniper's life hung in the balance, and Keith Barrington helps us understand how our newest technologies open the door not only to hope, but also to a pain that we, as humans, have kept hidden for most of our history.</span></p>\n<p>And finally, Kelley, Tom, Nita Farahany and Juniper herself, nearly 5 years old, give us an update on her life and what has happened since our story originally aired. </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/640/1136/l/80/1/IMG_8460.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Juniper and Kelley</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(Photo Credit: Kelley Benham)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"An update on Juniper French, a&nbsp;tiny baby, born at 23 Weeks and 6 days -- roughly halfway to full term. And a whole universe of medical and moral questions.", "publishTime"=>1458691147000, "publish-at"=>"2016-03-22T19:59:07", "date-line-ts"=>1458707760, "estimated-duration"=>3777, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Juniper French in the NICU", "name"=>"1/Kelley_juniper_4.15greathatphoto.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/851/c/80/1/Kelley_juniper_4.15greathatphoto.jpg", "h"=>851, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Juniper French in the NICU", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Kelley_juniper_4.15greathatphoto.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>96186, "credits-name"=>"Cherie Diez"}, "objectID"=>"586996", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Update: 23 Weeks 6 Days", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>An update on Juniper French, a tiny baby, born at 23 Weeks and 6 days -- roughly halfway to full term. And a whole universe of medical and moral questions.</p>\n<p><span>Technology has had a profound effect on how we get pregnant, give birth, and think about life and death. The decision to become parents was not an easy one for Kelley and Tom. Even after they sorted out their relationship issues and hopes for the future, getting pregnant wasn't easy. But, thanks to a lot of technology, they found a way to a baby. Then, about halfway through the pregnancy, the trouble began. Neonatal nurse practitioner Diane Loisel describes helping Kelley and Tom make the most important decision of their lives. And Nita Farahany helps Jad and Robert understand the significance of viability, and how technology has influenced its meaning...making a difficult idea even harder to pin down.</span></p>\n<p><span>Kelley and Tom had hoped that meeting their daughter would be the happiest moment of their life. But when she came early -- at just 23 weeks and 6 days, that moment was full of terror and an impossibly difficult decision. And when the time came to face it, Tom and Kelley turned to their baby for help. Seeing their daughter for the first time, they looked for her to \"declare herself.\" That's a phrase that comes up again and again to help guide decisions in Neonatal Intensive Care Units. But parents and medical professionals have very different ideas about what the phrase really means. Nurse Tracy Hullet and Neonatologist Keith Barrington describe the difficulty of interpreting the fuzzy boundary between a baby's strength of will, and simple physiology. Meanwhile Kelley and Tom are left to wonder, and wait.</span></p>\n<p><span>The Neonatal Intensive Care Unit, or NICU, is a land of emotional and medical limbo. Kelley, Tom, and their daughter Juniper got stranded in this limbo for months, fighting to survive, and finally get to the next chapter of their lives. Their doctor, Fauzia Shakeel, describes the moment when Juniper's life hung in the balance, and Keith Barrington helps us understand how our newest technologies open the door not only to hope, but also to a pain that we, as humans, have kept hidden for most of our history.</span></p>\n<p>And finally, Kelley, Tom, Nita Farahany and Juniper herself, nearly 5 years old, give us an update on her life and what has happened since our story originally aired. </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media2.wnyc.org/i/640/1136/l/80/1/IMG_8460.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Juniper and Kelley</div>\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(Photo Credit: Kelley Benham)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-03-22T19:59:07", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"debatable", "title"=>"Debatable", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16debatable.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=583435", "description"=>"<p><span>Unclasp your briefcase. It’s time for a showdown. </span><br><br><span>In competitive debate future presidents, supreme court justices, and titans of industry pummel each other with logic and rhetoric. </span><br><span> </span><br><span>But a couple years ago Ryan Wash, a queer, Black, first-generation college student from Kansas City, Missouri joined the debate team at Emporia State University. When he started going up against fast-talking, well-funded, “name-brand” teams, it was clear he wasn’t in Kansas anymore. So Ryan became the vanguard of a movement that made everything about debate debatable. In the end, he made himself a home in a strange and hostile land. Whether he was able to change what counts as rigorous academic argument … well, that’s still up for debate.</span></p>\n<p><em>Produced by Matt Kielty. Reported by Abigail Keel</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Will Baker, Myra Milam, John Dellamore, Sam Mauer, Tiffany Dillard Knox, Mary Mudd, Darren \"Chief\" Elliot, Jodee Hobbs, Rashad Evans and Luke Hill. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks also to Torgeir Kinne Solsvik for use of the song <span>h-lydisk / B Lydian from the album <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B007IVZUEU/ref=dm_ws_sp_ps_dp\" target=\"_blank\"><span>Geirr Tveitt Piano Works and Songs</span></a></span></em></p>\n", "tease"=>"How an outsider&nbsp;became the vanguard of a movement that made everything about debate debatable.", "publishTime"=>1457665245000, "publish-at"=>"2016-03-10T22:00:45", "date-line-ts"=>1457733600, "estimated-duration"=>3458, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/debate_matthew_montgomery.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/c/80/1/debate_matthew_montgomery.jpg", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/debate_matthew_montgomery.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>150449, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Matthew Montgomery"}, "objectID"=>"583435", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Debatable", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Unclasp your briefcase. It’s time for a showdown. </span><br><br><span>In competitive debate future presidents, supreme court justices, and titans of industry pummel each other with logic and rhetoric. </span><br><span> </span><br><span>But a couple years ago Ryan Wash, a queer, Black, first-generation college student from Kansas City, Missouri joined the debate team at Emporia State University. When he started going up against fast-talking, well-funded, “name-brand” teams, it was clear he wasn’t in Kansas anymore. So Ryan became the vanguard of a movement that made everything about debate debatable. In the end, he made himself a home in a strange and hostile land. Whether he was able to change what counts as rigorous academic argument … well, that’s still up for debate.</span></p>\n<p><em>Produced by Matt Kielty. Reported by Abigail Keel</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Will Baker, Myra Milam, John Dellamore, Sam Mauer, Tiffany Dillard Knox, Mary Mudd, Darren \"Chief\" Elliot, Jodee Hobbs, Rashad Evans and Luke Hill. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks also to Torgeir Kinne Solsvik for use of the song <span>h-lydisk / B Lydian from the album <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B007IVZUEU/ref=dm_ws_sp_ps_dp\" target=\"_blank\"><span>Geirr Tveitt Piano Works and Songs</span></a></span></em></p>\n", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-03-10T22:00:45", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"kpoparazzi", "title"=>"K-poparazzi", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16kpoperazzi.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=578186", "description"=>"<p>In the U.S., paparazzi are pretty much synonymous with invasion of privacy. But today we travel to a place where the prying press create something more like a prison break. </p>\n<p>K-pop is a global juggernaut - with billions in sales and millions of fans hanging on every note, watching K-pop idols synchronize and strut. And that fame rests on a fantasy, K-pop stars have to be chaste and pure, but also … available. Until recently, Korean music agencies and K-pop fans held their pop stars to a strict set of rules designed to keep that fantasy alive. That is, until Dispatch showed up.</p>\n<p>Taking a cue from American and British paparazzi, a group of South Korean reporters started hiding in their cars and snapping photos of stars on their secret dates. The first-ever paparazzi photos turned the world of K-pop upside down and introduced sort of a puzzle … how much do you want to know about the people you idolize, and when is enough enough?</p>\n<p><em>Produced by Matthew Kielty and Alexandra Young. Reported by Alexandra Young with Brenna Farrell.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special Thanks to Dispatch, Haeryun Kang, Joseph Kim, Charlie Cho, Hyena, Crayon Pop, Jeremy Bloom, The Kirukkiruk Guesthouse, Choi Baekseol</span><span>, Jiin Choi, David Bevan, and The One Shots. </span></em></p>\n<p>And if, like us, this story leaves you with an insatiable desire to listen to K-pop here is a starter list of our recommendations: </p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"380\" src=\"https://embed.spotify.com/?uri=spotify:user:radiolab:playlist:7yKAfkmsJbiDVmZ0f0ZVXl\" width=\"300\"></iframe></p>", "tease"=>"In the U.S., paparazzi are pretty much synonymous with invasion of privacy. But today we travel to a place where the prying press create something more like a prison break.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1456269444000, "publish-at"=>"2016-02-23T18:17:24", "date-line-ts"=>1456300800, "estimated-duration"=>2243, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/tumblr_laxt5jSZYh1qcl8qx.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/500/375/c/80/1/tumblr_laxt5jSZYh1qcl8qx.jpg", "h"=>375, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/tumblr_laxt5jSZYh1qcl8qx.jpg", "w"=>500, "id"=>149776, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Sports Seoul"}, "objectID"=>"578186", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"K-poparazzi", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In the U.S., paparazzi are pretty much synonymous with invasion of privacy. But today we travel to a place where the prying press create something more like a prison break. </p>\n<p>K-pop is a global juggernaut - with billions in sales and millions of fans hanging on every note, watching K-pop idols synchronize and strut. And that fame rests on a fantasy, K-pop stars have to be chaste and pure, but also … available. Until recently, Korean music agencies and K-pop fans held their pop stars to a strict set of rules designed to keep that fantasy alive. That is, until Dispatch showed up.</p>\n<p>Taking a cue from American and British paparazzi, a group of South Korean reporters started hiding in their cars and snapping photos of stars on their secret dates. The first-ever paparazzi photos turned the world of K-pop upside down and introduced sort of a puzzle … how much do you want to know about the people you idolize, and when is enough enough?</p>\n<p><em>Produced by Matthew Kielty and Alexandra Young. Reported by Alexandra Young with Brenna Farrell.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special Thanks to Dispatch, Haeryun Kang, Joseph Kim, Charlie Cho, Hyena, Crayon Pop, Jeremy Bloom, The Kirukkiruk Guesthouse, Choi Baekseol</span><span>, Jiin Choi, David Bevan, and The One Shots. </span></em></p>\n<p>And if, like us, this story leaves you with an insatiable desire to listen to K-pop here is a starter list of our recommendations: </p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"380\" src=\"https://embed.spotify.com/?uri=spotify:user:radiolab:playlist:7yKAfkmsJbiDVmZ0f0ZVXl\" width=\"300\"></iframe></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-02-23T18:17:24", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"hard-knock-life", "title"=>"Hard Knock Life", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16hardknocklife.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=574879", "description"=>"<p><span>This Valentine's Day, a mysterious tap tap tapping leads us into a world of sex, death, and head-banging. </span></p>\n<p>Biologist Dave Goulson introduces us to the lonely yearnings of an especially pathetic beetle and snatches a sound back from the hands of the devil himself.  Featuring rapping about rapping from extra special guests Lin-Manuel Miranda, Utkarsh Ambudkar and Freestyle Love Supreme. </p>\n<p><em>Produced by Simon Adler. We had engineering help from Rick Kwan.</em></p>", "tease"=>"This Valentine's Day, a mysterious tap tap tapping leads us into a world of sex, death, and head-banging.", "publishTime"=>1455320824000, "publish-at"=>"2016-02-12T18:47:04", "date-line-ts"=>1455320824, "estimated-duration"=>1156, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Deathbeetle_2260048k.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/858/536/h/80/1/Deathbeetle_2260048k.jpg", "h"=>536, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Deathbeetle_2260048k.jpg", "w"=>858, "id"=>149442, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Getty Images"}, "objectID"=>"574879", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Hard Knock Life", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This Valentine's Day, a mysterious tap tap tapping leads us into a world of sex, death, and head-banging. </span></p>\n<p>Biologist Dave Goulson introduces us to the lonely yearnings of an especially pathetic beetle and snatches a sound back from the hands of the devil himself.  Featuring rapping about rapping from extra special guests Lin-Manuel Miranda, Utkarsh Ambudkar and Freestyle Love Supreme. </p>\n<p><em>Produced by Simon Adler. We had engineering help from Rick Kwan.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-02-12T18:47:04", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"i-dont-have-answer", "title"=>"I Don't Have To Answer That ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast16dontanswer.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=570521", "description"=>"<p>Roosevelt, Kennedy, Eisenhower … they all got a pass. But today we peer back at the moment when poking into the private lives of political figures became standard practice.</p>\n<p>In 1987, Gary Hart was a young charismatic Democrat, poised to win his party’s nomination and possibly the presidency. Many of us know the story of what happened next, and even if you don’t, it’s a familiar tale. But at the time, politicians and political reporters found themselves in uncharted territory. With help from author Matt Bai, we look at how the events of that May shaped the way we cover politics, and expanded our sense of what's appropriate when it comes to judging a candidate.  </p>\n<p><em>Produced by Simon Adler</em></p>\n<p><em>Special Thanks to Joe Trippi</em></p>", "tease"=>"Roosevelt, Kennedy, Eisenhower &hellip; they all got a pass. But today we peer back at the moment when poking into the private lives of political figures became standard practice.", "publishTime"=>1454126400000, "publish-at"=>"2016-01-29T23:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1454126400, "estimated-duration"=>2078, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Hart.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Getty"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/594/393/c/80/1/Hart.jpg", "h"=>393, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Hart.jpg", "w"=>594, "id"=>148875, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Getty Images"}, "objectID"=>"570521", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"I Don't Have To Answer That ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Roosevelt, Kennedy, Eisenhower … they all got a pass. But today we peer back at the moment when poking into the private lives of political figures became standard practice.</p>\n<p>In 1987, Gary Hart was a young charismatic Democrat, poised to win his party’s nomination and possibly the presidency. Many of us know the story of what happened next, and even if you don’t, it’s a familiar tale. But at the time, politicians and political reporters found themselves in uncharted territory. With help from author Matt Bai, we look at how the events of that May shaped the way we cover politics, and expanded our sense of what's appropriate when it comes to judging a candidate.  </p>\n<p><em>Produced by Simon Adler</em></p>\n<p><em>Special Thanks to Joe Trippi</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2016-01-29T23:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2017=>[{"slug"=>"inside-radiolab", "title"=>"Inside Radiolab (Video)", "audio"=>[], "description"=>"<p>Take a stroll through where Radiolab is made and meet some of the people who have created your favorite episodes.</p>\n<p>Help make another year of curiosity possible. <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Radiolab.org/Support\">Radiolab.org/support</a></p>\n<p>If you're having trouble watching the video you can view it by clicking <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TOy2taTTtG0&amp;t=1s\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"video alt\">here</a>. </p>", "tease"=>"Meet our team and take a look behind the scenes here at Radiolab. Help make another year of curiosity possible.&nbsp;Radiolab.org/support", "publishTime"=>1514588400000, "publish-at"=>"2017-12-29T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1514523600, "estimated-duration"=>0, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Radiolab_YearEnd_Image.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1045/l/80/1/Radiolab_YearEnd_Image.png", "h"=>1045, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Meet our team and take a look behind the scenes here at Radiolab.", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Radiolab_YearEnd_Image.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>171543, "credits-name"=>"Billy Graves & Caroline English"}, "objectID"=>"822645", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Inside Radiolab (Video)", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Take a stroll through where Radiolab is made and meet some of the people who have created your favorite episodes.</p>\n<p>Help make another year of curiosity possible. <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Radiolab.org/Support\">Radiolab.org/support</a></p>\n<p>If you're having trouble watching the video you can view it by clicking <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TOy2taTTtG0&amp;t=1s\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"video alt\">here</a>. </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-12-29T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"bigger-little-questions", "title"=>"Bigger Little Questions", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18biggerlittlequestions.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=821639", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>We're back with Part 2! When we dumped out our bucket of questions, there was a lot of spillover. Like, A LOT of spillover. So today, we’re chasing down answers to some bigger, little questions.  </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>This episode was reported and produced by Annie McEwen, Bethel Habte, Latif Nasser, Matt Kielty, Simon Adler and Tracie Hunte.</em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks to Stephen Brady and Staff Sergeant Erica Picariello in the US Air Force's 21st Space Wing.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Today, we're back with Part 2 of our questions episodes. This time, we're chasing down answers to&nbsp;some bigger, little questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1513933140000, "publish-at"=>"2017-12-22T03:59:00", "date-line-ts"=>1513933140, "estimated-duration"=>3351, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Unknown_By15E1u.jpeg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/819/c/80/1/Unknown_By15E1u.jpeg", "h"=>819, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.flickr.com/photos/zeevveez/8022482779/in/photolist-ddVjCB-rsQ6-RqoiAV-RwrzYU-SW6CsX-SCCKrc-SKGfGH-RJidXW-3Hy59P-T3A6MC-SasG5V-RMtapr-zh7vG-RC7Fqz-TED1WK-5koRnU-SYWQxc-6Ax7wx-cGk1yS-8X7nVL-", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Unknown_By15E1u.jpeg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>171485, "credits-name"=>"zeevveez"}, "objectID"=>"821639", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Bigger Little Questions", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>We're back with Part 2! When we dumped out our bucket of questions, there was a lot of spillover. Like, A LOT of spillover. So today, we’re chasing down answers to some bigger, little questions.  </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>This episode was reported and produced by Annie McEwen, Bethel Habte, Latif Nasser, Matt Kielty, Simon Adler and Tracie Hunte.</em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks to Stephen Brady and Staff Sergeant Erica Picariello in the US Air Force's 21st Space Wing.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-12-22T03:59:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"big-little-questions", "title"=>"Big Little Questions ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17biglittlequestions.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=821080", "description"=>"<p>Here at the show, we get a lot of questions. Like, A LOT of questions. Tiny questions, big questions, short questions, long questions. Weird questions. Poop questions. We get them all.</p>\n<p>And over the years, as more and more of these questions arrived in our inbox, what happened was, guiltily, we put them off to the side, in a bucket of sorts, where they just sat around, unanswered. But now, we’re dumping the bucket out.</p>\n<p>Today, our producers pick up a few of the questions that spilled out of that bucket, and venture out into the great unknown to find answers to some of life's greatest mysteries: coincidences; miracles; life; death; fate; will; and, of course, poop.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Rachael Cusick, Tracie Hunte and Matt Kielty. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Blake Nguyen, Sarah Murphy and the New York Public Library. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Here at the show, we get A LOT of questions, tiny questions, big questions, weird questions, poop questions.&nbsp;Today, we&rsquo;re dumping the bucket out.", "publishTime"=>1513757520000, "publish-at"=>"2017-12-20T03:12:00", "date-line-ts"=>1513757520, "estimated-duration"=>2767, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/BlueSignQuestion.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1740/1860/l/80/1/BlueSignQuestion.jpg", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.flickr.com/photos/armydre2008/3415206747/in/photolist-s29nxg-RLqyyB-atXxSw-TySfWo-bjqDsc-Trg6yy-cPvNh-RQiJAm-QVrSzD-214n8gV-JB65mG-REiixL-8G8h9m-Ts2jea-6v7oBz-q12zJQ-UdXunp-Ry6xFS-5GLFEo-q", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/BlueSignQuestion.jpg", "w"=>1740, "id"=>171393, "credits-name"=>"frankieleon"}, "objectID"=>"821080", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Big Little Questions ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Here at the show, we get a lot of questions. Like, A LOT of questions. Tiny questions, big questions, short questions, long questions. Weird questions. Poop questions. We get them all.</p>\n<p>And over the years, as more and more of these questions arrived in our inbox, what happened was, guiltily, we put them off to the side, in a bucket of sorts, where they just sat around, unanswered. But now, we’re dumping the bucket out.</p>\n<p>Today, our producers pick up a few of the questions that spilled out of that bucket, and venture out into the great unknown to find answers to some of life's greatest mysteries: coincidences; miracles; life; death; fate; will; and, of course, poop.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Rachael Cusick, Tracie Hunte and Matt Kielty. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Blake Nguyen, Sarah Murphy and the New York Public Library. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-12-20T03:12:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"super-cool-2017", "title"=>"Super Cool", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17supercool.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=816935", "description"=>"<p>When we started reporting a fantastic, surreal story about one very cold night, more than 70 years ago, in northern Russia, we had no idea we'd end up thinking about cosmology. Or dropping toy horses in test tubes of water. Or talking about bacteria. Or arguing, for a year. Walter Murch (aka, the Godfather of <em>The Godfather</em>), joined by a team of scientists, leads us on what felt like the magical mystery tour of super cool science.</p>\n<p>Our supercooling demonstration (with a tiny horse):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>For more video of our trip to the lab, check out:</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/jad-grows-ice-with-one-finger\" target=\"_blank\">Jad grows ice, with one finger (sorta)</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/flash-freeze-high-def/\" target=\"_blank\">A flash freezing, in high-def</a></p>\n<p>And it turns out, our podcast <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/radiolab-physicists-on-the-same-wavelength/\" target=\"_blank\">has something to do with this pret-ty big physics discovery</a>, about possibly one of the earliest supercooling events in the universe, moments after the Big Bang.</p>\n<p><em>This piece was produced by Molly Webster and Matt Kielty with help from Amanda Aronczyk. </em><em> It originally aired in March of 2014.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"&nbsp;Walter Murch (aka, the Godfather of&nbsp;The Godfather), joined by a team of scientists, leads us on what felt like the magical mystery tour of super cool science.", "publishTime"=>1512515520000, "publish-at"=>"2017-12-05T18:12:00", "date-line-ts"=>1512515520, "estimated-duration"=>1524, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/icefox_38tE8PB.JPG", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/1125/l/80/1/icefox_38tE8PB.JPG", "h"=>1125, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/icefox_38tE8PB.JPG", "w"=>1500, "id"=>170953, "credits-name"=>"Jeffer Sandstrom"}, "objectID"=>"816935", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Super Cool", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>When we started reporting a fantastic, surreal story about one very cold night, more than 70 years ago, in northern Russia, we had no idea we'd end up thinking about cosmology. Or dropping toy horses in test tubes of water. Or talking about bacteria. Or arguing, for a year. Walter Murch (aka, the Godfather of <em>The Godfather</em>), joined by a team of scientists, leads us on what felt like the magical mystery tour of super cool science.</p>\n<p>Our supercooling demonstration (with a tiny horse):</p>\n<p></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>For more video of our trip to the lab, check out:</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/jad-grows-ice-with-one-finger\" target=\"_blank\">Jad grows ice, with one finger (sorta)</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/flash-freeze-high-def/\" target=\"_blank\">A flash freezing, in high-def</a></p>\n<p>And it turns out, our podcast <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/radiolab-physicists-on-the-same-wavelength/\" target=\"_blank\">has something to do with this pret-ty big physics discovery</a>, about possibly one of the earliest supercooling events in the universe, moments after the Big Bang.</p>\n<p><em>This piece was produced by Molly Webster and Matt Kielty with help from Amanda Aronczyk. </em><em> It originally aired in March of 2014.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-12-05T18:12:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-presents-more-perfect-mr-graham-reasonable-man", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - Mr. Graham and the Reasonable Man", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17mrgrahammp.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=815654", "description"=>"<p><span><em>This story comes from the second season of Radiolab's spin-off podcast, More Perfect. To hear more, subscribe <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolabmoreperfect/\">here</a>.</em></span></p>\n<p><span>On a fall afternoon in 1984, Dethorne Graham ran into a convenience store for a bottle of orange juice. Minutes later he was unconscious, injured, and in police handcuffs. In this episode, we explore a case that sent two Charlotte lawyers on a quest for true objectivity, and changed the face of policing in the US.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The key voices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dethorne Graham Jr., son of Dethorne Graham, appellant in <em>Graham v. Connor</em></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.essexrichards.com/attorneys/edward-g-woody-connette/\">Edward G. (Woody) Connette</a>, lawyer who represented Graham in the lower courts</li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.beavercourie.com/lawyers/h-gerald-beaver-partner/\">Gerald Beaver</a>, lawyer who represented Graham at the Supreme Court</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.npr.org/people/131876588/kelly-mcevers\">Kelly McEvers</a>, host of <em>Embedded</em> and <em>All Things Considered</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><span> </span></span>The key case:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>1989: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1988/87-6571\">Graham v. Connor</a></em></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><em>Additional production for this episode by Dylan Keefe and Derek John; additional music by Matt Kielty and Nicolas Carter.</em></span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Cynthia Lee, Frank B. Aycock III, Josh Rosenkrantz, </em><em>Leonard Feldman, and Ben Montgomery.</em></p>\n<p><em>Leadership support for More Perfect is provided by The Joyce Foundation. Additional funding is provided by The Charles Evans Hughes Memorial Foundation.</em></p>\n<p><em>Supreme Court archival audio comes from </em><a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\"><em>Oyez®</em></a><em>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</em></p>", "tease"=>"How a diabetic, a cop, and a bottle of orange juice changed the way we police, forever.", "publishTime"=>1512031260000, "publish-at"=>"2017-11-30T03:41:00", "date-line-ts"=>1512031260, "estimated-duration"=>4095, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/badge-v03.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1860/c/80/1/badge-v03.jpg", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://mitchboyer.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/badge-v03.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>170805, "credits-name"=>"Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"815654", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - Mr. Graham and the Reasonable Man", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span><em>This story comes from the second season of Radiolab's spin-off podcast, More Perfect. To hear more, subscribe <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolabmoreperfect/\">here</a>.</em></span></p>\n<p><span>On a fall afternoon in 1984, Dethorne Graham ran into a convenience store for a bottle of orange juice. Minutes later he was unconscious, injured, and in police handcuffs. In this episode, we explore a case that sent two Charlotte lawyers on a quest for true objectivity, and changed the face of policing in the US.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>The key voices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dethorne Graham Jr., son of Dethorne Graham, appellant in <em>Graham v. Connor</em></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.essexrichards.com/attorneys/edward-g-woody-connette/\">Edward G. (Woody) Connette</a>, lawyer who represented Graham in the lower courts</li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.beavercourie.com/lawyers/h-gerald-beaver-partner/\">Gerald Beaver</a>, lawyer who represented Graham at the Supreme Court</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.npr.org/people/131876588/kelly-mcevers\">Kelly McEvers</a>, host of <em>Embedded</em> and <em>All Things Considered</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><span> </span></span>The key case:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>1989: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1988/87-6571\">Graham v. Connor</a></em></li>\n</ul>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><em>Additional production for this episode by Dylan Keefe and Derek John; additional music by Matt Kielty and Nicolas Carter.</em></span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Cynthia Lee, Frank B. Aycock III, Josh Rosenkrantz, </em><em>Leonard Feldman, and Ben Montgomery.</em></p>\n<p><em>Leadership support for More Perfect is provided by The Joyce Foundation. Additional funding is provided by The Charles Evans Hughes Memorial Foundation.</em></p>\n<p><em>Supreme Court archival audio comes from </em><a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\"><em>Oyez®</em></a><em>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-11-30T03:41:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"stereothreat", "title"=>"Stereothreat", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17stereothreat.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=814334", "description"=>"<p>Back in 1995, Claude Steele published a study that showed that negative stereotypes could have a detrimental effect on students' academic performance. But the big surprise was that he could make that effect disappear with just a few simple changes in language. We were completely enamoured with this research when we first heard about it, but in the current roil of replications and self-examination in the field of social psychology, we have to wonder whether we can still cling to the hopes of our earlier selves, or if we might have to grow up just a little bit.</p>\n<p><em>This piece was produced by Simon Adler and Amanda Aronczyk and reported by Dan Engber and Amanda Aronczyk.</em></p>\n<p> <em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Today, we look back at&nbsp;a story&nbsp;we ran almost a decade ago,&nbsp;run into&nbsp;an unfolding upheaval in the field of social psychology, and wonder what might happen to one of our favorite ideas.", "publishTime"=>1511478000000, "publish-at"=>"2017-11-23T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1511478000, "estimated-duration"=>2206, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Replication-monochrome-Jagel.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1567/l/80/1/Replication-monochrome-Jagel.jpg", "h"=>1567, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.jasonjagel.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Replication-monochrome-Jagel.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>170639, "credits-name"=>"Jason Jagel"}, "objectID"=>"814334", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Stereothreat", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Back in 1995, Claude Steele published a study that showed that negative stereotypes could have a detrimental effect on students' academic performance. But the big surprise was that he could make that effect disappear with just a few simple changes in language. We were completely enamoured with this research when we first heard about it, but in the current roil of replications and self-examination in the field of social psychology, we have to wonder whether we can still cling to the hopes of our earlier selves, or if we might have to grow up just a little bit.</p>\n<p><em>This piece was produced by Simon Adler and Amanda Aronczyk and reported by Dan Engber and Amanda Aronczyk.</em></p>\n<p> <em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-11-23T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"match-made-in-marrow", "title"=>"Match Made in Marrow", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17matchmadeinmarrow.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=811385", "description"=>"<p>You never know what might happen when you sign up to donate bone marrow. You might save a life… or you might be magically transported across a cultural chasm and find yourself starring in a modern adaptation of the greatest story ever told.</p>\n<p>One day, without thinking much of it, Jennell Jenney swabbed her cheek and signed up to be a donor.  Across the country, Jim Munroe desperately needed a miracle, a one-in-eight-million connection that would save him. It proved to be a match made in marrow, a bit of magic in the world that hadn’t been there before.  But when Jennell and Jim had a heart-to-heart in his suburban Dallas backyard, they realized they had contradictory ideas about where that magic came from. Today, an allegory for how to walk through the world in a way that lets you be deeply different, but totally together. </p>\n<p><em>This piece was reported by Latif Nasser.  It was produced by Annie McEwen, with help from Bethel Habte and Alex Overington.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to <span>Dr. Matthew J. Matasar, Dr. John Hill, Stephen Spellman at CIBMTR, St. Cloud State University’s Cru Chapter, and Mandy Naglich.</span></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Join Be The Match's bone marrow registry <a href=\"https://bethematch.org/support-the-cause/donate-bone-marrow/join-the-marrow-registry/\">here</a>.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"If you donate bone marrow, you might save a life&hellip; or you might land a starring role in the greatest story ever told.", "publishTime"=>1510280700000, "publish-at"=>"2017-11-09T21:25:00", "date-line-ts"=>1510280700, "estimated-duration"=>3653, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/image1_PipoPFl.JPG", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/1/image1_PipoPFl.JPG", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Jim Munroe and Jennell Jenney at the Majestic Theatre on May 7th 2017. ", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/image1_PipoPFl.JPG", "w"=>1860, "id"=>170196, "credits-name"=>"Brice Harney"}, "objectID"=>"811385", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Match Made in Marrow", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>You never know what might happen when you sign up to donate bone marrow. You might save a life… or you might be magically transported across a cultural chasm and find yourself starring in a modern adaptation of the greatest story ever told.</p>\n<p>One day, without thinking much of it, Jennell Jenney swabbed her cheek and signed up to be a donor.  Across the country, Jim Munroe desperately needed a miracle, a one-in-eight-million connection that would save him. It proved to be a match made in marrow, a bit of magic in the world that hadn’t been there before.  But when Jennell and Jim had a heart-to-heart in his suburban Dallas backyard, they realized they had contradictory ideas about where that magic came from. Today, an allegory for how to walk through the world in a way that lets you be deeply different, but totally together. </p>\n<p><em>This piece was reported by Latif Nasser.  It was produced by Annie McEwen, with help from Bethel Habte and Alex Overington.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to <span>Dr. Matthew J. Matasar, Dr. John Hill, Stephen Spellman at CIBMTR, St. Cloud State University’s Cru Chapter, and Mandy Naglich.</span></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Join Be The Match's bone marrow registry <a href=\"https://bethematch.org/support-the-cause/donate-bone-marrow/join-the-marrow-registry/\">here</a>.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-11-09T21:25:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"oliver-sacks-journey-where-to-where", "title"=>"Oliver Sacks: A Journey From Where to Where", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17oliversacksjourney.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=807863", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>There’s nothing quite like the sound of someone thinking out loud, struggling to find words and ideas to match what’s in their head. Today, we are allowed to dip into the unfiltered thoughts of Oliver Sacks, one of our heroes, in the last months of his life. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Oliver died in 2015, but before he passed he and his partner Bill Hayes, in an effort to preserve some of Oliver’s thoughts on his work and his life, bought a little tape recorder. Over a year and half after Oliver’s death, Bill dug up the recorder and turned it on. Through snippets of conversation with Bill, and in moments Oliver recorded whispering to himself as he wrote, we get a peek inside the head, and the life, of one of the greatest science essayists of all time.<br><span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>The passages read in this piece all come from Oliver’s recently released, posthumous book, <a href=\"https://www.oliversacks.com/books-by-oliver-sacks/the-river-of-consciousness/\">The River of Consciousness</a>. </em><br><span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Special thanks to Billy Hayes for letting us use Oliver’s tapes, you can check out his work at <a href=\"http://www.billhayes.com/\"><span>http://www.billhayes.com/</span></a><br><span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"> <br><span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><br><span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"> </p>", "tease"=>"There&rsquo;s nothing quite like the sound of someone thinking out loud, struggling to find words and ideas to match what&rsquo;s in their head.", "publishTime"=>1509077340000, "publish-at"=>"2017-10-27T00:09:00", "date-line-ts"=>1509077340, "estimated-duration"=>2240, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/OliverSacks.png", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Penguin Random House"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1267/1236/l/80/1/OliverSacks.png", "h"=>1236, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Oliver Sacks", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/OliverSacks.png", "w"=>1267, "id"=>169546, "credits-name"=>"Adam Scourfield"}, "objectID"=>"807863", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Oliver Sacks: A Journey From Where to Where", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>There’s nothing quite like the sound of someone thinking out loud, struggling to find words and ideas to match what’s in their head. Today, we are allowed to dip into the unfiltered thoughts of Oliver Sacks, one of our heroes, in the last months of his life. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Oliver died in 2015, but before he passed he and his partner Bill Hayes, in an effort to preserve some of Oliver’s thoughts on his work and his life, bought a little tape recorder. Over a year and half after Oliver’s death, Bill dug up the recorder and turned it on. Through snippets of conversation with Bill, and in moments Oliver recorded whispering to himself as he wrote, we get a peek inside the head, and the life, of one of the greatest science essayists of all time.<br><span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>The passages read in this piece all come from Oliver’s recently released, posthumous book, <a href=\"https://www.oliversacks.com/books-by-oliver-sacks/the-river-of-consciousness/\">The River of Consciousness</a>. </em><br><span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Special thanks to Billy Hayes for letting us use Oliver’s tapes, you can check out his work at <a href=\"http://www.billhayes.com/\"><span>http://www.billhayes.com/</span></a><br><span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"> <br><span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><br><span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-10-27T00:09:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"father-k", "title"=>"Father K", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17bayridge.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=804065", "description"=>"<p>Today, while the divisions between different groups in this country feel more and more insurmountable, we zero in on a particular neighborhood to see if one man can draw people together in a potentially history-making election. </p>\n<p>Khader El-Yateem is a Palestinian American running for office in Bay Ridge, Brooklyn, one of the most divided, and most conservative neighborhoods in New York City. To win, he'll need to convince a wildly diverse population that he can speak for all of them, and he'll need to pull one particular group of people, Arab American Muslims, out of the shadows and into the political process. And to make things just a bit more interesting, El-Yateem is a Lutheran minister.</p>\n<p><em>This story was reported and produced by Simon Adler, with help from Bethel Habte, Annie McEwen, and Sarah Qari.</em></p>\n<p> Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</p>\n", "tease"=>"What happens when a Palestinian American Lutheran Minister runs for office in one of the most divided and most conservative neighborhoods in all of New York City?", "publishTime"=>1507794660000, "publish-at"=>"2017-10-12T03:51:00", "date-line-ts"=>1507863555, "estimated-duration"=>4266, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/FullSizeRender-2.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1859/l/80/1/FullSizeRender-2.jpg", "h"=>1859, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/FullSizeRender-2.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>169247, "credits-name"=>"photo by Simon Adler"}, "objectID"=>"804065", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Father K", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Today, while the divisions between different groups in this country feel more and more insurmountable, we zero in on a particular neighborhood to see if one man can draw people together in a potentially history-making election. </p>\n<p>Khader El-Yateem is a Palestinian American running for office in Bay Ridge, Brooklyn, one of the most divided, and most conservative neighborhoods in New York City. To win, he'll need to convince a wildly diverse population that he can speak for all of them, and he'll need to pull one particular group of people, Arab American Muslims, out of the shadows and into the political process. And to make things just a bit more interesting, El-Yateem is a Lutheran minister.</p>\n<p><em>This story was reported and produced by Simon Adler, with help from Bethel Habte, Annie McEwen, and Sarah Qari.</em></p>\n<p> Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</p>\n", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-10-12T03:51:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-presents-more-perfect-american-pendulum-i", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - American Pendulum I", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17americanpendulummp.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=801346", "description"=>"<p><em>This story comes from the second season of Radiolab's spin-off podcast, More Perfect. To hear more, subscribe <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolabmoreperfect/\">here</a>.</em></p>\n<p>What happens when the Supreme Court, the highest court in the land, seems to get it wrong? <em>Korematsu v. United States </em>is a case that’s been widely denounced and discredited, but it still remains on the books. This is the case that upheld President Franklin Roosevelt’s internment of American citizens during World War II based solely on their Japanese heritage, for the sake of national security. In this episode, we follow Fred Korematsu’s path to the Supreme Court, and we ask the question: if you can’t get justice in the Supreme Court, can you find it someplace else?</p>\n<p><span><span> </span></span>The key voices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fred Korematsu, plaintiff in Korematsu v. United States who resisted evacuation orders during World War II.</li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.korematsuinstitute.org/karen-korematsu/\">Karen Korematsu</a>, Fred’s daughter, Founder &amp; Executive Director of Fred T. Korematsu Institute</li>\n<li>Ernest Besig, ACLU lawyer who helped Fred Korematsu bring his case</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://law.seattleu.edu/faculty/profiles/lorraine-bannai\">Lorraine Bannai</a>, Professor at Seattle University School of Law and friend of Fred's family</li>\n<li>Richard Posner, recently retired Circuit Judge for the U.S. Court of Appeals, 7th Circuit</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span><span> </span></span>The key cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1944: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1940-1955/323us214\">Korematsu v. United States</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span><span> </span></span>The key links:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.korematsuinstitute.org/fred-t-korematsu-lifetime/\">Fred T. Korematsu Institute</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://densho.org/archives/\">Densho Archives</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span><span><br><br></span></span></p>\n<p><em>Additional music for this episode by The Flamingos, Lulu, <a href=\"https://bridgerecords.com/pages/paul-lansky\">Paul Lansky</a> and <a href=\"http://austinvaughnmusic.com/\">Austin Vaughn</a>.</em></p>\n<p><span><span> </span></span><em>Special thanks to the Densho Archives for use of archival tape of Fred Korematsu and Ernest Besig.</em></p>\n<p><span><span> </span></span><em>Leadership support for More Perfect is provided by The Joyce Foundation. Additional funding is provided by The Charles Evans Hughes Memorial Foundation.</em></p>\n<p><span><em>Supreme Court archival audio comes from </em><a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\"><em>Oyez®</em></a><em>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</em></span></p>", "tease"=>"If you can&rsquo;t find justice in the Supreme Court, can you find it someplace else?", "publishTime"=>1506917460000, "publish-at"=>"2017-10-02T00:11:00", "date-line-ts"=>1506917400, "estimated-duration"=>3114, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/fred-v03.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1860/c/80/1/fred-v03.jpg", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://mitchboyer.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/fred-v03.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>168681, "credits-name"=>"Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"801346", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - American Pendulum I", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><em>This story comes from the second season of Radiolab's spin-off podcast, More Perfect. To hear more, subscribe <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/shows/radiolabmoreperfect/\">here</a>.</em></p>\n<p>What happens when the Supreme Court, the highest court in the land, seems to get it wrong? <em>Korematsu v. United States </em>is a case that’s been widely denounced and discredited, but it still remains on the books. This is the case that upheld President Franklin Roosevelt’s internment of American citizens during World War II based solely on their Japanese heritage, for the sake of national security. In this episode, we follow Fred Korematsu’s path to the Supreme Court, and we ask the question: if you can’t get justice in the Supreme Court, can you find it someplace else?</p>\n<p><span><span> </span></span>The key voices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fred Korematsu, plaintiff in Korematsu v. United States who resisted evacuation orders during World War II.</li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.korematsuinstitute.org/karen-korematsu/\">Karen Korematsu</a>, Fred’s daughter, Founder &amp; Executive Director of Fred T. Korematsu Institute</li>\n<li>Ernest Besig, ACLU lawyer who helped Fred Korematsu bring his case</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://law.seattleu.edu/faculty/profiles/lorraine-bannai\">Lorraine Bannai</a>, Professor at Seattle University School of Law and friend of Fred's family</li>\n<li>Richard Posner, recently retired Circuit Judge for the U.S. Court of Appeals, 7th Circuit</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span><span> </span></span>The key cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1944: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1940-1955/323us214\">Korematsu v. United States</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span><span> </span></span>The key links:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.korematsuinstitute.org/fred-t-korematsu-lifetime/\">Fred T. Korematsu Institute</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://densho.org/archives/\">Densho Archives</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span><span><br><br></span></span></p>\n<p><em>Additional music for this episode by The Flamingos, Lulu, <a href=\"https://bridgerecords.com/pages/paul-lansky\">Paul Lansky</a> and <a href=\"http://austinvaughnmusic.com/\">Austin Vaughn</a>.</em></p>\n<p><span><span> </span></span><em>Special thanks to the Densho Archives for use of archival tape of Fred Korematsu and Ernest Besig.</em></p>\n<p><span><span> </span></span><em>Leadership support for More Perfect is provided by The Joyce Foundation. Additional funding is provided by The Charles Evans Hughes Memorial Foundation.</em></p>\n<p><span><em>Supreme Court archival audio comes from </em><a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\"><em>Oyez®</em></a><em>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-10-02T00:11:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"driverless-dilemma", "title"=>"Driverless Dilemma", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17trolleyproblemupdate.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=799466", "description"=>"<div>\n<p>Most of us would sacrifice one person to save five. It’s a pretty straightforward bit of moral math. But if we have to actually kill that person ourselves, the math gets fuzzy.</p>\n<p>That’s the lesson of the classic Trolley Problem, a moral puzzle that fried our brains in an episode we did about 11 years ago. Luckily, the Trolley Problem has always been little more than a thought experiment, mostly confined to conversations at a certain kind of cocktail party. That is until now. New technologies are forcing that moral quandry out of our philosophy departments and onto our streets. So today we revisit the Trolley Problem and wonder how a two-ton hunk of speeding metal will make moral calculations about life and death that we can’t even figure out ourselves.</p>\n<p><em>This story was reported and produced by Amanda Aronczyk and Bethel Habte.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Thanks to Iyad Rahwan, Edmond Awad and Sydney Levine from the Moral Machine group at MIT. Also thanks to Fiery Cushman, Matthew DeBord, Sertac Karaman, Martine Powers, Xin Xiang, and Roborace for all of their help. </span></em><em><span>Thanks to the CUNY Graduate School of Journalism students who collected the vox: Chelsea Donohue, Ivan Flores, David Gentile, Maite Hernandez, Claudia Irizarry-Aponte, Comice Johnson, Richard Loria, Nivian Malik, Avery Miles, Alexandra Semenova, Kalah Siegel, Mark Suleymanov, Andee Tagle, Shaydanay Urbani, Isvett Verde and Reece Williams.</span></em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n", "tease"=>"What happens if we hand over our stickiest moral quandary to a two-ton hunk of metal and let it loose on our streets?", "publishTime"=>1506401760000, "publish-at"=>"2017-09-26T00:56:00", "date-line-ts"=>1506462946, "estimated-duration"=>2401, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/road-2573477_1920.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/", "name"=>"Creative Commons"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/l/80/1/road-2573477_1920.jpg", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/road-2573477_1920.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>168725, "credits-name"=>"pixabay.com"}, "objectID"=>"799466", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Driverless Dilemma", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div>\n<p>Most of us would sacrifice one person to save five. It’s a pretty straightforward bit of moral math. But if we have to actually kill that person ourselves, the math gets fuzzy.</p>\n<p>That’s the lesson of the classic Trolley Problem, a moral puzzle that fried our brains in an episode we did about 11 years ago. Luckily, the Trolley Problem has always been little more than a thought experiment, mostly confined to conversations at a certain kind of cocktail party. That is until now. New technologies are forcing that moral quandry out of our philosophy departments and onto our streets. So today we revisit the Trolley Problem and wonder how a two-ton hunk of speeding metal will make moral calculations about life and death that we can’t even figure out ourselves.</p>\n<p><em>This story was reported and produced by Amanda Aronczyk and Bethel Habte.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Thanks to Iyad Rahwan, Edmond Awad and Sydney Levine from the Moral Machine group at MIT. Also thanks to Fiery Cushman, Matthew DeBord, Sertac Karaman, Martine Powers, Xin Xiang, and Roborace for all of their help. </span></em><em><span>Thanks to the CUNY Graduate School of Journalism students who collected the vox: Chelsea Donohue, Ivan Flores, David Gentile, Maite Hernandez, Claudia Irizarry-Aponte, Comice Johnson, Richard Loria, Nivian Malik, Avery Miles, Alexandra Semenova, Kalah Siegel, Mark Suleymanov, Andee Tagle, Shaydanay Urbani, Isvett Verde and Reece Williams.</span></em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</p>\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-09-26T00:56:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"oliver-sipple", "title"=>"Oliver Sipple", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17oliversipple.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=798181", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>One morning, Oliver Sipple went out for a walk. A couple hours later, to his own surprise, he saved the life of the President of the United States. But in the days that followed, Sipple’s split-second act of heroism turned into a rationale for making his personal life into political opportunity. What happens next makes us wonder what a moment, or a movement, or a whole society can demand of one person. And how much is too much? </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Through newly unearthed archival tape, we hear Sipple himself grapple with some of the most vexing topics of his day and ours - privacy, identity, the freedom of the press - not to mention the bonds of family and friendship. </p>\n<p><em>Reported by Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte. Produced by Matt Kielty, Annie McEwen, Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Jerry Pritikin, Michael Yamashita, Stan Smith, Duffy Jennings; Ann Dolan, Megan Filly and Ginale Harris at the Superior Court of San Francisco; Leah Gracik, Karyn Hunt, Jesse Hamlin, The San Francisco Bay Area Television Archive, Mike Amico, Jennifer Vanasco and Joey Plaster.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/\"><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></a>.</span></p>", "tease"=>"In a flash of heroism and humanity, Oliver Sipple saved a life and became something he never wanted to be.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1506062040000, "publish-at"=>"2017-09-22T02:34:00", "date-line-ts"=>1506047501, "estimated-duration"=>3731, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/AP_7509220401.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"AP Images"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1224/1860/l/80/1/AP_7509220401.jpg", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Oliver Sipple, a 33-year-old former Marine, tells how he struck the gun just as it was fired in the direction of President Ford as he left a downtown hotel in San Francisco on Sept. 22, 1975.", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/AP_7509220401.jpg", "w"=>1224, "id"=>168505, "credits-name"=>"ASSOCIATED PRESS"}, "objectID"=>"798181", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Oliver Sipple", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>One morning, Oliver Sipple went out for a walk. A couple hours later, to his own surprise, he saved the life of the President of the United States. But in the days that followed, Sipple’s split-second act of heroism turned into a rationale for making his personal life into political opportunity. What happens next makes us wonder what a moment, or a movement, or a whole society can demand of one person. And how much is too much? </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Through newly unearthed archival tape, we hear Sipple himself grapple with some of the most vexing topics of his day and ours - privacy, identity, the freedom of the press - not to mention the bonds of family and friendship. </p>\n<p><em>Reported by Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte. Produced by Matt Kielty, Annie McEwen, Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Jerry Pritikin, Michael Yamashita, Stan Smith, Duffy Jennings; Ann Dolan, Megan Filly and Ginale Harris at the Superior Court of San Francisco; Leah Gracik, Karyn Hunt, Jesse Hamlin, The San Francisco Bay Area Television Archive, Mike Amico, Jennifer Vanasco and Joey Plaster.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/\"><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></a>.</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-09-22T02:34:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-presents-rough-translation", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: Anna in Somalia", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17annainsomalia.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=795342", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">This week, we are presenting a story from NPR foreign correspondent Gregory Warner and his new globe-trotting podcast <a href=\"http://www.npr.org/podcasts/510324/rough-translation\"><em>Rough Translation</em>.</a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Mohammed was having the best six months of his life - working a job he loved, making mixtapes for his sweetheart - when the communist Somali regime perp-walked him out of his own home, and sentenced him to a lifetime of solitary confinement.  With only concrete walls and cockroaches to keep him company, Mohammed felt miserable, alone, despondent.  But then one day, eight months into his sentence, he heard a whisper, a whisper that would open up a portal to - of all places and times - 19th century Russia, and that would teach him how to live and love again. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes%20%20%20%20%20%20\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</span></p>", "tease"=>"This week, we are presenting&nbsp;a story from NPR foreign correspondent Gregory Warner and his new globe-trotting podcast&nbsp;Rough Translation.", "publishTime"=>1505195100000, "publish-at"=>"2017-09-12T01:45:00", "date-line-ts"=>1505195100, "estimated-duration"=>2081, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/IMG_0036.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.npr.org/", "name"=>"NPR"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1395/1860/l/80/1/IMG_0036.jpg", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/IMG_0036.jpg", "w"=>1395, "id"=>168203, "credits-name"=>"Photo courtesy of Mohamed Barud Ali"}, "objectID"=>"795342", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: Anna in Somalia", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">This week, we are presenting a story from NPR foreign correspondent Gregory Warner and his new globe-trotting podcast <a href=\"http://www.npr.org/podcasts/510324/rough-translation\"><em>Rough Translation</em>.</a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Mohammed was having the best six months of his life - working a job he loved, making mixtapes for his sweetheart - when the communist Somali regime perp-walked him out of his own home, and sentenced him to a lifetime of solitary confinement.  With only concrete walls and cockroaches to keep him company, Mohammed felt miserable, alone, despondent.  But then one day, eight months into his sentence, he heard a whisper, a whisper that would open up a portal to - of all places and times - 19th century Russia, and that would teach him how to live and love again. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes%20%20%20%20%20%20\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-09-12T01:45:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"sun-dont-shine", "title"=>"Where the Sun Don't Shine", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17voyagerupdate.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=790298", "description"=>"<p>Today we take a quick look up at a hole in the sky and follow an old story as it travels beyond the reach of the sun. We hear from some moon-peeping listeners and then, on the 40th anniversary of their launch, we check in with the Voyager space probes. We revisit the story of the romantic time capsules that were placed onboard, and a question we asked five years ago: where exactly is Voyager 1? </p>\n<p><em>Original piece reported by Lynn Levy. This update was produced by Amanda Aronczyk and Annie McEwen.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Don Gurnett, Elizabeth Landau, Sarah Mozal, and Andrew Good.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes%20%20%20%20%20%20\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "tease"=>"Today we take a quick look up at a hole in the sky and follow an old story as it travels beyond the reach of the sun.", "publishTime"=>1503463380000, "publish-at"=>"2017-08-23T00:43:00", "date-line-ts"=>1503497145, "estimated-duration"=>1947, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/mounting-the-golden-record_30269499363_o.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1280/1015/l/80/1/mounting-the-golden-record_30269499363_o.jpg", "h"=>1015, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Mounting the Golden Record onto Voyager", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/mounting-the-golden-record_30269499363_o.jpg", "w"=>1280, "id"=>167558, "credits-name"=>"NASA/JPL-Caltech"}, "objectID"=>"790298", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Where the Sun Don't Shine", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Today we take a quick look up at a hole in the sky and follow an old story as it travels beyond the reach of the sun. We hear from some moon-peeping listeners and then, on the 40th anniversary of their launch, we check in with the Voyager space probes. We revisit the story of the romantic time capsules that were placed onboard, and a question we asked five years ago: where exactly is Voyager 1? </p>\n<p><em>Original piece reported by Lynn Levy. This update was produced by Amanda Aronczyk and Annie McEwen.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Don Gurnett, Elizabeth Landau, Sarah Mozal, and Andrew Good.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes%20%20%20%20%20%20\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-08-23T00:43:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"revising-fault-line", "title"=>"Revising the Fault Line", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17revisitingfaultline.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=777882", "description"=>"<p>A new tussle over an old story, and some long-held beliefs, with neurologist and author Robert Sapolsky.</p>\n<p>Four years ago, we did a story about a man with a starling obsession that made us question our ideas of responsibility and justice. We thought we’d found some solid ground, but today Dr. Sapolsky shows up and takes us down a rather disturbing rabbit hole. </p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"A fresh look at how, why, and who&nbsp;we&nbsp;blame.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1498600800000, "publish-at"=>"2017-06-27T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1498600800, "estimated-duration"=>2850, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Making their points", "name"=>"1/point_fingers.png", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1000/535/c/80/1/point_fingers.png", "h"=>535, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/19251296@N00/2255468498/in/photolist-4riScU-eGiLyC-5dorYC-9tHFVK-bzsdeU-4Y4SCS-4LjsGa-75S19L-bNmSbr-anvH5K-6HSWSN-ax9sif-dGmsXr-ceGsJL-dLJSi-5uVQz-w1oiY-9CZbWb-97hcz9-4whn", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/point_fingers.png", "w"=>1000, "id"=>103567, "credits-name"=>"Susan Sermoneta"}, "objectID"=>"777882", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Revising the Fault Line", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>A new tussle over an old story, and some long-held beliefs, with neurologist and author Robert Sapolsky.</p>\n<p>Four years ago, we did a story about a man with a starling obsession that made us question our ideas of responsibility and justice. We thought we’d found some solid ground, but today Dr. Sapolsky shows up and takes us down a rather disturbing rabbit hole. </p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-06-27T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"gondo", "title"=>"The Gondolier", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17gondolier.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=763607", "description"=>"<p><span>What happens when doing what you want to do means giving up who you really are? </span></p>\n<p><span>We travel to Venice, Italy with reporters Kristen Clark and David Conrad, where they meet gondolier Alex Hai. On the winding canals in the hidden parts of Venice, we learn about the nearly 1000-year old tradition of the Venetian Gondolier, and how the global media created a 20-year battle between that tradition and a supposed feminist icon.</span> </p>\n<p><em>Reported by David Conrad and Kristen Clark. Produced by Annie McEwen and Molly Webster.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to<span> Alexis Ungerer, Summer, Alex Hai, Kevin Gotkin, Silvia Del Fabbro, Sandro Mariot, Aldo Rosso and Marta Vannucci, The Longest Shortest Time (Hillary Frank, Peter Clowney and Abigail Keel), </span>Tim Howard, Nick Adams/GLAAD, Valentina Powers, Florence Ursino, Ann Marie Somma, Alex Overington, Jeremy Bloom and the people of Little Italy. </em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>\n<p>You can find Alex Hai's website <a href=\"https://alexhaigondolatours.com/\">here</a>, where you can check out the photographs discussed in the piece. </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"What happens when doing what you want to do means giving up who you really are?&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1497512460000, "publish-at"=>"2017-06-15T03:41:00", "date-line-ts"=>1497543410, "estimated-duration"=>3275, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Unknown-1_cTbaW6m.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1266/l/80/1/Unknown-1_cTbaW6m.jpeg", "h"=>1266, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Alex Hai", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Unknown-1_cTbaW6m.jpeg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>165475, "credits-name"=>"AlexHaicopyright2017 "}, "objectID"=>"763607", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Gondolier", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>What happens when doing what you want to do means giving up who you really are? </span></p>\n<p><span>We travel to Venice, Italy with reporters Kristen Clark and David Conrad, where they meet gondolier Alex Hai. On the winding canals in the hidden parts of Venice, we learn about the nearly 1000-year old tradition of the Venetian Gondolier, and how the global media created a 20-year battle between that tradition and a supposed feminist icon.</span> </p>\n<p><em>Reported by David Conrad and Kristen Clark. Produced by Annie McEwen and Molly Webster.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to<span> Alexis Ungerer, Summer, Alex Hai, Kevin Gotkin, Silvia Del Fabbro, Sandro Mariot, Aldo Rosso and Marta Vannucci, The Longest Shortest Time (Hillary Frank, Peter Clowney and Abigail Keel), </span>Tim Howard, Nick Adams/GLAAD, Valentina Powers, Florence Ursino, Ann Marie Somma, Alex Overington, Jeremy Bloom and the people of Little Italy. </em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>\n<p>You can find Alex Hai's website <a href=\"https://alexhaigondolatours.com/\">here</a>, where you can check out the photographs discussed in the piece. </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-06-15T03:41:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"15-years", "title"=>"The Radio Lab", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17theradiolab.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=758517", "description"=>"<div><span><span>15 years ago the very first episode of Radiolab, fittingly called \"Firsts,\" hit the airwaves. It was a 3-hour long collection of documentaries and musings produced by a solitary sleep-deprived producer named Jad Abumrad. Things have changed a bit since then.</span></span></div>\n<div><span><span> </span></span></div>\n<div><span><span>Today, with help from our long time Executive Producer Ellen Horne, we celebrate our 15th birthday by surprising Jad and Robert in the studio and forcing them to look back on a time when “Radiolab” was just that: a lab for experimenting with radio.   </span></span></div>\n\n<div><span><span> Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </span></span></div>\n\n", "tease"=>"We celebrate our 15th birthday by surprising Jad and Robert with a&nbsp;look back at&nbsp;when &ldquo;Radiolab&rdquo; was just that: a lab for experimenting with radio. &nbsp;&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1495746000000, "publish-at"=>"2017-05-25T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1495767840, "estimated-duration"=>2395, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/FullSizeRender_1f0Ix2p.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1395/1860/c/80/1/FullSizeRender_1f0Ix2p.jpg", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/FullSizeRender_1f0Ix2p.jpg", "w"=>1395, "id"=>164872, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Arianne Wack"}, "objectID"=>"758517", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Radio Lab", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div><span><span>15 years ago the very first episode of Radiolab, fittingly called \"Firsts,\" hit the airwaves. It was a 3-hour long collection of documentaries and musings produced by a solitary sleep-deprived producer named Jad Abumrad. Things have changed a bit since then.</span></span></div>\n<div><span><span> </span></span></div>\n<div><span><span>Today, with help from our long time Executive Producer Ellen Horne, we celebrate our 15th birthday by surprising Jad and Robert in the studio and forcing them to look back on a time when “Radiolab” was just that: a lab for experimenting with radio.   </span></span></div>\n\n<div><span><span> Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </span></span></div>\n\n", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-05-25T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"null-and-void", "title"=>"Null and Void", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17nullandvoid.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=755877", "description"=>"<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<div><span><span>Today, a hidden power that is either the cornerstone of our democracy or a trapdoor to anarchy.</span></span></div>\n<div><span><span> </span></span></div>\n<div><span><span>Should a juror be able to ignore the law? From a Quaker prayer meeting in the streets of London, to riots in the streets of LA, we trace the history of a quiet act of rebellion and struggle with how much power “we the people” should really have.</span></span></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><em>Produced by Matt Kielty and Tracie Hunte</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Darryl K. Brown, professor of law at the University of Virginia, Andrew Leipold, professor of law at the University of Illinois, at Urbana-Champaign, Nancy King, professor of law at Vanderbilt University, Buzz Scherr law professor at University of New Hampshire, Eric Verlo and attorneys David Lane, Mark Sisto, David Kallman and Paul Grant. </em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>\n<div dir=\"ltr\"></div>", "tease"=>"Today, a hidden power that is either the cornerstone of our democracy or a trapdoor to anarchy.", "publishTime"=>1494541419000, "publish-at"=>"2017-05-11T18:23:39", "date-line-ts"=>1494565200, "estimated-duration"=>3046, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/AP_060111012633.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"AP Images"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/l/80/1/AP_060111012633.jpg", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/AP_060111012633.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>164494, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Associated Press"}, "objectID"=>"755877", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Null and Void", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<div><span><span>Today, a hidden power that is either the cornerstone of our democracy or a trapdoor to anarchy.</span></span></div>\n<div><span><span> </span></span></div>\n<div><span><span>Should a juror be able to ignore the law? From a Quaker prayer meeting in the streets of London, to riots in the streets of LA, we trace the history of a quiet act of rebellion and struggle with how much power “we the people” should really have.</span></span></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><em>Produced by Matt Kielty and Tracie Hunte</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Darryl K. Brown, professor of law at the University of Virginia, Andrew Leipold, professor of law at the University of Illinois, at Urbana-Champaign, Nancy King, professor of law at Vanderbilt University, Buzz Scherr law professor at University of New Hampshire, Eric Verlo and attorneys David Lane, Mark Sisto, David Kallman and Paul Grant. </em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>\n<div dir=\"ltr\"></div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-05-11T18:23:39", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"funky-hand-jive", "title"=>"Funky Hand Jive", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17funkyhandjive.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=752399", "description"=>"<p><span>Back when Robert was kid, he had a chance encounter with then President John F. Kennedy. The interaction began with a hello and ended with a handshake. And like many of us who have touched greatness, 14 year old Robert was left wondering if maybe some of Kennedy would stay with him.  Now, 50 years later, Robert still finds himself pondering that encounter and question. And so with the help of brand new science and Neil Degrasse Tyson, he sets out to satisfy this curiosity once and for all. </span></p>\n<p><em>Produced by Simon Adler with help from Only Human: Amanda Aronczyk, Kenny Malone, Jillian Weinberger and Elaine Chen.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Neil deGrasse Tyson's newest book is called \"<a href=\"http://books.wwnorton.com/books/978-0-393-60939-4/\">Astrophysics for People in A Hurry</a>.\"</span></p>\n<p><em><span><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm139682527312032606159f1-35f7-47f2-af52-be2289a43980\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/BCvBF32zgPA?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a5106077161854089080\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BCvBF32zgPA&amp;feature=youtu.be\"></iframe></div></div>  </span></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab needs your help! Please visit </span><span><strong>wnyc.podcastingsurvey.com</strong></span><span> and tell us a little about you and the podcasts you love in a 5-minute, anonymous survey. We really appreciate your help - knowing more about you helps us make more of the shows you enjoy. Thank you from all of us at Radiolab!</span> </em></p>\n<p><span>*** As of Wednesday, May 3rd, 2017 we've run out of kits. Thanks so much to uBiome for generously donating over 13,000 free kits, and thanks to everyone for participating. ***</span><span> </span></p>\n<h3>FAQ:</h3>\n<p><a name=\"who\"></a><strong><em>Who is uBiome?</em></strong></p>\n<p>uBiome is a California-based biotech company started in 2012 that sequences the DNA of the microbes that live on and in you.</p>\n<p><a name=\"pay\"></a><strong><em>Do I have to pay for my results?</em></strong></p>\n<p>No, as long as you use the code for Radiolab/Only Human listeners, the sequencing results are free! uBiome otherwise charges $89 to have a skin sample analyzed.</p>\n<p><strong><a name=\"sick\"></a><em>Am I going to find out if I’m sick?</em></strong></p>\n<p>This uBiome information isn’t for diagnosing any health condition.</p>\n<p><em><a name=\"long\"></a><strong>How long will it take to get my results?</strong></em></p>\n<p>It can take from 3-6 weeks from when uBiome receives your sample to sequence, process and compile the material. So please send those samples back to the uBiome labs soon, so we can report back to you about the Radiolab/Only Human group.   </p>\n<p><strong><em><a name=\"data\"></a>What is uBiome going to do with my microbiome info?</em></strong></p>\n<p>uBiome scientists are going to share aggregate level analysis with Radiolab and Only Human so we can give general results about our group’s skin microbiome. Aside from that, what uBiome does with your results generally depends on whether you choose to be included in research or share your information. uBiome is HIPAA-compliant, and their practices are reviewed by an independent committee for ethical research (an IRB). For more information, see uBiome’s summary of <a href=\"https://ubiome.com/doc/privacy-notice\">its privacy practices</a> (just 6 pages in regular-sized font).</p>\n<p><em><a name=\"download\"></a><strong>Will I be able to get my raw data?</strong></em></p>\n<p>Yes! Once your results are in, you’ll be able to download it as a CSV, JSON or FASTQ file.</p>\n<p><strong><a name=\"clone\"></a><em>Will they take my DNA and clone me?</em></strong></p>\n<p>If by “me”, you mean the human you, then no, uBiome isn’t going to clone, let alone even sequence human DNA.</p>\n<p><strong>More questions? Email onlyhuman@wnyc.org.</strong></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>", "tease"=>"Neil Degrasse Tyson and some new microbiome science help answer the question -&nbsp;when we touch greatness how much of it stays with us?&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1493186400000, "publish-at"=>"2017-04-26T02:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1493186400, "estimated-duration"=>1684, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/AP131940509_utV6HbQ.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Associated Press"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/720/540/l/80/1/AP131940509_utV6HbQ.jpg", "h"=>540, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/AP131940509_utV6HbQ.jpg", "w"=>720, "id"=>163963, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Associated Press"}, "objectID"=>"752399", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Funky Hand Jive", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Back when Robert was kid, he had a chance encounter with then President John F. Kennedy. The interaction began with a hello and ended with a handshake. And like many of us who have touched greatness, 14 year old Robert was left wondering if maybe some of Kennedy would stay with him.  Now, 50 years later, Robert still finds himself pondering that encounter and question. And so with the help of brand new science and Neil Degrasse Tyson, he sets out to satisfy this curiosity once and for all. </span></p>\n<p><em>Produced by Simon Adler with help from Only Human: Amanda Aronczyk, Kenny Malone, Jillian Weinberger and Elaine Chen.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Neil deGrasse Tyson's newest book is called \"<a href=\"http://books.wwnorton.com/books/978-0-393-60939-4/\">Astrophysics for People in A Hurry</a>.\"</span></p>\n<p><em><span><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm139682527312032606159f1-35f7-47f2-af52-be2289a43980\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/BCvBF32zgPA?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a5106077161854089080\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BCvBF32zgPA&amp;feature=youtu.be\"></iframe></div></div>  </span></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab needs your help! Please visit </span><span><strong>wnyc.podcastingsurvey.com</strong></span><span> and tell us a little about you and the podcasts you love in a 5-minute, anonymous survey. We really appreciate your help - knowing more about you helps us make more of the shows you enjoy. Thank you from all of us at Radiolab!</span> </em></p>\n<p><span>*** As of Wednesday, May 3rd, 2017 we've run out of kits. Thanks so much to uBiome for generously donating over 13,000 free kits, and thanks to everyone for participating. ***</span><span> </span></p>\n<h3>FAQ:</h3>\n<p><a name=\"who\"></a><strong><em>Who is uBiome?</em></strong></p>\n<p>uBiome is a California-based biotech company started in 2012 that sequences the DNA of the microbes that live on and in you.</p>\n<p><a name=\"pay\"></a><strong><em>Do I have to pay for my results?</em></strong></p>\n<p>No, as long as you use the code for Radiolab/Only Human listeners, the sequencing results are free! uBiome otherwise charges $89 to have a skin sample analyzed.</p>\n<p><strong><a name=\"sick\"></a><em>Am I going to find out if I’m sick?</em></strong></p>\n<p>This uBiome information isn’t for diagnosing any health condition.</p>\n<p><em><a name=\"long\"></a><strong>How long will it take to get my results?</strong></em></p>\n<p>It can take from 3-6 weeks from when uBiome receives your sample to sequence, process and compile the material. So please send those samples back to the uBiome labs soon, so we can report back to you about the Radiolab/Only Human group.   </p>\n<p><strong><em><a name=\"data\"></a>What is uBiome going to do with my microbiome info?</em></strong></p>\n<p>uBiome scientists are going to share aggregate level analysis with Radiolab and Only Human so we can give general results about our group’s skin microbiome. Aside from that, what uBiome does with your results generally depends on whether you choose to be included in research or share your information. uBiome is HIPAA-compliant, and their practices are reviewed by an independent committee for ethical research (an IRB). For more information, see uBiome’s summary of <a href=\"https://ubiome.com/doc/privacy-notice\">its privacy practices</a> (just 6 pages in regular-sized font).</p>\n<p><em><a name=\"download\"></a><strong>Will I be able to get my raw data?</strong></em></p>\n<p>Yes! Once your results are in, you’ll be able to download it as a CSV, JSON or FASTQ file.</p>\n<p><strong><a name=\"clone\"></a><em>Will they take my DNA and clone me?</em></strong></p>\n<p>If by “me”, you mean the human you, then no, uBiome isn’t going to clone, let alone even sequence human DNA.</p>\n<p><strong>More questions? Email onlyhuman@wnyc.org.</strong></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-04-26T02:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-extra-henrietta-lacks", "title"=>"Radiolab Extra: Henrietta Lacks", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/rl_extras/rl_extras17henriettalacks.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=750910", "description"=>"<p><span>With all the recent talk about HBO's upcoming film, we decided it would be good time to re-run our story of one woman's medically miraculous cancer cells, and how Henrietta Lacks changed modern science and, eventually, her family's understanding of itself.</span></p>\n<p><span> Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </span></p>", "tease"=>"One woman's medically miraculous cancer cells, and how Henrietta Lacks changed modern science and, eventually, her family's understanding of itself.", "publishTime"=>1492567200000, "publish-at"=>"2017-04-18T22:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1492567200, "estimated-duration"=>2113, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"HeLa S3 cells", "name"=>"1/hela_cells_green_web.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/605/h/80/1/hela_cells_green_web.jpg", "h"=>605, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"HeLa S3 cells", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/8674020@N04/5420346423/in/photolist-9fYG9F-dYxbuo-dZVM8G", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/hela_cells_green_web.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>105921, "credits-name"=>"opiado"}, "objectID"=>"750910", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Extra: Henrietta Lacks", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>With all the recent talk about HBO's upcoming film, we decided it would be good time to re-run our story of one woman's medically miraculous cancer cells, and how Henrietta Lacks changed modern science and, eventually, her family's understanding of itself.</span></p>\n<p><span> Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-04-18T22:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"nukes", "title"=>"Nukes", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17nukes.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=747788", "description"=>"<p>President Richard Nixon once boasted that at any moment he could pick up a telephone and - in 20 minutes - kill 60 million people.  Such is the power of the US President over the nation’s nuclear arsenal.  But what if you were the military officer on the receiving end of that phone call? Could you refuse the order?</p>\n<p>This episode, we profile one Air Force Major who asked that question back in the 1970s and learn how the very act of asking it was so dangerous it derailed his career. We also pick up the question ourselves and pose it to veterans both high and low on the nuclear chain of command. Their responses reveal once and for all whether there are any legal checks and balances between us and a phone call for Armageddon.</p>\n<p><em>Reported by Latif Nasser. Produced by Annie McEwen and Simon Adler with production help from Arianne Wack. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to: Elaine Scarry, Sam Kean, Ron Rosenbaum, Lisa Perry, Ryan Furtkamp, Robin Perry, Thom Woodroofe, Doreen de Brum, Jackie Conley, Sean Malloy, Ray Peter, Jack D’Annibale, Ryan Pettigrew at the Nixon Presidential Library and Samuel Rushay at the Truman Presidential Library.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>", "tease"=>"A&nbsp;look&nbsp;up and down the US nuclear chain of command to find out who gets to authorize their use&nbsp;and&nbsp;who can stand in the way of Armageddon.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1491548400000, "publish-at"=>"2017-04-07T03:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1491548400, "estimated-duration"=>3340, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/AP_551259520546.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Associated Press"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1417/c/80/1/AP_551259520546.jpg", "h"=>1417, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/AP_551259520546.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>163190, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Associated Press"}, "objectID"=>"747788", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Nukes", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>President Richard Nixon once boasted that at any moment he could pick up a telephone and - in 20 minutes - kill 60 million people.  Such is the power of the US President over the nation’s nuclear arsenal.  But what if you were the military officer on the receiving end of that phone call? Could you refuse the order?</p>\n<p>This episode, we profile one Air Force Major who asked that question back in the 1970s and learn how the very act of asking it was so dangerous it derailed his career. We also pick up the question ourselves and pose it to veterans both high and low on the nuclear chain of command. Their responses reveal once and for all whether there are any legal checks and balances between us and a phone call for Armageddon.</p>\n<p><em>Reported by Latif Nasser. Produced by Annie McEwen and Simon Adler with production help from Arianne Wack. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to: Elaine Scarry, Sam Kean, Ron Rosenbaum, Lisa Perry, Ryan Furtkamp, Robin Perry, Thom Woodroofe, Doreen de Brum, Jackie Conley, Sean Malloy, Ray Peter, Jack D’Annibale, Ryan Pettigrew at the Nixon Presidential Library and Samuel Rushay at the Truman Presidential Library.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-04-07T03:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"shots-fired-part-2", "title"=>"Shots Fired: Part 2", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17shotsfiredtwo.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=744773", "description"=>"<p>A couple years ago, Ben Montgomery, reporter at the Tampa Bay Times, started emailing every police station in Florida.</p>\n<p>He was asking for any documents created - from 2009 to 2014 - when an officer discharged his weapon in the line of duty. He ended up with a six foot tall stack of reports, pictures, and press clippings cataloging the death or injury of <span>828 people by Florida police. </span></p>\n<p>In part 2 of Shots Fired, Jad and Robert talk to Ben about how communication breakdowns too often lead to violence and our reporter Matt Kielty sits with one man who found himself at the center of a police visit gone horribly wrong.</p>\n<p><em>Produced and reported by Matt Kielty.</em></p>\n<p><em>For the full presentation of Ben Montgomery's reporting please visit the Tampa Bay Times' <a href=\"http://www.tampabay.com/projects/2017/investigations/florida-police-shootings/\">'Why Do Cops Shoot?\"</a> We can't recommend it highly enough. </em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "tease"=>"We again join Ben Montgomery, reporter at the Tampa Bay Times, as he looks at data on every person&nbsp;killed or injured by Florida police over six years.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1490331600000, "publish-at"=>"2017-03-24T01:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1490331600, "estimated-duration"=>1614, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/3957814193_6fd835e7c0_o.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/1/3957814193_6fd835e7c0_o.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/3957814193_6fd835e7c0_o.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>162512, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Bob B. Brown"}, "objectID"=>"744773", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Shots Fired: Part 2", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>A couple years ago, Ben Montgomery, reporter at the Tampa Bay Times, started emailing every police station in Florida.</p>\n<p>He was asking for any documents created - from 2009 to 2014 - when an officer discharged his weapon in the line of duty. He ended up with a six foot tall stack of reports, pictures, and press clippings cataloging the death or injury of <span>828 people by Florida police. </span></p>\n<p>In part 2 of Shots Fired, Jad and Robert talk to Ben about how communication breakdowns too often lead to violence and our reporter Matt Kielty sits with one man who found himself at the center of a police visit gone horribly wrong.</p>\n<p><em>Produced and reported by Matt Kielty.</em></p>\n<p><em>For the full presentation of Ben Montgomery's reporting please visit the Tampa Bay Times' <a href=\"http://www.tampabay.com/projects/2017/investigations/florida-police-shootings/\">'Why Do Cops Shoot?\"</a> We can't recommend it highly enough. </em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-03-24T01:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"shots-fired-part-1", "title"=>"Shots Fired: Part 1", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17shotsfiredone.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=743201", "description"=>"<p>A couple years ago, Ben Montgomery, reporter at the Tampa Bay Times, started emailing every police station in Florida.</p>\n<p>He was asking for any documents created - from 2009 to 2014 - when an officer discharged his weapon in the line of duty. He ended up with a six foot tall stack of reports, pictures, and press clippings cataloging the death or injury of <span>828 people by Florida police. </span></p>\n<p>Jad and Robert talk to Ben about what he found, crunch some numbers, and then our reporter Matt Kielty takes a couple files off Ben's desk and brings us the stories inside them - from a network of grief to a Daytona police chief.</p>\n<p>And next week, we bring you another, very different story of a police encounter gone wrong.</p>\n<p><em>Produced and reported by Matt Kielty</em></p>\n<p><em>For the full presentation of Ben Montgomery's reporting please visit the Tampa Bay Times' <a href=\"http://www.tampabay.com/projects/2017/investigations/florida-police-shootings/\">'Why Cops Shoot?\"</a> We can't recommend it highly enough. </em></p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this story incorrectly stated that in reporter Ben Montgomery's six years of Florida data there were, on average, 130 people shot and killed each year. Police officers did indeed shoot 130 people per year, on average, but only half of those shootings were fatal. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>", "tease"=>"We join Ben Montgomery, a reporter at the Tampa Bay Times, as he looks at every person killed or injured by Florida police over six years.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1489734000000, "publish-at"=>"2017-03-17T03:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1489734000, "estimated-duration"=>3334, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/3957814193_6fd835e7c0_o.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/1/3957814193_6fd835e7c0_o.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/3957814193_6fd835e7c0_o.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>162512, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Bob B. Brown"}, "objectID"=>"743201", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Shots Fired: Part 1", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>A couple years ago, Ben Montgomery, reporter at the Tampa Bay Times, started emailing every police station in Florida.</p>\n<p>He was asking for any documents created - from 2009 to 2014 - when an officer discharged his weapon in the line of duty. He ended up with a six foot tall stack of reports, pictures, and press clippings cataloging the death or injury of <span>828 people by Florida police. </span></p>\n<p>Jad and Robert talk to Ben about what he found, crunch some numbers, and then our reporter Matt Kielty takes a couple files off Ben's desk and brings us the stories inside them - from a network of grief to a Daytona police chief.</p>\n<p>And next week, we bring you another, very different story of a police encounter gone wrong.</p>\n<p><em>Produced and reported by Matt Kielty</em></p>\n<p><em>For the full presentation of Ben Montgomery's reporting please visit the Tampa Bay Times' <a href=\"http://www.tampabay.com/projects/2017/investigations/florida-police-shootings/\">'Why Cops Shoot?\"</a> We can't recommend it highly enough. </em></p>\n<p><em>Correction: An earlier version of this story incorrectly stated that in reporter Ben Montgomery's six years of Florida data there were, on average, 130 people shot and killed each year. Police officers did indeed shoot 130 people per year, on average, but only half of those shootings were fatal. The audio has been adjusted to reflect this fact.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-03-17T03:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"update-crispr", "title"=>"Update: CRISPR", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17updatecrispr.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=738864", "description"=>"<p><span>It's been almost two years since we learned about CRISPR, a ninja-assassin-meets-DNA-editing-tool that has been billed as one of the most powerful, and potentially controversial, technologies ever discovered by scientists. In this episode, we catch up on what's been happening (it's a lot), and learn about CRISPR's potential to not only change human evolution, but every organism on the entire planet.</span></p>\n<p>Out drinking with a few biologists, Jad finds out about something called CRISPR. No, it’s not a robot or the latest dating app, it’s a method for genetic manipulation that is rewriting the way we change DNA. Scientists say they’ll someday be able to use CRISPR to fight cancer and maybe even bring animals back from the dead. Or, pretty much do whatever you want. Jad and Robert delve into how CRISPR does what it does, and consider whether we should be worried about a future full of flying pigs, or the simple fact that scientists have now used CRISPR to tweak the genes of human embryos.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Molly Webster and Soren Wheeler. Special thanks to Jacob S. Sherkow.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   <em>  </em></p>", "tease"=>"In 2012, scientists had a realization: hidden inside one of the world&rsquo;s smallest organisms, was one of the world&rsquo;s most powerful tools.", "publishTime"=>1487973600000, "publish-at"=>"2017-02-24T17:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1487973600, "estimated-duration"=>2977, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/5927204872_5a6d669faf_o.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/611/c/80/1/5927204872_5a6d669faf_o.jpg", "h"=>611, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/5927204872_5a6d669faf_o.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>138497, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: NIH/NIAID"}, "objectID"=>"738864", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Update: CRISPR", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>It's been almost two years since we learned about CRISPR, a ninja-assassin-meets-DNA-editing-tool that has been billed as one of the most powerful, and potentially controversial, technologies ever discovered by scientists. In this episode, we catch up on what's been happening (it's a lot), and learn about CRISPR's potential to not only change human evolution, but every organism on the entire planet.</span></p>\n<p>Out drinking with a few biologists, Jad finds out about something called CRISPR. No, it’s not a robot or the latest dating app, it’s a method for genetic manipulation that is rewriting the way we change DNA. Scientists say they’ll someday be able to use CRISPR to fight cancer and maybe even bring animals back from the dead. Or, pretty much do whatever you want. Jad and Robert delve into how CRISPR does what it does, and consider whether we should be worried about a future full of flying pigs, or the simple fact that scientists have now used CRISPR to tweak the genes of human embryos.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Molly Webster and Soren Wheeler. Special thanks to Jacob S. Sherkow.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.   <em>  </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-02-24T17:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-presents-ponzi-supernova", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: Ponzi Supernova", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17ponzi.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=735990", "description"=>"<p>We thought we knew the story of Bernie Madoff.  How he masterminded the biggest Ponzi scheme in history, leaving behind scores of distraught investors and a $65 billion black hole. </p>\n<p>But we had never heard the story from Madoff himself.</p>\n<p>This week, reporter Steve Fishman and former Radiolabber Ellen Horne visit our studio to play us snippets from their extraordinary Audible series <em>Ponzi Supernova</em>, which features exclusive footage of the man who bamboozled the world.  After years of investigative reporting – including interviews with dozens of FBI and SEC agents, investors, traders, and attorneys – the pair scrutinize Madoff’s account to understand exactly why he did it, how he managed to pull it off, and how culpable he actually was. Was he a puppetmaster or a puppet? And if the latter, who else is to blame for the biggest financial fraud in history?</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://itunes.apple.com/us/podcast/ponzi-supernova/id1232020925?mt=2\">You can hear the entire series on iTunes</a> or <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/FREE-Ponzi-Supernova/dp/B06Y4F5JLT\">for free on Audible</a></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "tease"=>"Madoff speaks. &nbsp;Investigating the world's largest con with the team behind @Audible_com's #PonziSupernova.", "publishTime"=>1486705500000, "publish-at"=>"2017-02-10T00:45:00", "date-line-ts"=>1486705500, "estimated-duration"=>2280, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/ponzi.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1200/627/c/80/1/ponzi.jpg", "h"=>627, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/ponzi.jpg", "w"=>1200, "id"=>161522, "credits-name"=>"Courtesy of Audible"}, "objectID"=>"735990", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: Ponzi Supernova", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>We thought we knew the story of Bernie Madoff.  How he masterminded the biggest Ponzi scheme in history, leaving behind scores of distraught investors and a $65 billion black hole. </p>\n<p>But we had never heard the story from Madoff himself.</p>\n<p>This week, reporter Steve Fishman and former Radiolabber Ellen Horne visit our studio to play us snippets from their extraordinary Audible series <em>Ponzi Supernova</em>, which features exclusive footage of the man who bamboozled the world.  After years of investigative reporting – including interviews with dozens of FBI and SEC agents, investors, traders, and attorneys – the pair scrutinize Madoff’s account to understand exactly why he did it, how he managed to pull it off, and how culpable he actually was. Was he a puppetmaster or a puppet? And if the latter, who else is to blame for the biggest financial fraud in history?</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://itunes.apple.com/us/podcast/ponzi-supernova/id1232020925?mt=2\">You can hear the entire series on iTunes</a> or <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/FREE-Ponzi-Supernova/dp/B06Y4F5JLT\">for free on Audible</a></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-02-10T00:45:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"man-vs-machine", "title"=>"Man vs Machine", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21manvmachine.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=735738", "description"=>"<p><span>Are new ideas and new inventions inevitable? Are they driven by us or by a larger force of nature? In this episode, we look at the things we make—from spoons to microwaves to computers—as an extension of the same evolutionary processes that made us. And we may need to adapt to the idea that our technology could someday truly have a mind of its own. </span></p>", "tease"=>"We may need to adapt to the idea that our technology could someday truly have a mind of its own.", "publishTime"=>1486488060000, "publish-at"=>"2017-02-07T12:21:00", "date-line-ts"=>1486486800, "estimated-duration"=>3751, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/399400066_af8ec28547_z.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/427/l/80/1/399400066_af8ec28547_z.jpg", "h"=>427, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/399400066_af8ec28547_z.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>111303, "credits-name"=>"Jerry Bowley"}, "objectID"=>"735738", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Man vs Machine", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Are new ideas and new inventions inevitable? Are they driven by us or by a larger force of nature? In this episode, we look at the things we make—from spoons to microwaves to computers—as an extension of the same evolutionary processes that made us. And we may need to adapt to the idea that our technology could someday truly have a mind of its own. </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-02-07T12:21:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"stanger-paradise", "title"=>"Stranger in Paradise", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17strangerparadise.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=733334", "description"=>"<div>Back in 1911, a box with a dead raccoon in it showed up in Washington D.C., at the office of Gerrit S. Miller. After pulling it out and inspecting it, he realized this raccoon was from the Caribbean islands of Guadeloupe, and unlike anything he’d ever seen before.  He christened it <em>Procyon minor</em> and in doing so changed the history of Guadeloupe forever.  </div>\n\n<p>Today we travel from the storage rooms of the Smithsonian to the sandy beaches of Guadeloupe, chasing the tale of this trash can tipping critter. All the while trying to uncover what it means to be special. </p>\n\n<p><em>Produced and reported by Simon Adler.</em></p>\n\n<p><em>Special thanks to Sally Stainier and Allie Pinel for all their help translating in Guadeloupe and New York respectively. </em></p>\n\n<p><em>Thanks to Bernie Beelmeon, Paola Dvihally, Hervé Magnin, Guillaume Aricique, Laurence Baptiste-Salomon, David Xavier-Albert, Florian Kirchner, Matt Chew, and everyone at the ONCFS. </em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "tease"=>"Today we travel from the storage rooms of the Smithsonian to the sandy beaches of Guadeloupe, chasing the tale of one&nbsp;trash can tipping raccoon.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1485501164000, "publish-at"=>"2017-01-27T02:12:44", "date-line-ts"=>1485507600, "estimated-duration"=>2616, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/HMP_7941.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1241/l/80/1/HMP_7941.jpg", "h"=>1241, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/HMP_7941.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>161112, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Guillaume Aricique"}, "objectID"=>"733334", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Stranger in Paradise", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div>Back in 1911, a box with a dead raccoon in it showed up in Washington D.C., at the office of Gerrit S. Miller. After pulling it out and inspecting it, he realized this raccoon was from the Caribbean islands of Guadeloupe, and unlike anything he’d ever seen before.  He christened it <em>Procyon minor</em> and in doing so changed the history of Guadeloupe forever.  </div>\n\n<p>Today we travel from the storage rooms of the Smithsonian to the sandy beaches of Guadeloupe, chasing the tale of this trash can tipping critter. All the while trying to uncover what it means to be special. </p>\n\n<p><em>Produced and reported by Simon Adler.</em></p>\n\n<p><em>Special thanks to Sally Stainier and Allie Pinel for all their help translating in Guadeloupe and New York respectively. </em></p>\n\n<p><em>Thanks to Bernie Beelmeon, Paola Dvihally, Hervé Magnin, Guillaume Aricique, Laurence Baptiste-Salomon, David Xavier-Albert, Florian Kirchner, Matt Chew, and everyone at the ONCFS. </em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.    </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-01-27T02:12:44", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-presents-media-busted-americas-poverty-myths", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: On the Media: Busted, America's Poverty Myths", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast17otmbusted.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=731237", "description"=>"<p>We love to share great radio, even if we didn’t make it. Today, On the Media’s Brooke Gladstone tells Jad and Robert about a mammoth project they launched to take a critical look at the tales we tell ourselves when we talk about poverty.</p>\n<p>In a 5-part series called \"Busted: America’s Poverty Myths,” On the Media picked apart numerous oft-repeated narratives about what it's like to be poor in America. From Ben Franklin to a brutal eviction, Brooke gives us just a little taste of what she learned and shares a couple stories of the struggle to get ahead, or even just get by.</p>\n<p>Go check out the full series, it’s well worth it. You can <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/series/busted-americas-poverty-myths\">hear all 5 episodes of Busted here</a> or <a href=\"https://itunes.apple.com/us/podcast/on-the-media/id73330715?mt=2\">subscribe to On the Media in iTunes</a> (or wherever you get your podcasts) to listen to this series or all their other great work.</p>\n<p><em>\"Busted: America’s Poverty Myths\" was produced by Meara Sharma and Eve Claxton and edited by Katya Rogers. They produced the series in collaboration with WNET’s Chasing the Dream; poverty and opportunity in America.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.     </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"On the Media&rsquo;s Brooke Gladstone tells Jad and Robert about a project they launched to look at the tales we tell when we talk about poverty.", "publishTime"=>1484719200000, "publish-at"=>"2017-01-18T01:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1484719200, "estimated-duration"=>2005, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/708px--Freedom_From_Want-_-_NARA_-_513539.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.archives.gov/", "name"=>"National Archives and Records Administration"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/708/900/c/80/1/708px--Freedom_From_Want-_-_NARA_-_513539.jpg", "h"=>900, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Norman Rockwell's \"Freedom From Want\" ", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/708px--Freedom_From_Want-_-_NARA_-_513539.jpg", "w"=>708, "id"=>160746, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: U.S. National Archives and Records Administration"}, "objectID"=>"731237", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: On the Media: Busted, America's Poverty Myths", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>We love to share great radio, even if we didn’t make it. Today, On the Media’s Brooke Gladstone tells Jad and Robert about a mammoth project they launched to take a critical look at the tales we tell ourselves when we talk about poverty.</p>\n<p>In a 5-part series called \"Busted: America’s Poverty Myths,” On the Media picked apart numerous oft-repeated narratives about what it's like to be poor in America. From Ben Franklin to a brutal eviction, Brooke gives us just a little taste of what she learned and shares a couple stories of the struggle to get ahead, or even just get by.</p>\n<p>Go check out the full series, it’s well worth it. You can <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/series/busted-americas-poverty-myths\">hear all 5 episodes of Busted here</a> or <a href=\"https://itunes.apple.com/us/podcast/on-the-media/id73330715?mt=2\">subscribe to On the Media in iTunes</a> (or wherever you get your podcasts) to listen to this series or all their other great work.</p>\n<p><em>\"Busted: America’s Poverty Myths\" was produced by Meara Sharma and Eve Claxton and edited by Katya Rogers. They produced the series in collaboration with WNET’s Chasing the Dream; poverty and opportunity in America.</em></p>\n<p>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.     </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2017-01-18T01:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2018=>[{"slug"=>"bonus-radiolab-scavenger-hunt", "title"=>"BONUS: Radiolab Scavenger Hunt", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18bonusscavengerhunt.mp3?aisGetOriginalStream=true&awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=905125", "description"=>"<p><span>The question we get more than any other here at Radiolab is “Where do all those stories come from?”  Today, for the first time ever, we divulge our secret recipe for story-finding.  Veteran Radiolab story scout Latif Nasser takes our newest producer Rachael Cusick along for what he calls “the world’s biggest scavenger hunt.”  Together, they’ll make you want to bake some cookies and find some true stories.  </span><span>But we can’t find, much less tell, true stories without you. Find it in yourself to donate and help us make another year of this possible. </span><strong>It's a choice only you can make. <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\" title=\"Radiolab.org/Support\" previewremoved=\"true\" id=\"LPlnk819512\" class=\"OWAAutoLink\">Radiolab.org/support</a></strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Here are story-finding resources mentioned in this episode:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://transom.org/2018/latif-nasser/\"><em>The World's Biggest Scavenger Hunt</em></a>: Latif's Transom post on story scouting</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.google.com/alerts\">Google Alerts</a>: Set up your own!</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Main_Page\">Wikipedia Random Article</a>: Play wiki roulette by clicking \"random article\" in the far-left column</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.worldcat.org/\">WorldCat</a>: to find where a book exists in a library near you</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://beta.worldcat.org/archivegrid/\">ArchiveGrid</a>: to search libraries' special collections and oral histories</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.webwire.com/IndustryList.asp\">Trade Publications</a>: Search for trade magazines by industry</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/cusick-cookies\">Cusick Cookies</a>: Rachael's cookie recipe...you're welcome.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"The question we get more than any other&nbsp;is &ldquo;Where do all those stories come from?&rdquo;&nbsp; Today, for the first time ever, we spill our story-finding&nbsp;secrets.", "publishTime"=>1546038000000, "publish-at"=>"2018-12-28T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1546038000, "estimated-duration"=>1096, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/12/9276565426_03648e0515_z.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/498/640/l/80/2018/12/9276565426_03648e0515_z.jpg", "h"=>640, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/12/9276565426_03648e0515_z.jpg", "w"=>498, "id"=>180723, "credits-name"=>"Tullio Saba"}, "objectID"=>"905125", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"BONUS: Radiolab Scavenger Hunt", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>The question we get more than any other here at Radiolab is “Where do all those stories come from?”  Today, for the first time ever, we divulge our secret recipe for story-finding.  Veteran Radiolab story scout Latif Nasser takes our newest producer Rachael Cusick along for what he calls “the world’s biggest scavenger hunt.”  Together, they’ll make you want to bake some cookies and find some true stories.  </span><span>But we can’t find, much less tell, true stories without you. Find it in yourself to donate and help us make another year of this possible. </span><strong>It's a choice only you can make. <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\" title=\"Radiolab.org/Support\" previewremoved=\"true\" id=\"LPlnk819512\" class=\"OWAAutoLink\">Radiolab.org/support</a></strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Here are story-finding resources mentioned in this episode:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://transom.org/2018/latif-nasser/\"><em>The World's Biggest Scavenger Hunt</em></a>: Latif's Transom post on story scouting</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.google.com/alerts\">Google Alerts</a>: Set up your own!</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Main_Page\">Wikipedia Random Article</a>: Play wiki roulette by clicking \"random article\" in the far-left column</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.worldcat.org/\">WorldCat</a>: to find where a book exists in a library near you</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://beta.worldcat.org/archivegrid/\">ArchiveGrid</a>: to search libraries' special collections and oral histories</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.webwire.com/IndustryList.asp\">Trade Publications</a>: Search for trade magazines by industry</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/cusick-cookies\">Cusick Cookies</a>: Rachael's cookie recipe...you're welcome.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-12-28T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-clockwork-miracle", "title"=>"A Clockwork Miracle", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18clockworkmiracle.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=905095", "description"=>"<p>As legend goes, in 1562, King Philip II needed a miracle. So he commissioned one from a highly-skilled clockmaker. In this short, a king's deal with God leads to an intricate mechanical creation, and Jad heads to the Smithsonian to investigate. </p>\n<p>When the 17-year-old crown prince of Spain, Don Carlos, fell down a set of stairs in 1562, he threw his whole country into a state of uncertainty about the future. Especially his father, King Philip II, who despite being the most powerful man in the world, was helpless in the face of his heir's terrible head wound. When none of the leading remedies of the day--bleeding, blistering, purging, or drilling--helped, the king enlisted the help of a relic...the corpse of a local holy man who had died 100 years earlier. Then, Philip II promised that if God saved his son, he'd repay him with a miracle of his own.</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/thesizesofthings.com\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Elizabeth King</strong></a>, a professor at Virginia Commonwealth University, describes how--according to legend--Philip II held up his end of the bargain with the help of a renowned clockmaker and an intricate invention. Jad and Latif head to the Smithsonian to meet curator Carlene E. Stephens, who shows them the inner workings of a nearly 450-year-old monkbot. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm139682526091264cffd560c-85b2-4318-be16-b0d4d5bd71dc\"><iframe width=\"465\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Ycyj76VPOtc?start=143&amp;wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a-2362078696609496012\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?time_continue=143&amp;v=Ycyj76VPOtc\"></iframe></div></div>  </p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser. </em></p>\n<p><em>In the time since this episode was first produced, Elizabeth King has written a book about the clockwork monk. More details can be found at <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/automatonmonk.com\" target=\"_blank\">automatonmonk.com</a><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Today, a king, a clockmaker, and a monk come together to forge a manmade miracle.&nbsp;&nbsp;We tell a five-hundred-year-old legend about robots, death, and a divine deal.", "publishTime"=>1545952980000, "publish-at"=>"2018-12-27T18:23:00", "date-line-ts"=>1545952980, "estimated-duration"=>1272, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/12/monkbot.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/384/c/80/2018/12/monkbot.jpg", "h"=>384, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"monkbot", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/12/monkbot.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>180718, "credits-name"=>"Rosamond Purcell"}, "objectID"=>"905095", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"A Clockwork Miracle", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>As legend goes, in 1562, King Philip II needed a miracle. So he commissioned one from a highly-skilled clockmaker. In this short, a king's deal with God leads to an intricate mechanical creation, and Jad heads to the Smithsonian to investigate. </p>\n<p>When the 17-year-old crown prince of Spain, Don Carlos, fell down a set of stairs in 1562, he threw his whole country into a state of uncertainty about the future. Especially his father, King Philip II, who despite being the most powerful man in the world, was helpless in the face of his heir's terrible head wound. When none of the leading remedies of the day--bleeding, blistering, purging, or drilling--helped, the king enlisted the help of a relic...the corpse of a local holy man who had died 100 years earlier. Then, Philip II promised that if God saved his son, he'd repay him with a miracle of his own.</p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/thesizesofthings.com\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>Elizabeth King</strong></a>, a professor at Virginia Commonwealth University, describes how--according to legend--Philip II held up his end of the bargain with the help of a renowned clockmaker and an intricate invention. Jad and Latif head to the Smithsonian to meet curator Carlene E. Stephens, who shows them the inner workings of a nearly 450-year-old monkbot. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm139682526091264cffd560c-85b2-4318-be16-b0d4d5bd71dc\"><iframe width=\"465\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Ycyj76VPOtc?start=143&amp;wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a-2362078696609496012\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?time_continue=143&amp;v=Ycyj76VPOtc\"></iframe></div></div>  </p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser. </em></p>\n<p><em>In the time since this episode was first produced, Elizabeth King has written a book about the clockwork monk. More details can be found at <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/automatonmonk.com\" target=\"_blank\">automatonmonk.com</a><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-12-27T18:23:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-apologetical", "title"=>"Apologetical", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18_apologetical.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=903879", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>How do you fix a word that’s broken? A word we need when we bump into someone on the street, or break someone’s heart. In our increasingly disconnected secular world, “sorry” has been stretched and twisted, and in some cases weaponized. But it’s also one of the only ways we have to piece together a sense of shared values and beliefs. Through today's sea of sorry-not-sorries, empty apologies, and just straight up non-apologies, we wonder what it looks like to make amends.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>The program at Stanford that Leilani went through (and now works for) was a joint creation between Stanford and Lee Taft. Find out more here: <a href=\"http://www.stanfordchildrens.org/en/patient-family-resources/pearl\"><strong>www.stanfordchildrens.org/en/patient-family-resources/pearl</strong></a></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was reported by Annie McEwen and was produced by Annie McEwen and Simon Adler. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks to Mark Bressler, Nancy Kielty, and Patty Walters. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em><br></em></p>", "tease"=>"When someone hurts you, what do you really want from them? From the Canadian House of Commons to a California hospital, we go looking for the right way to say you&rsquo;re sorry. &nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1545394860000, "publish-at"=>"2018-12-21T07:21:00", "date-line-ts"=>1545394860, "estimated-duration"=>3509, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/12/sorrymedium.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/469/640/l/80/2018/12/sorrymedium.jpg", "h"=>640, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/12/sorrymedium.jpg", "w"=>469, "id"=>180627, "credits-name"=>"Lovelorn Poets"}, "objectID"=>"903879", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Apologetical", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>How do you fix a word that’s broken? A word we need when we bump into someone on the street, or break someone’s heart. In our increasingly disconnected secular world, “sorry” has been stretched and twisted, and in some cases weaponized. But it’s also one of the only ways we have to piece together a sense of shared values and beliefs. Through today's sea of sorry-not-sorries, empty apologies, and just straight up non-apologies, we wonder what it looks like to make amends.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>The program at Stanford that Leilani went through (and now works for) was a joint creation between Stanford and Lee Taft. Find out more here: <a href=\"http://www.stanfordchildrens.org/en/patient-family-resources/pearl\"><strong>www.stanfordchildrens.org/en/patient-family-resources/pearl</strong></a></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was reported by Annie McEwen and was produced by Annie McEwen and Simon Adler. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks to Mark Bressler, Nancy Kielty, and Patty Walters. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em><br></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-12-21T07:21:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"unerased-smid", "title"=>"UnErased: Smid ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18unerasedsmid4.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=898615", "description"=>"<p><span>Today on Radiolab, we're playing the fourth and final episode of a series Jad worked on called UnErased: The history of conversion therapy in America.</span></p>\n<p>Imagine... You’re openly gay. Then, you become the leader of the largest ex-gay organization and, under your leadership, many lives are destroyed. You leave that organization, come out as gay - again - and find love. Do you deserve to be happy? This is a story of identity, making amends and John Smid’s reckoning with his life. </p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1287\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1304\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<p><em>UnErased is a series with Focus Features, Stitcher and Limina House in conjunction with the feature film, BOY ERASED. Special thanks go out to the folks at Anonymous Content for their support of UnErased. </em></p>\n<div><em>If you want to hear the whole series, you can find UnErased in all the usual podcast places. </em></div>\n\n<div><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></div>\n<p><em> </em></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1312\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n<div class=\"story-credits__producing-org-credits producing-org-credits\"></div>\n</div>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"This is a story of identity, making amends and John Smid&rsquo;s reckoning with his life.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1543364280000, "publish-at"=>"2018-11-27T19:18:00", "date-line-ts"=>1543364280, "estimated-duration"=>2980, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/11/unErased4.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1300/740/c/80/2018/11/unErased4.jpg", "h"=>740, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/11/unErased4.jpg", "w"=>1300, "id"=>180045, "credits-name"=>"UnErased"}, "objectID"=>"898615", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"UnErased: Smid ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Today on Radiolab, we're playing the fourth and final episode of a series Jad worked on called UnErased: The history of conversion therapy in America.</span></p>\n<p>Imagine... You’re openly gay. Then, you become the leader of the largest ex-gay organization and, under your leadership, many lives are destroyed. You leave that organization, come out as gay - again - and find love. Do you deserve to be happy? This is a story of identity, making amends and John Smid’s reckoning with his life. </p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1287\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1304\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<p><em>UnErased is a series with Focus Features, Stitcher and Limina House in conjunction with the feature film, BOY ERASED. Special thanks go out to the folks at Anonymous Content for their support of UnErased. </em></p>\n<div><em>If you want to hear the whole series, you can find UnErased in all the usual podcast places. </em></div>\n\n<div><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></div>\n<p><em> </em></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1312\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n<div class=\"story-credits__producing-org-credits producing-org-credits\"></div>\n</div>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-11-27T19:18:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"unerased-davidson-gay-cure", "title"=>"UnErased: Dr. Davison and the Gay Cure ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18unerased1.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=897409", "description"=>"<p><span>Today on Radiolab, we're playing part of a series that Jad worked on called UnErased: The history of conversion therapy in America.</span></p>\n<p><span>The episode we're playing today, the third in the series, is one of the rarest stories of all: a man who publicly experiences a profound change of heart. </span><span>This is a profile of one of the gods of psychotherapy, who through a reckoning with his own work (oddly enough in the pages of </span><span>Playboy </span><span>magazine), becomes the first domino to fall in science’s ultimate disowning of the “gay cure.”</span></p>\n<p><em>UnErased is a series with Focus Features, Stitcher and Limina House in conjunction with the feature film, BOY ERASED. Special thanks go out to the folks at Anonymous Content for their support of UnErased. </em></p>\n<div><em>If you want to hear the whole series, you can find UnErased in all the usual podcast places. </em></div>\n\n<div><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></div>", "tease"=>"A series of&nbsp;serendipitous moments leads to a change of heart in one man that eventually changes an entire field of science.", "publishTime"=>1542861180000, "publish-at"=>"2018-11-21T23:33:00", "date-line-ts"=>1542855600, "estimated-duration"=>2480, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/11/unerased2.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1300/740/c/80/2018/11/unerased2.jpg", "h"=>740, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/11/unerased2.jpg", "w"=>1300, "id"=>179907, "credits-name"=>"UnErased"}, "objectID"=>"897409", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"UnErased: Dr. Davison and the Gay Cure ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Today on Radiolab, we're playing part of a series that Jad worked on called UnErased: The history of conversion therapy in America.</span></p>\n<p><span>The episode we're playing today, the third in the series, is one of the rarest stories of all: a man who publicly experiences a profound change of heart. </span><span>This is a profile of one of the gods of psychotherapy, who through a reckoning with his own work (oddly enough in the pages of </span><span>Playboy </span><span>magazine), becomes the first domino to fall in science’s ultimate disowning of the “gay cure.”</span></p>\n<p><em>UnErased is a series with Focus Features, Stitcher and Limina House in conjunction with the feature film, BOY ERASED. Special thanks go out to the folks at Anonymous Content for their support of UnErased. </em></p>\n<div><em>If you want to hear the whole series, you can find UnErased in all the usual podcast places. </em></div>\n\n<div><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-11-21T23:33:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"front-runner", "title"=>"The Front Runner", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18radiolabrunner2.mp3?aisGetOriginalStream=true&awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=895089", "description"=>"<p>So, a cool thing happened for the show recently. A couple years ago, our episode \"<a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/i-dont-have-answer\">I Don't Have to Answer That</a>\" made it to the ears of director Jason Reitman. The story is about presidential candidate Gary Hart, who, in 1987, was caught in an extramarital affair that forced him to drop out of the race. And at the time, this sort of personal scandal was the first of its kind in politics. It pushed politicians and political reporters into unchartered territory that forever changed the way we scrutinize political figures and judge their fitness for office. When Reitman heard this, he saw a major motion picture in his head. And today, that film, The Front Runner, is out in theaters. Listen to Reitman and his team talk about how Radiolab inspired them to make this movie. </p>\n<p><em>This piece was produced by Jackson Roach.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Film director Jason Reitman and his team talk about how an old Radiolab story inspired his latest movie, The Front Runner.", "publishTime"=>1542074400000, "publish-at"=>"2018-11-12T21:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1542074400, "estimated-duration"=>678, "image-main"=>nil, "objectID"=>"895089", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Front Runner", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>So, a cool thing happened for the show recently. A couple years ago, our episode \"<a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/i-dont-have-answer\">I Don't Have to Answer That</a>\" made it to the ears of director Jason Reitman. The story is about presidential candidate Gary Hart, who, in 1987, was caught in an extramarital affair that forced him to drop out of the race. And at the time, this sort of personal scandal was the first of its kind in politics. It pushed politicians and political reporters into unchartered territory that forever changed the way we scrutinize political figures and judge their fitness for office. When Reitman heard this, he saw a major motion picture in his head. And today, that film, The Front Runner, is out in theaters. Listen to Reitman and his team talk about how Radiolab inspired them to make this movie. </p>\n<p><em>This piece was produced by Jackson Roach.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-11-12T21:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"tweak-vote", "title"=>"Tweak the Vote", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18tweakthevote.mp3?aisGetOriginalStream=true&awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=893784", "description"=>"<p>Democracy is on the ropes.  In the United States and abroad, citizens of democracies are feeling increasingly alienated, disaffected, and powerless.  Some are even asking themselves a question that feels almost too dangerous to say out loud: is democracy fundamentally broken?  </p>\n<p>Today on Radiolab, just a day before the American midterm elections, we ask a different question: how do we fix it?  We scrutinize one proposed tweak to the way we vote that could make politics in this country more representative, more moderate, and most shocking of all, more civil.  Could this one surprisingly do-able mathematical fix really turn political campaigning from a rude bloodsport to a campfire singalong? And even if we could do that, would we want to?</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser, Simon Adler, Sarah Qari, Suzie Lechtenberg and Tracie Hunte, and was produced by Simon Adler, Matt Kielty, Sarah Qari, and Suzie Lechtenberg.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Rob Richie (and everyone else at Fairvote), Don Saari, Diana Leygerman, Caroline Tolbert, Bobby Agee, Edward Still, Jim Blacksher, Allen Caton, Nikolas Bowie, John Hale, and Anna Luhrmann and the rest of the team at the Varieties of Democracy Institute in Sweden.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>\n<p>oh...and GO VOTE!</p>", "tease"=>"On the eve of the midterm elections, we look at one tweak to voting that could help bring democracy back from the brink &hellip;", "publishTime"=>1541396520000, "publish-at"=>"2018-11-05T00:42:00", "date-line-ts"=>1541396520, "estimated-duration"=>4003, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/11/radiolab-democracy2.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/636/481/c/80/2018/11/radiolab-democracy2.jpg", "h"=>481, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/11/radiolab-democracy2.jpg", "w"=>636, "id"=>179498, "credits-name"=>"Curt Merlo"}, "objectID"=>"893784", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Tweak the Vote", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Democracy is on the ropes.  In the United States and abroad, citizens of democracies are feeling increasingly alienated, disaffected, and powerless.  Some are even asking themselves a question that feels almost too dangerous to say out loud: is democracy fundamentally broken?  </p>\n<p>Today on Radiolab, just a day before the American midterm elections, we ask a different question: how do we fix it?  We scrutinize one proposed tweak to the way we vote that could make politics in this country more representative, more moderate, and most shocking of all, more civil.  Could this one surprisingly do-able mathematical fix really turn political campaigning from a rude bloodsport to a campfire singalong? And even if we could do that, would we want to?</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser, Simon Adler, Sarah Qari, Suzie Lechtenberg and Tracie Hunte, and was produced by Simon Adler, Matt Kielty, Sarah Qari, and Suzie Lechtenberg.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Rob Richie (and everyone else at Fairvote), Don Saari, Diana Leygerman, Caroline Tolbert, Bobby Agee, Edward Still, Jim Blacksher, Allen Caton, Nikolas Bowie, John Hale, and Anna Luhrmann and the rest of the team at the Varieties of Democracy Institute in Sweden.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>\n<p>oh...and GO VOTE!</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-11-05T00:42:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"war-worlds", "title"=>"War of the Worlds", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18waroftheworlds.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=892907", "description"=>"<p><span>It's been 80 years to the day since Orson Welles' infamous radio drama \"The War of the Worlds\" echoed far and wide over the airwaves. So we want to bring you back to our very first live hour, where we take a deep dive into what was one of the most controversial moments in broadcasting history. \"The War of the Worlds,\" a radio play about Martians invading New Jersey, caused panic when it originally aired, and it's continued to fool people since--from Santiago, Chile to Buffalo, New York to a particularly disastrous evening in Quito, Ecuador.</span></p>\n<p><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></p>", "tease"=>"For the 80th anniversary of Orson Welles' 1938 radio play \"The War of the Worlds,\" we take a deep dive into one of the most controversial moments in broadcasting history.", "publishTime"=>1540936800000, "publish-at"=>"2018-10-30T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1540936800, "estimated-duration"=>3487, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/10/Mercury-Theatre-Radio-Rehearsal-1938.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1280/782/c/80/2018/10/Mercury-Theatre-Radio-Rehearsal-1938.jpg", "h"=>782, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/10/Mercury-Theatre-Radio-Rehearsal-1938.jpg", "w"=>1280, "id"=>179394, "credits-name"=>"Acme Telephoto"}, "objectID"=>"892907", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"War of the Worlds", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>It's been 80 years to the day since Orson Welles' infamous radio drama \"The War of the Worlds\" echoed far and wide over the airwaves. So we want to bring you back to our very first live hour, where we take a deep dive into what was one of the most controversial moments in broadcasting history. \"The War of the Worlds,\" a radio play about Martians invading New Jersey, caused panic when it originally aired, and it's continued to fool people since--from Santiago, Chile to Buffalo, New York to a particularly disastrous evening in Quito, Ecuador.</span></p>\n<p><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-10-30T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"no-part-3", "title"=>"In the No Part 3", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18intheno3.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=891906", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>In the final episode of our “In The No” series, we sat down with several different groups of college-age women to talk about their sexual experiences. And we found that despite colleges now being steeped in conversations about consent, there was another conversation in intimate moments that just wasn't happening. In search of a script, we dive into the details of BDSM negotiations and are left wondering if all of this talk about consent is ignoring a larger problem.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Further reading:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/socf.12460\">\"It's all about the Journey\": Skepticism and Spirituality in the BDSM Subculture</a>, by Julie Fennell</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><a href=\"https://www.ucpress.edu/book/9780520295414/screw-consent\">Screw Consent</a>, </em>by Joe Fischel</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was reported by Becca Bressler and Shima Oliaee, and was produced by Bethel Habte.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks to Ray Matienzo, Janet Hardy, Jay Wiseman, Peter Tupper, Susan Wright, and Dominus Eros of Pagan's Paradise. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "tease"=>"From hookups to apps to whips, we go looking for the key to a conversation that should be happening, but isn't.", "publishTime"=>1540519560000, "publish-at"=>"2018-10-25T22:06:00", "date-line-ts"=>1540519560, "estimated-duration"=>1638, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/10/181010_Turett_InTheNo_Part3.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1350/900/l/80/2018/10/181010_Turett_InTheNo_Part3.jpg", "h"=>900, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Illustration by Cara Turett", "credits-url"=>"https://unsplash.com/photos/KPalS49Ny34?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/10/181010_Turett_InTheNo_Part3.jpg", "w"=>1350, "id"=>179285, "credits-name"=>" Photo by Justine Camacho on Unsplash"}, "objectID"=>"891906", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"In the No Part 3", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>In the final episode of our “In The No” series, we sat down with several different groups of college-age women to talk about their sexual experiences. And we found that despite colleges now being steeped in conversations about consent, there was another conversation in intimate moments that just wasn't happening. In search of a script, we dive into the details of BDSM negotiations and are left wondering if all of this talk about consent is ignoring a larger problem.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Further reading:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/socf.12460\">\"It's all about the Journey\": Skepticism and Spirituality in the BDSM Subculture</a>, by Julie Fennell</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><a href=\"https://www.ucpress.edu/book/9780520295414/screw-consent\">Screw Consent</a>, </em>by Joe Fischel</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was reported by Becca Bressler and Shima Oliaee, and was produced by Bethel Habte.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks to Ray Matienzo, Janet Hardy, Jay Wiseman, Peter Tupper, Susan Wright, and Dominus Eros of Pagan's Paradise. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-10-25T22:06:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"no-part-2", "title"=>"In the No Part 2", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18intheno2.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=890393", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>In the year since accusations of sexual assault were first brought against Harvey Weinstein, our news has been flooded with stories of sexual misconduct, indicting very visible figures in our public life. Most of these cases have involved unequivocal breaches of consent, some of which have been criminal. But what have also emerged are conversations surrounding more difficult situations to parse </span><span>–</span><span> </span><span>ones that exist in a much grayer space. When we started our own reporting through this gray zone, we stumbled into a challenging conversation that we can’t stop thinking about. In this second episode of ‘In the No’, radio-maker Kaitlin Prest joins us for a conversation with Hanna Stotland, an educational consultant who specializes in crisis management. Her clients include students who have been expelled from school for sexual misconduct. In the aftermath, Hanna helps them reapply to school. While Hanna shares some of her more nuanced and confusing cases, we wrestle with questions of culpability, generational divides, and the utility of fear in changing our culture.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p3\"><span><em>Advisory: </em></span><span><em>This episode contains some graphic language and descriptions of very sensitive sexual situations, including discussions of sexual assault, consent and accountability, which may be very difficult for people to listen to. Visit The National Sexual Assault Hotline at online.rainn.org for resources and support.</em></span><span> </span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span><em>This episode was reported with help from Becca Bressler and Shima Oliaee, and produced with help from Rachael Cusick. </em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Special thanks to <em>Ben Burke and Jackson Prince.</em></em></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "tease"=>"We dive into the gray zone of consent and&nbsp;wrestle with questions of culpability, generational divides, and the utility of fear in changing our culture.", "publishTime"=>1539921600000, "publish-at"=>"2018-10-19T00:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1539921600, "estimated-duration"=>2340, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/10/181010_Turett_InTheNo_Part2.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1350/900/c/80/2018/10/181010_Turett_InTheNo_Part2.jpg", "h"=>900, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Illustration by Cara Turett", "credits-url"=>"https://unsplash.com/photos/KPalS49Ny34?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/10/181010_Turett_InTheNo_Part2.jpg", "w"=>1350, "id"=>179101, "credits-name"=>" Photo by Justine Camacho on Unsplash "}, "objectID"=>"890393", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"In the No Part 2", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>In the year since accusations of sexual assault were first brought against Harvey Weinstein, our news has been flooded with stories of sexual misconduct, indicting very visible figures in our public life. Most of these cases have involved unequivocal breaches of consent, some of which have been criminal. But what have also emerged are conversations surrounding more difficult situations to parse </span><span>–</span><span> </span><span>ones that exist in a much grayer space. When we started our own reporting through this gray zone, we stumbled into a challenging conversation that we can’t stop thinking about. In this second episode of ‘In the No’, radio-maker Kaitlin Prest joins us for a conversation with Hanna Stotland, an educational consultant who specializes in crisis management. Her clients include students who have been expelled from school for sexual misconduct. In the aftermath, Hanna helps them reapply to school. While Hanna shares some of her more nuanced and confusing cases, we wrestle with questions of culpability, generational divides, and the utility of fear in changing our culture.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p3\"><span><em>Advisory: </em></span><span><em>This episode contains some graphic language and descriptions of very sensitive sexual situations, including discussions of sexual assault, consent and accountability, which may be very difficult for people to listen to. Visit The National Sexual Assault Hotline at online.rainn.org for resources and support.</em></span><span> </span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span><em>This episode was reported with help from Becca Bressler and Shima Oliaee, and produced with help from Rachael Cusick. </em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Special thanks to <em>Ben Burke and Jackson Prince.</em></em></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-10-19T00:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"no-part-1", "title"=>"In the No Part 1", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18intheno1.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=888838", "description"=>"<p>In 2017, radio-maker Kaitlin Prest released a mini-series called \"No\" about her personal struggle to understand and communicate about sexual consent. That show, which dives into the experience, moment by moment, of navigating sexual intimacy, struck a chord with many of us. It's gorgeous, deeply personal, and incredibly thoughtful. And it seemed to presage a much larger conversation that is happening all around us in this moment. And so we decided to embark, with Kaitlin, on our own exploration of this topic. Over the next three episodes, we'll wander into rooms full of college students, hear from academics and activists, and sit in on classes about BDSM. But to start things off, we are going to share with you the story that started it all. Today, meet Kaitlin (if you haven't already). </p>\n<p><em>In The No Part 1 is a collaboration with Kaitlin Prest. It was produced with help from Becca Bressler and Shima Oliaee. </em></p>\n<p><em>The \"No\" series, from The Heart was created by writer/director Kaitlin Prest, editors Sharon Mashihi and Mitra Kaboli, assistant producer Ariel Hahn and associate producer Phoebe Wang, associate sound designer Shani Aviram. Special thanks to actor Tommy Schell.</em></p>\n<p><em>Check out Kaitlin's new show, <a href=\"https://itunes.apple.com/us/podcast/the-shadows/id1420121326?mt=2\">The Shadows</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "tease"=>"Kaitlin Prest had a lot of things to say about the word \"No.\" And on her podcast \"The Heart,\" she said them in a way we couldn't shake. Today, we talk to&nbsp;Kaitlin, and hear her story.", "publishTime"=>1539295200000, "publish-at"=>"2018-10-11T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1539295200, "estimated-duration"=>3345, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/10/181010_Turett_InTheNo_Part1.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1350/900/c/80/2018/10/181010_Turett_InTheNo_Part1.jpg", "h"=>900, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Illustration by Cara Turett", "credits-url"=>"https://unsplash.com/photos/KPalS49Ny34?utm_source=unsplash&utm_medium=referral&utm_content=creditCopyText", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/10/181010_Turett_InTheNo_Part1.jpg", "w"=>1350, "id"=>178935, "credits-name"=>"Photo by Justine Camacho on Unsplash"}, "objectID"=>"888838", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"In the No Part 1", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In 2017, radio-maker Kaitlin Prest released a mini-series called \"No\" about her personal struggle to understand and communicate about sexual consent. That show, which dives into the experience, moment by moment, of navigating sexual intimacy, struck a chord with many of us. It's gorgeous, deeply personal, and incredibly thoughtful. And it seemed to presage a much larger conversation that is happening all around us in this moment. And so we decided to embark, with Kaitlin, on our own exploration of this topic. Over the next three episodes, we'll wander into rooms full of college students, hear from academics and activists, and sit in on classes about BDSM. But to start things off, we are going to share with you the story that started it all. Today, meet Kaitlin (if you haven't already). </p>\n<p><em>In The No Part 1 is a collaboration with Kaitlin Prest. It was produced with help from Becca Bressler and Shima Oliaee. </em></p>\n<p><em>The \"No\" series, from The Heart was created by writer/director Kaitlin Prest, editors Sharon Mashihi and Mitra Kaboli, assistant producer Ariel Hahn and associate producer Phoebe Wang, associate sound designer Shani Aviram. Special thanks to actor Tommy Schell.</em></p>\n<p><em>Check out Kaitlin's new show, <a href=\"https://itunes.apple.com/us/podcast/the-shadows/id1420121326?mt=2\">The Shadows</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-10-11T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"breaking-bad-news-bears", "title"=>"Breaking Bad News Bears", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18breakingbadnews.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=885942", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Today, a challenge: bear with us.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">We decided to shake things up at the show so we threw our staff a curveball, Walter Matthau-style. In two weeks time we told our producers to pitch, report, and produce stories about breaking news….or bears. What emerged was a sort of love letter for our honey-loving friends and a discovery that they embody so much more than we could have imagined: a town’s symbol for hope, a celebrity, a foe, and a clue to future ways we’ll deal with our changing environment. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>This episode was reported and produced by Simon Adler, Molly Webster, Bethel Habte, Pat Walters, Matt Kielty, Rachael Cusick, Annie McEwen and Latif Nasser.</em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks to Wendy Card, Marlene Zuk, Karyn Rode, Barbara Nielsen and Steven Amstrup at Polar Bears International, Jimmy Thomson, Adam Kudlak, Greg Durner, Todd Atwood, and Dawn Curtis and the Environment and Natural Resources Department of Northwest Territories.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>And thanks to composer Anthony Plog for allowing us to use the Fourth Movement of his \"Fantasy Movement,\" \"Very Fast and Manic,\" performed by Eufonix Quartet off of their album <a href=\"http://www.potenzamusic.com/nuclear-breakfast-134347.cfm\">Nuclear Breakfast</a>, available from <a href=\"http://www.potenzamusic.com/index.cfm/do/site.home\">Potenza Music</a>. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "tease"=>"We&nbsp;decided to shake things up at the show...bear with us.", "publishTime"=>1538128800000, "publish-at"=>"2018-09-28T06:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1538128800, "estimated-duration"=>3717, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/09/BNBphoto.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/600/l/80/2018/09/BNBphoto.jpg", "h"=>600, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/09/BNBphoto.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>178645, "credits-name"=>"Cara Turett"}, "objectID"=>"885942", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Breaking Bad News Bears", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Today, a challenge: bear with us.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">We decided to shake things up at the show so we threw our staff a curveball, Walter Matthau-style. In two weeks time we told our producers to pitch, report, and produce stories about breaking news….or bears. What emerged was a sort of love letter for our honey-loving friends and a discovery that they embody so much more than we could have imagined: a town’s symbol for hope, a celebrity, a foe, and a clue to future ways we’ll deal with our changing environment. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>This episode was reported and produced by Simon Adler, Molly Webster, Bethel Habte, Pat Walters, Matt Kielty, Rachael Cusick, Annie McEwen and Latif Nasser.</em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks to Wendy Card, Marlene Zuk, Karyn Rode, Barbara Nielsen and Steven Amstrup at Polar Bears International, Jimmy Thomson, Adam Kudlak, Greg Durner, Todd Atwood, and Dawn Curtis and the Environment and Natural Resources Department of Northwest Territories.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>And thanks to composer Anthony Plog for allowing us to use the Fourth Movement of his \"Fantasy Movement,\" \"Very Fast and Manic,\" performed by Eufonix Quartet off of their album <a href=\"http://www.potenzamusic.com/nuclear-breakfast-134347.cfm\">Nuclear Breakfast</a>, available from <a href=\"http://www.potenzamusic.com/index.cfm/do/site.home\">Potenza Music</a>. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-09-28T06:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"infective-heredity", "title"=>"Infective Heredity", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18infectiveheredity.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=884449", "description"=>"<p>Today, a fast moving, sidestepping, gene-swapping free-for-all that would’ve made Darwin’s head spin.</p>\n<p>David Quammen tells us about a shocking way that life can evolve - infective heredity. To figure it all out we go back to the earliest versions of life, and we revisit an earlier version of Radiolab. After reckoning with a scientific icon, we find ourselves in a tangle of genes that sheds new light on peppered moths, drug-resistant bugs, and a key moment in the evolution of life when mammals went a little viral.</p>\n<p>Check out David Quammen's book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Tangled-Tree-Radical-History-Life/dp/1476776628\">The Tangled Tree: A Radical New History of Life</a> </p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Soren Wheeler. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "tease"=>"Today, a fast moving, sidestepping, gene-swapping free-for-all that would&rsquo;ve made Darwin&rsquo;s head spin.", "publishTime"=>1537498800000, "publish-at"=>"2018-09-20T23:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1537498800, "estimated-duration"=>1647, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/09/8616064981_a3dcd76d65_z.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/481/l/80/2018/09/8616064981_a3dcd76d65_z.jpg", "h"=>481, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.flickr.com/photos/nribeiro/8616064981/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/09/8616064981_a3dcd76d65_z.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>178451, "credits-name"=>"Nuno Ribeiro"}, "objectID"=>"884449", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Infective Heredity", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Today, a fast moving, sidestepping, gene-swapping free-for-all that would’ve made Darwin’s head spin.</p>\n<p>David Quammen tells us about a shocking way that life can evolve - infective heredity. To figure it all out we go back to the earliest versions of life, and we revisit an earlier version of Radiolab. After reckoning with a scientific icon, we find ourselves in a tangle of genes that sheds new light on peppered moths, drug-resistant bugs, and a key moment in the evolution of life when mammals went a little viral.</p>\n<p>Check out David Quammen's book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Tangled-Tree-Radical-History-Life/dp/1476776628\">The Tangled Tree: A Radical New History of Life</a> </p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Soren Wheeler. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-09-20T23:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"27-most-perfect-album", "title"=>"27: The Most Perfect Album", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18mostperfectalbum.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=883840", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>More Perfect is back with something totally new and exciting. They just dropped an </span>ALBUM. <a href=\"https://project.wnyc.org/themostperfectalbum/\">27: The Most Perfect Album</a> is like a Constitutional mix-tape, a Schoolhouse Rock for the 21st century. The album features original tracks by artists like Dolly Parton, Kash Doll, and Devendra Banhart: 27+ songs inspired by the 27 Amendments. Alongside the album they'll be releasing short stories deep-diving into each amendment's history and resonance. In this episode, we preview a few songs and dive into the poetic dream behind the First Amendment. The whole album, plus the <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/most-perfect-album-episode-one-first-second-third-amendments\">first episode</a> of More Perfect Season 3 is out now.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "tease"=>"More Perfect is back with a Constitutional mix-tape and off-beat stories about each of the 27 Amendments.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1537329600000, "publish-at"=>"2018-09-19T00:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1537329600, "estimated-duration"=>1160, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/09/MorePerfect_MostPerfectAlbum_27_Amendments.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1860/c/80/2018/09/MorePerfect_MostPerfectAlbum_27_Amendments.jpg", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/09/MorePerfect_MostPerfectAlbum_27_Amendments.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>178391, "credits-name"=>"Adalis Martinez"}, "objectID"=>"883840", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"27: The Most Perfect Album", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>More Perfect is back with something totally new and exciting. They just dropped an </span>ALBUM. <a href=\"https://project.wnyc.org/themostperfectalbum/\">27: The Most Perfect Album</a> is like a Constitutional mix-tape, a Schoolhouse Rock for the 21st century. The album features original tracks by artists like Dolly Parton, Kash Doll, and Devendra Banhart: 27+ songs inspired by the 27 Amendments. Alongside the album they'll be releasing short stories deep-diving into each amendment's history and resonance. In this episode, we preview a few songs and dive into the poetic dream behind the First Amendment. The whole album, plus the <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/most-perfect-album-episode-one-first-second-third-amendments\">first episode</a> of More Perfect Season 3 is out now.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-09-19T00:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"post-no-evil", "title"=>"Post No Evil", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18postnoevil.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=876785", "description"=>"<p>Back in 2008 Facebook began writing a document. It was a constitution of sorts, laying out what could and what couldn’t be posted on the site. Back then, the rules were simple, outlawing nudity and gore. Today, they’re anything but. </p>\n<p>How do you define hate speech? Where’s the line between a joke and an attack? How much butt is too much butt? Facebook has answered these questions. And from these answers they’ve written a rulebook that all 2.2 billion of us are expected to follow. Today, we explore that rulebook. We dive into its details and untangle its logic. All the while wondering what does this mean for the future of free speech?</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Simon Adler with help from Tracie Hunte and was produced by Simon Adler with help from Bethel Habte.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Sarah Roberts, Jeffrey Rosen, Carolyn Glanville, Ruchika Budhraja, Brian Dogan, Ellen Silver, James Mitchell, Guy Rosen, and our voice actor Michael Chernus.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Breastfeeding, beheadings and bombings, Facebook has rules to handle them all. Today, we explore those rules and ask what they tell us about the future of free speech. &nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1534504800000, "publish-at"=>"2018-08-17T07:20:00", "date-line-ts"=>1534504800, "estimated-duration"=>4112, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/08/Facebookimage.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1035/c/80/2018/08/Facebookimage.png", "h"=>1035, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/08/Facebookimage.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>177734, "credits-name"=>"Simon Adler"}, "objectID"=>"876785", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Post No Evil", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Back in 2008 Facebook began writing a document. It was a constitution of sorts, laying out what could and what couldn’t be posted on the site. Back then, the rules were simple, outlawing nudity and gore. Today, they’re anything but. </p>\n<p>How do you define hate speech? Where’s the line between a joke and an attack? How much butt is too much butt? Facebook has answered these questions. And from these answers they’ve written a rulebook that all 2.2 billion of us are expected to follow. Today, we explore that rulebook. We dive into its details and untangle its logic. All the while wondering what does this mean for the future of free speech?</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Simon Adler with help from Tracie Hunte and was produced by Simon Adler with help from Bethel Habte.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Sarah Roberts, Jeffrey Rosen, Carolyn Glanville, Ruchika Budhraja, Brian Dogan, Ellen Silver, James Mitchell, Guy Rosen, and our voice actor Michael Chernus.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-08-17T07:20:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"bad-show", "title"=>"The Bad Show", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18badshow.mp3?aisGetOriginalStream=true&awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=871961", "description"=>"<p>With all of the black-and-white moralizing in our world today, we decided to bring back an old show about the little bit of bad that's in all of us...and the little bit of really, <em>really </em>bad that's in some of us.  </p>\n<p>Cruelty, violence, badness... in this episode we begin with a chilling statistic: 91% of men, and 84% of women, have fantasized about killing someone. We take a look at one particular fantasy lurking behind these numbers, and wonder what this shadow world might tell us about ourselves and our neighbors. Then, we reconsider what Stanley Milgram's famous experiment really revealed about human nature (it's both better and worse than we thought). Next, we meet a man who scrambles our notions of good and evil: chemist Fritz Haber, who won a Nobel Prize in 1918...around the same time officials in the US were calling him a war criminal. And we end with the story of a man who chased one of the most prolific serial killers in US history, then got a chance to ask him the question that had haunted him for years: why?</p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced with help from Carter Hodge.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"We wrestle with the dark side of human nature, and ask whether it's something we can ever really understand, or fully escape.", "publishTime"=>1532739600000, "publish-at"=>"2018-07-27T21:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1532739600, "estimated-duration"=>4155, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"The Bad Show", "name"=>"photologue/photos/badshow_reupload.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"WNYC"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/620/277/c/80/photologue/photos/badshow_reupload.jpg", "h"=>277, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/badshow_reupload.jpg", "w"=>620, "id"=>67638, "credits-name"=>"Adam Cole"}, "objectID"=>"871961", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Bad Show", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>With all of the black-and-white moralizing in our world today, we decided to bring back an old show about the little bit of bad that's in all of us...and the little bit of really, <em>really </em>bad that's in some of us.  </p>\n<p>Cruelty, violence, badness... in this episode we begin with a chilling statistic: 91% of men, and 84% of women, have fantasized about killing someone. We take a look at one particular fantasy lurking behind these numbers, and wonder what this shadow world might tell us about ourselves and our neighbors. Then, we reconsider what Stanley Milgram's famous experiment really revealed about human nature (it's both better and worse than we thought). Next, we meet a man who scrambles our notions of good and evil: chemist Fritz Haber, who won a Nobel Prize in 1918...around the same time officials in the US were calling him a war criminal. And we end with the story of a man who chased one of the most prolific serial killers in US history, then got a chance to ask him the question that had haunted him for years: why?</p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced with help from Carter Hodge.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-07-27T21:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"sex-ed", "title"=>"Gonads: Sex Ed", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18sexed.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=870978", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>If there’s one thing Gonads taught us, it’s just how complicated human reproduction is. All the things we thought we knew about biology and sex determination are up for debate in a way that feels both daunting and full of potential. At the same time, we're at a moment where we’re wrestling with how to approach conversations around sex, consent, and boundaries, at a time that may be more divisive than ever. So host Molly Webster thought: what if we took on sex ed, and tried to tackle questions from listeners, youth, reddit (oh boy), and staff.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">But instead of approaching these questions the way your high school health teacher might’ve (or government teacher, who knows), Molly invited a cast of storytellers, educators, artists, and comedians to grapple with sex ed in unexpected and thoughtful ways. To help us think about how we can change the conversation. In this episode, an edited down version of a Gonads Live show, Molly's team takes a crack at responding to the intimate questions you asked when you were younger but probably never got a straight answer to. Featuring:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><strong>How Do You Talk About Condoms Without Condom Demonstrations?</strong> Sanford Johnson. Wanna see how to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=06kT9yfj7QE\">put on a sock</a>?</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><strong>What Are Periods?</strong> Sindha Agha and Gul Agha. Check out Sindha's photography <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/gonads-presents-sindha-agha/?token=c1522cc189aba26b95e7ead97eb15588&amp;content_type_id=26&amp;object_id=871232&amp;_=e09f8a73\">here</a>.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Is Anything Off-Limits?</strong> Ericka Hart, Dalia Mahgoub, and Jonathan Zimmerman </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><strong>Why Do We Do This Anyway? And Other Queries from Fifth Graders</strong> Jo Firestone</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>\"Sex Ed\" is an edited* recording of a live event hosted by Radiolab at the Skirball Center in New York City on May 16, 2018. Radiolab Team Gonads is Molly Webster, Pat Walters, and Rachael Cusick, with Jad Abumrad. Live music, including the sex ed questions, and the Gonads theme song, were written, performed, and produced by Majel Connery and Alex Overington. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>One more thing! </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Over the past few months, Radiolab has been collecting sex ed book suggestions from listeners and staff, about the books that helped them understand the birds and the bees.</p>\n<p>Check out the full Gonads Presents: Sex Ed Bookshelf <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/gonads-presents-sex-ed-bookshelf/\">here</a>! For now, a few of our favorites:</p>\n<div id=\"gr_grid_widget_1532374115\"><!-- Show static html as a placeholder in case js is not enabled - javascript include will override this if things work -->\n<div class=\"gr_grid_container\">\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"It's Perfectly Normal: A Book about Changing Bodies, Growing Up, Sex, and Sexual Health\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/222507.It_s_Perfectly_Normal\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1388191208m/222507.jpg\" alt=\"It's Perfectly Normal: A Book about Changing Bodies, Growing Up, Sex, and Sexual Health\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"Are You There God? It's Me, Margaret\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/37732.Are_You_There_God_It_s_Me_Margaret\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1388356524m/37732.jpg\" alt=\"Are You There God? It's Me, Margaret\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"Our Bodies, Ourselves for the New Century\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/97869.Our_Bodies_Ourselves_for_the_New_Century\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1392953859m/97869.jpg\" alt=\"Our Bodies, Ourselves for the New Century\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"The Ultimate Guide to Sex and Disability: For All of Us Who Live with Disabilities, Chronic Pain, and Illness\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/967095.The_Ultimate_Guide_to_Sex_and_Disability\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1179874304m/967095.jpg\" alt=\"The Ultimate Guide to Sex and Disability: For All of Us Who Live with Disabilities, Chronic Pain, and Illness\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"Guide to Getting It On!\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/72834.Guide_to_Getting_It_On_\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1386924754m/72834.jpg\" alt=\"Guide to Getting It On!\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"I Am Jazz\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/18763344-i-am-jazz\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1392585999m/18763344.jpg\" alt=\"I Am Jazz\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"The Boy &amp; the Bindi\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/28818768-the-boy-the-bindi\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1461594895m/28818768.jpg\" alt=\"The Boy &amp; the Bindi\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"Dr. Tatiana's Sex Advice to All Creation\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/91842.Dr_Tatiana_s_Sex_Advice_to_All_Creation\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1171236134m/91842.jpg\" alt=\"Dr. Tatiana's Sex Advice to All Creation\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"What Makes a Baby\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/15701778-what-makes-a-baby\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1340338597m/15701778.jpg\" alt=\"What Makes a Baby\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"Where Did I Come From?\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/98678.Where_Did_I_Come_From_\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1347744548m/98678.jpg\" alt=\"Where Did I Come From?\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<noscript><br>Share <a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/\">book reviews</a> and ratings with Radiolab, and even join a <a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/group\">book club</a> on Goodreads.</noscript></div>\n</div>\n<script type=\"text/javascript\" src=\"https://www.goodreads.com/review/grid_widget/83213904.Radiolab's%20sex-ed-the-short-list%20book%20montage?cover_size=medium&amp;hide_link=true&amp;hide_title=true&amp;num_books=20&amp;order=a&amp;shelf=sex-ed-the-short-list&amp;sort=position&amp;widget_id=1532374115\"></script>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>*Our live show featured the following additional questions and answerers:</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>How do you talk to your partner in bed without sound like an asshold or a slut? Upright Citizens Brigade, featuring Lou Gonzales, Molly Thomas, and Alexandra Dickson</em></p>\n<p><em>What Happens to All the Condom Bananas? Rachael Cusick</em></p>\n<p><em>With live event production help from Melissa LaCasse and Alicia Allen; engineering by Ed Haber and George Wellington; and balloons by Candy Brigham from Candy Twisted Balloons </em><em>Special. Special thanks to Larry Siegel, Upright Citizens Brigade, and Emily Rothman and the Start Strong Initiative at the Boston Public Health Commission. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Radiolab is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science. And the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation, enhancing public understanding of science and technology in the modern world. More information about Sloan at www.sloan.org.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em> </p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember895\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember912\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"If gonads are magical, and male and female are blurry&nbsp;categories, how the hell do you tell young&nbsp;people&nbsp;about the birds and the bees?", "publishTime"=>1532666460000, "publish-at"=>"2018-07-27T00:41:00", "date-line-ts"=>1532666460, "estimated-duration"=>2891, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/07/JasuHu_ep6.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2018/07/JasuHu_ep6.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/07/JasuHu_ep6.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>177191, "credits-name"=>"Jasu Hu"}, "objectID"=>"870978", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Gonads: Sex Ed", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>If there’s one thing Gonads taught us, it’s just how complicated human reproduction is. All the things we thought we knew about biology and sex determination are up for debate in a way that feels both daunting and full of potential. At the same time, we're at a moment where we’re wrestling with how to approach conversations around sex, consent, and boundaries, at a time that may be more divisive than ever. So host Molly Webster thought: what if we took on sex ed, and tried to tackle questions from listeners, youth, reddit (oh boy), and staff.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">But instead of approaching these questions the way your high school health teacher might’ve (or government teacher, who knows), Molly invited a cast of storytellers, educators, artists, and comedians to grapple with sex ed in unexpected and thoughtful ways. To help us think about how we can change the conversation. In this episode, an edited down version of a Gonads Live show, Molly's team takes a crack at responding to the intimate questions you asked when you were younger but probably never got a straight answer to. Featuring:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><strong>How Do You Talk About Condoms Without Condom Demonstrations?</strong> Sanford Johnson. Wanna see how to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=06kT9yfj7QE\">put on a sock</a>?</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><strong>What Are Periods?</strong> Sindha Agha and Gul Agha. Check out Sindha's photography <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/gonads-presents-sindha-agha/?token=c1522cc189aba26b95e7ead97eb15588&amp;content_type_id=26&amp;object_id=871232&amp;_=e09f8a73\">here</a>.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Is Anything Off-Limits?</strong> Ericka Hart, Dalia Mahgoub, and Jonathan Zimmerman </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><strong>Why Do We Do This Anyway? And Other Queries from Fifth Graders</strong> Jo Firestone</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>\"Sex Ed\" is an edited* recording of a live event hosted by Radiolab at the Skirball Center in New York City on May 16, 2018. Radiolab Team Gonads is Molly Webster, Pat Walters, and Rachael Cusick, with Jad Abumrad. Live music, including the sex ed questions, and the Gonads theme song, were written, performed, and produced by Majel Connery and Alex Overington. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong>One more thing! </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Over the past few months, Radiolab has been collecting sex ed book suggestions from listeners and staff, about the books that helped them understand the birds and the bees.</p>\n<p>Check out the full Gonads Presents: Sex Ed Bookshelf <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/gonads-presents-sex-ed-bookshelf/\">here</a>! For now, a few of our favorites:</p>\n<div id=\"gr_grid_widget_1532374115\"><!-- Show static html as a placeholder in case js is not enabled - javascript include will override this if things work -->\n<div class=\"gr_grid_container\">\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"It's Perfectly Normal: A Book about Changing Bodies, Growing Up, Sex, and Sexual Health\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/222507.It_s_Perfectly_Normal\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1388191208m/222507.jpg\" alt=\"It's Perfectly Normal: A Book about Changing Bodies, Growing Up, Sex, and Sexual Health\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"Are You There God? It's Me, Margaret\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/37732.Are_You_There_God_It_s_Me_Margaret\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1388356524m/37732.jpg\" alt=\"Are You There God? It's Me, Margaret\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"Our Bodies, Ourselves for the New Century\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/97869.Our_Bodies_Ourselves_for_the_New_Century\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1392953859m/97869.jpg\" alt=\"Our Bodies, Ourselves for the New Century\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"The Ultimate Guide to Sex and Disability: For All of Us Who Live with Disabilities, Chronic Pain, and Illness\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/967095.The_Ultimate_Guide_to_Sex_and_Disability\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1179874304m/967095.jpg\" alt=\"The Ultimate Guide to Sex and Disability: For All of Us Who Live with Disabilities, Chronic Pain, and Illness\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"Guide to Getting It On!\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/72834.Guide_to_Getting_It_On_\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1386924754m/72834.jpg\" alt=\"Guide to Getting It On!\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"I Am Jazz\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/18763344-i-am-jazz\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1392585999m/18763344.jpg\" alt=\"I Am Jazz\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"The Boy &amp; the Bindi\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/28818768-the-boy-the-bindi\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1461594895m/28818768.jpg\" alt=\"The Boy &amp; the Bindi\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"Dr. Tatiana's Sex Advice to All Creation\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/91842.Dr_Tatiana_s_Sex_Advice_to_All_Creation\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1171236134m/91842.jpg\" alt=\"Dr. Tatiana's Sex Advice to All Creation\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"What Makes a Baby\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/15701778-what-makes-a-baby\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1340338597m/15701778.jpg\" alt=\"What Makes a Baby\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<div class=\"gr_grid_book_container\"><a title=\"Where Did I Come From?\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/98678.Where_Did_I_Come_From_\"><img src=\"https://images.gr-assets.com/books/1347744548m/98678.jpg\" alt=\"Where Did I Come From?\" border=\"0\"></a></div>\n<noscript><br>Share <a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/\">book reviews</a> and ratings with Radiolab, and even join a <a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/group\">book club</a> on Goodreads.</noscript></div>\n</div>\n<script type=\"text/javascript\" src=\"https://www.goodreads.com/review/grid_widget/83213904.Radiolab's%20sex-ed-the-short-list%20book%20montage?cover_size=medium&amp;hide_link=true&amp;hide_title=true&amp;num_books=20&amp;order=a&amp;shelf=sex-ed-the-short-list&amp;sort=position&amp;widget_id=1532374115\"></script>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>*Our live show featured the following additional questions and answerers:</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>How do you talk to your partner in bed without sound like an asshold or a slut? Upright Citizens Brigade, featuring Lou Gonzales, Molly Thomas, and Alexandra Dickson</em></p>\n<p><em>What Happens to All the Condom Bananas? Rachael Cusick</em></p>\n<p><em>With live event production help from Melissa LaCasse and Alicia Allen; engineering by Ed Haber and George Wellington; and balloons by Candy Brigham from Candy Twisted Balloons </em><em>Special. Special thanks to Larry Siegel, Upright Citizens Brigade, and Emily Rothman and the Start Strong Initiative at the Boston Public Health Commission. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Radiolab is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science. And the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation, enhancing public understanding of science and technology in the modern world. More information about Sloan at www.sloan.org.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em> </p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember895\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember912\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<p> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-07-27T00:41:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dana", "title"=>"Gonads: Dana", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18dana.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=870129", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">When Dana Zzyym applied for their first passport back in 2014, they were handed a pretty straightforward application. Name, place of birth, photo ID -- the usual. But one question on the application stopped Dana in their tracks: male or female? Dana, technically, wasn’t either.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In this episode, we follow the story of Dana Zzyym, Navy veteran and activist, which starts long before they scribble the word \"intersex” on their passport application. Along the way, we see what happens when our inner biological realities bump into the outside world, and the power of words to shape us.</p>\n<p>This episode is a companion piece to Gonads, Episode 4, <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/dutee/?token=3b43528f14699fa0de7a4032dcaa31ba&amp;content_type_id=26&amp;object_id=870128&amp;_=66bc7662\">Dutee</a>.</p>\n<p><em>\"Dana\" was reported by Molly Webster, and co-produced with Jad Abumrad. It had production help from Rachael Cusick, and editing by Pat Walters. Wordplay categories were written, performed, and produced by Majel Connery and Alex Overington. </em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Paula Stone Williams, Gerry Callahan, Lambda Legal, Kathy Tu, Matt Collette, Arianne Wack, Carter Hodge, and Liza Yeager.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science. And the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation, enhancing public understanding of science and technology in the modern world. More information about Sloan at www.sloan.org.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em> </p>", "tease"=>"When Dana Zzyym applied for their first passport in 2014, there was one question they couldn&rsquo;t answer: male or female? This episode,&nbsp;biological realities and&nbsp;the power of words.", "publishTime"=>1532235360000, "publish-at"=>"2018-07-22T00:56:00", "date-line-ts"=>1532235360, "estimated-duration"=>1658, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/07/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHuDana.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2018/07/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHuDana.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/07/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHuDana.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>177024, "credits-name"=>"Jasu Hu"}, "objectID"=>"870129", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Gonads: Dana", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">When Dana Zzyym applied for their first passport back in 2014, they were handed a pretty straightforward application. Name, place of birth, photo ID -- the usual. But one question on the application stopped Dana in their tracks: male or female? Dana, technically, wasn’t either.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In this episode, we follow the story of Dana Zzyym, Navy veteran and activist, which starts long before they scribble the word \"intersex” on their passport application. Along the way, we see what happens when our inner biological realities bump into the outside world, and the power of words to shape us.</p>\n<p>This episode is a companion piece to Gonads, Episode 4, <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/dutee/?token=3b43528f14699fa0de7a4032dcaa31ba&amp;content_type_id=26&amp;object_id=870128&amp;_=66bc7662\">Dutee</a>.</p>\n<p><em>\"Dana\" was reported by Molly Webster, and co-produced with Jad Abumrad. It had production help from Rachael Cusick, and editing by Pat Walters. Wordplay categories were written, performed, and produced by Majel Connery and Alex Overington. </em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Paula Stone Williams, Gerry Callahan, Lambda Legal, Kathy Tu, Matt Collette, Arianne Wack, Carter Hodge, and Liza Yeager.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science. And the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation, enhancing public understanding of science and technology in the modern world. More information about Sloan at www.sloan.org.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-07-22T00:56:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"gonads-xy", "title"=>"Gonads: X & Y", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18x_y_.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=866183", "description"=>"<p>A lot of us understand biological sex with a pretty fateful underpinning: if you’re born with XX chromosomes, you’re female; if you’re born with XY chromosomes, you’re male. But it turns out, our relationship to the opposite sex is more complicated than we think.</p>\n<p>And if you caught this show on-air, and would like to listen to the full version of our Sex Ed Live Show, you can check it out <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/sex-ed\">here</a>. </p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Molly Webster, and produced by Matt Kielty. With scoring, original composition and mixing by Matt Kielty and Alex Overington. Additional production by Rachael Cusick, and editing by Pat Walters. The “Ballad of Daniel Webster” and “Gonads” was written, performed and produced by Majel Connery and Alex Overington.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Erica Todd, Andrew Sinclair, Robin Lovell-Badge, and Sarah S. Richardson. Plus, a big t</span>hank you to the musicians who gave us permission to use their work in this episode—composer Erik Friedlander, for \"<a href=\"http://bit.ly/ClawsWings\">Frail as a Breeze, Part II</a>,\" and musician <a href=\"http://www.thrilljockey.com/artists/sam-prekop\">Sam Prekop</a>, whose work \"A Geometric,\" from his album </em>The Republic<em>, is out on Thrill Jockey.</em></p>\n<p><span><span><em>Radiolab is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science. And the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation, enhancing public understanding of science and technology in the modern world. More information about Sloan at www.sloan.org.</em><br></span></span></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Science pushes back against the century-old notion that X equals girl and Y equals boy, and wonders: are we who we think we are?", "publishTime"=>1530352800000, "publish-at"=>"2018-06-30T06:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1530352800, "estimated-duration"=>2342, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHuEp3.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/1/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHuEp3.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHuEp3.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>176638, "credits-name"=>"Jasu Hu"}, "objectID"=>"866183", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Gonads: X & Y", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>A lot of us understand biological sex with a pretty fateful underpinning: if you’re born with XX chromosomes, you’re female; if you’re born with XY chromosomes, you’re male. But it turns out, our relationship to the opposite sex is more complicated than we think.</p>\n<p>And if you caught this show on-air, and would like to listen to the full version of our Sex Ed Live Show, you can check it out <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/sex-ed\">here</a>. </p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Molly Webster, and produced by Matt Kielty. With scoring, original composition and mixing by Matt Kielty and Alex Overington. Additional production by Rachael Cusick, and editing by Pat Walters. The “Ballad of Daniel Webster” and “Gonads” was written, performed and produced by Majel Connery and Alex Overington.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Erica Todd, Andrew Sinclair, Robin Lovell-Badge, and Sarah S. Richardson. Plus, a big t</span>hank you to the musicians who gave us permission to use their work in this episode—composer Erik Friedlander, for \"<a href=\"http://bit.ly/ClawsWings\">Frail as a Breeze, Part II</a>,\" and musician <a href=\"http://www.thrilljockey.com/artists/sam-prekop\">Sam Prekop</a>, whose work \"A Geometric,\" from his album </em>The Republic<em>, is out on Thrill Jockey.</em></p>\n<p><span><span><em>Radiolab is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science. And the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation, enhancing public understanding of science and technology in the modern world. More information about Sloan at www.sloan.org.</em><br></span></span></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-06-30T06:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"fronads", "title"=>"Gonads: Fronads", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18fronadsreupload.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=864098", "description"=>"<p>At 28 years old, Annie Dauer was living a full life. She had a job she loved as a highschool PE teacher, a big family who lived nearby, and a serious boyfriend. Then, cancer struck. Annie would come to find out she had Stage 4 non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. It was so aggressive, there was a real chance she might die. Her oncologists wanted her to start treatment immediately. Like, end-of-the-week immediately. But before Annie started treatment, she walked out of the doctor’s office and crossed the street to see a fertility doctor doing an experimental procedure that sounded like science fiction: ovary freezing.</p>\n<p><strong>Further Reading<br></strong>A medical <a href=\"https://www.fertstert.org/article/S0015-0282(10)02198-9/fulltext\">case report</a> on Annie’s frozen ovaries<br>What’s primordial germ cells <a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/humrep/article/21/6/1345/724245\">got to do with it?</a></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Molly Webster, and produced by Pat Walters. With original music and scoring by Dylan Keefe and Alex Overington. The Gonads theme was written, performed, and produced by Majel Connery and Alex Overington. Additional production by Rachael Cusick, and editing by Jad Abumrad.</span></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Radiolab is supported in part by <a href=\"https://www.simonsfoundation.org/outreach/science-sandbox/\">Science Sandbox</a>, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science. And the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation, enhancing public understanding of science and technology in the modern world. More information about Sloan at <a href=\"applewebdata://98F02D21-82D9-4896-A630-23984C56BA70/www.sloan.org\">www.sloan.org</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"When Annie Dauer was diagnosed with non-Hodgkin&rsquo;s lymphoma at 29, she took a chance on an experimental fertility procedure that sounds like science fiction: ovary freezing.", "publishTime"=>1529793780000, "publish-at"=>"2018-06-23T18:43:00", "date-line-ts"=>1529793900, "estimated-duration"=>2207, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHuEp2.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/1/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHuEp2.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHuEp2.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>176477, "credits-name"=>"Jasu Hu"}, "objectID"=>"864098", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Gonads: Fronads", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>At 28 years old, Annie Dauer was living a full life. She had a job she loved as a highschool PE teacher, a big family who lived nearby, and a serious boyfriend. Then, cancer struck. Annie would come to find out she had Stage 4 non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. It was so aggressive, there was a real chance she might die. Her oncologists wanted her to start treatment immediately. Like, end-of-the-week immediately. But before Annie started treatment, she walked out of the doctor’s office and crossed the street to see a fertility doctor doing an experimental procedure that sounded like science fiction: ovary freezing.</p>\n<p><strong>Further Reading<br></strong>A medical <a href=\"https://www.fertstert.org/article/S0015-0282(10)02198-9/fulltext\">case report</a> on Annie’s frozen ovaries<br>What’s primordial germ cells <a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/humrep/article/21/6/1345/724245\">got to do with it?</a></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Molly Webster, and produced by Pat Walters. With original music and scoring by Dylan Keefe and Alex Overington. The Gonads theme was written, performed, and produced by Majel Connery and Alex Overington. Additional production by Rachael Cusick, and editing by Jad Abumrad.</span></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Radiolab is supported in part by <a href=\"https://www.simonsfoundation.org/outreach/science-sandbox/\">Science Sandbox</a>, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science. And the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation, enhancing public understanding of science and technology in the modern world. More information about Sloan at <a href=\"applewebdata://98F02D21-82D9-4896-A630-23984C56BA70/www.sloan.org\">www.sloan.org</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-06-23T18:43:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"theprimordialjourney", "title"=>"Gonads: The Primordial Journey", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18theprimordialjourney_.mp3?aisGetOriginalStream=true&awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=861700", "description"=>"<p><span>At two weeks old, the human embryo has only just begun its months-long journey to become a baby. The embryo is tiny, still invisible to the naked eye. But inside it, an epic struggle plays out, as a nomadic band of cells marches toward a mysterious destiny, with the future of humanity resting on their microscopic shoulders.</span></p>\n<p><span>If you happened to have caught this show on air, you can find the second half of our broadcast version <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/fronads\">here. </a></span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Molly Webster, and produced by Jad Abumrad. With scoring and original composition by Alex Overington and Dylan Keefe. Additional production by Rachael Cusick, and editing by Pat Walters. The “Ballad of the Fish” and “Gonads” was composed and sung by Majel Connery, and produced by Alex Overington.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Ruth Lehmann and Dagmar Wilhelm.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Radiolab is supported in part by <a href=\"https://www.simonsfoundation.org/outreach/science-sandbox/\">Science Sandbox</a>, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science. And the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation, enhancing public understanding of science and technology in the modern world. More information about Sloan at <a href=\"applewebdata://98F02D21-82D9-4896-A630-23984C56BA70/www.sloan.org\">www.sloan.org</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Deep inside the human embryo, a band of nomadic cells embarks on an epic journey, with the future of humanity resting on their microscopic shoulders.", "publishTime"=>1529049600000, "publish-at"=>"2018-06-15T04:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1529049600, "estimated-duration"=>2014, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHu.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/1/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHu.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHu.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>176261, "credits-name"=>"Jasu Hu"}, "objectID"=>"861700", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Gonads: The Primordial Journey", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>At two weeks old, the human embryo has only just begun its months-long journey to become a baby. The embryo is tiny, still invisible to the naked eye. But inside it, an epic struggle plays out, as a nomadic band of cells marches toward a mysterious destiny, with the future of humanity resting on their microscopic shoulders.</span></p>\n<p><span>If you happened to have caught this show on air, you can find the second half of our broadcast version <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/fronads\">here. </a></span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Molly Webster, and produced by Jad Abumrad. With scoring and original composition by Alex Overington and Dylan Keefe. Additional production by Rachael Cusick, and editing by Pat Walters. The “Ballad of the Fish” and “Gonads” was composed and sung by Majel Connery, and produced by Alex Overington.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Ruth Lehmann and Dagmar Wilhelm.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Radiolab is supported in part by <a href=\"https://www.simonsfoundation.org/outreach/science-sandbox/\">Science Sandbox</a>, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science. And the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation, enhancing public understanding of science and technology in the modern world. More information about Sloan at <a href=\"applewebdata://98F02D21-82D9-4896-A630-23984C56BA70/www.sloan.org\">www.sloan.org</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-06-15T04:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"birthstory2018", "title"=>"Birthstory", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18birthstory.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=860958", "description"=>"<div>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>We originally posted this episode in 2015, and it inspired producer Molly Webster to take a deep dive into the wild and mysterious world of human reproduction. Starting next week, she’ll be taking over the Radiolab podcast feed for a month to present a series of mind-bending stories that make us rethink the ways we make more of us.</span></p>\n</div>\n<div>You know the drill - all it takes is one sperm, one egg, and blammo - you got yourself a baby. Right? Well, in this episode, conception takes on a new form - it’s the sperm and the egg, plus: two wombs, four countries, and money. Lots of money. </div>\n<p>At first, this is the story of an Israeli couple, two guys, who go to another continent to get themselves a baby - three, in fact - by hiring surrogates to carry the children for them. As we follow them on their journey, an earth shaking revelation shifts our focus from them, to the surrogate mothers. Unfolding in real time, as countries around the world consider bans on surrogacy, this episode looks at a relationship that manages to feel deeply affecting, and deeply uncomfortable, all at the same time. </p>\n<p><em>Birthstory is a collaboration with the brilliant radio show and podcast Israel Story, created to tell stories for, and about, Israel. <a href=\"https://israelstory.org/en/episodes/\">Go check ‘em out! </a></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Israel Story's five English-language seasons were produced in partnership with <a href=\"http://www.tabletmag.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Tablet Magazine</a> and we highly recommend you listen to all of their work at  <a href=\"http://www.tabletmag.com/tag/israel-story\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.tabletmag.com/tag/israel-story</a></span></em></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced and reported by Molly Webster. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks go to: Israel Story, and their producers Maya Kosover, and Yochai Maital; reporters Nilanjana Bhowmick in India and Bhrikuti Rai in Nepal plus the <a href=\"http://internationalreportingproject.org/\">International Reporting Project</a>; Doron Mamet, Dr Nayana Patel, and Vicki Ferrara; with translation help from Aya Keefe, Karthik Ravindra, Turna Ray, Tom Wasserman, Pradeep Thapa, and <a href=\"http://www.adhikaar.org/\">Adhikaar</a>, an organization in Ridgewood, Queens advocating for the Nepali-speaking community. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Audio Extra:</strong></p>\n<p>Tal and Amir had a chance to meet each surrogate once - just after the deliveries, after all the paperwork was sorted out, and before any one left Nepal. As Amir says, they wanted to say \"a big thank you.\" These meetings between intended parents, surrogate, and new babies are a traditional part of the surrogacy process in India and Nepal, and we heard reports from the surrogates that they also look forward to them. These moments do not stigmatize, reveal the identity of, or endanger the surrogates. Tal and Amir provided the audio for this web extra.</p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"54\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/widgets/ondemand_player/radiolab/#file=http://audio.wnyc.org//rl_extras/rl_extras15tippingscene.mp3\" width=\"474\"></iframe></p>", "tease"=>"In this episode, conception takes on a new form&mdash;it&rsquo;s the sperm and the egg, plus two wombs, four countries, and money. Lots of money.", "publishTime"=>1528421820000, "publish-at"=>"2018-06-07T21:37:00", "date-line-ts"=>1528421820, "estimated-duration"=>3595, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/birthstorypic.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/897/613/h/80/1/birthstorypic.jpg", "h"=>613, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/birthstorypic.jpg", "w"=>897, "id"=>176002, "credits-name"=>"Chance Bone"}, "objectID"=>"860958", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Birthstory", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>We originally posted this episode in 2015, and it inspired producer Molly Webster to take a deep dive into the wild and mysterious world of human reproduction. Starting next week, she’ll be taking over the Radiolab podcast feed for a month to present a series of mind-bending stories that make us rethink the ways we make more of us.</span></p>\n</div>\n<div>You know the drill - all it takes is one sperm, one egg, and blammo - you got yourself a baby. Right? Well, in this episode, conception takes on a new form - it’s the sperm and the egg, plus: two wombs, four countries, and money. Lots of money. </div>\n<p>At first, this is the story of an Israeli couple, two guys, who go to another continent to get themselves a baby - three, in fact - by hiring surrogates to carry the children for them. As we follow them on their journey, an earth shaking revelation shifts our focus from them, to the surrogate mothers. Unfolding in real time, as countries around the world consider bans on surrogacy, this episode looks at a relationship that manages to feel deeply affecting, and deeply uncomfortable, all at the same time. </p>\n<p><em>Birthstory is a collaboration with the brilliant radio show and podcast Israel Story, created to tell stories for, and about, Israel. <a href=\"https://israelstory.org/en/episodes/\">Go check ‘em out! </a></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Israel Story's five English-language seasons were produced in partnership with <a href=\"http://www.tabletmag.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Tablet Magazine</a> and we highly recommend you listen to all of their work at  <a href=\"http://www.tabletmag.com/tag/israel-story\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.tabletmag.com/tag/israel-story</a></span></em></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced and reported by Molly Webster. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks go to: Israel Story, and their producers Maya Kosover, and Yochai Maital; reporters Nilanjana Bhowmick in India and Bhrikuti Rai in Nepal plus the <a href=\"http://internationalreportingproject.org/\">International Reporting Project</a>; Doron Mamet, Dr Nayana Patel, and Vicki Ferrara; with translation help from Aya Keefe, Karthik Ravindra, Turna Ray, Tom Wasserman, Pradeep Thapa, and <a href=\"http://www.adhikaar.org/\">Adhikaar</a>, an organization in Ridgewood, Queens advocating for the Nepali-speaking community. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Audio Extra:</strong></p>\n<p>Tal and Amir had a chance to meet each surrogate once - just after the deliveries, after all the paperwork was sorted out, and before any one left Nepal. As Amir says, they wanted to say \"a big thank you.\" These meetings between intended parents, surrogate, and new babies are a traditional part of the surrogacy process in India and Nepal, and we heard reports from the surrogates that they also look forward to them. These moments do not stigmatize, reveal the identity of, or endanger the surrogates. Tal and Amir provided the audio for this web extra.</p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"54\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/widgets/ondemand_player/radiolab/#file=http://audio.wnyc.org//rl_extras/rl_extras15tippingscene.mp3\" width=\"474\"></iframe></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-06-07T21:37:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"poison-control", "title"=>"Poison Control", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18poisoncontrol.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=859448", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>When reporter Brenna Farrell was a new mom, her son gave her and her husband a scare -- prompting them to call Poison Control. For Brenna, the experience was so odd, and oddly comforting, that she decided to dive into the birth story of this invisible network of poison experts, and try to understand the evolving relationship we humans have with our poisonous planet. As we learn about how poison control has changed over the years, we end up wondering what a place devoted to data and human connection can tell us about ourselves in this cultural moment of anxiety and information-overload.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>Call the national Poison Help Hotline at 1-800-222-1222 or text POISON to 797979 to save the number in your phone.</em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was reported by Brenna Farrell and was produced by Annie McEwen.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks to Wendy Blair Stephan, Whitney Pennington, Richard Dart, Marian Moser Jones, and Nathalie Wheaton. Thanks also to Lewis Goldfrank, Robert Hoffman, Steven Marcus, Toby Litovitz, James O'Donnell, and Joseph Botticelli.  </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Further Reading: </p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Poisoners-Handbook-Murder-Forensic-Medicine/dp/014311882X\">The Poisoner's Handbook,</a> by Deborah Blum</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/312067/the-poison-squad-by-deborah-blum/9781594205149/\">The Poison Squad,</a> by Deborah Blum</p>\n<p>Illinois Poison Center’s latest <a href=\"http://ipcblog.org/2018/03/19/you-wont-believea-day-in-the-life-of-a-poison-center/\">“A Day in the Life of a Poison Center”</a> post</p>\n<p><strong>You can find out more about the country’s 55 poison centers at the<a href=\"http://www.aapcc.org/\"> American Association of Poison Control Centers</a></strong><strong><a href=\"http://www.aapcc.org/\">,</a> </strong>including a snapshot of the latest available from the <a href=\"https://aapcc.s3.amazonaws.com/pdfs/annual_reports/2016_AAPCC_NPDS_Annual_Report_Data_Snapshot.pdf\">National Poison Data System (2106)</a>: </p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3698836/\">\"Poison Politics: A Contentious History of Consumer Protection Against Dangerous Household Chemicals in the United States,\" </a>by Marian Moser Jones: </p>\n<p>2011 article from The Annals of Emergency Medicine: <a href=\"https://www.annemergmed.com/article/S0196-0644(11)01802-6/fulltext?code=ymem-site\">\"The Secret Life of America's Poison Centers,\"</a> Richard Dart </p>\n<p>A 1954 article from Edward Press -- one of the key figures in creating a formalized poison control system in Chicago in the early 1950s, Press and Gdalman are credited with starting the first poison control center in the US in 1953 in Chicago: <a href=\"https://ajph.aphapublications.org/doi/pdf/10.2105/AJPH.44.12.1515\">\"A Poisoning Control Program\"</a> Edward Press and Robert B Mellins </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><br><br></span></p>", "tease"=>"A phone call plunges us into a network of unsung experts, who since the 1950s have helped us navigate our poison-filled planet.", "publishTime"=>1527843360000, "publish-at"=>"2018-06-01T04:56:00", "date-line-ts"=>1527843360, "estimated-duration"=>2150, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/yellowphone.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/683/l/80/1/yellowphone.jpg", "h"=>683, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.flickr.com/photos/lensletter/22389615419/in/dateposted-public/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/yellowphone.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>175793, "credits-name"=>"lensletter"}, "objectID"=>"859448", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Poison Control", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>When reporter Brenna Farrell was a new mom, her son gave her and her husband a scare -- prompting them to call Poison Control. For Brenna, the experience was so odd, and oddly comforting, that she decided to dive into the birth story of this invisible network of poison experts, and try to understand the evolving relationship we humans have with our poisonous planet. As we learn about how poison control has changed over the years, we end up wondering what a place devoted to data and human connection can tell us about ourselves in this cultural moment of anxiety and information-overload.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>Call the national Poison Help Hotline at 1-800-222-1222 or text POISON to 797979 to save the number in your phone.</em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was reported by Brenna Farrell and was produced by Annie McEwen.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks to Wendy Blair Stephan, Whitney Pennington, Richard Dart, Marian Moser Jones, and Nathalie Wheaton. Thanks also to Lewis Goldfrank, Robert Hoffman, Steven Marcus, Toby Litovitz, James O'Donnell, and Joseph Botticelli.  </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Further Reading: </p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Poisoners-Handbook-Murder-Forensic-Medicine/dp/014311882X\">The Poisoner's Handbook,</a> by Deborah Blum</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/312067/the-poison-squad-by-deborah-blum/9781594205149/\">The Poison Squad,</a> by Deborah Blum</p>\n<p>Illinois Poison Center’s latest <a href=\"http://ipcblog.org/2018/03/19/you-wont-believea-day-in-the-life-of-a-poison-center/\">“A Day in the Life of a Poison Center”</a> post</p>\n<p><strong>You can find out more about the country’s 55 poison centers at the<a href=\"http://www.aapcc.org/\"> American Association of Poison Control Centers</a></strong><strong><a href=\"http://www.aapcc.org/\">,</a> </strong>including a snapshot of the latest available from the <a href=\"https://aapcc.s3.amazonaws.com/pdfs/annual_reports/2016_AAPCC_NPDS_Annual_Report_Data_Snapshot.pdf\">National Poison Data System (2106)</a>: </p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3698836/\">\"Poison Politics: A Contentious History of Consumer Protection Against Dangerous Household Chemicals in the United States,\" </a>by Marian Moser Jones: </p>\n<p>2011 article from The Annals of Emergency Medicine: <a href=\"https://www.annemergmed.com/article/S0196-0644(11)01802-6/fulltext?code=ymem-site\">\"The Secret Life of America's Poison Centers,\"</a> Richard Dart </p>\n<p>A 1954 article from Edward Press -- one of the key figures in creating a formalized poison control system in Chicago in the early 1950s, Press and Gdalman are credited with starting the first poison control center in the US in 1953 in Chicago: <a href=\"https://ajph.aphapublications.org/doi/pdf/10.2105/AJPH.44.12.1515\">\"A Poisoning Control Program\"</a> Edward Press and Robert B Mellins </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><br><br></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-06-01T04:56:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"unraveling-bolero", "title"=>"Unraveling Bolero", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18unravelingbolero.mp3?aisGetOriginalStream=true&awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=857219", "description"=>"<p>This week, we're throwing it back to an old favorite: a story about obsession, creativity, and a strange symmetry between a biologist and a composer that revolves around one famously repetitive piece of music.</p>\n<p>Anne Adams was a brilliant biologist. But when her son Alex was in a bad car accident, she decided to stay home to help him recover. And then, rather suddenly, she decided to quit science altogether and become a full-time artist. After that, her husband <span>Robert Adams</span> tells us, she just painted and painted and painted. First houses and buildings, then a series of paintings involving strawberries, and then ... \"Bolero.\"</p>\n<p>At some point, Anne became obsessed with Maurice Ravel's famous composition and decided to put an elaborate visual rendition of the song to canvas. She called it \"Unraveling Bolero.\" But at the time, she had no idea that both she and Ravel would themselves unravel shortly after their experiences with this odd piece of music. <span>Arbie Orenstein</span> tells us what happened to Ravel after he wrote \"Bolero,\" and neurologist <span>Bruce Miller</span> helps us understand how, for both Anne and Ravel, \"Bolero\" might have been the first symptom of a deadly disease.</p>\n<p> <em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p><a title=\"Unravelling Bolero\" href=\"http://brain.oxfordjournals.org/content/131/1/39.full\" target=\"_blank\">Unravelling Bolero: progressive aphasia, transmodal creativity and the right posterior neocortex</a></p>\n<p>Arbie Orenstein's <a title=\"Ravel: Man and Musician\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Ravel-Musician-Dover-Books-Music/dp/0486266338/ref=la_B001HCY5JC_1_1?ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1340121834&amp;sr=1-1\" target=\"_blank\">Ravel: Man and Musician</a></p>", "tease"=>"A strange symmetry in the minds of two artists, and the one piece of music that connects them.", "publishTime"=>1527029100000, "publish-at"=>"2018-05-22T18:45:00", "date-line-ts"=>1527029100, "estimated-duration"=>1629, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/bolero_large.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1080/743/c/80/1/bolero_large.jpeg", "h"=>743, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/bolero_large.jpeg", "w"=>1080, "id"=>175461, "credits-name"=>"Anne Adam's \"Unraveling Bolero\""}, "objectID"=>"857219", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Unraveling Bolero", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This week, we're throwing it back to an old favorite: a story about obsession, creativity, and a strange symmetry between a biologist and a composer that revolves around one famously repetitive piece of music.</p>\n<p>Anne Adams was a brilliant biologist. But when her son Alex was in a bad car accident, she decided to stay home to help him recover. And then, rather suddenly, she decided to quit science altogether and become a full-time artist. After that, her husband <span>Robert Adams</span> tells us, she just painted and painted and painted. First houses and buildings, then a series of paintings involving strawberries, and then ... \"Bolero.\"</p>\n<p>At some point, Anne became obsessed with Maurice Ravel's famous composition and decided to put an elaborate visual rendition of the song to canvas. She called it \"Unraveling Bolero.\" But at the time, she had no idea that both she and Ravel would themselves unravel shortly after their experiences with this odd piece of music. <span>Arbie Orenstein</span> tells us what happened to Ravel after he wrote \"Bolero,\" and neurologist <span>Bruce Miller</span> helps us understand how, for both Anne and Ravel, \"Bolero\" might have been the first symptom of a deadly disease.</p>\n<p> <em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p>Read more:</p>\n<p><a title=\"Unravelling Bolero\" href=\"http://brain.oxfordjournals.org/content/131/1/39.full\" target=\"_blank\">Unravelling Bolero: progressive aphasia, transmodal creativity and the right posterior neocortex</a></p>\n<p>Arbie Orenstein's <a title=\"Ravel: Man and Musician\" href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Ravel-Musician-Dover-Books-Music/dp/0486266338/ref=la_B001HCY5JC_1_1?ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1340121834&amp;sr=1-1\" target=\"_blank\">Ravel: Man and Musician</a></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-05-22T18:45:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"more-or-less-human", "title"=>"More or Less Human", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18morelesshumanfix.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=856428", "description"=>"<p>Seven years ago chatbots - those robotic texting machines - were a mere curiosity. They were noticeably robotic and at their most malicious seemed only capable of scamming men looking for love online. Today, the chatbot landscape is wildly different. From election interference to spreading hate, chatbots have become online weapons.</p>\n<p>And so, we decided to reinvestigate the role these robotic bits of code play in our lives and the effects they’re having on us. We begin with a little theater. In our live show “Robert or Robot?” Jad and Robert test 100 people to see if they can spot a bot. We then take a brief detour to revisit the humanity of the Furby, and finish in a virtual house where the line between technology and humanity becomes blurrier than ever before.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Simon Adler. Our live event was produced by Simon Adler and Suzie Lechtenberg.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p><span><em>Note</em></span><span><em> from the Managing Editor:</em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>In the original version of our “More or Less Human” podcast, our introduction of neuroscientist Mavi Sanchez-Vives began with mention of her husband, Mel Slater. </span><span>We’ve edited that introduction because i</span><span>t was a mistake to introduce her first as someone’s wife. Dr. Sanchez-Vives is an exceptional scientist and we’re sorry that the original introduction distracted from or diminished her work. </span> </p>\n<p class=\"p3\"><span>On a personal note, I failed to take due note of this while editing the piece, and in doing so, I flubbed what’s known as the <a href=\"http://www.lastwordonnothing.com/2017/10/26/the-finkbeiner-test-a-tool-for-writing-about-women-in-their-professions/%20\"><strong>Finkbeiner Test</strong> </a>(all the more embarrassing given that Ann Finkebeiner is a mentor and one of my favorite science journalists). In addition to being a mistake, this is also a reminder to all of us at Radiolab that we need to be more aware of our blind spots. We should’ve done better, and we will do better.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span> </span><span>- </span><span>Soren</span><span> Wheeler </span></p>", "tease"=>"As the line between technology and humanity becomes blurrier, we wonder, are we&nbsp;becoming more or less human?&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1526611140000, "publish-at"=>"2018-05-17T22:39:00", "date-line-ts"=>1526611140, "estimated-duration"=>3660, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/coloranimation.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/900/c/80/1/coloranimation.jpg", "h"=>900, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/coloranimation.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>175409, "credits-name"=>"Nate Milton"}, "objectID"=>"856428", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"More or Less Human", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Seven years ago chatbots - those robotic texting machines - were a mere curiosity. They were noticeably robotic and at their most malicious seemed only capable of scamming men looking for love online. Today, the chatbot landscape is wildly different. From election interference to spreading hate, chatbots have become online weapons.</p>\n<p>And so, we decided to reinvestigate the role these robotic bits of code play in our lives and the effects they’re having on us. We begin with a little theater. In our live show “Robert or Robot?” Jad and Robert test 100 people to see if they can spot a bot. We then take a brief detour to revisit the humanity of the Furby, and finish in a virtual house where the line between technology and humanity becomes blurrier than ever before.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Simon Adler. Our live event was produced by Simon Adler and Suzie Lechtenberg.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p><span><em>Note</em></span><span><em> from the Managing Editor:</em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>In the original version of our “More or Less Human” podcast, our introduction of neuroscientist Mavi Sanchez-Vives began with mention of her husband, Mel Slater. </span><span>We’ve edited that introduction because i</span><span>t was a mistake to introduce her first as someone’s wife. Dr. Sanchez-Vives is an exceptional scientist and we’re sorry that the original introduction distracted from or diminished her work. </span> </p>\n<p class=\"p3\"><span>On a personal note, I failed to take due note of this while editing the piece, and in doing so, I flubbed what’s known as the <a href=\"http://www.lastwordonnothing.com/2017/10/26/the-finkbeiner-test-a-tool-for-writing-about-women-in-their-professions/%20\"><strong>Finkbeiner Test</strong> </a>(all the more embarrassing given that Ann Finkebeiner is a mentor and one of my favorite science journalists). In addition to being a mistake, this is also a reminder to all of us at Radiolab that we need to be more aware of our blind spots. We should’ve done better, and we will do better.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span> </span><span>- </span><span>Soren</span><span> Wheeler </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-05-17T22:39:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dark-side-earth", "title"=>"Dark Side of the Earth", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18darksideearth.mp3?aisGetOriginalStream=true&awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=850816", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Astronauts at the International Space Station can make one request to talk to an earthling of their choice. For some reason, Astronaut Mark Vande Hei chose us. A couple weeks ago, we were able to video chat with Mark and peer over his shoulder through the Cupola, an observatory room in the ISS. Traveling at 17,000 miles an hour, we zoomed from the Rockies to the East Coast in minutes. And from where Mark sits, the total darkness of space isn’t very far away. </span></p>\n<p>Talking to Mark brought us back to 2012, when we spoke to another astronaut, Dave Wolf. When we were putting together our live show <em>In the Dark</em>, Jad and Robert called up Dave Wolf to ask him if he had any stories about darkness. And boy, did he. Dave told us two stories that  became the finale of our show.</p>\n<p><img style=\"float: right; margin: 5px 10px 5px 10px;\" src=\"https://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/imagery/photos/sts-86/86p-036.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\"><img style=\"float: right; margin: 5px 10px 5px 10px;\" src=\"https://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/imagery/photos/sts-86/86p-027.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\">Back in late 1997, Dave Wolf was on his first spacewalk, to perform work on the Mir (the photo to the right was taken during that mission, courtesy of <a href=\"http://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/sts86/photo/sts-86-photo-36.htm\" target=\"_blank\">NASA.</a>). Dave wasn't alone -- with him was veteran Russian cosmonaut Anatoly Solovyev. (That's a picture of Dave giving Anatoly a hug on board the Mir, also courtesy of <a href=\"http://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/sts86/photo/sts-86-photo-27.htm\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a>).</p>\n<p>Out in blackness of space, the contrast between light and dark is almost unimaginably extreme -- every 45 minutes, you plunge between absolute darkness on the night-side of Earth, and blazing light as the sun screams into view. Dave and Anatoly were tethered to the spacecraft, traveling 5 miles per second. That's 16 times faster than we travel on Earth's surface as it rotates -- so as they orbited, they experienced 16 nights and 16 days for every Earth day.</p>\n<p>Dave's description of his first spacewalk was all we could've asked for, and more. But what happened next ... well, it's just one of those stories that you always hope an astronaut will tell. Dave and Anatoly were ready to call it a job and head back into the Mir when something went wrong with the airlock. They couldn't get it to re-pressurize. In other words, they were locked out. After hours of trying to fix the airlock, they were running out of the resources that kept them alive in their space suits and facing a grisly death. So, they unhooked their tethers, and tried one last desperate move.</p>\n<p>In the end, they made it through, and Dave went on to perform dozens more spacewalks in the years to come, but he never again experienced anything like those harrowing minutes trying to improvise his way back into the Mir.</p>\n<p>After that terrifying tale, Dave told us about another moment he and Anatoly shared, floating high above Earth, staring out into the universe ... a moment so beautiful, and peaceful, we decided to use the audience recreate it, as best we could, for the final act of our live show.</p>\n<p>Pilobolus creates a shadow astronaut during Dave Wolf's story on stage (photo by Lars Topelmann):</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/bb/itd_astro_shadow_MG_9001.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p>The audience turns Portland's Keller auditorium into a view of outer space with thousands of LED lights (photo by Lars Topelmann):</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/29/itd_leds_MG_0717.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p>Here's Dave Wolf in the dark darkness of space, performing a spacewalk in 2009 (courtesy of <a href=\"http://spaceflight.nasa.gov/gallery/images/shuttle/sts-127/html/s127e007210.html\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a>):</p>\n<p><em><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/3e/s127e007096.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></em></p>\n<p>To give you an idea of what it looks like during the brightness of day, here's another photo taken in 2009 -- more than a decade after the adventure described in our podcast -- this time of astronaut Tom Marshburn (Dave Wolf is with him, out of frame, photo courtesy of <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/multimedia/imagegallery/image_feature_1425.html\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a>):</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/56/Astro_TM_372055main_image_1425_1024_768.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty and Soren Wheeler. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Astronauts at the International Space Station can make one request to talk to an earthling of their choice. For some reason, Astronaut Mark Vande Hei chose us.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1524780000000, "publish-at"=>"2018-04-26T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1524780000, "estimated-duration"=>1657, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Astronaut Dave Wolf performing a spacewalk on the Endeavour in July, 2009.", "name"=>"photologue/images/ca/s127e007210_1.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/900/540/h/80/photologue/images/ca/s127e007210_1.jpg", "h"=>540, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"Astronaut Dave Wolf performing a spacewalk on the Endeavour in July, 2009.", "credits-url"=>"http://spaceflight.nasa.gov/gallery/images/shuttle/sts-127/html/s127e007210.html", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/images/ca/s127e007210_1.jpg", "w"=>900, "id"=>81784, "credits-name"=>"NASA"}, "objectID"=>"850816", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dark Side of the Earth", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Astronauts at the International Space Station can make one request to talk to an earthling of their choice. For some reason, Astronaut Mark Vande Hei chose us. A couple weeks ago, we were able to video chat with Mark and peer over his shoulder through the Cupola, an observatory room in the ISS. Traveling at 17,000 miles an hour, we zoomed from the Rockies to the East Coast in minutes. And from where Mark sits, the total darkness of space isn’t very far away. </span></p>\n<p>Talking to Mark brought us back to 2012, when we spoke to another astronaut, Dave Wolf. When we were putting together our live show <em>In the Dark</em>, Jad and Robert called up Dave Wolf to ask him if he had any stories about darkness. And boy, did he. Dave told us two stories that  became the finale of our show.</p>\n<p><img style=\"float: right; margin: 5px 10px 5px 10px;\" src=\"https://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/imagery/photos/sts-86/86p-036.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\"><img style=\"float: right; margin: 5px 10px 5px 10px;\" src=\"https://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/imagery/photos/sts-86/86p-027.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\">Back in late 1997, Dave Wolf was on his first spacewalk, to perform work on the Mir (the photo to the right was taken during that mission, courtesy of <a href=\"http://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/sts86/photo/sts-86-photo-36.htm\" target=\"_blank\">NASA.</a>). Dave wasn't alone -- with him was veteran Russian cosmonaut Anatoly Solovyev. (That's a picture of Dave giving Anatoly a hug on board the Mir, also courtesy of <a href=\"http://history.nasa.gov/SP-4225/sts86/photo/sts-86-photo-27.htm\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a>).</p>\n<p>Out in blackness of space, the contrast between light and dark is almost unimaginably extreme -- every 45 minutes, you plunge between absolute darkness on the night-side of Earth, and blazing light as the sun screams into view. Dave and Anatoly were tethered to the spacecraft, traveling 5 miles per second. That's 16 times faster than we travel on Earth's surface as it rotates -- so as they orbited, they experienced 16 nights and 16 days for every Earth day.</p>\n<p>Dave's description of his first spacewalk was all we could've asked for, and more. But what happened next ... well, it's just one of those stories that you always hope an astronaut will tell. Dave and Anatoly were ready to call it a job and head back into the Mir when something went wrong with the airlock. They couldn't get it to re-pressurize. In other words, they were locked out. After hours of trying to fix the airlock, they were running out of the resources that kept them alive in their space suits and facing a grisly death. So, they unhooked their tethers, and tried one last desperate move.</p>\n<p>In the end, they made it through, and Dave went on to perform dozens more spacewalks in the years to come, but he never again experienced anything like those harrowing minutes trying to improvise his way back into the Mir.</p>\n<p>After that terrifying tale, Dave told us about another moment he and Anatoly shared, floating high above Earth, staring out into the universe ... a moment so beautiful, and peaceful, we decided to use the audience recreate it, as best we could, for the final act of our live show.</p>\n<p>Pilobolus creates a shadow astronaut during Dave Wolf's story on stage (photo by Lars Topelmann):</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/bb/itd_astro_shadow_MG_9001.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p>The audience turns Portland's Keller auditorium into a view of outer space with thousands of LED lights (photo by Lars Topelmann):</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/29/itd_leds_MG_0717.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p>Here's Dave Wolf in the dark darkness of space, performing a spacewalk in 2009 (courtesy of <a href=\"http://spaceflight.nasa.gov/gallery/images/shuttle/sts-127/html/s127e007210.html\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a>):</p>\n<p><em><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/3e/s127e007096.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></em></p>\n<p>To give you an idea of what it looks like during the brightness of day, here's another photo taken in 2009 -- more than a decade after the adventure described in our podcast -- this time of astronaut Tom Marshburn (Dave Wolf is with him, out of frame, photo courtesy of <a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/multimedia/imagegallery/image_feature_1425.html\" target=\"_blank\">NASA</a>):</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/media/photologue/images/56/Astro_TM_372055main_image_1425_1024_768.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty and Soren Wheeler. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-04-26T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"border-trilogy-part-3-what-remains", "title"=>"Border Trilogy Part 3: What Remains", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18bordertrilogyp3fix.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=849226", "description"=>"<p><strong>Border Trilogy</strong></p>\n<p>While scouring the Sonoran Desert for objects left behind by migrants crossing into the United States, anthropologist Jason De León happened upon something he didn't expect to get left behind: a human arm, stripped of flesh.</p>\n<p>This macabre discovery sent him reeling, needing to know what exactly happened to the body, and how many migrants die that way in the wilderness. In researching border-crosser deaths in the Arizona desert, he noticed something surprising. Sometime in the late-1990s, the number of migrant deaths shot up dramatically and have stayed high since. Jason traced this increase to a Border Patrol policy still in effect, called “Prevention Through Deterrence.”</p>\n<p>Over three episodes, Radiolab will investigate this policy, its surprising origins, and the people whose lives were changed forever because of it.</p>\n<p><span><span> </span></span></p>\n<p><strong>Part 3: What Remains </strong></p>\n<p>The third episode in our Border Trilogy follows anthropologist Jason De León after he makes a grisly discovery in Arivaca, Arizona. In the middle of carrying out his pig experiments with his students, Jason finds the body of a 30-year-old female migrant. With the help of the medical examiner and some local humanitarian groups, Jason discovers her identity. Her name was Maricela. Jason then connects with her family, including her brother-in-law, who survived his own harrowing journey through Central America and the Arizona desert.</p>\n<p>With the human cost of Prevention Through Deterrence weighing on our minds, we try to parse what drives migrants like Maricela to cross through such deadly terrain, and what, if anything, could deter them.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte and was produced by Matt Kielty and Tracie Hunte. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Carlo Alb<span>á</span>n, Sandra Lopez-Monsalve, Chava Gourarie, Lynn M. Morgan, Mike Wells and Tom Barry.</em></p>\n<p><em><em>Jason de Leon's latest work is a global participatory art project called<span> </span><a href=\"https://hostileterrain94.wordpress.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://hostileterrain94.wordpress.com/about/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1556764160070000&amp;usg=AFQjCNGSJvsArbQMwUfqT2Q39qq-RX4fqA\">Hostile Terrain 94</a>, which will be exhibited at over 70 different locations around the world in 2020.  Read more about it<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation/project-exploring-migrant-deaths-in-us-aims-to-go-global/\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation/project-exploring-migrant-deaths-in-us-aims-to-go-global/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1556764160070000&amp;usg=AFQjCNF-xUt3D7--UBvgX1ajHD9gdIyONw\">here</a>.  </em></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>CORRECTION: An earlier version of this episode incorrectly stated that a person's gender can be identified from bone remains. We've adjusted the audio to say that a person's sex can be identified from bone remains. </em></p>", "tease"=>"Part Three of our Border Trilogy, in which we hear the story of a woman from Ecuador who died in the Arizona desert. And we ask, what could stop migrants from risking so much?", "publishTime"=>1524215160000, "publish-at"=>"2018-04-20T05:06:00", "date-line-ts"=>1524215100, "estimated-duration"=>2138, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Maricela.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/l/80/1/Maricela.jpg", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Maricela.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>174663, "credits-name"=>"Michael Wells"}, "objectID"=>"849226", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Border Trilogy Part 3: What Remains", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><strong>Border Trilogy</strong></p>\n<p>While scouring the Sonoran Desert for objects left behind by migrants crossing into the United States, anthropologist Jason De León happened upon something he didn't expect to get left behind: a human arm, stripped of flesh.</p>\n<p>This macabre discovery sent him reeling, needing to know what exactly happened to the body, and how many migrants die that way in the wilderness. In researching border-crosser deaths in the Arizona desert, he noticed something surprising. Sometime in the late-1990s, the number of migrant deaths shot up dramatically and have stayed high since. Jason traced this increase to a Border Patrol policy still in effect, called “Prevention Through Deterrence.”</p>\n<p>Over three episodes, Radiolab will investigate this policy, its surprising origins, and the people whose lives were changed forever because of it.</p>\n<p><span><span> </span></span></p>\n<p><strong>Part 3: What Remains </strong></p>\n<p>The third episode in our Border Trilogy follows anthropologist Jason De León after he makes a grisly discovery in Arivaca, Arizona. In the middle of carrying out his pig experiments with his students, Jason finds the body of a 30-year-old female migrant. With the help of the medical examiner and some local humanitarian groups, Jason discovers her identity. Her name was Maricela. Jason then connects with her family, including her brother-in-law, who survived his own harrowing journey through Central America and the Arizona desert.</p>\n<p>With the human cost of Prevention Through Deterrence weighing on our minds, we try to parse what drives migrants like Maricela to cross through such deadly terrain, and what, if anything, could deter them.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte and was produced by Matt Kielty and Tracie Hunte. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Carlo Alb<span>á</span>n, Sandra Lopez-Monsalve, Chava Gourarie, Lynn M. Morgan, Mike Wells and Tom Barry.</em></p>\n<p><em><em>Jason de Leon's latest work is a global participatory art project called<span> </span><a href=\"https://hostileterrain94.wordpress.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://hostileterrain94.wordpress.com/about/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1556764160070000&amp;usg=AFQjCNGSJvsArbQMwUfqT2Q39qq-RX4fqA\">Hostile Terrain 94</a>, which will be exhibited at over 70 different locations around the world in 2020.  Read more about it<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation/project-exploring-migrant-deaths-in-us-aims-to-go-global/\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation/project-exploring-migrant-deaths-in-us-aims-to-go-global/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1556764160070000&amp;usg=AFQjCNF-xUt3D7--UBvgX1ajHD9gdIyONw\">here</a>.  </em></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>CORRECTION: An earlier version of this episode incorrectly stated that a person's gender can be identified from bone remains. We've adjusted the audio to say that a person's sex can be identified from bone remains. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-04-20T05:06:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"border-trilogy-part-2-hold-line", "title"=>"Border Trilogy Part 2: Hold the Line ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18bordertrilogyp2.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=845921", "description"=>"<p><strong>Border Trilogy </strong></p>\n<p>While scouring the Sonoran Desert for objects left behind by migrants crossing into the United States, anthropologist Jason De León happened upon something he didn't expect to get left behind: a human arm, stripped of flesh.</p>\n<p>This macabre discovery sent him reeling, needing to know what exactly happened to the body, and how many migrants die that way in the wilderness.  In researching border-crosser deaths in the Arizona desert, he noticed something surprising. Sometime in the late-1990s, the number of migrant deaths shot up dramatically and have stayed high since. Jason traced this increase to a Border Patrol policy still in effect, called “Prevention Through Deterrence.”</p>\n<p>Over three episodes, Radiolab will investigate this policy, its surprising origins, and the people whose lives were changed forever because of it.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Part 2: Hold the Line</strong></p>\n<p>After the showdown in court with Bowie High School, Border Patrol brings in a fresh face to head its dysfunctional El Paso Sector: Silvestre Reyes. The first Mexican-American to ever hold the position, Reyes knows something needs to change and has an idea how to do it. One Saturday night at midnight, with the element of surprise on his side, Reyes unveils ... Operation Blockade. It wins widespread support for the Border Patrol in El Paso, but sparks major protests across the Rio Grande. Soon after, he gets a phone call that catapults his little experiment onto the national stage, where it works so well that it diverts migrant crossing patterns along the entire U.S.-Mexico Border.</p>\n<p>Years later, in the Arizona desert, anthropologist Jason de León realizes that in order to accurately gauge how many migrants die crossing the desert, he must first understand how human bodies decompose in such an extreme environment. He sets up a macabre experiment, and what he finds is more drastic than anything he could have expected.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte, and was produced by Matt Kielty, Bethel Habte and Latif Nasser.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Sherrie Kossoudji at the University of Michigan, Lynn M. Morgan,</em> <em>Cheryl Howard, Andrew Hansen, William Sabol, Donald B. White, Daniel Martinez, Michelle Mittelstadt at the Migration Policy Institute, Former Executive Assistant to the El Paso Mayor Mark Smith, Retired Assistant Border Patrol Sector Chief Clyde Benzenhoefer, Paul Anderson, Eric Robledo, Maggie Southard Gladstone and Kate Hall.</em></p>\n<p><em><em>Jason de Leon's latest work is a global participatory art project called<span> </span><a href=\"https://hostileterrain94.wordpress.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://hostileterrain94.wordpress.com/about/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1556764160070000&amp;usg=AFQjCNGSJvsArbQMwUfqT2Q39qq-RX4fqA\">Hostile Terrain 94</a>, which will be exhibited at over 70 different locations around the world in 2020.  Read more about it<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation/project-exploring-migrant-deaths-in-us-aims-to-go-global/\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation/project-exploring-migrant-deaths-in-us-aims-to-go-global/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1556764160070000&amp;usg=AFQjCNF-xUt3D7--UBvgX1ajHD9gdIyONw\">here</a>.  </em></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><strong>CORRECTION:</strong> An earlier version of this piece incorrectly stated that Silvestre Reyes's brother died in a car accident in 1968; it was actually his father who died in the accident.  We also omitted a detail about the 1997 GAO report that we quote, namely that it predicted that as deaths in the mountains and deserts might rise, deaths in other areas might also fall. The audio has been adjusted accordingly.</em></p>\n<p><em> </em></p>", "tease"=>"Part Two of our Border Trilogy, in which one Border Patrol agent changes the entire agency&rsquo;s enforcement strategy, and one anthropologist tries to measure its deadly consequences.", "publishTime"=>1522906260000, "publish-at"=>"2018-04-05T01:31:00", "date-line-ts"=>1522992660, "estimated-duration"=>2974, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/bordercrossingimage_vipT6yl.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/512/366/c/80/1/bordercrossingimage_vipT6yl.png", "h"=>366, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/bordercrossingimage_vipT6yl.png", "w"=>512, "id"=>174295, "credits-name"=>"original: John Hood, Caltrans graphic artist"}, "objectID"=>"845921", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Border Trilogy Part 2: Hold the Line ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><strong>Border Trilogy </strong></p>\n<p>While scouring the Sonoran Desert for objects left behind by migrants crossing into the United States, anthropologist Jason De León happened upon something he didn't expect to get left behind: a human arm, stripped of flesh.</p>\n<p>This macabre discovery sent him reeling, needing to know what exactly happened to the body, and how many migrants die that way in the wilderness.  In researching border-crosser deaths in the Arizona desert, he noticed something surprising. Sometime in the late-1990s, the number of migrant deaths shot up dramatically and have stayed high since. Jason traced this increase to a Border Patrol policy still in effect, called “Prevention Through Deterrence.”</p>\n<p>Over three episodes, Radiolab will investigate this policy, its surprising origins, and the people whose lives were changed forever because of it.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Part 2: Hold the Line</strong></p>\n<p>After the showdown in court with Bowie High School, Border Patrol brings in a fresh face to head its dysfunctional El Paso Sector: Silvestre Reyes. The first Mexican-American to ever hold the position, Reyes knows something needs to change and has an idea how to do it. One Saturday night at midnight, with the element of surprise on his side, Reyes unveils ... Operation Blockade. It wins widespread support for the Border Patrol in El Paso, but sparks major protests across the Rio Grande. Soon after, he gets a phone call that catapults his little experiment onto the national stage, where it works so well that it diverts migrant crossing patterns along the entire U.S.-Mexico Border.</p>\n<p>Years later, in the Arizona desert, anthropologist Jason de León realizes that in order to accurately gauge how many migrants die crossing the desert, he must first understand how human bodies decompose in such an extreme environment. He sets up a macabre experiment, and what he finds is more drastic than anything he could have expected.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte, and was produced by Matt Kielty, Bethel Habte and Latif Nasser.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Sherrie Kossoudji at the University of Michigan, Lynn M. Morgan,</em> <em>Cheryl Howard, Andrew Hansen, William Sabol, Donald B. White, Daniel Martinez, Michelle Mittelstadt at the Migration Policy Institute, Former Executive Assistant to the El Paso Mayor Mark Smith, Retired Assistant Border Patrol Sector Chief Clyde Benzenhoefer, Paul Anderson, Eric Robledo, Maggie Southard Gladstone and Kate Hall.</em></p>\n<p><em><em>Jason de Leon's latest work is a global participatory art project called<span> </span><a href=\"https://hostileterrain94.wordpress.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://hostileterrain94.wordpress.com/about/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1556764160070000&amp;usg=AFQjCNGSJvsArbQMwUfqT2Q39qq-RX4fqA\">Hostile Terrain 94</a>, which will be exhibited at over 70 different locations around the world in 2020.  Read more about it<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation/project-exploring-migrant-deaths-in-us-aims-to-go-global/\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation/project-exploring-migrant-deaths-in-us-aims-to-go-global/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1556764160070000&amp;usg=AFQjCNF-xUt3D7--UBvgX1ajHD9gdIyONw\">here</a>.  </em></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><strong>CORRECTION:</strong> An earlier version of this piece incorrectly stated that Silvestre Reyes's brother died in a car accident in 1968; it was actually his father who died in the accident.  We also omitted a detail about the 1997 GAO report that we quote, namely that it predicted that as deaths in the mountains and deserts might rise, deaths in other areas might also fall. The audio has been adjusted accordingly.</em></p>\n<p><em> </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-04-05T01:31:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"border-trilogy-part-1", "title"=>"Border Trilogy Part 1: Hole in the Fence", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18bordertrilogyp1.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=842207", "description"=>"<p><strong>Border Trilogy</strong></p>\n<p>While scouring the Sonoran Desert for objects left behind by migrants crossing into the United States, anthropologist Jason De León happened upon something he didn't expect to get left behind: a human arm, stripped of flesh.</p>\n<p>This macabre discovery sent him reeling, needing to know what exactly happened to the body, and how many migrants die that way in the wilderness. In researching border-crosser deaths in the Arizona desert, he noticed something surprising. Sometime in the late-1990s, the number of migrant deaths shot up dramatically and have stayed high since. Jason traced this increase to a Border Patrol policy still in effect, called “Prevention Through Deterrence.”</p>\n<p>Over three episodes, Radiolab will investigate this policy, its surprising origins, and the people whose lives were changed forever because of it.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Part 1: Hole in the Fence:</strong></p>\n<p><span>We begin one afternoon in May 1992, when a student named Albert stumbled in late for history class at Bowie High School in El Paso, Texas. His excuse: Border Patrol. Soon more stories of students getting stopped and harassed by Border Patrol started pouring in. So begins the unlikely story of how a handful of Mexican-American high schoolers in one of the poorest neighborhoods in the country stood up to what is today the country’s largest federal law enforcement agency. They had no way of knowing at the time, but what would follow was a chain of events that would drastically change the US-Mexico border. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte and was produced by Matt Kielty, Bethel Habte, Tracie Hunte and Latif Nasser. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Centro de Salud Familiar La Fe, Estela Reyes López, Barbara Hines, Lynn M. Morgan, Mallory Falk, Francesca Begos and Nancy Wiese from Hachette Book Group, Professor Michael Olivas at the University of Houston Law Center, and Josiah McC. Heyman, Ph.D, Director, Center for Interamerican and Border Studies and Professor of Anthropology.</em></p>\n<p><em><em>Jason de Leon's latest work is a global participatory art project called<span> </span><a href=\"https://hostileterrain94.wordpress.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://hostileterrain94.wordpress.com/about/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1556764160070000&amp;usg=AFQjCNGSJvsArbQMwUfqT2Q39qq-RX4fqA\">Hostile Terrain 94</a>, which will be exhibited at over 70 different locations around the world in 2020.  Read more about it<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation/project-exploring-migrant-deaths-in-us-aims-to-go-global/\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation/project-exploring-migrant-deaths-in-us-aims-to-go-global/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1556764160070000&amp;usg=AFQjCNF-xUt3D7--UBvgX1ajHD9gdIyONw\">here</a>.  </em></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Part One of our Border Trilogy, in which we chronicle an unlikely legal showdown between&nbsp;high schoolers in one of the poorest neighborhoods in the country and the US Border Patrol.", "publishTime"=>1521790800000, "publish-at"=>"2018-03-23T03:40:00", "date-line-ts"=>1521790800, "estimated-duration"=>2912, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/backpacksfinal.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/l/80/1/backpacksfinal.jpg", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Backpacks left by migrants crossing the Sonoran Desert in Arizona. ", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/backpacksfinal.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>173960, "credits-name"=>"State of Exception/Estado de Excepción, Parsons School of Design at The New School. Created by photographer Richard Barnes and curator Amanda Krugliak with Jason De León.  Photo: Marc Tatti."}, "objectID"=>"842207", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Border Trilogy Part 1: Hole in the Fence", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><strong>Border Trilogy</strong></p>\n<p>While scouring the Sonoran Desert for objects left behind by migrants crossing into the United States, anthropologist Jason De León happened upon something he didn't expect to get left behind: a human arm, stripped of flesh.</p>\n<p>This macabre discovery sent him reeling, needing to know what exactly happened to the body, and how many migrants die that way in the wilderness. In researching border-crosser deaths in the Arizona desert, he noticed something surprising. Sometime in the late-1990s, the number of migrant deaths shot up dramatically and have stayed high since. Jason traced this increase to a Border Patrol policy still in effect, called “Prevention Through Deterrence.”</p>\n<p>Over three episodes, Radiolab will investigate this policy, its surprising origins, and the people whose lives were changed forever because of it.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Part 1: Hole in the Fence:</strong></p>\n<p><span>We begin one afternoon in May 1992, when a student named Albert stumbled in late for history class at Bowie High School in El Paso, Texas. His excuse: Border Patrol. Soon more stories of students getting stopped and harassed by Border Patrol started pouring in. So begins the unlikely story of how a handful of Mexican-American high schoolers in one of the poorest neighborhoods in the country stood up to what is today the country’s largest federal law enforcement agency. They had no way of knowing at the time, but what would follow was a chain of events that would drastically change the US-Mexico border. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte and was produced by Matt Kielty, Bethel Habte, Tracie Hunte and Latif Nasser. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Centro de Salud Familiar La Fe, Estela Reyes López, Barbara Hines, Lynn M. Morgan, Mallory Falk, Francesca Begos and Nancy Wiese from Hachette Book Group, Professor Michael Olivas at the University of Houston Law Center, and Josiah McC. Heyman, Ph.D, Director, Center for Interamerican and Border Studies and Professor of Anthropology.</em></p>\n<p><em><em>Jason de Leon's latest work is a global participatory art project called<span> </span><a href=\"https://hostileterrain94.wordpress.com/about/\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://hostileterrain94.wordpress.com/about/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1556764160070000&amp;usg=AFQjCNGSJvsArbQMwUfqT2Q39qq-RX4fqA\">Hostile Terrain 94</a>, which will be exhibited at over 70 different locations around the world in 2020.  Read more about it<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation/project-exploring-migrant-deaths-in-us-aims-to-go-global/\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/nation/project-exploring-migrant-deaths-in-us-aims-to-go-global/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1556764160070000&amp;usg=AFQjCNF-xUt3D7--UBvgX1ajHD9gdIyONw\">here</a>.  </em></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-03-23T03:40:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"rippin-the-rainbow-an-even-newer-one", "title"=>"Rippin’ the Rainbow an Even Newer One", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18rippinrainbownewer3.mp3?aisGetOriginalStream=true&awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=840261", "description"=>"<p>One of our most popular episodes of all time was our <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/211119-colors/\">Colors episode</a>, where we introduced you to a sea creature that could see a rainbow far beyond what humans can experience.</p>\n<p>Peacock mantis shrimps are as extraordinary as they are strange and boast what may well be the most complicated visual system in the world. They each have 16 photoreceptors compared to our measly three. But recently researchers in Australia put the mantis shrimps’ eyes to the test only to discover that sure, they can SEE lots of colors, but that doesn't mean they can tell them apart.</p>\n<p>In fact, when two colors are close together - like yellow and yellow-y green - they can’t seem to tell them apart at all.  </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>MORE ON COLORS: There was a time -- between the flickery black-and-white films of yore and the hi-def color-corrected movies we watch today -- when color was in flux. Check out this <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/219452-ringmaster-rainbow/\">blog post</a> on how colors made it to the big screen from our director of research, Latif Nasser. </p>\n<p><em>Our original episode was produced by Tim Howard and Pat Walters. This update was produced by Amanda Aronczyk.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Chris Martin of <a href=\"http://www.creativeaquariumnation.com/\">Creative Aquarium Nation</a>, Phil Weissman, David Gebel and Kate Hinds for lending us their colorful garments. Also thanks to Michael Kerschner, Elisa Nikoloulias and the <a href=\"http://ynyc.org/\">Young New Yorkers’ Chorus</a>, as well as Chase Culpon and The Greene Space team.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at </em><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"><em>Radiolab.org/donate</em></a><em>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"The creature with the world&rsquo;s most complex visual system seems to be terrible at telling colors apart.", "publishTime"=>1521151200000, "publish-at"=>"2018-03-15T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1521151200, "estimated-duration"=>1982, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/OscyllarusmaleMaxilla_JwAEKI5.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1831/l/80/1/OscyllarusmaleMaxilla_JwAEKI5.jpg", "h"=>1831, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"An adult male Odontodactylus scyllarus, also known as a peacock mantis shrimp.", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/OscyllarusmaleMaxilla_JwAEKI5.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>173609, "credits-name"=>"Roy Caldwell"}, "objectID"=>"840261", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Rippin’ the Rainbow an Even Newer One", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>One of our most popular episodes of all time was our <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/211119-colors/\">Colors episode</a>, where we introduced you to a sea creature that could see a rainbow far beyond what humans can experience.</p>\n<p>Peacock mantis shrimps are as extraordinary as they are strange and boast what may well be the most complicated visual system in the world. They each have 16 photoreceptors compared to our measly three. But recently researchers in Australia put the mantis shrimps’ eyes to the test only to discover that sure, they can SEE lots of colors, but that doesn't mean they can tell them apart.</p>\n<p>In fact, when two colors are close together - like yellow and yellow-y green - they can’t seem to tell them apart at all.  </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>MORE ON COLORS: There was a time -- between the flickery black-and-white films of yore and the hi-def color-corrected movies we watch today -- when color was in flux. Check out this <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/story/219452-ringmaster-rainbow/\">blog post</a> on how colors made it to the big screen from our director of research, Latif Nasser. </p>\n<p><em>Our original episode was produced by Tim Howard and Pat Walters. This update was produced by Amanda Aronczyk.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Chris Martin of <a href=\"http://www.creativeaquariumnation.com/\">Creative Aquarium Nation</a>, Phil Weissman, David Gebel and Kate Hinds for lending us their colorful garments. Also thanks to Michael Kerschner, Elisa Nikoloulias and the <a href=\"http://ynyc.org/\">Young New Yorkers’ Chorus</a>, as well as Chase Culpon and The Greene Space team.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at </em><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"><em>Radiolab.org/donate</em></a><em>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-03-15T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-presents-more-perfect-gun-show", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - The Gun Show ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18thegunshowmp.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=836374", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>The shooting in Parkland, Florida on February 14, 2018, reignited an increasingly familiar debate about guns in this country. Today, we’re re-releasing a <em>More Perfect </em>episode that aired just after the Las Vegas shooting last year that attempts to make sense of our country’s fraught relationship with the Second Amendment.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">For nearly 200 years of our nation’s history, the Second Amendment was an all-but-forgotten rule about the importance of militias. But in the 1960s and 70s, a movement emerged — led by Black Panthers and a recently-repositioned NRA — that insisted owning a firearm was the right of each and every American. So began a constitutional debate that only the Supreme Court could solve. That didn’t happen until 2008, when a Washington, D.C. security guard named Dick Heller made a compelling case.</p>", "tease"=>"In 2008, the Supreme Court stepped in to settle our fight over the Second Amendment&rsquo;s meaning. They did. And they didn&rsquo;t.&nbsp;&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1519426800000, "publish-at"=>"2018-02-23T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1519426800, "estimated-duration"=>4145, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/dick-v04.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1860/l/80/1/dick-v04.jpg", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Image of Dick Heller", "credits-url"=>"http://mitchboyer.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/dick-v04.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>169089, "credits-name"=>"Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"836374", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - The Gun Show ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>The shooting in Parkland, Florida on February 14, 2018, reignited an increasingly familiar debate about guns in this country. Today, we’re re-releasing a <em>More Perfect </em>episode that aired just after the Las Vegas shooting last year that attempts to make sense of our country’s fraught relationship with the Second Amendment.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">For nearly 200 years of our nation’s history, the Second Amendment was an all-but-forgotten rule about the importance of militias. But in the 1960s and 70s, a movement emerged — led by Black Panthers and a recently-repositioned NRA — that insisted owning a firearm was the right of each and every American. So began a constitutional debate that only the Supreme Court could solve. That didn’t happen until 2008, when a Washington, D.C. security guard named Dick Heller made a compelling case.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-02-23T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"curious-case-russian-flash-mob-west-palm-beach-cheesecake-factory", "title"=>"The Curious Case of the Russian Flash Mob at the West Palm Beach Cheesecake Factory ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18russianflashmob2.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=835325", "description"=>"<p><span>We don’t do breaking news. But when Robert Mueller released his indictment a few days ago, alleging that 13 Russian nationals colluded to disrupt the 2016 elections, we had a lot of questions. Who are these Russian individuals sowing discord? And who are these Americans that were manipulated?? Join us as we follow a trail of likes and tweets that takes us from a Troll Factory to a Cheesecake Factory.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen with reporting help from Becca Bressler and Charles Maynes. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "tease"=>"When Robert Mueller released his indictment a few days ago, alleging that 13 Russian nationals colluded to disrupt the 2016 elections, we had a lot of questions.", "publishTime"=>1519109580000, "publish-at"=>"2018-02-20T01:53:00", "date-line-ts"=>1519109580, "estimated-duration"=>0, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/14100517_10210419432147208_4922567249388727233_n.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/720/720/l/80/1/14100517_10210419432147208_4922567249388727233_n.jpg", "h"=>720, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/14100517_10210419432147208_4922567249388727233_n.jpg", "w"=>720, "id"=>172973, "credits-name"=>"Anne Marie Margaret Thomas "}, "objectID"=>"835325", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Curious Case of the Russian Flash Mob at the West Palm Beach Cheesecake Factory ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>We don’t do breaking news. But when Robert Mueller released his indictment a few days ago, alleging that 13 Russian nationals colluded to disrupt the 2016 elections, we had a lot of questions. Who are these Russian individuals sowing discord? And who are these Americans that were manipulated?? Join us as we follow a trail of likes and tweets that takes us from a Troll Factory to a Cheesecake Factory.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen with reporting help from Becca Bressler and Charles Maynes. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-02-20T01:53:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"smarty-plants", "title"=>"Smarty Plants", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18smartyplants.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=833539", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Do you really need a brain to sense the world around you? To remember? Or even learn? Well, it depends on who you ask. Jad and Robert, they are split on this one. Today, Robert drags Jad along on a parade for the surprising feats of brainless plants. Along with a home-inspection duo, a science writer, and some enterprising scientists at Princeton University, we dig into the work of evolutionary ecologist Monica Gagliano, who turns our brain-centered worldview on its head through a series of clever experiments that show plants doing things we never would've imagined. </span>Can Robert get Jad to join the march?</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>This episode was produced by Annie McEwen. </em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "tease"=>"Do you really need a brain to sense the world around you? To remember? Or even learn?", "publishTime"=>1518583320000, "publish-at"=>"2018-02-13T23:42:00", "date-line-ts"=>1518583320, "estimated-duration"=>2094, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/3_brain-vs-plant.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/863/c/80/1/3_brain-vs-plant.jpg", "h"=>863, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/3_brain-vs-plant.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>172780, "credits-name"=>"Robert Krulwich "}, "objectID"=>"833539", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Smarty Plants", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Do you really need a brain to sense the world around you? To remember? Or even learn? Well, it depends on who you ask. Jad and Robert, they are split on this one. Today, Robert drags Jad along on a parade for the surprising feats of brainless plants. Along with a home-inspection duo, a science writer, and some enterprising scientists at Princeton University, we dig into the work of evolutionary ecologist Monica Gagliano, who turns our brain-centered worldview on its head through a series of clever experiments that show plants doing things we never would've imagined. </span>Can Robert get Jad to join the march?</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>This episode was produced by Annie McEwen. </em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-02-13T23:42:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"ghosts-football-past", "title"=>"Ghosts of Football Past ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18ghostsfootballpast.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=831441", "description"=>"<p>In anticipation of Super Bowl LII (Go Eagles), we're revisiting an old episode about the surprising history of how the game came to be. It's the end of the 19th century -- the Civil War is over, and the frontier is dead. And young college men are anxious. What great struggle will test their character? Then along comes a new craze: football. A brutally violent game where young men can show a stadium full of fans just what they're made of. Harvard, Yale, Princeton, Penn -- the sons of the most powerful men in the country are literally knocking themselves out to win these gladiatorial battles. And then the most American team of all, with the most to prove, gets in the game and owns it. The Carlisle Indian School, formed in 1879 to assimilate the children and grandchildren of the men who fought the final Plains Wars against the fathers and grandfathers of the Ivy Leaguers, starts challenging the best teams in the country. On the football field, Carlisle had a chance for a fair fight with high stakes -- a chance to earn respect, a chance to be winners, and a chance to go forward in a changing world that was destroying theirs. </p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "tease"=>"In anticipation of Super Bowl LII (Go Eagles), we're revisiting an old episode about the surprising history of how the game came to be.", "publishTime"=>1517702880000, "publish-at"=>"2018-02-03T19:08:00", "date-line-ts"=>1517702880, "estimated-duration"=>2200, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/4187033520_542a868206_o.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"Flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1235/c/80/1/4187033520_542a868206_o.jpg", "h"=>1235, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.flickr.com/photos/gavinpeterson/4187033520/in/photolist-7nZDc7-dmpM3C-amLWkw-a6NDp5-ah3o4m-4zH26P-ah3nn7-ah3tuE-21TdmQh-8js9es-ah3uJL-agZyg6-agZASx-4D7P9s-82ogR6-agZG3M-ah3uzS-5Cgtko-fWKu8", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/4187033520_542a868206_o.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>172561, "credits-name"=>"Gavin's Goods"}, "objectID"=>"831441", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Ghosts of Football Past ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In anticipation of Super Bowl LII (Go Eagles), we're revisiting an old episode about the surprising history of how the game came to be. It's the end of the 19th century -- the Civil War is over, and the frontier is dead. And young college men are anxious. What great struggle will test their character? Then along comes a new craze: football. A brutally violent game where young men can show a stadium full of fans just what they're made of. Harvard, Yale, Princeton, Penn -- the sons of the most powerful men in the country are literally knocking themselves out to win these gladiatorial battles. And then the most American team of all, with the most to prove, gets in the game and owns it. The Carlisle Indian School, formed in 1879 to assimilate the children and grandchildren of the men who fought the final Plains Wars against the fathers and grandfathers of the Ivy Leaguers, starts challenging the best teams in the country. On the football field, Carlisle had a chance for a fair fight with high stakes -- a chance to earn respect, a chance to be winners, and a chance to go forward in a changing world that was destroying theirs. </p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-02-03T19:08:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-presents-more-perfect-one-nation-under-money", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - One Nation, Under Money", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18onenationundermoneymp.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=830365", "description"=>"<p>An unassuming string of 16 words tucked into the Constitution grants Congress extensive power to make laws that impact the entire nation. The Commerce Clause has allowed Congress to intervene in all kinds of situations — from penalizing one man for growing too much wheat on his farm, to enforcing the end of racial segregation nationwide. That is, if the federal government can make an economic case for it. This seemingly all-powerful tool has the potential to unite the 50 states into one nation and protect the civil liberties of all. But it also challenges us to consider: when we make everything about money, what does it cost us?</p>\n<p><strong>The key voices:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Roscoe Filbrun Jr., Son of Roscoe Filbrun Sr., respondent in Wickard v. Filburn</li>\n<li>Ollie McClung Jr., Son of Ollie McClung Sr., respondent in Katzenbach v. McClung</li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.law.msu.edu/faculty_staff/profile.php?prof=880\">James M. Chen</a>, professor at Michigan State University College of Law</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.wnyc.org/people/jami-floyd/\">Jami Floyd</a>, legal analyst and host of WNYC’s All Things Considered who, as a domestic policy advisor in the Clinton White House, worked on the Violence Against Women Act</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.wilmerhale.com/ari_savitzky/\">Ari J. Savitzky</a>, lawyer at WilmerHale </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The key cases:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>1824: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1789-1850/22us1\"><em>Gibbons v. Ogden</em></a></li>\n<li>1942: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1940-1955/317us111\"><em>Wickard v. Filburn</em></a></li>\n<li>1964: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1964/543\"><em>Katzenbach v. McClung</em></a></li>\n<li>2000: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1999/99-5\"><em>United States v. Morrison</em></a></li>\n<li>2012: <em><a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2011/11-393\">National Federation of Independent Businesses v. Sebelius</a></em></li>\n</ul>\n<p> <em>Additional production for this episode by Derek John and Louis Mitchell.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Jess Mador, Andrew Yeager, and Rachel Iacovone.                                                 </em></p>\n<p><em>Leadership support for More Perfect is provided by The Joyce Foundation. Additional funding is provided by The Charles Evans Hughes Memorial Foundation.</em></p>\n<p><em>Supreme Court archival audio comes from </em><a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\"><em>Oyez®</em></a><em>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"A look at the government&rsquo;s secret power to control almost everything around you.", "publishTime"=>1517385600000, "publish-at"=>"2018-01-31T03:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1517385600, "estimated-duration"=>3304, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Ollie's Barbecue employee in front of the restaurant's 'World's Best Bar-B-Q' sign", "name"=>"1/OLLIES-v03.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1860/l/80/1/OLLIES-v03.jpg", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Ollie's Barbecue employee in front of the restaurant's \"World's Best Bar-B-Q\" sign", "credits-url"=>"http://mitchboyer.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/OLLIES-v03.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>172394, "credits-name"=>"Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"830365", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: More Perfect - One Nation, Under Money", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>An unassuming string of 16 words tucked into the Constitution grants Congress extensive power to make laws that impact the entire nation. The Commerce Clause has allowed Congress to intervene in all kinds of situations — from penalizing one man for growing too much wheat on his farm, to enforcing the end of racial segregation nationwide. That is, if the federal government can make an economic case for it. This seemingly all-powerful tool has the potential to unite the 50 states into one nation and protect the civil liberties of all. But it also challenges us to consider: when we make everything about money, what does it cost us?</p>\n<p><strong>The key voices:</strong> </p>\n<ul>\n<li>Roscoe Filbrun Jr., Son of Roscoe Filbrun Sr., respondent in Wickard v. Filburn</li>\n<li>Ollie McClung Jr., Son of Ollie McClung Sr., respondent in Katzenbach v. McClung</li>\n<li><a href=\"http://www.law.msu.edu/faculty_staff/profile.php?prof=880\">James M. Chen</a>, professor at Michigan State University College of Law</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.wnyc.org/people/jami-floyd/\">Jami Floyd</a>, legal analyst and host of WNYC’s All Things Considered who, as a domestic policy advisor in the Clinton White House, worked on the Violence Against Women Act</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.wilmerhale.com/ari_savitzky/\">Ari J. Savitzky</a>, lawyer at WilmerHale </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The key cases:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>1824: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1789-1850/22us1\"><em>Gibbons v. Ogden</em></a></li>\n<li>1942: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1940-1955/317us111\"><em>Wickard v. Filburn</em></a></li>\n<li>1964: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1964/543\"><em>Katzenbach v. McClung</em></a></li>\n<li>2000: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1999/99-5\"><em>United States v. Morrison</em></a></li>\n<li>2012: <em><a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/2011/11-393\">National Federation of Independent Businesses v. Sebelius</a></em></li>\n</ul>\n<p> <em>Additional production for this episode by Derek John and Louis Mitchell.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Jess Mador, Andrew Yeager, and Rachel Iacovone.                                                 </em></p>\n<p><em>Leadership support for More Perfect is provided by The Joyce Foundation. Additional funding is provided by The Charles Evans Hughes Memorial Foundation.</em></p>\n<p><em>Supreme Court archival audio comes from </em><a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\"><em>Oyez®</em></a><em>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-01-31T03:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"voice-your-head-tribute-joe-frank", "title"=>"The Voice in Your Head - A Tribute to Joe Frank", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18joefranktribute.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=828669", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>How do you pay proper tribute to a legend that many people haven’t heard of?</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>We began asking ourselves this question last week when the visionary radio producer Joe Frank passed away, after a long struggle with colon cancer.  Joe Frank was the radio producer’s radio producer.  He told stories that were thrillingly weird, deeply mischievous (and sometimes head-spinningly confusing!). He had a big impact on us at Radiolab.  For Jad, his Joe Frank moment happened in 2002, while sitting at a mixing console in an AM radio studio waiting to read the weather.  Joe Frank's Peabody Award-winning series \"Rent-A-Family” came on the air.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Time stood still.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>We’ve since learned that many of our peers have had similar Joe Frank moments.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>In this episode, we commemorate one of the greats with Brooke Gladstone from On the Media and Ira Glass from This American Life.</span> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>This episode was produced by Jad Abumrad with help from Kelly Prime and Sarah Qari. </em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>A very special thanks to Michal Story.</em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"How do you pay proper tribute to a legend that many people haven&rsquo;t heard of?", "publishTime"=>1516683780000, "publish-at"=>"2018-01-23T00:03:00", "date-line-ts"=>1516683780, "estimated-duration"=>1562, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/JoeFrank2004byMichalStory.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/325/392/c/80/1/JoeFrank2004byMichalStory.jpg", "h"=>392, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/JoeFrank2004byMichalStory.jpg", "w"=>325, "id"=>172219, "credits-name"=>"Michal Story"}, "objectID"=>"828669", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Voice in Your Head - A Tribute to Joe Frank", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>How do you pay proper tribute to a legend that many people haven’t heard of?</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>We began asking ourselves this question last week when the visionary radio producer Joe Frank passed away, after a long struggle with colon cancer.  Joe Frank was the radio producer’s radio producer.  He told stories that were thrillingly weird, deeply mischievous (and sometimes head-spinningly confusing!). He had a big impact on us at Radiolab.  For Jad, his Joe Frank moment happened in 2002, while sitting at a mixing console in an AM radio studio waiting to read the weather.  Joe Frank's Peabody Award-winning series \"Rent-A-Family” came on the air.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Time stood still.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>We’ve since learned that many of our peers have had similar Joe Frank moments.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>In this episode, we commemorate one of the greats with Brooke Gladstone from On the Media and Ira Glass from This American Life.</span> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>This episode was produced by Jad Abumrad with help from Kelly Prime and Sarah Qari. </em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>A very special thanks to Michal Story.</em></span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at<a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\"> Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-01-23T00:03:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"how-be-hero", "title"=>"How to Be a Hero", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast18howtohero.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=825085", "description"=>"<p>What are people thinking when they risk their lives for someone else? Are they making complicated calculations of risk or diving in without a second thought? Is heroism an act of sympathy or empathy?  </p>\n<p>A few years ago, we spoke with Walter F. Rutkowski about how the Carnegie Hero Fund selects its heroes, an honor the fund bestows upon ordinary people who have done extraordinary acts.</p>\n<p>When some of these heroes were asked what they were thinking when they leapt into action, they replied: they didn’t think about it, they just went in.</p>\n<p>Neuroscientist Robert Sapolsky says there is a certain kind of empathy that leads to action. But feeling the pain of another person deeply is not necessarily what makes a hero.  </p>\n<p><em>Our original episode was reported and produced by Lynn Levy and Tim Howard. This update was produced by Amanda Aronczyk.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"What&nbsp;are people thinking when they risk their lives for someone else?&nbsp; Is heroism an act of sympathy or empathy?&nbsp;&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1515539340000, "publish-at"=>"2018-01-09T18:09:00", "date-line-ts"=>1515539340, "estimated-duration"=>1718, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/EMPATHYPHOTO.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1400/1086/c/80/1/EMPATHYPHOTO.jpg", "h"=>1086, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/EMPATHYPHOTO.jpg", "w"=>1400, "id"=>171832, "credits-name"=>" Carnegie Hero Fund Commission"}, "objectID"=>"825085", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"How to Be a Hero", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>What are people thinking when they risk their lives for someone else? Are they making complicated calculations of risk or diving in without a second thought? Is heroism an act of sympathy or empathy?  </p>\n<p>A few years ago, we spoke with Walter F. Rutkowski about how the Carnegie Hero Fund selects its heroes, an honor the fund bestows upon ordinary people who have done extraordinary acts.</p>\n<p>When some of these heroes were asked what they were thinking when they leapt into action, they replied: they didn’t think about it, they just went in.</p>\n<p>Neuroscientist Robert Sapolsky says there is a certain kind of empathy that leads to action. But feeling the pain of another person deeply is not necessarily what makes a hero.  </p>\n<p><em>Our original episode was reported and produced by Lynn Levy and Tim Howard. This update was produced by Amanda Aronczyk.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2018-01-09T18:09:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2019=>[{"slug"=>"man-against-horse", "title"=>"Man Against Horse", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_membership/radiolab_membership19managainsthorse.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=983504", "description"=>"<p>This is a story about your butt. It’s a story about how you got your butt, why you have your butt, and how your butt might be one of the most important and essential things for you being you, for being human.<span> </span></p>\n<p>Today, reporters Heather Radke and Matt Kielty talk to two researchers who followed the butt from our ancient beginnings, through millions of years of evolution, and all the way to today, out to a valley in Arizona, where our butts are put to the ultimate test.<span> </span> </p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Heather Radke and Matt Kielty and was produced by Matt Kielty, Rachael Cusick and Simon Adler. Sound design and mixing by Jeremy Bloom. Fact-checking by Dorie Chevlen.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Michelle Legro.</em></p>\n<p><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></p>", "tease"=>"An exploration into our very beginnings, of the first strides we took to become us.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1577509440000, "publish-at"=>"2019-12-28T00:04:00", "date-line-ts"=>1577509440, "estimated-duration"=>3524, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/12/managainsthorse.JPG", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1860/c/80/2019/12/managainsthorse.JPG", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/12/managainsthorse.JPG", "w"=>1860, "id"=>187604, "credits-name"=>"Matt Kielty"}, "objectID"=>"983504", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Man Against Horse", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This is a story about your butt. It’s a story about how you got your butt, why you have your butt, and how your butt might be one of the most important and essential things for you being you, for being human.<span> </span></p>\n<p>Today, reporters Heather Radke and Matt Kielty talk to two researchers who followed the butt from our ancient beginnings, through millions of years of evolution, and all the way to today, out to a valley in Arizona, where our butts are put to the ultimate test.<span> </span> </p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Heather Radke and Matt Kielty and was produced by Matt Kielty, Rachael Cusick and Simon Adler. Sound design and mixing by Jeremy Bloom. Fact-checking by Dorie Chevlen.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Michelle Legro.</em></p>\n<p><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-12-28T00:04:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"there-and-back-again", "title"=>"There and Back Again", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19thereandbackagain.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=980741", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Here's a simple question: When an animal disappears in the winter, where does it go? Oddly enough, this question completely stumped European scientists for thousands of years. And even today, the more we learn about the comings and goings of the animals, the deeper the mystery seems to get. We visit a Bavarian farm with an 11 year old, follow warblers and wildebeests around the world, and get a totally new kind of view of the pulsing flow of animals across the globe.  </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>This episode was reported by Robert Krulwich and Jackson Roach and produced by Pat Walters, Matt Kielty, and Jackson Roach. </em></span><em>Mix &amp; original music by Jeremy Bloom.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span> </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>Special thanks to </em></span><em>Allison Shaw, David Barrie, Auriel Fournier, and Moritz Matschke.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">And check out:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Truth-About-Animals-Lovelorn-Wildlife/dp/0465094643\">The Truth about Animals</a> by Lucy Cooke</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/No-Way-Home-Decline-Migrations/dp/1559639857/ref=sr_1_7?crid=2TDT0UHCEU45P&amp;keywords=no+way+home&amp;qid=1576670927&amp;s=books&amp;sprefix=no+way+home%2Cstripbooks%2C146&amp;sr=1-7\">No Way Home: The Decline of the Great Animal Migrations</a> by David Wilcove </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>The migration <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=nUKh0fr1Od8\">video</a> Jad and Robert watch in this episode!</span></p>", "tease"=>"In 1822 a stork that got speared like a kabob helped baffled scientists answer a&nbsp;thousand-year-old question. Of course that led to another question, then another, then another ...", "publishTime"=>1576673880000, "publish-at"=>"2019-12-18T07:58:00", "date-line-ts"=>1576673880, "estimated-duration"=>2698, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/12/migrationphoto.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/533/l/80/2019/12/migrationphoto.jpeg", "h"=>533, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/12/migrationphoto.jpeg", "w"=>800, "id"=>187466, "credits-name"=>"Duncan Wright"}, "objectID"=>"980741", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"There and Back Again", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Here's a simple question: When an animal disappears in the winter, where does it go? Oddly enough, this question completely stumped European scientists for thousands of years. And even today, the more we learn about the comings and goings of the animals, the deeper the mystery seems to get. We visit a Bavarian farm with an 11 year old, follow warblers and wildebeests around the world, and get a totally new kind of view of the pulsing flow of animals across the globe.  </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>This episode was reported by Robert Krulwich and Jackson Roach and produced by Pat Walters, Matt Kielty, and Jackson Roach. </em></span><em>Mix &amp; original music by Jeremy Bloom.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span> </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em>Special thanks to </em></span><em>Allison Shaw, David Barrie, Auriel Fournier, and Moritz Matschke.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">And check out:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Truth-About-Animals-Lovelorn-Wildlife/dp/0465094643\">The Truth about Animals</a> by Lucy Cooke</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/No-Way-Home-Decline-Migrations/dp/1559639857/ref=sr_1_7?crid=2TDT0UHCEU45P&amp;keywords=no+way+home&amp;qid=1576670927&amp;s=books&amp;sprefix=no+way+home%2Cstripbooks%2C146&amp;sr=1-7\">No Way Home: The Decline of the Great Animal Migrations</a> by David Wilcove </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>The migration <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=nUKh0fr1Od8\">video</a> Jad and Robert watch in this episode!</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-12-18T07:58:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"things", "title"=>"Things", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab/radiolab053014.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=374816", "description"=>"<p><span>From a piece of the Wright brother's plane to a child’s sugar egg, today: Things! Important things, little things, personal things, things you can hold and things that can take hold of you. This hour, we investigate the objects around us, their power to move us, and whether it's better to look back or move on, hold on tight or just let go.</span></p>", "tease"=>"This hour we investigate the objects around us, their power to move us, and whether it's better to look back or move on, hold on tight or just let go.", "publishTime"=>1576191960000, "publish-at"=>"2019-12-12T18:06:00", "date-line-ts"=>1576170000, "estimated-duration"=>3678, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/1831225132_fcb1acc074_z.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/428/l/80/1/1831225132_fcb1acc074_z.jpg", "h"=>428, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/1831225132_fcb1acc074_z.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>118689, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Rachel Pasch"}, "objectID"=>"374816", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Things", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>From a piece of the Wright brother's plane to a child’s sugar egg, today: Things! Important things, little things, personal things, things you can hold and things that can take hold of you. This hour, we investigate the objects around us, their power to move us, and whether it's better to look back or move on, hold on tight or just let go.</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-12-12T18:06:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"breaking-bongo", "title"=>"Breaking Bongo ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19breakingbongo.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=975490", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Deep fake videos have the potential to make it impossible to sort fact from fiction. And some have argued that this blackhole of doubt will eventually send truth itself into a death spiral. But a series of recent events in the small African nation of Gabon suggest it's already happening. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Today, we follow a ragtag group of freedom fighters as they troll Gabon’s president - Ali Bongo - from afar. Using tweets, videos and the uncertainty they can carry, these insurgents test the limits of using truth to create political change and, confusingly, force us to ask: Can fake news be used for good?</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was reported and produced by Simon Adler.</em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1283\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1292\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1312\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1306\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1329\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<p><span><em><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></em></span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1338\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n<div class=\"story-credits__producing-org-credits producing-org-credits\"></div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"What happens when a ragtag bunch of freedom fighters troll their despotic dictator from afar?&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1574814913000, "publish-at"=>"2019-11-26T19:35:13", "date-line-ts"=>1574814913, "estimated-duration"=>3816, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/11/alibongophoto.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1241/c/80/2019/11/alibongophoto.jpg", "h"=>1241, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/11/alibongophoto.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>187058, "credits-name"=>"Travis Bozeman"}, "objectID"=>"975490", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Breaking Bongo ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Deep fake videos have the potential to make it impossible to sort fact from fiction. And some have argued that this blackhole of doubt will eventually send truth itself into a death spiral. But a series of recent events in the small African nation of Gabon suggest it's already happening. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Today, we follow a ragtag group of freedom fighters as they troll Gabon’s president - Ali Bongo - from afar. Using tweets, videos and the uncertainty they can carry, these insurgents test the limits of using truth to create political change and, confusingly, force us to ask: Can fake news be used for good?</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was reported and produced by Simon Adler.</em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1283\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1292\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1312\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1306\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1329\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<p><span><em><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></em></span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1338\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n<div class=\"story-credits__producing-org-credits producing-org-credits\"></div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-11-26T19:35:13", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"breaking-news", "title"=>"Breaking News", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19breakingnews.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=784333", "description"=>"<p>Today, we're re-releasing an old episode a<span>bout how hard it is getting to decipher fact from fiction. Because <span>next week, we’ll be putting out a story showing what happens when certain reality-altering tools get released into the wild. </span></span></p>\n<p>Simon Adler takes us down a technological rabbit hole of strangely contorted faces and words made out of thin air. And a wonderland full of computer scientists, journalists, and digital detectives forces us to rethink even the things we see with our very own eyes. </p>\n<p>Oh, and by the way, we decided to put the dark secrets we learned into action, and unleash <a href=\"http://www.futureoffakenews.com\">this</a> on the internet. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Reported by Simon Adler. Produced by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to everyone on the University of Southern California team who helped out with the facial manipulation: Kyle Olszewski, Koki Nagano, Ronald Yu, Yi Zhou, Jaewoo Seo, Shunsuke Saito, and Hao Li. Check out more of their work <a href=\"http://www.pinscreen.com\">pinscreen.com</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Special thanks also to Matthew Aylett, Supasorn Suwajanakorn, Rachel Axler, Angus Kneale, David Carroll, Amy Pearl and Nick Bilton. You can check out Nick’s latest book, </em>American Kingpin<em>, <a href=\"https://www.audible.com/pd/Nonfiction/American-Kingpin-Audiobook/B06Y1RH224?mkwid=DSATitle_dc&amp;pcrid=158258695635&amp;pmt=b&amp;pkw=&amp;source_code=GO1GB907OSH060513&amp;cvosrc=ppc%20dynamic%20search.google.97175169&amp;cvo_crid=158258695635&amp;cvo_pid=5075902449&amp;gclid=Cj0KCQjwnubLBRC_ARIsAASsNNk5E0ryUTkXEriNiKNrY4b4lTIFQuR9ktcO2nJFL65KxYsLXXZ6i_MaAuy8EALw_wcB\">here.</a></em><br><span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Today, two new technological tricks that together could&nbsp;invade our past selves&nbsp;and rewrite the rules of credibility. Also,&nbsp;we release something terrible into&nbsp;the world.", "publishTime"=>1574204700000, "publish-at"=>"2019-11-19T18:05:00", "date-line-ts"=>1574204700, "estimated-duration"=>2951, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/ObamaAdler2.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/985/658/l/80/1/ObamaAdler2.png", "h"=>658, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/ObamaAdler2.png", "w"=>985, "id"=>166896, "credits-name"=>"Photo by Simon Adler"}, "objectID"=>"784333", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Breaking News", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Today, we're re-releasing an old episode a<span>bout how hard it is getting to decipher fact from fiction. Because <span>next week, we’ll be putting out a story showing what happens when certain reality-altering tools get released into the wild. </span></span></p>\n<p>Simon Adler takes us down a technological rabbit hole of strangely contorted faces and words made out of thin air. And a wonderland full of computer scientists, journalists, and digital detectives forces us to rethink even the things we see with our very own eyes. </p>\n<p>Oh, and by the way, we decided to put the dark secrets we learned into action, and unleash <a href=\"http://www.futureoffakenews.com\">this</a> on the internet. </p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Reported by Simon Adler. Produced by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to everyone on the University of Southern California team who helped out with the facial manipulation: Kyle Olszewski, Koki Nagano, Ronald Yu, Yi Zhou, Jaewoo Seo, Shunsuke Saito, and Hao Li. Check out more of their work <a href=\"http://www.pinscreen.com\">pinscreen.com</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Special thanks also to Matthew Aylett, Supasorn Suwajanakorn, Rachel Axler, Angus Kneale, David Carroll, Amy Pearl and Nick Bilton. You can check out Nick’s latest book, </em>American Kingpin<em>, <a href=\"https://www.audible.com/pd/Nonfiction/American-Kingpin-Audiobook/B06Y1RH224?mkwid=DSATitle_dc&amp;pcrid=158258695635&amp;pmt=b&amp;pkw=&amp;source_code=GO1GB907OSH060513&amp;cvosrc=ppc%20dynamic%20search.google.97175169&amp;cvo_crid=158258695635&amp;cvo_pid=5075902449&amp;gclid=Cj0KCQjwnubLBRC_ARIsAASsNNk5E0ryUTkXEriNiKNrY4b4lTIFQuR9ktcO2nJFL65KxYsLXXZ6i_MaAuy8EALw_wcB\">here.</a></em><br><span></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-11-19T18:05:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dolly-partons-america-neon-moss", "title"=>"Dolly Parton's America: Neon Moss", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19neonmoss.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=972038", "description"=>"<p><span><span>Today on Radiolab, we're bringing you the fourth episode of Jad's special series,</span><span><span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/dolly-partons-america\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\"><span>Dolly Parton's America</span></a></span><span>. In this episode, Jad</span> goes back up the mountain to visit Dolly’s actual Tennessee mountain home, where she tells stories about her first trips out of the holler. Back on the mountaintop, standing under the rain by the Little Pigeon River, the trip triggers memories of Jad’s first visit to his father's childhood home, and opens the gateway to dizzying stories of music and migration.</span></p>\n<p><span><em><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></em></span></p>", "tease"=>"On the mountaintop, we go back in time,&nbsp;and across the world - to discover&nbsp;the unlikely connection between Dolly's home and Jad's father Naji.", "publishTime"=>1573181160000, "publish-at"=>"2019-11-07T21:46:00", "date-line-ts"=>1573181160, "estimated-duration"=>2702, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/10/Neon-Moss-DollyParton.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1200/900/l/80/2019/10/Neon-Moss-DollyParton.jpg", "h"=>900, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Neon Moss Dolly Parton's America", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/10/Neon-Moss-DollyParton.jpg", "w"=>1200, "id"=>186563, "credits-name"=>"Christine De Carvalho"}, "objectID"=>"972038", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dolly Parton's America: Neon Moss", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span><span>Today on Radiolab, we're bringing you the fourth episode of Jad's special series,</span><span><span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/dolly-partons-america\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\"><span>Dolly Parton's America</span></a></span><span>. In this episode, Jad</span> goes back up the mountain to visit Dolly’s actual Tennessee mountain home, where she tells stories about her first trips out of the holler. Back on the mountaintop, standing under the rain by the Little Pigeon River, the trip triggers memories of Jad’s first visit to his father's childhood home, and opens the gateway to dizzying stories of music and migration.</span></p>\n<p><span><em><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-11-07T21:46:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"songs-cross-borders", "title"=>"Songs that Cross Borders", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19songsthatcrossborders.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=970220", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Coming off our adventures with Square Dancing, and Jad's dive into the world of</span><span> Dolly Parton, we look back at one our favorites. </span><span>About a decade ago, we found out that American country music is surprising popular in places like Zimbabwe, Thailand, and South Africa. Aaron Fox, an anthropologist of music at Columbia University, tells us that quite simply, country music tells a story that a lot of us get. Then, intrepid international reporter Gregory Warner takes us along on one of his very first forays into another country, where he discovers an unexpected </span><span>taste of home.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></em><br></span></p>\n<p class=\"p3\"><span>Aaron Foxes book: <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0822333481/wnycorg-20\"><span><em>Real Country: Music And Language In Working-Class Culture</em></span></a></span><span> </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Gregory Warner's podcast <a href=\"https://www.npr.org/podcasts/510324/rough-translation\">Rough Translation </a></span></p>", "tease"=>"We explore songs with the power to transcend language and the triumphant return of the Elvis of Afghanistan.", "publishTime"=>1572400380000, "publish-at"=>"2019-10-29T21:53:00", "date-line-ts"=>1572400380, "estimated-duration"=>1562, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/10/songsthatcross.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/536/l/80/2019/10/songsthatcross.jpg", "h"=>536, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/10/songsthatcross.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>186596, "credits-name"=>"Megan Westerby"}, "objectID"=>"970220", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Songs that Cross Borders", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Coming off our adventures with Square Dancing, and Jad's dive into the world of</span><span> Dolly Parton, we look back at one our favorites. </span><span>About a decade ago, we found out that American country music is surprising popular in places like Zimbabwe, Thailand, and South Africa. Aaron Fox, an anthropologist of music at Columbia University, tells us that quite simply, country music tells a story that a lot of us get. Then, intrepid international reporter Gregory Warner takes us along on one of his very first forays into another country, where he discovers an unexpected </span><span>taste of home.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span><em><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></em><br></span></p>\n<p class=\"p3\"><span>Aaron Foxes book: <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0822333481/wnycorg-20\"><span><em>Real Country: Music And Language In Working-Class Culture</em></span></a></span><span> </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Gregory Warner's podcast <a href=\"https://www.npr.org/podcasts/510324/rough-translation\">Rough Translation </a></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-10-29T21:53:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"birdie-cage", "title"=>"Birdie in the Cage", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19birdieinthecage.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=968572", "description"=>"<p><span>People have been doing the square dance since before the Declaration of Independence. But does that mean it should be THE American folk dance? That question took us on a journey from Appalachian front porches, to dance classes across our nation, to the halls of Congress, and finally a Kansas City convention center. And along the way, we uncovered a secret history of square dancing that made us see how much of our national identity we could stuff into that square, and what it means for a dance to be of the people, by the people, and for the people. </span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Jim Mayo, Claude Fowler, Paul Gifford, Jim Maczko, Jim Davis, Paul Moore, Jack Pladdys, Mary Jane Wegener, Kinsey Brooke and Connie Keener.</em> </p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Tracie Hunte and produced by Annie McEwen, Tracie Hunte, and Matt Kielty. Mix by Jeremy Bloom.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span>Check out Phil Jamison's book,  “<a href=\"http://www.philjamison.com/hoedowns-reels-and-frolics\">Hoedowns, Reels, and Frolics: Roots and Branches of Southern Appalachian Dance</a>”</span></p>\n<p>Watch this 1948 <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lVwhobTwRuo\">Lucky Strike Cigarette Square Dancing Commercial</a></p>\n<p>A rare image of <a href=\"https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/young-african-americans-square-dance-at-the-guys-and-dolls-news-photo/576823714?adppopup=true\">Black Square Dancers in 1948</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://squaredancehistory.org/\">The Square Dance History Project</a></p>\n<p>Read “<a href=\"https://qz.com/1153516/americas-wholesome-square-dancing-tradition-is-a-tool-of-white-supremacy/\">America’s Wholesome Square Dancing Tradition is a Tool of White Supremacy</a>,” by Robyn Pennachia for Quartz</p>\n<p>And Pennachia’s <a href=\"https://twitter.com/RobynElyse/status/938916412248199168?s=20\">original Twitter thread</a></p>\n<p>Read “<a href=\"https://juliannemangin.com/the-state-folk-dance-conspiracy/\">The State Folk Dance Conspiracy: Fabricating a National Folk Dance</a>,” by Julianne Mangin</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Can you fit the identity of a whole nation into a dance? Of course not. But we tried anyway.", "publishTime"=>1571808180000, "publish-at"=>"2019-10-23T01:23:00", "date-line-ts"=>1571808180, "estimated-duration"=>2719, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/10/squaredancing.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/l/80/2019/10/squaredancing.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/10/squaredancing.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>186471, "credits-name"=>"The National Square Dancing Convention in Atlanta, Georgia in 2019."}, "objectID"=>"968572", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Birdie in the Cage", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>People have been doing the square dance since before the Declaration of Independence. But does that mean it should be THE American folk dance? That question took us on a journey from Appalachian front porches, to dance classes across our nation, to the halls of Congress, and finally a Kansas City convention center. And along the way, we uncovered a secret history of square dancing that made us see how much of our national identity we could stuff into that square, and what it means for a dance to be of the people, by the people, and for the people. </span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Jim Mayo, Claude Fowler, Paul Gifford, Jim Maczko, Jim Davis, Paul Moore, Jack Pladdys, Mary Jane Wegener, Kinsey Brooke and Connie Keener.</em> </p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Tracie Hunte and produced by Annie McEwen, Tracie Hunte, and Matt Kielty. Mix by Jeremy Bloom.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span>Check out Phil Jamison's book,  “<a href=\"http://www.philjamison.com/hoedowns-reels-and-frolics\">Hoedowns, Reels, and Frolics: Roots and Branches of Southern Appalachian Dance</a>”</span></p>\n<p>Watch this 1948 <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lVwhobTwRuo\">Lucky Strike Cigarette Square Dancing Commercial</a></p>\n<p>A rare image of <a href=\"https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/young-african-americans-square-dance-at-the-guys-and-dolls-news-photo/576823714?adppopup=true\">Black Square Dancers in 1948</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://squaredancehistory.org/\">The Square Dance History Project</a></p>\n<p>Read “<a href=\"https://qz.com/1153516/americas-wholesome-square-dancing-tradition-is-a-tool-of-white-supremacy/\">America’s Wholesome Square Dancing Tradition is a Tool of White Supremacy</a>,” by Robyn Pennachia for Quartz</p>\n<p>And Pennachia’s <a href=\"https://twitter.com/RobynElyse/status/938916412248199168?s=20\">original Twitter thread</a></p>\n<p>Read “<a href=\"https://juliannemangin.com/the-state-folk-dance-conspiracy/\">The State Folk Dance Conspiracy: Fabricating a National Folk Dance</a>,” by Julianne Mangin</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-10-23T01:23:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-presents-dolly-partons-america", "title"=>"Radiolab Presents: Dolly Parton's America", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19sadasssongs.mp3?aw_0_awz.podcast_clash_enabled=1&awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=967473", "description"=>"<p><span>Radiolab creator and host Jad Abumrad spent the last two years following around music legend Dolly Parton, and we're here to say you should tune in! In this episode of Radiolab, we showcase the first of Jad's special series,</span><span><span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/dolly-partons-america\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\"><span>Dolly Parton's America</span></a></span><span>. In this intensely divided moment, one of the few things everyone still seems to agree on is Dolly Parton—but why? That simple question leads to a deeply personal, historical, and musical rethinking of one of America’s great icons. </span></p>\n<p><span>We begin with a simple question: How did the queen of the boob joke become a feminist icon? Helen Morales, author of “Pilgrimage to Dollywood,” gave us a stern directive – look at the lyrics! </span><span>So we dive into </span><span>Dolly’s discography, starting with the early period of what Dolly calls “sad ass songs” to find remarkably prescient words of female pain, slut-shaming, domestic violence, and women being locked away in asylums by cheating husbands. We explore how Dolly took the centuries-old tradition of the Appalachian “murder ballad”—an oral tradition of men singing songs about brutally killing women—and flipped the script, singing from the woman’s point of view. And as her career progresses, the songs expand beyond the pain to tell tales of leaving abuse behind.</span></p>\n<p><span>How can such pro-woman lyrics come from someone who despises the word feminism? Dolly explains.  </span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><span>Check out Dolly Parton's America here at: </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/dolly-partons-america\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\"><span>https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/dolly-partons-america</span></a><span> </span></span></p>", "tease"=>"We enter the Dollyverse.", "publishTime"=>1571188020000, "publish-at"=>"2019-10-15T21:07:00", "date-line-ts"=>1571188080, "estimated-duration"=>3634, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"A graphic of Dolly Parton holding a lamb, both with crowns on their heads. They are against a floral background.", "name"=>"2019/09/dolly-parton-america-trailer-image.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/l/80/2019/09/dolly-parton-america-trailer-image.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Dolly Parton's America", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/09/dolly-parton-america-trailer-image.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>186079, "credits-name"=>"WNYC Studios"}, "objectID"=>"967473", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab Presents: Dolly Parton's America", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Radiolab creator and host Jad Abumrad spent the last two years following around music legend Dolly Parton, and we're here to say you should tune in! In this episode of Radiolab, we showcase the first of Jad's special series,</span><span><span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/dolly-partons-america\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\"><span>Dolly Parton's America</span></a></span><span>. In this intensely divided moment, one of the few things everyone still seems to agree on is Dolly Parton—but why? That simple question leads to a deeply personal, historical, and musical rethinking of one of America’s great icons. </span></p>\n<p><span>We begin with a simple question: How did the queen of the boob joke become a feminist icon? Helen Morales, author of “Pilgrimage to Dollywood,” gave us a stern directive – look at the lyrics! </span><span>So we dive into </span><span>Dolly’s discography, starting with the early period of what Dolly calls “sad ass songs” to find remarkably prescient words of female pain, slut-shaming, domestic violence, and women being locked away in asylums by cheating husbands. We explore how Dolly took the centuries-old tradition of the Appalachian “murder ballad”—an oral tradition of men singing songs about brutally killing women—and flipped the script, singing from the woman’s point of view. And as her career progresses, the songs expand beyond the pain to tell tales of leaving abuse behind.</span></p>\n<p><span>How can such pro-woman lyrics come from someone who despises the word feminism? Dolly explains.  </span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><span>Check out Dolly Parton's America here at: </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/dolly-partons-america\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\"><span>https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/dolly-partons-america</span></a><span> </span></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-10-15T21:07:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"silky-love", "title"=>"Silky Love", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19silkylove.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=964229", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>We eat eels in sushi, stews, and pasta. Eels eat <em>anything</em>. Also they can survive outside of water for hours and live for up to 80 years. But this slippery snake of the sea harbors an even deeper mystery, one that has tormented the minds of Aristotle and Sigmund Freud and apparently the entire country of Italy: Where do they come from? We travel from the estuaries of New York to the darkest part of the ocean in search of the limits of human knowledge.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty and Becca Bressler. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Further reading:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Lucy Cooke's book <em><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Truth-About-Animals-Lovelorn-Wildlife/dp/0465094643\">The Truth about Animals</a>!</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Chris Bowser's <a href=\"https://www.dec.ny.gov/lands/49580.html\">Eel Research Project</a></p>", "tease"=>"We scour the Atlantic Ocean for an answer to one of life&rsquo;s slipperiest mysteries.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1569565080000, "publish-at"=>"2019-09-27T02:18:00", "date-line-ts"=>1569565020, "estimated-duration"=>2143, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/09/silkylove.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2019/09/silkylove.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/09/silkylove.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>186037, "credits-name"=>"Hudson River Estuary Program"}, "objectID"=>"964229", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Silky Love", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>We eat eels in sushi, stews, and pasta. Eels eat <em>anything</em>. Also they can survive outside of water for hours and live for up to 80 years. But this slippery snake of the sea harbors an even deeper mystery, one that has tormented the minds of Aristotle and Sigmund Freud and apparently the entire country of Italy: Where do they come from? We travel from the estuaries of New York to the darkest part of the ocean in search of the limits of human knowledge.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty and Becca Bressler. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Further reading:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Lucy Cooke's book <em><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Truth-About-Animals-Lovelorn-Wildlife/dp/0465094643\">The Truth about Animals</a>!</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Chris Bowser's <a href=\"https://www.dec.ny.gov/lands/49580.html\">Eel Research Project</a></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-09-27T02:18:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"104010-one-good-deed-deserves-another", "title"=>"Tit for Tat", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19titfortat.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=104010", "description"=>"<p>In the early 60s, <strong>Robert Axelrod </strong>was a math major messing around with refrigerator-sized computers. Then a dramatic global crisis made him wonder about the space between a rock and a hard place, and whether being good may be a good strategy. With help from <strong>Andrew Zolli </strong>and <strong>Steve Strogatz</strong>, we tackle the prisoner’s dilemma, a classic thought experiment, and learn about a simple strategy to navigate the waters of cooperation and betrayal. Then Axelrod, along with <strong>Stanley Weintraub</strong>, takes us back to the trenches of World War I, to the winter of 1914, and an unlikely Christmas party along the Western Front.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Do we really live in a selfish, dog-eat-dog world? Or has evolution carved out a hidden code that rewards genuine cooperation?", "publishTime"=>1568768580000, "publish-at"=>"2019-09-17T21:03:00", "date-line-ts"=>1568736000, "estimated-duration"=>1485, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"tic tac toe", "name"=>"photologue/photos/tic tac toe.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/427/c/80/photologue/photos/tic tac toe.jpg", "h"=>427, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"tic tac toe", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/edri/3212445507/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/tic tac toe.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>37555, "credits-name"=>"cigno5!"}, "objectID"=>"104010", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Tit for Tat", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In the early 60s, <strong>Robert Axelrod </strong>was a math major messing around with refrigerator-sized computers. Then a dramatic global crisis made him wonder about the space between a rock and a hard place, and whether being good may be a good strategy. With help from <strong>Andrew Zolli </strong>and <strong>Steve Strogatz</strong>, we tackle the prisoner’s dilemma, a classic thought experiment, and learn about a simple strategy to navigate the waters of cooperation and betrayal. Then Axelrod, along with <strong>Stanley Weintraub</strong>, takes us back to the trenches of World War I, to the winter of 1914, and an unlikely Christmas party along the Western Front.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-09-17T21:03:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"whats-left-when-youre-right", "title"=>"What's Left When You're Right?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19whatsleftwhenright.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=353132", "description"=>"<p>More often than not, a fight is just a fight... Someone wins, someone loses. But this hour, we have a series of face-offs that shine a light on the human condition, the benefit of coming at something from a different side, and the price of being right.</p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Mark Dresser for the use of <a href=\"http://www.cleanfeed-records.com/disco2US.asp?intID=249\">his music</a>.</em></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"From the stage to the cage, a series of showdowns that leave us wondering about the price of being right ... or coming from the left.", "publishTime"=>1567721040000, "publish-at"=>"2019-09-05T18:04:00", "date-line-ts"=>1567699200, "estimated-duration"=>3644, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/5548413026_896de1e3a7.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/375/500/c/80/1/5548413026_896de1e3a7.jpg", "h"=>500, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/5548413026_896de1e3a7.jpg", "w"=>375, "id"=>112995, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"353132", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"What's Left When You're Right?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>More often than not, a fight is just a fight... Someone wins, someone loses. But this hour, we have a series of face-offs that shine a light on the human condition, the benefit of coming at something from a different side, and the price of being right.</p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Mark Dresser for the use of <a href=\"http://www.cleanfeed-records.com/disco2US.asp?intID=249\">his music</a>.</em></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-09-05T18:04:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"memory-palace", "title"=>"The Memory Palace", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19thememorypalace.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=958870", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Nate DiMeo was preoccupied with the past, and how we relate to it, from a very young age. For the last decade or so he's been scratching this itch with <a href=\"http://www.thememorypalace.org/\"><strong>The Memory Palace</strong></a>, a <a href=\"https://apple.co/thememorypalace\"><strong>podcast</strong></a> he created. He does things very differently than we do, but his show has captured the hearts of Radiolab staffers, past and present, time and time again. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>So we decided to get Nate into the studio to share a few of his episodes with us and talk to us about how and why he does what he does. He brought us stories about the Morse Code, the draft lottery, and then he hit us with a brand new episode about a bull on trial, that bounces off a story we did pretty recently.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>More <a href=\"https://pvi.virginia.edu/gabriel-rosenberg-no-scrubs-livestock-breeding-state-power-and-eugenic-knowledge-in-the-early-20th-century-united-states/\"><span><strong>history</strong></span></a> on scrub bulls.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Follow <a href=\"https://twitter.com/thememorypalace\"><span><strong>@thememorypalace</strong></span></a> on Twitter.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was produced with help from Bethel Habte. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span>Other staff favorites:</span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"http://thememorypalace.us/2015/07/zulu-charlie-romeo/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\">Zulu Charlie Romeo</a></span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"http://thememorypalace.us/2015/08/notes-on-an-imagined-plaque/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\">Notes on an Imagined Plaque</a></span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"http://thememorypalace.us/2018/04/snakes/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\">Snakes!</a></span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"http://thememorypalace.us/2018/03/outliers/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\">Outliers</a></span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"Historians say the&nbsp;past is a foreign country,&nbsp;but often&nbsp;it feels more like a dream. Today we talk to someone&nbsp;who has mastered the art of&nbsp;navigating the&nbsp;dreamscapes of our past.", "publishTime"=>1566968520000, "publish-at"=>"2019-08-28T01:02:00", "date-line-ts"=>1566968520, "estimated-duration"=>2494, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/08/natedimeo_xq6GPNw.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1387/1178/c/80/2019/08/natedimeo_xq6GPNw.jpg", "h"=>1178, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/08/natedimeo_xq6GPNw.jpg", "w"=>1387, "id"=>185529, "credits-name"=>"Nate DiMeo"}, "objectID"=>"958870", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Memory Palace", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Nate DiMeo was preoccupied with the past, and how we relate to it, from a very young age. For the last decade or so he's been scratching this itch with <a href=\"http://www.thememorypalace.org/\"><strong>The Memory Palace</strong></a>, a <a href=\"https://apple.co/thememorypalace\"><strong>podcast</strong></a> he created. He does things very differently than we do, but his show has captured the hearts of Radiolab staffers, past and present, time and time again. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>So we decided to get Nate into the studio to share a few of his episodes with us and talk to us about how and why he does what he does. He brought us stories about the Morse Code, the draft lottery, and then he hit us with a brand new episode about a bull on trial, that bounces off a story we did pretty recently.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>More <a href=\"https://pvi.virginia.edu/gabriel-rosenberg-no-scrubs-livestock-breeding-state-power-and-eugenic-knowledge-in-the-early-20th-century-united-states/\"><span><strong>history</strong></span></a> on scrub bulls.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Follow <a href=\"https://twitter.com/thememorypalace\"><span><strong>@thememorypalace</strong></span></a> on Twitter.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was produced with help from Bethel Habte. </em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span>Other staff favorites:</span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"http://thememorypalace.us/2015/07/zulu-charlie-romeo/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\">Zulu Charlie Romeo</a></span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"http://thememorypalace.us/2015/08/notes-on-an-imagined-plaque/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\">Notes on an Imagined Plaque</a></span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"http://thememorypalace.us/2018/04/snakes/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\">Snakes!</a></span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"http://thememorypalace.us/2018/03/outliers/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\">Outliers</a></span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-08-28T01:02:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-right-be-forgotten", "title"=>"Right to be Forgotten", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19rtbf.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=958032", "description"=>"<p><span>In an online world, that story about you lives forever. The tipsy photograph of you at the college football game? It’s up there. That news article about the political rally you were marching at? It’s up there. A DUI? That’s there, too. But what if ... it wasn’t.</span></p>\n<p><span>In Cleveland, Ohio, a group of journalists are trying out an experiment that has the potential to turn things upside down: they are unpublishing content they’ve already published. Photographs, names, entire articles. Every month or so, they get together to decide what content stays, and what content goes. On today’s episode, reporter Molly Webster goes inside the room where the decisions are being made, listening case-by-case as editors decide who, or what, gets to be deleted. It’s a story about time and memory; mistakes and second chances; and society as we know it.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Molly Webster, and produced by Molly Webster and Bethel Habte. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Kathy English, David Erdos, Ed Haber, Brewster Kahle, Imani Leonard, Ruth Samuel, </span>James Bennett II, Alice Wilder, </em><span><em>Alex Overington,</em> </span><em><span>Jane Kamensky and all the people who helped shape this story.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></p>\n<p><span>To learn more about Cleveland.com’s “right to be forgotten experiment,” check out the very first <a href=\"https://www.cleveland.com/opinion/2018/07/right_to_be_forgotten_clevelan.html\">column</a> Molly read about the project.</span></p>", "tease"=>"Today, we find ourselves in a room in Cleveland, Ohio, where a group of journalists are challenging the way we think about newspapers, and ourselves.", "publishTime"=>1566560640000, "publish-at"=>"2019-08-23T07:44:00", "date-line-ts"=>1566560640, "estimated-duration"=>2842, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/08/RTBF.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/770/511/c/80/2019/08/RTBF.png", "h"=>511, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/08/RTBF.png", "w"=>770, "id"=>185456, "credits-name"=>"Santa Cruz County Sheriff's Office"}, "objectID"=>"958032", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Right to be Forgotten", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In an online world, that story about you lives forever. The tipsy photograph of you at the college football game? It’s up there. That news article about the political rally you were marching at? It’s up there. A DUI? That’s there, too. But what if ... it wasn’t.</span></p>\n<p><span>In Cleveland, Ohio, a group of journalists are trying out an experiment that has the potential to turn things upside down: they are unpublishing content they’ve already published. Photographs, names, entire articles. Every month or so, they get together to decide what content stays, and what content goes. On today’s episode, reporter Molly Webster goes inside the room where the decisions are being made, listening case-by-case as editors decide who, or what, gets to be deleted. It’s a story about time and memory; mistakes and second chances; and society as we know it.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Molly Webster, and produced by Molly Webster and Bethel Habte. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Kathy English, David Erdos, Ed Haber, Brewster Kahle, Imani Leonard, Ruth Samuel, </span>James Bennett II, Alice Wilder, </em><span><em>Alex Overington,</em> </span><em><span>Jane Kamensky and all the people who helped shape this story.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></p>\n<p><span>To learn more about Cleveland.com’s “right to be forgotten experiment,” check out the very first <a href=\"https://www.cleveland.com/opinion/2018/07/right_to_be_forgotten_clevelan.html\">column</a> Molly read about the project.</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-08-23T07:44:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-more-perfect-cruel-and-unusual", "title"=>" More Perfect: Cruel and Unusual", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19cruelandunusual.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=955352", "description"=>"<p>On the inaugural episode of<span> </span><em>More Perfect</em>, we explore three little words embedded in the 8th Amendment of the U.S. Constitution: “cruel and unusual.” America has long wrestled with this concept in the context of our strongest punishment, the death penalty. A majority of “we the people” (<a href=\"http://www.gallup.com/poll/1606/death-penalty.aspx\">61 percent</a>, to be exact) are in favor of having it, but inside the Supreme Court, opinions have evolved over time in surprising ways.</p>\n<p>And outside of the court, the debate drove one woman in the UK to take on the U.S. death penalty system from Europe. It also caused states to resuscitate old methods used for executing prisoners on death row. And perhaps more than anything, it forced a conversation on what constitutes cruel and unusual punishment.</p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Claire Phillips, Nina Perry, Stephanie Jenkins, Ralph Dellapiana, Byrd Pinkerton, Elisabeth Semel, Christina Spaulding, and The Marshall Project</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Also! We’re working on collecting some audience feedback so we can do a better job of getting our show out to all of you, interacting with you, and reaching new people. We’d love to hear from you. Go to <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/survey\">www.radiolab.org/survey</a> to participate. </em></p>", "tease"=>"We explore three little words embedded in the 8th Amendment of the U.S. Constitution: &ldquo;cruel and unusual.&rdquo;", "publishTime"=>1565313120000, "publish-at"=>"2019-08-08T21:12:00", "date-line-ts"=>1565313120, "estimated-duration"=>3484, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/08/mayapic.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/533/c/80/2019/08/mayapic.jpg", "h"=>533, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/08/mayapic.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>185197, "credits-name"=>"Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"955352", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>" More Perfect: Cruel and Unusual", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>On the inaugural episode of<span> </span><em>More Perfect</em>, we explore three little words embedded in the 8th Amendment of the U.S. Constitution: “cruel and unusual.” America has long wrestled with this concept in the context of our strongest punishment, the death penalty. A majority of “we the people” (<a href=\"http://www.gallup.com/poll/1606/death-penalty.aspx\">61 percent</a>, to be exact) are in favor of having it, but inside the Supreme Court, opinions have evolved over time in surprising ways.</p>\n<p>And outside of the court, the debate drove one woman in the UK to take on the U.S. death penalty system from Europe. It also caused states to resuscitate old methods used for executing prisoners on death row. And perhaps more than anything, it forced a conversation on what constitutes cruel and unusual punishment.</p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Claire Phillips, Nina Perry, Stephanie Jenkins, Ralph Dellapiana, Byrd Pinkerton, Elisabeth Semel, Christina Spaulding, and The Marshall Project</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Also! We’re working on collecting some audience feedback so we can do a better job of getting our show out to all of you, interacting with you, and reaching new people. We’d love to hear from you. Go to <a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/survey\">www.radiolab.org/survey</a> to participate. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-08-08T21:12:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"worlds-smartest-animal", "title"=>"G: The World's Smartest Animal", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19worldssmartestanimal_intelligence-series.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=953534", "description"=>"<p><span>This episode begins with a rant. This rant, in particular, comes from Dan Engber - a science writer who loves animals but despises animal intelligence research. Dan told us that so much of the way we study animals involves tests that we think show a </span><em><span>human</span></em><span> is smart ... not the animals we intend to study. </span></p>\n<p><span>Dan’s rant got us thinking: What </span><em><span>is</span></em><span> the smartest animal in the world? And if we threw out our human intelligence rubric, is there a fair way to figure it out?</span></p>\n<p><span>Obviously, there is. And it’s a live game show, judged by Jad, Robert … and a dog.</span></p>\n<p>For the last episode of G, Radiolab’s miniseries on intelligence, we’re sharing that game show with you. It was recorded as a live show back in May 2019 at the Greene Space in New York City. We invited two science writers, Dan Engber and Laurel Braitman, and two comedians, Tracy Clayton and Jordan Mendoza, to compete against one another to find the world’s smartest animal. What resulted were a series of funny, delightful stories about unexpectedly smart animals and a shift in the way we think about intelligence across all the animals - including us.</p>\n<p>Check out the video of our live event <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/thegreenespace/videos/whats-the-smartest-animal-in-the-world-radiolab-is-live-from-the-greene-space-at/1680681042075725/\">here</a>! </p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Rachael Cusick and Pat Walters, with help from Nora Keller and Suzie Lechtenberg. Fact-checking by Michelle Harris and Dorie Chevlin.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Bill Berloni and Macy (the dog) and everyone at The Greene Space.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab’s “G” is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"What&rsquo;s the smartest animal in the world? We figure it out the best way we know how... an animal game show.", "publishTime"=>1564459740000, "publish-at"=>"2019-07-30T00:09:00", "date-line-ts"=>1564459740, "estimated-duration"=>2917, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/07/DLRadiolab6final.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2019/07/DLRadiolab6final.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/07/DLRadiolab6final.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>185022, "credits-name"=>"Deborah Lee"}, "objectID"=>"953534", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"G: The World's Smartest Animal", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This episode begins with a rant. This rant, in particular, comes from Dan Engber - a science writer who loves animals but despises animal intelligence research. Dan told us that so much of the way we study animals involves tests that we think show a </span><em><span>human</span></em><span> is smart ... not the animals we intend to study. </span></p>\n<p><span>Dan’s rant got us thinking: What </span><em><span>is</span></em><span> the smartest animal in the world? And if we threw out our human intelligence rubric, is there a fair way to figure it out?</span></p>\n<p><span>Obviously, there is. And it’s a live game show, judged by Jad, Robert … and a dog.</span></p>\n<p>For the last episode of G, Radiolab’s miniseries on intelligence, we’re sharing that game show with you. It was recorded as a live show back in May 2019 at the Greene Space in New York City. We invited two science writers, Dan Engber and Laurel Braitman, and two comedians, Tracy Clayton and Jordan Mendoza, to compete against one another to find the world’s smartest animal. What resulted were a series of funny, delightful stories about unexpectedly smart animals and a shift in the way we think about intelligence across all the animals - including us.</p>\n<p>Check out the video of our live event <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/thegreenespace/videos/whats-the-smartest-animal-in-the-world-radiolab-is-live-from-the-greene-space-at/1680681042075725/\">here</a>! </p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Rachael Cusick and Pat Walters, with help from Nora Keller and Suzie Lechtenberg. Fact-checking by Michelle Harris and Dorie Chevlin.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Bill Berloni and Macy (the dog) and everyone at The Greene Space.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab’s “G” is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-07-30T00:09:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"g-unnatural-selection", "title"=>"G: Unnatural Selection", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19unnaturalselection_intelligence-series.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=952872", "description"=>"<p><span>This past fall, a scientist named Steve Hsu made headlines with a provocative announcement. He would start selling a genetic intelligence test to couples doing IVF: a sophisticated prediction tool, built on big data and machine learning, designed to help couples select the best embryo in their batch. We wondered, how does that work? What can the test really say? And do we want to live in a world where certain people can decide how smart their babies will be?</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Simon Adler, with help from Rachael Cusick and Pat Walters. Fact-checking by Michelle Harris. Engineering help from Jeremy Bloom.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Catherine Bliss.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab’s “G” is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"A scientist launches a controversial genetic test for intelligence: what does it really tell us?", "publishTime"=>1564107300000, "publish-at"=>"2019-07-25T22:15:00", "date-line-ts"=>1564107300, "estimated-duration"=>2047, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/07/intel6art.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2019/07/intel6art.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/07/intel6art.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>184969, "credits-name"=>"Deborah Lee"}, "objectID"=>"952872", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"G: Unnatural Selection", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This past fall, a scientist named Steve Hsu made headlines with a provocative announcement. He would start selling a genetic intelligence test to couples doing IVF: a sophisticated prediction tool, built on big data and machine learning, designed to help couples select the best embryo in their batch. We wondered, how does that work? What can the test really say? And do we want to live in a world where certain people can decide how smart their babies will be?</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Simon Adler, with help from Rachael Cusick and Pat Walters. Fact-checking by Michelle Harris. Engineering help from Jeremy Bloom.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Catherine Bliss.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab’s “G” is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-07-25T22:15:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"g-relative-genius", "title"=>"G: Relative Genius", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19relativegenius_intelligence-series.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=946682", "description"=>"<p><span>Albert Einstein asked that when he died, his body be cremated and his ashes be scattered in a secret location. He didn’t want his grave, or his body, becoming a shrine to his genius. When he passed away in the early morning hours of April, 18, 1955, his family knew his wishes. There was only one problem: the pathologist who did the autopsy had different plans.</span></p>\n<p><span>In the third episode of “G”, Radiolab’s miniseries on intelligence, we go on one of the strangest scavenger hunts for genius the world has ever seen. We follow Einstein’s stolen brain from that Princeton autopsy table, to a cider box in Wichita, Kansas, to labs all across the country. And eventually, beyond the brain itself entirely. All the while wondering, where exactly </span><em><span>is</span></em><span> the genius of a man who changed the way we view the world? </span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Rachael Cusick and Pat Walters, and produced by Bethel Habte, Rachael Cusick, and Pat Walters. Music by Alex Overington and Jad Abumrad. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to: Elanor Taylor, Claudia Kalb, Dustin O’Halloran, Tim Huson, The Einstein Papers Project, and all the physics for (us) dummies Youtube videos that accomplished the near-impossible feat of helping us understand relativity.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em>Radiolab’s “G” is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science.</em></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"When Albert Einstein died, someone stole his brain &mdash; and kicked off a scavenger hunt for genius that won&rsquo;t seem to let us go.", "publishTime"=>1561763340000, "publish-at"=>"2019-06-28T19:09:00", "date-line-ts"=>1561763280, "estimated-duration"=>3775, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/06/DLRadiolab3final.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1394/c/80/2019/06/DLRadiolab3final.jpg", "h"=>1394, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/06/DLRadiolab3final.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>184569, "credits-name"=>"Deborah Lee"}, "objectID"=>"946682", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"G: Relative Genius", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Albert Einstein asked that when he died, his body be cremated and his ashes be scattered in a secret location. He didn’t want his grave, or his body, becoming a shrine to his genius. When he passed away in the early morning hours of April, 18, 1955, his family knew his wishes. There was only one problem: the pathologist who did the autopsy had different plans.</span></p>\n<p><span>In the third episode of “G”, Radiolab’s miniseries on intelligence, we go on one of the strangest scavenger hunts for genius the world has ever seen. We follow Einstein’s stolen brain from that Princeton autopsy table, to a cider box in Wichita, Kansas, to labs all across the country. And eventually, beyond the brain itself entirely. All the while wondering, where exactly </span><em><span>is</span></em><span> the genius of a man who changed the way we view the world? </span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Rachael Cusick and Pat Walters, and produced by Bethel Habte, Rachael Cusick, and Pat Walters. Music by Alex Overington and Jad Abumrad. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to: Elanor Taylor, Claudia Kalb, Dustin O’Halloran, Tim Huson, The Einstein Papers Project, and all the physics for (us) dummies Youtube videos that accomplished the near-impossible feat of helping us understand relativity.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em>Radiolab’s “G” is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science.</em></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-06-28T19:09:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"g-problem-space", "title"=>"G: Problem Space", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19problemspace_intelligence-series.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=941851", "description"=>"<p><span>In the first episode of G, Radiolab’s miniseries on intelligence, we went back to the 1970s to meet a group of Black parents who put the IQ test on trial. The lawsuit, Larry P v Riles, ended with a ban on IQ tests for all Black students in the state of California, a ban that’s still in place today. </span></p>\n<p>This week, we meet the families in California dealing with that ban forty years later. Families the ban was designed to protect, but who now say it discriminates against their children. How much have IQ tests changed since the 70s? And can they be used for good? We talk to the people responsible for designing the most widely used modern IQ test, and along the way, we find out that at the very same moment the IQ test was being put on trial in California, on the other side of the country, it was being used to solve one of the biggest public health problems of the 20th century.</p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported and produced by Pat Walters, Rachael Cusick and Jad Abumrad, with production help from Bethel Habte.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Music by Alex Overington. Fact-checking by Diane Kelly.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Lee Romney, Chenjerai Kumanyika, Moira Gunn and Tech Nation, and Lee Rosevere for his song All the Answers.</em></p>\n<p><em>Radiolab’s “G” is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"Can IQ tests ever be used ... for good? This episode, we meet a few people who think they can be.", "publishTime"=>1560486300000, "publish-at"=>"2019-06-14T00:25:00", "date-line-ts"=>1560486300, "estimated-duration"=>2425, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/06/IntelligenceSeriesArt_QvSwLHv.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2019/06/IntelligenceSeriesArt_QvSwLHv.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/06/IntelligenceSeriesArt_QvSwLHv.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>184091, "credits-name"=>"Deborah Lee"}, "objectID"=>"941851", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"G: Problem Space", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In the first episode of G, Radiolab’s miniseries on intelligence, we went back to the 1970s to meet a group of Black parents who put the IQ test on trial. The lawsuit, Larry P v Riles, ended with a ban on IQ tests for all Black students in the state of California, a ban that’s still in place today. </span></p>\n<p>This week, we meet the families in California dealing with that ban forty years later. Families the ban was designed to protect, but who now say it discriminates against their children. How much have IQ tests changed since the 70s? And can they be used for good? We talk to the people responsible for designing the most widely used modern IQ test, and along the way, we find out that at the very same moment the IQ test was being put on trial in California, on the other side of the country, it was being used to solve one of the biggest public health problems of the 20th century.</p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported and produced by Pat Walters, Rachael Cusick and Jad Abumrad, with production help from Bethel Habte.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Music by Alex Overington. Fact-checking by Diane Kelly.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Lee Romney, Chenjerai Kumanyika, Moira Gunn and Tech Nation, and Lee Rosevere for his song All the Answers.</em></p>\n<p><em>Radiolab’s “G” is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-06-14T00:25:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"g-miseducation-larry-p", "title"=>"G: The Miseducation of Larry P", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19larryp1__intelligence-series.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=941108", "description"=>"<p><span>Are some ideas so dangerous we shouldn’t even talk about them? That question brought </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s senior editor, Pat Walters, to a subject that at first he thought was long gone: the measuring of human intelligence with IQ tests. Turns out, the tests are all around us. In the workplace. The criminal justice system. Even the NFL. And they’re massive in schools. More than a million US children are IQ tested every year.</span></p>\n<p>We begin Radiolab Presents: “G” with a sentence that stopped us all in our tracks: In the state of California, it is off-limits to administer an IQ test to a child if he or she is Black. That’s because of a little-known case called Larry P v Riles that in the 1970s … put the IQ test itself on trial. With the help of reporter Lee Romney, we investigate how that lawsuit came to be, where IQ tests came from, and what happened to one little boy who got caught in the crossfire.</p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported and produced by Lee Romney, Rachael Cusick and Pat Walters.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Music by Alex Overington. </span></em><em><span>Fact-checking by Diane Kelly.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Elie Mistal, Chenjerai Kumanyika, Amanda Stern, Nora Lyons, Ki Sung, Public Advocates, Michelle Wilson, Peter Fernandez, John Schaefer. Lee Romney’s reporting was supported in part by USC’s Center for Health Journalism.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Radiolab’s “G” is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science.</em></p>\n<p><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></em></p>", "tease"=>"More than a million American kids a year get IQ tested, but in the state of California, if your kid is Black, they almost surely won&rsquo;t be given one.", "publishTime"=>1559908680000, "publish-at"=>"2019-06-07T07:58:00", "date-line-ts"=>1559908680, "estimated-duration"=>3841, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/06/Larryp1art.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2019/06/Larryp1art.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/06/Larryp1art.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>184022, "credits-name"=>"Deborah Lee"}, "objectID"=>"941108", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"G: The Miseducation of Larry P", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Are some ideas so dangerous we shouldn’t even talk about them? That question brought </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s senior editor, Pat Walters, to a subject that at first he thought was long gone: the measuring of human intelligence with IQ tests. Turns out, the tests are all around us. In the workplace. The criminal justice system. Even the NFL. And they’re massive in schools. More than a million US children are IQ tested every year.</span></p>\n<p>We begin Radiolab Presents: “G” with a sentence that stopped us all in our tracks: In the state of California, it is off-limits to administer an IQ test to a child if he or she is Black. That’s because of a little-known case called Larry P v Riles that in the 1970s … put the IQ test itself on trial. With the help of reporter Lee Romney, we investigate how that lawsuit came to be, where IQ tests came from, and what happened to one little boy who got caught in the crossfire.</p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported and produced by Lee Romney, Rachael Cusick and Pat Walters.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Music by Alex Overington. </span></em><em><span>Fact-checking by Diane Kelly.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Elie Mistal, Chenjerai Kumanyika, Amanda Stern, Nora Lyons, Ki Sung, Public Advocates, Michelle Wilson, Peter Fernandez, John Schaefer. Lee Romney’s reporting was supported in part by USC’s Center for Health Journalism.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Radiolab’s “G” is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science.</em></p>\n<p><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-06-07T07:58:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"confirm-nor-deny", "title"=>"Neither Confirm Nor Deny", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19glomarredux.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=347919", "description"=>"<p> How a sunken nuclear submarine, a crazy billionaire, and a mechanical claw gave birth to a phrase that has hounded journalists and lawyers for 40 years and embodies the tension between the public’s desire for transparency and the government’s need to keep secrets.  </p><p>Whether it comes from government spokespeople or celebrity publicists, the phrase “can neither confirm nor deny” is the perfect non-denial denial. It’s such a perfect deflection that it seems like it’s been around forever, but reporter <a href=\"http://www.juliabarton.com\">Julia Barton</a> takes us back to the 1970s and the surprising origin story of what’s now known as a “Glomar Response.” With help from David Sharp and Walt Logan, we tell the story of a clandestine CIA operation to lift a sunken Soviet submarine from the ocean floor and the dilemma they faced when the world found out about it.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span> </span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span>In the 40 years since that operation, the Glomar Response has become boilerplate language from an array of government agencies. With help from ProPublica editor Jeff Larson and NPR’s Dina Temple-Raston, we explore the implications of this ultimate information dodge. ACLU lawyer Jameel Jaffer explains how it stymies oversight, and we learn that, even 40 years later, governmental secrecy can be emotionally painful.</span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span><strong>After listening to the story ... </strong></span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\">After 40 years, many of the details of Project Azorian are only now coming to light. The US government’s default position has been to keep as much of it classified as possible. It took three years for retired CIA employee David Sharp to get permission <a href=\"http://www.kansaspress.ku.edu/shacia.html\">to publish his account</a> of Project Azorian. And FOIA played an indirect role in that, as Cold War historians got the CIA to release, in redacted form, <a href=\"http://www2.gwu.edu/~nsarchiv/nukevault/ebb305/\">an internal history of the mission</a>. After that and a threat of legal action, Sharp was finally able to publish his manuscript in 2012.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\">We mentioned conspiracy theories that have swirled around Project Azorian filling the void where official silence has reigned. One of them is promulgated in the 2005 book <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Red-Star-Rogue-Submarines-Nuclear/dp/1416527338\">“Red Star Rogue” by Kenneth Sewell and Clint Richmond.</a> They posit that the K-129 was taken over by rogue Stalinist KGB agents in order to start a nuclear conflict. But the conflict was to be between the US and China, as, according to the authors, the sub had powers to disguise its sonic signature as a Chinese Navy vessel.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\">This book is the basis of the 2013 drama <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/title/tt1922685/\">“Phantom,”</a> which features Ed Harris and David Duchovny as Soviet military officers who sip vodka in a very un-Russian way.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\">Russian Naval historians, <a href=\"http://www.northfleet.ru/\">like Nikolai Cherkashin</a>, are not only insulted by this take on the cause of the K-129’s demise, they say the true cause is much easier to pinpoint: They say an American vessel, possibly the USS Swordfish, <a href=\"http://english.pravda.ru/news/russia/10-09-2007/96959-sunken_submarine-0/\">collided with the Soviet submarine.</a> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span>Despite the fact that the US government has turned over many documents about Project Azorian and what it found to the Russian government, many in the Russian Navy stand by their theory that it was far too easy for the US to locate the K-129 on the bottom of the Pacific, given the technology of the time. According to these theories, Project Azorian was nothing more than an elaborate cover-up disguised as... an elaborate cover-up. We can neither confirm nor deny that we exactly understand how that would have worked in practice or execution.</span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span> </span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\">But for our money, there’s probably no stranger and more telling document from this time <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kFWMo7aHDRo&amp;noredirect=1\">than a video of the funeral at sea for Soviet sailors</a> ostensibly recovered by the US during Project Azorian. Audio of the service starts at 1:25 in this post. Eulogies and rites are performed in both English and Russian (albeit with an American accent).</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p><span>It’s one of the more solemn moments of the Cold War, and one that the Glomar Response helped keep a secret for a very long time.</span></p>\n<div><span><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></span></div>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Whether it comes from government spokespeople or celebrity publicists, the phrase &ldquo;can neither confirm nor deny&rdquo; is the perfect non-denial denial.", "publishTime"=>1559685600000, "publish-at"=>"2019-06-04T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1559685600, "estimated-duration"=>1651, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Glomar.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/804/529/l/80/1/Glomar.jpg", "h"=>529, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Glomar.jpg", "w"=>804, "id"=>112213, "credits-name"=>""}, "objectID"=>"347919", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Neither Confirm Nor Deny", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p> How a sunken nuclear submarine, a crazy billionaire, and a mechanical claw gave birth to a phrase that has hounded journalists and lawyers for 40 years and embodies the tension between the public’s desire for transparency and the government’s need to keep secrets.  </p><p>Whether it comes from government spokespeople or celebrity publicists, the phrase “can neither confirm nor deny” is the perfect non-denial denial. It’s such a perfect deflection that it seems like it’s been around forever, but reporter <a href=\"http://www.juliabarton.com\">Julia Barton</a> takes us back to the 1970s and the surprising origin story of what’s now known as a “Glomar Response.” With help from David Sharp and Walt Logan, we tell the story of a clandestine CIA operation to lift a sunken Soviet submarine from the ocean floor and the dilemma they faced when the world found out about it.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span> </span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span>In the 40 years since that operation, the Glomar Response has become boilerplate language from an array of government agencies. With help from ProPublica editor Jeff Larson and NPR’s Dina Temple-Raston, we explore the implications of this ultimate information dodge. ACLU lawyer Jameel Jaffer explains how it stymies oversight, and we learn that, even 40 years later, governmental secrecy can be emotionally painful.</span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span><strong>After listening to the story ... </strong></span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\">After 40 years, many of the details of Project Azorian are only now coming to light. The US government’s default position has been to keep as much of it classified as possible. It took three years for retired CIA employee David Sharp to get permission <a href=\"http://www.kansaspress.ku.edu/shacia.html\">to publish his account</a> of Project Azorian. And FOIA played an indirect role in that, as Cold War historians got the CIA to release, in redacted form, <a href=\"http://www2.gwu.edu/~nsarchiv/nukevault/ebb305/\">an internal history of the mission</a>. After that and a threat of legal action, Sharp was finally able to publish his manuscript in 2012.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\">We mentioned conspiracy theories that have swirled around Project Azorian filling the void where official silence has reigned. One of them is promulgated in the 2005 book <a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Red-Star-Rogue-Submarines-Nuclear/dp/1416527338\">“Red Star Rogue” by Kenneth Sewell and Clint Richmond.</a> They posit that the K-129 was taken over by rogue Stalinist KGB agents in order to start a nuclear conflict. But the conflict was to be between the US and China, as, according to the authors, the sub had powers to disguise its sonic signature as a Chinese Navy vessel.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\">This book is the basis of the 2013 drama <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/title/tt1922685/\">“Phantom,”</a> which features Ed Harris and David Duchovny as Soviet military officers who sip vodka in a very un-Russian way.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\">Russian Naval historians, <a href=\"http://www.northfleet.ru/\">like Nikolai Cherkashin</a>, are not only insulted by this take on the cause of the K-129’s demise, they say the true cause is much easier to pinpoint: They say an American vessel, possibly the USS Swordfish, <a href=\"http://english.pravda.ru/news/russia/10-09-2007/96959-sunken_submarine-0/\">collided with the Soviet submarine.</a> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span>Despite the fact that the US government has turned over many documents about Project Azorian and what it found to the Russian government, many in the Russian Navy stand by their theory that it was far too easy for the US to locate the K-129 on the bottom of the Pacific, given the technology of the time. According to these theories, Project Azorian was nothing more than an elaborate cover-up disguised as... an elaborate cover-up. We can neither confirm nor deny that we exactly understand how that would have worked in practice or execution.</span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"><span> </span></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\">But for our money, there’s probably no stranger and more telling document from this time <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kFWMo7aHDRo&amp;noredirect=1\">than a video of the funeral at sea for Soviet sailors</a> ostensibly recovered by the US during Project Azorian. Audio of the service starts at 1:25 in this post. Eulogies and rites are performed in both English and Russian (albeit with an American accent).</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 0pt;\"> </p>\n<p><span>It’s one of the more solemn moments of the Cold War, and one that the Glomar Response helped keep a secret for a very long time.</span></p>\n<div><span><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></span></div>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-06-04T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"good-samaritan", "title"=>"The Good Samaritan", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19thegoodsamaritan.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=937622", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>On a Tuesday afternoon back in the summer of 2017, Scotty Hatton and Scottie Wightman both made a decision to help someone in need. They both paid a price for their actions that day, which have led to a legal, moral, and scientific puzzle about how we balance accountability and forgiveness. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>In this episode, we go to Bath County, Kentucky, where, as one health official put it,<strong> </strong>opioids have created “a hole the size of Kentucky.” We talk to the people on all sides of this story about stemming the tide of overdoses, we wrestle with the science of poison and fear, and we try to figure out when the drive to protect and help those around us should rise above the law.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This story was reported by Peter Andrey Smith with Matt Kielty, and produced by Matt Kielty.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Earl Willis, Bobby Ratliff, Ronnie Goldie, <span>Megan Fisher, Alan Caudill, Nick Jones, </span><span>Dan Wermerling, </span><span>Terry Bunn, Robin Thompson and the staff at KIPRC, </span><span>Charles Landon, </span><span>Charles P Gore, </span><span>Jim McCarthy, </span><span>Ann Marie Farina, </span><span>Dr. Jeremy Faust and Dr. Ed Boyer, </span><span>Justin Brower, Kathy Robinson, Zoe Renfro, John Bucknell, Chris Moraff, Jeremiah Laster, Tommy Kane, Jim McCarthy, </span><span>Sarah Wakeman, and </span><span>Al Tompkins. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p2\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p5\"><span>CDC recommendations on helping people who overdose: <a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/drugoverdose/pdf/patients/Preventing-an-Opioid-Overdose-Tip-Card-a.pdf\"><span>https://www.cdc.gov/drugoverdose/pdf/patients/Preventing-an-Opioid-Overdose-Tip-Card-a.pdf</span></a></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Find out where to get naloxone: <a href=\"https://prevent-protect.org/\"><span>https://prevent-protect.org/</span></a></p>\n<p class=\"p3\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span></span> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Two people make a decision that would lead to a legal and moral puzzle about how we balance accountability and forgiveness.", "publishTime"=>1558738200000, "publish-at"=>"2019-05-24T18:50:00", "date-line-ts"=>1558738200, "estimated-duration"=>4259, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/05/goodsamaritan.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/600/c/80/2019/05/goodsamaritan.jpg", "h"=>600, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Chartres_Bay_44_Good_Samaritan_Panel_12.jpg", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/05/goodsamaritan.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>183708, "credits-name"=>"Wikipedia"}, "objectID"=>"937622", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Good Samaritan", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>On a Tuesday afternoon back in the summer of 2017, Scotty Hatton and Scottie Wightman both made a decision to help someone in need. They both paid a price for their actions that day, which have led to a legal, moral, and scientific puzzle about how we balance accountability and forgiveness. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>In this episode, we go to Bath County, Kentucky, where, as one health official put it,<strong> </strong>opioids have created “a hole the size of Kentucky.” We talk to the people on all sides of this story about stemming the tide of overdoses, we wrestle with the science of poison and fear, and we try to figure out when the drive to protect and help those around us should rise above the law.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This story was reported by Peter Andrey Smith with Matt Kielty, and produced by Matt Kielty.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Earl Willis, Bobby Ratliff, Ronnie Goldie, <span>Megan Fisher, Alan Caudill, Nick Jones, </span><span>Dan Wermerling, </span><span>Terry Bunn, Robin Thompson and the staff at KIPRC, </span><span>Charles Landon, </span><span>Charles P Gore, </span><span>Jim McCarthy, </span><span>Ann Marie Farina, </span><span>Dr. Jeremy Faust and Dr. Ed Boyer, </span><span>Justin Brower, Kathy Robinson, Zoe Renfro, John Bucknell, Chris Moraff, Jeremiah Laster, Tommy Kane, Jim McCarthy, </span><span>Sarah Wakeman, and </span><span>Al Tompkins. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p2\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p5\"><span>CDC recommendations on helping people who overdose: <a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/drugoverdose/pdf/patients/Preventing-an-Opioid-Overdose-Tip-Card-a.pdf\"><span>https://www.cdc.gov/drugoverdose/pdf/patients/Preventing-an-Opioid-Overdose-Tip-Card-a.pdf</span></a></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Find out where to get naloxone: <a href=\"https://prevent-protect.org/\"><span>https://prevent-protect.org/</span></a></p>\n<p class=\"p3\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span></span> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-05-24T18:50:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"bit-flip", "title"=>"Bit Flip ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19bitflipfix.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=934264", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Back in 2003, Belgium was holding a national election. One of their first where the votes would be cast and counted on computers. Thousands of hours of preparation went into making it unhackable. And when the day of the vote came, everything seemed to have gone well. That was, until a cosmic chain of events caused a single bit to flip and called the outcome into question.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Today on Radiolab, we travel from a voting booth in Brussels to the driver's seat of a runaway car in the Carolinas, exploring the massive effects tiny bits of stardust can have on us unwitting humans.</span></p>\n<p><em>T</em><em>his episode was reported and produced by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen. </em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Check out our accompanying short video Bit Flip: <span>the tale of a Belgian election and a cosmic ray that got in the way.</span> </p>\n<p><em><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm140274526330624a2829c02-41cb-4d7a-a62e-46de2784069b\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/AoSELdhL0N4?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a-6530824264408054140\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AoSELdhL0N4&amp;feature=youtu.be\"></iframe></div></div></em></p>\n<p><em>This video was produced by Simon Adler with animation from <a href=\"https://purpleriot.com/\">Kelly Gallagher</a>. </em></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"What do a hacked Belgian election, a falling airplane and a fleet of runaway cars all have in common? The answer just might lie in the stars.", "publishTime"=>1557336600000, "publish-at"=>"2019-05-08T13:30:00", "date-line-ts"=>1557336600, "estimated-duration"=>3367, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/05/bitflip.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1427/c/80/2019/05/bitflip.jpg", "h"=>1427, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/05/bitflip.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>183360, "credits-name"=>" Kelly Gallagher"}, "objectID"=>"934264", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Bit Flip ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Back in 2003, Belgium was holding a national election. One of their first where the votes would be cast and counted on computers. Thousands of hours of preparation went into making it unhackable. And when the day of the vote came, everything seemed to have gone well. That was, until a cosmic chain of events caused a single bit to flip and called the outcome into question.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Today on Radiolab, we travel from a voting booth in Brussels to the driver's seat of a runaway car in the Carolinas, exploring the massive effects tiny bits of stardust can have on us unwitting humans.</span></p>\n<p><em>T</em><em>his episode was reported and produced by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen. </em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Check out our accompanying short video Bit Flip: <span>the tale of a Belgian election and a cosmic ray that got in the way.</span> </p>\n<p><em><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm140274526330624a2829c02-41cb-4d7a-a62e-46de2784069b\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/AoSELdhL0N4?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a-6530824264408054140\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AoSELdhL0N4&amp;feature=youtu.be\"></iframe></div></div></em></p>\n<p><em>This video was produced by Simon Adler with animation from <a href=\"https://purpleriot.com/\">Kelly Gallagher</a>. </em></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-05-08T13:30:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dinopocalypse-redux", "title"=>"Dinopocalypse Redux", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19dinopocalypseredux.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=933402", "description"=>"<p><span>Using high-powered ballistics experiments, fancy computer algorithms, and good old-fashioned ancient geology, scientists have woven together a theory about the extinction of the dinosaurs that is so precise, so hot, so </span><em><span>instantaneous</span></em><span>, as to seem unimaginable. Today, we bring you this story, first published on Radiolab in 2013, plus an update: a spot on planet Earth, newly discovered, that - if it holds true - has the potential to tell us about the first </span><em><span>three</span></em> <em><span>hours</span></em><span> after the dinos died.</span></p>\n<p><em>This update was reported by Molly Webster and was produced with help from Audrey Quinn. </em></p>\n<p><em>We teamed up with some amazing collaborators for Apocalyptical, the Radiolab live show that this episode is based on. Find out more about these wildly talented <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/shows/radiolab/live#answer03\">folks</a>: <em>comedians Reggie Watts, Patton Oswalt, Simon Amstell, Ophira Eisenberg and Kurt Braunohler; musicians On Fillmore and Noveller, and Erth Visual &amp; Physical Inc.</em></em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span>To learn more about the North Dakota site - known as Tanis, for all you Indiana Jones fans - check out </span><a href=\"https://www.pnas.org/content/116/17/8190\"><span>the recent paper</span></a><span>. Make sure you spend time digging into those supplemental materials, it contains all the juice ! </span></p>\n<p><span>And, </span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K52vD4WBdLw\"><span>go watch </span><em><span>Apocalyptical</span></em></a><span>; to dinosaurs and beyond!</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"At a mysterious, muddy site in North Dakota, scientists claim they have a three-hour snapshot of the earth 66 million years ago, on the day the dinosaurs died.", "publishTime"=>1556848200000, "publish-at"=>"2019-05-02T21:50:00", "date-line-ts"=>1556848200, "estimated-duration"=>2715, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/05/donoredux.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1444/1336/c/80/2019/05/donoredux.jpg", "h"=>1336, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Pseudomorphed tektites from Powder River Basin, Wyoming. Aka, a type of those glassy “hell ball” spherules.", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/05/donoredux.jpg", "w"=>1444, "id"=>183271, "credits-name"=>"Alan Hildebrand, University of Calgary"}, "objectID"=>"933402", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dinopocalypse Redux", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Using high-powered ballistics experiments, fancy computer algorithms, and good old-fashioned ancient geology, scientists have woven together a theory about the extinction of the dinosaurs that is so precise, so hot, so </span><em><span>instantaneous</span></em><span>, as to seem unimaginable. Today, we bring you this story, first published on Radiolab in 2013, plus an update: a spot on planet Earth, newly discovered, that - if it holds true - has the potential to tell us about the first </span><em><span>three</span></em> <em><span>hours</span></em><span> after the dinos died.</span></p>\n<p><em>This update was reported by Molly Webster and was produced with help from Audrey Quinn. </em></p>\n<p><em>We teamed up with some amazing collaborators for Apocalyptical, the Radiolab live show that this episode is based on. Find out more about these wildly talented <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/shows/radiolab/live#answer03\">folks</a>: <em>comedians Reggie Watts, Patton Oswalt, Simon Amstell, Ophira Eisenberg and Kurt Braunohler; musicians On Fillmore and Noveller, and Erth Visual &amp; Physical Inc.</em></em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span>To learn more about the North Dakota site - known as Tanis, for all you Indiana Jones fans - check out </span><a href=\"https://www.pnas.org/content/116/17/8190\"><span>the recent paper</span></a><span>. Make sure you spend time digging into those supplemental materials, it contains all the juice ! </span></p>\n<p><span>And, </span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K52vD4WBdLw\"><span>go watch </span><em><span>Apocalyptical</span></em></a><span>; to dinosaurs and beyond!</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-05-02T21:50:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"fu-go", "title"=>"Fu-Go", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19fugo.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=436500", "description"=>"<p><span>This week we’re going back to a favorite episode from 2015.</span></p>\n<p>During World War II, something happened that nobody ever talks about. This is a tale of mysterious balloons, cowboy sheriffs, and young children caught up in the winds of war. And silence, the terror of silence.</p>\n<p>Reporters Peter Lang-Stanton and Nick Farago tell us the story of a seemingly ridiculous, almost whimsical series of attacks on the US between November of 1944 and May of 1945. With the help of writer Ross Coen, geologist Elisa Bergslien, and professor Mike Sweeney, we uncover a national secret that led to tragedy in a sleepy logging town in south central Oregon.</p>\n<p> Check out p<span>ictures of the ghostly balloons <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/pictures-fu-go\">here</a>. </span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Annie Patzke, Leda and Wayne Hunter, and Ilana Sol. Special thanks also for the use of their music to <a href=\"http://jefftayloralive.bandcamp.com/\">Jeff Taylor</a>, <a href=\"http://david-wingo.com/\">David Wingo</a> for the use of \"Opening\" and \"Doghouse\" - from the <a href=\"http://store.milanrecords.com/take-shelter-lp.html\">Take Shelter </a>soundtrack, <a href=\"http://www.justinwalter.net/\">Justin Walter</a>'s \"Mind Shapes\" from his album Lullabies and Nightmares, and <a href=\"http://www.manningaudio.com/\">Michael Manning</a> for the use of <a href=\"http://store.milanrecords.com/take-shelter-lp.html\">\"Save\"</a>. </em></p>\n<p><em><span> </span><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"During World War II, something happened that nobody ever talks about. A&nbsp;tale of mysterious balloons, children caught up in the winds of war. And the terror of silence.", "publishTime"=>1556233620000, "publish-at"=>"2019-04-25T19:07:00", "date-line-ts"=>1556233500, "estimated-duration"=>2130, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Balloon_in_hangar_NARA.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1106/1280/l/80/1/Balloon_in_hangar_NARA.jpg", "h"=>1280, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Balloon in hangar: A caption reads: \"Overall photograph of Japanese balloon inflated with apparatus properly suspended.\"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Balloon_in_hangar_NARA.jpg", "w"=>1106, "id"=>133890, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: National Archives and Records Administration"}, "objectID"=>"436500", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Fu-Go", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This week we’re going back to a favorite episode from 2015.</span></p>\n<p>During World War II, something happened that nobody ever talks about. This is a tale of mysterious balloons, cowboy sheriffs, and young children caught up in the winds of war. And silence, the terror of silence.</p>\n<p>Reporters Peter Lang-Stanton and Nick Farago tell us the story of a seemingly ridiculous, almost whimsical series of attacks on the US between November of 1944 and May of 1945. With the help of writer Ross Coen, geologist Elisa Bergslien, and professor Mike Sweeney, we uncover a national secret that led to tragedy in a sleepy logging town in south central Oregon.</p>\n<p> Check out p<span>ictures of the ghostly balloons <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/pictures-fu-go\">here</a>. </span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Annie Patzke, Leda and Wayne Hunter, and Ilana Sol. Special thanks also for the use of their music to <a href=\"http://jefftayloralive.bandcamp.com/\">Jeff Taylor</a>, <a href=\"http://david-wingo.com/\">David Wingo</a> for the use of \"Opening\" and \"Doghouse\" - from the <a href=\"http://store.milanrecords.com/take-shelter-lp.html\">Take Shelter </a>soundtrack, <a href=\"http://www.justinwalter.net/\">Justin Walter</a>'s \"Mind Shapes\" from his album Lullabies and Nightmares, and <a href=\"http://www.manningaudio.com/\">Michael Manning</a> for the use of <a href=\"http://store.milanrecords.com/take-shelter-lp.html\">\"Save\"</a>. </em></p>\n<p><em><span> </span><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-04-25T19:07:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"americanish", "title"=>"Americanish", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_membership/radiolab_membership19americanish.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=930100", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>In 1903 the US Supreme Court </span><span>refused to say that Isabel González was a citizen of the United States. Then again, they said, she wasn’t a exactly an immigrant either. </span><span>And they said that the US territory of Puerto Rico, Isabel’s home, was “foreign to the United States in a domestic sense.” Since then, the US has cleared up at least some of the confusion about US territories and the status of people born in them.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>But, more than a hundred years later, there is still a US territory that has been left in limbo: American Samoa. It is the only place on earth that is US soil, but people who are born there are not automatically US citizens. When we visit American Samoa, we discover that there are some pretty surprising reasons why many American Samoans prefer it that way.</span><span> </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> <em><span>This episode was reported and produced by Julia Longoria.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to John Wasko.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Check out Sam Erman's book</em> <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Almost-Citizens-Constitution-Studies-History/dp/1108415490\"><em><span>Almost Citizens</span></em></a> and <em><span>Doug Mack's book </span></em><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Not-Quite-States-America-Dispatches-Territories-ebook/dp/B01HDSU3YY\"><em><span>The Not Quite States of America</span></em></a><em>.</em></p>\n<p><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"We travel to the limbo space between foreign and domestic to find the up and downsides of being a US citizen.", "publishTime"=>1555679880000, "publish-at"=>"2019-04-19T09:18:00", "date-line-ts"=>1555679880, "estimated-duration"=>3855, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/04/AS.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1280/852/c/80/2019/04/AS.jpeg", "h"=>852, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/04/AS.jpeg", "w"=>1280, "id"=>182966, "credits-name"=>"National Park Service of American Samoa"}, "objectID"=>"930100", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Americanish", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>In 1903 the US Supreme Court </span><span>refused to say that Isabel González was a citizen of the United States. Then again, they said, she wasn’t a exactly an immigrant either. </span><span>And they said that the US territory of Puerto Rico, Isabel’s home, was “foreign to the United States in a domestic sense.” Since then, the US has cleared up at least some of the confusion about US territories and the status of people born in them.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>But, more than a hundred years later, there is still a US territory that has been left in limbo: American Samoa. It is the only place on earth that is US soil, but people who are born there are not automatically US citizens. When we visit American Samoa, we discover that there are some pretty surprising reasons why many American Samoans prefer it that way.</span><span> </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> <em><span>This episode was reported and produced by Julia Longoria.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to John Wasko.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Check out Sam Erman's book</em> <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Almost-Citizens-Constitution-Studies-History/dp/1108415490\"><em><span>Almost Citizens</span></em></a> and <em><span>Doug Mack's book </span></em><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Not-Quite-States-America-Dispatches-Territories-ebook/dp/B01HDSU3YY\"><em><span>The Not Quite States of America</span></em></a><em>.</em></p>\n<p><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-04-19T09:18:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"whom-cowbell-tolls", "title"=>"For Whom the Cowbell Tolls", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast040519forwhomcowbelltolls.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=925768", "description"=>"<p>When Nancy Holten was 8 years old her mom put her in a moving van. She fell asleep, woke up in Switzerland, and she's been there ever since. Nancy is big into animal rights, crystals, and various forms of natural and holistic healing. She’s also a viral sensation: the Dutch woman apparently so annoying, her Swiss town denied her citizenship. In this episode we go to the little village of Gipf-Oberfrick to meet Nancy, talk with the town, and ask the question: what does it mean and what does it take to belong to a place?</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Kelly Prime and was produced by Kelly Prime and Annie McEwen. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to reporter Anna Mayumi Kerber, the tireless fixer and translator for this story. Thanks also to Dominik Hangartner and to the very talented yodelers Ai Dineen and Gregory Corbino.</em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1256\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1273\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1281\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n<div class=\"story-credits__producing-org-credits producing-org-credits\"><em>A tasty note from Latif: Towards the end of the story, I casually mentioned a place called Greg's Poutine in Toronto.  Turns out, it's actually called <a href=\"http://smokespoutinerie.com/\">Smoke's Poutinerie</a>. (Confused it with <a href=\"https://nowtoronto.com/locations/gregs-ice-cream-distillery-district/\">Greg's Ice Cream</a>.) Go. It's delicious. </em><strong><strong><br></strong></strong></div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"What if citizenship was a popularity contest... and you just lost?", "publishTime"=>1553863140000, "publish-at"=>"2019-03-29T08:39:00", "date-line-ts"=>1553863080, "estimated-duration"=>3433, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/03/RTR2RQUM.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1277/c/80/2019/03/RTR2RQUM.jpg", "h"=>1277, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/03/RTR2RQUM.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>182558, "credits-name"=>"Lisi Niesner"}, "objectID"=>"925768", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"For Whom the Cowbell Tolls", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>When Nancy Holten was 8 years old her mom put her in a moving van. She fell asleep, woke up in Switzerland, and she's been there ever since. Nancy is big into animal rights, crystals, and various forms of natural and holistic healing. She’s also a viral sensation: the Dutch woman apparently so annoying, her Swiss town denied her citizenship. In this episode we go to the little village of Gipf-Oberfrick to meet Nancy, talk with the town, and ask the question: what does it mean and what does it take to belong to a place?</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Kelly Prime and was produced by Kelly Prime and Annie McEwen. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to reporter Anna Mayumi Kerber, the tireless fixer and translator for this story. Thanks also to Dominik Hangartner and to the very talented yodelers Ai Dineen and Gregory Corbino.</em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1256\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1273\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1281\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n<div class=\"story-credits__producing-org-credits producing-org-credits\"><em>A tasty note from Latif: Towards the end of the story, I casually mentioned a place called Greg's Poutine in Toronto.  Turns out, it's actually called <a href=\"http://smokespoutinerie.com/\">Smoke's Poutinerie</a>. (Confused it with <a href=\"https://nowtoronto.com/locations/gregs-ice-cream-distillery-district/\">Greg's Ice Cream</a>.) Go. It's delicious. </em><strong><strong><br></strong></strong></div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-03-29T08:39:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"bliss", "title"=>"Bliss", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19bliss.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=923869", "description"=>"<p><span>This week Jad and Radiolab alum Tim Howard revisit a favorite episode from 2012.</span></p>\n<p><span>Because moments of total, world-shaking bliss are not easy to come by. Maybe that's what makes them feel so life-altering when they strike. And so worth chasing. This hour: stories of striving, grasping, tripping, and falling for happiness, perfection, and ideals.  </span></p>\n<p><span>With </span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vC8gJ0_9o4M\"><span>Alexander Gamme</span></a><span>, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/1587890.Arika_Okrent\"><span>Arika Okrent</span></a><span>, </span><a href=\"https://rws.xoba.com/\"><span>Richard Sproat</span></a><span>, and </span><a href=\"http://www.snowcrystals.com/\"><span>Ken Libbrecht</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><em>This update was produced with help from Audrey Quinn.</em></p>\n<p><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></p>", "tease"=>"Stories of striving, grasping, tripping, and falling for happiness, perfection, and Bliss.", "publishTime"=>1553206140000, "publish-at"=>"2019-03-21T18:09:00", "date-line-ts"=>1553206140, "estimated-duration"=>3102, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/03/Blissphoto.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/479/c/80/2019/03/Blissphoto.jpg", "h"=>479, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://daniel-horowitz.com/category/illustration/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/03/Blissphoto.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>182381, "credits-name"=>"Daniel Horowitz "}, "objectID"=>"923869", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Bliss", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This week Jad and Radiolab alum Tim Howard revisit a favorite episode from 2012.</span></p>\n<p><span>Because moments of total, world-shaking bliss are not easy to come by. Maybe that's what makes them feel so life-altering when they strike. And so worth chasing. This hour: stories of striving, grasping, tripping, and falling for happiness, perfection, and ideals.  </span></p>\n<p><span>With </span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vC8gJ0_9o4M\"><span>Alexander Gamme</span></a><span>, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/1587890.Arika_Okrent\"><span>Arika Okrent</span></a><span>, </span><a href=\"https://rws.xoba.com/\"><span>Richard Sproat</span></a><span>, and </span><a href=\"http://www.snowcrystals.com/\"><span>Ken Libbrecht</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><em>This update was produced with help from Audrey Quinn.</em></p>\n<p><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-03-21T18:09:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"asking-another-friend", "title"=>"Asking for Another Friend", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19askinganotherfriend.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=920854", "description"=>"<p><span>Part 2: Last year, we ran a pair of episodes that explored the greatest mysteries in our listeners’ lives - the big ones, little ones, and the ones in between. This year, we’re back on the hunt, tracking down answers to the big little questions swirling around our own heads.</span></p>\n<p><span>Today, we take a look at a strange human emotion, and investigate the mysteries lurking behind the trees, sounds, and furry friends in our lives. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Tracie Hunte, Pat Walters, Molly Webster, Arianne Wack, Carter Hodge, Sarah Qari and Annie McEwen, and was produced by Matt Kielty, <em>Tracie Hunte, Pat Walters, Molly Webster, Arianne Wack, Sarah Qari, Annie McEwen, and Simon Adler. </em></em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Yiyun Huang, lab manager at Yale's Canine Cognition Center. Check out Code Switch's <a href=\"https://www.npr.org/templates/transcript/transcript.php?storyId=671354988\">\"Dog Show!\"</a> </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "tease"=>"Parasitic genes, public poop, and the eerie sound of a subway train. What is ... the answer to your stupid question?", "publishTime"=>1552018380000, "publish-at"=>"2019-03-07T23:13:00", "date-line-ts"=>1552018380, "estimated-duration"=>4700, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/03/radiolab2blackbackgroundflat_RDsyiEm.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2019/03/radiolab2blackbackgroundflat_RDsyiEm.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/03/radiolab2blackbackgroundflat_RDsyiEm.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>182169, "credits-name"=>"Justin Buschardt"}, "objectID"=>"920854", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Asking for Another Friend", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Part 2: Last year, we ran a pair of episodes that explored the greatest mysteries in our listeners’ lives - the big ones, little ones, and the ones in between. This year, we’re back on the hunt, tracking down answers to the big little questions swirling around our own heads.</span></p>\n<p><span>Today, we take a look at a strange human emotion, and investigate the mysteries lurking behind the trees, sounds, and furry friends in our lives. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Tracie Hunte, Pat Walters, Molly Webster, Arianne Wack, Carter Hodge, Sarah Qari and Annie McEwen, and was produced by Matt Kielty, <em>Tracie Hunte, Pat Walters, Molly Webster, Arianne Wack, Sarah Qari, Annie McEwen, and Simon Adler. </em></em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Yiyun Huang, lab manager at Yale's Canine Cognition Center. Check out Code Switch's <a href=\"https://www.npr.org/templates/transcript/transcript.php?storyId=671354988\">\"Dog Show!\"</a> </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-03-07T23:13:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"asking-friend", "title"=>"Asking for a Friend", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19askingforfriend.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=917198", "description"=>"<p><span>Last year, we ran a pair of episodes that explored the greatest mysteries in our listeners’ lives - the big ones, little ones, and the ones in between. This year, we’re back on the hunt, tracking down answers to the big little questions swirling around our own heads.</span></p>\n<p>We reached out to some of our favorite people and asked them to come along with us as we journeyed back in time, to outer space, and inside our very own bodies.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Rachael Cusick, Simon Adler, Becca Bressler, and Annie McEwen and was produced by <em>Rachael Cusick, Simon Adler, Matt Kielty, Becca Bressler, and Annie McEwen.</em></em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"This might be a stupid question, but...", "publishTime"=>1551411900000, "publish-at"=>"2019-02-28T22:45:00", "date-line-ts"=>1551411900, "estimated-duration"=>4026, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/02/askingforafriend1_MbKI3cc.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2019/02/askingforafriend1_MbKI3cc.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/02/askingforafriend1_MbKI3cc.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>182014, "credits-name"=>"Justin Buschardt"}, "objectID"=>"917198", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Asking for a Friend", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Last year, we ran a pair of episodes that explored the greatest mysteries in our listeners’ lives - the big ones, little ones, and the ones in between. This year, we’re back on the hunt, tracking down answers to the big little questions swirling around our own heads.</span></p>\n<p>We reached out to some of our favorite people and asked them to come along with us as we journeyed back in time, to outer space, and inside our very own bodies.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Rachael Cusick, Simon Adler, Becca Bressler, and Annie McEwen and was produced by <em>Rachael Cusick, Simon Adler, Matt Kielty, Becca Bressler, and Annie McEwen.</em></em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-02-28T22:45:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-loops", "title"=>"Loops", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19loops.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=917407", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Our lives are filled with loops that hurt us, heal us, make us laugh, and, sometimes, leave us wanting more. This hour, Radiolab revisits the strange things that emerge when something happens, then happens again, and again, and again, and again, and again, and… well, again.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>In this episode of Radiolab, Jad and Robert try to explain an inexplicable comedy act, listen to a loop that literally dies in your ear, and they learn about a loop that sent a shudder up the collective spine of mathematicians everywhere. Finally, they talk to a woman who got to watch herself think the thought that she was watching herself think the thought that she was watching herself think the thought that ... you get the point.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>With <a href=\"https://twitter.com/kristenschaaled?ref_src=twsrc%5Egoogle%7Ctwcamp%5Eserp%7Ctwgr%5Eauthor\"><span>Kristen Schaal</span></a> and <a href=\"https://www.kurtbraunohler.com/\"><span>Kurt Braunohler</span></a>,  <a href=\"http://www.alexbellos.com/\"><span>Alex Bellos</span></a>, <a href=\"http://www.stevenstrogatz.com/\"><span>Steven Strogatz</span></a>, <a href=\"http://jannalevin.com/\"><span>Janna Levin</span></a>, and <a href=\"http://melaniethernstrom.com/\"><span>Melanie Thernstrom</span></a>. Plus mind-bending musical accompaniment from <a href=\"https://www.laguardiahs.org/\"><span>Laguardia Arts High School</span></a> singers Nathaniel Sabat, Julian Soto, Eli Greenhoe, Kelly Efthimiu, Julia Egan, and Ruby Froom.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>You can find the video Christine Campbell made of her mom Mary Sue <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3fA5uzWDU8\"><span>here</span></a>.</span></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1273\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1282\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1302\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1296\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1319\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em><br></em></span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1328\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"The surprising ways that loops steer&hellip; and sometimes derail&hellip; our lives.", "publishTime"=>1550799600000, "publish-at"=>"2019-02-21T20:40:00", "date-line-ts"=>1550799600, "estimated-duration"=>3725, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/02/train_loop.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/384/c/80/2019/02/train_loop.jpg", "h"=>384, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.flickr.com/photos/vitroids/3746539068/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/02/train_loop.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>181852, "credits-name"=>"Masakazu Matsumoto"}, "objectID"=>"917407", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Loops", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Our lives are filled with loops that hurt us, heal us, make us laugh, and, sometimes, leave us wanting more. This hour, Radiolab revisits the strange things that emerge when something happens, then happens again, and again, and again, and again, and again, and… well, again.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>In this episode of Radiolab, Jad and Robert try to explain an inexplicable comedy act, listen to a loop that literally dies in your ear, and they learn about a loop that sent a shudder up the collective spine of mathematicians everywhere. Finally, they talk to a woman who got to watch herself think the thought that she was watching herself think the thought that she was watching herself think the thought that ... you get the point.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>With <a href=\"https://twitter.com/kristenschaaled?ref_src=twsrc%5Egoogle%7Ctwcamp%5Eserp%7Ctwgr%5Eauthor\"><span>Kristen Schaal</span></a> and <a href=\"https://www.kurtbraunohler.com/\"><span>Kurt Braunohler</span></a>,  <a href=\"http://www.alexbellos.com/\"><span>Alex Bellos</span></a>, <a href=\"http://www.stevenstrogatz.com/\"><span>Steven Strogatz</span></a>, <a href=\"http://jannalevin.com/\"><span>Janna Levin</span></a>, and <a href=\"http://melaniethernstrom.com/\"><span>Melanie Thernstrom</span></a>. Plus mind-bending musical accompaniment from <a href=\"https://www.laguardiahs.org/\"><span>Laguardia Arts High School</span></a> singers Nathaniel Sabat, Julian Soto, Eli Greenhoe, Kelly Efthimiu, Julia Egan, and Ruby Froom.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>You can find the video Christine Campbell made of her mom Mary Sue <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N3fA5uzWDU8\"><span>here</span></a>.</span></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1273\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1282\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1302\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1296\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1319\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em><br></em></span></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1328\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-02-21T20:40:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"beauty-puzzle", "title"=>"The Beauty Puzzle", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19beautypuzzle.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=914301", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>When a female animal is checking out her prospects, natural selection would dictate that she pay attention to how healthy, or strong, or fit he is. But when it comes to finding a mate, some animals seem to be engaged in a very different game. What if a female were looking for something else - something that has nothing to do with fitness? Something...beautiful? Today we explore a different way of looking at evolution and what it may mean for the course of science.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span><em>This episode was reported by Robert Krulwich and Bethel Habte and was produced by Bethel Habte.</em><br></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em><br></em></span></p>", "tease"=>"In one corner of the natural world, beauty may be beating brawn.", "publishTime"=>1549608600000, "publish-at"=>"2019-02-08T01:50:00", "date-line-ts"=>1549608600, "estimated-duration"=>2574, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/02/satinbower.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/529/c/80/2019/02/satinbower.jpg", "h"=>529, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/02/satinbower.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>181570, "credits-name"=>"wikipedia "}, "objectID"=>"914301", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Beauty Puzzle", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>When a female animal is checking out her prospects, natural selection would dictate that she pay attention to how healthy, or strong, or fit he is. But when it comes to finding a mate, some animals seem to be engaged in a very different game. What if a female were looking for something else - something that has nothing to do with fitness? Something...beautiful? Today we explore a different way of looking at evolution and what it may mean for the course of science.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span><em>This episode was reported by Robert Krulwich and Bethel Habte and was produced by Bethel Habte.</em><br></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em><br></em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-02-08T01:50:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-punchline", "title"=>"The Punchline", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast19punchline.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=908744", "description"=>"<p>John Scott was the professional hockey player that every fan loved to hate.  A tough guy. A brawler. A goon. But when an impish pundit named Puck Daddy called on fans to vote for Scott to play alongside the world’s greatest players in the NHL All-Star Game, Scott found himself facing off against fans, commentators, and the powers that be.  Was this the realization of Scott’s childhood dreams? Or a nightmarish prank gone too far? Today on Radiolab, a goof on a goon turns into a parable of the agony and the ecstasy of the internet, and democracy in the age of Boaty McBoatface.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and was produced by Matt Kielty.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Larry Lynch and <span>Morgan Springer.</span> Check out John Scott's \"Dropping the Gloves\" <a href=\"https://www.johnscottallstar.com/podcast/\">podcast</a> and his <a href=\"https://www.johnscottallstar.com/book/\">book</a> \"A Guy Like Me\".</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"One of the worst players in the NHL gets voted into the All-Star Game.", "publishTime"=>1547625780000, "publish-at"=>"2019-01-16T03:03:00", "date-line-ts"=>1547625780, "estimated-duration"=>3125, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/01/johnscott.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"AP Images"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1297/c/80/2019/01/johnscott.jpg", "h"=>1297, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/01/johnscott.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>181053, "credits-name"=>"Jeff Roberson"}, "objectID"=>"908744", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Punchline", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>John Scott was the professional hockey player that every fan loved to hate.  A tough guy. A brawler. A goon. But when an impish pundit named Puck Daddy called on fans to vote for Scott to play alongside the world’s greatest players in the NHL All-Star Game, Scott found himself facing off against fans, commentators, and the powers that be.  Was this the realization of Scott’s childhood dreams? Or a nightmarish prank gone too far? Today on Radiolab, a goof on a goon turns into a parable of the agony and the ecstasy of the internet, and democracy in the age of Boaty McBoatface.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and was produced by Matt Kielty.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Larry Lynch and <span>Morgan Springer.</span> Check out John Scott's \"Dropping the Gloves\" <a href=\"https://www.johnscottallstar.com/podcast/\">podcast</a> and his <a href=\"https://www.johnscottallstar.com/book/\">book</a> \"A Guy Like Me\".</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2019-01-16T03:03:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2020=>[{"slug"=>"covid-christmas-special", "title"=>"A Terrible Covid Christmas Special", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20covidchristmasspecial.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1076528", "description"=>"<p><span>This year was the worst. And as our staff tried to figure out what to do for our last episode of 2020, co-host Latif Nasser thought, what if we stare straight into the darkness … and make a damn Christmas special about it.</span></p>\n<p><span>Latif begins with a story about Santa, and a back-room deal he made with the Trump administration to jump to the front of the vaccine line, a tale that travels from an absurd quid-pro-quo to a deep question: who really is an essential worker? </span></p>\n<p><span>From there, we take a whistle-stop tour through the numbers that scientists say you need to know as you wind your way (or preferably, don’t wind your way) through our COVID-infested world. Producer Sarah Qari brings us her version of the Christmas classic nobody ever dreamt they’d want to hear: The Twelve Numbers of COVID.</span></p>\n<p><span>You can check out Martin Bazant’s COVID “calculator” </span><a href=\"https://indoor-covid-safety.herokuapp.com/\"><span>here</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and Sarah Qari, and was produced by Matt Kielty, Sarah Qari, and Pat Walters.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Anna Weggel and Brant Miller, Catherine, Rohan, and Finn Munro, Noam Osband, Amber D’Souza, Chris Zangmeister, John Volckens, Joshua Santarpia, Laurel Bristow, Michael Mina,  Mohammad Sajadi, <span>James V. Grimaldi, <span>Stephanie Armour, <span>Joshuah Bearman, <span>Brendan Nyhan</span></span></span></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><span><span><span><span>And for more on the proposed Santa vaccine deal, see Julie Wernau and her colleagues' reporting at the Wall Street Journal <a href=\"https://www.wsj.com/articles/health-agency-scraps-coronavirus-ad-campaign-leaving-santa-claus-in-the-cold-11603630802\">here</a>.</span></span></span></span></span></em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span><em>Original art for this episode by Zara Stasi. Check out her work at: <span><span> </span></span><a href=\"https://protect-us.mimecast.com/s/g6t2CW6lYwi5RrXAi1tGY6?domain=goodforthebees.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\">www.goodforthebees.com</a><span>. </span></em></span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"A terrible Christmas special befitting a terrible year.&nbsp;&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1608727980000, "publish-at"=>"2020-12-23T07:53:00", "date-line-ts"=>1608727980, "estimated-duration"=>2976, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/12/RadioLab_Santa_Cough_12_2020.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/c/80/2020/12/RadioLab_Santa_Cough_12_2020.png", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/12/RadioLab_Santa_Cough_12_2020.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>193564, "credits-name"=>"Zara Stasi"}, "objectID"=>"1076528", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"A Terrible Covid Christmas Special", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This year was the worst. And as our staff tried to figure out what to do for our last episode of 2020, co-host Latif Nasser thought, what if we stare straight into the darkness … and make a damn Christmas special about it.</span></p>\n<p><span>Latif begins with a story about Santa, and a back-room deal he made with the Trump administration to jump to the front of the vaccine line, a tale that travels from an absurd quid-pro-quo to a deep question: who really is an essential worker? </span></p>\n<p><span>From there, we take a whistle-stop tour through the numbers that scientists say you need to know as you wind your way (or preferably, don’t wind your way) through our COVID-infested world. Producer Sarah Qari brings us her version of the Christmas classic nobody ever dreamt they’d want to hear: The Twelve Numbers of COVID.</span></p>\n<p><span>You can check out Martin Bazant’s COVID “calculator” </span><a href=\"https://indoor-covid-safety.herokuapp.com/\"><span>here</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and Sarah Qari, and was produced by Matt Kielty, Sarah Qari, and Pat Walters.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Anna Weggel and Brant Miller, Catherine, Rohan, and Finn Munro, Noam Osband, Amber D’Souza, Chris Zangmeister, John Volckens, Joshua Santarpia, Laurel Bristow, Michael Mina,  Mohammad Sajadi, <span>James V. Grimaldi, <span>Stephanie Armour, <span>Joshuah Bearman, <span>Brendan Nyhan</span></span></span></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><span><span><span><span>And for more on the proposed Santa vaccine deal, see Julie Wernau and her colleagues' reporting at the Wall Street Journal <a href=\"https://www.wsj.com/articles/health-agency-scraps-coronavirus-ad-campaign-leaving-santa-claus-in-the-cold-11603630802\">here</a>.</span></span></span></span></span></em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span><em>Original art for this episode by Zara Stasi. Check out her work at: <span><span> </span></span><a href=\"https://protect-us.mimecast.com/s/g6t2CW6lYwi5RrXAi1tGY6?domain=goodforthebees.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\">www.goodforthebees.com</a><span>. </span></em></span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-12-23T07:53:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"ashes-lawn", "title"=>"The Ashes on the Lawn", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_membership/radiolab_membership20ashesonlawn.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1075484", "description"=>"<p><span>A global pandemic. An afflicted, angry group. A seemingly indifferent government. Reporter Tracie Hunte wanted to understand this moment of pain and confusion by looking back 30 years, and she found a complicated answer to a simple question: When nothing seems to work, how do you make change?</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Tracie Hunte, and produced by Annie McEwen and Tobin Low. Fact-checking by Diane Kelly. </em></p>\n<p><span><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></span></p>", "tease"=>"A global pandemic. An afflicted, angry group. A seemingly indifferent government. When nothing seems to work, how do you make change?", "publishTime"=>1608277620000, "publish-at"=>"2020-12-18T02:47:00", "date-line-ts"=>1608277620, "estimated-duration"=>3119, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/12/image_8MNBzYZ.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/541/c/80/2020/12/image_8MNBzYZ.png", "h"=>541, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/12/image_8MNBzYZ.png", "w"=>800, "id"=>193480, "credits-name"=>"Meg Handler"}, "objectID"=>"1075484", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Ashes on the Lawn", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>A global pandemic. An afflicted, angry group. A seemingly indifferent government. Reporter Tracie Hunte wanted to understand this moment of pain and confusion by looking back 30 years, and she found a complicated answer to a simple question: When nothing seems to work, how do you make change?</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Tracie Hunte, and produced by Annie McEwen and Tobin Low. Fact-checking by Diane Kelly. </em></p>\n<p><span><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-12-18T02:47:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-enemy-mankind", "title"=>"Enemy of Mankind", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20enemyofmankindrerun.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1073903", "description"=>"<p>Should the U.S. Supreme Court be the court of the world? In the 18th century, two feuding Frenchmen inspired a one-sentence law that helped launch American human rights litigation into the 20th century. The Alien Tort Statute allowed a Paraguayan woman to find justice for a terrible crime committed in her homeland. But as America reached further and further out into the world, the court was forced to confront the contradictions in our country’s ideology: sympathy vs. sovereignty. Earlier this month, the Supreme Court heard arguments in<span> </span><em>Jesner v. Arab Bank</em>, a case that could reshape the way America responds to human rights abuses abroad. Does the A.T.S. secure human rights or is it a dangerous overreach?</p>\n<p><span><span><em>Additional music for this episode by<span> </span><a href=\"http://www.nicholascarter.com/\">Nicolas Carter</a>.</em></span></span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to William J. Aceves, William Baude, Diego Calles, Alana Casanova-Burgess, William Dodge, Susan Farbstein, Jeffery Fisher, Joanne Freeman, Julian Ku, Nicholas Rosenkranz, Susan Simpson, Emily Vinson, Benjamin Wittes and Jamison York. Ken Saro-Wiwa Jr., who appears in this episode, passed away in October 2016</em><em>.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Supreme Court archival audio comes from<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\">Oyez®</a>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</span></em></p>\n<p><span><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></span></p>", "tease"=>"Nine justices hold the fate of a pivotal American human rights law in their hands. Should the U.S. Supreme Court also be the court of the world?", "publishTime"=>1607643780000, "publish-at"=>"2020-12-10T18:43:00", "date-line-ts"=>1607643780, "estimated-duration"=>3393, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/12/enemyofmankind.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/800/c/80/2020/12/enemyofmankind.jpg", "h"=>800, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/12/enemyofmankind.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>193333, "credits-name"=>"Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"1073903", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Enemy of Mankind", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Should the U.S. Supreme Court be the court of the world? In the 18th century, two feuding Frenchmen inspired a one-sentence law that helped launch American human rights litigation into the 20th century. The Alien Tort Statute allowed a Paraguayan woman to find justice for a terrible crime committed in her homeland. But as America reached further and further out into the world, the court was forced to confront the contradictions in our country’s ideology: sympathy vs. sovereignty. Earlier this month, the Supreme Court heard arguments in<span> </span><em>Jesner v. Arab Bank</em>, a case that could reshape the way America responds to human rights abuses abroad. Does the A.T.S. secure human rights or is it a dangerous overreach?</p>\n<p><span><span><em>Additional music for this episode by<span> </span><a href=\"http://www.nicholascarter.com/\">Nicolas Carter</a>.</em></span></span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to William J. Aceves, William Baude, Diego Calles, Alana Casanova-Burgess, William Dodge, Susan Farbstein, Jeffery Fisher, Joanne Freeman, Julian Ku, Nicholas Rosenkranz, Susan Simpson, Emily Vinson, Benjamin Wittes and Jamison York. Ken Saro-Wiwa Jr., who appears in this episode, passed away in October 2016</em><em>.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Supreme Court archival audio comes from<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\">Oyez®</a>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</span></em></p>\n<p><span><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-12-10T18:43:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"great_vaccinator", "title"=>"The Great Vaccinator", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20greatvaccinator.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1072188", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Until now, the fastest vaccine ever made - for mumps - took four years. And while our current effort to develop a covid-19 vaccine involves thousands of people working around the clock, the mumps vaccine was developed almost exclusively by one person: Maurice Hilleman. Hilleman cranked out more than 40 other vaccines over the course of his career, including 8 of the 14 routinely given to children. He arguably saved more lives than any other single person. And through his work, Hilleman embodied the instincts, drive, and guts it takes to marshall the human body’s defenses against a disease. But through him we also see the struggle and the costs of these monumental scientific efforts.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Matt Kielty and Heather Radke, and produced by Matt Kielty.</em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><span><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></span></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"A foul-mouthed, chicken-loving Montanan completely altered the course of human health.&nbsp;His story&nbsp;also lays bare the struggles inherent in&nbsp;our global effort to&nbsp;create a&nbsp;covid-19 vaccine.", "publishTime"=>1607012966000, "publish-at"=>"2020-12-03T11:29:26", "date-line-ts"=>1607012966, "estimated-duration"=>2519, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/12/NMAH-AHB2018q018568.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/683/1000/l/80/2020/12/NMAH-AHB2018q018568.jpg", "h"=>1000, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/12/NMAH-AHB2018q018568.jpg", "w"=>683, "id"=>193200, "credits-name"=>"Division of Medicine and Science, National Museum of American History, Smithsonian Institution"}, "objectID"=>"1072188", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Great Vaccinator", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Until now, the fastest vaccine ever made - for mumps - took four years. And while our current effort to develop a covid-19 vaccine involves thousands of people working around the clock, the mumps vaccine was developed almost exclusively by one person: Maurice Hilleman. Hilleman cranked out more than 40 other vaccines over the course of his career, including 8 of the 14 routinely given to children. He arguably saved more lives than any other single person. And through his work, Hilleman embodied the instincts, drive, and guts it takes to marshall the human body’s defenses against a disease. But through him we also see the struggle and the costs of these monumental scientific efforts.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Matt Kielty and Heather Radke, and produced by Matt Kielty.</em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><span><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></span></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-12-03T11:29:26", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dispatch-13-challenge-trials", "title"=>"Dispatch 13: Challenge Trials", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20dispatchchallengetrials.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1070359", "description"=>"<p><span>What if someone asked you to get infected with the COVID-19 virus, deliberately, in order to speed up the development of a vaccine? Would you do it? Would you risk your life to save others?</span></p>\n<p><span>For months, dozens of companies have been racing to create coronavirus vaccines. Finally, three have done it. But according to the experts, we’re not out of the woods yet; we’ll need several vaccines to satisfy the global demand. One way to speed up the development process is a controversial technique called a human challenge trial, in which human subjects are intentionally infected with the virus. Senior correspondent Molly Webster gets the lowdown from Public News Service reporter Laura Rosbrow-Telem and then tracks down some of the tens of thousands of people who have volunteered to participate in a challenge trial.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Jonathan Miller.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Molly Webster and Laura Rosbrow-Telem and produced by Molly Webster and Pat Walters.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><span><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></span></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Would you volunteer to get infected with COVID-19 to save someone&rsquo;s life? Thousands of people already have.", "publishTime"=>1606277760000, "publish-at"=>"2020-11-24T23:16:00", "date-line-ts"=>1606277760, "estimated-duration"=>1592, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/11/donotenter2.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1712/c/80/2020/11/donotenter2.jpg", "h"=>1712, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/11/donotenter2.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>193054, "credits-name"=>"Pan Stock"}, "objectID"=>"1070359", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dispatch 13: Challenge Trials", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>What if someone asked you to get infected with the COVID-19 virus, deliberately, in order to speed up the development of a vaccine? Would you do it? Would you risk your life to save others?</span></p>\n<p><span>For months, dozens of companies have been racing to create coronavirus vaccines. Finally, three have done it. But according to the experts, we’re not out of the woods yet; we’ll need several vaccines to satisfy the global demand. One way to speed up the development process is a controversial technique called a human challenge trial, in which human subjects are intentionally infected with the virus. Senior correspondent Molly Webster gets the lowdown from Public News Service reporter Laura Rosbrow-Telem and then tracks down some of the tens of thousands of people who have volunteered to participate in a challenge trial.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Jonathan Miller.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Molly Webster and Laura Rosbrow-Telem and produced by Molly Webster and Pat Walters.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><span><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></span></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-11-24T23:16:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91612-deception", "title"=>"Deception", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20deceptionrerun.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91612", "description"=>"<p>Lies, liars, and lie catchers. This hour of Radiolab asks if it's possible for anyone to lead a life without deception.</p><p>We consult a cast of characters, from pathological liars to lying snakes to drunken psychiatrists, to try and understand the strange power of lying to yourself and others.</p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"Lies, liars, and lie catchers. And the strange power of lying to yourself. &nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1605837720000, "publish-at"=>"2020-11-19T21:02:00", "date-line-ts"=>1605805200, "estimated-duration"=>3440, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Fingers crossed", "name"=>"photologue/photos/deception.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/576/c/80/photologue/photos/deception.jpg", "h"=>576, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Fingers crossed", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/indigo-eyes/5978065808/in/photostream/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/deception.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>67080, "credits-name"=>"Charlotte McKnight"}, "objectID"=>"91612", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Deception", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Lies, liars, and lie catchers. This hour of Radiolab asks if it's possible for anyone to lead a life without deception.</p><p>We consult a cast of characters, from pathological liars to lying snakes to drunken psychiatrists, to try and understand the strange power of lying to yourself and others.</p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-11-19T21:02:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"breaking-benford", "title"=>"Breaking Benford", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20breakingbenford.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1067938", "description"=>"<p><span>In the days after the US Presidential election was called for Joe Biden, many supporters of Donald Trump are crying foul.  Voter fraud. And a key piece of evidence? A century-old quirk of math called Benford’s Law.  We at Radiolab know Benford’s Law well, and have covered it before.  In this political dispatch, Latif and Soren Sherlock their way through the precinct numbers to see if these claims hold up. Spoiler: they </span><em><span>don’t</span></em><span>. But the reason why is more interesting than you’d expect.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser. </em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></p>\n<p>Links: </p>\n<p><span>Walter Mebane, <a href=\"http://www-personal.umich.edu/~wmebane/inapB.pdf\">“Inappropriate Applications of Benford’s Law Regularities to Some Data from the 2020 Presidential Election in the United States”</a></span></p>", "tease"=>"The internet says&nbsp;an&nbsp;obscure mathematical law can tell us something about the 2020 US election. It can't. But it can tell us about us.", "publishTime"=>1605318900000, "publish-at"=>"2020-11-13T20:55:00", "date-line-ts"=>1605300951, "estimated-duration"=>1775, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/11/benford-01.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1200/900/c/80/2020/11/benford-01.jpg", "h"=>900, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/11/benford-01.jpg", "w"=>1200, "id"=>192869, "credits-name"=>"Latif Nasser"}, "objectID"=>"1067938", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Breaking Benford", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In the days after the US Presidential election was called for Joe Biden, many supporters of Donald Trump are crying foul.  Voter fraud. And a key piece of evidence? A century-old quirk of math called Benford’s Law.  We at Radiolab know Benford’s Law well, and have covered it before.  In this political dispatch, Latif and Soren Sherlock their way through the precinct numbers to see if these claims hold up. Spoiler: they </span><em><span>don’t</span></em><span>. But the reason why is more interesting than you’d expect.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser. </em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></p>\n<p>Links: </p>\n<p><span>Walter Mebane, <a href=\"http://www-personal.umich.edu/~wmebane/inapB.pdf\">“Inappropriate Applications of Benford’s Law Regularities to Some Data from the 2020 Presidential Election in the United States”</a></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-11-13T20:55:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"bloc-party", "title"=>"Bloc Party", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20blocparty.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1064930", "description"=>"<p><span>In the 1996 election, Bill Clinton had a problem. The women who came out in droves for him in ‘92, split their vote in the ‘94 midterms, handing over control of the House and the Senate to the Republican Party. As his team stared ahead at his re-election bid, they knew they had to win those women back. So, after a major polling effort to determine who exactly their undecided ladies were, Clinton turned his focus toward the most important swing vote in the election: the soccer moms. </span></p>\n<p><span>The soccer mom ushered in a new era of political campaigning, an era of slicing and dicing the electorate, engineering the (predominately white) voting bloc characters that campaigns have chased after. Security Moms. Nascar Dads. Joe Six Pack. Walmart Moms. </span></p>\n<p><span>But what about everyone else? What about the surprisingly swingable corners of this country without a soccer mom in sight?  Inspired by this </span><a href=\"https://www.thinglink.com/scene/810170295397646337?buttonSource=viewLimits\"><span>exceedingly cool interactive map</span></a><span> from Politico, we set out on a mission to make an audio-map of our own. We asked pollsters, reporters and political operatives in swing states: </span><span>what slice of your population is up for grabs? A slice that no one talks about? In this episode, </span><span>we crawl inside the places that </span><span>might hold our country’s future in its hands</span><span>, all the while asking: are these slices even real? Are there people inside them that might swing this election? </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported and produced by Becca Bressler, Tobin Low, Sarah Qari, Tracie Hunte, Pat Walters and Matt Kielty, with help from Jonny Moens.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Darren Samuelsohn, Josh Cochran, Elizabeth Ralph, and the Politico team for the original reporting and map that inspired this episode. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Also thanks to: Elissa Schneider, Wisam Naoum, Martin Manna, Ashourina Slewo, Eli Newman, Zoe Clark, Erin Roselio, Jess Kamm Broomell, Will Doran, John Zogby, Matt Dickinson, Tom Jensen, Ross Grogg, Joel Andrus, Jonathan Tilove, Steve Contorno, Heaven Hale, Jeff Shapiro, Nicole Cobler, Marie Albiges, Matt Dole, Robin Goist, Katie Paris, Julie Womack, Matt Dole, Jackie Borchardt, Jessica Locklear, Twinkle Patel, Bobby Das, Dharmesh Ahir,  Nimesh Dhinubhai, Jay Desai, Rishi Bagga, and </span></em><em><span>Sanjeev Joshipura.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Christina Greer’s book is </span></em><a href=\"https://oxford.universitypressscholarship.com/view/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199989300.001.0001/acprof-9780199989300\"><em><span>Black Ethnics: Race, Immigration, and the Pursuit of the American Dream</span></em></a><em><span>, and Corey Fields book is </span></em><a href=\"https://www.ucpress.edu/book/9780520291904/black-elephants-in-the-room\"><em><span>Black Elephants in the Room: The Unexpected Politics of African American</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Original art for this episode by Zara Stasi. Check out her work at: <span><span> </span></span><a href=\"https://protect-us.mimecast.com/s/g6t2CW6lYwi5RrXAi1tGY6?domain=goodforthebees.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\">www.goodforthebees.com</a><span>. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Who are the soccer moms of the 2020 election? We set out to find them.", "publishTime"=>1604321040000, "publish-at"=>"2020-11-02T07:44:00", "date-line-ts"=>1604321040, "estimated-duration"=>3037, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/11/RadioLab_Voting__FINAL_JFYprtl.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/l/80/2020/11/RadioLab_Voting__FINAL_JFYprtl.png", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/11/RadioLab_Voting__FINAL_JFYprtl.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>192632, "credits-name"=>"Zara Stasi"}, "objectID"=>"1064930", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Bloc Party", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In the 1996 election, Bill Clinton had a problem. The women who came out in droves for him in ‘92, split their vote in the ‘94 midterms, handing over control of the House and the Senate to the Republican Party. As his team stared ahead at his re-election bid, they knew they had to win those women back. So, after a major polling effort to determine who exactly their undecided ladies were, Clinton turned his focus toward the most important swing vote in the election: the soccer moms. </span></p>\n<p><span>The soccer mom ushered in a new era of political campaigning, an era of slicing and dicing the electorate, engineering the (predominately white) voting bloc characters that campaigns have chased after. Security Moms. Nascar Dads. Joe Six Pack. Walmart Moms. </span></p>\n<p><span>But what about everyone else? What about the surprisingly swingable corners of this country without a soccer mom in sight?  Inspired by this </span><a href=\"https://www.thinglink.com/scene/810170295397646337?buttonSource=viewLimits\"><span>exceedingly cool interactive map</span></a><span> from Politico, we set out on a mission to make an audio-map of our own. We asked pollsters, reporters and political operatives in swing states: </span><span>what slice of your population is up for grabs? A slice that no one talks about? In this episode, </span><span>we crawl inside the places that </span><span>might hold our country’s future in its hands</span><span>, all the while asking: are these slices even real? Are there people inside them that might swing this election? </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported and produced by Becca Bressler, Tobin Low, Sarah Qari, Tracie Hunte, Pat Walters and Matt Kielty, with help from Jonny Moens.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Darren Samuelsohn, Josh Cochran, Elizabeth Ralph, and the Politico team for the original reporting and map that inspired this episode. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Also thanks to: Elissa Schneider, Wisam Naoum, Martin Manna, Ashourina Slewo, Eli Newman, Zoe Clark, Erin Roselio, Jess Kamm Broomell, Will Doran, John Zogby, Matt Dickinson, Tom Jensen, Ross Grogg, Joel Andrus, Jonathan Tilove, Steve Contorno, Heaven Hale, Jeff Shapiro, Nicole Cobler, Marie Albiges, Matt Dole, Robin Goist, Katie Paris, Julie Womack, Matt Dole, Jackie Borchardt, Jessica Locklear, Twinkle Patel, Bobby Das, Dharmesh Ahir,  Nimesh Dhinubhai, Jay Desai, Rishi Bagga, and </span></em><em><span>Sanjeev Joshipura.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Christina Greer’s book is </span></em><a href=\"https://oxford.universitypressscholarship.com/view/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199989300.001.0001/acprof-9780199989300\"><em><span>Black Ethnics: Race, Immigration, and the Pursuit of the American Dream</span></em></a><em><span>, and Corey Fields book is </span></em><a href=\"https://www.ucpress.edu/book/9780520291904/black-elephants-in-the-room\"><em><span>Black Elephants in the Room: The Unexpected Politics of African American</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Original art for this episode by Zara Stasi. Check out her work at: <span><span> </span></span><a href=\"https://protect-us.mimecast.com/s/g6t2CW6lYwi5RrXAi1tGY6?domain=goodforthebees.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\">www.goodforthebees.com</a><span>. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-11-02T07:44:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"how-win-friends-and-influence-baboons", "title"=>"How to Win Friends and Influence Baboons", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20howtowinfriendsandinfluencebaboons.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1064570", "description"=>"<p><span>Baboon troops. We all know they’re hierarchical. There’s the big brutish alpha male who rules with a hairy iron fist, and then there’s everybody else. Which is what Meg Crofoot thought too, before she used GPS collars to track the movements of a troop of baboons for a whole month. What she and her team learned from this data gave them a whole new understanding of baboon troop dynamics, and, moment to moment, who really has the power.</span><span> </span></p>\n<p><span><em>This episode was reported and produced by Annie McEwen.</em><br></span></p>\n<p><span><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></span></p>", "tease"=>"How important is the way you walk? Well, if you&rsquo;re a baboon it can flip the entire power structure of your troop on it&rsquo;s head. At least when it comes to breakfast.", "publishTime"=>1604118600000, "publish-at"=>"2020-10-31T00:30:00", "date-line-ts"=>1604118600, "estimated-duration"=>1759, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/10/baboon_group_kenya.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1162/c/80/2020/10/baboon_group_kenya.jpg", "h"=>1162, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/10/baboon_group_kenya.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>192622, "credits-name"=>"Stu Porter"}, "objectID"=>"1064570", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"How to Win Friends and Influence Baboons", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Baboon troops. We all know they’re hierarchical. There’s the big brutish alpha male who rules with a hairy iron fist, and then there’s everybody else. Which is what Meg Crofoot thought too, before she used GPS collars to track the movements of a troop of baboons for a whole month. What she and her team learned from this data gave them a whole new understanding of baboon troop dynamics, and, moment to moment, who really has the power.</span><span> </span></p>\n<p><span><em>This episode was reported and produced by Annie McEwen.</em><br></span></p>\n<p><span><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-10-31T00:30:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"what-if", "title"=>"What If?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_membership/radiolab_membership2020whatifwithfix.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1062755", "description"=>"<p><span>There’s plenty of speculation about what Donald Trump might do in the wake of the election. Would he dispute the results if he loses? Would he simply refuse to leave office, or even try to use the military to maintain control? Last summer, Rosa Brooks got together a team of experts and political operatives from both sides of the aisle to ask a slightly different question. Rather than arguing about whether he’d do those things, they dug into what exactly would happen if he did. Part war game part choose your own adventure, Rosa’s Transition Integrity Project doesn’t give us any predictions, and it isn’t a referendum on Trump. Instead, it’s a deeply illuminating stress test on our laws, our institutions, and on the commitment to democracy written into the constitution.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Bethel Habte, with help from Tracie Hunte, and produced by Bethel Habte. Jeremy Bloom provided original music.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>You can read The Transition Integrity Project’s report <a href=\"https://assets.documentcloud.org/documents/7013152/Preventing-a-Disrupted-Presidential-Election-and.pdf\">here</a>.</span></em></em></span></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"If a president happened to break a few political norms and decide, in the face of defeat, to fight instead of concede, what would actually happen? Today we choose our own adventure.", "publishTime"=>1603431660000, "publish-at"=>"2020-10-23T01:41:00", "date-line-ts"=>1603431660, "estimated-duration"=>2483, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/10/strangelove.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1237/c/80/2020/10/strangelove.jpg", "h"=>1237, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/10/strangelove.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>192461, "credits-name"=>"Dr. Strangelove"}, "objectID"=>"1062755", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"What If?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>There’s plenty of speculation about what Donald Trump might do in the wake of the election. Would he dispute the results if he loses? Would he simply refuse to leave office, or even try to use the military to maintain control? Last summer, Rosa Brooks got together a team of experts and political operatives from both sides of the aisle to ask a slightly different question. Rather than arguing about whether he’d do those things, they dug into what exactly would happen if he did. Part war game part choose your own adventure, Rosa’s Transition Integrity Project doesn’t give us any predictions, and it isn’t a referendum on Trump. Instead, it’s a deeply illuminating stress test on our laws, our institutions, and on the commitment to democracy written into the constitution.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Bethel Habte, with help from Tracie Hunte, and produced by Bethel Habte. Jeremy Bloom provided original music.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>You can read The Transition Integrity Project’s report <a href=\"https://assets.documentcloud.org/documents/7013152/Preventing-a-Disrupted-Presidential-Election-and.pdf\">here</a>.</span></em></em></span></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-10-23T01:41:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"kittens-kick-giggly-blue-robot-all-summer-radiolab", "title"=>"Kittens Kick The Giggly Blue Robot All Summer", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20kittenskick.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1059338", "description"=>"<div>\n<p><span data-stringify-type=\"bold\">With the recent passing of Ruth Bader Ginsburg, there's been a lot of debate about how much power the Supreme Court should</span><span data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> re</span><span data-stringify-type=\"bold\">ally</span><span data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> have.</span></p>\n</div>\n<p>We tend to think of the Supreme Court justices as all-powerful guardians of the constitution, issuing momentous rulings from on high. They seem at once powerful, and unknowable; all lacy collars and black robes.</p>\n<p>But they haven’t always been so, you know, supreme. On this episode of<span> </span><em>More Perfect</em>, we go all the way back to the case that, in a lot of ways, is the beginning of the court we know today.</p>\n<p><span>Also: we listen back to a mnemonic device (and song) that we created back in 2016 to help people remember the names of the justices. Listen, create a new one, and share with us!</span></p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"315\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/j4TENcocyss\" width=\"100%\"></iframe></p>\n<p><a class=\"twitter-share-button\" href=\"https://twitter.com/share\" data-url=\"https://youtu.be/j4TENcocyss\" data-text=\"My #SCOTUSsong would be ___\" data-via=\"Radiolab\" data-size=\"large\">Tweet</a></p>\n<p><strong>The key links:</strong></p>\n<p>- Akhil Reed Amar's forthcoming book, <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Constitution-Today-Timeless-Lessons-Issues/dp/0465096336\">The Constitution Today: Timeless Lessons for the Issues of Our Era</a><br>- Linda Monk's book, <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Words-Live-Annotated-Constitution-Stonesong/dp/078688620X/ref=sr_1_1?s=books&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1467405824&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Linda+Monk\">The Words We Live By: Your Annotated Guide to the Constitution</a></p>\n<p><strong>The key voices:</strong></p>\n<p>- Linda Monk, <a href=\"http://lindamonk.com/\">author and constitutional scholar</a><br>- Akhil Reed Amar, <a href=\"https://www.law.yale.edu/akhil-reed-amar\">Sterling Professor of Law at Yale</a><br>- Ari J. Savitzky, <a href=\"https://www.wilmerhale.com/ari_savitzky/\">lawyer at WilmerHale</a></p>\n<p><strong>The key cases:</strong></p>\n<p>- 1803: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1789-1850/5us137\">Marbury v. Madison</a><br>- 1832: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1789-1850/31us515\">Worcester v. Georgia</a><br>- 1954: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1940-1955/347us483\">Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka (1)</a><br>- 1955: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1940-1955/349us294\">Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka (2)</a></p>\n<p><em>Additional music for this episode by <a href=\"http://www.podingtonbear.com/wpnew/\">Podington Bear</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Dylan Keefe and<span> </span><a href=\"http://mitchboyer.com/\">Mitch Boyer</a> for their work on the above video.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"How did the Supreme Court get so supreme? On this episode of&nbsp;More Perfect, we go all the way back to&nbsp;a case that, in a lot of ways, started it all.", "publishTime"=>1602174208000, "publish-at"=>"2020-10-08T12:23:28", "date-line-ts"=>1602189000, "estimated-duration"=>2376, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/10/MPKittens.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/533/c/80/2020/10/MPKittens.jpg", "h"=>533, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/10/MPKittens.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>192184, "credits-name"=>"Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"1059338", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Kittens Kick The Giggly Blue Robot All Summer", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div>\n<p><span data-stringify-type=\"bold\">With the recent passing of Ruth Bader Ginsburg, there's been a lot of debate about how much power the Supreme Court should</span><span data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> re</span><span data-stringify-type=\"bold\">ally</span><span data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> have.</span></p>\n</div>\n<p>We tend to think of the Supreme Court justices as all-powerful guardians of the constitution, issuing momentous rulings from on high. They seem at once powerful, and unknowable; all lacy collars and black robes.</p>\n<p>But they haven’t always been so, you know, supreme. On this episode of<span> </span><em>More Perfect</em>, we go all the way back to the case that, in a lot of ways, is the beginning of the court we know today.</p>\n<p><span>Also: we listen back to a mnemonic device (and song) that we created back in 2016 to help people remember the names of the justices. Listen, create a new one, and share with us!</span></p>\n<p><iframe frameborder=\"0\" height=\"315\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/j4TENcocyss\" width=\"100%\"></iframe></p>\n<p><a class=\"twitter-share-button\" href=\"https://twitter.com/share\" data-url=\"https://youtu.be/j4TENcocyss\" data-text=\"My #SCOTUSsong would be ___\" data-via=\"Radiolab\" data-size=\"large\">Tweet</a></p>\n<p><strong>The key links:</strong></p>\n<p>- Akhil Reed Amar's forthcoming book, <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Constitution-Today-Timeless-Lessons-Issues/dp/0465096336\">The Constitution Today: Timeless Lessons for the Issues of Our Era</a><br>- Linda Monk's book, <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Words-Live-Annotated-Constitution-Stonesong/dp/078688620X/ref=sr_1_1?s=books&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1467405824&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Linda+Monk\">The Words We Live By: Your Annotated Guide to the Constitution</a></p>\n<p><strong>The key voices:</strong></p>\n<p>- Linda Monk, <a href=\"http://lindamonk.com/\">author and constitutional scholar</a><br>- Akhil Reed Amar, <a href=\"https://www.law.yale.edu/akhil-reed-amar\">Sterling Professor of Law at Yale</a><br>- Ari J. Savitzky, <a href=\"https://www.wilmerhale.com/ari_savitzky/\">lawyer at WilmerHale</a></p>\n<p><strong>The key cases:</strong></p>\n<p>- 1803: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1789-1850/5us137\">Marbury v. Madison</a><br>- 1832: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1789-1850/31us515\">Worcester v. Georgia</a><br>- 1954: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1940-1955/347us483\">Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka (1)</a><br>- 1955: <a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/cases/1940-1955/349us294\">Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka (2)</a></p>\n<p><em>Additional music for this episode by <a href=\"http://www.podingtonbear.com/wpnew/\">Podington Bear</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Dylan Keefe and<span> </span><a href=\"http://mitchboyer.com/\">Mitch Boyer</a> for their work on the above video.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-10-08T12:23:28", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"no-special-duty", "title"=>"No Special Duty", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20nospecialduty.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1057756", "description"=>"<p><em><span>What are the police for?</span></em><span> Producer </span>B.A. Parker<span> started wondering this back in June, as Black Lives Matter protests and calls to “defund the police” ramped up. The question led her to a wild story of a stabbing on a New York City subway train, and the realization that, according to the law, the police don’t </span><em><span>always</span></em><span> have to protect us. Producer </span>Sarah Qari<span> joins Parker to dig into the legal background, which takes her all the way up to the Supreme Court... and then all the way back down to on-duty officers themselves.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode contains strong language and graphic violence.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Reported and produced by B.A. Parker and Sarah Qari, and produced by Matt Kielty and Pat Walters.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to April Hayes and Katia Maguire for their documentary Home Truth about Jessica Gonzales, </span></em><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jAfUI_hETy0&amp;ab_channel=Cracked\"><em><span>Cracked.com</span></em></a><em><span> for sending us down this rabbit hole, Caroline Bettinger-López, Geoff Grimwood, Christy Lopez, Anthony Herron, Mike Wells, and Keith Taylor.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"A look at what happens when the police don&rsquo;t do the one thing you expect them to.", "publishTime"=>1601638560000, "publish-at"=>"2020-10-02T07:36:00", "date-line-ts"=>1601638560, "estimated-duration"=>2714, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/10/protectandserve2.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/799/533/c/80/2020/10/protectandserve2.jpg", "h"=>533, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.flickr.com/photos/51035555243@N01/28846639285", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/10/protectandserve2.jpg", "w"=>799, "id"=>192081, "credits-name"=>"Thomas Hawk"}, "objectID"=>"1057756", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"No Special Duty", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><em><span>What are the police for?</span></em><span> Producer </span>B.A. Parker<span> started wondering this back in June, as Black Lives Matter protests and calls to “defund the police” ramped up. The question led her to a wild story of a stabbing on a New York City subway train, and the realization that, according to the law, the police don’t </span><em><span>always</span></em><span> have to protect us. Producer </span>Sarah Qari<span> joins Parker to dig into the legal background, which takes her all the way up to the Supreme Court... and then all the way back down to on-duty officers themselves.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode contains strong language and graphic violence.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Reported and produced by B.A. Parker and Sarah Qari, and produced by Matt Kielty and Pat Walters.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to April Hayes and Katia Maguire for their documentary Home Truth about Jessica Gonzales, </span></em><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jAfUI_hETy0&amp;ab_channel=Cracked\"><em><span>Cracked.com</span></em></a><em><span> for sending us down this rabbit hole, Caroline Bettinger-López, Geoff Grimwood, Christy Lopez, Anthony Herron, Mike Wells, and Keith Taylor.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-10-02T07:36:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"insomnia-line", "title"=>"Insomnia Line", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20insomnialine.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1056017", "description"=>"<p><a href=\"https://health.ucdavis.edu/health-news/newsroom/covid-19-is-wrecking-our-sleep-with-coronasomnia--tips-to-fight-back-/2020/09\"><span>Coronasomnia</span></a><span> is a not-so-surprising </span><a href=\"https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2020/09/03/coronavirus-sleep-insomnia/\"><span>side-effect</span></a><span> of the global pandemic. More and more of us are having trouble falling asleep. We wanted to find a way to get inside that nighttime world, to see why people are awake and what they are thinking about.</span></p>\n<p><span>So what’d </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span> decide to do? </span></p>\n<p>Open up the phone lines and talk to you.</p>\n<p><span>We created an insomnia hotline and on this week’s experimental episode, we stayed up all night, taking hundreds of calls, spilling secrets, and at long last, watching the sunrise peek through.  </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Lulu Miller with Rachael Cusick, Tracie Hunte, Tobin Low, Sarah Qari, Molly Webster, Pat Walters, Shima Oliaee, and Jonny Moens.</em></p>\n<p><em>Want more Radiolab in your life? <em><a target=\"_blank\" class=\"c-link\" data-stringify-link=\"http://radiolab.org/newsletter\" delay=\"150\" data-sk=\"tooltip_parent\" href=\"http://radiolab.org/newsletter\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sign up for our newsletter</a>! We share our latest favorites: articles, tv shows, funny Youtube videos, chocolate chip cookie recipes, and more.</em></em></p>\n<p><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"It&rsquo;s the dead of night, you&rsquo;re wide awake. And you&rsquo;re not alone. So we put a phone number on twitter, and spent all night talking to the sleepless among us.", "publishTime"=>1601034900000, "publish-at"=>"2020-09-25T07:55:00", "date-line-ts"=>1601034900, "estimated-duration"=>2070, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/09/AP219533349462.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"", "name"=>"AP Images"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1596/l/80/2020/09/AP219533349462.jpg", "h"=>1596, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"British telephone handset mounted lengthwise instead of horizontally on the base, pictured in London, May 26, 1966.", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/09/AP219533349462.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>191965, "credits-name"=>"ASSOCIATED PRESS"}, "objectID"=>"1056017", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Insomnia Line", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><a href=\"https://health.ucdavis.edu/health-news/newsroom/covid-19-is-wrecking-our-sleep-with-coronasomnia--tips-to-fight-back-/2020/09\"><span>Coronasomnia</span></a><span> is a not-so-surprising </span><a href=\"https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2020/09/03/coronavirus-sleep-insomnia/\"><span>side-effect</span></a><span> of the global pandemic. More and more of us are having trouble falling asleep. We wanted to find a way to get inside that nighttime world, to see why people are awake and what they are thinking about.</span></p>\n<p><span>So what’d </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span> decide to do? </span></p>\n<p>Open up the phone lines and talk to you.</p>\n<p><span>We created an insomnia hotline and on this week’s experimental episode, we stayed up all night, taking hundreds of calls, spilling secrets, and at long last, watching the sunrise peek through.  </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Lulu Miller with Rachael Cusick, Tracie Hunte, Tobin Low, Sarah Qari, Molly Webster, Pat Walters, Shima Oliaee, and Jonny Moens.</em></p>\n<p><em>Want more Radiolab in your life? <em><a target=\"_blank\" class=\"c-link\" data-stringify-link=\"http://radiolab.org/newsletter\" delay=\"150\" data-sk=\"tooltip_parent\" href=\"http://radiolab.org/newsletter\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sign up for our newsletter</a>! We share our latest favorites: articles, tv shows, funny Youtube videos, chocolate chip cookie recipes, and more.</em></em></p>\n<p><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-09-25T07:55:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"more-perfect-sex-appeal", "title"=>"More Perfect: Sex Appeal", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20sexappeal.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=910242", "description"=>"<p><span>We lost a legend. Supreme Court Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg died on September 18th, 2020. She was 87. In honor of her passing we are re-airing the More Perfect episode dedicated to one of her cases, because it </span><span>offers a unique portrait of how one person can make change in the world. </span></p>\n<p><span> This is the story of how Ginsburg, as a young </span><span>lawyer at the ACLU, convinced an all-male Supreme Court to take discrimination against women seriously - using a case on discrimination against men. </span></p>\n<p><span><em>This episode was reported by Julia Longoria.</em></span></p>\n<p><span><em><em>Special thanks to Stephen Wiesenfeld, Alison Keith, and Bob Darcy.</em></em></span></p>\n<p><span><em><em>Supreme Court archival audio comes from<span> </span></em><a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\"><em>Oyez®</em></a><em>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</em></em></span></p>\n<p><span><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></span></p>", "tease"=>"How&nbsp;Ruth Bader Ginsburg used a&nbsp;trojan horse, filled&nbsp;with frat boys and beer, to win a battle for gender equality.", "publishTime"=>1600484040000, "publish-at"=>"2020-09-18T22:54:00", "date-line-ts"=>1600484040, "estimated-duration"=>3197, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/01/RBG-v03.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1860/l/80/2019/01/RBG-v03.jpg", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Supreme Court Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg", "credits-url"=>"https://mitchboyer.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/01/RBG-v03.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>181181, "credits-name"=>"Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"910242", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"More Perfect: Sex Appeal", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>We lost a legend. Supreme Court Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg died on September 18th, 2020. She was 87. In honor of her passing we are re-airing the More Perfect episode dedicated to one of her cases, because it </span><span>offers a unique portrait of how one person can make change in the world. </span></p>\n<p><span> This is the story of how Ginsburg, as a young </span><span>lawyer at the ACLU, convinced an all-male Supreme Court to take discrimination against women seriously - using a case on discrimination against men. </span></p>\n<p><span><em>This episode was reported by Julia Longoria.</em></span></p>\n<p><span><em><em>Special thanks to Stephen Wiesenfeld, Alison Keith, and Bob Darcy.</em></em></span></p>\n<p><span><em><em>Supreme Court archival audio comes from<span> </span></em><a href=\"https://www.oyez.org/\"><em>Oyez®</em></a><em>, a free law project in collaboration with the Legal Information Institute at Cornell.</em></em></span></p>\n<p><span><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-09-18T22:54:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91726-falling", "title"=>"Falling", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20falling.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91726", "description"=>"<p><span>There are so many ways to fall—in love, asleep, even flat on your face. This hour, Radiolab dives into stories of great falls. </span></p>\n<p>We jump into a black hole, take a trip over Niagara Falls, upend some myths about falling cats, and plunge into our favorite songs about falling.</p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></p>", "tease"=>"We plunge into a black hole, take a trip over Niagara Falls, and upend some myths about falling cats.", "publishTime"=>1600386600000, "publish-at"=>"2020-09-17T19:50:00", "date-line-ts"=>1600358400, "estimated-duration"=>3387, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"trip and fall", "name"=>"photologue/photos/trip-500.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/375/500/l/80/photologue/photos/trip-500.jpg", "h"=>500, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/denverjeffrey/2792749020/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/trip-500.jpg", "w"=>375, "id"=>26943, "credits-name"=>"Jeffrey Beall"}, "objectID"=>"91726", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Falling", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>There are so many ways to fall—in love, asleep, even flat on your face. This hour, Radiolab dives into stories of great falls. </span></p>\n<p>We jump into a black hole, take a trip over Niagara Falls, upend some myths about falling cats, and plunge into our favorite songs about falling.</p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-09-17T19:50:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"bringing-gamma-back", "title"=>"Bringing Gamma Back, Again", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20bringinggammabackagain.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=690533", "description"=>"<div><span>Today, we return to the lab of neuroscientist Li-Huei Tsai, which brought us one of our favorite stories from four years ago - about the power of flashing lights on an Alzheimer’s-addled (mouse) brain. In this update, Li-Huei tells us about her team’s latest research, which now includes flashing </span><em><span>sound</span></em><span>, and ways in which light and sound together might retrieve lost memories. </span>This new science is not a cure, and is far from a treatment, but it’s a finding so … simple, you won’t be able to shake it. Come join us for a lab visit, where we’ll meet some mice, stare at some light, and come face-to-face with the mystery of memory. We can promise you: by the end, you’ll never think the same way about Christmas lights again. Or jingle bells.</div>\n<div>\n<p><em>This update was reported by Molly Webster, and <em>produced by Rachael Cusick. </em>The original episode was produced by Annie McEwen, Matt Kielty, and Molly Webster, with help from Simon Adler. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Ed Boyden, Cognito Therapeutics, Brad Dickerson, Karen Duff, Zaven Khachaturian, Michael Lutz, Kevin M. Spencer, and Peter Uhlhaas.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Molly's note about the image:</strong></em></p>\n<p><em>Those neon green things in the image are microglia, the brain’s immune cells, or, as we describe them in our episode, the janitor cells of the brain. Straight from MIT’s research files, this image shows microglia who have gotten light stimulation therapy (one can only hope in the flicker room). You can see their many, super-long tentacles, which would be used to feel out anything that didn’t belong in the brain. And then they’d eat it!</em></p>\n<p><strong><em>Further reading: </em></strong></p>\n<p><span>Li-Huei and co’s gamma sound and light <a href=\"https://www.cell.com/cell/pdf/S0092-8674(19)30163-1.pdf\">paper</a>: </span><em><span>Multi-sensory Gamma Stimulation Ameliorates Alzheimer’s-Associated Pathology and Improves Cognition</span></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n</div>", "tease"=>"What can flashing lights and an eerie reverberating sound do for the brain of someone suffering from Alzheimer&rsquo;s? We update one of our favorite episodes.", "publishTime"=>1599799500000, "publish-at"=>"2020-09-11T00:45:00", "date-line-ts"=>1599799500, "estimated-duration"=>2207, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Iba1_microglia.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/772/772/c/80/1/Iba1_microglia.jpg", "h"=>772, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Brain cells called microglia (green) stained for lba1, a microglia marker", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Iba1_microglia.jpg", "w"=>772, "id"=>159667, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Hannah Iaccarino, Anthony Martorell"}, "objectID"=>"690533", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Bringing Gamma Back, Again", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div><span>Today, we return to the lab of neuroscientist Li-Huei Tsai, which brought us one of our favorite stories from four years ago - about the power of flashing lights on an Alzheimer’s-addled (mouse) brain. In this update, Li-Huei tells us about her team’s latest research, which now includes flashing </span><em><span>sound</span></em><span>, and ways in which light and sound together might retrieve lost memories. </span>This new science is not a cure, and is far from a treatment, but it’s a finding so … simple, you won’t be able to shake it. Come join us for a lab visit, where we’ll meet some mice, stare at some light, and come face-to-face with the mystery of memory. We can promise you: by the end, you’ll never think the same way about Christmas lights again. Or jingle bells.</div>\n<div>\n<p><em>This update was reported by Molly Webster, and <em>produced by Rachael Cusick. </em>The original episode was produced by Annie McEwen, Matt Kielty, and Molly Webster, with help from Simon Adler. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Ed Boyden, Cognito Therapeutics, Brad Dickerson, Karen Duff, Zaven Khachaturian, Michael Lutz, Kevin M. Spencer, and Peter Uhlhaas.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Molly's note about the image:</strong></em></p>\n<p><em>Those neon green things in the image are microglia, the brain’s immune cells, or, as we describe them in our episode, the janitor cells of the brain. Straight from MIT’s research files, this image shows microglia who have gotten light stimulation therapy (one can only hope in the flicker room). You can see their many, super-long tentacles, which would be used to feel out anything that didn’t belong in the brain. And then they’d eat it!</em></p>\n<p><strong><em>Further reading: </em></strong></p>\n<p><span>Li-Huei and co’s gamma sound and light <a href=\"https://www.cell.com/cell/pdf/S0092-8674(19)30163-1.pdf\">paper</a>: </span><em><span>Multi-sensory Gamma Stimulation Ameliorates Alzheimer’s-Associated Pathology and Improves Cognition</span></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-09-11T00:45:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"fungus-amungus", "title"=>"Fungus Amungus", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20fungusamungus_reup.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1051034", "description"=>"<p>Six years ago, a new infection began popping up in four different hospitals on three different continents, all around the same time. It wasn’t a bacteria, or a virus. It was ... a killer fungus. No one knew where it came from, or why. Today, the story of an ancient showdown between fungus and mammals that started when dinosaurs disappeared from the earth. Back then, the battle swung in our favor (spoiler alert!) and we’ve been hanging onto that win ever since. But one scientist suggests that the rise of this new infectious fungus indicates our edge is slipping, degree by increasing degree.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Molly Webster, and produced by Molly and Bethel Habte, with production help from Tad Davis. Special thanks to Julie Parsonnet and Aviv Bergman. </em></p>\n<p><strong><em>If you caught this segment in our radio broadcast and want to hear the segment it aired with, <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/articles/dispatch-14-covid-crystal-ball\">check out Covid Crystal Ball here.</a></em></strong></p>\n<p><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></span></em></em><span> </span></em></p>\n<p><span>Further Fungus Reading: </span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/06/health/drug-resistant-candida-auris.html\">NYTimes feature</a> on the mysterious rise of </span><em><span>Candida auris.</span></em><span> </span></p>\n<p> <span>Arturo's <a href=\"https://mbio.asm.org/content/10/4/e01397-19#:~:text=Candida%20auris%20is%20a%20new,exhibiting%20nonsusceptibility%20to%20antifungal%20agents.\">paper</a>: “On the emergence of </span><em><span>Candida auris</span></em><span>, Climate Change, Azoles, Swamps, and Birds”, by Arturo Casadevall, et al.</span></p>\n<p><span>“On the Origins of a Species: What Might Explain the Rise of </span><em><span>Candida auris</span></em><span>?”, a <a href=\"https://www.mdpi.com/2309-608X/5/3/58/htm\">report from the CDC</a>.</span></p>\n<h1> </h1>", "tease"=>"There&rsquo;s a new bug popping up around the world. Or it might just be an old bug that finally met its moment.", "publishTime"=>1599206760000, "publish-at"=>"2020-09-04T04:06:00", "date-line-ts"=>1599206760, "estimated-duration"=>1908, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/09/funguspic_x4Fglyh.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1671/1233/c/80/2020/09/funguspic_x4Fglyh.png", "h"=>1233, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Microscopic image of Candida auris. Oval budding yeast cells without pseudohyphae.", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/09/funguspic_x4Fglyh.png", "w"=>1671, "id"=>191677, "credits-name"=>"Joveria Farooqi"}, "objectID"=>"1051034", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Fungus Amungus", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Six years ago, a new infection began popping up in four different hospitals on three different continents, all around the same time. It wasn’t a bacteria, or a virus. It was ... a killer fungus. No one knew where it came from, or why. Today, the story of an ancient showdown between fungus and mammals that started when dinosaurs disappeared from the earth. Back then, the battle swung in our favor (spoiler alert!) and we’ve been hanging onto that win ever since. But one scientist suggests that the rise of this new infectious fungus indicates our edge is slipping, degree by increasing degree.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Molly Webster, and produced by Molly and Bethel Habte, with production help from Tad Davis. Special thanks to Julie Parsonnet and Aviv Bergman. </em></p>\n<p><strong><em>If you caught this segment in our radio broadcast and want to hear the segment it aired with, <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/articles/dispatch-14-covid-crystal-ball\">check out Covid Crystal Ball here.</a></em></strong></p>\n<p><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></span></em></em><span> </span></em></p>\n<p><span>Further Fungus Reading: </span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/06/health/drug-resistant-candida-auris.html\">NYTimes feature</a> on the mysterious rise of </span><em><span>Candida auris.</span></em><span> </span></p>\n<p> <span>Arturo's <a href=\"https://mbio.asm.org/content/10/4/e01397-19#:~:text=Candida%20auris%20is%20a%20new,exhibiting%20nonsusceptibility%20to%20antifungal%20agents.\">paper</a>: “On the emergence of </span><em><span>Candida auris</span></em><span>, Climate Change, Azoles, Swamps, and Birds”, by Arturo Casadevall, et al.</span></p>\n<p><span>“On the Origins of a Species: What Might Explain the Rise of </span><em><span>Candida auris</span></em><span>?”, a <a href=\"https://www.mdpi.com/2309-608X/5/3/58/htm\">report from the CDC</a>.</span></p>\n<h1> </h1>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-09-04T04:06:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"translation", "title"=>"Translation", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20translationrerun.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=407876", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">How close can words get you to the truth and feel and force of life? That's the question poking at our ribs this hour, as we wonder how it is that the right words can have the wrong meanings, and why sometimes the best translations lead us to an understanding that's way deeper than language. This episode, a bunch of stories that play out in the middle space between one reality and another — where poetry, insult comedy, 911 calls, and even our own bodies work to close the gap.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></span></em></em> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks for the music of <a href=\"http://ghosttrainorchestra.com/\">Brian Carpenter's Ghost Train Orchestra</a></em></p>", "tease"=>"How&nbsp;the right words can have the wrong meanings, and the best translations lead us to an understanding that's way deeper than language.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1598558100000, "publish-at"=>"2020-08-27T15:55:00", "date-line-ts"=>1598544000, "estimated-duration"=>3909, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Three-faces-of-Rouen-Cathedral.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/500/250/l/80/1/Three-faces-of-Rouen-Cathedral.jpg", "h"=>250, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Three-faces-of-Rouen-Cathedral.jpg", "w"=>500, "id"=>126776, "credits-name"=>"Image Credit: Claude Monet"}, "objectID"=>"407876", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Translation", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">How close can words get you to the truth and feel and force of life? That's the question poking at our ribs this hour, as we wonder how it is that the right words can have the wrong meanings, and why sometimes the best translations lead us to an understanding that's way deeper than language. This episode, a bunch of stories that play out in the middle space between one reality and another — where poetry, insult comedy, 911 calls, and even our own bodies work to close the gap.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></span></em></em> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Special thanks for the music of <a href=\"http://ghosttrainorchestra.com/\">Brian Carpenter's Ghost Train Orchestra</a></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-08-27T15:55:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"lebanon-usa", "title"=>"Lebanon, USA", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20lebanonusa.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1046882", "description"=>"<p><span><span>This is a story of a road trip. After a particularly traumatic Valentine's Day, Fadi Boukaram was surfing google maps and noticed that there was a town called Lebanon... in Oregon. Being Lebanese himself, he wondered, how many Lebanons exist in the US? The answer: 47. Thus began his journey to visit them all and find an America he'd never expected, and the homeland he'd been searching for all along. </span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>This episode </span><span>was made in collaboration with Kerning Cultures, a podcast that tells stories from the Middle East and North Africa.  </span></span><span>The original \"Lebanon USA\" story was reported by Alex Atack with editorial support from Bella Ibrahim, Dana Ballout</span><span>, Zeina Dowidar, and Hebah Fisher. Original sound design by Alex Atack. </span></p>\n<p><span></span><em>The new update of the story was produced and reported by Shima Oliaee. </em></p>\n<p><em>We had original music by Thomas Koner and Jad Atoui.</em></p>\n<p><em>Be sure to check out Kerning Cultures at their website <a href=\"http://www.kerningcultures.com\">www.kerningcultures.com</a>, instagram <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/kerningcultures/\">@kerningcultures</a>, or twitter <a href=\"https://twitter.com/kerningcultures\">@kerningcultures</a>. You can read more about Fadi’s trips and see his photographs at <a href=\"http://www.lebanonusa.com\">lebanonusa.com</a> or on his Instagram at <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/instagram.com/lebanonusa\">@lebanonusa</a>.</em></p>\n<p><span><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></span></em></em></span></p>\n<p><span><em>Editor's<span> </span>Note: In an earlier version of this episode, we inaccurately described a grain elevator. We have updated the audio to reflect the correction.</em></span></p>\n<p>---</p>\n<p>If you would like to donate to Beirut at this time, we have links here (from NYT):</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://www.supportlrc.app/donate/\">Lebanese Red Cross</a> dispatches every ambulance from North Lebanon, Bekaa, and South Lebanon to Beirut to treat the wounded and help in search-and-rescue operations. You can make a <a href=\"https://www.supportlrc.app/donate/donate.html\">contribution here</a>. </p>\n<p>The United Nations’ <a href=\"https://www.wfp.org/\">World Food Program</a> provides food to people displaced or made homeless after the blast. Lebanon imports nearly 85% of its food, and the port of Beirut, the epicenter of the explosion, played a central role in that supply chain. With the port now severely damaged, food prices are likely to be beyond the reach of many. <a href=\"https://secure.wfpusa.org/donate/urgent-support-emergency-humanitarian-response-lebanon?ms=2008_UNR_LebanonEmergency_NYT_WEB\">You can donate here</a>.</p>\n<p>The NGO <a href=\"https://twitter.com/HI_UnitedStates/status/1291039281037926400\">Humanity and Inclusion</a> has 100 workers in Lebanon, including physical therapists, psychologists and social workers. They are focusing on post-surgical therapy in Beirut following the explosion. You can make a <a href=\"https://www.hi-us.org/beirut_explosion\">contribution here</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://internationalmedicalcorps.org/\">International Medical Corps</a> is deploying medical units and will provide mental health care to those affected in Lebanon. The humanitarian aid organization also provides health services to Syrian refugees in Lebanon, and vulnerable Lebanese. You can <a href=\"https://give.internationalmedicalcorps.org/page/65328/donate/1?ea.tracking.id=DP~LB21~DPHPL2108\">donate here</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?time_continue=18&amp;v=1Z-eqCGUm68&amp;feature=emb_title\">Islamic Relief</a>, which specializes in food aid and emergency response, is helping to put a supply chain in place for emergency aid in Beirut. You can <a href=\"https://irusa.org/middle-east/lebanon/?utm_source=Website&amp;utm_medium=Social%20Media&amp;utm_campaign=Lebanon%20Emergency\">donate here</a>.</p>\n<p>Save the Children have launched a Lebanon’s children relief fund, to which you can <a href=\"https://support.savethechildren.org/site/Donation2?df_id=4450&amp;4450.donation=form1\">donate here</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.unicefusa.org/stories/deadly-blast-rocks-lebanon-already-crisis/37528https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/unicef-sad-and-shocked-beirut-explosions-concerned-about-wellbeing-children-and\">UNICEF</a>, the United Nations agency specializing in aid to children, is providing medical and vaccine supplies in Beirut, and supplying drinking water to rescue workers at the Beirut port. Its on-the-ground team is also counseling children traumatized by the blast. You can <a href=\"https://www.unicefusa.org/?form=LebanonEmergency&amp;utm_campaign=20200805_Emergencies&amp;utm_medium=Referral&amp;utm_source=PRGeneralOnline+Broadcast&amp;utm_content=LebanonDonate&amp;ms=Referral_PRL_2020_Emergencies_20200805_PRGeneralOnline+Broadcast_LebanonDonate_none_none&amp;initialms=Referral_PRL_2020_Emergencies_20200805_PRGeneralOnline+Broadcast_LebanonDonate_none_none\">donate here</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.impactlebanon.org/initiatives/disaster-relief-after-explosion-in-beirut?event=false\">Impact Lebanon</a>, a nonprofit organization, has set up a <a href=\"https://www.justgiving.com/crowdfunding/lebanon-relief?utm_term=PYp7gXzyD\">crowdfunding campaign</a> to help organizations on the ground, and is helping to share information about people still missing after the explosion. The group had raised over $3 million as of Wednesday and donated the first $100,000 to the Lebanese Red Cross.</p>\n<p>The health care organization <a href=\"https://www.projecthope.org/explosion-in-beirut/\">Project HOPE</a> is bringing medical supplies and protective gear to Beirut and assisting the authorities on the ground. A donation page is <a href=\"https://secure.projecthope.org/site/SPageNavigator/2020_02_Main_lightboxupdate.html?autologin=true&amp;mfc_pref=T&amp;s_subsrc=rd1&amp;utm_source=fundraising&amp;utm_medium=web&amp;utm_campaign=evergreen\">available here</a>.</p>\n<p>Over 300,000 people in Beirut were displaced from their homes by the explosion. <a href=\"https://www.justgiving.com/crowdfunding/impact-lebanon\">Baytna Baytak</a>, a charity that provided free housing to health care workers during the coronavirus pandemic, is now raising funds with Impact Lebanon to shelter those who have been displaced.</p>\n<p>For those in Beirut, here is <a href=\"https://www.daleelthawra.com/category/urgent-needs/\">a list of urgent blood needs</a>. Several social media accounts have also been set up to <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/locatevictimsbeirut/\">help locate victims</a>.</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"In this&nbsp;adapted piece&nbsp;by Kerning Cultures, we take a road trip across America -&nbsp;starting&nbsp;with two Jads and&nbsp;ending&nbsp;with forty-seven Lebanons.", "publishTime"=>1597977900000, "publish-at"=>"2020-08-20T22:45:00", "date-line-ts"=>1597977900, "estimated-duration"=>2541, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/08/Lebanon_USA_Radiolab_Fadi.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/l/80/2020/08/Lebanon_USA_Radiolab_Fadi.jpg", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Lebanon, USA", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/08/Lebanon_USA_Radiolab_Fadi.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>191434, "credits-name"=>"Fadi BouKaram"}, "objectID"=>"1046882", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Lebanon, USA", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span><span>This is a story of a road trip. After a particularly traumatic Valentine's Day, Fadi Boukaram was surfing google maps and noticed that there was a town called Lebanon... in Oregon. Being Lebanese himself, he wondered, how many Lebanons exist in the US? The answer: 47. Thus began his journey to visit them all and find an America he'd never expected, and the homeland he'd been searching for all along. </span></span></p>\n<p><span><span>This episode </span><span>was made in collaboration with Kerning Cultures, a podcast that tells stories from the Middle East and North Africa.  </span></span><span>The original \"Lebanon USA\" story was reported by Alex Atack with editorial support from Bella Ibrahim, Dana Ballout</span><span>, Zeina Dowidar, and Hebah Fisher. Original sound design by Alex Atack. </span></p>\n<p><span></span><em>The new update of the story was produced and reported by Shima Oliaee. </em></p>\n<p><em>We had original music by Thomas Koner and Jad Atoui.</em></p>\n<p><em>Be sure to check out Kerning Cultures at their website <a href=\"http://www.kerningcultures.com\">www.kerningcultures.com</a>, instagram <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/kerningcultures/\">@kerningcultures</a>, or twitter <a href=\"https://twitter.com/kerningcultures\">@kerningcultures</a>. You can read more about Fadi’s trips and see his photographs at <a href=\"http://www.lebanonusa.com\">lebanonusa.com</a> or on his Instagram at <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/instagram.com/lebanonusa\">@lebanonusa</a>.</em></p>\n<p><span><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></span></em></em></span></p>\n<p><span><em>Editor's<span> </span>Note: In an earlier version of this episode, we inaccurately described a grain elevator. We have updated the audio to reflect the correction.</em></span></p>\n<p>---</p>\n<p>If you would like to donate to Beirut at this time, we have links here (from NYT):</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://www.supportlrc.app/donate/\">Lebanese Red Cross</a> dispatches every ambulance from North Lebanon, Bekaa, and South Lebanon to Beirut to treat the wounded and help in search-and-rescue operations. You can make a <a href=\"https://www.supportlrc.app/donate/donate.html\">contribution here</a>. </p>\n<p>The United Nations’ <a href=\"https://www.wfp.org/\">World Food Program</a> provides food to people displaced or made homeless after the blast. Lebanon imports nearly 85% of its food, and the port of Beirut, the epicenter of the explosion, played a central role in that supply chain. With the port now severely damaged, food prices are likely to be beyond the reach of many. <a href=\"https://secure.wfpusa.org/donate/urgent-support-emergency-humanitarian-response-lebanon?ms=2008_UNR_LebanonEmergency_NYT_WEB\">You can donate here</a>.</p>\n<p>The NGO <a href=\"https://twitter.com/HI_UnitedStates/status/1291039281037926400\">Humanity and Inclusion</a> has 100 workers in Lebanon, including physical therapists, psychologists and social workers. They are focusing on post-surgical therapy in Beirut following the explosion. You can make a <a href=\"https://www.hi-us.org/beirut_explosion\">contribution here</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://internationalmedicalcorps.org/\">International Medical Corps</a> is deploying medical units and will provide mental health care to those affected in Lebanon. The humanitarian aid organization also provides health services to Syrian refugees in Lebanon, and vulnerable Lebanese. You can <a href=\"https://give.internationalmedicalcorps.org/page/65328/donate/1?ea.tracking.id=DP~LB21~DPHPL2108\">donate here</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?time_continue=18&amp;v=1Z-eqCGUm68&amp;feature=emb_title\">Islamic Relief</a>, which specializes in food aid and emergency response, is helping to put a supply chain in place for emergency aid in Beirut. You can <a href=\"https://irusa.org/middle-east/lebanon/?utm_source=Website&amp;utm_medium=Social%20Media&amp;utm_campaign=Lebanon%20Emergency\">donate here</a>.</p>\n<p>Save the Children have launched a Lebanon’s children relief fund, to which you can <a href=\"https://support.savethechildren.org/site/Donation2?df_id=4450&amp;4450.donation=form1\">donate here</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.unicefusa.org/stories/deadly-blast-rocks-lebanon-already-crisis/37528https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/unicef-sad-and-shocked-beirut-explosions-concerned-about-wellbeing-children-and\">UNICEF</a>, the United Nations agency specializing in aid to children, is providing medical and vaccine supplies in Beirut, and supplying drinking water to rescue workers at the Beirut port. Its on-the-ground team is also counseling children traumatized by the blast. You can <a href=\"https://www.unicefusa.org/?form=LebanonEmergency&amp;utm_campaign=20200805_Emergencies&amp;utm_medium=Referral&amp;utm_source=PRGeneralOnline+Broadcast&amp;utm_content=LebanonDonate&amp;ms=Referral_PRL_2020_Emergencies_20200805_PRGeneralOnline+Broadcast_LebanonDonate_none_none&amp;initialms=Referral_PRL_2020_Emergencies_20200805_PRGeneralOnline+Broadcast_LebanonDonate_none_none\">donate here</a>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.impactlebanon.org/initiatives/disaster-relief-after-explosion-in-beirut?event=false\">Impact Lebanon</a>, a nonprofit organization, has set up a <a href=\"https://www.justgiving.com/crowdfunding/lebanon-relief?utm_term=PYp7gXzyD\">crowdfunding campaign</a> to help organizations on the ground, and is helping to share information about people still missing after the explosion. The group had raised over $3 million as of Wednesday and donated the first $100,000 to the Lebanese Red Cross.</p>\n<p>The health care organization <a href=\"https://www.projecthope.org/explosion-in-beirut/\">Project HOPE</a> is bringing medical supplies and protective gear to Beirut and assisting the authorities on the ground. A donation page is <a href=\"https://secure.projecthope.org/site/SPageNavigator/2020_02_Main_lightboxupdate.html?autologin=true&amp;mfc_pref=T&amp;s_subsrc=rd1&amp;utm_source=fundraising&amp;utm_medium=web&amp;utm_campaign=evergreen\">available here</a>.</p>\n<p>Over 300,000 people in Beirut were displaced from their homes by the explosion. <a href=\"https://www.justgiving.com/crowdfunding/impact-lebanon\">Baytna Baytak</a>, a charity that provided free housing to health care workers during the coronavirus pandemic, is now raising funds with Impact Lebanon to shelter those who have been displaced.</p>\n<p>For those in Beirut, here is <a href=\"https://www.daleelthawra.com/category/urgent-needs/\">a list of urgent blood needs</a>. Several social media accounts have also been set up to <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/locatevictimsbeirut/\">help locate victims</a>.</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-08-20T22:45:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"wubi-effect", "title"=>"The Wubi Effect", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20thewubieffect.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1046078", "description"=>"<p><span>When we think of China today, we think of a technological superpower. From <span>Huawei</span> and 5G to TikTok and viral social media, China is stride for stride with the United States in the world of computing. However, China’s technological renaissance almost didn’t happen. And for one very basic reason: The Chinese language, with its 70,000 plus characters, couldn’t fit on a keyboard. </span></p>\n<p>Today, we tell the story of Professor Wang Yongmin, a hard headed computer programmer who solved this puzzle and laid the foundation for the China we know today.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Simon Adler with reporting assistance from <a href=\"https://yang-yang.space/\">Yang Yang</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Martin Howard. You can view his renowned collection of typewriters at: <a href=\"http://www.antiquetypewriters.com\">antiquetypewriters.com</a></em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></span></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"In the 1980s, as China was modernizing, they ran into a problem. There was no way to fit their 70,000 plus character language on a QWERTY keyboard. Today, the story of how they did it.", "publishTime"=>1597388700000, "publish-at"=>"2020-08-14T03:05:00", "date-line-ts"=>1597388700, "estimated-duration"=>3302, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/08/QWERTY.JPG", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1355/1600/c/80/2020/08/QWERTY.JPG", "h"=>1600, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/08/QWERTY.JPG", "w"=>1355, "id"=>191400, "credits-name"=>"Prof. Wang Yongmin"}, "objectID"=>"1046078", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Wubi Effect", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>When we think of China today, we think of a technological superpower. From <span>Huawei</span> and 5G to TikTok and viral social media, China is stride for stride with the United States in the world of computing. However, China’s technological renaissance almost didn’t happen. And for one very basic reason: The Chinese language, with its 70,000 plus characters, couldn’t fit on a keyboard. </span></p>\n<p>Today, we tell the story of Professor Wang Yongmin, a hard headed computer programmer who solved this puzzle and laid the foundation for the China we know today.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Simon Adler with reporting assistance from <a href=\"https://yang-yang.space/\">Yang Yang</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Martin Howard. You can view his renowned collection of typewriters at: <a href=\"http://www.antiquetypewriters.com\">antiquetypewriters.com</a></em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></span></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-08-14T03:05:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"uncounted", "title"=>"Uncounted", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20uncounted.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1044433", "description"=>"<p><span>First things first: our very own Latif Nasser has an exciting new show on Netflix. He talks to Jad about the hidden forces of the world that connect us all.</span></p>\n<p><span>Then, with an eye on the upcoming election, we take a look back: at two pieces from </span><em><span>More Perfect</span></em><span> Season 3 about Constitutional amendments that determine who gets to vote.</span></p>\n<p><span>Former </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span> producer Julia Longoria takes us to Washington, D.C. The capital is at the heart of our democracy, but it’s not a state, and it wasn’t until the 23rd Amendment that its people got the right to vote for president. But that still left DC without full representation in Congress; D.C. sends a \"non-voting delegate\" to the House. Julia profiles that delegate, Congresswoman Eleanor Holmes Norton, and her unique approach to fighting for power in a virtually powerless role.</span></p>\n<p><span>Second, </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span> producer Sarah Qari looks at a current fight to lower the US voting age to 16 that harkens back to the fight for the 26th Amendment in the 1960s. Eighteen-year-olds at the time argued that if they were old enough to be drafted to fight in the War, they were old enough to have a voice in our democracy. But what about today, when even younger Americans are finding themselves at the center of national political debates? Does it mean we should lower the voting age even further?</span></p>\n<p><em>Music in this episode by <a href=\"https://carlingandwill.com/#eluidae6de72f\">Carling &amp; Will</a></em></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported and produced by Julia Longoria and Sarah Qari.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Check out Latif Nasser’s new Netflix show Connected </span></em><a href=\"https://www.netflix.com/title/81031737\"><em><span>here</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"A look at two groups of uncounted Americans who want to have a say in our democracy. Plus, Latif has a new show on Netflix!", "publishTime"=>1596783240000, "publish-at"=>"2020-08-07T02:54:00", "date-line-ts"=>1596783180, "estimated-duration"=>3035, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/08/ballotbox.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/880/586/c/80/2020/08/ballotbox.jpg", "h"=>586, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/08/ballotbox.jpg", "w"=>880, "id"=>191298, "credits-name"=>"https://www.pikist.com/"}, "objectID"=>"1044433", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Uncounted", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>First things first: our very own Latif Nasser has an exciting new show on Netflix. He talks to Jad about the hidden forces of the world that connect us all.</span></p>\n<p><span>Then, with an eye on the upcoming election, we take a look back: at two pieces from </span><em><span>More Perfect</span></em><span> Season 3 about Constitutional amendments that determine who gets to vote.</span></p>\n<p><span>Former </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span> producer Julia Longoria takes us to Washington, D.C. The capital is at the heart of our democracy, but it’s not a state, and it wasn’t until the 23rd Amendment that its people got the right to vote for president. But that still left DC without full representation in Congress; D.C. sends a \"non-voting delegate\" to the House. Julia profiles that delegate, Congresswoman Eleanor Holmes Norton, and her unique approach to fighting for power in a virtually powerless role.</span></p>\n<p><span>Second, </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span> producer Sarah Qari looks at a current fight to lower the US voting age to 16 that harkens back to the fight for the 26th Amendment in the 1960s. Eighteen-year-olds at the time argued that if they were old enough to be drafted to fight in the War, they were old enough to have a voice in our democracy. But what about today, when even younger Americans are finding themselves at the center of national political debates? Does it mean we should lower the voting age even further?</span></p>\n<p><em>Music in this episode by <a href=\"https://carlingandwill.com/#eluidae6de72f\">Carling &amp; Will</a></em></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported and produced by Julia Longoria and Sarah Qari.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Check out Latif Nasser’s new Netflix show Connected </span></em><a href=\"https://www.netflix.com/title/81031737\"><em><span>here</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-08-07T02:54:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"invisible-allies", "title"=>"Invisible Allies", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20invisibleallies.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1041515", "description"=>"<p><span>As scientists have been scrambling to find new and better ways to treat <span>covid-19,</span> they’ve come across some unexpected allies. Invisible and primordial, these protectors have been with us all along. And they just might help us to better weather this viral storm.</span></p>\n<p><span>To kick things off, we travel through time from a homeless shelter to a military hospital, pondering the pandemic-fighting power of the sun. And then, we dive deep into the periodic table to look at how a simple element might actually be a microbe’s biggest foe.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Simon Adler and Molly Webster, and produced by Annie McEwen, Pat Walters, Simon Adler, and Molly Webster, with production help from Tad Davis.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"From the brightest star, to the most elemental particle, scientists are considering strange solutions for covid-19.", "publishTime"=>1596163260000, "publish-at"=>"2020-07-30T22:41:00", "date-line-ts"=>1596163260, "estimated-duration"=>2519, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/07/vitaminD.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/720/428/l/80/2020/07/vitaminD.jpg", "h"=>428, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/07/vitaminD.jpg", "w"=>720, "id"=>191202, "credits-name"=>"Health.mil"}, "objectID"=>"1041515", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Invisible Allies", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>As scientists have been scrambling to find new and better ways to treat <span>covid-19,</span> they’ve come across some unexpected allies. Invisible and primordial, these protectors have been with us all along. And they just might help us to better weather this viral storm.</span></p>\n<p><span>To kick things off, we travel through time from a homeless shelter to a military hospital, pondering the pandemic-fighting power of the sun. And then, we dive deep into the periodic table to look at how a simple element might actually be a microbe’s biggest foe.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Simon Adler and Molly Webster, and produced by Annie McEwen, Pat Walters, Simon Adler, and Molly Webster, with production help from Tad Davis.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-07-30T22:41:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"baby-blue-blood-drive", "title"=>"Baby Blue Blood Drive", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20babybluebloodupdate.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=879269", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Horseshoe crabs are not much to look at.  But beneath their unassuming catcher’s-mitt shell, they harbor a half-billion-year-old secret: a superpower that helped them outlive the dinosaurs and survive all the Earth’s mass extinctions.  And what is that secret superpower? Their blood. Their baby blue blood.  And it’s so miraculous that for decades, it hasn’t just been saving their butts, it’s been saving ours too.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>But that all might be about to change.  </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Follow us as we follow these ancient critters - from a raunchy beach orgy to a marine blood drive to the most secluded waterslide - and learn a thing or two from them about how much we depend on nature and how much it depends on us.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">BONUS: If you want to know more about how miraculous horseshoe crabs are, here's a bunch of our favorite reads:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Alexis Madrigal, <a href=\"https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2014/02/the-blood-harvest/284078/\">\"The Blood Harvest\"</a> in The Atlantic, and Sarah Zhang's recent follow up in The Atlantic, <a href=\"https://www.theatlantic.com/science/archive/2018/05/blood-in-the-water/559229/\">\"The Last Days of the Blue Blood Harvest\" </a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Deborah Cramer, <em><a href=\"http://www.deborahcramer.com/books/the-narrow-edge-red-knot/\">The Narrow Edge</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Deborah Cramer, <a href=\"https://www.audubon.org/magazine/summer-2018/inside-biomedical-revolution-save-horseshoe-crabs\">\"Inside the Biomedical Revolution to Save Horseshoe Crabs\"</a> in Audubon Magazine </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Richard Fortey, <em><a href=\"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/54786/horseshoe-crabs-and-velvet-worms-by-richard-fortey/9780307275530/\">Horseshoe Crabs and Velvet Worms</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Ian Frazier, <a href=\"https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2014/04/14/blue-bloods\">\"Blue Bloods\" </a> in The New Yorker </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Lulu Miller's short story, <a href=\"https://catapult.co/stories/me-and-jane\">\"Me and Jane\" </a> in Catapult Magazine</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Jerry Gault, <a href=\"http://eureka.criver.com/the-most-noble-fishing-there-is/\">\"The Most Noble Fishing There Is\" </a> in Charles River's Eureka Magazine</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">or check out Glenn Gauvry's horseshoe crab <a href=\"http://www.horseshoecrab.org/research/\">research database</a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser with help from Damiano Marchetti and Lulu Miller, and was produced by Annie McEwen and Matt Kielty with help from Liza Yeager.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Arlene Shaner at the NY Academy of Medicine, Tim Wisniewski at the Alan Mason Chesney Medical Archives at Johns Hopkins University, Jennifer Walton at the library of the Marine Biological Lab of the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution, and Glenn Gauvry at the Ecological Research and Development Group.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Horseshoe crabs harbor a half-billion-year-old secret: a superpower that helped them outlive&nbsp;the dinosaurs. But it hasn&rsquo;t just been saving their butts, it&rsquo;s been saving ours too.", "publishTime"=>1595559960000, "publish-at"=>"2020-07-23T23:06:00", "date-line-ts"=>1595559960, "estimated-duration"=>4005, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/08/Hcrabs.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/599/747/c/80/2018/08/Hcrabs.png", "h"=>747, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Milne-Edwards’ Recherches sur l’anatomie des Limules", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/08/Hcrabs.png", "w"=>599, "id"=>177921, "credits-name"=>"American Museum of Natural History"}, "objectID"=>"879269", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Baby Blue Blood Drive", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>Horseshoe crabs are not much to look at.  But beneath their unassuming catcher’s-mitt shell, they harbor a half-billion-year-old secret: a superpower that helped them outlive the dinosaurs and survive all the Earth’s mass extinctions.  And what is that secret superpower? Their blood. Their baby blue blood.  And it’s so miraculous that for decades, it hasn’t just been saving their butts, it’s been saving ours too.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>But that all might be about to change.  </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Follow us as we follow these ancient critters - from a raunchy beach orgy to a marine blood drive to the most secluded waterslide - and learn a thing or two from them about how much we depend on nature and how much it depends on us.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">BONUS: If you want to know more about how miraculous horseshoe crabs are, here's a bunch of our favorite reads:</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Alexis Madrigal, <a href=\"https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2014/02/the-blood-harvest/284078/\">\"The Blood Harvest\"</a> in The Atlantic, and Sarah Zhang's recent follow up in The Atlantic, <a href=\"https://www.theatlantic.com/science/archive/2018/05/blood-in-the-water/559229/\">\"The Last Days of the Blue Blood Harvest\" </a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Deborah Cramer, <em><a href=\"http://www.deborahcramer.com/books/the-narrow-edge-red-knot/\">The Narrow Edge</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Deborah Cramer, <a href=\"https://www.audubon.org/magazine/summer-2018/inside-biomedical-revolution-save-horseshoe-crabs\">\"Inside the Biomedical Revolution to Save Horseshoe Crabs\"</a> in Audubon Magazine </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Richard Fortey, <em><a href=\"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/54786/horseshoe-crabs-and-velvet-worms-by-richard-fortey/9780307275530/\">Horseshoe Crabs and Velvet Worms</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Ian Frazier, <a href=\"https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2014/04/14/blue-bloods\">\"Blue Bloods\" </a> in The New Yorker </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Lulu Miller's short story, <a href=\"https://catapult.co/stories/me-and-jane\">\"Me and Jane\" </a> in Catapult Magazine</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Jerry Gault, <a href=\"http://eureka.criver.com/the-most-noble-fishing-there-is/\">\"The Most Noble Fishing There Is\" </a> in Charles River's Eureka Magazine</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">or check out Glenn Gauvry's horseshoe crab <a href=\"http://www.horseshoecrab.org/research/\">research database</a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"> </p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser with help from Damiano Marchetti and Lulu Miller, and was produced by Annie McEwen and Matt Kielty with help from Liza Yeager.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Arlene Shaner at the NY Academy of Medicine, Tim Wisniewski at the Alan Mason Chesney Medical Archives at Johns Hopkins University, Jennifer Walton at the library of the Marine Biological Lab of the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution, and Glenn Gauvry at the Ecological Research and Development Group.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-07-23T23:06:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dispatches-1918", "title"=>"Dispatches from 1918", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20dispatchesfrom1918.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1036588", "description"=>"<p><span>It’s hard to imagine what the world will look like when COVID-19 has passed. So in this episode, we look back to the years after 1918, at the political, artistic, and viral aftermath of the flu pandemic that killed between 50 and 100 million people and left our world permanently transformed.</span></p>\n<p><span><em>This episode was reported and produced by Rachael Cusick, Tad Davis, Tracie Hunte, Matt Kielty, Latif Nasser, Sarah Qari, Pat Walters, Molly Webster, with production assistance from Tad Davis and Bethel Habte.</em></span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to the <a href=\"http://www.radiodiaries.org/\"><em><span>Radio Diaries</span></em></a><em><span> podcast for letting us use an excerpt of their interview with Harry Mills. You can find the original episode </span></em><a href=\"http://www.radiodiaries.org/conrads-garage/\"><em><span>here</span></em></a><em><span>. For more on Egon Schiele’s life, check out </span></em><a href=\"http://www.schiele-dokumentation.at/home_en.php\"><em><span>the Leopold Museum’s biography</span></em></a><em><span>, by Verena Gamper.</span></em></em></p>\n<p><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em></em><span> </span></span></p>", "tease"=>"Thinking about our future, we look back on the aftermath of a century-old pandemic.", "publishTime"=>1594985280000, "publish-at"=>"2020-07-17T07:28:00", "date-line-ts"=>1594985280, "estimated-duration"=>4231, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/07/Egon_Schiele_012.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://commons.wikimedia.org/", "name"=>"Wikimedia Commons"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1564/l/80/2020/07/Egon_Schiele_012.jpg", "h"=>1564, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Egon Schiele -- Death and the Maiden", "credits-url"=>"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egon_Schiele_012.jpg", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/07/Egon_Schiele_012.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>190999, "credits-name"=>" Egon Schiele / Public domain "}, "objectID"=>"1036588", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dispatches from 1918", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>It’s hard to imagine what the world will look like when COVID-19 has passed. So in this episode, we look back to the years after 1918, at the political, artistic, and viral aftermath of the flu pandemic that killed between 50 and 100 million people and left our world permanently transformed.</span></p>\n<p><span><em>This episode was reported and produced by Rachael Cusick, Tad Davis, Tracie Hunte, Matt Kielty, Latif Nasser, Sarah Qari, Pat Walters, Molly Webster, with production assistance from Tad Davis and Bethel Habte.</em></span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to the <a href=\"http://www.radiodiaries.org/\"><em><span>Radio Diaries</span></em></a><em><span> podcast for letting us use an excerpt of their interview with Harry Mills. You can find the original episode </span></em><a href=\"http://www.radiodiaries.org/conrads-garage/\"><em><span>here</span></em></a><em><span>. For more on Egon Schiele’s life, check out </span></em><a href=\"http://www.schiele-dokumentation.at/home_en.php\"><em><span>the Leopold Museum’s biography</span></em></a><em><span>, by Verena Gamper.</span></em></em></p>\n<p><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em></em><span> </span></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-07-17T07:28:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"flag-and-fury", "title"=>"The Flag and the Fury", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20flagandfury.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1035124", "description"=>"<p><span>How do you actually make change in the world? For 126 years, Mississippi has had the Confederate battle flag on their state flag, and they were the last state in the nation where that emblem remained “officially” flying.  A few days ago, that flag came down. A few days before<span> </span></span><em><span>that</span></em><span>, it coming down would have seemed impossible. Shima Oliaee dives into the story behind this de-flagging: a journey involving a clash of histories, designs, families, and even cheerleading. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Shima Oliaee.</em></p>\n<p>To read or listen to Kiese Laymon's memoir <em>Heavy</em>: <a href=\"https://www.simonandschuster.com/books/Heavy/Kiese-Laymon/9781501125669\">https://www.simonandschuster.com/books/Heavy/Kiese-Laymon/9781501125669</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"For 126 years, Mississippi has had the Confederate battle flag on their state flag.&nbsp;Today, the dramatic behind-the-scenes story of it coming down.", "publishTime"=>1594591740000, "publish-at"=>"2020-07-12T18:09:00", "date-line-ts"=>1594591740, "estimated-duration"=>4404, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/07/The_Flag_and_the_Fury_OSM_Audio_Radiolab_Mississippi.JPG", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1132/l/80/2020/07/The_Flag_and_the_Fury_OSM_Audio_Radiolab_Mississippi.JPG", "h"=>1132, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"The Flag and the Fury OSM Audio", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/07/The_Flag_and_the_Fury_OSM_Audio_Radiolab_Mississippi.JPG", "w"=>1860, "id"=>190937, "credits-name"=>"Ashton Pittman"}, "objectID"=>"1035124", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Flag and the Fury", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>How do you actually make change in the world? For 126 years, Mississippi has had the Confederate battle flag on their state flag, and they were the last state in the nation where that emblem remained “officially” flying.  A few days ago, that flag came down. A few days before<span> </span></span><em><span>that</span></em><span>, it coming down would have seemed impossible. Shima Oliaee dives into the story behind this de-flagging: a journey involving a clash of histories, designs, families, and even cheerleading. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Shima Oliaee.</em></p>\n<p>To read or listen to Kiese Laymon's memoir <em>Heavy</em>: <a href=\"https://www.simonandschuster.com/books/Heavy/Kiese-Laymon/9781501125669\">https://www.simonandschuster.com/books/Heavy/Kiese-Laymon/9781501125669</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-07-12T18:09:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"third_ted_talk", "title"=>"The Third. A TED Talk.", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_membership/radiolab_membership20thethird.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1030935", "description"=>"<p><span>Jad gives a <a href=\"https://www.ted.com/talks/jad_abumrad_how_dolly_parton_led_me_to_an_epiphany\" target=\"_blank\">TED</a> talk about his life as a journalist and how <em>Radiolab</em> has evolved over the years. <br><br>Here's how TED described it:<br><br><span>How do you end a story? Host of <em>Radiolab</em> Jad Abumrad tells how his search for an answer led him home to the mountains of Tennessee, where he met an unexpected teacher: Dolly Parton.</span><br><br>Jad Nicholas Abumrad is a Lebanese-American radio host, composer and producer. He is the founder of the syndicated public radio program </span><a href=\"http://radiolab.org/\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Radiolab</em></a><span>, which is broadcast on over 600 radio stations nationwide and is downloaded more than 120 million times a year as a podcast. He also created </span><em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolabmoreperfect\" target=\"_blank\">More Perfect</a>, </em><span>a podcast that tells the stories behind the Supreme Court's most famous decisions. And most recently, </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/dolly-partons-america\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Dolly Parton's America</em></a><span>, a nine-episode podcast exploring the life and times of the iconic country music star. Abumrad has received three Peabody Awards and was named a MacArthur Fellow in 2011.</span></p>", "tease"=>"Jad gives a TED talk about his life as a journalist and how&nbsp;Radiolab&nbsp;has evolved over the years.", "publishTime"=>1593115707000, "publish-at"=>"2020-06-25T16:08:27", "date-line-ts"=>1593115707, "estimated-duration"=>1086, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/06/b144f40b-8285-45d3-99ef-c13fe76bd22b.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1038/l/80/2020/06/b144f40b-8285-45d3-99ef-c13fe76bd22b.jpg", "h"=>1038, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Jad Abumrad", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/06/b144f40b-8285-45d3-99ef-c13fe76bd22b.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>190693, "credits-name"=>"TED "}, "objectID"=>"1030935", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Third. A TED Talk.", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Jad gives a <a href=\"https://www.ted.com/talks/jad_abumrad_how_dolly_parton_led_me_to_an_epiphany\" target=\"_blank\">TED</a> talk about his life as a journalist and how <em>Radiolab</em> has evolved over the years. <br><br>Here's how TED described it:<br><br><span>How do you end a story? Host of <em>Radiolab</em> Jad Abumrad tells how his search for an answer led him home to the mountains of Tennessee, where he met an unexpected teacher: Dolly Parton.</span><br><br>Jad Nicholas Abumrad is a Lebanese-American radio host, composer and producer. He is the founder of the syndicated public radio program </span><a href=\"http://radiolab.org/\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Radiolab</em></a><span>, which is broadcast on over 600 radio stations nationwide and is downloaded more than 120 million times a year as a podcast. He also created </span><em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolabmoreperfect\" target=\"_blank\">More Perfect</a>, </em><span>a podcast that tells the stories behind the Supreme Court's most famous decisions. And most recently, </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/dolly-partons-america\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Dolly Parton's America</em></a><span>, a nine-episode podcast exploring the life and times of the iconic country music star. Abumrad has received three Peabody Awards and was named a MacArthur Fellow in 2011.</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-06-25T16:08:27", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"post-no-evil-redux", "title"=>"Post No Evil Redux", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20postnoevilredux.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1029294", "description"=>"<p><span>Today we revisit our story on Facebook and its rulebook, looking at what’s changed in the past two years and exploring how these rules will impact the 2020 Presidential Election. </span></p>\n<p>Back in 2008 Facebook began writing a document. It was a constitution of sorts, laying out what could and what couldn’t be posted on the site. Back then, the rules were simple, outlawing nudity and gore. Today, they’re anything but. </p>\n<p>How do you define hate speech? Where’s the line between a joke and an attack? How much butt is too much butt? Facebook has answered these questions. And from these answers they’ve written a rulebook that all 2.2 billion of us are expected to follow. Today, we explore that rulebook. We dive into its details and untangle its logic. All the while wondering what does this mean for the future of free speech?</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Simon Adler with help from Tracie Hunte and was produced by Simon Adler with help from Bethel Habte.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Sarah Roberts, Jeffrey Rosen, Carolyn Glanville, Ruchika Budhraja, Brian Dogan, Ellen Silver, James Mitchell, Guy Rosen, <span>Mike Masnick, </span>and our voice actor Michael Chernus.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Breastfeeding, beheadings and bombings, Facebook has rules to handle them all. Today, we explore those rules and ask what they tell us about the future of free speech. &nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1592564520000, "publish-at"=>"2020-06-19T07:02:00", "date-line-ts"=>1592564520, "estimated-duration"=>4258, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/06/facebookredux.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1035/c/80/2020/06/facebookredux.png", "h"=>1035, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/06/facebookredux.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>190576, "credits-name"=>"Simon Adler"}, "objectID"=>"1029294", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Post No Evil Redux", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Today we revisit our story on Facebook and its rulebook, looking at what’s changed in the past two years and exploring how these rules will impact the 2020 Presidential Election. </span></p>\n<p>Back in 2008 Facebook began writing a document. It was a constitution of sorts, laying out what could and what couldn’t be posted on the site. Back then, the rules were simple, outlawing nudity and gore. Today, they’re anything but. </p>\n<p>How do you define hate speech? Where’s the line between a joke and an attack? How much butt is too much butt? Facebook has answered these questions. And from these answers they’ve written a rulebook that all 2.2 billion of us are expected to follow. Today, we explore that rulebook. We dive into its details and untangle its logic. All the while wondering what does this mean for the future of free speech?</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Simon Adler with help from Tracie Hunte and was produced by Simon Adler with help from Bethel Habte.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Sarah Roberts, Jeffrey Rosen, Carolyn Glanville, Ruchika Budhraja, Brian Dogan, Ellen Silver, James Mitchell, Guy Rosen, <span>Mike Masnick, </span>and our voice actor Michael Chernus.</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.<span> </span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-06-19T07:02:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"liberation-rna", "title"=>"The Liberation of RNA", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20liberationrnafix.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1027810", "description"=>"<p>In June of 2019, Brandon Ogbunu got on stage and told a story for The Story Collider, a podcast and live storytelling show. Starting when he was a senior in college being shook down by a couple cops, Brandon tells us about navigating his ups and downs of a career in science, his startling connection to scientific racism, and his battle against biology's central dogma. </p>\n<p>Brandon’s story was recorded by The Story Collider as part of the 2019 Evolution Meeting in Providence, Rhode Island. You can find the full episode and learn more about The Story Collider <a href=\"https://www.storycollider.org/stories/2019/12/3/justice-stories-about-righteous-determination\">here</a>.</p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em></em><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"Today, an incident of racial profiling, a confrontation with scientific racism, and the liberation of RNA.", "publishTime"=>1592050740000, "publish-at"=>"2020-06-13T08:19:00", "date-line-ts"=>1592050740, "estimated-duration"=>1624, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/06/storycolliderbrandon.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1241/l/80/2020/06/storycolliderbrandon.jpg", "h"=>1241, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/06/storycolliderbrandon.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>190485, "credits-name"=>"David DelPoio"}, "objectID"=>"1027810", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Liberation of RNA", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In June of 2019, Brandon Ogbunu got on stage and told a story for The Story Collider, a podcast and live storytelling show. Starting when he was a senior in college being shook down by a couple cops, Brandon tells us about navigating his ups and downs of a career in science, his startling connection to scientific racism, and his battle against biology's central dogma. </p>\n<p>Brandon’s story was recorded by The Story Collider as part of the 2019 Evolution Meeting in Providence, Rhode Island. You can find the full episode and learn more about The Story Collider <a href=\"https://www.storycollider.org/stories/2019/12/3/justice-stories-about-righteous-determination\">here</a>.</p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em></em><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-06-13T08:19:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"graham", "title"=>"Graham", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20graham.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1026103", "description"=>"<p><span>If former Minneapolis police officer Derek Chauvin’s case for the death of George Floyd goes to trial, there will be this one, controversial legal principle looming over the proceedings: The reasonable officer.</span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode, we explore the origin of the reasonable officer standard, with the case that sent two Charlotte lawyers on a quest for true objectivity, and changed the face of policing in the US.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty with help from Kelly Prime and Annie McEwen.</em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1451\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1452\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<div>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em></em> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1454\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n<div class=\"story-credits__producing-org-credits producing-org-credits\"></div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"How a diabetic, a cop, and a bottle of orange juice changed the way we police, forever.", "publishTime"=>1591502160000, "publish-at"=>"2020-06-06T23:56:00", "date-line-ts"=>1591502160, "estimated-duration"=>3580, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/06/graham.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/800/c/80/2020/06/graham.jpg", "h"=>800, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/06/graham.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>190384, "credits-name"=>"Mitch Boyer"}, "objectID"=>"1026103", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Graham", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>If former Minneapolis police officer Derek Chauvin’s case for the death of George Floyd goes to trial, there will be this one, controversial legal principle looming over the proceedings: The reasonable officer.</span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode, we explore the origin of the reasonable officer standard, with the case that sent two Charlotte lawyers on a quest for true objectivity, and changed the face of policing in the US.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty with help from Kelly Prime and Annie McEwen.</em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1451\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1452\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<div>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em></em> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1454\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n<div class=\"story-credits__producing-org-credits producing-org-credits\"></div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-06-06T23:56:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"nina", "title"=>"Nina", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_extras/radiolab_extras20nina.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1026079", "description"=>"<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<p><span>Producer Tracie Hunte stumbled into a duet between Nina Simone and the sounds of protest outside her apartment. Then she discovered a performance by Nina on April 7, 1968 - three days after the assassination of Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. Tracie talks about what Nina’s music, born during another time when our country was facing questions that seemed to have no answer, meant then and why it still resonates today.</span></p>\n<p><em> Listen to Nina's brother, Samuel Waymon, talk about that April 7th concert <a href=\"https://www.npr.org/2008/04/06/89418339/why-remembering-nina-simones-tribute-to-the-rev-martin-luther-king-jr\">here.</a></em></p>\n</div>", "tease"=>"Nina Simone: what she told us then, and tells us now.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1591423500000, "publish-at"=>"2020-06-06T02:05:00", "date-line-ts"=>1591423500, "estimated-duration"=>nil, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/06/AP670810073.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1497/l/80/2020/06/AP670810073.jpg", "h"=>1497, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"H. Rap Brown, national chairman of the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee, and Nina Simone.", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/06/AP670810073.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>190383, "credits-name"=>"ASSOCIATED PRESS"}, "objectID"=>"1026079", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Nina", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<p><span>Producer Tracie Hunte stumbled into a duet between Nina Simone and the sounds of protest outside her apartment. Then she discovered a performance by Nina on April 7, 1968 - three days after the assassination of Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. Tracie talks about what Nina’s music, born during another time when our country was facing questions that seemed to have no answer, meant then and why it still resonates today.</span></p>\n<p><em> Listen to Nina's brother, Samuel Waymon, talk about that April 7th concert <a href=\"https://www.npr.org/2008/04/06/89418339/why-remembering-nina-simones-tribute-to-the-rev-martin-luther-king-jr\">here.</a></em></p>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-06-06T02:05:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dispatch-6-strange-times", "title"=>"Dispatch 6: Strange Times", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20strangetimes.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1023945", "description"=>"<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<div>\n<p><span>Covid has disrupted the most basic routines of our days and nights. But in the middle of a conversation about how to fight the virus, we find a place <span>impervious to</span> the stalled plans and frenetic demands of the outside world. It’s a very different kind of front line, where urgent work means moving slow, and time is marked out in tiny pre-planned steps. Then, on a walk through the woods, we consider how the tempo of our lives affects our minds and discover how the beats of biology shape our bodies.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced with help from Molly Webster and Tracie Hunte.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em></em> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"From a bio-safety lab to the woods of Tennessee, we explore the rhythms that shape our work, our lives, and our bodies.", "publishTime"=>1590753600000, "publish-at"=>"2020-05-29T08:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1590753600, "estimated-duration"=>2011, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/05/strangetimes.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2020/05/strangetimes.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/05/strangetimes.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>190216, "credits-name"=>"Justin Buschardt"}, "objectID"=>"1023945", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dispatch 6: Strange Times", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\n<div>\n<p><span>Covid has disrupted the most basic routines of our days and nights. But in the middle of a conversation about how to fight the virus, we find a place <span>impervious to</span> the stalled plans and frenetic demands of the outside world. It’s a very different kind of front line, where urgent work means moving slow, and time is marked out in tiny pre-planned steps. Then, on a walk through the woods, we consider how the tempo of our lives affects our minds and discover how the beats of biology shape our bodies.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced with help from Molly Webster and Tracie Hunte.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em></em> </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-05-29T08:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"269783-speedy-beet", "title"=>"Speedy Beet", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20speedybeetrerun.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=269783", "description"=>"<p><span>There are few musical moments more well-worn than the first four notes of Beethoven's Fifth Symphony. But in this short, we find out that Beethoven might have made a last-ditch effort to keep his music from ever feeling familiar, to keep pushing his listeners to a kind of psychological limit.</span></p>\n<p>Big thanks to the folks at <a href=\"http://www.alanpierson.com/\">Brooklyn Philharmonic</a>: Conductor Alan Pierson, Deborah Buck and Suzy Perelman on violin, Arash Amini on cello, and Ah Ling Neu on viola.</p>\n<p>And check out <a href=\"https://www.symphonystore.com/the-first-four-notes-guerrieri.html\">The First Four Notes</a>, Matthew Guerrieri's book on Beethoven's Fifth.</p>\n<p><span>Support</span><em><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><span> </span>Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Beethoven, like you've never heard him before.", "publishTime"=>1590147720000, "publish-at"=>"2020-05-22T07:42:00", "date-line-ts"=>1590147720, "estimated-duration"=>1445, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Metronome", "name"=>"1/metronome.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/1023/c/80/1/metronome.jpg", "h"=>1023, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/derekgavey/5500981048/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/metronome.jpg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>87805, "credits-name"=>"derekGavey"}, "objectID"=>"269783", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Speedy Beet", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>There are few musical moments more well-worn than the first four notes of Beethoven's Fifth Symphony. But in this short, we find out that Beethoven might have made a last-ditch effort to keep his music from ever feeling familiar, to keep pushing his listeners to a kind of psychological limit.</span></p>\n<p>Big thanks to the folks at <a href=\"http://www.alanpierson.com/\">Brooklyn Philharmonic</a>: Conductor Alan Pierson, Deborah Buck and Suzy Perelman on violin, Arash Amini on cello, and Ah Ling Neu on viola.</p>\n<p>And check out <a href=\"https://www.symphonystore.com/the-first-four-notes-guerrieri.html\">The First Four Notes</a>, Matthew Guerrieri's book on Beethoven's Fifth.</p>\n<p><span>Support</span><em><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><span> </span>Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-05-22T07:42:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"octomom", "title"=>"Octomom", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20octomom.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1020788", "description"=>"<p><span></span><span>In 2007, Bruce Robison’s robot submarine stumbled across an octopus settling in to brood her eggs. It seemed like a small moment. But as he went back to visit her, month after month, what began as a simple act of motherhood became a heroic feat that has never been equaled by any known species on Earth. </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported and produced by Annie McEwen. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Kim Fulton-Bennett and Rob Sherlock at the Monterey Bay Aquarium Research Institute. And thanks to the Indianapolis Chamber Orchestra for the use of their piece, “Concerto for Bassoon &amp; Chamber Orchestra: II. Beautiful.” </span></em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1287\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1296\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1316\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1310\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1333\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<p>Support<em><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><span> </span>Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></span></em></p>\n<p><span>If you need more ocean in your life, check out the incredible Monterey Bay Aquarium live cams (especially the jellies!): </span><a href=\"https://www.montereybayaquarium.org/animals/live-cams\"><span>www.montereybayaquarium.org/animals/live-cams</span></a></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><span> </span><span>Here’s a pic of Octomom sitting on her eggs, Nov. 1, 2007.  </span></p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\" style=\"max-width: 800px;\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/449/c/80/2020/05/GraneledoneT1146_09_02_52_23.png\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(© 2007 MBARI)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"A mile under the ocean, we get to watch an octopus perform a heroic act of heart and determination.", "publishTime"=>1589543760000, "publish-at"=>"2020-05-15T07:56:00", "date-line-ts"=>1589543760, "estimated-duration"=>1980, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/05/octomom_final_v2copy.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/c/80/2020/05/octomom_final_v2copy.jpg", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/05/octomom_final_v2copy.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>189974, "credits-name"=>"Kim Salt"}, "objectID"=>"1020788", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Octomom", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span></span><span>In 2007, Bruce Robison’s robot submarine stumbled across an octopus settling in to brood her eggs. It seemed like a small moment. But as he went back to visit her, month after month, what began as a simple act of motherhood became a heroic feat that has never been equaled by any known species on Earth. </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported and produced by Annie McEwen. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Kim Fulton-Bennett and Rob Sherlock at the Monterey Bay Aquarium Research Institute. And thanks to the Indianapolis Chamber Orchestra for the use of their piece, “Concerto for Bassoon &amp; Chamber Orchestra: II. Beautiful.” </span></em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1287\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1296\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1316\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1310\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1333\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<p>Support<em><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><span> </span>Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></span></em></p>\n<p><span>If you need more ocean in your life, check out the incredible Monterey Bay Aquarium live cams (especially the jellies!): </span><a href=\"https://www.montereybayaquarium.org/animals/live-cams\"><span>www.montereybayaquarium.org/animals/live-cams</span></a></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><span> </span><span>Here’s a pic of Octomom sitting on her eggs, Nov. 1, 2007.  </span></p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\" style=\"max-width: 800px;\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/449/c/80/2020/05/GraneledoneT1146_09_02_52_23.png\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-credit\">(© 2007 MBARI)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-05-15T07:56:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"why-fish-dont-exist", "title"=>"Why Fish Don't Exist", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20whyfishdontexist.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1020199", "description"=>"<p><span>Our old friend Lulu Miller — former Radiolab producer, co-creator of Invisibilia — has been obsessed by the chaos that rules the universe since long before it showed up as a global pandemic, and a few weeks ago, she published a book about it. It’s called <em>Why Fish Don’t Exist</em>. It’s part scientific adventure story, part philosophical manifesto, part chest-ripped-open memoir. Jad called her up to talk about how an obscure 19th century ichthyologist with a checkered past helped her find meaning in the world, and what she means when she says fish aren’t real.</span></p>\n<p><span>You can buy Lulu's book </span><em class=\"\">Why Fish Don’t Exist</em><span> </span><a href=\"https://bookshop.org/books/why-fish-don-t-exist-a-story-of-loss-love-and-the-hidden-order-of-life/9781501160271\" title=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9781501160271\">here</a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><span>This episode was produced by Pat Walters. </span></em></em></em></em></em></em></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><span><span>Special thanks to <a href=\"https://panamerican.bandcamp.com/music\">Pan•American</a>.</span></span></em></em></em></em></em></em></span></em></p>\n<p>Support<em><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><em> Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"A conversation with Lulu Miller about chaos, science, and her new book, Why Fish Don&rsquo;t Exist.", "publishTime"=>1589369760000, "publish-at"=>"2020-05-13T07:36:00", "date-line-ts"=>1589369760, "estimated-duration"=>1675, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/05/whyfishdontexist.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1351/c/80/2020/05/whyfishdontexist.png", "h"=>1351, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/05/whyfishdontexist.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>189930, "credits-name"=>"Kate Samworth"}, "objectID"=>"1020199", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Why Fish Don't Exist", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Our old friend Lulu Miller — former Radiolab producer, co-creator of Invisibilia — has been obsessed by the chaos that rules the universe since long before it showed up as a global pandemic, and a few weeks ago, she published a book about it. It’s called <em>Why Fish Don’t Exist</em>. It’s part scientific adventure story, part philosophical manifesto, part chest-ripped-open memoir. Jad called her up to talk about how an obscure 19th century ichthyologist with a checkered past helped her find meaning in the world, and what she means when she says fish aren’t real.</span></p>\n<p><span>You can buy Lulu's book </span><em class=\"\">Why Fish Don’t Exist</em><span> </span><a href=\"https://bookshop.org/books/why-fish-don-t-exist-a-story-of-loss-love-and-the-hidden-order-of-life/9781501160271\" title=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9781501160271\">here</a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><span>This episode was produced by Pat Walters. </span></em></em></em></em></em></em></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><span><span>Special thanks to <a href=\"https://panamerican.bandcamp.com/music\">Pan•American</a>.</span></span></em></em></em></em></em></em></span></em></p>\n<p>Support<em><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><em> Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-05-13T07:36:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"david-and-dominique_radiolab", "title"=>"David and Dominique", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20davidanddominique.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1019036", "description"=>"<p><span>David Gebel and Dominique Crisden have a couple of things in common: they both live in New York, they’re both gay, and they’re both HIV-positive. But David is in his 60s, and has been living with the disease since moving to New York in the ‘80s. Dominique, on the other hand, is only in his early 30s. From our friends at WNYC's “Nancy”, this episode features a very special conversation between David and Dominique about the similarities and differences in their experiences living with HIV.</span></p>\n<p>Special thanks to Krishna Stone at<span> </span><a href=\"http://www.gmhc.org/\">Gay Men's Health Crisis</a>, an HIV/AIDS prevention, care, and advocacy organization in New York. </p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Tobin Low, Kathy Tu and <span>Matt Collette.</span> Music in this episode by <a href=\"https://www.jeremyb.com/\">Jeremy Bloom</a> and <a href=\"https://www.overington.info/\">Alex Overington</a>. Theme by Alexander Overington.</em></p>\n<p><span>Note: </span><span>A version of this episode first ran on May 7, 2017.</span></p>\n<p><span><em>Support our work. Become a Nancy member today at<span> </span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/nancy-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=nancy-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Nancypodcast.org/donate</a>.    </em></span></p>", "tease"=>"David and Dominique have something in common.", "publishTime"=>1588935600000, "publish-at"=>"2020-05-08T07:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1588935600, "estimated-duration"=>1795, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/03/nancy-podcast-david-dominique.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/600/l/80/2019/03/nancy-podcast-david-dominique.jpg", "h"=>600, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/03/nancy-podcast-david-dominique.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>182298, "credits-name"=>" Danie Drankwalter"}, "objectID"=>"1019036", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"David and Dominique", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>David Gebel and Dominique Crisden have a couple of things in common: they both live in New York, they’re both gay, and they’re both HIV-positive. But David is in his 60s, and has been living with the disease since moving to New York in the ‘80s. Dominique, on the other hand, is only in his early 30s. From our friends at WNYC's “Nancy”, this episode features a very special conversation between David and Dominique about the similarities and differences in their experiences living with HIV.</span></p>\n<p>Special thanks to Krishna Stone at<span> </span><a href=\"http://www.gmhc.org/\">Gay Men's Health Crisis</a>, an HIV/AIDS prevention, care, and advocacy organization in New York. </p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Tobin Low, Kathy Tu and <span>Matt Collette.</span> Music in this episode by <a href=\"https://www.jeremyb.com/\">Jeremy Bloom</a> and <a href=\"https://www.overington.info/\">Alex Overington</a>. Theme by Alexander Overington.</em></p>\n<p><span>Note: </span><span>A version of this episode first ran on May 7, 2017.</span></p>\n<p><span><em>Support our work. Become a Nancy member today at<span> </span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/nancy-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=nancy-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Nancypodcast.org/donate</a>.    </em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-05-08T07:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dispatch-5-dont-stop-believin", "title"=>"Dispatch 5: Don't Stop Believin' ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20dontstopbelievinfix.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1018435", "description"=>"<p><span>Covid-19 has put emergency room doctors on the frontlines treating an illness that is still perplexing and unknown. Jad tracks one ER doctor in NYC as the doctor puzzles through clues, doing research of his own, trying desperately to save patients' lives. </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Jad Abumrad and Suzie Lechtenberg.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Jad tracks an emergency room doctor on the frontlines treating Covid-19.", "publishTime"=>1588764120000, "publish-at"=>"2020-05-06T07:22:00", "date-line-ts"=>1588764120, "estimated-duration"=>2018, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/05/covidcolorsfinalblack.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1394/l/80/2020/05/covidcolorsfinalblack.jpg", "h"=>1394, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/05/covidcolorsfinalblack.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>189777, "credits-name"=>"Justin Buschardt"}, "objectID"=>"1018435", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dispatch 5: Don't Stop Believin' ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Covid-19 has put emergency room doctors on the frontlines treating an illness that is still perplexing and unknown. Jad tracks one ER doctor in NYC as the doctor puzzles through clues, doing research of his own, trying desperately to save patients' lives. </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Jad Abumrad and Suzie Lechtenberg.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-05-06T07:22:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"atomic-artifacts", "title"=>"Atomic Artifacts", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_membership/radiolab_membership20atomicartifacts.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1015527", "description"=>"<p><span>Back in the 1950s, f</span>acing the threat of nuclear annihilation, federal officials sat down and pondered what American life would actually look like after an atomic attack. They faced a slew of practical questions like: Who would count the dead and where would they build the refugee camps? But they faced a more spiritual question as well. If Washington DC were hit, every object in the the National Archives would be eviscerated in a moment. Terrified by this reality, they set out to save some of America’s most precious stuff.<span> </span></p>\n<p>Today, we look back at the items our Cold War era planners sought to save and we ask the question: In the year 2020, what objects would we preserve now?<span> </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by <span data-markjs=\"true\" data-ogac=\"\" data-ogab=\"\" data-ogsc=\"\" data-ogsb=\"\">Simon</span> <span data-markjs=\"true\" data-ogac=\"\" data-ogab=\"\" data-ogsc=\"\" data-ogsb=\"\">Adler</span> with editing from Pat Walters and reporting assistance from Tad Davis. </em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></p>", "tease"=>"America represents many different things to many different people. What if you could only choose one thing, one physical object, to represent it all?&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1587729060000, "publish-at"=>"2020-04-24T07:51:00", "date-line-ts"=>1587729060, "estimated-duration"=>2502, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/04/AtomicArtifactUPDATE.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2020/04/AtomicArtifactUPDATE.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/04/AtomicArtifactUPDATE.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>189581, "credits-name"=>"Justin Buschardt"}, "objectID"=>"1015527", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Atomic Artifacts", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Back in the 1950s, f</span>acing the threat of nuclear annihilation, federal officials sat down and pondered what American life would actually look like after an atomic attack. They faced a slew of practical questions like: Who would count the dead and where would they build the refugee camps? But they faced a more spiritual question as well. If Washington DC were hit, every object in the the National Archives would be eviscerated in a moment. Terrified by this reality, they set out to save some of America’s most precious stuff.<span> </span></p>\n<p>Today, we look back at the items our Cold War era planners sought to save and we ask the question: In the year 2020, what objects would we preserve now?<span> </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by <span data-markjs=\"true\" data-ogac=\"\" data-ogab=\"\" data-ogsc=\"\" data-ogsb=\"\">Simon</span> <span data-markjs=\"true\" data-ogac=\"\" data-ogab=\"\" data-ogsc=\"\" data-ogsb=\"\">Adler</span> with editing from Pat Walters and reporting assistance from Tad Davis. </em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-04-24T07:51:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"cataclysm-sentence", "title"=>"The Cataclysm Sentence", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20thecataclysmsentencefix2.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1013524", "description"=>"<p><span>One day in 1961, the famous physicist Richard Feynman stepped in front of a Caltech lecture hall and posed this question to a group of undergraduate students: “If, in some cataclysm, all of scientific knowledge were to be destroyed, and only one sentence was passed on to the next generation of creatures, what statement would contain the most information in the fewest words?” </span><span data-stringify-type=\"paragraph-break\"></span><span>Now, Feynman had an answer to his own question - a good one. But his question got the entire team at Radiolab wondering, what did his sentence leave out? </span><span data-stringify-type=\"paragraph-break\"></span><span>So we posed Feynman’s cataclysm question to some of our favorite writers, artists, historians, futurists - all kinds of great thinkers. We asked them, “What’s the one sentence<span> </span></span><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\">you</em><span><span> </span>would want to pass on to the next generation that would contain the most information in the fewest words?” </span><span data-stringify-type=\"paragraph-break\"></span><span>What came back was an explosive collage of what it means to be alive right here and now, and what we want to say before we go.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Featuring</strong><span>:</span></p>\n<p><span>Richard Feynman, physicist (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9780465023950\"><em><span>The Pleasure of Finding Things Out</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Caitlin Doughty, mortician (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9780393652703\"><em><span>Will My Cat Eat My Eyeballs</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Esperanza Spalding, musician (</span><a href=\"http://esperanzaspalding.limitedrun.com/\"><em><span>12 Little Spells</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Cord Jefferson, writer (</span><a href=\"https://www.hbo.com/watchmen\"><em><span>Watchmen</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Merrill Garbus, musician (</span><a href=\"https://tune-yards.com/#album\"><em><span>I Can Feel You Creep Into My Private Life</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Jenny Odell, writer (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9781612197494\"><em><span>How to do Nothing</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Maria Popova, writer (</span><a href=\"https://www.brainpickings.org/\"><em><span>Brainpickings</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Alison Gopnik, developmental psychologist (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9781250132253\"><em><span>The Gardener and the Carpenter</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Rebecca Sugar, animator (</span><a href=\"https://www.cartoonnetwork.com/video/steven-universe/index.html\"><em><span>Steven Universe</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Nicholson Baker, writer (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9780399160998\"><em><span>Substitute</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>James Gleick, writer (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9780307908797\"><em><span>Time Travel</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Lady Pink, artist (</span><a href=\"http://www.ladypinknyc.com/\"><span>too many amazing works to pick just one</span></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Jenny Hollwell, writer (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9780805091199\"><em><span>Everything Lovely, Effortless, Safe</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Jaron Lanier, futurist (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9781250239082\"><em><span>Ten Arguments for Deleting Your Social Media Accounts Right Now</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Missy Mazzoli, composer (</span><a href=\"http://www.missymazzoli.com/opera\"><em><span>Proving Up</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty and Rachael Cusick, <span>with help from Jeremy Bloom, Zakiya Gibbons, and the entire Radiolab staff.<span> </span></span></em><strong><br></strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong><em>Special Thanks to:</em></strong></p>\n<p><em>Ella Frances Sanders, and her book, <a href=\"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/567035/eating-the-sun-by-ella-frances-sanders/\">\"Eating the Sun\"</a>, for inspiring this whole episode.</em></p>\n<p><span>Caltech for letting us use original audio of The Feynman Lectures on Physics. The entirety of the lectures are available to read for free online at </span><a href=\"https://protect-us.mimecast.com/s/6f_iCmZnD2SoJp0CB8FL-?domain=feynmanlectures.caltech.edu\"><span>www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>All the musicians who helped make the Primordial Chord, including:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/siavash_kamkar/?hl=en\"><span>Siavash Kamkar</span></a><span>, from Iran </span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://soundcloud.com/kooshkar\"><span>Koosha Pashangpour</span></a><span>, from Iran</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.curtismacdonald.com/\"><span>Curtis MacDonald</span></a><span>, from Canada</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://twitter.com/meadebernard\"><span>Meade Bernard</span></a><span>, from US</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.barnabyrea.com/\"><span>Barnaby Rea</span></a><span>, from UK</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://twitter.com/liavkerbel\"><span>Liav Kerbel</span></a><span>, from Belgium</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://twitter.com/tallsamtrombone\"><span>Sam Crittenden</span></a><span>, from US</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.saskialankhoorn.com/\"><span>Saskia Lankhoorn</span></a><span>, from Netherlands</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://twitter.com/BryanEHarris\"><span>Bryan Harris</span></a><span>, from US</span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/ameliawatkins.com\">Amelia Watkins</a>, from Canada</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://twitter.com/Claire_NoelleJ\"><span>Claire James</span></a><span>, from US</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.larsenale.com/?p=83\"><span>Ilario <span>Morciano</span></span></a><span>, from Italy</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/public/Matthias-Kowalczyk\"><span>Matthias Kowalczyk</span></a><span>, from Germany</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://soundcloud.com/solmaz-badri\"><span>Solmaz Badri</span></a><span>, from Iran</span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<p><span>All the wonderful people we interviewed </span><span>for sentences </span><span>but weren’t able to fit in this episode, including: Daniel Abrahm, Julia Alvarez, Aimee Bender, Sandra Cisneros, Stanley Chen, Lewis Dartnell, Ann Druyan, Rose Eveleth, Ty Frank, Julia Galef, Ross Gay, Gary Green, Cesar Harada, Dolores Huerta, Robin Hunicke, Brittany Kamai, Priya Krishna, Ken Liu, Carmen Maria Machado, James Martin, Judith Matloff, Ryan McMahon, Hasan Minhaj, Lorrie Moore, Priya Natarajan, Larry Owens, Sunni Patterson, Amy Pearl, Alison Roman, Domee Shi, Will Shortz, Sam Stein, Sohaib Sultan, Kara Swisher, </span><span>Jill Tarter, </span><span>Olive Watkins, Reggie Watts, Deborah Waxman, Alex Wellerstein, Caveh Zahedi.</span></p>\n<p><span><br><br></span></p>", "tease"=>"&nbsp;What&rsquo;s the one thing you&rsquo;d pass on if we all disappeared tomorrow?", "publishTime"=>1587193020000, "publish-at"=>"2020-04-18T02:57:00", "date-line-ts"=>1587193020, "estimated-duration"=>3960, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/04/CataconnectedFinal.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1394/l/80/2020/04/CataconnectedFinal.jpg", "h"=>1394, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/04/CataconnectedFinal.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>189482, "credits-name"=>"Justin Buschardt"}, "objectID"=>"1013524", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Cataclysm Sentence", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>One day in 1961, the famous physicist Richard Feynman stepped in front of a Caltech lecture hall and posed this question to a group of undergraduate students: “If, in some cataclysm, all of scientific knowledge were to be destroyed, and only one sentence was passed on to the next generation of creatures, what statement would contain the most information in the fewest words?” </span><span data-stringify-type=\"paragraph-break\"></span><span>Now, Feynman had an answer to his own question - a good one. But his question got the entire team at Radiolab wondering, what did his sentence leave out? </span><span data-stringify-type=\"paragraph-break\"></span><span>So we posed Feynman’s cataclysm question to some of our favorite writers, artists, historians, futurists - all kinds of great thinkers. We asked them, “What’s the one sentence<span> </span></span><em data-stringify-type=\"italic\">you</em><span><span> </span>would want to pass on to the next generation that would contain the most information in the fewest words?” </span><span data-stringify-type=\"paragraph-break\"></span><span>What came back was an explosive collage of what it means to be alive right here and now, and what we want to say before we go.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Featuring</strong><span>:</span></p>\n<p><span>Richard Feynman, physicist (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9780465023950\"><em><span>The Pleasure of Finding Things Out</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Caitlin Doughty, mortician (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9780393652703\"><em><span>Will My Cat Eat My Eyeballs</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Esperanza Spalding, musician (</span><a href=\"http://esperanzaspalding.limitedrun.com/\"><em><span>12 Little Spells</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Cord Jefferson, writer (</span><a href=\"https://www.hbo.com/watchmen\"><em><span>Watchmen</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Merrill Garbus, musician (</span><a href=\"https://tune-yards.com/#album\"><em><span>I Can Feel You Creep Into My Private Life</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Jenny Odell, writer (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9781612197494\"><em><span>How to do Nothing</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Maria Popova, writer (</span><a href=\"https://www.brainpickings.org/\"><em><span>Brainpickings</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Alison Gopnik, developmental psychologist (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9781250132253\"><em><span>The Gardener and the Carpenter</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Rebecca Sugar, animator (</span><a href=\"https://www.cartoonnetwork.com/video/steven-universe/index.html\"><em><span>Steven Universe</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Nicholson Baker, writer (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9780399160998\"><em><span>Substitute</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>James Gleick, writer (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9780307908797\"><em><span>Time Travel</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Lady Pink, artist (</span><a href=\"http://www.ladypinknyc.com/\"><span>too many amazing works to pick just one</span></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Jenny Hollwell, writer (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9780805091199\"><em><span>Everything Lovely, Effortless, Safe</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Jaron Lanier, futurist (</span><a href=\"https://www.indiebound.org/book/9781250239082\"><em><span>Ten Arguments for Deleting Your Social Media Accounts Right Now</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span>Missy Mazzoli, composer (</span><a href=\"http://www.missymazzoli.com/opera\"><em><span>Proving Up</span></em></a><span>)</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty and Rachael Cusick, <span>with help from Jeremy Bloom, Zakiya Gibbons, and the entire Radiolab staff.<span> </span></span></em><strong><br></strong></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong><em>Special Thanks to:</em></strong></p>\n<p><em>Ella Frances Sanders, and her book, <a href=\"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/567035/eating-the-sun-by-ella-frances-sanders/\">\"Eating the Sun\"</a>, for inspiring this whole episode.</em></p>\n<p><span>Caltech for letting us use original audio of The Feynman Lectures on Physics. The entirety of the lectures are available to read for free online at </span><a href=\"https://protect-us.mimecast.com/s/6f_iCmZnD2SoJp0CB8FL-?domain=feynmanlectures.caltech.edu\"><span>www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>All the musicians who helped make the Primordial Chord, including:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/siavash_kamkar/?hl=en\"><span>Siavash Kamkar</span></a><span>, from Iran </span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://soundcloud.com/kooshkar\"><span>Koosha Pashangpour</span></a><span>, from Iran</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.curtismacdonald.com/\"><span>Curtis MacDonald</span></a><span>, from Canada</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://twitter.com/meadebernard\"><span>Meade Bernard</span></a><span>, from US</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.barnabyrea.com/\"><span>Barnaby Rea</span></a><span>, from UK</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://twitter.com/liavkerbel\"><span>Liav Kerbel</span></a><span>, from Belgium</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://twitter.com/tallsamtrombone\"><span>Sam Crittenden</span></a><span>, from US</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.saskialankhoorn.com/\"><span>Saskia Lankhoorn</span></a><span>, from Netherlands</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://twitter.com/BryanEHarris\"><span>Bryan Harris</span></a><span>, from US</span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/ameliawatkins.com\">Amelia Watkins</a>, from Canada</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://twitter.com/Claire_NoelleJ\"><span>Claire James</span></a><span>, from US</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.larsenale.com/?p=83\"><span>Ilario <span>Morciano</span></span></a><span>, from Italy</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/public/Matthias-Kowalczyk\"><span>Matthias Kowalczyk</span></a><span>, from Germany</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://soundcloud.com/solmaz-badri\"><span>Solmaz Badri</span></a><span>, from Iran</span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<p><span>All the wonderful people we interviewed </span><span>for sentences </span><span>but weren’t able to fit in this episode, including: Daniel Abrahm, Julia Alvarez, Aimee Bender, Sandra Cisneros, Stanley Chen, Lewis Dartnell, Ann Druyan, Rose Eveleth, Ty Frank, Julia Galef, Ross Gay, Gary Green, Cesar Harada, Dolores Huerta, Robin Hunicke, Brittany Kamai, Priya Krishna, Ken Liu, Carmen Maria Machado, James Martin, Judith Matloff, Ryan McMahon, Hasan Minhaj, Lorrie Moore, Priya Natarajan, Larry Owens, Sunni Patterson, Amy Pearl, Alison Roman, Domee Shi, Will Shortz, Sam Stein, Sohaib Sultan, Kara Swisher, </span><span>Jill Tarter, </span><span>Olive Watkins, Reggie Watts, Deborah Waxman, Alex Wellerstein, Caveh Zahedi.</span></p>\n<p><span><br><br></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-04-18T02:57:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dispatch-4-six-feet", "title"=>"Dispatch 4: Six Feet", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20dispatch4sixfeet.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1011736", "description"=>"<p><span>Since the onset of the pandemic, we exist in a constant state of calculation, trying to define our own personal bubble. We’ve all been given a simple rule: maintain six feet of distance between yourself and others. But why six? Producer Sarah Qari uncovers the answer, and talks to some scientists who now say six might not be the right number after all. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Sarah Qari and Pat Walters.</em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1288\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1305\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1313\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"Everyone on the planet has been told to stay six feet away from each other. Why six? And is six even the right number?", "publishTime"=>1586581320000, "publish-at"=>"2020-04-11T01:02:00", "date-line-ts"=>1586581320, "estimated-duration"=>1146, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/04/Dispatch4sixfeet.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2020/04/Dispatch4sixfeet.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/04/Dispatch4sixfeet.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>189365, "credits-name"=>"Justin Buschardt"}, "objectID"=>"1011736", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dispatch 4: Six Feet", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Since the onset of the pandemic, we exist in a constant state of calculation, trying to define our own personal bubble. We’ve all been given a simple rule: maintain six feet of distance between yourself and others. But why six? Producer Sarah Qari uncovers the answer, and talks to some scientists who now say six might not be the right number after all. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Sarah Qari and Pat Walters.</em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1288\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1305\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1313\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-04-11T01:02:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"91520-space", "title"=>"Space", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20spacererun.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=91520", "description"=>"<p><span>One of the most consistent questions we get at<span> </span></span><span data-markjs=\"true\" data-ogac=\"\" data-ogab=\"\" data-ogsc=\"\" data-ogsb=\"\">the show </span><span>is from parents who want to know which episodes are kid-friendly and which aren’t. So today, we're releasing a separate <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab-kids\">feed</a>, Radiolab for Kids. To kick it off, we're rerunning an all-time favorite episode: Space.</span></p>\n<p><span><span>In the 60’s, space exploration was an American obsession. This hour, we chart the path from romance to increasing cynicism.</span></span></p>\n<p>We begin with Ann Druyan, widow of Carl Sagan, with a story about the Voyager expedition, true love, and a golden record that travels through space. And astrophysicist Neil de Grasse Tyson explains the Coepernican Principle, and just how insignificant we are.</p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"We ponder our insignificant place in the universe, and boldly go after stories of romance &amp; cynicism in Outer Space.", "publishTime"=>1586208060000, "publish-at"=>"2020-04-06T17:21:00", "date-line-ts"=>1586188800, "estimated-duration"=>3524, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Horsehead nebula", "name"=>"photologue/photos/space.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "name"=>"flickr/CC-BY-2.0"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/450/c/80/photologue/photos/space.jpg", "h"=>450, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Horsehead nebula", "credits-url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/photos/s58y/4145491816/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/space.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>65982, "credits-name"=>"s5By"}, "objectID"=>"91520", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Space", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>One of the most consistent questions we get at<span> </span></span><span data-markjs=\"true\" data-ogac=\"\" data-ogab=\"\" data-ogsc=\"\" data-ogsb=\"\">the show </span><span>is from parents who want to know which episodes are kid-friendly and which aren’t. So today, we're releasing a separate <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab-kids\">feed</a>, Radiolab for Kids. To kick it off, we're rerunning an all-time favorite episode: Space.</span></p>\n<p><span><span>In the 60’s, space exploration was an American obsession. This hour, we chart the path from romance to increasing cynicism.</span></span></p>\n<p>We begin with Ann Druyan, widow of Carl Sagan, with a story about the Voyager expedition, true love, and a golden record that travels through space. And astrophysicist Neil de Grasse Tyson explains the Coepernican Principle, and just how insignificant we are.</p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-04-06T17:21:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dispatch-3-shared-immunity", "title"=>"Dispatch 3: Shared Immunity ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20dispatch3sharedimmunity.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1008399", "description"=>"<p><span>More than a million people have caught Covid-19, and tens of thousands have died. But thousands more have survived and recovered. A week or so ago (aka, what feels like ten years in corona time) producer Molly Webster learned that many of those survivors possess a kind of superpower: antibodies trained to fight the virus. Not only that, they might be able to pass this power on to the people who are sick with corona, and still in the fight. Today we have the story of an experimental treatment that’s popping up all over the country: convalescent plasma transfusion, a century-old procedure that some say may become one of our best weapons against this devastating, new disease.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>If you have recovered from Covid-19</strong> <strong>and want to donate plasma</strong><span>, national and local donation registries are gearing up to collect blood. </span></p>\n<p><span>To sign up with the American Red Cross, a national organization that works in local communities, head </span><a href=\"http://redcrossblood.org\"><span>here</span></a><span>. </span></p>\n<p><span>To find </span><span>out more about the The National COVID-19 Convalescent Plasma Project, which we spoke about in our episode, including information on clinical trials or plasma donation projects in your community, go </span><a href=\"https://ccpp19.org/index.html\"><span>here</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p> <span>And if you are in the greater New York City area, and want to donate convalescent plasma, head over to the New York Blood Center </span><a href=\"https://nybloodcenter.org/donate-blood/covid-19-and-blood-donation-copy/\"><span>to sign up</span></a><span>. Or, register with specific NYC hospitals </span><a href=\"https://nybloodcenter.org/news/articles/new-york-blood-center-collect-first-blood-plasma-donations-recovered-covid-19-patients-treat-severe-cases/\"><span>here</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>If you are sick with Covid-19</strong><span>, and are interested in participating in a clinical trial, or are looking for a plasma donor match, check in with your local hospital, university, or blood center for more; you can also find more information on trials at </span><a href=\"https://www.trialsitenews.com/the-national-covid-19-convalescent-plasma-project/\"><span>The National COVID-19 Convalescent Plasma Project</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><span>And lastly, Tatiana Prowell’s tweet that tipped us off is</span><a href=\"https://twitter.com/tmprowell/status/1242995804786302984?s=21\"> <span>here</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Molly Webster and produced by Pat Walters. Special thanks to Drs. Evan Bloch and Tim Byun, as well as the Albert Einstein College of Medicine. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></p>", "tease"=>"Could a century-old treatment become our best weapon in the fight against Covid-19?", "publishTime"=>1585914180000, "publish-at"=>"2020-04-03T07:43:00", "date-line-ts"=>1585914180, "estimated-duration"=>2293, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/04/Plasma.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2020/04/Plasma.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/04/Plasma.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>189180, "credits-name"=>"Justin Buschardt"}, "objectID"=>"1008399", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dispatch 3: Shared Immunity ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>More than a million people have caught Covid-19, and tens of thousands have died. But thousands more have survived and recovered. A week or so ago (aka, what feels like ten years in corona time) producer Molly Webster learned that many of those survivors possess a kind of superpower: antibodies trained to fight the virus. Not only that, they might be able to pass this power on to the people who are sick with corona, and still in the fight. Today we have the story of an experimental treatment that’s popping up all over the country: convalescent plasma transfusion, a century-old procedure that some say may become one of our best weapons against this devastating, new disease.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>If you have recovered from Covid-19</strong> <strong>and want to donate plasma</strong><span>, national and local donation registries are gearing up to collect blood. </span></p>\n<p><span>To sign up with the American Red Cross, a national organization that works in local communities, head </span><a href=\"http://redcrossblood.org\"><span>here</span></a><span>. </span></p>\n<p><span>To find </span><span>out more about the The National COVID-19 Convalescent Plasma Project, which we spoke about in our episode, including information on clinical trials or plasma donation projects in your community, go </span><a href=\"https://ccpp19.org/index.html\"><span>here</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p> <span>And if you are in the greater New York City area, and want to donate convalescent plasma, head over to the New York Blood Center </span><a href=\"https://nybloodcenter.org/donate-blood/covid-19-and-blood-donation-copy/\"><span>to sign up</span></a><span>. Or, register with specific NYC hospitals </span><a href=\"https://nybloodcenter.org/news/articles/new-york-blood-center-collect-first-blood-plasma-donations-recovered-covid-19-patients-treat-severe-cases/\"><span>here</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>If you are sick with Covid-19</strong><span>, and are interested in participating in a clinical trial, or are looking for a plasma donor match, check in with your local hospital, university, or blood center for more; you can also find more information on trials at </span><a href=\"https://www.trialsitenews.com/the-national-covid-19-convalescent-plasma-project/\"><span>The National COVID-19 Convalescent Plasma Project</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><span>And lastly, Tatiana Prowell’s tweet that tipped us off is</span><a href=\"https://twitter.com/tmprowell/status/1242995804786302984?s=21\"> <span>here</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Molly Webster and produced by Pat Walters. Special thanks to Drs. Evan Bloch and Tim Byun, as well as the Albert Einstein College of Medicine. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em> </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-04-03T07:43:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dispatch-2-every-day-ignaz-semmelweis-day", "title"=>"Dispatch 2: Every Day is Ignaz Semmelweis Day", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20dispatch2ignazsemmelweisday.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1007766", "description"=>"<p>It began with a tweet: “EVERY DAY IS IGNAZ SEMMELWEIS DAY.” Carl Zimmer — tweet author, acclaimed science writer and friend of the show — tells the story of a mysterious, deadly illness that struck 19th century Vienna, and the ill-fated hero who uncovered its cure … and gave us our best weapon (so far) against the current global pandemic.</p>\n<p><em><br>This episode was reported and produced with help from Bethel Habte and Latif Nasser.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"The forgotten origins of a lifesaving ritual we&rsquo;re all acting out 20 times a day, every single day.", "publishTime"=>1585741380000, "publish-at"=>"2020-04-01T07:43:00", "date-line-ts"=>1585741380, "estimated-duration"=>2046, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/03/handwashing.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1446/c/80/2020/03/handwashing.png", "h"=>1446, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/03/handwashing.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>189132, "credits-name"=>"Justin Buschardt"}, "objectID"=>"1007766", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dispatch 2: Every Day is Ignaz Semmelweis Day", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>It began with a tweet: “EVERY DAY IS IGNAZ SEMMELWEIS DAY.” Carl Zimmer — tweet author, acclaimed science writer and friend of the show — tells the story of a mysterious, deadly illness that struck 19th century Vienna, and the ill-fated hero who uncovered its cure … and gave us our best weapon (so far) against the current global pandemic.</p>\n<p><em><br>This episode was reported and produced with help from Bethel Habte and Latif Nasser.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-04-01T07:43:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dispatch-numbers", "title"=>"Dispatch 1: Numbers", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20dispatch1numbers.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1006526", "description"=>"<p><span>In a recent Radiolab group huddle, with coronavirus unraveling around us, the team found themselves grappling with all the numbers connected to COVID-19. Our new found 6 foot bubbles of personal space. Three percent mortality rate (or 1, or 2, or 4). 7,000 cases (now, much much more). So in the wake of that meeting, we reflect on the onslaught of numbers - what they reveal, and what they hide. </span></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"On this episode, we offer a numerical duet - one, an essay about the trick at the center of exponential curves, the other, about all the things we aren&rsquo;t counting.", "publishTime"=>1585289220000, "publish-at"=>"2020-03-27T02:07:00", "date-line-ts"=>1585289160, "estimated-duration"=>1950, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/03/NUMBERS2.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2020/03/NUMBERS2.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/03/NUMBERS2.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>189055, "credits-name"=>"Justin Buschardt"}, "objectID"=>"1006526", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dispatch 1: Numbers", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In a recent Radiolab group huddle, with coronavirus unraveling around us, the team found themselves grappling with all the numbers connected to COVID-19. Our new found 6 foot bubbles of personal space. Three percent mortality rate (or 1, or 2, or 4). 7,000 cases (now, much much more). So in the wake of that meeting, we reflect on the onslaught of numbers - what they reveal, and what they hide. </span></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-03-27T02:07:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"other-latif-episode-6", "title"=>"The Other Latif: Episode 6", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_membership/radiolab_membership20theotherlatif6.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1001739", "description"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><span>Radiolab’s Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads Radiolab’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, Radiolab’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Episode 6: Washington, D.C.</strong></p>\n<p><span>Despite being cleared for transfer back to his family in Morocco in 2016, Abdul Latif Nasser remains stuck at Guantanamo Bay. Why? Latif talks to some of the civil servants actually responsible for Abdul Latif’s transfer and they tell him a dramatic story of what went on behind the scenes at some of the highest levels of government.  It’s a <span>surprisingly </span>riveting story of paperwork, where what’s at stake is not only the fate of one man, but also the soul of America.  </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Sarah Qari, Annie McEwen, Suzie Lechtenberg, and Latif Nasser, and reported by Sarah Qari and Latif Nasser. Fact checking by Diane Kelly and Margot Williams. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. Original music by Jad Abumrad, Dylan Keefe, Alex Overington, and Amino Belyamani. </em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"Despite being cleared for transfer home in 2016, Abdul Latif Nasser is still stuck at Guantanamo. Why? Latif digs for answers, and spoiler: it goes all the way to the top.", "publishTime"=>1584446400000, "publish-at"=>"2020-03-17T08:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1584446400, "estimated-duration"=>3046, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/03/The_Other_Latif_Ep_6.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2020/03/The_Other_Latif_Ep_6.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/03/The_Other_Latif_Ep_6.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>188866, "credits-name"=>"Will Paybarah"}, "objectID"=>"1001739", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Other Latif: Episode 6", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><span>Radiolab’s Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads Radiolab’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, Radiolab’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Episode 6: Washington, D.C.</strong></p>\n<p><span>Despite being cleared for transfer back to his family in Morocco in 2016, Abdul Latif Nasser remains stuck at Guantanamo Bay. Why? Latif talks to some of the civil servants actually responsible for Abdul Latif’s transfer and they tell him a dramatic story of what went on behind the scenes at some of the highest levels of government.  It’s a <span>surprisingly </span>riveting story of paperwork, where what’s at stake is not only the fate of one man, but also the soul of America.  </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Sarah Qari, Annie McEwen, Suzie Lechtenberg, and Latif Nasser, and reported by Sarah Qari and Latif Nasser. Fact checking by Diane Kelly and Margot Williams. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. Original music by Jad Abumrad, Dylan Keefe, Alex Overington, and Amino Belyamani. </em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-03-17T08:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"other-latif-episode-5", "title"=>"The Other Latif: Episode 5", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20theotherlatif5.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=998199", "description"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><span>Radiolab’s Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads Radiolab’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, Radiolab’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Episode 5: Cuba-ish</strong></p>\n<p><span>Latif heads to Guantanamo Bay to try to speak to his namesake.  Before he gets there, he attempts to answer a seemingly simple question: why Cuba? Why in the world did the United States pick this sleepy military base in the Caribbean to house “the worst of the worst”?  He tours the “legal equivalent of outer space,” and against all odds, manages to see his doppelgänger… maybe.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Bethel Habte and Simon Adler, with Sarah Qari, Suzie Lechtenberg, and Latif Nasser. Help from W. Harry Fortuna and Neel Dhanesha. Fact checking by Diane Kelly and Margot Williams. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. Original music by Jad Abumrad, Simon Adler, Alex Overington, and Amino Belyamani.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"What happened to Abdul Latif at Guantanamo Bay?&nbsp; Latif visits the &ldquo;legal equivalent of outer space,&rdquo; and manages to see his namesake &hellip; maybe.", "publishTime"=>1583499600000, "publish-at"=>"2020-03-06T08:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1583499600, "estimated-duration"=>3441, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/03/The_Other_Latif_Ep_5_1200x1600px.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2020/03/The_Other_Latif_Ep_5_1200x1600px.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>" ", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/03/The_Other_Latif_Ep_5_1200x1600px.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>188628, "credits-name"=>"Will Paybarah"}, "objectID"=>"998199", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Other Latif: Episode 5", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><span>Radiolab’s Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads Radiolab’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, Radiolab’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Episode 5: Cuba-ish</strong></p>\n<p><span>Latif heads to Guantanamo Bay to try to speak to his namesake.  Before he gets there, he attempts to answer a seemingly simple question: why Cuba? Why in the world did the United States pick this sleepy military base in the Caribbean to house “the worst of the worst”?  He tours the “legal equivalent of outer space,” and against all odds, manages to see his doppelgänger… maybe.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Bethel Habte and Simon Adler, with Sarah Qari, Suzie Lechtenberg, and Latif Nasser. Help from W. Harry Fortuna and Neel Dhanesha. Fact checking by Diane Kelly and Margot Williams. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. Original music by Jad Abumrad, Simon Adler, Alex Overington, and Amino Belyamani.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-03-06T08:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"other-latif-bonus-episode", "title"=>"The Other Latif: Bonus Episode! ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20theotherlatifbonus.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=998294", "description"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><span>Radiolab’s Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads Radiolab’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, Radiolab’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p><br><br></p>\n<p><strong>BONUS EPISODE</strong></p>\n<p><span>Since we released the first episode of </span><em><span>The Other Latif</span></em><span>, we’ve been contacted by many new sources. Which is great! But it also means we need a little extra time to cobble together Episodes 5 and 6. So while we wait, Jad and Latif chat about Abdul Latif’s response to the series, a character who fell out of episode 4, and a tiny moment in Latif’s youth that helped put him on the path he’s on now. </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Suzie Lechtenberg and Latif Nasser. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. With help from Sarah Qari. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"We&rsquo;re not done with Episode 5 yet! While we wait, Jad and Latif chat about Abdul Latif&rsquo;s response to the series, deleted scenes, and the nudges that make us who we are.", "publishTime"=>1583237700000, "publish-at"=>"2020-03-03T07:15:00", "date-line-ts"=>1583237700, "estimated-duration"=>1204, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/03/The_Other_Latif_bonus_ep4.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1737/1303/l/80/2020/03/The_Other_Latif_bonus_ep4.jpg", "h"=>1303, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/03/The_Other_Latif_bonus_ep4.jpg", "w"=>1737, "id"=>188640, "credits-name"=>"Matthew Septimus"}, "objectID"=>"998294", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Other Latif: Bonus Episode! ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><span>Radiolab’s Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads Radiolab’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, Radiolab’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p><br><br></p>\n<p><strong>BONUS EPISODE</strong></p>\n<p><span>Since we released the first episode of </span><em><span>The Other Latif</span></em><span>, we’ve been contacted by many new sources. Which is great! But it also means we need a little extra time to cobble together Episodes 5 and 6. So while we wait, Jad and Latif chat about Abdul Latif’s response to the series, a character who fell out of episode 4, and a tiny moment in Latif’s youth that helped put him on the path he’s on now. </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Suzie Lechtenberg and Latif Nasser. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. With help from Sarah Qari. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-03-03T07:15:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"other-latif-episode-4", "title"=>"The Other Latif: Episode 4", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20theotherlatif4.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=996635", "description"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><span>Radiolab’s Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads Radiolab’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, Radiolab’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<p><strong>Episode 4: Afghanistan </strong></p>\n<p><span>Latif investigates the mystery around Abdul Latif’s classified time in Afghanistan. He traces the government’s story through scrappy training camps, bombed out Buddhas, and McDonald’s apple pies to the very center of the Battle of Tora Bora.  Could Abdul Latif have helped the most sought-after and hated terrorist in modern history, Osama bin Laden, escape? The episode ends with a bombshell jailhouse interview with Abdul Latif, the most reliable evidence yet of what was going on in this man’s mind in the months after 9/11.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Annie McEwen, Sarah Qari, Suzie Lechtenberg, and Latif Nasser. Fact checking by Diane Kelly and Margot Williams. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. With help from Neel Dhanesha, Kelly Prime, and Audrey Quinn. Original music by Jad Abumrad, Alex Overington, Annie McEwen, and Amino Belyamani. </em></p>\n<p><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"Latif investigates Abdul Latif&rsquo;s classified time in Afghanistan, and amidst the explosions and rubble, finds a jailhouse interview that changes how he sees his namesake.", "publishTime"=>1582635600000, "publish-at"=>"2020-02-25T08:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1582635600, "estimated-duration"=>3719, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/02/The_Other_Latif_Ep_4_1200x1600.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2020/02/The_Other_Latif_Ep_4_1200x1600.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/02/The_Other_Latif_Ep_4_1200x1600.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>188505, "credits-name"=>"Will Paybarah"}, "objectID"=>"996635", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Other Latif: Episode 4", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><span>Radiolab’s Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads Radiolab’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, Radiolab’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<p><strong>Episode 4: Afghanistan </strong></p>\n<p><span>Latif investigates the mystery around Abdul Latif’s classified time in Afghanistan. He traces the government’s story through scrappy training camps, bombed out Buddhas, and McDonald’s apple pies to the very center of the Battle of Tora Bora.  Could Abdul Latif have helped the most sought-after and hated terrorist in modern history, Osama bin Laden, escape? The episode ends with a bombshell jailhouse interview with Abdul Latif, the most reliable evidence yet of what was going on in this man’s mind in the months after 9/11.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Annie McEwen, Sarah Qari, Suzie Lechtenberg, and Latif Nasser. Fact checking by Diane Kelly and Margot Williams. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. With help from Neel Dhanesha, Kelly Prime, and Audrey Quinn. Original music by Jad Abumrad, Alex Overington, Annie McEwen, and Amino Belyamani. </em></p>\n<p><em><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-02-25T08:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"other-latif-episode-3", "title"=>"The Other Latif: Episode 3", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20theotherlatif3.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=995036", "description"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab’s</span></em><span> Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Episode 3: Sudan</strong></p>\n<p><span>Latif turns his focus to Sudan, where his namesake spent time working on a sunflower farm. What could be suspicious about that?  Latif scrutinizes the evidence to try to discover whether - as Abdul Latif’s lawyer insists - it was just an innocent clerical job, or - as the government alleges - it was where he decided to become an extremist fighter.  </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Suzie Lechtenberg, Sarah Qari, and Latif Nasser. With help from Niza Nondo and Maaki Monem. Fact checking by Diane Kelly and Margot Williams. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. Original music by Jad Abumrad, Alex Overington, Jeremy Bloom, and Amino Belyamani. </em></p>\n<p><em>If you caught this episode on the radio, and want to learn or hear more from the excellent podcast Love Me, check them out here: <a href=\"https://www.cbc.ca/radio/loveme\">https://www.cbc.ca/radio/loveme</a> and to learn more about Mansoor Adafyi, check out his new book <a href=\"https://www.hachettebooks.com/titles/mansoor-adayfi/dont-forget-us-here/9780306923876/\">Don't Forget Us</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></p>", "tease"=>"Abdul Latif Nasser gets a job working on a sunflower farm in Sudan.&nbsp; What on earth could be suspicious about that?", "publishTime"=>1582030800000, "publish-at"=>"2020-02-18T08:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1582030800, "estimated-duration"=>2214, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/02/The_Other_Latif_Ep_3_wfADMyh.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2020/02/The_Other_Latif_Ep_3_wfADMyh.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/02/The_Other_Latif_Ep_3_wfADMyh.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>188395, "credits-name"=>"Will Paybarah"}, "objectID"=>"995036", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Other Latif: Episode 3", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab’s</span></em><span> Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Episode 3: Sudan</strong></p>\n<p><span>Latif turns his focus to Sudan, where his namesake spent time working on a sunflower farm. What could be suspicious about that?  Latif scrutinizes the evidence to try to discover whether - as Abdul Latif’s lawyer insists - it was just an innocent clerical job, or - as the government alleges - it was where he decided to become an extremist fighter.  </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Suzie Lechtenberg, Sarah Qari, and Latif Nasser. With help from Niza Nondo and Maaki Monem. Fact checking by Diane Kelly and Margot Williams. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. Original music by Jad Abumrad, Alex Overington, Jeremy Bloom, and Amino Belyamani. </em></p>\n<p><em>If you caught this episode on the radio, and want to learn or hear more from the excellent podcast Love Me, check them out here: <a href=\"https://www.cbc.ca/radio/loveme\">https://www.cbc.ca/radio/loveme</a> and to learn more about Mansoor Adafyi, check out his new book <a href=\"https://www.hachettebooks.com/titles/mansoor-adayfi/dont-forget-us-here/9780306923876/\">Don't Forget Us</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-02-18T08:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"other-latif-episode-2", "title"=>"The Other Latif: Episode 2", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20theotherlatif2.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=993397", "description"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab’s</span></em><span> Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Episode 2: Morocco</strong></p>\n<p><span>Latif travels to Abdul Latif’s hometown of Casablanca, Morocco, to try and find out: was he radicalized? And if so, how? Latif begins by visiting the man’s family, but the family’s reaction to him gets complicated as Latif digs for the truth. He finds out surprising information on a political group Abdul Latif joined in his youth, his alleged onramp to extremism. Tensions escalate when Latif realizes he’s being tailed. </span></p>\n<p><span>Read more about Abdul Latif Nasser at the </span><em><span>New York Times’ </span></em><a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/projects/guantanamo/detainees/244-abdul-latif-nasir\"><span>Guantanamo Docket.</span></a><span> </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Sarah Qari, Suzie Lechtenberg, and Latif Nasser. With help from Tarik El Barakah and Amira Karaoud. Fact checking by Diane Kelly and Margot Williams. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. Original music by Jad Abumrad, Alex Overington, and Amino Belyamani. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Latif travels to Morocco to trace his namesake's possible onramp into extremism and is surprised by what he finds.", "publishTime"=>1581426000000, "publish-at"=>"2020-02-11T08:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1581426000, "estimated-duration"=>2760, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/02/The_Other_Latif_Ep_2_1600x1200px_eitAEvz.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2020/02/The_Other_Latif_Ep_2_1600x1200px_eitAEvz.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/02/The_Other_Latif_Ep_2_1600x1200px_eitAEvz.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>188298, "credits-name"=>"Will Paybarah"}, "objectID"=>"993397", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Other Latif: Episode 2", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab’s</span></em><span> Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Episode 2: Morocco</strong></p>\n<p><span>Latif travels to Abdul Latif’s hometown of Casablanca, Morocco, to try and find out: was he radicalized? And if so, how? Latif begins by visiting the man’s family, but the family’s reaction to him gets complicated as Latif digs for the truth. He finds out surprising information on a political group Abdul Latif joined in his youth, his alleged onramp to extremism. Tensions escalate when Latif realizes he’s being tailed. </span></p>\n<p><span>Read more about Abdul Latif Nasser at the </span><em><span>New York Times’ </span></em><a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/projects/guantanamo/detainees/244-abdul-latif-nasir\"><span>Guantanamo Docket.</span></a><span> </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Sarah Qari, Suzie Lechtenberg, and Latif Nasser. With help from Tarik El Barakah and Amira Karaoud. Fact checking by Diane Kelly and Margot Williams. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. Original music by Jad Abumrad, Alex Overington, and Amino Belyamani. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-02-11T08:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"other-latif-episode-1", "title"=>"The Other Latif: Episode 1", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20theotherlatif1.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=991689", "description"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab’s</span></em><span> Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Episode 1: My Namesake</strong></p>\n<p><span>We hear the evidence against Abdul Latif Nasser -- at least the evidence that has been leaked or declassified -- and we meet Shelby Sullivan-Bennis, his attorney, who contests more or less every government claim against him. Sullivan-Bennis walks us through the excruciating process that came close to releasing Abdul Latif Nasser in the waning days of the Obama administration, but fell apart at the last minute. He is now technically a free man -- he was cleared for transfer home in 2016 -- yet he remains stuck at Guantanamo Bay, thanks in part to a Presidential Tweet.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\" style=\"max-width: 535px;\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\"></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\" style=\"max-width: 800px;\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/i/428/428/l/80/2020/02/ALN.png\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\"><span>A photo of Abdul Latif Nasser</span> </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><span>Read more about Abdul Latif Nasser at the </span><em><span>New York Times’ </span></em><a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/projects/guantanamo/detainees/244-abdul-latif-nasir\"><span>Guantanamo Docket.</span></a><span> </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Annie McEwen, Latif Nasser, Sarah Qari, and Suzie Lechtenberg. Fact checking by Diane Kelly and Margot Williams. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. Original music by Jad Abumrad, Alex Overington, Annie McEwen, and Amino<span> Belyamani. </span></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><span><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></span></span></em></p>\n<p><span>  </span></p>", "tease"=>"Radiolab&rsquo;s Latif Nasser is stunned to find he shares his name with detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. This man was cleared to leave Gitmo in 2016, so why is he still stuck there?", "publishTime"=>1580821200000, "publish-at"=>"2020-02-04T08:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1580821200, "estimated-duration"=>2506, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/02/OtherLatif_Art.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/974/c/80/2020/02/OtherLatif_Art.png", "h"=>974, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/02/OtherLatif_Art.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>188155, "credits-name"=>"Will Paybarah"}, "objectID"=>"991689", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Other Latif: Episode 1", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><strong>The Other Latif</strong></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab’s</span></em><span> Latif Nasser always believed his name was unique, singular, completely his own. Until one day when he makes a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with another man: Abdul Latif Nasser, detainee 244 at Guantanamo Bay. The U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of The Other Latif as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash leads </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what this man actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span>’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how his namesake, a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>Episode 1: My Namesake</strong></p>\n<p><span>We hear the evidence against Abdul Latif Nasser -- at least the evidence that has been leaked or declassified -- and we meet Shelby Sullivan-Bennis, his attorney, who contests more or less every government claim against him. Sullivan-Bennis walks us through the excruciating process that came close to releasing Abdul Latif Nasser in the waning days of the Obama administration, but fell apart at the last minute. He is now technically a free man -- he was cleared for transfer home in 2016 -- yet he remains stuck at Guantanamo Bay, thanks in part to a Presidential Tweet.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\" style=\"max-width: 535px;\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\"></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\" style=\"max-width: 800px;\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/i/428/428/l/80/2020/02/ALN.png\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\"><span>A photo of Abdul Latif Nasser</span> </div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><span>Read more about Abdul Latif Nasser at the </span><em><span>New York Times’ </span></em><a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/projects/guantanamo/detainees/244-abdul-latif-nasir\"><span>Guantanamo Docket.</span></a><span> </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Annie McEwen, Latif Nasser, Sarah Qari, and Suzie Lechtenberg. Fact checking by Diane Kelly and Margot Williams. Editing by Jad Abumrad and Soren Wheeler. Original music by Jad Abumrad, Alex Overington, Annie McEwen, and Amino<span> Belyamani. </span></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><span><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></span></span></em></p>\n<p><span>  </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-02-04T08:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"bobbys", "title"=>"The Bobbys", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20thebobbys.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=990666", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>On the occasion of his retirement as cohost of Radiolab, Robert sat down with Jad to reflect on his long and storied career in radio and television, and their work together over the past decade and a half. And we pay tribute to Robert, inspired by a peculiar tradition of his.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty. Sound design &amp; mix by Jeremy Bloom.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "tease"=>"An award show you&rsquo;ve never heard of before.", "publishTime"=>1580446320000, "publish-at"=>"2020-01-30T23:52:00", "date-line-ts"=>1580446320, "estimated-duration"=>2889, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/01/RobertKrulwich.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/533/c/80/2020/01/RobertKrulwich.jpg", "h"=>533, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/01/RobertKrulwich.jpg", "w"=>800, "id"=>188109, "credits-name"=>"WNYC"}, "objectID"=>"990666", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Bobbys", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>On the occasion of his retirement as cohost of Radiolab, Robert sat down with Jad to reflect on his long and storied career in radio and television, and their work together over the past decade and a half. And we pay tribute to Robert, inspired by a peculiar tradition of his.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty. Sound design &amp; mix by Jeremy Bloom.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-01-30T23:52:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"body-count", "title"=>"Body Count", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20bodycount.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=989192", "description"=>"<p><span>Right now, at this very moment, all across the planet, there are 7.6 billion human beings eating, breathing, sleeping, brushing their teeth, walking their dogs, drinking coffee, walking down the street or running onto the subway or hopping in their car, maybe reading a summary of a podcast they’re about to hit play on … and the number is only going up. Everyday 386,000 babies are born (16,000 an hour). We’re adding a billion new people every 12 years.</span></p>\n<p><span>So here’s a question you’ve probably never thought about: Are there more people alive right now than have ever lived on the planet in history? Do the living outnumber the dead? Robert got obsessed with this odd question, and in this episode we bring you the answer. Or, well,<span> </span><em>answers</em>.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Robert Krulwich and produced by Annie McEwen and Pat Walters, with help from Neel Dhanesha. Fact-checking by Michelle Harris. Music and mixing by Jeremy Bloom. Special thanks to Jeffrey Dobereiner.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></p>", "tease"=>"When you run the math, who comes out on top, the living or the dead?", "publishTime"=>1579869780000, "publish-at"=>"2020-01-24T07:43:00", "date-line-ts"=>1579869780, "estimated-duration"=>2753, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/01/bodycount.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2020/01/bodycount.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/01/bodycount.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>187995, "credits-name"=>"Justin Buschardt"}, "objectID"=>"989192", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Body Count", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Right now, at this very moment, all across the planet, there are 7.6 billion human beings eating, breathing, sleeping, brushing their teeth, walking their dogs, drinking coffee, walking down the street or running onto the subway or hopping in their car, maybe reading a summary of a podcast they’re about to hit play on … and the number is only going up. Everyday 386,000 babies are born (16,000 an hour). We’re adding a billion new people every 12 years.</span></p>\n<p><span>So here’s a question you’ve probably never thought about: Are there more people alive right now than have ever lived on the planet in history? Do the living outnumber the dead? Robert got obsessed with this odd question, and in this episode we bring you the answer. Or, well,<span> </span><em>answers</em>.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Robert Krulwich and produced by Annie McEwen and Pat Walters, with help from Neel Dhanesha. Fact-checking by Michelle Harris. Music and mixing by Jeremy Bloom. Special thanks to Jeffrey Dobereiner.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-01-24T07:43:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"60-words", "title"=>"60 Words", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast20_60words.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=364447", "description"=>"<p>This hour we pull apart one sentence, written in the hours after September 11th, 2001, that has led to the longest war in U.S. history. We examine how just 60 words of legal language have blurred the line between war and peace.</p><p>Last weekend President Trump authorized a strike that killed Iranian General Qasem Soleimani in Iraq. The news had us thinking about an episode we produced in 2014. We pulled apart one sentence, written in the hours after September 11th, 2001, that has led to the longest war in U.S. history. We examine how just 60 words of legal language have blurred the line between war and peace.</p>\n<p>In the hours after the attacks of Sept. 11, 2001, a lawyer sat down in front of a computer and started writing a legal justification for taking action against those responsible. The language that he drafted and that President George W. Bush signed into law - called the Authorization for Use of Military Force (AUMF) -  has at its heart one single sentence, 60 words long. Over the last decade, those 60 words have become the legal foundation for the \"war on terror.\"</p>\n<p>In this collaboration with BuzzFeed, reporter Gregory Johnsen tells us the story of how this has come to be one of the most important, confusing, troubling sentences of the last two decades. We go into the meetings that took place in the chaotic days just after 9/11, speak with Congresswoman Barbara Lee and former Congressman Ron Dellums about the vote on the AUMF. We hear from former White House and State Department lawyers John Bellinger &amp; Harold Koh. We learn how this legal language unleashed Guantanamo, Navy Seal raids and drone strikes. And we speak with journalist Daniel Klaidman, legal expert Benjamin Wittes and Virginia Senator Tim Kaine about how these words came to be interpreted, and what they mean for the future of war and peace.</p>\n<p>Finally, we check back in with Wittes, to see how the AUMF has trickled into the 2020s.</p>\n<p>Produced by Matt Kielty and Kelsey Padgett with original music by Dylan Keefe. </p>\n<p>Watch Congresswomen Barbara Lee's speech <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvnLtMKzX6Y\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\"><strong>here</strong></a>. </p>", "tease"=>"How one sentence -- just 60 words written in the hours after the September 11 attacks -- became the legal foundation for the \"war on terror.\"", "publishTime"=>1578456660000, "publish-at"=>"2020-01-07T23:11:00", "date-line-ts"=>1578416400, "estimated-duration"=>3899, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/siteimageFIN.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/900/h/80/1/siteimageFIN.jpeg", "h"=>900, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/siteimageFIN.jpeg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>115987, "credits-name"=>"Original artwork & Radiolab logo by Lucy Engelman"}, "objectID"=>"364447", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"60 Words", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This hour we pull apart one sentence, written in the hours after September 11th, 2001, that has led to the longest war in U.S. history. We examine how just 60 words of legal language have blurred the line between war and peace.</p><p>Last weekend President Trump authorized a strike that killed Iranian General Qasem Soleimani in Iraq. The news had us thinking about an episode we produced in 2014. We pulled apart one sentence, written in the hours after September 11th, 2001, that has led to the longest war in U.S. history. We examine how just 60 words of legal language have blurred the line between war and peace.</p>\n<p>In the hours after the attacks of Sept. 11, 2001, a lawyer sat down in front of a computer and started writing a legal justification for taking action against those responsible. The language that he drafted and that President George W. Bush signed into law - called the Authorization for Use of Military Force (AUMF) -  has at its heart one single sentence, 60 words long. Over the last decade, those 60 words have become the legal foundation for the \"war on terror.\"</p>\n<p>In this collaboration with BuzzFeed, reporter Gregory Johnsen tells us the story of how this has come to be one of the most important, confusing, troubling sentences of the last two decades. We go into the meetings that took place in the chaotic days just after 9/11, speak with Congresswoman Barbara Lee and former Congressman Ron Dellums about the vote on the AUMF. We hear from former White House and State Department lawyers John Bellinger &amp; Harold Koh. We learn how this legal language unleashed Guantanamo, Navy Seal raids and drone strikes. And we speak with journalist Daniel Klaidman, legal expert Benjamin Wittes and Virginia Senator Tim Kaine about how these words came to be interpreted, and what they mean for the future of war and peace.</p>\n<p>Finally, we check back in with Wittes, to see how the AUMF has trickled into the 2020s.</p>\n<p>Produced by Matt Kielty and Kelsey Padgett with original music by Dylan Keefe. </p>\n<p>Watch Congresswomen Barbara Lee's speech <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mvnLtMKzX6Y\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-auth=\"NotApplicable\"><strong>here</strong></a>. </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2020-01-07T23:11:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2021=>[{"slug"=>"vanishing-words-2112", "title"=>"Vanishing Words", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast121721_vanishingwords.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1161806", "description"=>"<p><span>When Alana Casanova-Burgess set out to make a podcast series about Puerto Rico, she struggled with what to call it. Until one word came to mind, a word that captures a certain essence of life in Puerto Rico, but eludes easy translation into English. We talk to Alana about her series, and that particular word, then turn to an old story about treating words as signals of something happening just beneath the surface. </span></p>\n<p><span>Agatha Christie's clever detective novels may reveal more about the inner workings of the human mind than she intended. According to </span><a href=\"http://www.chass.utoronto.ca/~ian/\"><span>Dr. Ian Lancashire</span></a><span> at the University of Toronto, the Queen of Crime left behind hidden clues to the real-life mysteries of human aging in her writing. Meanwhile, </span><a href=\"http://www.cmrr.umn.edu/facultystaff/kelvin.shtml\"><span>Dr. Kelvin Lim</span></a><span> and </span><a href=\"http://www.pharmacy.umn.edu/faculty/pakhomov_sergey/home.html\"><span>Dr. Serguei Pakhomov</span></a><span> from the University of Minnesota add to the intrigue with the story of an unexpected find in a convent archive that could someday help pinpoint very early warning signs for Alzheimer's disease and dementia. Sister Alberta Sheridan, a 94-year-old</span><a href=\"http://www.healthstudies.umn.edu/nunstudy/\"><span> Nun Study</span></a><span> participant, reads an essay she wrote more than 70 years ago.</span></p>\n<p><span><strong>La Brega update</strong> <em><span>was produced by Maria Paz Gutierrez</span></em> </span></p>", "tease"=>"Alana Casanova-Burgess had a puzzle to solve, what to name her podcast about Puerto Rico.&nbsp;Researchers&nbsp;use&nbsp;words put to paper by&nbsp;Agatha Christie and 678 nuns to solve mysteries of aging.", "publishTime"=>1639753200000, "publish-at"=>"2021-12-17T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1639753200, "estimated-duration"=>1442, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/12/RL_VanishingWords_EpisodeImage_4x3.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2021/12/RL_VanishingWords_EpisodeImage_4x3.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/12/RL_VanishingWords_EpisodeImage_4x3.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>198361, "credits-name"=>"Dan Gold on Unsplash"}, "objectID"=>"1161806", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Vanishing Words", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>When Alana Casanova-Burgess set out to make a podcast series about Puerto Rico, she struggled with what to call it. Until one word came to mind, a word that captures a certain essence of life in Puerto Rico, but eludes easy translation into English. We talk to Alana about her series, and that particular word, then turn to an old story about treating words as signals of something happening just beneath the surface. </span></p>\n<p><span>Agatha Christie's clever detective novels may reveal more about the inner workings of the human mind than she intended. According to </span><a href=\"http://www.chass.utoronto.ca/~ian/\"><span>Dr. Ian Lancashire</span></a><span> at the University of Toronto, the Queen of Crime left behind hidden clues to the real-life mysteries of human aging in her writing. Meanwhile, </span><a href=\"http://www.cmrr.umn.edu/facultystaff/kelvin.shtml\"><span>Dr. Kelvin Lim</span></a><span> and </span><a href=\"http://www.pharmacy.umn.edu/faculty/pakhomov_sergey/home.html\"><span>Dr. Serguei Pakhomov</span></a><span> from the University of Minnesota add to the intrigue with the story of an unexpected find in a convent archive that could someday help pinpoint very early warning signs for Alzheimer's disease and dementia. Sister Alberta Sheridan, a 94-year-old</span><a href=\"http://www.healthstudies.umn.edu/nunstudy/\"><span> Nun Study</span></a><span> participant, reads an essay she wrote more than 70 years ago.</span></p>\n<p><span><strong>La Brega update</strong> <em><span>was produced by Maria Paz Gutierrez</span></em> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-12-17T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"return-alpha-gal", "title"=>"Return of Alpha Gal", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast121021_returnofalphagal.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1159278", "description"=>"<p><span>Tuck your napkin under your chin.  We’re about to serve up a tale of love, loss, and lamb chops - with a side of genetic modification.</span></p>\n<p><span>Several years ago we told a story about Amy Pearl. For as long as she could remember, Amy loved meat in all its glorious cuts and marbled flavors. And then one day, for seemingly no reason, her body wouldn’t tolerate it.  No steaks. No brisket. No weenies. It made no sense: why couldn’t she eat something that she had routinely enjoyed for decades? </span></p>\n<p><span>It turned out Amy was not alone. And the answer to her mysterious allergy involved maps, a dancing lone star tick, and a very particular sugar called Alpha Gal. </span></p>\n<p><span>In this update, we discover that our troubles with Alpha Gal go way beyond food. We go to NYU Langone Health hospital to see the second ever transplant of a kidney from a pig into a human, talk to some people at Revivicor, the company that bred the pig in question, and go back to Amy to find out what she thinks about this brave new world.</span></p>\n<p><em><strong>The original episode</strong> was reported by Latif Nasser, and produced by Annie McEwen and Matt Kielty. Sound design and scoring from Dylan Keefe, Annie McEwen, and Matt Kielty. Mix by Dylan Keefe with Arianne Wack.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>The update</strong> was reported and produced by Sarah Qari. It was sound designed, scored, and mixed by Jeremy Bloom. </em></p>\n<p><span></span><strong><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </em></strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><strong><em>.  </em></strong><em><span> </span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Tuck your napkin under your chin.&nbsp; We&rsquo;re about to serve up a tale of love, loss, and lamb chops - with a side of genetic modification.", "publishTime"=>1639148400000, "publish-at"=>"2021-12-10T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1639148400, "estimated-duration"=>3438, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/12/RL_AlphaGal_EpisodeImage_4x3_he443cX.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2021/12/RL_AlphaGal_EpisodeImage_4x3_he443cX.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/12/RL_AlphaGal_EpisodeImage_4x3_he443cX.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>198271, "credits-name"=>"Centers for Disease Control"}, "objectID"=>"1159278", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Return of Alpha Gal", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Tuck your napkin under your chin.  We’re about to serve up a tale of love, loss, and lamb chops - with a side of genetic modification.</span></p>\n<p><span>Several years ago we told a story about Amy Pearl. For as long as she could remember, Amy loved meat in all its glorious cuts and marbled flavors. And then one day, for seemingly no reason, her body wouldn’t tolerate it.  No steaks. No brisket. No weenies. It made no sense: why couldn’t she eat something that she had routinely enjoyed for decades? </span></p>\n<p><span>It turned out Amy was not alone. And the answer to her mysterious allergy involved maps, a dancing lone star tick, and a very particular sugar called Alpha Gal. </span></p>\n<p><span>In this update, we discover that our troubles with Alpha Gal go way beyond food. We go to NYU Langone Health hospital to see the second ever transplant of a kidney from a pig into a human, talk to some people at Revivicor, the company that bred the pig in question, and go back to Amy to find out what she thinks about this brave new world.</span></p>\n<p><em><strong>The original episode</strong> was reported by Latif Nasser, and produced by Annie McEwen and Matt Kielty. Sound design and scoring from Dylan Keefe, Annie McEwen, and Matt Kielty. Mix by Dylan Keefe with Arianne Wack.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>The update</strong> was reported and produced by Sarah Qari. It was sound designed, scored, and mixed by Jeremy Bloom. </em></p>\n<p><span></span><strong><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </em></strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><strong><em>.  </em></strong><em><span> </span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-12-10T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"animal-minds-211125", "title"=>"Animal Minds ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast112621_animalminds.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1155612", "description"=>"<p>In this hour of Radiolab, stories of cross-species communication.</p>\n<p>When we gaze into the eyes of a wild animal, or even a beloved pet, can we ever really know what they might be thinking? Is it naive to assume they're experiencing something close to human emotions? Or is it ridiculous to assume that they AREN'T feeling something like that? We get the story of a rescued whale that may have found a way to say thanks, ask whether dogs feel guilt, and wonder if a successful predator may have fallen in love with a photographer.</p>", "tease"=>"Can a whale be gracious? Can a leopard seal fall in love with its photographer?&nbsp;That guilty look a dog gives you, does it really FEEL bad? What is it exactly are these animals thinking?", "publishTime"=>1637938800000, "publish-at"=>"2021-11-26T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1637938800, "estimated-duration"=>3549, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/05/animalmindsepisodeimage.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2022/05/animalmindsepisodeimage.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/05/animalmindsepisodeimage.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>200522, "credits-name"=>"WNYC Studios"}, "objectID"=>"1155612", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Animal Minds ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In this hour of Radiolab, stories of cross-species communication.</p>\n<p>When we gaze into the eyes of a wild animal, or even a beloved pet, can we ever really know what they might be thinking? Is it naive to assume they're experiencing something close to human emotions? Or is it ridiculous to assume that they AREN'T feeling something like that? We get the story of a rescued whale that may have found a way to say thanks, ask whether dogs feel guilt, and wonder if a successful predator may have fallen in love with a photographer.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-11-26T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"mixtape-help", "title"=>"Mixtape: Help?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast111921_mixtapehelp.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1153494", "description"=>"<p><span>In tape five, three stories: first, a tale of how the cassette tape <span>supercharged the self-help industry. Second, cassettes filled with history make an epic journey across Africa with a group of Lost Boys. And finally, Simon meets up with fellow Radiolabber David Gebel to dig through an old box of mixtapes and rediscover the unique power of these bygone love letters.</span></span></p>\n<p><em>Mixtape was reported, produced, scored and sound designed by me, Simon Adler, with music throughout by me. Unending reporting and production assistance was provided by Eli Cohen.</em></p>\n<p><span><em><span><strong>Special Thanks to:</strong><span> Shad Helmstetter, Vic Conan, Glenna Salisbury, Jerry Rosen, Richard Petty, Sharon Arkin, Angela Impey, William Mulwill for sharing his cassettes with me, and to the British library for sharing some of their recordings from their South Sudan collection, which is housed at the British Library Sound Archive.</span></span></em></span></p>\n<p><span><em><span><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em>Radiolab.org/donate</em></a><em>. </em></span></span></em></span></p>", "tease"=>"Three stories of cassette tapes as peculiar helpers, carrying self-help, a village's history and lost love.", "publishTime"=>1637334000000, "publish-at"=>"2021-11-19T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1637334000, "estimated-duration"=>2902, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/11/Mixtape_EP5_Help_4x3.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2021/11/Mixtape_EP5_Help_4x3.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/11/Mixtape_EP5_Help_4x3.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>198013, "credits-name"=>"Bryan Ahonen"}, "objectID"=>"1153494", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Mixtape: Help?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In tape five, three stories: first, a tale of how the cassette tape <span>supercharged the self-help industry. Second, cassettes filled with history make an epic journey across Africa with a group of Lost Boys. And finally, Simon meets up with fellow Radiolabber David Gebel to dig through an old box of mixtapes and rediscover the unique power of these bygone love letters.</span></span></p>\n<p><em>Mixtape was reported, produced, scored and sound designed by me, Simon Adler, with music throughout by me. Unending reporting and production assistance was provided by Eli Cohen.</em></p>\n<p><span><em><span><strong>Special Thanks to:</strong><span> Shad Helmstetter, Vic Conan, Glenna Salisbury, Jerry Rosen, Richard Petty, Sharon Arkin, Angela Impey, William Mulwill for sharing his cassettes with me, and to the British library for sharing some of their recordings from their South Sudan collection, which is housed at the British Library Sound Archive.</span></span></em></span></p>\n<p><span><em><span><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em>Radiolab.org/donate</em></a><em>. </em></span></span></em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-11-19T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"mixtape-cassetternet", "title"=>"Mixtape: Cassetternet", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast111221_mixtapecassetternet.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1150232", "description"=>"<p><span>In 1983, Simon Goodwin had a strange thought. Would it be possible to broadcast computer software over the radio? If so, could listeners record it off the air and onto a cassette tape? This experiment and dozens of others in the early 80s created a series of cassette fueled, analog internets. They copied and moved information like never before, upended power structures and created a poisonous social network that brought down a regime. </span></p>\n<p><span>In tape four of Mixtape, we examine how these early internet came about, and how the societal and cultural impacts of these analog information networks can still be felt today.</span></p>\n<p><em>Mixtape is reported, produced, scored and sound designed by Simon Adler with original music throughout by Simon. Top tier reporting and production assistance was provided by Eli Cohen.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Special thanks to: </strong>Alex Sayf Cummings, Martin Maly, Piotr Gawrysiak, Joe Tozer, James Gleick, Jason Rezaian, Gholam Khiabany and Mo Jazi. And to Arash Aziz for helping us every step of the way with our story about Khomeini. And Simon Goodwin for making us that secret code. And to Micah Loewinger to tipping me off to these software radio broadcasts. </em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><strong><em>.  </em></strong><em><span> </span></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"How the cassette tape created the internet.", "publishTime"=>1636729200000, "publish-at"=>"2021-11-12T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1636729200, "estimated-duration"=>3498, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/11/Mixtape_EP4_Cassetternet_4x3_2U639ap.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2021/11/Mixtape_EP4_Cassetternet_4x3_2U639ap.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/11/Mixtape_EP4_Cassetternet_4x3_2U639ap.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>197896, "credits-name"=>"Bryan Ahonen"}, "objectID"=>"1150232", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Mixtape: Cassetternet", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In 1983, Simon Goodwin had a strange thought. Would it be possible to broadcast computer software over the radio? If so, could listeners record it off the air and onto a cassette tape? This experiment and dozens of others in the early 80s created a series of cassette fueled, analog internets. They copied and moved information like never before, upended power structures and created a poisonous social network that brought down a regime. </span></p>\n<p><span>In tape four of Mixtape, we examine how these early internet came about, and how the societal and cultural impacts of these analog information networks can still be felt today.</span></p>\n<p><em>Mixtape is reported, produced, scored and sound designed by Simon Adler with original music throughout by Simon. Top tier reporting and production assistance was provided by Eli Cohen.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Special thanks to: </strong>Alex Sayf Cummings, Martin Maly, Piotr Gawrysiak, Joe Tozer, James Gleick, Jason Rezaian, Gholam Khiabany and Mo Jazi. And to Arash Aziz for helping us every step of the way with our story about Khomeini. And Simon Goodwin for making us that secret code. And to Micah Loewinger to tipping me off to these software radio broadcasts. </em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><strong><em>.  </em></strong><em><span> </span></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-11-12T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"wandering-soul", "title"=>"Mixtape: The Wandering Soul", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast110521_mixtapewanderingsoul.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1148104", "description"=>"<p><span>As the Vietnam war dragged on, the US military began desperately searching for any vulnerability in their North Vietnamese enemy. In 1964, they found it. It was an old Vietnamese folktale involving a ghost, eternal damnation and fear - a tailor made weaponizable myth. And so, armed with tape recorders and microphones, the military set out to win the war by bringing this ghost story to life.</span></p>\n<p><span>Today, the story of these efforts and their ghosts that still haunt us today. </span></p>\n<p><em>Mixtape is reported, produced, scored and sound designed by Simon Adler with original music throughout by Simon. Indispensable reporting and production assistance was provided by Eli Cohen.</em></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Annie McEwen, with original music by Annie. Original reporting was contributed by Trung Dung Vo and Nguyễn Vân Hà.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Special thanks to: </strong><span><em>Allison Boccia, Jared Tracy and Herb Friedman. And to Mathew Campbell for introducing me to the Wandering Soul tape to begin with. And to Erik Villard for all his help pulling those tapes and voices for us.</em> </span></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</strong></em><strong> </strong></p>", "tease"=>"Many nights during the Vietnam war, if you listened closely, you&rsquo;d swear you heard a ghost. Today, the story of that ghost and how it still haunts us today.", "publishTime"=>1636120800000, "publish-at"=>"2021-11-05T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1636120800, "estimated-duration"=>2436, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/11/Mixtape_EP3_Wandering_4x3_xFpJbja.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2021/11/Mixtape_EP3_Wandering_4x3_xFpJbja.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/11/Mixtape_EP3_Wandering_4x3_xFpJbja.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>197852, "credits-name"=>"Bryan Ahonen"}, "objectID"=>"1148104", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Mixtape: The Wandering Soul", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>As the Vietnam war dragged on, the US military began desperately searching for any vulnerability in their North Vietnamese enemy. In 1964, they found it. It was an old Vietnamese folktale involving a ghost, eternal damnation and fear - a tailor made weaponizable myth. And so, armed with tape recorders and microphones, the military set out to win the war by bringing this ghost story to life.</span></p>\n<p><span>Today, the story of these efforts and their ghosts that still haunt us today. </span></p>\n<p><em>Mixtape is reported, produced, scored and sound designed by Simon Adler with original music throughout by Simon. Indispensable reporting and production assistance was provided by Eli Cohen.</em></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Annie McEwen, with original music by Annie. Original reporting was contributed by Trung Dung Vo and Nguyễn Vân Hà.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Special thanks to: </strong><span><em>Allison Boccia, Jared Tracy and Herb Friedman. And to Mathew Campbell for introducing me to the Wandering Soul tape to begin with. And to Erik Villard for all his help pulling those tapes and voices for us.</em> </span></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</strong></em><strong> </strong></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-11-05T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"mixtape-jack-and-bing", "title"=>"Mixtape: Jack and Bing", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast102921_mixtapejackandbing.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1145683", "description"=>"<p><span>In 1946 Bing Crosby was the king of media. He was the movie star, the pop star and his radio show was reaching a third of American living rooms each week.  But then, it all started to fall apart. His ratings were plummeting and his fans were fleeing. Bing however, was not going down without a fight. </span></p>\n<p><span>Today, the story of how Bing Crosby and some stolen Nazi technology won his audience back, changed media forever and accidentally broke reality along the way. </span></p>\n<p><em>Mixtape is reported, produced, scored and sound designed by Simon Adler with original music throughout by Simon Adler. Invaluable reporting and production assistance was provided by Eli Cohen.</em></p>\n<p><span><em><strong>Special thanks </strong><strong>to:</strong> Michele Hilmes, Pete Hammer, Rich Flores, Mara Mills, Jonathan Sterne, Claudia Mewes. Though their voices weren’t in the piece, input certainly was.</em></span></p>\n<p><em>And to Mary Crosby and Robert Bader, for opening up Bing’s archive for us, and enabling us to fill this episode with so much of Bing’s music.</em></p>\n<p><br><strong><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </em></strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><strong><em>.  </em></strong><em><span>  </span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Bing Crosby and some stolen Nazi technology won his audience back and changed media forever.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1635516000000, "publish-at"=>"2021-10-29T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1635516000, "estimated-duration"=>2193, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/10/radiolab_mixtape_jackandbing-4-3-EpisodeImage.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2021/10/radiolab_mixtape_jackandbing-4-3-EpisodeImage.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/10/radiolab_mixtape_jackandbing-4-3-EpisodeImage.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>197711, "credits-name"=>"Bryan Ahonen"}, "objectID"=>"1145683", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Mixtape: Jack and Bing", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In 1946 Bing Crosby was the king of media. He was the movie star, the pop star and his radio show was reaching a third of American living rooms each week.  But then, it all started to fall apart. His ratings were plummeting and his fans were fleeing. Bing however, was not going down without a fight. </span></p>\n<p><span>Today, the story of how Bing Crosby and some stolen Nazi technology won his audience back, changed media forever and accidentally broke reality along the way. </span></p>\n<p><em>Mixtape is reported, produced, scored and sound designed by Simon Adler with original music throughout by Simon Adler. Invaluable reporting and production assistance was provided by Eli Cohen.</em></p>\n<p><span><em><strong>Special thanks </strong><strong>to:</strong> Michele Hilmes, Pete Hammer, Rich Flores, Mara Mills, Jonathan Sterne, Claudia Mewes. Though their voices weren’t in the piece, input certainly was.</em></span></p>\n<p><em>And to Mary Crosby and Robert Bader, for opening up Bing’s archive for us, and enabling us to fill this episode with so much of Bing’s music.</em></p>\n<p><br><strong><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </em></strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><strong><em>.  </em></strong><em><span>  </span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-10-29T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"mixtape-dakou", "title"=>"Mixtape: Dakou", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast102221_mixtapedakou.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1142847", "description"=>"<p><span>Through the 1980s, the vast majority of people in China had never heard western music, save for John Denver, the Carpenters, and a few other artists included on the hand-picked list of songs sanctioned by the Communist Party. But in the late 90s, a mysterious man named Professor Ye made a discovery at a plastic recycling center in Heping.<br></span><br><span>In episode 1 of Mixtape, we talk to Chinese historians, music critics, and the musicians who took the damaged plastic scraps of western music, changed the</span><span> </span><span>musical landscape of China, and reimagined rock and roll in ways we never could’ve imagined.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Mixtape is reported, produced, scored and sound designed by Simon Adler with original music throughout by Simon. Invaluable reporting and production assistance was provided by Eli Cohen. </em><em>Additional reporting by Noriko Ishigaki, Rebecca Kanthor and our amazing anonymous Chinese reporter. </em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><strong>Special thanks </strong><span><strong>to: </strong>Paul de Gay, Juliette Kristensen, Rebecca Tuhus-Dubrow, Nick Lyons, Michael Bull, Jiro Ishikawa, Hayley Zhao, Megan Smalley and Deanne Totto.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode would not have happened without each and every one of them.</span></em></p>\n<p><span><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </em><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em>Radiolab.org/donate</em></a><em>. </em><span><span> </span></span><em><span> </span></em></span></p>", "tease"=>"Cassette tapes trashed as scrap brought western rock music to China and created a cultural remix on the grandest possible scale.", "publishTime"=>1634911200000, "publish-at"=>"2021-10-22T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1634911200, "estimated-duration"=>3065, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/10/Mixtape_EP1_Dakou_WK_4x3.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2021/10/Mixtape_EP1_Dakou_WK_4x3.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/10/Mixtape_EP1_Dakou_WK_4x3.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>197605, "credits-name"=>"Bryan Ahonen"}, "objectID"=>"1142847", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Mixtape: Dakou", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Through the 1980s, the vast majority of people in China had never heard western music, save for John Denver, the Carpenters, and a few other artists included on the hand-picked list of songs sanctioned by the Communist Party. But in the late 90s, a mysterious man named Professor Ye made a discovery at a plastic recycling center in Heping.<br></span><br><span>In episode 1 of Mixtape, we talk to Chinese historians, music critics, and the musicians who took the damaged plastic scraps of western music, changed the</span><span> </span><span>musical landscape of China, and reimagined rock and roll in ways we never could’ve imagined.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Mixtape is reported, produced, scored and sound designed by Simon Adler with original music throughout by Simon. Invaluable reporting and production assistance was provided by Eli Cohen. </em><em>Additional reporting by Noriko Ishigaki, Rebecca Kanthor and our amazing anonymous Chinese reporter. </em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><strong>Special thanks </strong><span><strong>to: </strong>Paul de Gay, Juliette Kristensen, Rebecca Tuhus-Dubrow, Nick Lyons, Michael Bull, Jiro Ishikawa, Hayley Zhao, Megan Smalley and Deanne Totto.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode would not have happened without each and every one of them.</span></em></p>\n<p><span><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </em><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em>Radiolab.org/donate</em></a><em>. </em><span><span> </span></span><em><span> </span></em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-10-22T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"bombs-and-butterflies", "title"=>"Of Bombs and Butterflies", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast101521_bombsandbutterfliesv2.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1140413", "description"=>"<p><span>Ecologist Nick Haddad was sitting in his new office at North Carolina State University when the phone rang. On the other end of the line was... The U.S. Army. The Army folks told him, “Look, there’s this endangered butterfly on our base at Fort Bragg, and it’s the only place in the world that it exists. But it’s about to go extinct. And we need your help to save it.” <br></span><br><span>Nick had never even heard of the butterfly. In fact, he barely knew much about butterflies in general. Nonetheless, he said yes to Uncle Sam. “</span><em><span>How hard could it be?”</span></em><span> he wondered. Turns out, pretty hard. He'd have to trick beavers, dodge bombs, and rethink the fundamental nature of life and death in order to rescue this butterfly before it disappeared forever.</span></p>\n<p><span></span><strong><em>**CORRECTION: An earlier version of this story incorrectly stated that the Army moved a beaver; in truth, they killed it.  We also overstated the current tally of St Francis Satyrs off range; they are around 200, not 800. The audio has been adjusted to reflect these changes.**<br><br></em></strong><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser, and produced by Rachael Cusick. Original music by Jeremy Bloom. Mixing by Arianne Wack.</em><strong><em><br></em></strong></p>\n<p><em><strong>Special thanks to:</strong> Snooki Puli, Cita Escalano, Jeffrey Glassberg, Margot Williams, Mark Romyn, Elizabeth Long, <span>Laura Verhegge, </span>the Public Affairs and Endangered Species Branches at Fort Bragg.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Want to learn more? you can ...<br></strong>...<strong> read Nick Haddad’s book</strong> <a href=\"https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691165004/the-last-butterflies\"><em>The Last Butterflies: A Scientist’s Quest to Save a Rare and Vanishing Creature<br></em></a><strong>... take a peek at Thomas Kral’s</strong> <a href=\"https://images.peabody.yale.edu/lepsoc/jls/1980s/1989/1989-43(2)114-Parshall.pdf\">original 1989 paper about the Saint Francis Satyr<br></a><span><strong>... visit Fort Bragg's</strong> <a href=\"https://home.army.mil/bragg/index.php/about/garrison/directorate-public-works/environmental-division/endangered-species-branch/saint-francis-satyr\">webpage about the Saint Francis Satyr<br></a></span> </p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </em><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em>Radiolab.org/donate</em></a><em>. </em> <em><span>  </span></em></p>", "tease"=>"One man&rsquo;s battle, and we mean battle, to save a tiny little butterfly before it vanishes.", "publishTime"=>1634306400000, "publish-at"=>"2021-10-15T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1634306400, "estimated-duration"=>2462, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/10/OfBombs_ButterFlies_Radiolab1600x1200_FP3ZZyP.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2021/10/OfBombs_ButterFlies_Radiolab1600x1200_FP3ZZyP.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/10/OfBombs_ButterFlies_Radiolab1600x1200_FP3ZZyP.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>197505, "credits-name"=>"Atieh Sohrabi"}, "objectID"=>"1140413", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Of Bombs and Butterflies", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Ecologist Nick Haddad was sitting in his new office at North Carolina State University when the phone rang. On the other end of the line was... The U.S. Army. The Army folks told him, “Look, there’s this endangered butterfly on our base at Fort Bragg, and it’s the only place in the world that it exists. But it’s about to go extinct. And we need your help to save it.” <br></span><br><span>Nick had never even heard of the butterfly. In fact, he barely knew much about butterflies in general. Nonetheless, he said yes to Uncle Sam. “</span><em><span>How hard could it be?”</span></em><span> he wondered. Turns out, pretty hard. He'd have to trick beavers, dodge bombs, and rethink the fundamental nature of life and death in order to rescue this butterfly before it disappeared forever.</span></p>\n<p><span></span><strong><em>**CORRECTION: An earlier version of this story incorrectly stated that the Army moved a beaver; in truth, they killed it.  We also overstated the current tally of St Francis Satyrs off range; they are around 200, not 800. The audio has been adjusted to reflect these changes.**<br><br></em></strong><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser, and produced by Rachael Cusick. Original music by Jeremy Bloom. Mixing by Arianne Wack.</em><strong><em><br></em></strong></p>\n<p><em><strong>Special thanks to:</strong> Snooki Puli, Cita Escalano, Jeffrey Glassberg, Margot Williams, Mark Romyn, Elizabeth Long, <span>Laura Verhegge, </span>the Public Affairs and Endangered Species Branches at Fort Bragg.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Want to learn more? you can ...<br></strong>...<strong> read Nick Haddad’s book</strong> <a href=\"https://press.princeton.edu/books/hardcover/9780691165004/the-last-butterflies\"><em>The Last Butterflies: A Scientist’s Quest to Save a Rare and Vanishing Creature<br></em></a><strong>... take a peek at Thomas Kral’s</strong> <a href=\"https://images.peabody.yale.edu/lepsoc/jls/1980s/1989/1989-43(2)114-Parshall.pdf\">original 1989 paper about the Saint Francis Satyr<br></a><span><strong>... visit Fort Bragg's</strong> <a href=\"https://home.army.mil/bragg/index.php/about/garrison/directorate-public-works/environmental-division/endangered-species-branch/saint-francis-satyr\">webpage about the Saint Francis Satyr<br></a></span> </p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </em><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em>Radiolab.org/donate</em></a><em>. </em> <em><span>  </span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-10-15T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"oliver-sipple-21", "title"=>"Oliver Sipple", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast100121_oliversipple.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1136403", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>One morning, Oliver Sipple went out for a walk. A couple hours later, to his own surprise, he saved the life of the President of the United States. But in the days that followed, Sipple’s split-second act of heroism turned into a rationale for making his personal life into political opportunity. What happens next makes us wonder what a moment, or a movement, or a whole society can demand of one person. And how much is too much? </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Through newly unearthed archival tape, we hear Sipple himself grapple with some of the most vexing topics of his day and ours - privacy, identity, the freedom of the press - not to mention the bonds of family and friendship. </p>\n<p><em>Reported by Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte. Produced by Matt Kielty, Annie McEwen, Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Jerry Pritikin, Michael Yamashita, Stan Smith, Duffy Jennings; Ann Dolan, Megan Filly and Ginale Harris at the Superior Court of San Francisco; Leah Gracik, Karyn Hunt, Jesse Hamlin, The San Francisco Bay Area Television Archive, Mike Amico, Jennifer Vanasco and Joey Plaster.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/articles/oliver-sipple\">Episode originally published 09/21/2017</a></em></p>", "tease"=>"In a flash of heroism and humanity, Oliver Sipple saved a life and became something he never wanted to be.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1633093200000, "publish-at"=>"2021-10-01T09:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1633093200, "estimated-duration"=>3809, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/05/Sipple-square-05.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1600/l/80/2022/05/Sipple-square-05.png", "h"=>1600, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Oliver Sipple, a 33-year-old former Marine, tells how he struck the gun just as it was fired in the direction of President Ford as he left a downtown hotel in San Francisco on Sept. 22, 1975.", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/05/Sipple-square-05.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>200503, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1136403", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Oliver Sipple", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>One morning, Oliver Sipple went out for a walk. A couple hours later, to his own surprise, he saved the life of the President of the United States. But in the days that followed, Sipple’s split-second act of heroism turned into a rationale for making his personal life into political opportunity. What happens next makes us wonder what a moment, or a movement, or a whole society can demand of one person. And how much is too much? </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Through newly unearthed archival tape, we hear Sipple himself grapple with some of the most vexing topics of his day and ours - privacy, identity, the freedom of the press - not to mention the bonds of family and friendship. </p>\n<p><em>Reported by Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte. Produced by Matt Kielty, Annie McEwen, Latif Nasser and Tracie Hunte.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Jerry Pritikin, Michael Yamashita, Stan Smith, Duffy Jennings; Ann Dolan, Megan Filly and Ginale Harris at the Superior Court of San Francisco; Leah Gracik, Karyn Hunt, Jesse Hamlin, The San Francisco Bay Area Television Archive, Mike Amico, Jennifer Vanasco and Joey Plaster.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/articles/oliver-sipple\">Episode originally published 09/21/2017</a></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-10-01T09:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"heavy-metal", "title"=>"HEAVY METAL", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast092421_heavymetal.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1133817", "description"=>"<p><span>Today we have a story about the sometimes obvious but sometimes sneaky effects of the way that we humans rearrange the elemental stuff around us. Reporter Avir Mitra and science journalist Lydia Denworth bring us a story about how one man’s relentless pursuit of a deep truth about the Earth led to an obsession that really changed the very air we breathe.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Avir Mitra, and produced by Matt Kielty, Becca Bressler, Rachael Cusick, and Maria Paz Gutiérrez.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Cliff Davidson, Paul M. Sutter, Denton Ebel, and Sam Kean. </span></em><br><span></span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><span></span><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a><span> </span><em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span><span> </span></span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"What happens when you&rsquo;re the only one who can see something?", "publishTime"=>1632492000000, "publish-at"=>"2021-09-24T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1632492000, "estimated-duration"=>2554, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/09/Radiolab_A-Slow-Decay-Blue-Tint.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1448/l/80/2021/09/Radiolab_A-Slow-Decay-Blue-Tint.png", "h"=>1448, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/09/Radiolab_A-Slow-Decay-Blue-Tint.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>197207, "credits-name"=>"Melanie St-Pierre"}, "objectID"=>"1133817", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"HEAVY METAL", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Today we have a story about the sometimes obvious but sometimes sneaky effects of the way that we humans rearrange the elemental stuff around us. Reporter Avir Mitra and science journalist Lydia Denworth bring us a story about how one man’s relentless pursuit of a deep truth about the Earth led to an obsession that really changed the very air we breathe.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Avir Mitra, and produced by Matt Kielty, Becca Bressler, Rachael Cusick, and Maria Paz Gutiérrez.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Cliff Davidson, Paul M. Sutter, Denton Ebel, and Sam Kean. </span></em><br><span></span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><span></span><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a><span> </span><em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span><span> </span></span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-09-24T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"122291-in-running", "title"=>"In the Running", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast091721_intherunning.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=122291", "description"=>"<p>Diane Van Deren is one of the best ultra-runners in the world, and it all started with a seizure. In this short, Diane tells us how her disability gave rise to an extraordinary ability.</p><p>For Diane Van Deren, a charming mother of three, daily life is a struggle. But as soon as she steps outdoors, she's capable of amazing feats. She can run for days on end with no sleep, covering hundreds of miles in extreme conditions. Reporter Mark Phillips heads to Colorado to get to know Diane, and to try to figure out what makes her so unstoppable.</p>\n<p><em><span><strong>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><strong>.</strong> </span></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Diane Van Deren is one of the best ultra-runners in the world, and it all started with a seizure.&nbsp;In this short, Diane tells us how her disability gave rise to an extraordinary ability.", "publishTime"=>1631887200000, "publish-at"=>"2021-09-17T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1631887200, "estimated-duration"=>1173, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Diane Van Deren", "name"=>"photologue/photos/dianerunner__.JPG", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/437/c/80/photologue/photos/dianerunner__.JPG", "h"=>437, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Diane Van Deren", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/photologue/photos/dianerunner__.JPG", "w"=>640, "id"=>47961, "credits-name"=>"Mark Phillips"}, "objectID"=>"122291", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"In the Running", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Diane Van Deren is one of the best ultra-runners in the world, and it all started with a seizure. In this short, Diane tells us how her disability gave rise to an extraordinary ability.</p><p>For Diane Van Deren, a charming mother of three, daily life is a struggle. But as soon as she steps outdoors, she's capable of amazing feats. She can run for days on end with no sleep, covering hundreds of miles in extreme conditions. Reporter Mark Phillips heads to Colorado to get to know Diane, and to try to figure out what makes her so unstoppable.</p>\n<p><em><span><strong>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><strong>.</strong> </span></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-09-17T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"60-words-20-years", "title"=>"60 Words, 20 Years", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast091021_60words20years.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1131129", "description"=>"<p><span>It has now been 20 years since September 11th, 2001. So we’re bringing you a Peabody Award-winning story from our archives about one sentence, written in the hours after the attacks, that has led to the longest war in U.S. history. We examine how just 60 words of legal language have blurred the line between war and peace.</span></p>\n<p><span>In the hours after the attacks of Sept. 11, 2001, a lawyer sat down in front of a computer and started writing a legal justification for taking action against those responsible. The language that he drafted and that President George W. Bush signed into law - called the Authorization for Use of Military Force (AUMF) -  has at its heart one single sentence, 60 words long. Over the last decade, those 60 words have become the legal foundation for the \"war on terror.\"</span></p>\n<p><span>In this collaboration with BuzzFeed, reporter Gregory Johnsen tells us the story of how this has come to be one of the most important, confusing, troubling sentences of the last two decades. We go into the meetings that took place in the chaotic days just after 9/11, speak with Congresswoman Barbara Lee and former Congressman Ron Dellums about the vote on the AUMF. We hear from former White House and State Department lawyers John Bellinger &amp; Harold Koh. We learn how this legal language unleashed Guantanamo, Navy Seal raids and drone strikes. And we speak with journalist Daniel Klaidman, legal expert Benjamin Wittes and Virginia Senator Tim Kaine about how these words came to be interpreted, and what they mean for the future of war and peace.</span></p>\n<p><span>Finally, we check back in with Congresswoman Lee, and talk to Yale law professor and national security expert Oona Hathaway, about how to move on from the original sixty words.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Original episode produced by Matt Kielty and Kelsey Padgett with original music by Dylan Keefe. Update reported and produced by Sarah Qari and Soren Wheeler.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Brian Finucane.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><strong>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><strong><em>.</em></strong><em><span> </span></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"It&rsquo;s been 20 years since 9/11. We bring you a story about the 60 words that ushered in everything that came after.", "publishTime"=>1631282400000, "publish-at"=>"2021-09-10T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1631282400, "estimated-duration"=>4167, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/siteimageFIN.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/900/h/80/1/siteimageFIN.jpeg", "h"=>900, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"h", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/siteimageFIN.jpeg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>115987, "credits-name"=>"Original artwork & Radiolab logo by Lucy Engelman"}, "objectID"=>"1131129", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"60 Words, 20 Years", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>It has now been 20 years since September 11th, 2001. So we’re bringing you a Peabody Award-winning story from our archives about one sentence, written in the hours after the attacks, that has led to the longest war in U.S. history. We examine how just 60 words of legal language have blurred the line between war and peace.</span></p>\n<p><span>In the hours after the attacks of Sept. 11, 2001, a lawyer sat down in front of a computer and started writing a legal justification for taking action against those responsible. The language that he drafted and that President George W. Bush signed into law - called the Authorization for Use of Military Force (AUMF) -  has at its heart one single sentence, 60 words long. Over the last decade, those 60 words have become the legal foundation for the \"war on terror.\"</span></p>\n<p><span>In this collaboration with BuzzFeed, reporter Gregory Johnsen tells us the story of how this has come to be one of the most important, confusing, troubling sentences of the last two decades. We go into the meetings that took place in the chaotic days just after 9/11, speak with Congresswoman Barbara Lee and former Congressman Ron Dellums about the vote on the AUMF. We hear from former White House and State Department lawyers John Bellinger &amp; Harold Koh. We learn how this legal language unleashed Guantanamo, Navy Seal raids and drone strikes. And we speak with journalist Daniel Klaidman, legal expert Benjamin Wittes and Virginia Senator Tim Kaine about how these words came to be interpreted, and what they mean for the future of war and peace.</span></p>\n<p><span>Finally, we check back in with Congresswoman Lee, and talk to Yale law professor and national security expert Oona Hathaway, about how to move on from the original sixty words.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Original episode produced by Matt Kielty and Kelsey Padgett with original music by Dylan Keefe. Update reported and produced by Sarah Qari and Soren Wheeler.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Brian Finucane.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><strong>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><strong><em>.</em></strong><em><span> </span></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-09-10T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"unsilencing", "title"=>"The Unsilencing", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21theunsilencing.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1128272", "description"=>"<p><span>Multiple sclerosis, lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, even psoriasis — these are diseases in which the body begins to attack itself, and they all have one thing in common: they affect women more than men. Most autoimmune disorders do. And not just by a little bit, often by a lot; in some cases, as much as sixteen times more. But why? On today’s episode, we talk to scientists trying to answer that question. We go back 100 million years, to when our placenta first evolved and consider how it might have shaped our immune system. We dive deep into the genome, to stare at one of the most famous chromosomes: the X. And we also try to unravel a mystery — why is it that for some females, autoimmune disorders seemingly disappear during pregnancy?</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Molly Webster, and produced by Sindhu </span></em><em><span>Gnanasambandan and Molly Webster</span></em><em><span>.</span></em> <em><span>The Gonads theme song was written, performed, and produced by Majel Connery and Alex Overington. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Looking for something else to listen to? We suggest pairing “The Unsilencing” with “</span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/articles/everybodys-got-one\"><em><span>Everybody’s Got One</span></em></a><em><span>,” an episode about an unknown super-organ that nobody on the planet would be here without: the placenta.</span></em></p>\n<p><strong>Want to learn more? You can …<br></strong><strong>...check out a <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4833277/\"><strong>Montserrat Anguera XX study</strong>,</a><br></strong><strong>...read </strong><a href=\"https://www.cell.com/trends/genetics/pdf/S0168-9525(19)30079-4.pdf\"><strong>Melissa Wilson’s placental, pregnancy hypothesis,</strong></a><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4833277/\"><strong><br></strong></a><strong>…and get a primer on </strong><a href=\"https://newsroom.ucla.edu/releases/preliminary-clinical-trial-shows-great-promise-for-new-multiple-sclerosis-treatment\"><strong>Rhonda Voskuhl’s estriol &amp; </strong><strong>Multiple Sclerosis</strong><strong> work.</strong></a></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a><span><span> </span></span><em><span>today.</span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Three scientists dive back in time, deep into our bodies, and down into our genome to tackle one question. Why do women tend to get autoimmune disease more than men?", "publishTime"=>1629964800000, "publish-at"=>"2021-08-26T04:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1629964800, "estimated-duration"=>1736, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/08/Radiolab_UNSILENCING_OC_WEBBHALLYE.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2021/08/Radiolab_UNSILENCING_OC_WEBBHALLYE.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/08/Radiolab_UNSILENCING_OC_WEBBHALLYE.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>196831, "credits-name"=>"Hallye Webb"}, "objectID"=>"1128272", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Unsilencing", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Multiple sclerosis, lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, even psoriasis — these are diseases in which the body begins to attack itself, and they all have one thing in common: they affect women more than men. Most autoimmune disorders do. And not just by a little bit, often by a lot; in some cases, as much as sixteen times more. But why? On today’s episode, we talk to scientists trying to answer that question. We go back 100 million years, to when our placenta first evolved and consider how it might have shaped our immune system. We dive deep into the genome, to stare at one of the most famous chromosomes: the X. And we also try to unravel a mystery — why is it that for some females, autoimmune disorders seemingly disappear during pregnancy?</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Molly Webster, and produced by Sindhu </span></em><em><span>Gnanasambandan and Molly Webster</span></em><em><span>.</span></em> <em><span>The Gonads theme song was written, performed, and produced by Majel Connery and Alex Overington. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Looking for something else to listen to? We suggest pairing “The Unsilencing” with “</span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/articles/everybodys-got-one\"><em><span>Everybody’s Got One</span></em></a><em><span>,” an episode about an unknown super-organ that nobody on the planet would be here without: the placenta.</span></em></p>\n<p><strong>Want to learn more? You can …<br></strong><strong>...check out a <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4833277/\"><strong>Montserrat Anguera XX study</strong>,</a><br></strong><strong>...read </strong><a href=\"https://www.cell.com/trends/genetics/pdf/S0168-9525(19)30079-4.pdf\"><strong>Melissa Wilson’s placental, pregnancy hypothesis,</strong></a><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4833277/\"><strong><br></strong></a><strong>…and get a primer on </strong><a href=\"https://newsroom.ucla.edu/releases/preliminary-clinical-trial-shows-great-promise-for-new-multiple-sclerosis-treatment\"><strong>Rhonda Voskuhl’s estriol &amp; </strong><strong>Multiple Sclerosis</strong><strong> work.</strong></a></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a><span><span> </span></span><em><span>today.</span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-08-26T04:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"everybodys-got-one", "title"=>"Everybody’s Got One", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21everybodysgotone.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1127193", "description"=>"<p><span>We all think we know the story of pregnancy. Sperm meets egg, f</span><span>ollowed by nine months of nurturing, nesting, and quiet incubation</span><span>. </span><span>But this story isn’t the nursery rhyme we think it is. In a way, it’s a struggle, almost like a tiny war. And right on the front lines of that battle is another major player on the stage of pregnancy that not a single person on the planet would be here without. An entirely </span><em><span>new </span></em><span>organ: the placenta.</span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode we take you on a journey through the 270-day life of this weird, squishy, gelatinous orb, and discover that it is so much more than an organ. It’s a foreign invader. A piece of meat. A friend and parent. And it’s perhaps the most essential piece in the survival of our kind.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was </span></em><em><span>reported by Heather Radke and Becca Bressler, and produced by Becca Bressler and Pat Walters, with help from Matt Kielty and Maria Paz Gutierrez. Additional reporting by Molly Webster.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Diana Bianchi, Julia Katz, Sam Behjati, Celia Bardwell-Jones, <span>Mathilde Cohen, </span>Hannah Ingraham, Pip Lipkin, and Molly Fassler.</span></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.  </em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>For cool new research on the placenta:</p>\n<p>Check out Harvey’s latest <a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0143400421001284\">paper</a> published with Julia Katz.</p>\n<p>Sam Behjati's latest<a href=\"https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2021/03/210310122521.htm\"> paper</a> on the placenta as a \"genetic dumping ground\". </p>", "tease"=>"The womb mate we&rsquo;ve all had, but barely know.", "publishTime"=>1629459900000, "publish-at"=>"2021-08-20T07:45:00", "date-line-ts"=>1629459900, "estimated-duration"=>1761, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/08/Radiolab_PLACENTA_OC_WebbHaylee.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2021/08/Radiolab_PLACENTA_OC_WebbHaylee.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/08/Radiolab_PLACENTA_OC_WebbHaylee.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>196770, "credits-name"=>"Hallye Webb"}, "objectID"=>"1127193", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Everybody’s Got One", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>We all think we know the story of pregnancy. Sperm meets egg, f</span><span>ollowed by nine months of nurturing, nesting, and quiet incubation</span><span>. </span><span>But this story isn’t the nursery rhyme we think it is. In a way, it’s a struggle, almost like a tiny war. And right on the front lines of that battle is another major player on the stage of pregnancy that not a single person on the planet would be here without. An entirely </span><em><span>new </span></em><span>organ: the placenta.</span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode we take you on a journey through the 270-day life of this weird, squishy, gelatinous orb, and discover that it is so much more than an organ. It’s a foreign invader. A piece of meat. A friend and parent. And it’s perhaps the most essential piece in the survival of our kind.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was </span></em><em><span>reported by Heather Radke and Becca Bressler, and produced by Becca Bressler and Pat Walters, with help from Matt Kielty and Maria Paz Gutierrez. Additional reporting by Molly Webster.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Diana Bianchi, Julia Katz, Sam Behjati, Celia Bardwell-Jones, <span>Mathilde Cohen, </span>Hannah Ingraham, Pip Lipkin, and Molly Fassler.</span></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.  </em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>For cool new research on the placenta:</p>\n<p>Check out Harvey’s latest <a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0143400421001284\">paper</a> published with Julia Katz.</p>\n<p>Sam Behjati's latest<a href=\"https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2021/03/210310122521.htm\"> paper</a> on the placenta as a \"genetic dumping ground\". </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-08-20T07:45:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dutee", "title"=>"Gonads: Dutee", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21duteeupdate.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=870128", "description"=>"<p><span>In 2014, India’s Dutee Chand was a rising female track and field star, crushing national records. But then, that summer, something unexpected happened: she failed a gender test. And was banned from the sport. Before she knew it, Dutee was thrown into the middle of a controversy that started long before her, and continues on today: how to separate males and females in sport. First aired in 2018, Dutee and the story of female athletes in sport are back in the spotlight this week, at the Tokyo Olympics. Join us for an update on Dutee’s second Olympic games, and the continued role testosterone has in shaping who is on the track, and who is off. </span></p>\n<p><span>This story was originally released as part of </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/projects/radiolab-presents-gonads\"><em><span>Gonads</span></em></a><span>, a six-part series on the parts of us that make more of us. It is a companion piece to Gonads, episode 5: </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/dana/?token=5d6b8203d6e51b8ef6483829f58431f1&amp;content_type_id=26&amp;object_id=870129&amp;_=c963e28f\"><strong>Dana</strong></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This update was reported by Molly Webster, with reporting and producing by Sarah Qari.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>\"Dutee\" was reported by Molly Webster, with co-reporting and translation by Sarah Qari. It was produced by Pat Walters, with production help from Jad Abumrad and Rachael Cusick. The Gonads theme was written, performed, and produced by Majel Connery and Alex Overington.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Geertje Mak, Maayan Sudai, Andrea Dunaif, Bhrikuti Rai, Joe Osmundson, and Payoshni Mitra. Plus, former Olympic runner Madeleine Pape, who is currently studying regulations around female, transgender, and intersex individuals in sport.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science. And the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation, enhancing public understanding of science and technology in the modern world. More information about Sloan at www.sloan.org.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em> </p>", "tease"=>"Olympian&nbsp;Dutee Chand has drawn the spotlight not just for being one of the fastest Indian female runners ever: in 2014, she was banned after failing a &ldquo;gender test.&rdquo;&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1628211600000, "publish-at"=>"2021-08-05T21:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1628208000, "estimated-duration"=>2773, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2018/07/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHuDutee.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2018/07/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHuDutee.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2018/07/NewYorkPublicRadioJasuHuDutee.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>177023, "credits-name"=>"Jasu Hu"}, "objectID"=>"870128", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Gonads: Dutee", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In 2014, India’s Dutee Chand was a rising female track and field star, crushing national records. But then, that summer, something unexpected happened: she failed a gender test. And was banned from the sport. Before she knew it, Dutee was thrown into the middle of a controversy that started long before her, and continues on today: how to separate males and females in sport. First aired in 2018, Dutee and the story of female athletes in sport are back in the spotlight this week, at the Tokyo Olympics. Join us for an update on Dutee’s second Olympic games, and the continued role testosterone has in shaping who is on the track, and who is off. </span></p>\n<p><span>This story was originally released as part of </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/projects/radiolab-presents-gonads\"><em><span>Gonads</span></em></a><span>, a six-part series on the parts of us that make more of us. It is a companion piece to Gonads, episode 5: </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/story/dana/?token=5d6b8203d6e51b8ef6483829f58431f1&amp;content_type_id=26&amp;object_id=870129&amp;_=c963e28f\"><strong>Dana</strong></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>This update was reported by Molly Webster, with reporting and producing by Sarah Qari.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>\"Dutee\" was reported by Molly Webster, with co-reporting and translation by Sarah Qari. It was produced by Pat Walters, with production help from Jad Abumrad and Rachael Cusick. The Gonads theme was written, performed, and produced by Majel Connery and Alex Overington.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Geertje Mak, Maayan Sudai, Andrea Dunaif, Bhrikuti Rai, Joe Osmundson, and Payoshni Mitra. Plus, former Olympic runner Madeleine Pape, who is currently studying regulations around female, transgender, and intersex individuals in sport.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science. And the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation, enhancing public understanding of science and technology in the modern world. More information about Sloan at www.sloan.org.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-08-05T21:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"queen-dying", "title"=>"The Queen of Dying", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21queenofdying.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1122115", "description"=>"<p><span>If you’ve ever lost someone, or watched a medical drama in the last 15 years, you’ve probably heard of The Five Stages of Grief. They’re sort of the world’s worst consolation prize for loss. But last year, we began wondering… Where did these stages come from in the first place?</span></p>\n<p><span>Turns out, </span><span>Elisabeth Kübler-Ross. But the story is much, </span><em><span>much</span></em><span> more complicated than that. T</span><span>hose stages of grieving? They actually started as stages of dying. After learning that, producer Rachael Cusick tumbled into a year-long journey through the life and work of the incredibly complicated and misunderstood woman who single-handedly changed the way all of us face dying, and the way we deal with being left behind.</span></p>\n<p><span>Special Note: Our friends over at Death Sex and Money have put together a very special companion to this story, featuring Rachael talking about this story with her grandmother.  Check it out <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/episodes/rachael-cusick-grief-radiolab-death-sex-money\">here.</a></span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/episodes/rachael-cusick-grief-radiolab-death-sex-money\"></a></span><em>This episode was reported and produced by Rachael Cusick, with production help from Carin Leong.</em></p>\n<p><em>This story wouldn’t have been possible without the folks you heard from in the episode, and the many, many people who touched this story, including: Anne Adams, Andrew Aronson, Audrey Gordon, Barbara Hogenson, Basit Qari, Bill Weese, Bob McGan, Carey Gauzens, Clifford Edwards, Cristina McGinniss, Dorothy Holinger, Frank Ostaseski, Ira Byock, Jamie Munson, Jessica Weisberg, Jillian Tullis, Joanna Treichler, Jonathan Green, Ken Bridbord, Ladybird Morgan, Laurel Braitman, Lawrence Lincoln, Leah Siegel, Liese Groot, Linda Mount, Lyn Frumkin, Mark Kuczewski, Martha Twaddle, Peter Nevraumont, Rosalie Roder, Sala Hilaire, Stefan Haupt, Stephanie Riley, Stephen Connor, and Tracie Hunte.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to all the folks who shared music for this episode, including:</em></p>\n<p><em>Lisa Stoll, who shared her Alpine horn music with us for this episode. You can hear more of her music <a href=\"https://www.lisastoll.ch/\">here</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Cliff Edwards, who shared original music from <a href=\"https://www.deannaedwards.com/\">Deanna Edwards.</a></em></p>\n<p><em><a href=\"http://www.martinhayes.com/martin-hayes-quartet\">The Martin Hayes Quartet</a>, who shared the last bit of music you hear in the piece that somehow puts a world of emotion into one beautiful tune.</em></p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.martinhayes.com/martin-hayes-quartet\"></a><em>And an extra special thank you to the folks over at Stanford University - Ben Stone, David Magnus, Karl Lorenz, Maren Monsen -  the caretakers of Elisabeth’s archival collection who made it possible to rummage through their library from halfway across the country. You can read more about the collection <a href=\"https://news.stanford.edu/thedish/2019/03/03/the-elisabeth-kubler-ross-archive-has-found-a-home-at-stanford/\">here</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>To learn more about Elisabeth and the folks who are furthering her work, you can visit the <span>Elisabeth Kübler-Ross Foundation website <a href=\"https://www.ekrfoundation.org/\">here</a>.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.  </em></p>", "tease"=>"The remarkable story of the woman who showed us all how to stare down death: Elisabeth K&uuml;bler-Ross.", "publishTime"=>1627041540000, "publish-at"=>"2021-07-23T07:59:00", "date-line-ts"=>1627041540, "estimated-duration"=>3648, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/05/queenofdyingepisodeimage.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2022/05/queenofdyingepisodeimage.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/05/queenofdyingepisodeimage.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>200523, "credits-name"=>"WNYC Studios"}, "objectID"=>"1122115", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Queen of Dying", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>If you’ve ever lost someone, or watched a medical drama in the last 15 years, you’ve probably heard of The Five Stages of Grief. They’re sort of the world’s worst consolation prize for loss. But last year, we began wondering… Where did these stages come from in the first place?</span></p>\n<p><span>Turns out, </span><span>Elisabeth Kübler-Ross. But the story is much, </span><em><span>much</span></em><span> more complicated than that. T</span><span>hose stages of grieving? They actually started as stages of dying. After learning that, producer Rachael Cusick tumbled into a year-long journey through the life and work of the incredibly complicated and misunderstood woman who single-handedly changed the way all of us face dying, and the way we deal with being left behind.</span></p>\n<p><span>Special Note: Our friends over at Death Sex and Money have put together a very special companion to this story, featuring Rachael talking about this story with her grandmother.  Check it out <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/episodes/rachael-cusick-grief-radiolab-death-sex-money\">here.</a></span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/episodes/rachael-cusick-grief-radiolab-death-sex-money\"></a></span><em>This episode was reported and produced by Rachael Cusick, with production help from Carin Leong.</em></p>\n<p><em>This story wouldn’t have been possible without the folks you heard from in the episode, and the many, many people who touched this story, including: Anne Adams, Andrew Aronson, Audrey Gordon, Barbara Hogenson, Basit Qari, Bill Weese, Bob McGan, Carey Gauzens, Clifford Edwards, Cristina McGinniss, Dorothy Holinger, Frank Ostaseski, Ira Byock, Jamie Munson, Jessica Weisberg, Jillian Tullis, Joanna Treichler, Jonathan Green, Ken Bridbord, Ladybird Morgan, Laurel Braitman, Lawrence Lincoln, Leah Siegel, Liese Groot, Linda Mount, Lyn Frumkin, Mark Kuczewski, Martha Twaddle, Peter Nevraumont, Rosalie Roder, Sala Hilaire, Stefan Haupt, Stephanie Riley, Stephen Connor, and Tracie Hunte.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to all the folks who shared music for this episode, including:</em></p>\n<p><em>Lisa Stoll, who shared her Alpine horn music with us for this episode. You can hear more of her music <a href=\"https://www.lisastoll.ch/\">here</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Cliff Edwards, who shared original music from <a href=\"https://www.deannaedwards.com/\">Deanna Edwards.</a></em></p>\n<p><em><a href=\"http://www.martinhayes.com/martin-hayes-quartet\">The Martin Hayes Quartet</a>, who shared the last bit of music you hear in the piece that somehow puts a world of emotion into one beautiful tune.</em></p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.martinhayes.com/martin-hayes-quartet\"></a><em>And an extra special thank you to the folks over at Stanford University - Ben Stone, David Magnus, Karl Lorenz, Maren Monsen -  the caretakers of Elisabeth’s archival collection who made it possible to rummage through their library from halfway across the country. You can read more about the collection <a href=\"https://news.stanford.edu/thedish/2019/03/03/the-elisabeth-kubler-ross-archive-has-found-a-home-at-stanford/\">here</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em>To learn more about Elisabeth and the folks who are furthering her work, you can visit the <span>Elisabeth Kübler-Ross Foundation website <a href=\"https://www.ekrfoundation.org/\">here</a>.</span></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.  </em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-07-23T07:59:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"breaking-news-about-other-latif", "title"=>"Breaking News about The Other Latif", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast071621_theotherlatifupdate.mp3?aisGetOriginalStream=true&awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1120968", "description"=>"<p><span>A major development in the case of Guantanamo detainee Abdul Latif Nasser.</span></p>\n<p><span>To listen to our series about him, go to <a href=\"http://theotherlatif.org\"><span>theotherlatif.org</span></a><span>.</span></span></p>", "tease"=>"A major development in the case of Guantanamo detainee Abdul Latif Nasser.", "publishTime"=>1626685200000, "publish-at"=>"2021-07-19T05:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1626685200, "estimated-duration"=>121, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/07/TheOtherLatif_2021_EpImage.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2021/07/TheOtherLatif_2021_EpImage.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/07/TheOtherLatif_2021_EpImage.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>196363, "credits-name"=>"Will Paybarah"}, "objectID"=>"1120968", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Breaking News about The Other Latif", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>A major development in the case of Guantanamo detainee Abdul Latif Nasser.</span></p>\n<p><span>To listen to our series about him, go to <a href=\"http://theotherlatif.org\"><span>theotherlatif.org</span></a><span>.</span></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-07-19T05:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"g-unfit", "title"=>"G: Unfit", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21unfitrerun_intelligence-series.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=950772", "description"=>"<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1302\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1311\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1331\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1325\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1348\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<p>In the past few weeks, most people have probably seen Britney Spears' name or face everywhere. When she stood in front of a judge (virtually) and protested the conservatorship she's been living under for the past 13 years, one harrowing detail in particular stood out. She told the judge, \"I was told right now in the conservatorship, I'm not able to get married or have a baby.\" Today, we look back at an old episode where we explore why it is that hundreds of thousands of people can have their reproductive rights denied...and spoiler: it goes back to Darwin.</p>\n<p>When a law student named Mark Bold came across a Supreme Court decision from the 1920s that allowed for the forced sterilization of people deemed “unfit,” he was shocked to discover that it had never been overturned. His law professors told him the case, Buck v Bell, was nothing to worry about, that the ruling was in a kind of legal limbo and could never be used against people. But he didn’t buy it. In this episode we follow Mark on a journey to one of the darkest consequences of humanity’s attempts to measure the human mind and put people in boxes, following him through history, science fiction and a version of eugenics that’s still very much alive today, and watch as he crusades to restore a dash of moral order to the universe.</p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty, Lulu Miller and Pat Walters. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Sara Luterman, Lynn Rainville, Alex Minna Stern, Steve Silberman and Lydia X.Z. Brown.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab’s “G” is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science.</span></em></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1357\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"A journey to one of the darkest sides of humanity&rsquo;s attempts to measure the human mind and put people in boxes.", "publishTime"=>1626382560000, "publish-at"=>"2021-07-15T16:56:00", "date-line-ts"=>1626382560, "estimated-duration"=>3215, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2019/07/IntelligenceEpisode4final.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2019/07/IntelligenceEpisode4final.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2019/07/IntelligenceEpisode4final.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>184795, "credits-name"=>"Deborah Lee"}, "objectID"=>"950772", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"G: Unfit", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1302\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1311\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1331\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1325\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1348\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<p>In the past few weeks, most people have probably seen Britney Spears' name or face everywhere. When she stood in front of a judge (virtually) and protested the conservatorship she's been living under for the past 13 years, one harrowing detail in particular stood out. She told the judge, \"I was told right now in the conservatorship, I'm not able to get married or have a baby.\" Today, we look back at an old episode where we explore why it is that hundreds of thousands of people can have their reproductive rights denied...and spoiler: it goes back to Darwin.</p>\n<p>When a law student named Mark Bold came across a Supreme Court decision from the 1920s that allowed for the forced sterilization of people deemed “unfit,” he was shocked to discover that it had never been overturned. His law professors told him the case, Buck v Bell, was nothing to worry about, that the ruling was in a kind of legal limbo and could never be used against people. But he didn’t buy it. In this episode we follow Mark on a journey to one of the darkest consequences of humanity’s attempts to measure the human mind and put people in boxes, following him through history, science fiction and a version of eugenics that’s still very much alive today, and watch as he crusades to restore a dash of moral order to the universe.</p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty, Lulu Miller and Pat Walters. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Sara Luterman, Lynn Rainville, Alex Minna Stern, Steve Silberman and Lydia X.Z. Brown.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab’s “G” is supported in part by Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation initiative dedicated to engaging everyone with the process of science.</span></em></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1357\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"><em><em><em><span><em>Support Radiolab today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=podcast&amp;utm_medium=notes&amp;utm_campaign=membership&amp;utm_content=radiolab\" target=\"_blank\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>. </em></span></em></em></em></div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-07-15T16:56:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"vanishing-harry-pace-episode-6", "title"=>"The Vanishing of Harry Pace: Episode 6", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast061921_ep6.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1116873", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Lift Every Voice. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Black Swan Records was first to record the anthem Lift Every Voice and Sing. From a family's Thanksgiving dinner, we portal through to the song's past, present, and future.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong><em>The Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> was created and produced by Shima Oliaee and Jad Abumrad. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This series was produced in collaboration with author Kiese Laymon, scholar Imani Perry, writer Cord Jefferson, WQXR’s Terrance McKnight, and WNYC's Jami Floyd. B<span>ased on the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swan-Blues-Americas-black-owned-ebook/dp/B00NJ2BS4S\">Black Swan Blues: the Hard Rise and Brutal Fall of America’s First Black Owned Record Label</a><span> </span>by Paul Slade. F</span>eaturing interviews with Pace's descendants and over forty musicians, historians, writers, and musicologists, all of whom grapple with Pace’s enduring legacy.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Thank you to young Miles Francis and his family for bringing our Thanksgiving scene to life. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This episode features the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/May-We-Forever-Stand-National/dp/1469638606\">May We Forever Stand</a> written by Imani Perry, all about the Black National Anthem.</p>", "tease"=>"Lift Every Voice. Black Swan Records was first to record the anthem&nbsp;Lift Every Voice and Sing. From a family Thanksgiving, we portal through to the song's&nbsp;past, present and future.", "publishTime"=>1625860800000, "publish-at"=>"2021-07-09T16:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1625860800, "estimated-duration"=>1629, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/07/Vanishing_Harry_Pace_Shima_Jad_OSMAudio_LiftEvery_HfHrMkw.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/938/702/l/80/2021/07/Vanishing_Harry_Pace_Shima_Jad_OSMAudio_LiftEvery_HfHrMkw.png", "h"=>702, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.instagram.com/creativepowerr/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/07/Vanishing_Harry_Pace_Shima_Jad_OSMAudio_LiftEvery_HfHrMkw.png", "w"=>938, "id"=>196272, "credits-name"=>"Katia Herrera"}, "objectID"=>"1116873", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Vanishing of Harry Pace: Episode 6", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Lift Every Voice. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>Black Swan Records was first to record the anthem Lift Every Voice and Sing. From a family's Thanksgiving dinner, we portal through to the song's past, present, and future.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong><em>The Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> was created and produced by Shima Oliaee and Jad Abumrad. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This series was produced in collaboration with author Kiese Laymon, scholar Imani Perry, writer Cord Jefferson, WQXR’s Terrance McKnight, and WNYC's Jami Floyd. B<span>ased on the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swan-Blues-Americas-black-owned-ebook/dp/B00NJ2BS4S\">Black Swan Blues: the Hard Rise and Brutal Fall of America’s First Black Owned Record Label</a><span> </span>by Paul Slade. F</span>eaturing interviews with Pace's descendants and over forty musicians, historians, writers, and musicologists, all of whom grapple with Pace’s enduring legacy.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Thank you to young Miles Francis and his family for bringing our Thanksgiving scene to life. </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This episode features the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/May-We-Forever-Stand-National/dp/1469638606\">May We Forever Stand</a> written by Imani Perry, all about the Black National Anthem.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-07-09T16:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"vanishing-harry-pace-episode-5", "title"=>"The Vanishing of Harry Pace: Episode 5", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast061921_ep5.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1114268", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Roland Hayes and the Lost Generation. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Here’s the extraordinary story of Roland Hayes, another great (and largely forgotten) creator of new cosmologies.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong><em>The Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> was created and produced by Shima Oliaee and Jad Abumrad. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This series was produced in collaboration with author Kiese Laymon, scholar Imani Perry, writer Cord Jefferson, WQXR’s Terrance McKnight, and WNYC's Jami Floyd. B<span>ased on the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swan-Blues-Americas-black-owned-ebook/dp/B00NJ2BS4S\">Black Swan Blues: the Hard Rise and Brutal Fall of America’s First Black Owned Record Label</a><span> </span>by Paul Slade. F</span>eaturing interviews with Pace's descendants and over forty musicians, historians, writers, and musicologists, all of whom grapple with Pace’s enduring legacy.</p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember989\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1006\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<p class=\"p2\">This episode featured scenes from Christopher Brooks' and Robert Sims' biography,<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Roland-Hayes-Legacy-American-Tenor/dp/0253023815\">Roland Hayes: The Legacy of an American Tenor</a>. Thank you to actor William Jackson Harper for helping us bring Berlin to life. </p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span></span><span>This episode featured the following music:</span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Robert-Sings-Spirituals-Roland-Hayes/dp/B00T0MO39S\"><em>Robert</em><span> </span>Sims<em><span> </span>Sings the Spirituals of Roland Hayes</em></a></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UC__v1VlZU14V_tFL-bdVtHQ\"><span>Bill Doggett's collection of<span> </span><em>Black Swan</em><span> </span>records</span></a></em> </p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swans-Recordings-Classical-Performers/dp/B07YN7VB14\"><em>Black Swans: The First Recordings of Black Classical Music Performers</em></a><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UC__v1VlZU14V_tFL-bdVtHQ\"><span></span></a> </p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8Sibe38cQPQ\"><em>Du Bist Die Ruh<span> </span></em>by Roland Hayes<em> </em></a></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TFOsVxQ_SmY\"><span><em>Were You There</em><span> </span>by Roland Hayes </span></a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XHQ4EparUgc\"><em>Vesti La Giubba</em><span> </span>by Roland Hayes</a></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"Roland Hayes and the Lost Generation. This is the extraordinary&nbsp;story of the life of Roland Hayes, another&nbsp;great&nbsp;(and largely&nbsp;forgotten) creator of new cosmologies.", "publishTime"=>1625227200000, "publish-at"=>"2021-07-02T08:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1625220000, "estimated-duration"=>2543, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/07/Vanishing_of_Harry_Pace_Shima_Jad_OsmAudio.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1066/799/l/80/2021/07/Vanishing_of_Harry_Pace_Shima_Jad_OsmAudio.png", "h"=>799, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.instagram.com/creativepowerr/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/07/Vanishing_of_Harry_Pace_Shima_Jad_OsmAudio.png", "w"=>1066, "id"=>196183, "credits-name"=>"Katia Herrera"}, "objectID"=>"1114268", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Vanishing of Harry Pace: Episode 5", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Roland Hayes and the Lost Generation. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Here’s the extraordinary story of Roland Hayes, another great (and largely forgotten) creator of new cosmologies.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong><em>The Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> was created and produced by Shima Oliaee and Jad Abumrad. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This series was produced in collaboration with author Kiese Laymon, scholar Imani Perry, writer Cord Jefferson, WQXR’s Terrance McKnight, and WNYC's Jami Floyd. B<span>ased on the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swan-Blues-Americas-black-owned-ebook/dp/B00NJ2BS4S\">Black Swan Blues: the Hard Rise and Brutal Fall of America’s First Black Owned Record Label</a><span> </span>by Paul Slade. F</span>eaturing interviews with Pace's descendants and over forty musicians, historians, writers, and musicologists, all of whom grapple with Pace’s enduring legacy.</p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember989\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1006\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<p class=\"p2\">This episode featured scenes from Christopher Brooks' and Robert Sims' biography,<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Roland-Hayes-Legacy-American-Tenor/dp/0253023815\">Roland Hayes: The Legacy of an American Tenor</a>. Thank you to actor William Jackson Harper for helping us bring Berlin to life. </p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span></span><span>This episode featured the following music:</span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Robert-Sings-Spirituals-Roland-Hayes/dp/B00T0MO39S\"><em>Robert</em><span> </span>Sims<em><span> </span>Sings the Spirituals of Roland Hayes</em></a></span></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><em><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UC__v1VlZU14V_tFL-bdVtHQ\"><span>Bill Doggett's collection of<span> </span><em>Black Swan</em><span> </span>records</span></a></em> </p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swans-Recordings-Classical-Performers/dp/B07YN7VB14\"><em>Black Swans: The First Recordings of Black Classical Music Performers</em></a><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UC__v1VlZU14V_tFL-bdVtHQ\"><span></span></a> </p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8Sibe38cQPQ\"><em>Du Bist Die Ruh<span> </span></em>by Roland Hayes<em> </em></a></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TFOsVxQ_SmY\"><span><em>Were You There</em><span> </span>by Roland Hayes </span></a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XHQ4EparUgc\"><em>Vesti La Giubba</em><span> </span>by Roland Hayes</a></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-07-02T08:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"vanishing-harry-pace-episode-4", "title"=>"The Vanishing of Harry Pace: Episode 4", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast061921_ep4.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1114259", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Our Harlem Moon. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In this spin-off tale, Ethel Waters hijacks a degrading song and makes the music her own.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong><em>The Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> was created and produced by Shima Oliaee and Jad Abumrad. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This series was produced in collaboration with author Kiese Laymon, scholar Imani Perry, writer Cord Jefferson, WQXR’s Terrance McKnight, and WNYC's Jami Floyd. B<span>ased on the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swan-Blues-Americas-black-owned-ebook/dp/B00NJ2BS4S\">Black Swan Blues: the Hard Rise and Brutal Fall of America’s First Black Owned Record Label</a><span> </span>by Paul Slade. F</span>eaturing interviews with Pace's descendants and over forty musicians, historians, writers, and musicologists, all of whom grapple with Pace’s enduring legacy.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Thank you to our podcast friends at <a href=\"https://www.npr.org/podcasts/510333/throughline\">Throughline</a> for featuring our series on their show. Check out their feed for an exclusive behind-the-scenes interview about the series with Rund, Ramtin, Jad and Shima.</p>", "tease"=>"Our Harlem Moon. In this spin-off tale, Ethel Waters hijacks a degrading&nbsp;song&nbsp;and makes the&nbsp;music her own.", "publishTime"=>1624937400000, "publish-at"=>"2021-06-28T23:30:00", "date-line-ts"=>1624937400, "estimated-duration"=>754, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/06/HarryPace_Episode4_Jad_Shima_OsmAudio.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1330/998/l/80/2021/06/HarryPace_Episode4_Jad_Shima_OsmAudio.jpg", "h"=>998, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.instagram.com/creativepowerr/?hl=en", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/06/HarryPace_Episode4_Jad_Shima_OsmAudio.jpg", "w"=>1330, "id"=>196117, "credits-name"=>"Katia Herrera"}, "objectID"=>"1114259", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Vanishing of Harry Pace: Episode 4", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Our Harlem Moon. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">In this spin-off tale, Ethel Waters hijacks a degrading song and makes the music her own.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong><em>The Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> was created and produced by Shima Oliaee and Jad Abumrad. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This series was produced in collaboration with author Kiese Laymon, scholar Imani Perry, writer Cord Jefferson, WQXR’s Terrance McKnight, and WNYC's Jami Floyd. B<span>ased on the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swan-Blues-Americas-black-owned-ebook/dp/B00NJ2BS4S\">Black Swan Blues: the Hard Rise and Brutal Fall of America’s First Black Owned Record Label</a><span> </span>by Paul Slade. F</span>eaturing interviews with Pace's descendants and over forty musicians, historians, writers, and musicologists, all of whom grapple with Pace’s enduring legacy.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Thank you to our podcast friends at <a href=\"https://www.npr.org/podcasts/510333/throughline\">Throughline</a> for featuring our series on their show. Check out their feed for an exclusive behind-the-scenes interview about the series with Rund, Ramtin, Jad and Shima.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-06-28T23:30:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"vanishing-harry-pace-episode-3", "title"=>"The Vanishing of Harry Pace: Episode 3", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast061921_ep3.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1114257", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Black No More, White No More. </strong></p>\n<p><span>We follow Harry's grandkids and great grandkids as they grapple with his legacy in their own lives. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong><em>The Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> was created and produced by Shima Oliaee and Jad Abumrad. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This series was produced in collaboration with author Kiese Laymon, scholar Imani Perry, writer Cord Jefferson, WQXR’s Terrance McKnight, and WNYC's Jami Floyd. B<span>ased on the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swan-Blues-Americas-black-owned-ebook/dp/B00NJ2BS4S\">Black Swan Blues: the Hard Rise and Brutal Fall of America’s First Black Owned Record Label</a><span> </span>by Paul Slade. F</span>eaturing interviews with Pace's descendants and over forty musicians, historians, writers, and musicologists, all of whom grapple with Pace’s enduring legacy.</p>", "tease"=>"Black No More, White No More. We follow Harry's grandkids and great grandkids as they grapple with&nbsp;his legacy in their own lives.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1624583700000, "publish-at"=>"2021-06-24T21:15:00", "date-line-ts"=>1624670100, "estimated-duration"=>2490, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/06/HarryPace_Episode3_Jad_Shima_OsmAudio.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/l/80/2021/06/HarryPace_Episode3_Jad_Shima_OsmAudio.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.instagram.com/creativepowerr/?hl=en", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/06/HarryPace_Episode3_Jad_Shima_OsmAudio.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>196072, "credits-name"=>"Katia Herrera"}, "objectID"=>"1114257", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Vanishing of Harry Pace: Episode 3", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Black No More, White No More. </strong></p>\n<p><span>We follow Harry's grandkids and great grandkids as they grapple with his legacy in their own lives. </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong><em>The Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> was created and produced by Shima Oliaee and Jad Abumrad. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This series was produced in collaboration with author Kiese Laymon, scholar Imani Perry, writer Cord Jefferson, WQXR’s Terrance McKnight, and WNYC's Jami Floyd. B<span>ased on the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swan-Blues-Americas-black-owned-ebook/dp/B00NJ2BS4S\">Black Swan Blues: the Hard Rise and Brutal Fall of America’s First Black Owned Record Label</a><span> </span>by Paul Slade. F</span>eaturing interviews with Pace's descendants and over forty musicians, historians, writers, and musicologists, all of whom grapple with Pace’s enduring legacy.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-06-24T21:15:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"vanishing-harry-pace-episode-2", "title"=>"The Vanishing of Harry Pace: Episode 2", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast061821_ep2.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1114250", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Dreams Deferred. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The story of the post Black Swan years. We follow Harry’s Supreme Court battle to desegregate the South Side of Chicago, and then the mysterious decision which forces him into seclusion, before his untimely death.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong><em>The Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> was created and produced by Shima Oliaee and Jad Abumrad. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This series was produced in collaboration with author Kiese Laymon, scholar Imani Perry, writer Cord Jefferson, WQXR’s Terrance McKnight, and WNYC's Jami Floyd. B<span>ased on the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swan-Blues-Americas-black-owned-ebook/dp/B00NJ2BS4S\">Black Swan Blues: the Hard Rise and Brutal Fall of America’s First Black Owned Record Label</a><span> </span>by Paul Slade. F</span>eaturing interviews with Pace's descendants and over forty musicians, historians, writers, and musicologists, all of whom grapple with Pace’s enduring legacy.</p>", "tease"=>"Dreams Deferred. We follow Harry&rsquo;s Supreme Court battle to desegregate the South Side of Chicago, and then&nbsp;a&nbsp;mysterious decision which forces him into seclusion.", "publishTime"=>1624071600000, "publish-at"=>"2021-06-18T23:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1624071600, "estimated-duration"=>2581, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/06/HarryPace_Episode2_Jad_Shima_OsmAudio.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/l/80/2021/06/HarryPace_Episode2_Jad_Shima_OsmAudio.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.instagram.com/creativepowerr/?hl=en", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/06/HarryPace_Episode2_Jad_Shima_OsmAudio.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>195949, "credits-name"=>"Katia Herrera"}, "objectID"=>"1114250", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Vanishing of Harry Pace: Episode 2", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><strong>Dreams Deferred. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">The story of the post Black Swan years. We follow Harry’s Supreme Court battle to desegregate the South Side of Chicago, and then the mysterious decision which forces him into seclusion, before his untimely death.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong><em>The Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> was created and produced by Shima Oliaee and Jad Abumrad. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">This series was produced in collaboration with author Kiese Laymon, scholar Imani Perry, writer Cord Jefferson, WQXR’s Terrance McKnight, and WNYC's Jami Floyd. B<span>ased on the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swan-Blues-Americas-black-owned-ebook/dp/B00NJ2BS4S\">Black Swan Blues: the Hard Rise and Brutal Fall of America’s First Black Owned Record Label</a><span> </span>by Paul Slade. F</span>eaturing interviews with Pace's descendants and over forty musicians, historians, writers, and musicologists, all of whom grapple with Pace’s enduring legacy.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-06-18T23:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"vanishing-harry-pace-episode-1", "title"=>"The Vanishing of Harry Pace: Episode 1", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast061821_ep1.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1114243", "description"=>"<p class=\"p2\"><strong>The Rise and Fall of Black Swan. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span>It was Motown before Motown, FUBU before FUBU: Black Swan Records, the record company founded by Harry Pace.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong><em>The Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> was created and produced by Shima Oliaee and Jad Abumrad. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Harry Pace founded Black Swan Records exactly 100 years ago. Pace launched the career of Ethel Waters, inadvertently invented the term rock n roll, <span>played an important role in W.C. Handy becoming \"Father of the Blues,\" </span>inspired <em>Ebony</em> and <em>Jet</em> magazines, and <span>helped desegregate </span>the South Side of Chicago in an epic Supreme Court battle. Then, he disappeared.  The <em>Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> is a series about the phenomenal but forgotten man who changed the American music scene. It's a story about betrayal, family, hidden identities, and a time like no other.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">This series was produced in collaboration with author Kiese Laymon, scholar Imani Perry, screenwriter Cord Jefferson, and WQXR’s Terrance McKnight. Jami Floyd is our consulting producer; our fact checker is Natalie Meade. Peter Pace lent his voice for our readings. B<span>ased on the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swan-Blues-Americas-black-owned-ebook/dp/B00NJ2BS4S\">Black Swan Blues: the Hard Rise and Brutal Fall of America’s First Black Owned Record Label</a><span> </span>by Paul Slade. The series features</span> interviews with Pace's descendants and over forty musicians, historians, writers, and musicologists, all of whom grapple with Pace’s enduring legacy.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">This series is also a partnership with <a href=\"https://www.radiodiaries.org/\">Radio Diaries</a>.  </p>", "tease"=>"The Rise and Fall of Black Swan. It was Motown before Motown, FUBU before FUBU: Black Swan Records, the record company founded by Harry Pace.", "publishTime"=>1624006800000, "publish-at"=>"2021-06-18T05:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1624006800, "estimated-duration"=>3964, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/06/HarryPace_Episode1_Jad_Shima_OsmAudio.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/l/80/2021/06/HarryPace_Episode1_Jad_Shima_OsmAudio.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.instagram.com/creativepowerr/?hl=en", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/06/HarryPace_Episode1_Jad_Shima_OsmAudio.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>195945, "credits-name"=>"Katia Herrera"}, "objectID"=>"1114243", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Vanishing of Harry Pace: Episode 1", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p2\"><strong>The Rise and Fall of Black Swan. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p2\"><span>It was Motown before Motown, FUBU before FUBU: Black Swan Records, the record company founded by Harry Pace.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><strong><em>The Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> was created and produced by Shima Oliaee and Jad Abumrad. </strong></p>\n<p class=\"p2\">Harry Pace founded Black Swan Records exactly 100 years ago. Pace launched the career of Ethel Waters, inadvertently invented the term rock n roll, <span>played an important role in W.C. Handy becoming \"Father of the Blues,\" </span>inspired <em>Ebony</em> and <em>Jet</em> magazines, and <span>helped desegregate </span>the South Side of Chicago in an epic Supreme Court battle. Then, he disappeared.  The <em>Vanishing of Harry Pace</em> is a series about the phenomenal but forgotten man who changed the American music scene. It's a story about betrayal, family, hidden identities, and a time like no other.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">This series was produced in collaboration with author Kiese Laymon, scholar Imani Perry, screenwriter Cord Jefferson, and WQXR’s Terrance McKnight. Jami Floyd is our consulting producer; our fact checker is Natalie Meade. Peter Pace lent his voice for our readings. B<span>ased on the book <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Black-Swan-Blues-Americas-black-owned-ebook/dp/B00NJ2BS4S\">Black Swan Blues: the Hard Rise and Brutal Fall of America’s First Black Owned Record Label</a><span> </span>by Paul Slade. The series features</span> interviews with Pace's descendants and over forty musicians, historians, writers, and musicologists, all of whom grapple with Pace’s enduring legacy.</p>\n<p class=\"p2\">This series is also a partnership with <a href=\"https://www.radiodiaries.org/\">Radio Diaries</a>.  </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-06-18T05:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"breath", "title"=>"Breath", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21breath.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1113440", "description"=>"<p><span>We’ve just barely made it to the other side of a year that took our collective breaths away. So more than ever we felt that this was the time to go deep on life’s rhythmic dance partner. Today we huff and we puff through a whole stack of stories about breath. We talk to scientists, musicians, activists, and breath mint experts, and try to climb into the very center of this thing we all do, are all </span><em><span>doing</span></em><span> right now, and now, and now. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Annie McEwen, Matt Kielty, and Molly Webster.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>. <span> </span></span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></em></em></em></p>\n<p><em><br></em><span>Further reading: </span></p>\n<p><span>Alice Wong’s book </span><a href=\"https://disabilityvisibilityproject.com/book/\"><span>Disability Visibility: First-Person Stories From the 21st Century</span></a></p>\n<p><span>Here’s a </span><a href=\"https://disabilityvisibilityproject.com/2020/08/14/message-from-the-future-disabled-oracle-society/\"><span>speech</span></a><span> Alice gave when first referring to her body as an oracle. </span></p>\n<p><span>And for more on ventilator allocation in NY State, check out this article by the </span><a href=\"https://gothamist.com/news/pandemic-threatened-their-ventilators-will-ny-officials-change-course-next-crisis\"><span>Gothamist</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Stories of first ones, last ones, and a whole bunch in between.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1623449820000, "publish-at"=>"2021-06-11T18:17:00", "date-line-ts"=>1623449820, "estimated-duration"=>5463, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/06/2579085568_02d9eabc5e_o.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1280/800/c/80/2021/06/2579085568_02d9eabc5e_o.jpg", "h"=>800, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/06/2579085568_02d9eabc5e_o.jpg", "w"=>1280, "id"=>195890, "credits-name"=>"Lisa Shahno"}, "objectID"=>"1113440", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Breath", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>We’ve just barely made it to the other side of a year that took our collective breaths away. So more than ever we felt that this was the time to go deep on life’s rhythmic dance partner. Today we huff and we puff through a whole stack of stories about breath. We talk to scientists, musicians, activists, and breath mint experts, and try to climb into the very center of this thing we all do, are all </span><em><span>doing</span></em><span> right now, and now, and now. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Annie McEwen, Matt Kielty, and Molly Webster.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>. <span> </span></span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></em></em></em></p>\n<p><em><br></em><span>Further reading: </span></p>\n<p><span>Alice Wong’s book </span><a href=\"https://disabilityvisibilityproject.com/book/\"><span>Disability Visibility: First-Person Stories From the 21st Century</span></a></p>\n<p><span>Here’s a </span><a href=\"https://disabilityvisibilityproject.com/2020/08/14/message-from-the-future-disabled-oracle-society/\"><span>speech</span></a><span> Alice gave when first referring to her body as an oracle. </span></p>\n<p><span>And for more on ventilator allocation in NY State, check out this article by the </span><a href=\"https://gothamist.com/news/pandemic-threatened-their-ventilators-will-ny-officials-change-course-next-crisis\"><span>Gothamist</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-06-11T18:17:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"rhino-hunter", "title"=>"The Rhino Hunter", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21rhinohunterrerun.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=529748", "description"=>"<p>Back in 2014, Corey Knowlton paid $350,000 for a hunting trip to Namibia to shoot and kill an endangered species.  He’s a professional hunter, who guides hunts all around the world, so going to Africa would be nothing new.  The target on the other hand would be. And so too, he quickly found, would be the attention. </p>\n<p>This episode, producer Simon Adler follows Corey as he dodges death threats and prepares to pull the trigger.  Along the way we stop to talk with Namibian hunters and government officials, American activists, and someone who's been here before - Kenya’s former Director of Wildlife, Richard Leakey.   All the while, we try to uncover what conservation really means in the 21st century.</p>\n<p><em>Reported &amp; produced by Simon Adler with production help from Matthew Kielty.</em></p>\n<div><span><span><span><em>Special thanks to Chris Weaver, Ian Wallace, Mark Barrow, the Lindstrom family, and everyone at the Aru Game Lodge in Namibia.</em></span></span></span></div>\n<p><em><span>Thanks also to Sarah Fogel, Ray Crow, Barbara Clucus, and </span><span>Diogo Veríssimo.</span></em></p>\n<p><span><span><em><em><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>. <span> </span></span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></em></em> </span></span></p>", "tease"=>"When shooting and killing an endangered species might be the best way to save&nbsp;it. This episode contains strong language.", "publishTime"=>1622153340000, "publish-at"=>"2021-05-27T18:09:00", "date-line-ts"=>1622153340, "estimated-duration"=>3130, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/rhino.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/1/rhino.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/rhino.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>142633, "credits-name"=>"Image Credit: Andrea Mongia"}, "objectID"=>"529748", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Rhino Hunter", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Back in 2014, Corey Knowlton paid $350,000 for a hunting trip to Namibia to shoot and kill an endangered species.  He’s a professional hunter, who guides hunts all around the world, so going to Africa would be nothing new.  The target on the other hand would be. And so too, he quickly found, would be the attention. </p>\n<p>This episode, producer Simon Adler follows Corey as he dodges death threats and prepares to pull the trigger.  Along the way we stop to talk with Namibian hunters and government officials, American activists, and someone who's been here before - Kenya’s former Director of Wildlife, Richard Leakey.   All the while, we try to uncover what conservation really means in the 21st century.</p>\n<p><em>Reported &amp; produced by Simon Adler with production help from Matthew Kielty.</em></p>\n<div><span><span><span><em>Special thanks to Chris Weaver, Ian Wallace, Mark Barrow, the Lindstrom family, and everyone at the Aru Game Lodge in Namibia.</em></span></span></span></div>\n<p><em><span>Thanks also to Sarah Fogel, Ray Crow, Barbara Clucus, and </span><span>Diogo Veríssimo.</span></em></p>\n<p><span><span><em><em><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>. <span> </span></span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></em></em> </span></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-05-27T18:09:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dirty-drug-and-ice-cream-tub", "title"=>"The Dirty Drug and the Ice Cream Tub", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21dirtydrug.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1109152", "description"=>"<p><span>This episode, a tale of a wonder drug that will make you wonder about way more than just drugs.  </span></p>\n<p><span>Doctor-reporter Avir Mitra follows the epic and fantastical journey of a molecule dug out of a distant patch of dirt that would go on to make billions of dollars, prolong millions of lives, and teach us something fundamental we didn’t know about ourselves. Along the way, he meets a geriatric mouse named Ike, an immigrant dad who’s a little bit cool sometimes, a prophetic dream that prompts a thousand-mile journey, an ice cream container that may or may not be an accessory to international drug smuggling, and - most important of all - an obscure protein that’s calling the shots in every one of your cells RIGHT NOW.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Avir Mitra and was produced by Sarah Qari, Pat Walters, Suzie Lechtenberg, with help from Carin Leong and Rachael Cusick.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Richard Miller, Stuart Schreiber, Joanne Van Tilburg, and Bethany Halford.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>. <span> <br><br><em><span>**This episode was taped prior to the news that David Sabatini was fired from The Howard Hughes Medical Institute and prior to his resignation from the Whitehead Institute. More information about Sabatini’s alleged misconduct and the investigation into his behavior can be found<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.sciencemag.org/news/2021/08/hhmi-fires-prominent-biologist-sexual-harassment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" title=\"https://www.sciencemag.org/news/2021/08/hhmi-fires-prominent-biologist-sexual-harassment\" data-linkindex=\"2\">here</a>.</span></em><br></span></span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"This episode, a tale of a wonder drug that will make you wonder about way more than just drugs.", "publishTime"=>1621598280000, "publish-at"=>"2021-05-21T07:58:00", "date-line-ts"=>1621598280, "estimated-duration"=>2783, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/05/rapa.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/c/80/2021/05/rapa.jpg", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/05/rapa.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>195650, "credits-name"=>"Sergio Rapu"}, "objectID"=>"1109152", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Dirty Drug and the Ice Cream Tub", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This episode, a tale of a wonder drug that will make you wonder about way more than just drugs.  </span></p>\n<p><span>Doctor-reporter Avir Mitra follows the epic and fantastical journey of a molecule dug out of a distant patch of dirt that would go on to make billions of dollars, prolong millions of lives, and teach us something fundamental we didn’t know about ourselves. Along the way, he meets a geriatric mouse named Ike, an immigrant dad who’s a little bit cool sometimes, a prophetic dream that prompts a thousand-mile journey, an ice cream container that may or may not be an accessory to international drug smuggling, and - most important of all - an obscure protein that’s calling the shots in every one of your cells RIGHT NOW.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Avir Mitra and was produced by Sarah Qari, Pat Walters, Suzie Lechtenberg, with help from Carin Leong and Rachael Cusick.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Richard Miller, Stuart Schreiber, Joanne Van Tilburg, and Bethany Halford.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>. <span> <br><br><em><span>**This episode was taped prior to the news that David Sabatini was fired from The Howard Hughes Medical Institute and prior to his resignation from the Whitehead Institute. More information about Sabatini’s alleged misconduct and the investigation into his behavior can be found<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.sciencemag.org/news/2021/08/hhmi-fires-prominent-biologist-sexual-harassment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" title=\"https://www.sciencemag.org/news/2021/08/hhmi-fires-prominent-biologist-sexual-harassment\" data-linkindex=\"2\">here</a>.</span></em><br></span></span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-05-21T07:58:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"brown-box", "title"=>"Brown Box", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21brownbox.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=344234", "description"=>"<p>You order some stuff on the Internet and it shows up three hours later. How could all the things that need to happen to make that happen happen so fast?</p>\n<p> </p><p>It used to be, when you ordered something on the Internet, you waited a week for it to show up. That was the deal: you didn’t have to get off the couch, but you had to wait. But in the last few years, that’s changed. Now, increasingly, the stuff we buy on the Internet shows up the next day or the same day, sometimes within hours. Free shipping included. Which got us wondering: How is this Internet voodoo possible?</p>\n<p>A fleet of robots? Vacuum tubes? Teleportation? Hardly. In this short, reporter <a href=\"http://gabrielmac.com/\">Gabriel Mac</a> travels into the belly of the beast that is the Internet retail system, and what he finds takes his breath away and makes him weak in the knees (in the worst way). Producer Pat Walters and <a href=\"http://www.businessweek.com/authors/411-brad-stone\">Brad Stone, author of <em>The Everything Store</em></a>, a book about Amazon.com, assist.</p>\n<p>*****This podcast contains some language and subject matter that might not be appropriate for young listeners******</p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"It used to be, when you ordered something on the Internet, you waited a week for it to show up.&nbsp;&nbsp;How is this Internet voodoo possible?", "publishTime"=>1620947940000, "publish-at"=>"2021-05-13T19:19:00", "date-line-ts"=>1620948060, "estimated-duration"=>1714, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/399400066_af8ec28547_z.jpg", "source"=>{"url"=>"http://www.flickr.com/", "name"=>"flickr"}, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/640/427/l/80/1/399400066_af8ec28547_z.jpg", "h"=>427, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/399400066_af8ec28547_z.jpg", "w"=>640, "id"=>111303, "credits-name"=>"Jerry Bowley"}, "objectID"=>"344234", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Brown Box", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>You order some stuff on the Internet and it shows up three hours later. How could all the things that need to happen to make that happen happen so fast?</p>\n<p> </p><p>It used to be, when you ordered something on the Internet, you waited a week for it to show up. That was the deal: you didn’t have to get off the couch, but you had to wait. But in the last few years, that’s changed. Now, increasingly, the stuff we buy on the Internet shows up the next day or the same day, sometimes within hours. Free shipping included. Which got us wondering: How is this Internet voodoo possible?</p>\n<p>A fleet of robots? Vacuum tubes? Teleportation? Hardly. In this short, reporter <a href=\"http://gabrielmac.com/\">Gabriel Mac</a> travels into the belly of the beast that is the Internet retail system, and what he finds takes his breath away and makes him weak in the knees (in the worst way). Producer Pat Walters and <a href=\"http://www.businessweek.com/authors/411-brad-stone\">Brad Stone, author of <em>The Everything Store</em></a>, a book about Amazon.com, assist.</p>\n<p>*****This podcast contains some language and subject matter that might not be appropriate for young listeners******</p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-05-13T19:19:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"kleptotherms", "title"=>"Kleptotherms", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21kleptotherms.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1105854", "description"=>"<p><span>In this episode, w<span>e break the thermometer watch the mercury spill out as we discover temperature is far stranger than it seems. Five stories that run the gamut from snakes to stars. </span>We start out underwater, with a snake that has evolved a devious trick for keeping warm. Then we hear the tale of a young man whose seemingly simple method of warming up might be the very thing making him cold. And Senior Correspondent Molly Webster blows the lid off the idea that 98.6 degrees Farenheight is a sound marker of health. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Lulu Miller and Molly Webster and was produced by Lulu Miller, Molly Webster, and Becca Bressler.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.</span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></span></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"We&nbsp;break the thermometer watch the mercury spill out as we discover&nbsp;temperature is far stranger than it seems. Five stories that run the gamut from snakes to stars.", "publishTime"=>1620225022000, "publish-at"=>"2021-05-05T10:30:22", "date-line-ts"=>1620225022, "estimated-duration"=>2646, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/05/6375885729_b5ac95b260_o.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/318/237/l/80/2021/05/6375885729_b5ac95b260_o.jpg", "h"=>237, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/05/6375885729_b5ac95b260_o.jpg", "w"=>318, "id"=>195473, "credits-name"=>"yellowcloud/Flickr"}, "objectID"=>"1105854", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Kleptotherms", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In this episode, w<span>e break the thermometer watch the mercury spill out as we discover temperature is far stranger than it seems. Five stories that run the gamut from snakes to stars. </span>We start out underwater, with a snake that has evolved a devious trick for keeping warm. Then we hear the tale of a young man whose seemingly simple method of warming up might be the very thing making him cold. And Senior Correspondent Molly Webster blows the lid off the idea that 98.6 degrees Farenheight is a sound marker of health. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Lulu Miller and Molly Webster and was produced by Lulu Miller, Molly Webster, and Becca Bressler.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.</span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></span></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-05-05T10:30:22", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"deep-cuts", "title"=>"Deep Cuts", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_/radiolab_21podcastdeepcuts.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1103026", "description"=>"<p><span>Today, Lulu and Latif talk about some of their favorite episodes from Radiolab’s past that hold new power today.  </span></p>\n<p><span>Lulu points to an episode from 2008: </span></p>\n<p><em><span>Imagine that you're a composer. Imagine getting the commission to write a song that will allow family members to face the death of a loved one. Well, composer </span></em><a href=\"http://www.bangonacan.org/about_us/david_lang\"><em><span>David Lang</span></em></a><em><span> had to do just that when a hospital in Garches, France, asked him to write music for their morgue, or </span></em><a href=\"http://www.bbc.co.uk/radio4/science/lastgoodbye.shtml\"><em><span>'Salle Des Departs.'</span></em></a><em><span> What do you do? This piece was produced by </span></em><a href=\"http://www.strangemusic.com/JGonzales.htm\"><em><span>Jocelyn Gonzales</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p><span>And Latif talks about an episode Jad made in 2009. Here’s how we described it back then:</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Jad--a brand new father--wonders what's going on inside the head of his baby Amil.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>(And don't worry, you don't need kids to enjoy this podcast.) The questions here are big: what is it like to be so brand new to the world? None of us have memories from this time, so how could we possibly ever know? Is it just chaos? Or, is there something more, some understanding from the very beginning? Jad found a development psychologist named </span></em><a href=\"http://www.charlesfernyhough.com/\"><em><span>Charles Fernyhough</span></em></a><em><span> to explore some of his questions.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"This week, we dive into the past for some special stories that hold new power today.", "publishTime"=>1619128800000, "publish-at"=>"2021-04-22T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1619128800, "estimated-duration"=>1450, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/04/Amil2.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/1066/l/80/2021/04/Amil2.jpeg", "h"=>1066, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/04/Amil2.jpeg", "w"=>800, "id"=>195277, "credits-name"=>"Jad Abumrad"}, "objectID"=>"1103026", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Deep Cuts", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Today, Lulu and Latif talk about some of their favorite episodes from Radiolab’s past that hold new power today.  </span></p>\n<p><span>Lulu points to an episode from 2008: </span></p>\n<p><em><span>Imagine that you're a composer. Imagine getting the commission to write a song that will allow family members to face the death of a loved one. Well, composer </span></em><a href=\"http://www.bangonacan.org/about_us/david_lang\"><em><span>David Lang</span></em></a><em><span> had to do just that when a hospital in Garches, France, asked him to write music for their morgue, or </span></em><a href=\"http://www.bbc.co.uk/radio4/science/lastgoodbye.shtml\"><em><span>'Salle Des Departs.'</span></em></a><em><span> What do you do? This piece was produced by </span></em><a href=\"http://www.strangemusic.com/JGonzales.htm\"><em><span>Jocelyn Gonzales</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p><span>And Latif talks about an episode Jad made in 2009. Here’s how we described it back then:</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Jad--a brand new father--wonders what's going on inside the head of his baby Amil.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>(And don't worry, you don't need kids to enjoy this podcast.) The questions here are big: what is it like to be so brand new to the world? None of us have memories from this time, so how could we possibly ever know? Is it just chaos? Or, is there something more, some understanding from the very beginning? Jad found a development psychologist named </span></em><a href=\"http://www.charlesfernyhough.com/\"><em><span>Charles Fernyhough</span></em></a><em><span> to explore some of his questions.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-04-22T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"septendecennial-sing-along", "title"=>"The Septendecennial Sing-Along", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21bugmusic.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=292987", "description"=>"<p>Every 17 years, a deafening sex orchestra hits the East Coast -- billions and billions of cicadas crawl out of the ground, sing their hearts out, then mate and die. In this short, Jad and Robert talk to a man who gets inside that noise to dissect its meaning and musical components.</p><p>While most of us hear a wall of white noise, squeaks, and squawks....David Rothenberg hears a symphony. He's trained his ear to listen for the music of animals, and he's always looking for chances to join in, with everything from lonely birds to giant whales to swarming cicadas.</p>\n<p>In this podcast, David explains his urge to connect and sing along, and helps break down the mysterious life cycle and mating rituals of the periodical cicadas into something we can all relate to.</p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/david_rothenberg_playing_cicadas_bug_music.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p><em>David Rothenberg making music with the cicadas. <a href=\"http://www.bugmusicbook.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Courtesy of David Rothenberg/Bug Music</a></em></p>\n<p>A visual breakdown of the cicada mating calls:</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/cooley_marshall_cicada_calls.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"><a href=\"http://www.bugmusicbook.com/\" target=\"_blank\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Courtesy of John Cooley and David Marshall at UConn</em>. <em>For more on cicada mating calls, take a look at this <a href=\"http://www.magicicada.org/cooley/reprints/Cooley_Marshall_2001.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">paper from Cooley and Marshall</a>. </em></p>\n<p>A close-up of cicadas getting down:</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/cicada_sex_bug_music.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.bugmusicbook.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Courtesy of David Rothenberg/Bug Music</em></a></p>\n<p>Enjoy a free download of our favorite track from David's CD <a href=\"http://www.bugmusicbook.com/#!music/czas\" target=\"_blank\">Bug Music</a> -- here's the description from the liner notes:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Katydid Prehistory: Named in honor of Archaboilus musicus, the 165 million year old prehistoric katydid, whose fossil remains reveal an ability to sing distinct pitches.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><div class=\"inline_audioplayer_wrapper inline_audioplayer_embedded_wrapper\"><h3>Katydid Prehistory</h3><iframe frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" height=\"54\" width=\"100%\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/widgets/ondemand_player/wnyc/#file=http://audio4.wnyc.org/rl_extras/rl_extras051413katydid.mp3&share=1\"></iframe></div></p>", "tease"=>"While most of us hear a wall of white noise, squeaks, and squawks....one man hears a symphony.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1618526880000, "publish-at"=>"2021-04-15T18:48:00", "date-line-ts"=>1618526880, "estimated-duration"=>1176, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Cicadas", "name"=>"1/cicadas_bug_music_web.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1473/1500/c/80/1/cicadas_bug_music_web.jpg", "h"=>1500, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.bugmusicbook.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/cicadas_bug_music_web.jpg", "w"=>1473, "id"=>97276, "credits-name"=>"Courtesy of David Rothenberg / Bug Music"}, "objectID"=>"292987", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Septendecennial Sing-Along", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Every 17 years, a deafening sex orchestra hits the East Coast -- billions and billions of cicadas crawl out of the ground, sing their hearts out, then mate and die. In this short, Jad and Robert talk to a man who gets inside that noise to dissect its meaning and musical components.</p><p>While most of us hear a wall of white noise, squeaks, and squawks....David Rothenberg hears a symphony. He's trained his ear to listen for the music of animals, and he's always looking for chances to join in, with everything from lonely birds to giant whales to swarming cicadas.</p>\n<p>In this podcast, David explains his urge to connect and sing along, and helps break down the mysterious life cycle and mating rituals of the periodical cicadas into something we can all relate to.</p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/david_rothenberg_playing_cicadas_bug_music.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p><em>David Rothenberg making music with the cicadas. <a href=\"http://www.bugmusicbook.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Courtesy of David Rothenberg/Bug Music</a></em></p>\n<p>A visual breakdown of the cicada mating calls:</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/cooley_marshall_cicada_calls.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"><a href=\"http://www.bugmusicbook.com/\" target=\"_blank\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Courtesy of John Cooley and David Marshall at UConn</em>. <em>For more on cicada mating calls, take a look at this <a href=\"http://www.magicicada.org/cooley/reprints/Cooley_Marshall_2001.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">paper from Cooley and Marshall</a>. </em></p>\n<p>A close-up of cicadas getting down:</p>\n<p><img src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/raw/1/cicada_sex_bug_music.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"620\"></p>\n<p><a href=\"http://www.bugmusicbook.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Courtesy of David Rothenberg/Bug Music</em></a></p>\n<p>Enjoy a free download of our favorite track from David's CD <a href=\"http://www.bugmusicbook.com/#!music/czas\" target=\"_blank\">Bug Music</a> -- here's the description from the liner notes:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Katydid Prehistory: Named in honor of Archaboilus musicus, the 165 million year old prehistoric katydid, whose fossil remains reveal an ability to sing distinct pitches.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><div class=\"inline_audioplayer_wrapper inline_audioplayer_embedded_wrapper\"><h3>Katydid Prehistory</h3><iframe frameborder=\"0\" scrolling=\"no\" height=\"54\" width=\"100%\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/widgets/ondemand_player/wnyc/#file=http://audio4.wnyc.org/rl_extras/rl_extras051413katydid.mp3&share=1\"></iframe></div></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-04-15T18:48:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"what-holmes", "title"=>"What Up Holmes? ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21whatupholmes.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1098264", "description"=>"<p><span>Love it or hate it, the freedom to say obnoxious and subversive things is the quintessence of what makes America America. But our say-almost-anything approach to free speech is actually relatively recent, and you can trace it back to one guy: a Supreme Court justice named Oliver Wendell Holmes. Even weirder, you can trace it back to one seemingly ordinary 8-month period in Holmes’s life when he seems to have done a logical U-turn on what should be say-able.  Why he changed his mind during those 8 months is one of the greatest mysteries in the history of the Supreme Court.  (Spoiler: the answer involves anarchists, a house of truth, and a cry for help from a dear friend.)  Join us as we investigate why he changed his mind, how that made the country change its mind, and whether it’s now time to change our minds again.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and was produced by Sarah Qari.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Jenny Lawton, Soren Shade, Kelsey Padgett, <span>Mahyad Tousi</span> and <span>Soroush Vosughi.</span></em></p>\n<p><span><em><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.   </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></em></span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span>further reading:</span></p>\n<p><span>Thomas Healy’s book </span><a href=\"https://us.macmillan.com/books/9781250058690\"><span>The Great Dissent: How Oliver Wendell Holmes CHanged His Mind - And Changed the History of Free Speech In America</span></a><span> (the inspiration for this episode) plus his latest book </span><a href=\"https://us.macmillan.com/books/9781627798624\"><span>Soul City: Race, Equality and the Lost Dream of an American Utopia</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><span>The </span><em><span>Science</span></em><span> article that Sinan Aral wrote in 2018, along with Soroush Vosughi and Deb Roy: </span><a href=\"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/359/6380/1146\"><span>“The Spread of True and False News Online”</span></a></p>\n<p><span>Sinan Aral’s recent book </span><a href=\"https://www.sinanaral.io/books\"><span>The Hype Machine: How Social Media Disrupts Our Elections, Our Economy and our Health - And How We Must Adapt</span></a></p>\n<p><span>Zeynep Tufekci’s newsletter “</span><a href=\"https://zeynep.substack.com/\"><span>The Insight</span></a><span>” plus her book </span><a href=\"https://www.twitterandteargas.org/\"><span>Twitter and Teargas: The Power and Fragility of Networked Protest</span></a></p>\n<p><span>Nabiha Syed’s news website </span><a href=\"https://themarkup.org/\"><span>The Markup</span></a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0EADzGGjuqI\"><span>Trailer</span></a><span> for “The Magnificent Yankee,” a 1950 biopic of Oliver Wendell Holmes</span></p>\n<p><span>Anthony Lewis, </span><a href=\"https://www.basicbooks.com/titles/anthony-lewis/freedom-for-the-thought-that-we-hate/9780465012930/\"><span>Freedom for the Thought that We Hate: A Biography of the First Amendment</span></a></p>", "tease"=>"Today, a Supreme Court mystery.&nbsp; How one judge changed his mind about free speech, how that changed all the rest of our minds, and whether it&rsquo;s time to change our minds again.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1617347340000, "publish-at"=>"2021-04-02T03:09:00", "date-line-ts"=>1617347340, "estimated-duration"=>2894, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/04/holmesphoto.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/457/467/c/80/2021/04/holmesphoto.jpg", "h"=>467, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/04/holmesphoto.jpg", "w"=>457, "id"=>195018, "credits-name"=>"United States Holocaust Memorial Museum Collection, Gift of David and Zelda Silberman"}, "objectID"=>"1098264", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"What Up Holmes? ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Love it or hate it, the freedom to say obnoxious and subversive things is the quintessence of what makes America America. But our say-almost-anything approach to free speech is actually relatively recent, and you can trace it back to one guy: a Supreme Court justice named Oliver Wendell Holmes. Even weirder, you can trace it back to one seemingly ordinary 8-month period in Holmes’s life when he seems to have done a logical U-turn on what should be say-able.  Why he changed his mind during those 8 months is one of the greatest mysteries in the history of the Supreme Court.  (Spoiler: the answer involves anarchists, a house of truth, and a cry for help from a dear friend.)  Join us as we investigate why he changed his mind, how that made the country change its mind, and whether it’s now time to change our minds again.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and was produced by Sarah Qari.</em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Jenny Lawton, Soren Shade, Kelsey Padgett, <span>Mahyad Tousi</span> and <span>Soroush Vosughi.</span></em></p>\n<p><span><em><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.   </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></em></span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span>further reading:</span></p>\n<p><span>Thomas Healy’s book </span><a href=\"https://us.macmillan.com/books/9781250058690\"><span>The Great Dissent: How Oliver Wendell Holmes CHanged His Mind - And Changed the History of Free Speech In America</span></a><span> (the inspiration for this episode) plus his latest book </span><a href=\"https://us.macmillan.com/books/9781627798624\"><span>Soul City: Race, Equality and the Lost Dream of an American Utopia</span></a><span>.</span></p>\n<p><span>The </span><em><span>Science</span></em><span> article that Sinan Aral wrote in 2018, along with Soroush Vosughi and Deb Roy: </span><a href=\"https://science.sciencemag.org/content/359/6380/1146\"><span>“The Spread of True and False News Online”</span></a></p>\n<p><span>Sinan Aral’s recent book </span><a href=\"https://www.sinanaral.io/books\"><span>The Hype Machine: How Social Media Disrupts Our Elections, Our Economy and our Health - And How We Must Adapt</span></a></p>\n<p><span>Zeynep Tufekci’s newsletter “</span><a href=\"https://zeynep.substack.com/\"><span>The Insight</span></a><span>” plus her book </span><a href=\"https://www.twitterandteargas.org/\"><span>Twitter and Teargas: The Power and Fragility of Networked Protest</span></a></p>\n<p><span>Nabiha Syed’s news website </span><a href=\"https://themarkup.org/\"><span>The Markup</span></a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0EADzGGjuqI\"><span>Trailer</span></a><span> for “The Magnificent Yankee,” a 1950 biopic of Oliver Wendell Holmes</span></p>\n<p><span>Anthony Lewis, </span><a href=\"https://www.basicbooks.com/titles/anthony-lewis/freedom-for-the-thought-that-we-hate/9780465012930/\"><span>Freedom for the Thought that We Hate: A Biography of the First Amendment</span></a></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-04-02T03:09:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"elements", "title"=>"Elements", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21elementsrerunfix.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=524435", "description"=>"<p><span>Scientists took about 300 years to lay out the Periodic Table into neat rows and columns. In one hour, we’re going to mess it all up.  This episode, we enlist journalists, poets, musicians, and even a physicist to help us tell stories of matter that matters. You’ll never look at that chart the same way again.</span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to <a href=\"http://emotivefruition.org/\">Emotive Fruition</a> for organizing poetry performances and to the mighty <a href=\"http://www.sylvanesso.com/\">Sylvan Esso</a> for composing 'Jaime's Song', both inspired by this episode.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Thanks also to Sam Kean, Chris Howk, Brian Fields and to Paul Dresher and Ned Rothenberg for the use of their song \"<a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Untold-Story-The-Edge-Sleep/dp/B0045EDG6M\">Untold Story:The Edge of Sleep\"</a>. </span></em></p>\n<p><em>Check out Jaime Lowe's book <a href=\"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/538318/mental-by-jaime-lowe/9780399574498/\">Mental: Lithium, Love and Losing My Mind</a></em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.   </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"Scientists took about 300 years to lay out the Periodic Table into neat rows and columns. In one hour, we&rsquo;re going to mess it all up. &nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1616709600000, "publish-at"=>"2021-03-25T18:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1616688000, "estimated-duration"=>4416, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/RADIOLAB_ELEMENTS_E3.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/550/525/c/80/1/RADIOLAB_ELEMENTS_E3.jpg", "h"=>525, "is-display"=>false, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/RADIOLAB_ELEMENTS_E3.jpg", "w"=>550, "id"=>142075, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Jamie York"}, "objectID"=>"524435", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Elements", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Scientists took about 300 years to lay out the Periodic Table into neat rows and columns. In one hour, we’re going to mess it all up.  This episode, we enlist journalists, poets, musicians, and even a physicist to help us tell stories of matter that matters. You’ll never look at that chart the same way again.</span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to <a href=\"http://emotivefruition.org/\">Emotive Fruition</a> for organizing poetry performances and to the mighty <a href=\"http://www.sylvanesso.com/\">Sylvan Esso</a> for composing 'Jaime's Song', both inspired by this episode.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Thanks also to Sam Kean, Chris Howk, Brian Fields and to Paul Dresher and Ned Rothenberg for the use of their song \"<a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/Untold-Story-The-Edge-Sleep/dp/B0045EDG6M\">Untold Story:The Edge of Sleep\"</a>. </span></em></p>\n<p><em>Check out Jaime Lowe's book <a href=\"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/538318/mental-by-jaime-lowe/9780399574498/\">Mental: Lithium, Love and Losing My Mind</a></em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.   </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-03-25T18:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"escapescape", "title"=>"Escapescape", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21escapescape.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1095959", "description"=>"<p><span>As we hit the one year mark since the first U.S. state (California) issued a stay-at-home order to prevent the spread of COVID-19, we put out a call to see if any of you would take us to your secret escape spot and record audio there.</span></p>\n<p><span>And you astounded us with what you brought in. </span></p>\n<p><span>In this soundrich, kaleidoscopic episode, we journey around the planet and then, quite literally, beyond it. Listen only if you want a boatload of fresh air, fields of wildflowers, stars, birds, frogs, and a </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/segments/187718-edge-heavens\"><span>riveting tale</span></a><span> involving Isaac Newton and a calm beyond any calm you knew could exist.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty and Lulu Miller, with production support from Jonny Moens and Suzie Lechtenberg. </em></p>\n<p><span><em>Special thanks to:</em><br></span></p>\n<p><em>Lynn Levy, who went on to host the space-a-licious series, <a href=\"https://gimletmedia.com/shows/the-habitat\">The Habitat</a>, and edit (among other things) the powerful and beautiful new podcast <a href=\"https://gimletmedia.com/shows/resistance\">Resistance</a>.<br></em></p>\n<p><em>Merav Opher, an astronomy professor at BU, who now directs the <a href=\"http://sites.bu.edu/shield-drive/\">SHIELD DRIVE Science Center</a> which is studying the data collected by the Voyagers at the edge of the heavens, or--err, the “heliosphere” as the scientists call it.</em></p>\n<p><em>Edward Dolnick,<a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/006171951X%20/radiolabbooks-20/\"> The Clockwork Universe: Isaac Newton, the Royal Society, and the Birth of the Modern World</a></em></p>\n<p><em>Ann Druyan, one of the creators of the 1977 Golden Album traveling on the Voyager probe, has recently released a new series on National Geographic,  “<a href=\"https://www.geekwire.com/2020/qa-cosmos-author-ann-druyan-muses-possible-worlds-carl-sagan/\">Cosmos: Possible Worlds</a>”</em></p>\n<p><em>A.J. Dungo, who submitted a postcard while surfing, is author of the mesmerizing graphic novel, <a href=\"https://nobrow.net/shop/in-waves/\">In Waves</a>, a memoir about surfing and grief.</em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.   </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"To ease the claustrophobia of our locked-down lives, we put out the call to listeners to take us to their secret escape spots. What rolled in was a mesmerizing journey around the planet.", "publishTime"=>1616123160000, "publish-at"=>"2021-03-18T23:06:00", "date-line-ts"=>1616123160, "estimated-duration"=>1939, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/03/alice-donovan-rouse-pZ61ZA8QgcY-unsplash.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1241/c/80/2021/03/alice-donovan-rouse-pZ61ZA8QgcY-unsplash.jpg", "h"=>1241, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/03/alice-donovan-rouse-pZ61ZA8QgcY-unsplash.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>194831, "credits-name"=>"Alice Donovan Rouse"}, "objectID"=>"1095959", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Escapescape", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>As we hit the one year mark since the first U.S. state (California) issued a stay-at-home order to prevent the spread of COVID-19, we put out a call to see if any of you would take us to your secret escape spot and record audio there.</span></p>\n<p><span>And you astounded us with what you brought in. </span></p>\n<p><span>In this soundrich, kaleidoscopic episode, we journey around the planet and then, quite literally, beyond it. Listen only if you want a boatload of fresh air, fields of wildflowers, stars, birds, frogs, and a </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/segments/187718-edge-heavens\"><span>riveting tale</span></a><span> involving Isaac Newton and a calm beyond any calm you knew could exist.</span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was produced by Matt Kielty and Lulu Miller, with production support from Jonny Moens and Suzie Lechtenberg. </em></p>\n<p><span><em>Special thanks to:</em><br></span></p>\n<p><em>Lynn Levy, who went on to host the space-a-licious series, <a href=\"https://gimletmedia.com/shows/the-habitat\">The Habitat</a>, and edit (among other things) the powerful and beautiful new podcast <a href=\"https://gimletmedia.com/shows/resistance\">Resistance</a>.<br></em></p>\n<p><em>Merav Opher, an astronomy professor at BU, who now directs the <a href=\"http://sites.bu.edu/shield-drive/\">SHIELD DRIVE Science Center</a> which is studying the data collected by the Voyagers at the edge of the heavens, or--err, the “heliosphere” as the scientists call it.</em></p>\n<p><em>Edward Dolnick,<a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/006171951X%20/radiolabbooks-20/\"> The Clockwork Universe: Isaac Newton, the Royal Society, and the Birth of the Modern World</a></em></p>\n<p><em>Ann Druyan, one of the creators of the 1977 Golden Album traveling on the Voyager probe, has recently released a new series on National Geographic,  “<a href=\"https://www.geekwire.com/2020/qa-cosmos-author-ann-druyan-muses-possible-worlds-carl-sagan/\">Cosmos: Possible Worlds</a>”</em></p>\n<p><em>A.J. Dungo, who submitted a postcard while surfing, is author of the mesmerizing graphic novel, <a href=\"https://nobrow.net/shop/in-waves/\">In Waves</a>, a memoir about surfing and grief.</em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.   </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-03-18T23:06:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dispatch-14-covid-crystal-ball", "title"=>"Dispatch 14: Covid Crystal Ball", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21covidcrystalballfix.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1094616", "description"=>"<p><span>Last summer, at a hospital in England, a man in his 70s being treated for complications with cancer tested positive for covid-19. He had lymphoma, and the disease plus his drugs weakened his immune system, making him particularly susceptible to the virus. He wasn’t too bad off, considering, and was sent home. That was Day 1. This is the story of what the doctors witnessed, over the course of his illness: the evolution of covid-19 inside his body. Before their eyes, they get a hint of what might be to come in the pandemic. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Molly Webster. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Ravindra Gupta, Jonathan Li.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.   </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span>Want to learn more about some of the covid case studies? Here are a couple papers to get you started:</span><span><br></span><span><br></span><span>The “U.K. Paper”, co-authored by Ravi Gupta, one of our sources for the episode:</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03291-y\"><span>https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03291-y</span></a></p>\n<p>A case study out of Boston, co-authored by Dr. Jonathan Li, one of our sources for the episode:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMc2031364\"><span>https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMc2031364</span></a></p>\n<p>For more on immune suppression and covid-19, check out this amazing Scientific American article: </p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/covid-variants-may-arise-in-people-with-compromised-immune-systems/\"><span>https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/covid-variants-may-arise-in-people-with-compromised-immune-systems/</span></a></p>", "tease"=>"On today&rsquo;s episode, doctors witness the worldwide pandemic playing out in a single human body.", "publishTime"=>1615529160000, "publish-at"=>"2021-03-12T01:06:00", "date-line-ts"=>1615529160, "estimated-duration"=>1634, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/03/covidcrystalball.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1500/1000/c/80/2021/03/covidcrystalball.jpeg", "h"=>1000, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/03/covidcrystalball.jpeg", "w"=>1500, "id"=>194717, "credits-name"=>"CDC"}, "objectID"=>"1094616", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Dispatch 14: Covid Crystal Ball", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Last summer, at a hospital in England, a man in his 70s being treated for complications with cancer tested positive for covid-19. He had lymphoma, and the disease plus his drugs weakened his immune system, making him particularly susceptible to the virus. He wasn’t too bad off, considering, and was sent home. That was Day 1. This is the story of what the doctors witnessed, over the course of his illness: the evolution of covid-19 inside his body. Before their eyes, they get a hint of what might be to come in the pandemic. </span></p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Molly Webster. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Ravindra Gupta, Jonathan Li.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.   </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span>Want to learn more about some of the covid case studies? Here are a couple papers to get you started:</span><span><br></span><span><br></span><span>The “U.K. Paper”, co-authored by Ravi Gupta, one of our sources for the episode:</span></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03291-y\"><span>https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03291-y</span></a></p>\n<p>A case study out of Boston, co-authored by Dr. Jonathan Li, one of our sources for the episode:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMc2031364\"><span>https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMc2031364</span></a></p>\n<p>For more on immune suppression and covid-19, check out this amazing Scientific American article: </p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/covid-variants-may-arise-in-people-with-compromised-immune-systems/\"><span>https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/covid-variants-may-arise-in-people-with-compromised-immune-systems/</span></a></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-03-12T01:06:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"ceremony", "title"=>"The Ceremony ", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21theceremony.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=780552", "description"=>"<p>In November of 2016, journalist Morgen Peck showed up at her friend Molly Webster's apartment in Brooklyn, told her to take her battery out of her phone, and began to tell her about The Ceremony, a moment last fall when a group of, well, let's just call them wizards, came together in an undisclosed location to launch a new currency. It's an undertaking that involves some of the most elaborate security and cryptography ever done (so we've been told). And math. Lots of math. It was all going great until, in the middle of it, something started to behave a little...strangely.</p>\n<p><em>Reported by Molly Webster. Produced by Matt Kielty and Molly Webster. Denver Ceremony station recordings were created by media maker Nathaniel Kramer, with help from Daniel Cooper. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "tease"=>"Today, paranoia sets in: we head to The Ceremony, the top-secret, three-day launch of a new currency, wizards and math included. Halfway through, something&nbsp;strange happens.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1614297540000, "publish-at"=>"2021-02-25T18:59:00", "date-line-ts"=>1614297540, "estimated-duration"=>2833, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Zcash 2", "name"=>"1/Radiolab_Cryptocurrency_Iterations-G.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/800/800/l/80/1/Radiolab_Cryptocurrency_Iterations-G.png", "h"=>800, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Radiolab_Cryptocurrency_Iterations-G.png", "w"=>800, "id"=>166476, "credits-name"=>"Colby Kirkpatrick"}, "objectID"=>"780552", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Ceremony ", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>In November of 2016, journalist Morgen Peck showed up at her friend Molly Webster's apartment in Brooklyn, told her to take her battery out of her phone, and began to tell her about The Ceremony, a moment last fall when a group of, well, let's just call them wizards, came together in an undisclosed location to launch a new currency. It's an undertaking that involves some of the most elaborate security and cryptography ever done (so we've been told). And math. Lots of math. It was all going great until, in the middle of it, something started to behave a little...strangely.</p>\n<p><em>Reported by Molly Webster. Produced by Matt Kielty and Molly Webster. Denver Ceremony station recordings were created by media maker Nathaniel Kramer, with help from Daniel Cooper. </em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at <a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a>.</em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-02-25T18:59:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"red-herring", "title"=>"Red Herring", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21redherring.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1089873", "description"=>"<p><span>It was the early 80s, the height of the Cold War, when something strange began happening off the coast of Sweden. The navy reported a mysterious sound deep below the surface of the ocean. Again, and again, and again they would hear it near their secret military bases, in their harbors, and up and down the Swedish coastline. </span></p>\n<p><span>After thorough analysis the navy was certain. The sound was an invasion into their waters, an act of war, the opening salvos of a possible nuclear annihilation. </span></p>\n<p>Or was it? </p>\n<p>Today, Annie McEwen pulls us down into a deep-sea mystery, one of international intrigue that asks you to consider the possibility that maybe, just maybe, your deepest beliefs could be as solid as...air.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Annie McEwen and produced by Annie McEwen, Matt Kielty, and Sarah Qari, with sound design by Jeremy Bloom. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to <span>Bosse Lindquist.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.   </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"A deep-sea Cold War mystery with subs and bombs and science.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1613714820000, "publish-at"=>"2021-02-19T01:07:00", "date-line-ts"=>1613714820, "estimated-duration"=>2118, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/02/red_herring.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1024/768/c/80/2021/02/red_herring.jpg", "h"=>768, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/02/red_herring.jpg", "w"=>1024, "id"=>194376, "credits-name"=>"Heath Alseike"}, "objectID"=>"1089873", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Red Herring", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>It was the early 80s, the height of the Cold War, when something strange began happening off the coast of Sweden. The navy reported a mysterious sound deep below the surface of the ocean. Again, and again, and again they would hear it near their secret military bases, in their harbors, and up and down the Swedish coastline. </span></p>\n<p><span>After thorough analysis the navy was certain. The sound was an invasion into their waters, an act of war, the opening salvos of a possible nuclear annihilation. </span></p>\n<p>Or was it? </p>\n<p>Today, Annie McEwen pulls us down into a deep-sea mystery, one of international intrigue that asks you to consider the possibility that maybe, just maybe, your deepest beliefs could be as solid as...air.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Annie McEwen and produced by Annie McEwen, Matt Kielty, and Sarah Qari, with sound design by Jeremy Bloom. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to <span>Bosse Lindquist.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.   </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-02-19T01:07:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"facebooks-supreme-court", "title"=>"Facebook's Supreme Court", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21facebookssupremecourtupdate.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1087999", "description"=>"<p>Since its inception, the perennial thorn in Facebook’s side has been content moderation. That is, deciding what you and I are allowed to post on the site and what we’re not. Missteps by Facebook in this area have fueled everything from a genocide in Myanmar to viral disinformation surrounding politics and the coronavirus. However, just this past year, conceding their failings, Facebook shifted its approach. They erected an independent body of twenty jurors that will make the final call on many of Facebook’s thorniest decisions. This body has been called: Facebook’s Supreme Court.</p>\n<p><span data-stringify-type=\"paragraph-break\"></span>So today, in collaboration with the New Yorker magazine and the New Yorker Radio Hour, we explore how this body came to be, what power it really has and how the consequences of its decisions will be nothing short of life or death.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Simon Adler.</em></p>\n<p><em>To hear more about the court's origin, their rulings so far, and their upcoming docket, check out David Remnick and reporter Kate Klonick’s conversation in the New Yorker Radio Hour podcast <a href=\"https://smarturl.it/newyorkerradiohour/spotify\">feed</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Facebook built its own Supreme Court to decide who or what is banned on the site. Who is on it, how will it work, and &mdash; how much power does it really have?", "publishTime"=>1613127600000, "publish-at"=>"2021-02-12T06:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1613127600, "estimated-duration"=>2648, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/02/TNY_fb_apple.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/c/80/2021/02/TNY_fb_apple.jpg", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/02/TNY_fb_apple.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>194255, "credits-name"=>"Golden Cosmos"}, "objectID"=>"1087999", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Facebook's Supreme Court", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Since its inception, the perennial thorn in Facebook’s side has been content moderation. That is, deciding what you and I are allowed to post on the site and what we’re not. Missteps by Facebook in this area have fueled everything from a genocide in Myanmar to viral disinformation surrounding politics and the coronavirus. However, just this past year, conceding their failings, Facebook shifted its approach. They erected an independent body of twenty jurors that will make the final call on many of Facebook’s thorniest decisions. This body has been called: Facebook’s Supreme Court.</p>\n<p><span data-stringify-type=\"paragraph-break\"></span>So today, in collaboration with the New Yorker magazine and the New Yorker Radio Hour, we explore how this body came to be, what power it really has and how the consequences of its decisions will be nothing short of life or death.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported and produced by Simon Adler.</em></p>\n<p><em>To hear more about the court's origin, their rulings so far, and their upcoming docket, check out David Remnick and reporter Kate Klonick’s conversation in the New Yorker Radio Hour podcast <a href=\"https://smarturl.it/newyorkerradiohour/spotify\">feed</a>.</em></p>\n<p><em><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-02-12T06:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"smile-my-ass", "title"=>"Smile My Ass", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21smilemyass.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=536722", "description"=>"<p>Candid Camera is one of the most original – and one of the most mischievous – TV shows of all time.  Admirers hailed its creator Allen Funt as a poet of the everyday. Critics denounced him as a Peeping Tom.  Funt sought to capture people at their most unguarded, their most spontaneous, their most natural.  And he did. But as the show succeeded, it started to change the way we thought not only of reality television, but also of reality itself.  Looking back at the show now, a half century later, it’s hard NOT to see so many of our preoccupations – privacy, propriety, publicity, authenticity – through a funhouse mirror, darkly.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and produced by Matt Kielty. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special Thanks to: Bertram van Munster, Fred Nadis, Alexa Conway, the Eastern Airlines Employee Association and Eastern Airlines Radio, Rebecca Lemov, Anna McCarthy, Jill Lepore, Cullie Bogacki Willis III, Barbara Titus and the Funt family. </em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1836\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1837\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1841\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1839\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1842\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div id=\"ember1068\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1845\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n<div class=\"story-credits__producing-org-credits producing-org-credits\"></div>\n</div>\n", "tease"=>"As Candid Camera succeeded, it started to change the way we thought not only of reality television, but also of reality itself.", "publishTime"=>1611891900000, "publish-at"=>"2021-01-28T22:45:00", "date-line-ts"=>1611891600, "estimated-duration"=>2096, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/GettyImages-86467821.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1476/c/80/1/GettyImages-86467821.jpg", "h"=>1476, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/GettyImages-86467821.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>144150, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Getty Images"}, "objectID"=>"536722", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Smile My Ass", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Candid Camera is one of the most original – and one of the most mischievous – TV shows of all time.  Admirers hailed its creator Allen Funt as a poet of the everyday. Critics denounced him as a Peeping Tom.  Funt sought to capture people at their most unguarded, their most spontaneous, their most natural.  And he did. But as the show succeeded, it started to change the way we thought not only of reality television, but also of reality itself.  Looking back at the show now, a half century later, it’s hard NOT to see so many of our preoccupations – privacy, propriety, publicity, authenticity – through a funhouse mirror, darkly.</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and produced by Matt Kielty. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special Thanks to: Bertram van Munster, Fred Nadis, Alexa Conway, the Eastern Airlines Employee Association and Eastern Airlines Radio, Rebecca Lemov, Anna McCarthy, Jill Lepore, Cullie Bogacki Willis III, Barbara Titus and the Funt family. </em></p>\n<p><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1836\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1837\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1841\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1839\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1842\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div id=\"ember1068\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1845\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n<div class=\"story-credits__producing-org-credits producing-org-credits\"></div>\n</div>\n", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-01-28T22:45:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"post-reports-four-hours-insurrection", "title"=>"Post Reports: Four Hours of Insurrection", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21postreports.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1082231", "description"=>"<p><span>We’re all still processing what happened on January 6th. Despite the hours and hours of video circulating online, we still didn’t feel like we had a visceral, on-the-ground sense of what happened that day. Until we heard the piece we’re featuring today. The </span><em><span>Washington Post</span></em><span>’s daily podcast </span><em><span>Post Reports</span></em><span> built a minute-by-minute replay of that day, from the rally, to the invasion, to the aftermath, told through the voices of people who were in the building that day -- reporters, photojournalists, Congresspeople, police officers and more. It’s some of the most visceral reporting we’ve heard anywhere on this historic moment. </span><a href=\"https://www.washingtonpost.com/podcasts/post-reports/four-hours-of-insurrection/\"><span>Listen to their full episode here.</span></a></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"The Washington Post&rsquo;s daily podcast takes us inside the Capitol and walks us step-by-step through the unprecedented events of January 6th.", "publishTime"=>1610798400000, "publish-at"=>"2021-01-16T07:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1610798400, "estimated-duration"=>2360, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/01/capitol.jpeg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1200/817/c/80/2021/01/capitol.jpeg", "h"=>817, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.flickr.com/photos/37527185@N05/50812356151/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/01/capitol.jpeg", "w"=>1200, "id"=>193894, "credits-name"=>"Tyler Merbler"}, "objectID"=>"1082231", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Post Reports: Four Hours of Insurrection", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>We’re all still processing what happened on January 6th. Despite the hours and hours of video circulating online, we still didn’t feel like we had a visceral, on-the-ground sense of what happened that day. Until we heard the piece we’re featuring today. The </span><em><span>Washington Post</span></em><span>’s daily podcast </span><em><span>Post Reports</span></em><span> built a minute-by-minute replay of that day, from the rally, to the invasion, to the aftermath, told through the voices of people who were in the building that day -- reporters, photojournalists, Congresspeople, police officers and more. It’s some of the most visceral reporting we’ve heard anywhere on this historic moment. </span><a href=\"https://www.washingtonpost.com/podcasts/post-reports/four-hours-of-insurrection/\"><span>Listen to their full episode here.</span></a></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-01-16T07:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"more-money-less-problems", "title"=>"More Money Less Problems", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21moremoneylessproblems.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1081999", "description"=>"<p><span>Back in March 2020, when the COVID-19 pandemic was just beginning and the shelter-in-place orders brought the economy to a screeching halt, a quirky-but-clever idea to save the economy made its way up to some of the highest levels of government. Congresswoman Rashida Tlaib proposed an ambitious relief </span><a href=\"https://tlaib.house.gov/sites/tlaib.house.gov/files/Automatic%20Boost%20to%20Communities%20Act%20.pdf\"><span>bill</span></a><span> to keep the country’s metaphorical lights on: recurring payments to people to help them stay afloat during the crisis. And the way Congress would pay for it? By minting two platinum $1 trillion coins. (You read that right). </span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode, we take a jaunt through the evolution of our currency, from the gold-backed bills of the 19th century, to the most powerful computer at the Federal Reserve. And we chase an idea that torpedoes what we thought was a fundamental law of economics. Can we </span><em><span>actually</span></em><span> just print more money? </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Becca Bressler and was produced by Becca Bressler and Simon Adler.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Carlos Mucha, Warren Mosler, David Cay Johnston, Alex Goldmark, Bryant Urstadt, and Amanda Aronczyk. </span></em></p>\n<p>To learn more about these ideas check out: </p>\n<p>Stephanie Kelton's <a href=\"https://www.publicaffairsbooks.com/titles/stephanie-kelton/the-deficit-myth/9781541736184/\">book</a> <em>The Deficit Myth</em></p>\n<p>Jacob Goldstein's <a href=\"https://www.hachettebooks.com/titles/jacob-goldstein/money/9780316417198/\">book</a> <em>Money: The True Story of a Made-Up Thing </em>and the <em>Planet Money</em> <a href=\"https://www.npr.org/sections/money/\">podcast</a></p>\n<p>Betsey Stevenson's <a href=\"https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/think-like-an-economist/id1523898793\">podcast</a> <em>Think Like an Economist </em></p>\n<p>This <a href=\"https://mintthecoin.org\">website</a> for more about #MintTheCoin</p>\n<p><span>And for a fun quick read, check out </span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/2013/01/trillion-dollar-coin-inventor/\"><span>this</span></a><span> WIRED article about the surprising origin of the trillion dollar coin.</span></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"A trillion dollar platinum coin sends us down a wormhole, re-examining a classic question: why can&rsquo;t we just print more money?", "publishTime"=>1610699520000, "publish-at"=>"2021-01-15T03:32:00", "date-line-ts"=>1610699520, "estimated-duration"=>1693, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2021/01/RadioLab_Money_01_2021.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1240/c/80/2021/01/RadioLab_Money_01_2021.png", "h"=>1240, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2021/01/RadioLab_Money_01_2021.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>193869, "credits-name"=>"Zara Stasi"}, "objectID"=>"1081999", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"More Money Less Problems", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Back in March 2020, when the COVID-19 pandemic was just beginning and the shelter-in-place orders brought the economy to a screeching halt, a quirky-but-clever idea to save the economy made its way up to some of the highest levels of government. Congresswoman Rashida Tlaib proposed an ambitious relief </span><a href=\"https://tlaib.house.gov/sites/tlaib.house.gov/files/Automatic%20Boost%20to%20Communities%20Act%20.pdf\"><span>bill</span></a><span> to keep the country’s metaphorical lights on: recurring payments to people to help them stay afloat during the crisis. And the way Congress would pay for it? By minting two platinum $1 trillion coins. (You read that right). </span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode, we take a jaunt through the evolution of our currency, from the gold-backed bills of the 19th century, to the most powerful computer at the Federal Reserve. And we chase an idea that torpedoes what we thought was a fundamental law of economics. Can we </span><em><span>actually</span></em><span> just print more money? </span></p>\n<p><em><span>This episode was reported by Becca Bressler and was produced by Becca Bressler and Simon Adler.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Carlos Mucha, Warren Mosler, David Cay Johnston, Alex Goldmark, Bryant Urstadt, and Amanda Aronczyk. </span></em></p>\n<p>To learn more about these ideas check out: </p>\n<p>Stephanie Kelton's <a href=\"https://www.publicaffairsbooks.com/titles/stephanie-kelton/the-deficit-myth/9781541736184/\">book</a> <em>The Deficit Myth</em></p>\n<p>Jacob Goldstein's <a href=\"https://www.hachettebooks.com/titles/jacob-goldstein/money/9780316417198/\">book</a> <em>Money: The True Story of a Made-Up Thing </em>and the <em>Planet Money</em> <a href=\"https://www.npr.org/sections/money/\">podcast</a></p>\n<p>Betsey Stevenson's <a href=\"https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/think-like-an-economist/id1523898793\">podcast</a> <em>Think Like an Economist </em></p>\n<p>This <a href=\"https://mintthecoin.org\">website</a> for more about #MintTheCoin</p>\n<p><span>And for a fun quick read, check out </span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/2013/01/trillion-dollar-coin-inventor/\"><span>this</span></a><span> WIRED article about the surprising origin of the trillion dollar coin.</span></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-01-15T03:32:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"sight-unseen", "title"=>"Sight Unseen", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast21sightunseenrerun.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=449398", "description"=>"<p>As the attacks were unfolding on the Capitol, a steady stream of images poured onto our screens. Photo editor Kainaz Amaria tells us what she was looking for--and seeing--that afternoon. And she runs into a dilemma we've talked about before. In<span> December of 2009, p<span>hotojournalist </span><span>Lynsey Addario, in</span> was embedded with a medevac team in Afghanistan. After days of waiting, one night they got the call - a marine was gravely wounded. What happened next happens all the time. But this time it was captured, picture by picture, in excruciating detail. Horrible, difficult, and at times strikingly beautiful, those photos raise some questions: Who should see them, who gets to decide who should see them, and what can pictures like that do, to those of us far away from the horrors of war and those of us who are all too close to it?</span></p>\n<p><span>Episode Notes:</span></p>\n<p><span>To hear Kainaz Amaria talk more about the filter, check out: </span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.vox.com/recode/2020/6/11/21281028/before-sharing-images-police-brutality-protest-george-floyd-ahmaud-arbery-facebook-instagram-twitter\">this post on ethical questions to consider around the sharing of images of police brutality</a> and h</span><span>er </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/otm/segments/bloody-image-double-standard\"><span>interview</span></a><span> on On The Media about the double-standard in many U.S. newsrooms when it comes to posting graphic images. </span></p>\n<p><span></span><em>Special thanks to <a href=\"http://www.heliumrecords.co.uk/releases/shift.php\">Chris Hughes and Helium Records for the use of Shift Part IV from the album Shift</a></em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1013\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1022\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1042\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1036\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1059\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<p><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1068\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"We try to contain the stream of photos coming at us in the last week and ask a question about an image that&nbsp;few of us get to see, a soldier fatally wounded on the battlefield.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1610518020000, "publish-at"=>"2021-01-13T01:07:00", "date-line-ts"=>1610518020, "estimated-duration"=>2214, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"1/Addario.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1237/c/80/1/Addario.jpg", "h"=>1237, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"A moment of silence after the death of Lance Corporal Jonathan Taylor, Killed in Action December 1st, 2009, Afghanistan", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/1/Addario.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>136651, "credits-name"=>"Photo Credit: Lynsey Addario/ Getty Images Reportage"}, "objectID"=>"449398", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Sight Unseen", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>As the attacks were unfolding on the Capitol, a steady stream of images poured onto our screens. Photo editor Kainaz Amaria tells us what she was looking for--and seeing--that afternoon. And she runs into a dilemma we've talked about before. In<span> December of 2009, p<span>hotojournalist </span><span>Lynsey Addario, in</span> was embedded with a medevac team in Afghanistan. After days of waiting, one night they got the call - a marine was gravely wounded. What happened next happens all the time. But this time it was captured, picture by picture, in excruciating detail. Horrible, difficult, and at times strikingly beautiful, those photos raise some questions: Who should see them, who gets to decide who should see them, and what can pictures like that do, to those of us far away from the horrors of war and those of us who are all too close to it?</span></p>\n<p><span>Episode Notes:</span></p>\n<p><span>To hear Kainaz Amaria talk more about the filter, check out: </span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.vox.com/recode/2020/6/11/21281028/before-sharing-images-police-brutality-protest-george-floyd-ahmaud-arbery-facebook-instagram-twitter\">this post on ethical questions to consider around the sharing of images of police brutality</a> and h</span><span>er </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/otm/segments/bloody-image-double-standard\"><span>interview</span></a><span> on On The Media about the double-standard in many U.S. newsrooms when it comes to posting graphic images. </span></p>\n<p><span></span><em>Special thanks to <a href=\"http://www.heliumrecords.co.uk/releases/shift.php\">Chris Hughes and Helium Records for the use of Shift Part IV from the album Shift</a></em></p>\n<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1013\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1022\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1042\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1036\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1059\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<div>\n<p><em><em><span><em><span><em><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at<span> </span></span><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab-it/onestep/?utm_source=wnyc&amp;utm_medium=radiolab-redirect&amp;utm_campaign=pledge&amp;utm_content=show-notes\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"Pledge\">Radiolab.org/donate</a><span>.    </span></em></em></span></em></span></em></em></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1068\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"></div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2021-01-13T01:07:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2022=>[{"slug"=>"new-normal-2212", "title"=>"New Normal", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast123022_newnormal.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1285103", "description"=>"<p><span>This episode —first released in 2009 and then again in 2015, with an update — asks, what is “normal”? Maybe it exists, maybe not. We examine peace-loving baboons with Stanford neuroscientist Robert Sapolsky, talk to Stu Rasmussen, whose preferred pronouns were he/him (https://zpr.io/nUdsZawNmhwt), and his neighbors in Silverton, Oregon about how a town chooses its community over outsider opinions. And lastly, we speak with an evolutionary anthropologist, Duke University’s own Brian Hare, and an evolutionary biologist Tecumseh Fitch, then at the University of St. Andrews, Scotland, now at the University of Vienna, Austria, about foxes who love to snuggle.<br></span><span><br></span><span>And what we find is that normal — maybe the only normal — is change.</span><span><br><br></span></p>\n<p><strong>EPISODE CREDITS <br></strong><span>Reported by - Aaron Cohen</span><span><br></span><span>Produced by - Soren Wheeler<br></span><span>with help from - Annie McEwen<br><br></span><span></span><strong>CITATIONS<br></strong><strong>Articles -</strong><span><br></span><span><a href=\"https://zpr.io/nUdsZawNmhwt\">Stu Rasmussen’s NYT Obituary</a> (https://zpr.io/nUdsZawNmhwt).<br></span></p>\n<p><strong>Theater - <br></strong><a href=\"https://www.intiman.org/plays-events/stu-for-silverton-a-new-musical/\"><span>Andrew Russel’s “Stu for Silverton”</span></a><span> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/Jn5JP276pwhj\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/Jn5JP276pwhj\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/Jn5JP276pwhj\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/Jn5JP276pwhj</a>)</span> the play based on Stu Rasmussen’s life.</span><strong> </strong></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><span><br><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em><span><br></span><span></span></a></span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><span><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation</span></em><em><span>.</span></em></span><span><br></span></p>", "tease"=>"Maybe &ldquo;normal&rdquo; exists. Maybe it doesn&rsquo;t. Maybe the only &ldquo;normal&rdquo; thing is change.", "publishTime"=>1672412400000, "publish-at"=>"2022-12-30T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1672412400, "estimated-duration"=>4082, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"A red fox looking into the camera.", "name"=>"2022/12/NEW-NORMAL_EPS-IMG-221229.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1601/1201/c/80/2022/12/NEW-NORMAL_EPS-IMG-221229.png", "h"=>1201, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/12/NEW-NORMAL_EPS-IMG-221229.png", "w"=>1601, "id"=>203432, "credits-name"=>"Jared Bartman"}, "objectID"=>"1285103", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"New Normal", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This episode —first released in 2009 and then again in 2015, with an update — asks, what is “normal”? Maybe it exists, maybe not. We examine peace-loving baboons with Stanford neuroscientist Robert Sapolsky, talk to Stu Rasmussen, whose preferred pronouns were he/him (https://zpr.io/nUdsZawNmhwt), and his neighbors in Silverton, Oregon about how a town chooses its community over outsider opinions. And lastly, we speak with an evolutionary anthropologist, Duke University’s own Brian Hare, and an evolutionary biologist Tecumseh Fitch, then at the University of St. Andrews, Scotland, now at the University of Vienna, Austria, about foxes who love to snuggle.<br></span><span><br></span><span>And what we find is that normal — maybe the only normal — is change.</span><span><br><br></span></p>\n<p><strong>EPISODE CREDITS <br></strong><span>Reported by - Aaron Cohen</span><span><br></span><span>Produced by - Soren Wheeler<br></span><span>with help from - Annie McEwen<br><br></span><span></span><strong>CITATIONS<br></strong><strong>Articles -</strong><span><br></span><span><a href=\"https://zpr.io/nUdsZawNmhwt\">Stu Rasmussen’s NYT Obituary</a> (https://zpr.io/nUdsZawNmhwt).<br></span></p>\n<p><strong>Theater - <br></strong><a href=\"https://www.intiman.org/plays-events/stu-for-silverton-a-new-musical/\"><span>Andrew Russel’s “Stu for Silverton”</span></a><span> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/Jn5JP276pwhj\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/Jn5JP276pwhj\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/Jn5JP276pwhj\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/Jn5JP276pwhj</a>)</span> the play based on Stu Rasmussen’s life.</span><strong> </strong></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><span><br><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em><span><br></span><span></span></a></span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><span><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation</span></em><em><span>.</span></em></span><span><br></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-12-30T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"flight-christmas", "title"=>"The Flight Before Christmas", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast122322_flightchristmas.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1283005", "description"=>"<p><span>At any given moment, nearly 500,000 people are crammed together in a metal tube, hurtling through the air. In this episode, we look at the strange human experiment that is flying together</span><span>.</span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Natalie Compton, Julia Longoria, Mike Arnot, and everyone at Gate Gourmet.<br><br></em><strong>EPISODE CREDITS: </strong></p>\n<p><span>Reported by</span><strong> - </strong><span>Matt Kielty, Simon Adler and Rachael Cusick<br></span><span>Produced by - Matt Kielty, Simon Adler and Rachael Cusick<br></span><span>With Production help from - Sindhu Gnanasambandan<br>Original music and sound design contributed by - Jeremy Bloom<br></span><span>and mixing help from - Arianne Wack<br></span><span>Fact-checking by - Natalie A. Middleton<br></span><span>Edited by  - Pat Walters<br><br></span></p>\n<p><strong>CITATIONS: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Videos</strong></p>\n<p>Lou Boyer, the animal-flying pilot from our episode, has a <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/loub747/\">great plane-forward Instagram account</a> (<span>https://www.instagram.com/loub747/)</span>. As well as <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/a%20whole%20YouTube%20channel\">a whole YouTube channel</a> (https://www.youtube.com/@loub747/videos) dedicated to snakes and planes. (Luckily, not both at the same time.)</p>\n<p><strong>Books</strong></p>\n<p>Richard Foss's <em><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/22620309-food-in-the-air-and-space\">Food in the Air and Space: The Surprising History of Food and Drink in the Skies</a> </em>(<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/KZyTPJkSENVq\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/KZyTPJkSENVq\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/KZyTPJkSENVq\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/KZyTPJkSENVq</a>)</span></p>\n<p>Michael Heller's and James Salzman's <em><a href=\"https://www.minethebook.com/\">Mine: How the Hidden Rules of Ownership Control our Lives</a> (<a href=\"https://www.minethebook.com/\" title=\"Mine\">https://www.minethebook.com/</a>)</em><br><br><strong>CHECK OUT:<br><br></strong>The Death, Sex and Money series <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/projects/estrangement\">Estrangement</a><em> (https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/projects/estrangement)</em><strong><br></strong><em><br><br>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. Sign up (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</em></p>\n<p><em>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of The Lab (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</em></p>\n<p><em>Follow our show on Instagram, Twitter and Facebook @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing radiolab@wnyc.org.<br></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"Tales of the endurance test that is flying on a commercial airliner with your fellow human.", "publishTime"=>1671807600000, "publish-at"=>"2022-12-23T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1671807600, "estimated-duration"=>2633, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/12/Planes_1600x1200.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/12/Planes_1600x1200.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/12/Planes_1600x1200.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>203395, "credits-name"=>"Jared Bartman"}, "objectID"=>"1283005", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Flight Before Christmas", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>At any given moment, nearly 500,000 people are crammed together in a metal tube, hurtling through the air. In this episode, we look at the strange human experiment that is flying together</span><span>.</span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Natalie Compton, Julia Longoria, Mike Arnot, and everyone at Gate Gourmet.<br><br></em><strong>EPISODE CREDITS: </strong></p>\n<p><span>Reported by</span><strong> - </strong><span>Matt Kielty, Simon Adler and Rachael Cusick<br></span><span>Produced by - Matt Kielty, Simon Adler and Rachael Cusick<br></span><span>With Production help from - Sindhu Gnanasambandan<br>Original music and sound design contributed by - Jeremy Bloom<br></span><span>and mixing help from - Arianne Wack<br></span><span>Fact-checking by - Natalie A. Middleton<br></span><span>Edited by  - Pat Walters<br><br></span></p>\n<p><strong>CITATIONS: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>Videos</strong></p>\n<p>Lou Boyer, the animal-flying pilot from our episode, has a <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/loub747/\">great plane-forward Instagram account</a> (<span>https://www.instagram.com/loub747/)</span>. As well as <a href=\"http://www.wnyc.org/a%20whole%20YouTube%20channel\">a whole YouTube channel</a> (https://www.youtube.com/@loub747/videos) dedicated to snakes and planes. (Luckily, not both at the same time.)</p>\n<p><strong>Books</strong></p>\n<p>Richard Foss's <em><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/22620309-food-in-the-air-and-space\">Food in the Air and Space: The Surprising History of Food and Drink in the Skies</a> </em>(<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/KZyTPJkSENVq\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/KZyTPJkSENVq\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/KZyTPJkSENVq\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/KZyTPJkSENVq</a>)</span></p>\n<p>Michael Heller's and James Salzman's <em><a href=\"https://www.minethebook.com/\">Mine: How the Hidden Rules of Ownership Control our Lives</a> (<a href=\"https://www.minethebook.com/\" title=\"Mine\">https://www.minethebook.com/</a>)</em><br><br><strong>CHECK OUT:<br><br></strong>The Death, Sex and Money series <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/projects/estrangement\">Estrangement</a><em> (https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/projects/estrangement)</em><strong><br></strong><em><br><br>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. Sign up (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</em></p>\n<p><em>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of The Lab (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</em></p>\n<p><em>Follow our show on Instagram, Twitter and Facebook @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing radiolab@wnyc.org.<br></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-12-23T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"null-and-void-2212", "title"=>"Null and Void", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast121622_nullvoid.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1280584", "description"=>"<p><span>This episode, first aired in 2017, has Reporter Tracie Hunte and Editor Soren Wheeler exploring a hidden power in the U.S. Court System that is either the cornerstone of our democracy or a trapdoor to anarchy.<br></span></p>\n<p><span>Should a juror be able to ignore the law? From a Quaker prayer meeting in the streets of London to riots in the streets of Los Angeles, we trace the history of a quiet act of rebellion and struggle with how much power “We the People” should really have.<br><br><em>Special thanks to Darryl K. Brown, professor of law at the University of Virginia, Andrew Leipold, professor of law at the University of Illinois, at Urbana-Champaign, Nancy King, professor of law at Vanderbilt University, Buzz Scherr law professor at University of New Hampshire, Eric Verlo and attorneys David Lane, Mark Sisto, David Kallman and Paul Grant. </em><br><br><strong>Episode Credits:<br><br></strong>Reported by Tracie Hunte<br>Produced by Matt Kielty</span></p>\n<p><strong>Citations:<br><br>Media: </strong>You can hear the whole On the Media series, <em>The Divided Dial, </em>and many of their other great work by following this <a href=\"link:%20https://link.chtbl.com/onthemedia?sid=divideddial.radiolab\">link</a>(https://zpr.io/hbkfxQDKdHz8). </p>\n<p><span><br></span><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a></p>\n<hr>\n<p><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><span><br></span><span><br></span></a><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation</span></em><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"There&rsquo;s a hidden power found in American juries that both creates and destroys democracy.", "publishTime"=>1671202800000, "publish-at"=>"2022-12-16T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1671202800, "estimated-duration"=>3632, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Judges gavel at rest with arcs emanating from the top.", "name"=>"2022/12/NullandVoid_1600x1200.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/12/NullandVoid_1600x1200.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/12/NullandVoid_1600x1200.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>203316, "credits-name"=>"Jared Bartman"}, "objectID"=>"1280584", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Null and Void", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This episode, first aired in 2017, has Reporter Tracie Hunte and Editor Soren Wheeler exploring a hidden power in the U.S. Court System that is either the cornerstone of our democracy or a trapdoor to anarchy.<br></span></p>\n<p><span>Should a juror be able to ignore the law? From a Quaker prayer meeting in the streets of London to riots in the streets of Los Angeles, we trace the history of a quiet act of rebellion and struggle with how much power “We the People” should really have.<br><br><em>Special thanks to Darryl K. Brown, professor of law at the University of Virginia, Andrew Leipold, professor of law at the University of Illinois, at Urbana-Champaign, Nancy King, professor of law at Vanderbilt University, Buzz Scherr law professor at University of New Hampshire, Eric Verlo and attorneys David Lane, Mark Sisto, David Kallman and Paul Grant. </em><br><br><strong>Episode Credits:<br><br></strong>Reported by Tracie Hunte<br>Produced by Matt Kielty</span></p>\n<p><strong>Citations:<br><br>Media: </strong>You can hear the whole On the Media series, <em>The Divided Dial, </em>and many of their other great work by following this <a href=\"link:%20https://link.chtbl.com/onthemedia?sid=divideddial.radiolab\">link</a>(https://zpr.io/hbkfxQDKdHz8). </p>\n<p><span><br></span><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a></p>\n<hr>\n<p><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><span><br></span><span><br></span></a><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation</span></em><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-12-16T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"middle-everything-ever", "title"=>"The Middle of Everything Ever", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast120922_averages.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1278127", "description"=>"<p><span>After graduating from high school, without a clear plan for what to do next, Laura Andrews started asking herself a lot of questions. A spiral of big philosophical thoughts that led her to sit down and write to us with a question that was… oddly mathematical.  What is the most average size thing, if you take into account everything in the universe. So, along with mathematician Steven Strogatz, we decided to see if we could sit down and, in a friendly throwdown of guesstimates and quick calculations, rough out an answer. </span></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to all the listeners who sent in their responses to this question.</span></em></p>\n<p><strong>Episode Credits:<br></strong><span></span><span>Reported by - Soren Wheeler and Alex Neason<br></span><span>Produced by - Annie McEwen<br></span><span>with mixing help from - Arianne Wack<br></span><span>Fact-checking by - Natalie A. Middleton<br></span><span>and Edited by  - Alex Neason</span></p>\n<p><strong>Citations:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Books<br></strong><em><span>You can find links to many books by Steven Strogatz here: </span></em><a href=\"https://www.stevenstrogatz.com/all-books\"><em><span>https://www.stevenstrogatz.com/all-books</span></em></a></p>\n<p><strong>Media<br></strong><em><span>And the podcast he does for </span></em>Quanta Magazine<em><span>, </span></em>The Joy of Why<em><span>, here: </span></em><a href=\"https://www.quantamagazine.org/tag/the-joy-of-why/\"><em><span>https://www.quantamagazine.org/tag/the-joy-of-why/</span></em></a></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br></span></em><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em><br></span></p>", "tease"=>"We all have moments when, facing the future or staring into the vastness of space, we feel small. But are we small? One listener asked us to find out.", "publishTime"=>1670598000000, "publish-at"=>"2022-12-09T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1670598000, "estimated-duration"=>1686, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/12/Average_1600x1200_BartmanJared.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/12/Average_1600x1200_BartmanJared.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/12/Average_1600x1200_BartmanJared.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>203225, "credits-name"=>"Jared Bartman"}, "objectID"=>"1278127", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Middle of Everything Ever", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>After graduating from high school, without a clear plan for what to do next, Laura Andrews started asking herself a lot of questions. A spiral of big philosophical thoughts that led her to sit down and write to us with a question that was… oddly mathematical.  What is the most average size thing, if you take into account everything in the universe. So, along with mathematician Steven Strogatz, we decided to see if we could sit down and, in a friendly throwdown of guesstimates and quick calculations, rough out an answer. </span></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to all the listeners who sent in their responses to this question.</span></em></p>\n<p><strong>Episode Credits:<br></strong><span></span><span>Reported by - Soren Wheeler and Alex Neason<br></span><span>Produced by - Annie McEwen<br></span><span>with mixing help from - Arianne Wack<br></span><span>Fact-checking by - Natalie A. Middleton<br></span><span>and Edited by  - Alex Neason</span></p>\n<p><strong>Citations:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Books<br></strong><em><span>You can find links to many books by Steven Strogatz here: </span></em><a href=\"https://www.stevenstrogatz.com/all-books\"><em><span>https://www.stevenstrogatz.com/all-books</span></em></a></p>\n<p><strong>Media<br></strong><em><span>And the podcast he does for </span></em>Quanta Magazine<em><span>, </span></em>The Joy of Why<em><span>, here: </span></em><a href=\"https://www.quantamagazine.org/tag/the-joy-of-why/\"><em><span>https://www.quantamagazine.org/tag/the-joy-of-why/</span></em></a></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br></span></em><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em><br></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-12-09T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"ashes-lawn-2212", "title"=>"The Ashes on the Lawn", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast120222_ashes.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1276180", "description"=>"<p><span>A global pandemic. Thousands dying. A passive government. An afflicted group fueled by grief and anger. In this episode, first aired in 2020, Reporter Tracie Hunte wanted to understand this moment of pain and confusion. As she looked back three decades, she found a complicated answer to a simple question: when nothing seems to work, how do you make change?</span><span><br></span></p>\n<p><span>Special thanks to<em> </em><em>Dr. Anthony Fauci.<br><br></em></span></p>\n<p><strong>Episode Credits:</strong></p>\n<p>Reported by Tracie Hunt<br>Produced by Matt Kielty<br><br></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a></p>\n<hr>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"When nothing seems to work, how do you make change?", "publishTime"=>1669993200000, "publish-at"=>"2022-12-02T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1669993200, "estimated-duration"=>3296, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/12/ASHES_EPS-221202_JaredBartman.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/12/ASHES_EPS-221202_JaredBartman.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Protesters gathered and burning an upside down American flag.", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/12/ASHES_EPS-221202_JaredBartman.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>203135, "credits-name"=>"Jared Bartman"}, "objectID"=>"1276180", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Ashes on the Lawn", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>A global pandemic. Thousands dying. A passive government. An afflicted group fueled by grief and anger. In this episode, first aired in 2020, Reporter Tracie Hunte wanted to understand this moment of pain and confusion. As she looked back three decades, she found a complicated answer to a simple question: when nothing seems to work, how do you make change?</span><span><br></span></p>\n<p><span>Special thanks to<em> </em><em>Dr. Anthony Fauci.<br><br></em></span></p>\n<p><strong>Episode Credits:</strong></p>\n<p>Reported by Tracie Hunt<br>Produced by Matt Kielty<br><br></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a></p>\n<hr>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-12-02T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"more-perfect-political-thicket-2211", "title"=>"More Perfect: The Political Thicket", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast112522_politicalthicket.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1274468", "description"=>"<p><span>When U.S. Supreme Court Chief Justice Earl Warren was asked at the end of his career, “What was the most important case of your tenure?”, there were a lot of answers he could have given. He had presided over some of the most important decisions in the court’s history — cases that dealt with segregation in schools, the right to an attorney, the right to remain silent, just to name a few. But his answer was a surprise: he said “Baker v. Carr,” a 1962 redistricting case. <br></span></p>\n<p><span>On this 2016 episode, part of our series </span><em><span>More Perfect</span></em><span>, we talk about why this case was so important. Important enough that it pushed one Supreme Court justice to a nervous breakdown, brought a boiling feud to a head, gave another justice a stroke, and changed the course of the Supreme Court — and the nation — forever.<br><br></span><span>This episode is the one of the few times you can hear the voice of our Executive Producer Suzie Lechtenberg. After years of leading the team, Suzie will leave WNYC to start her new adventure. <br><br></span><span>Suzie: re-publishing this episode is our way of saying thank you for all you’ve done — for the show and for each of us. Team </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span> wishes you nothing but success and so much happiness in the next stage of your career.</span></p>\n<p><span></span><strong>Episode Credits:<br></strong>Reported by Suzie Lechtenberg<br>Produced by Suzie Lechtenberg<br><br></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><br><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>", "tease"=>"How one U.S. Supreme Court case broke two justices and changed the nation forever.", "publishTime"=>1669388400000, "publish-at"=>"2022-11-25T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1669388400, "estimated-duration"=>2790, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Looking at the Supreme Court benches through a window of clear in thicket of lush vegetation.", "name"=>"2022/11/THICKET_EPS-221125_JaredBartman.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/11/THICKET_EPS-221125_JaredBartman.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/11/THICKET_EPS-221125_JaredBartman.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>203059, "credits-name"=>"Jared Bartman"}, "objectID"=>"1274468", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"More Perfect: The Political Thicket", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>When U.S. Supreme Court Chief Justice Earl Warren was asked at the end of his career, “What was the most important case of your tenure?”, there were a lot of answers he could have given. He had presided over some of the most important decisions in the court’s history — cases that dealt with segregation in schools, the right to an attorney, the right to remain silent, just to name a few. But his answer was a surprise: he said “Baker v. Carr,” a 1962 redistricting case. <br></span></p>\n<p><span>On this 2016 episode, part of our series </span><em><span>More Perfect</span></em><span>, we talk about why this case was so important. Important enough that it pushed one Supreme Court justice to a nervous breakdown, brought a boiling feud to a head, gave another justice a stroke, and changed the course of the Supreme Court — and the nation — forever.<br><br></span><span>This episode is the one of the few times you can hear the voice of our Executive Producer Suzie Lechtenberg. After years of leading the team, Suzie will leave WNYC to start her new adventure. <br><br></span><span>Suzie: re-publishing this episode is our way of saying thank you for all you’ve done — for the show and for each of us. Team </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span> wishes you nothing but success and so much happiness in the next stage of your career.</span></p>\n<p><span></span><strong>Episode Credits:<br></strong>Reported by Suzie Lechtenberg<br>Produced by Suzie Lechtenberg<br><br></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><br><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-11-25T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"whats-doc-2211", "title"=>"What's Up Doc?", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast111822_whatsupdoc.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1272577", "description"=>"<p><span>Mel Blanc was known as “the man of 1,000 voices,” but, to hear his son tell it, the actual number was closer to 1,500. Bugs Bunny, Porky Pig, Daffy Duck, Tweety, Barney Rubble, Woody Woodpecker, Sylvester, Foghorn Leghorn — all Mel. These characters made him one of the most beloved men in the United States.</span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode from 2012, Mel Blanc’s son Noel tells Producer Sean Cole how his father’s entire body would transform to bring life to these characters. But on a fateful day of 1961, after a crash left Mel in a lengthy coma, it was the characters who brought life to him.<br><br><strong>Episode Credits:<br></strong>Reported by Sean Cole<br></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><span><br><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org<br><br></span></em></a></span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><span><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><span></span><span><br></span></a><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation</span></em><em><span>.</span></em><br></span></p>", "tease"=>"Mel Blanc brought life to so many characters. Then, when he needed it most, the characters brought life to him.", "publishTime"=>1668783600000, "publish-at"=>"2022-11-18T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1668783600, "estimated-duration"=>1371, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Black and white image of Mel Blanc (famous voice actor) with bunny ears coming out of his head.", "name"=>"2022/11/WhatsUpDoc_EPS-221118_JaredBartman_JpOFppM.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2022/11/WhatsUpDoc_EPS-221118_JaredBartman_JpOFppM.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/11/WhatsUpDoc_EPS-221118_JaredBartman_JpOFppM.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>202966, "credits-name"=>"Jared Bartman"}, "objectID"=>"1272577", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"What's Up Doc?", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Mel Blanc was known as “the man of 1,000 voices,” but, to hear his son tell it, the actual number was closer to 1,500. Bugs Bunny, Porky Pig, Daffy Duck, Tweety, Barney Rubble, Woody Woodpecker, Sylvester, Foghorn Leghorn — all Mel. These characters made him one of the most beloved men in the United States.</span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode from 2012, Mel Blanc’s son Noel tells Producer Sean Cole how his father’s entire body would transform to bring life to these characters. But on a fateful day of 1961, after a crash left Mel in a lengthy coma, it was the characters who brought life to him.<br><br><strong>Episode Credits:<br></strong>Reported by Sean Cole<br></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><span><br><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org<br><br></span></em></a></span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><span><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><span></span><span><br></span></a><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation</span></em><em><span>.</span></em><br></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-11-18T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"butt-stuff", "title"=>"Butt Stuff", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast111122_butts.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1269971", "description"=>"<p>Why do we have a butt? Well, it’s not just for the convenience of a portable seat cushion. This week, we have a conversation with our Contributing Editor Heather Radke, who has spent the last several years going deep on one of our most noticeable surface features. She’s been working on a book called <em>Butts, a Backstory</em> and in this episode, she tells us about a fascinating history she uncovered that takes us from a eugenicist’s attempt in the late 1930s to concretize the most average human, to the rise of the garment industry, and the pain and shame we often feel today when we go looking for a pair of pants that actually fit.</p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Alexandra Primiani and Jordan Rodman</em></p>\n<p><strong>Episode Credits:</strong><br>Reported by Heather Radke<br>Produced by Matt Kielty<br>Original music and sound design contributed by Matt Kielty and Jeremy Bloom<br>Mixing by Jeremy Bloom<br>Fact-checking by Emily Krieger</p>\n<p><strong>Citations:<br></strong>You can Pre-order Heather’s book “Butts: A Backstory” <a href=\"https://zpr.io/QVFVLTTW9vpN\">here</a> (https://zpr.io/QVFVLTTW9vpN)</p>\n<p><br><em>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. Sign up (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</em></p>\n<p><em>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of The Lab (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</em></p>\n<p><em>Follow our show on Instagram, Twitter and Facebook @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing radiolab@wnyc.org.<br><br></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</p>", "tease"=>"How the myth of the perfectly normal butt was born and how it haunts our dressing rooms today.", "publishTime"=>1668178800000, "publish-at"=>"2022-11-11T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1668178800, "estimated-duration"=>2106, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/11/Butts_EPS-221111_JaredBartman.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2022/11/Butts_EPS-221111_JaredBartman.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/11/Butts_EPS-221111_JaredBartman.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>202868, "credits-name"=>"Jared Bartman"}, "objectID"=>"1269971", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Butt Stuff", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Why do we have a butt? Well, it’s not just for the convenience of a portable seat cushion. This week, we have a conversation with our Contributing Editor Heather Radke, who has spent the last several years going deep on one of our most noticeable surface features. She’s been working on a book called <em>Butts, a Backstory</em> and in this episode, she tells us about a fascinating history she uncovered that takes us from a eugenicist’s attempt in the late 1930s to concretize the most average human, to the rise of the garment industry, and the pain and shame we often feel today when we go looking for a pair of pants that actually fit.</p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Alexandra Primiani and Jordan Rodman</em></p>\n<p><strong>Episode Credits:</strong><br>Reported by Heather Radke<br>Produced by Matt Kielty<br>Original music and sound design contributed by Matt Kielty and Jeremy Bloom<br>Mixing by Jeremy Bloom<br>Fact-checking by Emily Krieger</p>\n<p><strong>Citations:<br></strong>You can Pre-order Heather’s book “Butts: A Backstory” <a href=\"https://zpr.io/QVFVLTTW9vpN\">here</a> (https://zpr.io/QVFVLTTW9vpN)</p>\n<p><br><em>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. Sign up (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</em></p>\n<p><em>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of The Lab (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</em></p>\n<p><em>Follow our show on Instagram, Twitter and Facebook @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing radiolab@wnyc.org.<br><br></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p> </p>\n<p>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-11-11T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"guts-2211", "title"=>"Guts", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast110422_guts.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1267909", "description"=>"<p>This hour, we dive into the messy mystery in the middle of us. What's going on down there? And what can the rumblings deep in our bellies tell us about ourselves? </p>\n<p><span>We join author Mary Roach and reach inside a live cow's stomach. Talk with writer Frederick Kaufman about our first peek into the wonderful world of human digestion that came about thanks to a hunting accident. And explore with show regular, science writer, and fellow water drinker, Carl Zimmer, about the trillions of microscopic creatures that keep us regulated, physically, but also, maybe, emotionally and spiritually.<br><br><br></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br><br></span></em><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br><br></span></em><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em><span><br><em><span><br></span></em></span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><span><em><span><br>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation</span></em><em><span>.</span></em><br></span></p>", "tease"=>"In this episode we will explore the grotesque tube slide inside all of us.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1667570400000, "publish-at"=>"2022-11-04T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1667570400, "estimated-duration"=>3281, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/11/Guts_EPS-IMG-221104-Jared-Bartman.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1601/1201/c/80/2022/11/Guts_EPS-IMG-221104-Jared-Bartman.png", "h"=>1201, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.instagram.com/jaredbartmandesign/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/11/Guts_EPS-IMG-221104-Jared-Bartman.png", "w"=>1601, "id"=>202777, "credits-name"=>"Jared Bartman"}, "objectID"=>"1267909", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Guts", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This hour, we dive into the messy mystery in the middle of us. What's going on down there? And what can the rumblings deep in our bellies tell us about ourselves? </p>\n<p><span>We join author Mary Roach and reach inside a live cow's stomach. Talk with writer Frederick Kaufman about our first peek into the wonderful world of human digestion that came about thanks to a hunting accident. And explore with show regular, science writer, and fellow water drinker, Carl Zimmer, about the trillions of microscopic creatures that keep us regulated, physically, but also, maybe, emotionally and spiritually.<br><br><br></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br><br></span></em><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br><br></span></em><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em><span><br><em><span><br></span></em></span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><span><em><span><br>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation</span></em><em><span>.</span></em><br></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-11-04T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"weather-report", "title"=>"The Weather Report", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast102822_weather.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1265727", "description"=>"<p><span>Meteorologists are as common as the clouds these days. Rolling onto the airwaves at morning, noon and night they tell us what to wear and where to plan our picnics. They’re local celebrities with an outsized influence. But in the 1940s, there was really only one of them: Irving P. Krick. He was suave and dapper, with the charm of a sunbeam and the boldness of a thunderclap. He was a salesman who turned the weather into a product.</span></p>\n<p><span>Today, listen to the story of Krick and his descendants, a crew of profit prophets who have found fame and fortune staring at the sky and seeing the future. We follow them from the bloody beaches of World War II to the climate changed coasts of today, exploring their impact and predicting what they’ll mean in our wackier weather world. </span></p>\n<p><strong>Special Thanks:<br></strong><em><span>Special thanks to Xandra Clark, Homa Sarabi, Santi Dharmawan, Francisco Alvarez, Maureen O’Leary and everyone at NOAA, Shimon Elkabetz, Jack Neff, Joe Pennington, Brad Colman, Morgan Yarker, Megan Walker, Eric Bramford, Jay Cohen and Irving Krick Jr for supplying us with tons of great archival footage and audio.</span></em> </p>\n<p><strong>Episode Credits:</strong></p>\n<p>Reported by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen<br>Produced by Annie McEwen and Simon Adler<br>Sound &amp; Music by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen and Jeremy Bloom<br>Mixing help from Arianne Wack<br>Fact-checking by Diane Kelly<br>Edited by Soren Wheeler</p>\n<p><strong>Citations:</strong></p>\n<p><span>Books: </span></p>\n<p>If you’re curious to know more about the history of weather forecasting, go check out Kris Harper’s book <a href=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262517355/weather-by-the-numbers/\">Weather by the Numbers</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Video:</strong></p>\n<p>We also asked Illustrator and Animator <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/sophiatwigt/\">Sophia Twigt</a> to make a little video explaining how the U.S. government agency NOAA collects and treats weather data to make weather forecasts. Here it is, narrated by Simon Adler. We hope you enjoy it:</p>\n<p><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm1404102124784000d38f3dc-cbed-4dc8-860f-a1c94a2ead63\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Vbyvy50-84s?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" title=\"The Fortune Tellers\" id=\"a3863924934486335945\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vbyvy50-84s\"></iframe></div></div>  </p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span><br>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.<br></span></em></p>", "tease"=>"The story of how one man and one woman changed the weather forever.", "publishTime"=>1666965600000, "publish-at"=>"2022-10-28T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1666965600, "estimated-duration"=>3143, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/10/WEATHER_EPS-221028.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/10/WEATHER_EPS-221028.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"Black and white radar image of a weather event with a crescendoing soundwave emanating from its middle", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/10/WEATHER_EPS-221028.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>202657, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1265727", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Weather Report", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Meteorologists are as common as the clouds these days. Rolling onto the airwaves at morning, noon and night they tell us what to wear and where to plan our picnics. They’re local celebrities with an outsized influence. But in the 1940s, there was really only one of them: Irving P. Krick. He was suave and dapper, with the charm of a sunbeam and the boldness of a thunderclap. He was a salesman who turned the weather into a product.</span></p>\n<p><span>Today, listen to the story of Krick and his descendants, a crew of profit prophets who have found fame and fortune staring at the sky and seeing the future. We follow them from the bloody beaches of World War II to the climate changed coasts of today, exploring their impact and predicting what they’ll mean in our wackier weather world. </span></p>\n<p><strong>Special Thanks:<br></strong><em><span>Special thanks to Xandra Clark, Homa Sarabi, Santi Dharmawan, Francisco Alvarez, Maureen O’Leary and everyone at NOAA, Shimon Elkabetz, Jack Neff, Joe Pennington, Brad Colman, Morgan Yarker, Megan Walker, Eric Bramford, Jay Cohen and Irving Krick Jr for supplying us with tons of great archival footage and audio.</span></em> </p>\n<p><strong>Episode Credits:</strong></p>\n<p>Reported by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen<br>Produced by Annie McEwen and Simon Adler<br>Sound &amp; Music by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen and Jeremy Bloom<br>Mixing help from Arianne Wack<br>Fact-checking by Diane Kelly<br>Edited by Soren Wheeler</p>\n<p><strong>Citations:</strong></p>\n<p><span>Books: </span></p>\n<p>If you’re curious to know more about the history of weather forecasting, go check out Kris Harper’s book <a href=\"https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262517355/weather-by-the-numbers/\">Weather by the Numbers</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Video:</strong></p>\n<p>We also asked Illustrator and Animator <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/sophiatwigt/\">Sophia Twigt</a> to make a little video explaining how the U.S. government agency NOAA collects and treats weather data to make weather forecasts. Here it is, narrated by Simon Adler. We hope you enjoy it:</p>\n<p><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm1404102124784000d38f3dc-cbed-4dc8-860f-a1c94a2ead63\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Vbyvy50-84s?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" title=\"The Fortune Tellers\" id=\"a3863924934486335945\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vbyvy50-84s\"></iframe></div></div>  </p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span><br>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.<br></span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-10-28T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"black-box-2210", "title"=>"Black Box", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast102122_blackbox-2210.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1263818", "description"=>"<p><span>In this episode, first aired in 2014, we examine three very different kinds of black boxes — spaces where we know what’s going in, we know what’s coming out, but can’t see what happens in that in-between space.</span></p>\n<p><span>From the darkest parts of metamorphosis to a sixty-year-old secret among magicians, and the nature of consciousness itself, we shine some light on three questions. But for each, we contend with an answerless space, leaving just enough room for the mystery and magic… always wondering what’s inside the Black Box.<br><br></span></p>\n<p><strong>Episode credits:</strong><br>Reported by<strong> </strong>Tim Howard and Molly Webster<br>Produced by Tim Howard and Molly Webster<br><br><strong>Citations:<br></strong>Radio Show<strong>: </strong>ABC's <em><a href=\"https://www.abc.net.au/radionational/programs/archived/360/keep-them-guessing/4119222\">Keep Them Guessing</a> (https://tinyurl.com/9r9zmftr)</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>", "tease"=>"A series of stories that celebrate the mystery and the magic of&hellip; the Black Box", "publishTime"=>1666360800000, "publish-at"=>"2022-10-21T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1666360800, "estimated-duration"=>3983, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"A shadowy black box with waves of light emanating from the top of it.", "name"=>"2022/10/BLACK-BOX_EPS-221021.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/10/BLACK-BOX_EPS-221021.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/10/BLACK-BOX_EPS-221021.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>202554, "credits-name"=>"(Radiolab)"}, "objectID"=>"1263818", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Black Box", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In this episode, first aired in 2014, we examine three very different kinds of black boxes — spaces where we know what’s going in, we know what’s coming out, but can’t see what happens in that in-between space.</span></p>\n<p><span>From the darkest parts of metamorphosis to a sixty-year-old secret among magicians, and the nature of consciousness itself, we shine some light on three questions. But for each, we contend with an answerless space, leaving just enough room for the mystery and magic… always wondering what’s inside the Black Box.<br><br></span></p>\n<p><strong>Episode credits:</strong><br>Reported by<strong> </strong>Tim Howard and Molly Webster<br>Produced by Tim Howard and Molly Webster<br><br><strong>Citations:<br></strong>Radio Show<strong>: </strong>ABC's <em><a href=\"https://www.abc.net.au/radionational/programs/archived/360/keep-them-guessing/4119222\">Keep Them Guessing</a> (https://tinyurl.com/9r9zmftr)</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-10-21T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"no-touch-abortion", "title"=>"No-Touch Abortion", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast101422_abortion.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1260808", "description"=>"<p><span>When the Dobbs decision went down, ER doctor Avir Mitra started to prepare for the worst — botched, at-home abortions that would land pregnant people in the emergency room. To prepare himself and his colleagues for the patients they might see, and to think through how best to treat them, Avir asked Laura MacIsaac, one of New York City’s leading gynecologists and abortion experts, to come talk to his ER department. But what Dr. MacIsaac had to say in her lecture wasn’t what Avir expected: she didn’t talk about how we’re going back in time and the horrors of self-harm as a means to an abortion. Instead, she painted a picture of progress — how in the last 40 years, through private practice and clinical trials all around the world, the process and science of providing and having an abortion has changed dramatically, mostly because of two types of pills: misoprostol and mifepristone. On this episode, Avir and Senior Correspondent Molly Webster visit Dr. MacIsaac to hear more, and also learn about a new study that indicates the process of abortion is on the precipice of even further change. </span></p>\n<p><span><em>Special thanks to </em><em><span>Mariana</span></em><em><span> Prandini Assis and Pam Belluck</span></em><em><span>.</span></em></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>Episode Credits:<br></strong>Reported by Avir Mitra and Molly Webster<br>Produced by Sarah Qari<br>Mixing help from Arianne Wack<br>Fact-checking by Diane Kelly<br>Edited by Becca Bressler<br><br></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>", "tease"=>"From surgery to pills, we look at how abortions have changed since the 1970s, and how they might still change.", "publishTime"=>1665756000000, "publish-at"=>"2022-10-14T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1665756000, "estimated-duration"=>1561, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/10/NO-TOUCH-ABORTION_EPS_221013_SophiaTwigt.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/10/NO-TOUCH-ABORTION_EPS_221013_SophiaTwigt.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.instagram.com/sophiatwigt", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/10/NO-TOUCH-ABORTION_EPS_221013_SophiaTwigt.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>202456, "credits-name"=>"Sophia Twigt"}, "objectID"=>"1260808", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"No-Touch Abortion", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>When the Dobbs decision went down, ER doctor Avir Mitra started to prepare for the worst — botched, at-home abortions that would land pregnant people in the emergency room. To prepare himself and his colleagues for the patients they might see, and to think through how best to treat them, Avir asked Laura MacIsaac, one of New York City’s leading gynecologists and abortion experts, to come talk to his ER department. But what Dr. MacIsaac had to say in her lecture wasn’t what Avir expected: she didn’t talk about how we’re going back in time and the horrors of self-harm as a means to an abortion. Instead, she painted a picture of progress — how in the last 40 years, through private practice and clinical trials all around the world, the process and science of providing and having an abortion has changed dramatically, mostly because of two types of pills: misoprostol and mifepristone. On this episode, Avir and Senior Correspondent Molly Webster visit Dr. MacIsaac to hear more, and also learn about a new study that indicates the process of abortion is on the precipice of even further change. </span></p>\n<p><span><em>Special thanks to </em><em><span>Mariana</span></em><em><span> Prandini Assis and Pam Belluck</span></em><em><span>.</span></em></span></p>\n<p><span><strong>Episode Credits:<br></strong>Reported by Avir Mitra and Molly Webster<br>Produced by Sarah Qari<br>Mixing help from Arianne Wack<br>Fact-checking by Diane Kelly<br>Edited by Becca Bressler<br><br></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-10-14T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"the-theater-of-david-byrnes-mind", "title"=>"The Theater of David Byrne's Mind", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast100722_livebyrne.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1255625", "description"=>"<p><span>It all started when the rockstar David Byrne did a Freaky-Friday-like body-swap with a Barbie Doll. That’s what inspired him — along with his collaborator Mala Gaonkar — to transform a 15,000 square-foot warehouse in Denver, Colorado into a brainy funhouse known as the <em>Theater of the Mind</em>.</span></p>\n<p><span>This episode, co-Host Latif Nasser moderates a live conversation between Byrne and Neuroscientist Thalia Wheatley at the Denver Center for the Performing Arts. The trio talk about how we don’t see what we think we see, don’t hear what we think we hear, and don’t know what we think we know, but also how all that… might actually be a good thing.</span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Charlie Miller and everyone else at the Denver Center for the Performing Arts, Emily Simoness and everyone else at the Arbutus Foundation, Boen Wang, and Heather Radke. </em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span>Episode Credits:</span></p>\n<p><span>Produced by Suzie Lechtenberg</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>CITATIONS</p>\n<p>Theater of the mind website: https://theateroftheminddenver.com/</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br><br></span></em><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org/\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span>(https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br><br></span></em><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<p><span><em><span> </span></em></span></p>", "tease"=>"A rockstar and a neuroscientist walk onto a stage...", "publishTime"=>1665147600000, "publish-at"=>"2022-10-07T09:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1665151200, "estimated-duration"=>2586, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/10/BYRNE_EPS_221007.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/l/80/2022/10/BYRNE_EPS_221007.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"Annie Barbour in Theater of the Mind", "credits-url"=>"https://matthewdefeo.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/10/BYRNE_EPS_221007.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>202373, "credits-name"=>"Matthew DeFeo | Theater of the Mind | DCPA"}, "objectID"=>"1255625", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Theater of David Byrne's Mind", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>It all started when the rockstar David Byrne did a Freaky-Friday-like body-swap with a Barbie Doll. That’s what inspired him — along with his collaborator Mala Gaonkar — to transform a 15,000 square-foot warehouse in Denver, Colorado into a brainy funhouse known as the <em>Theater of the Mind</em>.</span></p>\n<p><span>This episode, co-Host Latif Nasser moderates a live conversation between Byrne and Neuroscientist Thalia Wheatley at the Denver Center for the Performing Arts. The trio talk about how we don’t see what we think we see, don’t hear what we think we hear, and don’t know what we think we know, but also how all that… might actually be a good thing.</span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Charlie Miller and everyone else at the Denver Center for the Performing Arts, Emily Simoness and everyone else at the Arbutus Foundation, Boen Wang, and Heather Radke. </em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span>Episode Credits:</span></p>\n<p><span>Produced by Suzie Lechtenberg</span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p>CITATIONS</p>\n<p>Theater of the mind website: https://theateroftheminddenver.com/</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br><br></span></em><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org/\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span>(https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br><br></span></em><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<p><span><em><span> </span></em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-10-07T09:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"playing-god-2209", "title"=>"Playing God", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast093022_playinggod.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1255426", "description"=>"<p><span>When people are dying and you can only save some, how do you choose? Maybe you save the youngest. Or the sickest. Maybe you even just put all the names in a hat and pick at random. Would your answer change if a sick person was right in front of you?</span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode, first aired back in 2016, we follow </span><em><span>New York Times</span></em><span> reporter Sheri Fink as she searches for the answer. In a warzone, a hurricane, a church basement, and an earthquake, the question remains the same. What happens, what should happen, when humans are forced to play God?</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Very special thanks to Lilly Sullivan. </span></em></p>\n<p>Special thanks also to: <em><span>Pat Walters and Jim McCutcheon and Todd Menesses from WWL in New Orleans, the researchers for the allocation of scarce resources project in Maryland - Dr. Lee Daugherty Biddison from Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine, Howie Gwon from the Johns Hopkins Medicine Office of Emergency Management, Alan Regenberg of the Berman Institute of Bioethics and Dr. Eric Toner of the UPMC Center for Health Security.</span></em></p>\n<h3><strong>Episode Credits:</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Reported by</em> - <em>Reported by Sheri Fink.<br></em><em>Produced by</em><strong><em><span><span> - </span></span></em></strong><em><em>Produced by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen.</em></em></p>\n<h3><strong>Citations:</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Articles:<br></em><span>You can find more about the work going on in Maryland at: </span><a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/triage\"><span>www.nytimes.com/triage<br></span></a><em>Books: <br></em>The book that inspired this episode about what transpired at Memorial Hospital during Hurricane Katrina, <a href=\"http://www.sherifink.net/\">Sheri Fink’s</a> exhaustively reported <a href=\"https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/sheri-fink/five-days-memorial/\">Five Days at Memorial</a>, now a series on Apple TV+.</p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><span><span></span></span><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><span><span></span></span><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span> </span></em></p>", "tease"=>"When people are dying and you can only save some, how do you choose?", "publishTime"=>1664546400000, "publish-at"=>"2022-09-30T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1664546400, "estimated-duration"=>3530, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"A photo of hospital cots stretch around what looks like the wall of a stadium breezeway. A light pink outline of Radiolab's logo, the freq, is superimposed over the photo.", "name"=>"2022/09/playinggod_EPS_220930.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/09/playinggod_EPS_220930.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/09/playinggod_EPS_220930.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>202267, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1255426", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Playing God", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>When people are dying and you can only save some, how do you choose? Maybe you save the youngest. Or the sickest. Maybe you even just put all the names in a hat and pick at random. Would your answer change if a sick person was right in front of you?</span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode, first aired back in 2016, we follow </span><em><span>New York Times</span></em><span> reporter Sheri Fink as she searches for the answer. In a warzone, a hurricane, a church basement, and an earthquake, the question remains the same. What happens, what should happen, when humans are forced to play God?</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Very special thanks to Lilly Sullivan. </span></em></p>\n<p>Special thanks also to: <em><span>Pat Walters and Jim McCutcheon and Todd Menesses from WWL in New Orleans, the researchers for the allocation of scarce resources project in Maryland - Dr. Lee Daugherty Biddison from Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine, Howie Gwon from the Johns Hopkins Medicine Office of Emergency Management, Alan Regenberg of the Berman Institute of Bioethics and Dr. Eric Toner of the UPMC Center for Health Security.</span></em></p>\n<h3><strong>Episode Credits:</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Reported by</em> - <em>Reported by Sheri Fink.<br></em><em>Produced by</em><strong><em><span><span> - </span></span></em></strong><em><em>Produced by Simon Adler and Annie McEwen.</em></em></p>\n<h3><strong>Citations:</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Articles:<br></em><span>You can find more about the work going on in Maryland at: </span><a href=\"http://www.nytimes.com/triage\"><span>www.nytimes.com/triage<br></span></a><em>Books: <br></em>The book that inspired this episode about what transpired at Memorial Hospital during Hurricane Katrina, <a href=\"http://www.sherifink.net/\">Sheri Fink’s</a> exhaustively reported <a href=\"https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/sheri-fink/five-days-memorial/\">Five Days at Memorial</a>, now a series on Apple TV+.</p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><span><span></span></span><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><span><span></span></span><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span> </span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-09-30T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"terrestrials-mastermind", "title"=>"Terrestrials: The Mastermind", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast092322_tstmastermind.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1253958", "description"=>"<p><span>Lulu Miller, intrepid host and fearless mother of two, went off on her own and put together a little something for kids. All kids: hers, yours, and the one still living inside us all. </span></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab for Kids Presents: Terrestrials</span></em></p>\n<p><span>And it’s spellbinding. So much so, that we wanted to put this audio goodness in front of as many ears as possible. </span></p>\n<p><span>Which is why we’re running the first episode of that series here for you today. </span></p>\n<p><span>It’s called The Mastermind. In it, Sy Montgomery, an author and naturalist, shares the story of a color-changing creature many people assumed to be brainless who outsmarts his human captors. If you want a SPOILER of what the creature is, read on: It’s an octopus. We hear the story of one particularly devious octopus who lost a limb, was captured by humans, and then managed to make an escape from its aquarium tank—back into the ocean! The tale of “Inky” the octopus calls into question who we think of as intelligent (and kissable) in the animal kingdom.</span></p>\n<p><span>Learn about the storytellers, listen to music, and dig deeper into the stories you hear on Terrestrials with activities you can do at home or in the classroom on our website, Terrestrialspodcast.org </span></p>\n<p><span>Find MORE original </span><em><span>Terrestrials</span></em><span> fun on </span><a href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLLHAUHF-RPhkEwDeWKw0EO9WRkjXXrrmw\"><span>Youtube</span></a><strong>.</strong><strong><br></strong><span>And badger us on Social Media: @radiolab and #TerrestrialsPodcast</span></p>\n<p><span>And if your little ones or you want to hear more of Team Terrestrials amazing work on this series, please search for </span><em><span>Radiolab for Kids Presents: The Mastermind</span></em><span>, wherever you get podcasts <a href=\"https://link.chtbl.com/srqYcdlh?sid=radiolab.website\" target=\"_blank\">or subscribe here</a>. </span></p>\n<p><strong><em>Terrestrials</em></strong><span> is a production of WNYC Studios, created by Lulu Miller. This episode is produced by Ana González, Alan Goffinski and Lulu Miller. Original Music by Alan Goffinski. Help from Suzie Lechtenberg, Sarah Sandbach, Natalia Ramirez, and Sarita Bhatt. Fact-checking by Diane Kelley. Sound design by Mira Burt-Wintonick with additional engineering by Joe Plourde. Our storyteller this week is Sy Montgomery. Transcription by Caleb Codding.</span></p>\n<p><span>Our advisors are Theanne Griffith, Aliyah Elijah, Dominique Shabazz, John Green, Liza Steinberg-Demby, Tara Welty, and Alice Wong.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em><br><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span> </span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Lulu Miller, intrepid host and fearless mother of two boys, went off and put together a little something for kids. All kids: hers, yours, and the one still living inside us all.", "publishTime"=>1663941600000, "publish-at"=>"2022-09-23T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1663941600, "estimated-duration"=>1782, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Pink tentacle-like arms rise out of dark water, surrounded by lush greenery. In the background, a person dangles their feet off a dock, staring into the ripples they made, ignoring the enormous arms.", "name"=>"2022/09/TERRESTRIALS_MASTERMIND-IMAGE-220923_AnandTara.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/09/TERRESTRIALS_MASTERMIND-IMAGE-220923_AnandTara.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/09/TERRESTRIALS_MASTERMIND-IMAGE-220923_AnandTara.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>202189, "credits-name"=>" Tara Anand / WNYC Studios "}, "objectID"=>"1253958", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Terrestrials: The Mastermind", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Lulu Miller, intrepid host and fearless mother of two, went off on her own and put together a little something for kids. All kids: hers, yours, and the one still living inside us all. </span></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab for Kids Presents: Terrestrials</span></em></p>\n<p><span>And it’s spellbinding. So much so, that we wanted to put this audio goodness in front of as many ears as possible. </span></p>\n<p><span>Which is why we’re running the first episode of that series here for you today. </span></p>\n<p><span>It’s called The Mastermind. In it, Sy Montgomery, an author and naturalist, shares the story of a color-changing creature many people assumed to be brainless who outsmarts his human captors. If you want a SPOILER of what the creature is, read on: It’s an octopus. We hear the story of one particularly devious octopus who lost a limb, was captured by humans, and then managed to make an escape from its aquarium tank—back into the ocean! The tale of “Inky” the octopus calls into question who we think of as intelligent (and kissable) in the animal kingdom.</span></p>\n<p><span>Learn about the storytellers, listen to music, and dig deeper into the stories you hear on Terrestrials with activities you can do at home or in the classroom on our website, Terrestrialspodcast.org </span></p>\n<p><span>Find MORE original </span><em><span>Terrestrials</span></em><span> fun on </span><a href=\"https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLLHAUHF-RPhkEwDeWKw0EO9WRkjXXrrmw\"><span>Youtube</span></a><strong>.</strong><strong><br></strong><span>And badger us on Social Media: @radiolab and #TerrestrialsPodcast</span></p>\n<p><span>And if your little ones or you want to hear more of Team Terrestrials amazing work on this series, please search for </span><em><span>Radiolab for Kids Presents: The Mastermind</span></em><span>, wherever you get podcasts <a href=\"https://link.chtbl.com/srqYcdlh?sid=radiolab.website\" target=\"_blank\">or subscribe here</a>. </span></p>\n<p><strong><em>Terrestrials</em></strong><span> is a production of WNYC Studios, created by Lulu Miller. This episode is produced by Ana González, Alan Goffinski and Lulu Miller. Original Music by Alan Goffinski. Help from Suzie Lechtenberg, Sarah Sandbach, Natalia Ramirez, and Sarita Bhatt. Fact-checking by Diane Kelley. Sound design by Mira Burt-Wintonick with additional engineering by Joe Plourde. Our storyteller this week is Sy Montgomery. Transcription by Caleb Codding.</span></p>\n<p><span>Our advisors are Theanne Griffith, Aliyah Elijah, Dominique Shabazz, John Green, Liza Steinberg-Demby, Tara Welty, and Alice Wong.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em><br><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span> </span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-09-23T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"quicksaaaand-2209", "title"=>"Quicksaaaand!", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast091622_quicksand.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1251378", "description"=>"<p><span>For many of us, quicksand was once a real fear — it held a vise grip on our imaginations, from childish sandbox games to grown-up anxieties about venturing into unknown lands. But these days, quicksand can't even scare an 8-year-old. In this short, we try to find out why. </span></p>\n<p><span>Then-Producer Soren Wheeler introduces us to Dan Engber, writer and columnist for </span><em><span>Slate</span></em><span>, now with </span><em><span>The Atlantic</span></em><span>. Dan became obsessed with quicksand after happening upon a strange fact: kids are no longer afraid of it. In this episode, Dan recounts for Soren and Robert Krulwich the story of his obsession. He immersed himself in research, compiled mountains of data, met with quicksand fetishists and, in the end, formulated a theory about why the terror of his childhood seems to have lost its menacing allure. Then Carlton Cuse, who at the time we first aired this episode was best-known as the writer and executive producer of </span><em><span>Lost</span></em><span>, helps us think about whether giant pits of hero-swallowing mud might one day creep back into the spotlight.</span><span><br></span><span><br>And, as this episode first aired in 2013, we can see if we were right.<br></span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><strong>Episode Credits:<br><br></strong>Reported and produced by Soren Wheeler</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<hr>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"Quicksand! Once a visual metaphor of the unknown, it has all but disappeared from popular culture. We wanted to know why.", "publishTime"=>1663336800000, "publish-at"=>"2022-09-16T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1663336800, "estimated-duration"=>966, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"A hand reaching up and out of a sea of sand. ", "name"=>"2022/09/QUICKSAND-EPS-IMG-220916.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/09/QUICKSAND-EPS-IMG-220916.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/09/QUICKSAND-EPS-IMG-220916.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>202076, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1251378", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Quicksaaaand!", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>For many of us, quicksand was once a real fear — it held a vise grip on our imaginations, from childish sandbox games to grown-up anxieties about venturing into unknown lands. But these days, quicksand can't even scare an 8-year-old. In this short, we try to find out why. </span></p>\n<p><span>Then-Producer Soren Wheeler introduces us to Dan Engber, writer and columnist for </span><em><span>Slate</span></em><span>, now with </span><em><span>The Atlantic</span></em><span>. Dan became obsessed with quicksand after happening upon a strange fact: kids are no longer afraid of it. In this episode, Dan recounts for Soren and Robert Krulwich the story of his obsession. He immersed himself in research, compiled mountains of data, met with quicksand fetishists and, in the end, formulated a theory about why the terror of his childhood seems to have lost its menacing allure. Then Carlton Cuse, who at the time we first aired this episode was best-known as the writer and executive producer of </span><em><span>Lost</span></em><span>, helps us think about whether giant pits of hero-swallowing mud might one day creep back into the spotlight.</span><span><br></span><span><br>And, as this episode first aired in 2013, we can see if we were right.<br></span></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><span><strong>Episode Credits:<br><br></strong>Reported and produced by Soren Wheeler</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<hr>\n<p> </p>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-09-16T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"40000-recipes-murder", "title"=>"40,000 Recipes for Murder", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast090922_40krecipes.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1248400", "description"=>"<p><span>Two scientists realize that the very same AI technology they have developed to discover medicines for rare diseases can also discover the most potent chemical weapons known to humankind. Inadvertently opening the Pandora’s Box of WMDs. What should they do now?</span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to, Xander Davies, Timnit Gebru, Jessica Fjeld, Bert Gambini and Charlotte Hsu<br><br></em><strong>Episode Credits:</strong></p>\n<p>Reported by Latif Nasser<br>Produced by Matt Kielty<br>Original music and sound design contributed by Matt Kielty<br>Mixing help from Arianne Wack<br>Fact-checking by Emily Krieger<br><br><strong>CITATIONS:<br><br></strong><span></span><strong>Articles:<br></strong><span>Read the Sean and Fabio’s paper </span><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s42256-022-00465-9\"><span>here</span></a><span>. <br></span><span>Get Yan Liu’s book </span><em><span>Healing with Poisons: Potent Medicines in Medieval China</span></em> <a href=\"https://uwapress.uw.edu/book/9780295748993/healing-with-poisons/\"><span>here</span></a><span>. Yan is now Assistant Professor of History at the University at Buffalo.<br><br></span><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br></span></em><em><span><br>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><em><span><br>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br></span></em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<p><span></span><strong><strong><strong><br></strong></strong></strong></p>", "tease"=>"Two scientists inadvertently open&nbsp;the Pandora&rsquo;s Box of WMDs. What now?", "publishTime"=>1662732000000, "publish-at"=>"2022-09-09T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1662732000, "estimated-duration"=>1815, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/09/40krecipes-EPS-220909_eKr7lEX.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/09/40krecipes-EPS-220909_eKr7lEX.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/09/40krecipes-EPS-220909_eKr7lEX.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>201961, "credits-name"=>"(Radiolab)"}, "objectID"=>"1248400", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"40,000 Recipes for Murder", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Two scientists realize that the very same AI technology they have developed to discover medicines for rare diseases can also discover the most potent chemical weapons known to humankind. Inadvertently opening the Pandora’s Box of WMDs. What should they do now?</span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to, Xander Davies, Timnit Gebru, Jessica Fjeld, Bert Gambini and Charlotte Hsu<br><br></em><strong>Episode Credits:</strong></p>\n<p>Reported by Latif Nasser<br>Produced by Matt Kielty<br>Original music and sound design contributed by Matt Kielty<br>Mixing help from Arianne Wack<br>Fact-checking by Emily Krieger<br><br><strong>CITATIONS:<br><br></strong><span></span><strong>Articles:<br></strong><span>Read the Sean and Fabio’s paper </span><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s42256-022-00465-9\"><span>here</span></a><span>. <br></span><span>Get Yan Liu’s book </span><em><span>Healing with Poisons: Potent Medicines in Medieval China</span></em> <a href=\"https://uwapress.uw.edu/book/9780295748993/healing-with-poisons/\"><span>here</span></a><span>. Yan is now Assistant Professor of History at the University at Buffalo.<br><br></span><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br></span></em><em><span><br>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><em><span><br>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br></span></em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<p><span></span><strong><strong><strong><br></strong></strong></strong></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-09-09T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"rodney-v-death-2209", "title"=>"Rodney v. Death", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast090222_rodneyvdeath.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1245192", "description"=>"<p><span>In the fall of 2004, Jeanna Giese checked into the Children's Hospital of Wisconsin with a set of puzzling symptoms... and her condition was deteriorating fast. By the time Dr. Rodney Willoughby saw her, he only knew one thing for sure: if Jeanna's disturbing breakdown turned out to be rabies, she was doomed to die.</span></p>\n<p><span>What happened next seemed like a medical impossibility. In this episode, originally aired in 2013, Producer </span><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/people/tim-howard/\"><span>Tim Howard</span></a><span> tells Jeanna's story and talks to authors Monica Murphy and Bill Wasik, and scientists Amy Gilbert and Sergio Recuenco, while trying to unravel the mystery of an unusual patient and the doctor who dared to take on certain death.</span></p>\n<p><span><strong>Episode credits:</strong><br></span></p>\n<p><span>Reported and produced by Tim Howard<strong><strong><br></strong></strong></span></p>\n<p><strong>CITATIONS:</strong></p>\n<p><span></span><strong>Articles:<br></strong><strong>\"</strong><a href=\"http://www.wired.com/wiredscience/2012/07/ff_rabies/all/\"><strong>Undead: The Rabies Virus Remains a Medical Mystery</strong></a><strong>,\" </strong><strong><em>Wired</em></strong><strong> article by Monica Murphy and Bill Wasik</strong></p>\n<p><strong>\"</strong><a href=\"http://www.wired.com/wiredscience/2012/08/rabies-survivor-mystery-deepens/\"><strong>Bats Incredible: The Mystery of Rabies Survivorship Deepens</strong></a><strong>,\" </strong><strong><em>Wired</em></strong><strong> article by Monica Murphy and Bill Wasik</strong></p>\n<p><strong>\"</strong><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/media/releases/2012/p0801_rabies_immune.html\"><strong>Study Detects Rabies Immune Response in Amazon Populations</strong></a><strong>,\" the CDC's page on Amy Gilbert and Sergio Recuenco's work (inc. photos from Peru)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>\"</strong><a href=\"http://www.cdc.gov/rabies/resources/news/mp-haiti-lg.html\"><strong>Selection Criteria for Milwaukee Protocol</strong></a><strong>,\" when to try the Milwaukee Protoco</strong><span>l</span></p>\n<p><strong>Books:<br></strong><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0143123572/radiolabbooks-20/\"><strong><em>Rabid: A Cultural History of the World's Most Diabolical Virus</em></strong></a><strong>, by Bill Wasik and Monica Murphy<br><br></strong><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><span></span><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><span></span><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"What do you do in the face of a monstrous disease with a 100% fatality rate?", "publishTime"=>1662040800000, "publish-at"=>"2022-09-01T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1662127200, "estimated-duration"=>1995, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/09/RodneyVDeath_EPS-220901.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/09/RodneyVDeath_EPS-220901.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/09/RodneyVDeath_EPS-220901.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>201861, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1245192", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Rodney v. Death", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In the fall of 2004, Jeanna Giese checked into the Children's Hospital of Wisconsin with a set of puzzling symptoms... and her condition was deteriorating fast. By the time Dr. Rodney Willoughby saw her, he only knew one thing for sure: if Jeanna's disturbing breakdown turned out to be rabies, she was doomed to die.</span></p>\n<p><span>What happened next seemed like a medical impossibility. In this episode, originally aired in 2013, Producer </span><a href=\"http://www.radiolab.org/people/tim-howard/\"><span>Tim Howard</span></a><span> tells Jeanna's story and talks to authors Monica Murphy and Bill Wasik, and scientists Amy Gilbert and Sergio Recuenco, while trying to unravel the mystery of an unusual patient and the doctor who dared to take on certain death.</span></p>\n<p><span><strong>Episode credits:</strong><br></span></p>\n<p><span>Reported and produced by Tim Howard<strong><strong><br></strong></strong></span></p>\n<p><strong>CITATIONS:</strong></p>\n<p><span></span><strong>Articles:<br></strong><strong>\"</strong><a href=\"http://www.wired.com/wiredscience/2012/07/ff_rabies/all/\"><strong>Undead: The Rabies Virus Remains a Medical Mystery</strong></a><strong>,\" </strong><strong><em>Wired</em></strong><strong> article by Monica Murphy and Bill Wasik</strong></p>\n<p><strong>\"</strong><a href=\"http://www.wired.com/wiredscience/2012/08/rabies-survivor-mystery-deepens/\"><strong>Bats Incredible: The Mystery of Rabies Survivorship Deepens</strong></a><strong>,\" </strong><strong><em>Wired</em></strong><strong> article by Monica Murphy and Bill Wasik</strong></p>\n<p><strong>\"</strong><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/media/releases/2012/p0801_rabies_immune.html\"><strong>Study Detects Rabies Immune Response in Amazon Populations</strong></a><strong>,\" the CDC's page on Amy Gilbert and Sergio Recuenco's work (inc. photos from Peru)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>\"</strong><a href=\"http://www.cdc.gov/rabies/resources/news/mp-haiti-lg.html\"><strong>Selection Criteria for Milwaukee Protocol</strong></a><strong>,\" when to try the Milwaukee Protoco</strong><span>l</span></p>\n<p><strong>Books:<br></strong><a href=\"http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0143123572/radiolabbooks-20/\"><strong><em>Rabid: A Cultural History of the World's Most Diabolical Virus</em></strong></a><strong>, by Bill Wasik and Monica Murphy<br><br></strong><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><span></span><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><span></span><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>\n<p> </p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-09-01T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"gigaverse", "title"=>"Gigaverse", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast082622_gigaverse.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1243422", "description"=>"<p><span>A pizzeria owner in Kansas realizes that DoorDash is hijacking his pizzas. A Lyft driver conquers the streets of San Francisco until he unwittingly puts his family in danger. A Shipt shopper in Denton, Texas tries to crack the code of the delivery app that is slashing his pay. This week, Host Latif Nasser, Producer Becca Bressler, and Philosophy Professor Barry Lam dive into the ins and outs of a new and growing part of our world: the gig economy. <br><br><em>Special thanks to, Julie Wernau, Drew Ambrogi, David Condos, David Pickerell, Cory Doctorow, Katherine Mangu-Ward, Coby McDonald, Bret Jaspers, Peter Haden, Bill Pollock, Tanya Chawla, and Mateo Schimpf.</em><br></span></p>\n<p><strong>Episode Credits:</strong></p>\n<p>Reported by Becca Bressler, Latif Nasser, and Barry Lam<br><span>Produced by Becca Bressler, Eli Cohen, and Sindhu Gnanasambandan.</span><br><span>Original music and sound design contributed by Jeremy Bloom and Becca Bressler.<br>Mixing help from Arianne Wack <br>Fact-checking by Natalie Middleton <br>Edited by Pat Walters <br></span></p>\n<p><strong>CITATIONS<br></strong><strong>Articles:<br></strong>Subscribe to Ranjan Roy's newsletter, <a href=\"https://www.readmargins.com/\">Margins, here</a>.</p>\n<p><span>Jeffrey’s story was originally reported by Lauren Smiley for WIRED. Check out her </span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/story/gig-economy-uber-lyft-doordash-jeffrey-fang/\"><span>piece</span></a> <span>for an even more in-depth look at his life as a gig driver.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Audio:<br></strong><span>Check out Barry Lam’s </span><a href=\"https://hiphination.org/\"><span>podcast</span></a><span> Hi-Phi Nation, a show about philosophy that turns stories into ideas. <br><br></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br><br></span></em><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br><br></span></em><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br></span></em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span> </span></em></p>", "tease"=>"The gig economy is an entire universe. What is it like working inside of it?", "publishTime"=>1661522400000, "publish-at"=>"2022-08-26T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1661522400, "estimated-duration"=>2970, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/08/GIGAVERSE_EPS-IMG-220826.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/08/GIGAVERSE_EPS-IMG-220826.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/08/GIGAVERSE_EPS-IMG-220826.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>201784, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1243422", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Gigaverse", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>A pizzeria owner in Kansas realizes that DoorDash is hijacking his pizzas. A Lyft driver conquers the streets of San Francisco until he unwittingly puts his family in danger. A Shipt shopper in Denton, Texas tries to crack the code of the delivery app that is slashing his pay. This week, Host Latif Nasser, Producer Becca Bressler, and Philosophy Professor Barry Lam dive into the ins and outs of a new and growing part of our world: the gig economy. <br><br><em>Special thanks to, Julie Wernau, Drew Ambrogi, David Condos, David Pickerell, Cory Doctorow, Katherine Mangu-Ward, Coby McDonald, Bret Jaspers, Peter Haden, Bill Pollock, Tanya Chawla, and Mateo Schimpf.</em><br></span></p>\n<p><strong>Episode Credits:</strong></p>\n<p>Reported by Becca Bressler, Latif Nasser, and Barry Lam<br><span>Produced by Becca Bressler, Eli Cohen, and Sindhu Gnanasambandan.</span><br><span>Original music and sound design contributed by Jeremy Bloom and Becca Bressler.<br>Mixing help from Arianne Wack <br>Fact-checking by Natalie Middleton <br>Edited by Pat Walters <br></span></p>\n<p><strong>CITATIONS<br></strong><strong>Articles:<br></strong>Subscribe to Ranjan Roy's newsletter, <a href=\"https://www.readmargins.com/\">Margins, here</a>.</p>\n<p><span>Jeffrey’s story was originally reported by Lauren Smiley for WIRED. Check out her </span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/story/gig-economy-uber-lyft-doordash-jeffrey-fang/\"><span>piece</span></a> <span>for an even more in-depth look at his life as a gig driver.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Audio:<br></strong><span>Check out Barry Lam’s </span><a href=\"https://hiphination.org/\"><span>podcast</span></a><span> Hi-Phi Nation, a show about philosophy that turns stories into ideas. <br><br></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br><br></span></em><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br><br></span></em><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br></span></em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span> </span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-08-26T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"9-volt-nirvana-2208", "title"=>"9-Volt Nirvana", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast081922_ninevolt.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1241072", "description"=>"<p><span>Learn a new language faster than ever! Leave doubt in the dust! Be a better sniper! Could you do all that and more with just a zap to the noggin? Maybe.</span></p>\n<p><span>Back in the early 2010s, Sally Adee, then an editor at </span><em><span>New Scientist Magazine</span></em><span>, went to a DARPA (Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency) conference and heard about a way to speed up learning with something called trans-cranial direct current stimulation (tDCS). A couple of years later, Sally found herself wielding an M4 assault rifle to pick off simulated enemy combatants with a battery wired to her temple. But that got then-producer Soren Wheeler thinking about this burgeoning world of electroceuticals, and if real, what limits will it reach.</span></p>\n<p><span>For this episode, first aired back in 2014, we brought in Michael Weisend, then a neuroscientist at Wright State Research Institute, to tell us how it works (Bonus: you get to hear Jad get his brain zapped). And sat down with Peter Reiner and Nick Fitz, then at the University of British Columbia, to help us think through the consequences of a world where anyone with 20 dollars and access to a circuit board and a soldering iron, can make their own brain zapper. And then checked-in again to hear about the unexpected after-effects a day of super-charged sniper training can have on one mild-mannered science journalist.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Episode credits:</strong></p>\n<p>Reported by Sally Adee and Soren Wheeler<br>Original music by Brian Carpenter's Ghost Train Orchestra</p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><br><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>", "tease"=>"Learn a new language faster than ever! Leave doubt in the dust! Be a better sniper! Could you do all that and more with just a zap to the noggin? Maybe.", "publishTime"=>1660917600000, "publish-at"=>"2022-08-19T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1660917600, "estimated-duration"=>1576, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/08/9-volt-nirvana-EPS_220819-02.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/08/9-volt-nirvana-EPS_220819-02.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/08/9-volt-nirvana-EPS_220819-02.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>201702, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1241072", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"9-Volt Nirvana", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Learn a new language faster than ever! Leave doubt in the dust! Be a better sniper! Could you do all that and more with just a zap to the noggin? Maybe.</span></p>\n<p><span>Back in the early 2010s, Sally Adee, then an editor at </span><em><span>New Scientist Magazine</span></em><span>, went to a DARPA (Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency) conference and heard about a way to speed up learning with something called trans-cranial direct current stimulation (tDCS). A couple of years later, Sally found herself wielding an M4 assault rifle to pick off simulated enemy combatants with a battery wired to her temple. But that got then-producer Soren Wheeler thinking about this burgeoning world of electroceuticals, and if real, what limits will it reach.</span></p>\n<p><span>For this episode, first aired back in 2014, we brought in Michael Weisend, then a neuroscientist at Wright State Research Institute, to tell us how it works (Bonus: you get to hear Jad get his brain zapped). And sat down with Peter Reiner and Nick Fitz, then at the University of British Columbia, to help us think through the consequences of a world where anyone with 20 dollars and access to a circuit board and a soldering iron, can make their own brain zapper. And then checked-in again to hear about the unexpected after-effects a day of super-charged sniper training can have on one mild-mannered science journalist.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Episode credits:</strong></p>\n<p>Reported by Sally Adee and Soren Wheeler<br>Original music by Brian Carpenter's Ghost Train Orchestra</p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><br><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-08-19T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"infinities", "title"=>"Infinities", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast081222_infinities.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1238475", "description"=>"<p><span>In August 2018, Boen Wang was at a work retreat for a new job. Surrounded by mosquitoes and swampland in a tiny campsite in West Virginia, Boen’s mind underwent a sudden, dramatic transformation that would have profound consequences—for his work, his colleagues, and himself.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Grace Gilbert for voice acting and episode art, and to Professors Erin Anderson and Maggie Jones for editorial support. <br><br></span></em><strong>Episode credits:</strong><em><span><br></span></em></p>\n<p>Reported and produced by Boen Wang<br>Original Music provided by Alex Zhang Hungtai<br>Fact-checking by Diane Kelly<br>Edited by Pat Walters</p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br><br></span></em><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><br><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>", "tease"=>"One man desperately tries to hold on as his mind splits into infinities.", "publishTime"=>1660312800000, "publish-at"=>"2022-08-12T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1660312800, "estimated-duration"=>2491, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/08/INFINITIES_EPS-220812_GraceGilbert_K11pMQR.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/08/INFINITIES_EPS-220812_GraceGilbert_K11pMQR.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://gracegegilbert.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/08/INFINITIES_EPS-220812_GraceGilbert_K11pMQR.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>201623, "credits-name"=>"Grace Gilbert"}, "objectID"=>"1238475", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Infinities", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>In August 2018, Boen Wang was at a work retreat for a new job. Surrounded by mosquitoes and swampland in a tiny campsite in West Virginia, Boen’s mind underwent a sudden, dramatic transformation that would have profound consequences—for his work, his colleagues, and himself.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Grace Gilbert for voice acting and episode art, and to Professors Erin Anderson and Maggie Jones for editorial support. <br><br></span></em><strong>Episode credits:</strong><em><span><br></span></em></p>\n<p>Reported and produced by Boen Wang<br>Original Music provided by Alex Zhang Hungtai<br>Fact-checking by Diane Kelly<br>Edited by Pat Walters</p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!<br><br></span></em><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.<br></span></em><br><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-08-12T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"escape-2208", "title"=>"Escape", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast080522_escape.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1236240", "description"=>"<p><span>This episode originally aired in 2012.</span></p>\n<p><span>An all-star lineup of producers — Pat Walters, Lynn Levy, and Sean Cole — bring you stories about traps, getaways, perpetual cycles, and staggering breakthroughs. </span></p>\n<p><span>We kick things off with a true escape artist — a man who’s broken out of jail more times than anyone alive. Why does he keep running... and will he ever stop? Next, the ingeniously simple question that led Isaac Newton to an enormous intellectual breakthrough: why doesn’t the moon fall out of the sky? In the wake of Newton's new idea, we find ourselves in a strange space at the edge of the solar system, about to cross a boundary beyond which we know nothing. Finally, we hear the story of a blind kid who freed himself from an unhappy childhood by climbing into the telephone system, and bending it to his will.</span></p>\n<p><span>Now sit back, relax and enjoy what we hope will prove to be a welcomed Escape.<br><br><strong>Episode Credits:<br></strong><br><span>Reported and produced by Pat Walters, Lynn Levy, and Sean Cole</span></span></p>", "tease"=>"Whether to defy death or just to slip out of an awkward situation, we all need to sneak away now and again. Here&rsquo;s your escape!", "publishTime"=>1659708000000, "publish-at"=>"2022-08-05T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1659708000, "estimated-duration"=>4024, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/08/ESCAPE-EPS_220805.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/08/ESCAPE-EPS_220805.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/08/ESCAPE-EPS_220805.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>201536, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1236240", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Escape", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This episode originally aired in 2012.</span></p>\n<p><span>An all-star lineup of producers — Pat Walters, Lynn Levy, and Sean Cole — bring you stories about traps, getaways, perpetual cycles, and staggering breakthroughs. </span></p>\n<p><span>We kick things off with a true escape artist — a man who’s broken out of jail more times than anyone alive. Why does he keep running... and will he ever stop? Next, the ingeniously simple question that led Isaac Newton to an enormous intellectual breakthrough: why doesn’t the moon fall out of the sky? In the wake of Newton's new idea, we find ourselves in a strange space at the edge of the solar system, about to cross a boundary beyond which we know nothing. Finally, we hear the story of a blind kid who freed himself from an unhappy childhood by climbing into the telephone system, and bending it to his will.</span></p>\n<p><span>Now sit back, relax and enjoy what we hope will prove to be a welcomed Escape.<br><br><strong>Episode Credits:<br></strong><br><span>Reported and produced by Pat Walters, Lynn Levy, and Sean Cole</span></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-08-05T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"humpback-and-killer", "title"=>"The Humpback and the Killer", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast072922_whales.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1233831", "description"=>"<p><span>Killer whales — orcas — eat all sorts of animals, including humpback calves. But one day, biologists saw a group of humpback whales trying to stop some killer whales from eating… a seal. And then it happened again. And again. It turns out, all across the oceans, humpback whales are swimming around stopping killer whales from hunting all kinds of animals — from seals to gray whales to sunfish. And of course while many scientists explain this behavior as the result of blind instincts that are ultimately selfish, much of the world celebrates humpbacks as superhero vigilantes of the sea. But when Annie McEwen dug into what was really going on between humpbacks and killer whales, she found a set of stories that refused to fit in either of those two ways of seeing the world.<br><br></span><em>Special thanks to Eric J. Gleske and Brendan Brucker at Media Services, Oregon State University as well as Colleen Talty at Monterey Bay Whale Watch and California Killer Whale Project. Special thanks also to Doug McKnight and Giuliana Mayo.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Episode Credits:</strong><br>Reported and produced by Annie McEwen<br>Original music and sound design by Annie McEwen<br>Mixing help from Arianne Wack<br>Fact-checking by Diane Kelly<br>Edited by Becca Bressler</p>\n<p><em>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. Sign up (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</em></p>\n<p><em>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of <a href=\"https://members.radiolab.org/\">The Lab</a> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</em></p>\n<p><em>Follow our show on Instagram, Twitter and Facebook @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing radiolab@wnyc.org.</em></p>\n<p><strong>CITATIONS:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Videos:</strong><br>Alisa Schulman-Janiger took <strong><a href=\"https://zpr.io/5mYNTWpxs5GV\">this video</a></strong> (https://zpr.io/5mYNTWpxs5GV) of the humpbacks defending the gray whale calf’s carcass from the killer whales.</p>\n<p><strong>Articles:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://zpr.io/iU9shuNW9tAj\">Read Robert Pitman’s (et al) paper</a> </strong>(https://zpr.io/iU9shuNW9tAj) about the humpbacks saving the seal and a review of the 115 interactions they collected between humpbacks and killer whales.</p>\n<p><strong>Books:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://zpr.io/2BHBermJJfKj\"><em>The World in the Whale</em></a> </strong>(<a href=\"https://zpr.io/2BHBermJJfKj\">https://zpr.io/2BHBermJJfKj</a>). If you are interested in whales, you are going to love this book.</p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"Oceans also have their vigilantes.", "publishTime"=>1659103200000, "publish-at"=>"2022-07-29T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1659103200, "estimated-duration"=>2143, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/07/WHALES_EPS-220729.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/07/WHALES_EPS-220729.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/07/WHALES_EPS-220729.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>201409, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1233831", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Humpback and the Killer", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Killer whales — orcas — eat all sorts of animals, including humpback calves. But one day, biologists saw a group of humpback whales trying to stop some killer whales from eating… a seal. And then it happened again. And again. It turns out, all across the oceans, humpback whales are swimming around stopping killer whales from hunting all kinds of animals — from seals to gray whales to sunfish. And of course while many scientists explain this behavior as the result of blind instincts that are ultimately selfish, much of the world celebrates humpbacks as superhero vigilantes of the sea. But when Annie McEwen dug into what was really going on between humpbacks and killer whales, she found a set of stories that refused to fit in either of those two ways of seeing the world.<br><br></span><em>Special thanks to Eric J. Gleske and Brendan Brucker at Media Services, Oregon State University as well as Colleen Talty at Monterey Bay Whale Watch and California Killer Whale Project. Special thanks also to Doug McKnight and Giuliana Mayo.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Episode Credits:</strong><br>Reported and produced by Annie McEwen<br>Original music and sound design by Annie McEwen<br>Mixing help from Arianne Wack<br>Fact-checking by Diane Kelly<br>Edited by Becca Bressler</p>\n<p><em>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. Sign up (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</em></p>\n<p><em>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of <a href=\"https://members.radiolab.org/\">The Lab</a> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</em></p>\n<p><em>Follow our show on Instagram, Twitter and Facebook @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing radiolab@wnyc.org.</em></p>\n<p><strong>CITATIONS:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Videos:</strong><br>Alisa Schulman-Janiger took <strong><a href=\"https://zpr.io/5mYNTWpxs5GV\">this video</a></strong> (https://zpr.io/5mYNTWpxs5GV) of the humpbacks defending the gray whale calf’s carcass from the killer whales.</p>\n<p><strong>Articles:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://zpr.io/iU9shuNW9tAj\">Read Robert Pitman’s (et al) paper</a> </strong>(https://zpr.io/iU9shuNW9tAj) about the humpbacks saving the seal and a review of the 115 interactions they collected between humpbacks and killer whales.</p>\n<p><strong>Books:</strong><br><strong><a href=\"https://zpr.io/2BHBermJJfKj\"><em>The World in the Whale</em></a> </strong>(<a href=\"https://zpr.io/2BHBermJJfKj\">https://zpr.io/2BHBermJJfKj</a>). If you are interested in whales, you are going to love this book.</p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-07-29T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"you-v-you-2207", "title"=>"You v. You", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast072222_youvyou.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1231112", "description"=>"<p><span>This episode, originally aired more than a decade ago, attempts to answer one question: how do you win against your worst impulses? </span><span><br></span><span><br></span><span>Zelda Gamson tried for decades to stop smoking, but the part of her that wanted to quit couldn’t beat the part of her that refused to let go. Adam Davidson, a co-founder of the NPR podcast </span><a href=\"http://www.npr.org/blogs/money/\"><em><span>Planet Money</span></em></a><span>, talked to one of the greatest negotiators of all time, Nobel Prize-winning Economist Thomas Schelling, whose tactical skills saw him through high-stakes conflicts during the Cold War but fell apart when he tried them on himself in his battle to quit smoking. And a baby Pat Walters complicates things — in a good way — with the story of two brothers, Dennis and Kai Woo, who forged a deal with each other that wound up determining both of their futures.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span></span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org/\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span><span> </span>(https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on<span> </span></span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>,<span> </span></span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span><span> </span>and<span> </span></span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span><span> </span>@radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing<span> </span></span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br></span></em><em><br></em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"How does one trick one's self into doing what&rsquo;s right or, at the very least, avoiding what&rsquo;s wrong?", "publishTime"=>1658498400000, "publish-at"=>"2022-07-22T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1658498400, "estimated-duration"=>1560, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/07/YOUVYOU-EPS-220722_WilliamPatterson.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/07/YOUVYOU-EPS-220722_WilliamPatterson.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/07/YOUVYOU-EPS-220722_WilliamPatterson.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>201315, "credits-name"=>"William Patterson"}, "objectID"=>"1231112", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"You v. You", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This episode, originally aired more than a decade ago, attempts to answer one question: how do you win against your worst impulses? </span><span><br></span><span><br></span><span>Zelda Gamson tried for decades to stop smoking, but the part of her that wanted to quit couldn’t beat the part of her that refused to let go. Adam Davidson, a co-founder of the NPR podcast </span><a href=\"http://www.npr.org/blogs/money/\"><em><span>Planet Money</span></em></a><span>, talked to one of the greatest negotiators of all time, Nobel Prize-winning Economist Thomas Schelling, whose tactical skills saw him through high-stakes conflicts during the Cold War but fell apart when he tried them on himself in his battle to quit smoking. And a baby Pat Walters complicates things — in a good way — with the story of two brothers, Dennis and Kai Woo, who forged a deal with each other that wound up determining both of their futures.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span></span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org/\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span><span> </span>(https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on<span> </span></span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>,<span> </span></span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span><span> </span>and<span> </span></span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span><span> </span>@radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing<span> </span></span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br></span></em><em><br></em></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-07-22T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"gatekeeper", "title"=>"The Gatekeeper", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast071522_gatekeeper.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1228789", "description"=>"<p><span>This week, Reporter Peter Smith and Senior Producer Matt Kielty tell the story of the U.S. Supreme Court decision that set the standard for scientific expertise in a courtroom, i.e., whether an expert can testify in a lawsuit. They also tell the story of the Daubert family — yes, the Dauberts of “Daubert v Merrell Dow” — whose win before the nine justices translated into a deeper loss.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Special thanks to</strong> <em>Leah Litman, Rachel Rebouche, Jennifer Mnookin, David Savitz, Brooke Borel, and Tom Zeller Jr.<br></em></p>\n<p><strong>Credits:</strong> <em><span>Reporting by Peter Andrey Smith. </span><span>Produced by Matt Kielty. </span><span>Reporting and production assistance from Sarah Qari. Fact-checking by Natalie A. Middleton. Editing by Pat Walters. Sound Design by Matt Kielty. Mixing help from Arianne Wack.</span></em></p>\n<p><strong>Citations: </strong>If you're interested in reading more from Peter Smith, check out his work over at <a href=\"https://undark.org/undark-author/peter-andrey-smith/\">Undark.org</a></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br></span></em><em><br><em><span>And, by the way, Radiolab is looking for a remote intern! If you happen to be a creative, science-obsessed nerd who is interested in learning how to make longform radio… Apply before July 20, 2022! We would LOVE to work with you. You can find more info at </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnyc.org/careers/\"><em><span>wnyc.org/careers</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></em></p>", "tease"=>"How a U.S. Supreme Court decision made judges the ultimate gatekeepers of what makes good science in the courtroom.", "publishTime"=>1657893600000, "publish-at"=>"2022-07-15T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1657893600, "estimated-duration"=>2937, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/07/DAUBERT_EPS-IMG-220715_GoslinJosh.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/07/DAUBERT_EPS-IMG-220715_GoslinJosh.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://www.instagram.com/josh_gosfield/?hl=en", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/07/DAUBERT_EPS-IMG-220715_GoslinJosh.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>201238, "credits-name"=>"Josh Gosfield"}, "objectID"=>"1228789", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Gatekeeper", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This week, Reporter Peter Smith and Senior Producer Matt Kielty tell the story of the U.S. Supreme Court decision that set the standard for scientific expertise in a courtroom, i.e., whether an expert can testify in a lawsuit. They also tell the story of the Daubert family — yes, the Dauberts of “Daubert v Merrell Dow” — whose win before the nine justices translated into a deeper loss.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Special thanks to</strong> <em>Leah Litman, Rachel Rebouche, Jennifer Mnookin, David Savitz, Brooke Borel, and Tom Zeller Jr.<br></em></p>\n<p><strong>Credits:</strong> <em><span>Reporting by Peter Andrey Smith. </span><span>Produced by Matt Kielty. </span><span>Reporting and production assistance from Sarah Qari. Fact-checking by Natalie A. Middleton. Editing by Pat Walters. Sound Design by Matt Kielty. Mixing help from Arianne Wack.</span></em></p>\n<p><strong>Citations: </strong>If you're interested in reading more from Peter Smith, check out his work over at <a href=\"https://undark.org/undark-author/peter-andrey-smith/\">Undark.org</a></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br></span></em><em><br><em><span>And, by the way, Radiolab is looking for a remote intern! If you happen to be a creative, science-obsessed nerd who is interested in learning how to make longform radio… Apply before July 20, 2022! We would LOVE to work with you. You can find more info at </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnyc.org/careers/\"><em><span>wnyc.org/careers</span></em></a><em><span>.</span></em></em></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-07-15T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"baby-blue-blood-drive-2207", "title"=>"Baby Blue Blood Drive", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast070822_babyblue.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1226327", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>This is an episode that first aired in 2018 and then again in the thick of the pandemic in 2020. Why? Because though Horseshoe crabs are not much to look at, beneath their unassuming catcher’s-mitt shell, they harbor a half-billion-year-old secret: a superpower that helped them outlive the dinosaurs, survive all the Earth’s mass extinctions, and was essential in the development of the COVID vaccines.  And what is that secret superpower? Their blood. Their baby blue blood.  And it’s so miraculous that for decades, it hasn’t just been saving their butts, it’s been saving ours too.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>But that all might be about to change.  </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Follow us as we follow these ancient critters - from a raunchy beach orgy to a marine blood drive to the most secluded waterslide - and learn a thing or two from them about how much we depend on nature and how much it depends on us.</p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of <strong><a href=\"https://members.radiolab.org/\">The Lab</a> (https://members.radiolab.org/) </strong>today.</span></em></p>\n<p>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about special events.<span> </span><strong><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\">Sign up</a><span> </span>(<a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\">https://radiolab.org/newsletter</a>)</strong>!</p>\n<p>Follow our show on<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/radiolab/\">Instagram</a>,<span> </span><a href=\"https://twitter.com/Radiolab\">Twitter</a><span> </span>and<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/Radiolab\">Facebook</a><strong><span> </span>@radiolab</strong>, and share your thoughts with us by emailing<span> </span><strong>radiolab@wnyc.org</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>And, by the way, Radiolab is looking for a remote intern!</strong><span> </span>If you happen to be a creative, science-obsessed nerd who is interested in learning how to make longform radio… Apply! We would LOVE to work with you.  You can find more info at <a target=\"_blank\" class=\"c-link\" data-stringify-link=\"http://wnyc.org/careers\" delay=\"150\" data-sk=\"tooltip_parent\" href=\"http://wnyc.org/careers\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-remove-tab-index=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\">wnyc.org/careers</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Citations:</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Alexis Madrigal, <a href=\"https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2014/02/the-blood-harvest/284078/\">\"The Blood Harvest\"</a><span> </span>in The Atlantic, and Sarah Zhang's recent follow up in The Atlantic, <a href=\"https://www.theatlantic.com/science/archive/2018/05/blood-in-the-water/559229/\">\"The Last Days of the Blue Blood Harvest\" </a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Deborah Cramer, <em><a href=\"http://www.deborahcramer.com/books/the-narrow-edge-red-knot/\">The Narrow Edge</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Deborah Cramer, <a href=\"https://www.audubon.org/magazine/summer-2018/inside-biomedical-revolution-save-horseshoe-crabs\">\"Inside the Biomedical Revolution to Save Horseshoe Crabs\"</a><span> </span>in Audubon Magazine </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Richard Fortey, <em><a href=\"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/54786/horseshoe-crabs-and-velvet-worms-by-richard-fortey/9780307275530/\">Horseshoe Crabs and Velvet Worms</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Ian Frazier, <a href=\"https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2014/04/14/blue-bloods\">\"Blue Bloods\" </a> in The New Yorker </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Lulu Miller's short story, <a href=\"https://catapult.co/stories/me-and-jane\">\"Me and Jane\" </a> in Catapult Magazine</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Jerry Gault, <a href=\"http://eureka.criver.com/the-most-noble-fishing-there-is/\">\"The Most Noble Fishing There Is\" </a> in Charles River's Eureka Magazine</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">or check out Glenn Gauvry's horseshoe crab <a href=\"http://www.horseshoecrab.org/research/\">research database</a></p>", "tease"=>"We look at a half-billion-year-old secret: a superpower that helped this unassuming organism outlive the dinosaurs and survive all the Earth&rsquo;s mass extinctions", "publishTime"=>1657288800000, "publish-at"=>"2022-07-08T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1657288800, "estimated-duration"=>3111, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"A picture of a horseshoe crab from above. ", "name"=>"2022/07/BABY-BLUE-BLOOD-DRIVE_EPS-220708-03.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/07/BABY-BLUE-BLOOD-DRIVE_EPS-220708-03.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/07/BABY-BLUE-BLOOD-DRIVE_EPS-220708-03.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>201168, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1226327", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Baby Blue Blood Drive", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\"><span>This is an episode that first aired in 2018 and then again in the thick of the pandemic in 2020. Why? Because though Horseshoe crabs are not much to look at, beneath their unassuming catcher’s-mitt shell, they harbor a half-billion-year-old secret: a superpower that helped them outlive the dinosaurs, survive all the Earth’s mass extinctions, and was essential in the development of the COVID vaccines.  And what is that secret superpower? Their blood. Their baby blue blood.  And it’s so miraculous that for decades, it hasn’t just been saving their butts, it’s been saving ours too.</span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><span>But that all might be about to change.  </span></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Follow us as we follow these ancient critters - from a raunchy beach orgy to a marine blood drive to the most secluded waterslide - and learn a thing or two from them about how much we depend on nature and how much it depends on us.</p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of <strong><a href=\"https://members.radiolab.org/\">The Lab</a> (https://members.radiolab.org/) </strong>today.</span></em></p>\n<p>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about special events.<span> </span><strong><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\">Sign up</a><span> </span>(<a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\">https://radiolab.org/newsletter</a>)</strong>!</p>\n<p>Follow our show on<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/radiolab/\">Instagram</a>,<span> </span><a href=\"https://twitter.com/Radiolab\">Twitter</a><span> </span>and<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/Radiolab\">Facebook</a><strong><span> </span>@radiolab</strong>, and share your thoughts with us by emailing<span> </span><strong>radiolab@wnyc.org</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>And, by the way, Radiolab is looking for a remote intern!</strong><span> </span>If you happen to be a creative, science-obsessed nerd who is interested in learning how to make longform radio… Apply! We would LOVE to work with you.  You can find more info at <a target=\"_blank\" class=\"c-link\" data-stringify-link=\"http://wnyc.org/careers\" delay=\"150\" data-sk=\"tooltip_parent\" href=\"http://wnyc.org/careers\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-remove-tab-index=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\">wnyc.org/careers</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Citations:</strong></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Alexis Madrigal, <a href=\"https://www.theatlantic.com/technology/archive/2014/02/the-blood-harvest/284078/\">\"The Blood Harvest\"</a><span> </span>in The Atlantic, and Sarah Zhang's recent follow up in The Atlantic, <a href=\"https://www.theatlantic.com/science/archive/2018/05/blood-in-the-water/559229/\">\"The Last Days of the Blue Blood Harvest\" </a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Deborah Cramer, <em><a href=\"http://www.deborahcramer.com/books/the-narrow-edge-red-knot/\">The Narrow Edge</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Deborah Cramer, <a href=\"https://www.audubon.org/magazine/summer-2018/inside-biomedical-revolution-save-horseshoe-crabs\">\"Inside the Biomedical Revolution to Save Horseshoe Crabs\"</a><span> </span>in Audubon Magazine </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Richard Fortey, <em><a href=\"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/54786/horseshoe-crabs-and-velvet-worms-by-richard-fortey/9780307275530/\">Horseshoe Crabs and Velvet Worms</a></em></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Ian Frazier, <a href=\"https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2014/04/14/blue-bloods\">\"Blue Bloods\" </a> in The New Yorker </p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Lulu Miller's short story, <a href=\"https://catapult.co/stories/me-and-jane\">\"Me and Jane\" </a> in Catapult Magazine</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Jerry Gault, <a href=\"http://eureka.criver.com/the-most-noble-fishing-there-is/\">\"The Most Noble Fishing There Is\" </a> in Charles River's Eureka Magazine</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">or check out Glenn Gauvry's horseshoe crab <a href=\"http://www.horseshoecrab.org/research/\">research database</a></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-07-08T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"my-thymus-myself", "title"=>"My Thymus, Myself", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast063022_mythymus.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1224533", "description"=>"<p><span>Today, we go to a spot that may be one of the most philosophical places in the universe: the thymus, an organ that knows what is you, and what is not you. Its mood may be existential, but its role is practical — the thymus is the biological training ground where the body learns to protect itself from outside invaders (think: bacteria, coronaviruses). But this training is not the humdrum bit of science you might expect. It’s a magical shadowland with dire consequences. </span></p>\n<p><span>Then, we’ll leave the thymus to visit a team of doctors who are using this organ that protects </span><em><span>you</span></em><span> as a way to protect someone… else. Their work could change everything.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Special Thanks: </strong></p>\n<p><span>One thousand thanks to Hannah Meyer, Salomé Carcy, Josh Torres, and Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory for showing us a real-life (mouse) thymus for this episode. Special thanks also go to Diane Mathis and Kate Webb.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Further reading:</strong></p>\n<p><span>Wanna do a little light reading? Here’s the immunology textbook Jenni Punt and Sharon Stranford helped write, including a whole section on that funny little thing called AIRE! </span><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Kuby-Immunology-Jenni-Punt/dp/1464189781/ref=asc_df_1464189781/?tag=hyprod-20&amp;linkCode=df0&amp;hvadid=312091458201&amp;hvpos=&amp;hvnetw=g&amp;hvrand=5208068676084181524&amp;hvpone=&amp;hvptwo=&amp;hvqmt=&amp;hvdev=c&amp;hvdvcmdl=&amp;hvlocint=&amp;hvlocphy=9004368&amp;hvtargid=pla-525174969520&amp;psc=1&amp;tag=&amp;ref=&amp;adgrpid=63669393113&amp;hvpone=&amp;hvptwo=&amp;hvadid=312091458201&amp;hvpos=&amp;hvnetw=g&amp;hvrand=5208068676084181524&amp;hvqmt=&amp;hvdev=c&amp;hvdvcmdl=&amp;hvlocint=&amp;hvlocphy=9004368&amp;hvtargid=pla-525174969520\"><span>Kuby Immunology</span></a><span> </span></p>\n<p><span>The science <a href=\"https://www.science.org/doi/abs/10.1126/science.1075958\">paper</a> that first described what happens inside the thymus as an, “immunological self shadow”.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span><strong><a href=\"https://members.radiolab.org/\"><em>The Lab</em></a><span> </span>(https://members.radiolab.org/)<span> </span></strong><em><span>today.</span></em></span></em></p>\n<p>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about special events.<span> </span><strong><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\">Sign up</a><span> </span>(<a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\">https://radiolab.org/newsletter</a>)</strong>!</p>\n<p>Follow our show on<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/radiolab/\">Instagram</a>,<span> </span><a href=\"https://twitter.com/Radiolab\">Twitter</a><span> </span>and<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/Radiolab\">Facebook</a><strong><span> </span>@radiolab</strong>, and share your thoughts with us by emailing<span> </span><strong>radiolab@wnyc.org</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>And, by the way, Radiolab is looking for a remote intern!</strong> If you happen to be a creative, science-obsessed nerd who is interested in learning how to make longform radio… Apply! We would LOVE to work with you.  You can find more info at <a target=\"_blank\" class=\"c-link\" data-stringify-link=\"http://wnyc.org/careers\" delay=\"150\" data-sk=\"tooltip_parent\" href=\"http://wnyc.org/careers\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-remove-tab-index=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\">wnyc.org/careers</a>.</p>", "tease"=>"The story of an organ that knows what is you, and what is not you.", "publishTime"=>1656601200000, "publish-at"=>"2022-06-30T11:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1656640800, "estimated-duration"=>1692, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/07/20220701-THYMUS-ep.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/l/80/2022/07/20220701-THYMUS-ep.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/07/20220701-THYMUS-ep.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>201115, "credits-name"=>"William Patterson"}, "objectID"=>"1224533", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"My Thymus, Myself", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Today, we go to a spot that may be one of the most philosophical places in the universe: the thymus, an organ that knows what is you, and what is not you. Its mood may be existential, but its role is practical — the thymus is the biological training ground where the body learns to protect itself from outside invaders (think: bacteria, coronaviruses). But this training is not the humdrum bit of science you might expect. It’s a magical shadowland with dire consequences. </span></p>\n<p><span>Then, we’ll leave the thymus to visit a team of doctors who are using this organ that protects </span><em><span>you</span></em><span> as a way to protect someone… else. Their work could change everything.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Special Thanks: </strong></p>\n<p><span>One thousand thanks to Hannah Meyer, Salomé Carcy, Josh Torres, and Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory for showing us a real-life (mouse) thymus for this episode. Special thanks also go to Diane Mathis and Kate Webb.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Further reading:</strong></p>\n<p><span>Wanna do a little light reading? Here’s the immunology textbook Jenni Punt and Sharon Stranford helped write, including a whole section on that funny little thing called AIRE! </span><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Kuby-Immunology-Jenni-Punt/dp/1464189781/ref=asc_df_1464189781/?tag=hyprod-20&amp;linkCode=df0&amp;hvadid=312091458201&amp;hvpos=&amp;hvnetw=g&amp;hvrand=5208068676084181524&amp;hvpone=&amp;hvptwo=&amp;hvqmt=&amp;hvdev=c&amp;hvdvcmdl=&amp;hvlocint=&amp;hvlocphy=9004368&amp;hvtargid=pla-525174969520&amp;psc=1&amp;tag=&amp;ref=&amp;adgrpid=63669393113&amp;hvpone=&amp;hvptwo=&amp;hvadid=312091458201&amp;hvpos=&amp;hvnetw=g&amp;hvrand=5208068676084181524&amp;hvqmt=&amp;hvdev=c&amp;hvdvcmdl=&amp;hvlocint=&amp;hvlocphy=9004368&amp;hvtargid=pla-525174969520\"><span>Kuby Immunology</span></a><span> </span></p>\n<p><span>The science <a href=\"https://www.science.org/doi/abs/10.1126/science.1075958\">paper</a> that first described what happens inside the thymus as an, “immunological self shadow”.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span><strong><a href=\"https://members.radiolab.org/\"><em>The Lab</em></a><span> </span>(https://members.radiolab.org/)<span> </span></strong><em><span>today.</span></em></span></em></p>\n<p>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about special events.<span> </span><strong><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\">Sign up</a><span> </span>(<a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\">https://radiolab.org/newsletter</a>)</strong>!</p>\n<p>Follow our show on<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/radiolab/\">Instagram</a>,<span> </span><a href=\"https://twitter.com/Radiolab\">Twitter</a><span> </span>and<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/Radiolab\">Facebook</a><strong><span> </span>@radiolab</strong>, and share your thoughts with us by emailing<span> </span><strong>radiolab@wnyc.org</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>And, by the way, Radiolab is looking for a remote intern!</strong> If you happen to be a creative, science-obsessed nerd who is interested in learning how to make longform radio… Apply! We would LOVE to work with you.  You can find more info at <a target=\"_blank\" class=\"c-link\" data-stringify-link=\"http://wnyc.org/careers\" delay=\"150\" data-sk=\"tooltip_parent\" href=\"http://wnyc.org/careers\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-remove-tab-index=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\">wnyc.org/careers</a>.</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-06-30T11:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"galapagos-2206", "title"=>"Galápagos", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast062422_galapagos.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1221741", "description"=>"<p><span>As our co-Hosts Lulu Miller and Latif Nasser are out this week, we are re-sharing the perfect episode to start the summer season!</span></p>\n<p><span>This one, which first aired in 2014, tells the strange story of a small group of islands that keeps us wondering: will our most sacred natural landscapes inevitably get swallowed up by humans? How far are we willing to go to stop that from happening?</span></p>\n<p><span>This hour is about the Galápagos archipelago, which inspired Darwin’s theory of evolution and natural selection. Nearly 200 years later, the Galápagos are undergoing rapid changes that continue to pose — and perhaps answer — critical questions about the fragility and resilience of life on Earth.</span></p>\n<p><span><strong>Episode Credits:<br></strong><br><span>Reported and produced by Tim Howard.</span></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of <strong><a href=\"https://members.radiolab.org/\"><em>The Lab</em></a> (https://members.radiolab.org/) </strong><em><span>today.</span></em></span></em></p>\n<p>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about special events. <strong><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\">Sign up</a> (<a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\">https://radiolab.org/newsletter</a>)</strong>!</p>\n<p>Follow our show on <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/radiolab/\">Instagram</a>, <a href=\"https://twitter.com/Radiolab\">Twitter</a> and <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/Radiolab\">Facebook</a><strong> @radiolab</strong>, and share your thoughts with us by emailing <strong>radiolab@wnyc.org</strong>.</p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"Is it inevitable that even our most sacred natural landscapes will eventually get swallowed up by humans?", "publishTime"=>1656079200000, "publish-at"=>"2022-06-24T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1656079200, "estimated-duration"=>3876, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/06/GALAPAGOS_EPS-IMG-220623_rene_adobestock.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/06/GALAPAGOS_EPS-IMG-220623_rene_adobestock.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/06/GALAPAGOS_EPS-IMG-220623_rene_adobestock.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>201007, "credits-name"=>"Rene via Adobe Stock"}, "objectID"=>"1221741", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Galápagos", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>As our co-Hosts Lulu Miller and Latif Nasser are out this week, we are re-sharing the perfect episode to start the summer season!</span></p>\n<p><span>This one, which first aired in 2014, tells the strange story of a small group of islands that keeps us wondering: will our most sacred natural landscapes inevitably get swallowed up by humans? How far are we willing to go to stop that from happening?</span></p>\n<p><span>This hour is about the Galápagos archipelago, which inspired Darwin’s theory of evolution and natural selection. Nearly 200 years later, the Galápagos are undergoing rapid changes that continue to pose — and perhaps answer — critical questions about the fragility and resilience of life on Earth.</span></p>\n<p><span><strong>Episode Credits:<br></strong><br><span>Reported and produced by Tim Howard.</span></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of <strong><a href=\"https://members.radiolab.org/\"><em>The Lab</em></a> (https://members.radiolab.org/) </strong><em><span>today.</span></em></span></em></p>\n<p>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about special events. <strong><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\">Sign up</a> (<a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\">https://radiolab.org/newsletter</a>)</strong>!</p>\n<p>Follow our show on <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/radiolab/\">Instagram</a>, <a href=\"https://twitter.com/Radiolab\">Twitter</a> and <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/Radiolab\">Facebook</a><strong> @radiolab</strong>, and share your thoughts with us by emailing <strong>radiolab@wnyc.org</strong>.</p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-06-24T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"no-special-duty-2206", "title"=>"No Special Duty", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast061722_nospecialduty.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1219051", "description"=>"<p><span>Since the massacre that took the lives of 19 schoolchildren in Uvalde, Texas, people across the world began to ask versions of one question: why did police wait outside the door instead of protecting the kids?</span></p>\n<p><span>It's not the first time this question has come up. Two years ago, as she watched police respond to the protests that followed the death of George Floyd, Producer B.A. Parker wondered: what are police </span><em><span>for</span></em><span>? With the help of our Producer Sarah Qari, she found that the United States’ Supreme Court had given this a most consequential and bewildering answer.</span></p>\n<p><span>We decided to re-air this episode to shed light on how a case from 2005 upended our assumptions about the role police are meant to play in our lives.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://members.radiolab.org/\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> (https://members.radiolab.org/) <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/MTSFMLXQWDkE\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/MTSFMLXQWDkE\">https://zpr.io/MTSFMLXQWDkE)</span> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></span></p>", "tease"=>"What, exactly, is the police's obligation to us?", "publishTime"=>1655474400000, "publish-at"=>"2022-06-17T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1655474400, "estimated-duration"=>2778, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/06/20220617-NoSpecialDuty-episode-02.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/06/20220617-NoSpecialDuty-episode-02.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/06/20220617-NoSpecialDuty-episode-02.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>200907, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1219051", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"No Special Duty", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Since the massacre that took the lives of 19 schoolchildren in Uvalde, Texas, people across the world began to ask versions of one question: why did police wait outside the door instead of protecting the kids?</span></p>\n<p><span>It's not the first time this question has come up. Two years ago, as she watched police respond to the protests that followed the death of George Floyd, Producer B.A. Parker wondered: what are police </span><em><span>for</span></em><span>? With the help of our Producer Sarah Qari, she found that the United States’ Supreme Court had given this a most consequential and bewildering answer.</span></p>\n<p><span>We decided to re-air this episode to shed light on how a case from 2005 upended our assumptions about the role police are meant to play in our lives.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://members.radiolab.org/\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> (https://members.radiolab.org/) <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/MTSFMLXQWDkE\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/MTSFMLXQWDkE\">https://zpr.io/MTSFMLXQWDkE)</span> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-06-17T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"neanderthals-revenge", "title"=>"Neanderthal's Revenge", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast061022_neanderthals.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1216572", "description"=>"<p><span>A few months ago, co-Host Latif Nasser, who was otherwise healthy, saw blood in his poop. It was the start of a medical journey that made him not only question what was going on in his body, but also dig into the secret genetic story of how we became human. Curled up in a hospital bathroom, Latif tries to sort out whether his ordeal is the result of a long-lost sibling knifing him in the gut or, on the contrary, a long-forgotten kindness shared between two human-ish travelers. </span></p>\n<p><em><strong>Special thanks </strong>to Azra Premiji, Avir Mitra, Suzanne Lehrer, David Reich, Sriram Sankararaman, Ainara Sistiaga, </em><em>Carl Zimmer, </em><em>Carly Mensch, Nihal Kaur, Charlotte Hsu and Bert Gambini at the University at Buffalo Media Relations, and Latif's </em><em>GI Doctor Florence Damilola Odufalu and her entire team, as well as all</em><em> the staff at LA County-USC Medical Center and Keck USC hospitals who looked after Latif during his hospitalization.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!<br><br><em><strong>Editorial Note:</strong> This podcast was amended after initial release to change the way we refer to those afflicted by addiction. </em><br></span></p>", "tease"=>"A tale of guts, genes, and gratitude. Also, watching videos on your phone while on the toilet.", "publishTime"=>1654869600000, "publish-at"=>"2022-06-10T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1654869600, "estimated-duration"=>1614, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/06/20220610_NEANDERTHALS-REVENGE_episode-01.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/06/20220610_NEANDERTHALS-REVENGE_episode-01.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"https://instagram.com/m.gailstp?igshid=YmMyMTA2M2Y=", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/06/20220610_NEANDERTHALS-REVENGE_episode-01.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>200813, "credits-name"=>"Melanie St-Pierre"}, "objectID"=>"1216572", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Neanderthal's Revenge", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>A few months ago, co-Host Latif Nasser, who was otherwise healthy, saw blood in his poop. It was the start of a medical journey that made him not only question what was going on in his body, but also dig into the secret genetic story of how we became human. Curled up in a hospital bathroom, Latif tries to sort out whether his ordeal is the result of a long-lost sibling knifing him in the gut or, on the contrary, a long-forgotten kindness shared between two human-ish travelers. </span></p>\n<p><em><strong>Special thanks </strong>to Azra Premiji, Avir Mitra, Suzanne Lehrer, David Reich, Sriram Sankararaman, Ainara Sistiaga, </em><em>Carl Zimmer, </em><em>Carly Mensch, Nihal Kaur, Charlotte Hsu and Bert Gambini at the University at Buffalo Media Relations, and Latif's </em><em>GI Doctor Florence Damilola Odufalu and her entire team, as well as all</em><em> the staff at LA County-USC Medical Center and Keck USC hospitals who looked after Latif during his hospitalization.</em></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!<br><br><em><strong>Editorial Note:</strong> This podcast was amended after initial release to change the way we refer to those afflicted by addiction. </em><br></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-06-10T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"origin-stories", "title"=>"Origin Stories", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast060322_originstories.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1214117", "description"=>"<p><span>We’re all in a tizzy here at Radiolab on account of our 20-year anniversary. And, as one does upon passing a milestone, we’ve been looking back in all kinds of ways. Two weeks ago, we went out over the airwaves, “<a href=\"https://radiolab.org/episodes/radiolab-after-dark\">Live on your FM dial</a>,” a callback to our origins as a radio show. We revamped our logo and redid our website (get your Freq on, people!). More recently, Lulu's and Latif’s first stories came up in a meeting. They weren’t always the intrepid hosts of our collective journey in wonder. Soren Wheeler, our editor, thought it would be fun to highlight those firsts for you. </span></p>\n<p><span>So here they are, baby Latif and Lulu, doing their darndest to make audio magic.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"Lulu and Latif weren&rsquo;t always the intrepid hosts of our collective journey in wonder. They had to start somewhere.", "publishTime"=>1654264800000, "publish-at"=>"2022-06-03T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1654264800, "estimated-duration"=>2609, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/06/RL_ORIGIN-STORIES_20220603_site2_4x3site.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/06/RL_ORIGIN-STORIES_20220603_site2_4x3site.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/06/RL_ORIGIN-STORIES_20220603_site2_4x3site.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>200700, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1214117", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Origin Stories", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>We’re all in a tizzy here at Radiolab on account of our 20-year anniversary. And, as one does upon passing a milestone, we’ve been looking back in all kinds of ways. Two weeks ago, we went out over the airwaves, “<a href=\"https://radiolab.org/episodes/radiolab-after-dark\">Live on your FM dial</a>,” a callback to our origins as a radio show. We revamped our logo and redid our website (get your Freq on, people!). More recently, Lulu's and Latif’s first stories came up in a meeting. They weren’t always the intrepid hosts of our collective journey in wonder. Soren Wheeler, our editor, thought it would be fun to highlight those firsts for you. </span></p>\n<p><span>So here they are, baby Latif and Lulu, doing their darndest to make audio magic.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-06-03T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"radiolab-after-dark", "title"=>"Radiolab After Dark", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast052722_afterdark.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1212312", "description"=>"<p><span>Back in 2002, Jad Abumrad started Radiolab as a live radio show. He DJ’d out into the ether and 20 years later we do the same. To commemorate the 20-year anniversary of the show, the Radiolab team went old school and took over WNYC Radio, live on the FM band. We answered the phones, played some wonderfully weird audio, including one piece where Kurt Vonnegut—yes, <em>that</em> Kurt Vonnegut—interviews the dead, took part in some games and tomfoolery, and did everything we could to have and to share in our good time.</span></p>", "tease"=>"For our 20 year anniversary, we went out on the wireless, and completely without a net.", "publishTime"=>1653660000000, "publish-at"=>"2022-05-27T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1653660000, "estimated-duration"=>3526, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/05/EpisodeArtwork_RadiolabAfterDark_VersionB-02.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/05/EpisodeArtwork_RadiolabAfterDark_VersionB-02.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/05/EpisodeArtwork_RadiolabAfterDark_VersionB-02.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>200590, "credits-name"=>"Radiolab"}, "objectID"=>"1212312", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Radiolab After Dark", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Back in 2002, Jad Abumrad started Radiolab as a live radio show. He DJ’d out into the ether and 20 years later we do the same. To commemorate the 20-year anniversary of the show, the Radiolab team went old school and took over WNYC Radio, live on the FM band. We answered the phones, played some wonderfully weird audio, including one piece where Kurt Vonnegut—yes, <em>that</em> Kurt Vonnegut—interviews the dead, took part in some games and tomfoolery, and did everything we could to have and to share in our good time.</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-05-27T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"la-mancha-screwjob-2205", "title"=>"La Mancha Screwjob", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast052022_lamancha.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1209823", "description"=>"<p><span>All the world’s a stage. Or, sometimes it feels that way, especially these days. In this episode, originally aired in 2015, we push through the fourth wall, pierce the spandex-ed heart of professional wrestling, and travel 400 years into the past to unmask our obsession with authenticity and our desire to walk the line between reality and fantasy.</span></p>\n<p>Thanks to Nick Hakim for the use of <a href=\"https://soundcloud.com/enhakim\" target=\"_blank\">his song \"The Light\". </a></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a><span> </span><em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span><span> </span></span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p> </p>", "tease"=>"We push through the fourth wall and travel 400 years into the past to unmask our obsession with authenticity and our desire to walk the line between reality and fantasy.", "publishTime"=>1653055200000, "publish-at"=>"2022-05-20T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1653062400, "estimated-duration"=>3474, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/05/lamanchascrewjobepisodeimage.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2022/05/lamanchascrewjobepisodeimage.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/05/lamanchascrewjobepisodeimage.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>200516, "credits-name"=>"Marta P. (Milacroft)"}, "objectID"=>"1209823", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"La Mancha Screwjob", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>All the world’s a stage. Or, sometimes it feels that way, especially these days. In this episode, originally aired in 2015, we push through the fourth wall, pierce the spandex-ed heart of professional wrestling, and travel 400 years into the past to unmask our obsession with authenticity and our desire to walk the line between reality and fantasy.</span></p>\n<p>Thanks to Nick Hakim for the use of <a href=\"https://soundcloud.com/enhakim\" target=\"_blank\">his song \"The Light\". </a></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a><span> </span><em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span><span> </span></span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p> </p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-05-20T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"frailmales", "title"=>"Frailmales", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast051322_frailmales.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1207525", "description"=>"<p><span>This week, we bring you two stories about little guys trying to do big big things.</span></p>\n<p><span>First, self-proclaimed animal grinch producer Becca Bressler introduces us to perhaps the one creature that has warmed her heart: a cricket. And more specifically, a male cricket. This is a tale about a tiny Romeo insect trying to find a mate, and the ingenious lengths he’ll go to have his beckoning heard.</span></p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\" style=\"max-width: 800px;\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/i/660/742/l/80/2022/05/Frailmales_TreeCricket.JPG\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">The hero of our story</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<p><span>And second, producer Annie McEwen journeys through perhaps the zaniest game of football that has ever been played. When a ragtag group of players take on the top team, will it be an underdog tale for the ages or an absolute disaster?</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Stephen Spann and Joshua Baxter at the Doris and Harry Vice University Library at Cumberland University as well as Alison Reynolds at Georgia Tech Library. Thanks also to Rick Bell, and to Scott Larson who wrote a book all about this game called <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/36549726-cumberland\">Cumberland: The True Story of the Highest Scoring Football Game in History</a></span></em><em><span>. And finally, thanks so much to our tape syncer Ambriehl Crutchfield for her help with this episode. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>If you’re still interested in learning more about this epic football game, be sure to check out this brilliant and hilarious </span></em><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=doZzrsDJo-4&amp;ab_channel=JonBois\"><em><span>video</span></em></a><em><span> by sportswriter Jon Bois.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Lastly, don't forget to check out <strong><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/episodes\">Death Sex and Money</a></strong><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/episodes\"></a>. We recommend episode titled <strong><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/episodes/hard-erectile-dysfunction-death-sex-money\">Hard</a></strong>, which is deep dive into our relationship with erectile dysfunction, and the drugs developed to treat it.  </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a><span> </span><em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span><span> </span></span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span><span><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Nupfd0ZIpzMfWittBxsVgvf8NkZMNafq/view?usp=sharing\">DOWNLOAD BRAILLE READY FILE HERE</a> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/YPQjmqjec5g7\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/YPQjmqjec5g7\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/YPQjmqjec5g7\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/YPQjmqjec5g7</a></span></span><span>)</span></span></p>", "tease"=>"This week, we bring you two stories about little guys trying to do big big things.", "publishTime"=>1652450400000, "publish-at"=>"2022-05-13T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1652450400, "estimated-duration"=>2149, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/05/frailmalesepisodeimage.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/05/frailmalesepisodeimage.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/05/frailmalesepisodeimage.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>200517, "credits-name"=>"WNYC Studios"}, "objectID"=>"1207525", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Frailmales", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This week, we bring you two stories about little guys trying to do big big things.</span></p>\n<p><span>First, self-proclaimed animal grinch producer Becca Bressler introduces us to perhaps the one creature that has warmed her heart: a cricket. And more specifically, a male cricket. This is a tale about a tiny Romeo insect trying to find a mate, and the ingenious lengths he’ll go to have his beckoning heard.</span></p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\" style=\"max-width: 800px;\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://media.wnyc.org/i/660/742/l/80/2022/05/Frailmales_TreeCricket.JPG\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-metadata\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">The hero of our story</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p><span> </span></p>\n<p><span>And second, producer Annie McEwen journeys through perhaps the zaniest game of football that has ever been played. When a ragtag group of players take on the top team, will it be an underdog tale for the ages or an absolute disaster?</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Special thanks to Stephen Spann and Joshua Baxter at the Doris and Harry Vice University Library at Cumberland University as well as Alison Reynolds at Georgia Tech Library. Thanks also to Rick Bell, and to Scott Larson who wrote a book all about this game called <a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/36549726-cumberland\">Cumberland: The True Story of the Highest Scoring Football Game in History</a></span></em><em><span>. And finally, thanks so much to our tape syncer Ambriehl Crutchfield for her help with this episode. </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>If you’re still interested in learning more about this epic football game, be sure to check out this brilliant and hilarious </span></em><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=doZzrsDJo-4&amp;ab_channel=JonBois\"><em><span>video</span></em></a><em><span> by sportswriter Jon Bois.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Lastly, don't forget to check out <strong><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/episodes\">Death Sex and Money</a></strong><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/episodes\"></a>. We recommend episode titled <strong><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/deathsexmoney/episodes/hard-erectile-dysfunction-death-sex-money\">Hard</a></strong>, which is deep dive into our relationship with erectile dysfunction, and the drugs developed to treat it.  </span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a><span> </span><em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span><span> </span></span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span><span><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Nupfd0ZIpzMfWittBxsVgvf8NkZMNafq/view?usp=sharing\">DOWNLOAD BRAILLE READY FILE HERE</a> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/YPQjmqjec5g7\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/YPQjmqjec5g7\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/YPQjmqjec5g7\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/YPQjmqjec5g7</a></span></span><span>)</span></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-05-13T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"debatable-2205", "title"=>"Debatable", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast050622_debatable.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1205298", "description"=>"<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1024\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1033\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1053\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1047\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1070\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<p><span>In competitive debate future presidents, supreme court justices, and titans of industry pummel each other with logic and rhetoric. </span><br><span></span></p>\n<p><span>Unclasp your briefcase. It’s time for a showdown. Looking back on an episode originally aired in 2016, we take a good long look at the world of competitive college debate. <br></span><br><span>This is Ryan Wash's story. He's a queer, Black, first-generation college student from Kansas City, Missouri who joined the debate team at Emporia State University on a whim. When he started going up against fast-talking, well-funded, “name-brand” teams, from places like Northwestern and Harvard, it was clear he wasn’t in Kansas anymore. So Ryan became the vanguard of a movement that made everything about debate debatable. In the end, he made himself a home in a strange and hostile land. Whether he was able to change what counts as rigorous academic argument … well, that’s still up for debate.</span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Will Baker, Myra Milam, John Dellamore, Sam Mauer, Tiffany Dillard Knox, Mary Mudd, Darren \"Chief\" Elliot, Jodee Hobbs, Rashad Evans and Luke Hill. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks also to Torgeir Kinne Solsvik for use of the song <span>h-lydisk / B Lydian from the album <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B007IVZUEU/ref=dm_ws_sp_ps_dp\" target=\"_blank\">Geirr Tveitt Piano Works and Songs</a></span></em></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1079\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\">\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"How an outsider&nbsp;became the vanguard of a movement that made everything about debate debatable.", "publishTime"=>1651845600000, "publish-at"=>"2022-05-06T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1651845600, "estimated-duration"=>3640, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/05/Debatable_Radiolab_pnOEFFg.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2022/05/Debatable_Radiolab_pnOEFFg.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/05/Debatable_Radiolab_pnOEFFg.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>200554, "credits-name"=>"WNYC Studios"}, "objectID"=>"1205298", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Debatable", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1024\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1033\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1053\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1047\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1070\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<p><span>In competitive debate future presidents, supreme court justices, and titans of industry pummel each other with logic and rhetoric. </span><br><span></span></p>\n<p><span>Unclasp your briefcase. It’s time for a showdown. Looking back on an episode originally aired in 2016, we take a good long look at the world of competitive college debate. <br></span><br><span>This is Ryan Wash's story. He's a queer, Black, first-generation college student from Kansas City, Missouri who joined the debate team at Emporia State University on a whim. When he started going up against fast-talking, well-funded, “name-brand” teams, from places like Northwestern and Harvard, it was clear he wasn’t in Kansas anymore. So Ryan became the vanguard of a movement that made everything about debate debatable. In the end, he made himself a home in a strange and hostile land. Whether he was able to change what counts as rigorous academic argument … well, that’s still up for debate.</span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Will Baker, Myra Milam, John Dellamore, Sam Mauer, Tiffany Dillard Knox, Mary Mudd, Darren \"Chief\" Elliot, Jodee Hobbs, Rashad Evans and Luke Hill. </em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks also to Torgeir Kinne Solsvik for use of the song <span>h-lydisk / B Lydian from the album <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B007IVZUEU/ref=dm_ws_sp_ps_dp\" target=\"_blank\">Geirr Tveitt Piano Works and Songs</a></span></em></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1079\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\">\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-05-06T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"hello-my-name", "title"=>"Hello, My Name Is", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast042922_hellonames.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1203614", "description"=>"<p><span>As a species, we’re obsessed with names. They’re one of the first labels we get as kids. We name and rename absolutely </span><em><span>everything</span></em><span> around us. And these names carry our histories, they can open and close our eyes to the world around us, and they drag the weight of expectation and even irony along with them. This week on Radiolab, we’ve got six stories all about names. Horse names, the names of diseases, names for the beginning, and names for the end. Listen to “Hello, My Name Is” on Radiolab, wherever you find podcasts. </span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Jim Wright, author of “The Real James Bond”, Tad Davis, Cole delCharco, Peter Frick-Wright, Alexa Rose Miller, Katherine De La Cruz, and Fahima Haque.<br><br>Members of The Lab, watch for an audio extra on your exclusive feeds, a poem written and read by Mary Szybist, whom Molly Webster interviewed for her story in this episode about endlings. It is titled “We Think We Do Not Have Medieval Eyes.” If you are not yet a member and would like to listen to it, <a target=\"_blank\" class=\"c-link\" data-stringify-link=\"http://members.radiolab.org\" delay=\"150\" data-sk=\"tooltip_parent\" href=\"http://members.radiolab.org/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-remove-tab-index=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\">you can join here</a>.</em></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Gr9vrj1iJu2G8d6cC0zPEX9jELu3jYdv/view?usp=sharing\">DOWNLOAD BRAILLE READY FILE HERE</a> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/BmPeeLvvRDrD\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/BmPeeLvvRDrD\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/BmPeeLvvRDrD\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/BmPeeLvvRDrD</a></span></span><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span><span><br><strong>Citations:<br><br></strong></span></span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/7170627-the-emperor-of-all-maladies\"><em>The Emperor of All Maladies</em></a> by Siddhartha Mukherjee</span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/2753144-warhorse?from_search=true&amp;from_srp=true&amp;qid=7W3hFw2xmR&amp;rank=1\"><em>Warhorse: Cavalry in Ancient Warfare</em></a> by Philip Sidnell<br><br>Check out <a href=\"http://www.artspractica.com\">ArtsPractica.com</a>, a site focused on medical uncertainty. <a href=\"https://twitter.com/artspractica\">Alexa Rose Miller</a>. </span><span><span></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span></span><br></span></p>", "tease"=>"This week, we're talking names. They're some of the first labels we get, and they can both reveal and conceal. Do they shape us? Or the other way around?", "publishTime"=>1651240800000, "publish-at"=>"2022-04-29T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1651240800, "estimated-duration"=>4311, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/04/RL_HelloMyNameIs_EpisodeImage.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2022/04/RL_HelloMyNameIs_EpisodeImage.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/04/RL_HelloMyNameIs_EpisodeImage.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>200190, "credits-name"=>"iStockPhoto"}, "objectID"=>"1203614", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Hello, My Name Is", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>As a species, we’re obsessed with names. They’re one of the first labels we get as kids. We name and rename absolutely </span><em><span>everything</span></em><span> around us. And these names carry our histories, they can open and close our eyes to the world around us, and they drag the weight of expectation and even irony along with them. This week on Radiolab, we’ve got six stories all about names. Horse names, the names of diseases, names for the beginning, and names for the end. Listen to “Hello, My Name Is” on Radiolab, wherever you find podcasts. </span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Jim Wright, author of “The Real James Bond”, Tad Davis, Cole delCharco, Peter Frick-Wright, Alexa Rose Miller, Katherine De La Cruz, and Fahima Haque.<br><br>Members of The Lab, watch for an audio extra on your exclusive feeds, a poem written and read by Mary Szybist, whom Molly Webster interviewed for her story in this episode about endlings. It is titled “We Think We Do Not Have Medieval Eyes.” If you are not yet a member and would like to listen to it, <a target=\"_blank\" class=\"c-link\" data-stringify-link=\"http://members.radiolab.org\" delay=\"150\" data-sk=\"tooltip_parent\" href=\"http://members.radiolab.org/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" data-remove-tab-index=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\">you can join here</a>.</em></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Gr9vrj1iJu2G8d6cC0zPEX9jELu3jYdv/view?usp=sharing\">DOWNLOAD BRAILLE READY FILE HERE</a> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/BmPeeLvvRDrD\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/BmPeeLvvRDrD\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/BmPeeLvvRDrD\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/BmPeeLvvRDrD</a></span></span><span>)</span></p>\n<p><span><span><br><strong>Citations:<br><br></strong></span></span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/7170627-the-emperor-of-all-maladies\"><em>The Emperor of All Maladies</em></a> by Siddhartha Mukherjee</span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/2753144-warhorse?from_search=true&amp;from_srp=true&amp;qid=7W3hFw2xmR&amp;rank=1\"><em>Warhorse: Cavalry in Ancient Warfare</em></a> by Philip Sidnell<br><br>Check out <a href=\"http://www.artspractica.com\">ArtsPractica.com</a>, a site focused on medical uncertainty. <a href=\"https://twitter.com/artspractica\">Alexa Rose Miller</a>. </span><span><span></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span></span><br></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-04-29T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"cuba-ish-2204", "title"=>"The Other Latif: Cuba-ish", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast042222_cubaish.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1201105", "description"=>"<p><span>Almost exactly twenty years ago, detainee 244 got transferred to Guantanamo Bay. Captured by American forces at the battle Tora Bora five months previous, Abdul Latif Nasser was shaved, hooded, shackled, diapered, and flown halfway across the world.</span></p>\n<p><span>The Radiolab special series, <em><strong><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/other-latif\">The Other Latif</a></strong></em>, kicked off when one of our hosts, Latif Nasser, made a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with detainee 244. A man the U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims, on the other hand, that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash launched our Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what the man with whom he shares a name actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, Radiolab’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Episode 5: Cuba-ish </strong></p>\n<p><span>To mark the solemn occasion of the other Latif's transfer to, \"the legal equivalent of outer space,\" we thought we'd replay Cuba-ish, the fifth episode of our special series which first aired back in 2020. In this episode, our Latif heads to Guantanamo Bay to try to speak to his namesake. Before he gets there, he dives deep, seeking the answer to what seems like a simple question: why Cuba? Why in the world did the United States pick this sleepy military base in the Caribbean to house “the worst of the worst”?  <br><br></span><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"Almost exactly twenty years ago, detainee 244, Abdul Latif Nasser, got transferred to Guantanamo Bay.&nbsp;To mark&nbsp;the solemn occasion&nbsp;we examine the&nbsp;&ldquo;legal equivalent of outer space.&rdquo;", "publishTime"=>1650636000000, "publish-at"=>"2022-04-22T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1650636000, "estimated-duration"=>3896, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2020/03/The_Other_Latif_Ep_5_1200x1600px.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2020/03/The_Other_Latif_Ep_5_1200x1600px.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2020/03/The_Other_Latif_Ep_5_1200x1600px.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>188628, "credits-name"=>"Will Paybarah"}, "objectID"=>"1201105", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Other Latif: Cuba-ish", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Almost exactly twenty years ago, detainee 244 got transferred to Guantanamo Bay. Captured by American forces at the battle Tora Bora five months previous, Abdul Latif Nasser was shaved, hooded, shackled, diapered, and flown halfway across the world.</span></p>\n<p><span>The Radiolab special series, <em><strong><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/other-latif\">The Other Latif</a></strong></em>, kicked off when one of our hosts, Latif Nasser, made a bizarre and shocking discovery. He shares his name with detainee 244. A man the U.S. government paints a terrifying picture of as Al-Qaeda’s top explosives expert, and one of the most important advisors to Osama bin Laden. Nasser’s lawyer claims, on the other hand, that he was at the wrong place at the wrong time, and that he was never even in Al-Qaeda. This clash launched our Latif into a years-long investigation, picking apart evidence, attempting to separate fact from fiction, and trying to uncover what the man with whom he shares a name actually did or didn’t do. Along the way, Radiolab’s Latif reflects on American values and his own religious past, and wonders how a fellow nerdy, suburban Muslim kid, may have gone down such a strikingly different path.</span></p>\n<p><strong>Episode 5: Cuba-ish </strong></p>\n<p><span>To mark the solemn occasion of the other Latif's transfer to, \"the legal equivalent of outer space,\" we thought we'd replay Cuba-ish, the fifth episode of our special series which first aired back in 2020. In this episode, our Latif heads to Guantanamo Bay to try to speak to his namesake. Before he gets there, he dives deep, seeking the answer to what seems like a simple question: why Cuba? Why in the world did the United States pick this sleepy military base in the Caribbean to house “the worst of the worst”?  <br><br></span><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-04-22T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"null", "title"=>"NULL", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast041522_null.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1199033", "description"=>"<p><span>A one-word magical spell. Several years back, that’s exactly what Joseph Tartaro thought he’d discovered. It was a spell that, if used properly, promised to make one’s problems disappear. And so he crossed his fingers, uttered the word and cast the enchantment on himself. The result, however, was anything but magical.</span></p>\n<p><span>Unbeknownst to Joseph, by unleashing this spell, he’d earned a lifetime membership into a cursed community. A clan made up of folks who, through no fault of their own, had become nameless and invisible. Today, the story of these unfortunate souls, the dark digital arts that took so much from them and the wizardry needed to give them new life.</span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Sarah Chasins, Tony Hoare, Brian Kernighan and to Patrick McKenzie for writing that wonderful list of assumptions programmers believe about names. And also to all the folks who spoke to us and emailed us with stories of their own ‘problematic’ names.<br></em></p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vV8GOoarFHV91anbiuz_EHpiZQxy4Epn/view?usp=sharing\">DOWNLOAD BRAILLE READY FILE HERE</a></strong></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong>The Lab</strong></a><span> </span>today.    </em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span> </span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>", "tease"=>"A story about folks who&rsquo;ve disappeared and the computers that deleted them.", "publishTime"=>1650031200000, "publish-at"=>"2022-04-15T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1650031200, "estimated-duration"=>1181, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/04/Null-epimage-4x3-ben-hershey.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2022/04/Null-epimage-4x3-ben-hershey.jpg", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/04/Null-epimage-4x3-ben-hershey.jpg", "w"=>1860, "id"=>199965, "credits-name"=>"Ben Hershey"}, "objectID"=>"1199033", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"NULL", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>A one-word magical spell. Several years back, that’s exactly what Joseph Tartaro thought he’d discovered. It was a spell that, if used properly, promised to make one’s problems disappear. And so he crossed his fingers, uttered the word and cast the enchantment on himself. The result, however, was anything but magical.</span></p>\n<p><span>Unbeknownst to Joseph, by unleashing this spell, he’d earned a lifetime membership into a cursed community. A clan made up of folks who, through no fault of their own, had become nameless and invisible. Today, the story of these unfortunate souls, the dark digital arts that took so much from them and the wizardry needed to give them new life.</span></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Sarah Chasins, Tony Hoare, Brian Kernighan and to Patrick McKenzie for writing that wonderful list of assumptions programmers believe about names. And also to all the folks who spoke to us and emailed us with stories of their own ‘problematic’ names.<br></em></p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vV8GOoarFHV91anbiuz_EHpiZQxy4Epn/view?usp=sharing\">DOWNLOAD BRAILLE READY FILE HERE</a></strong></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong>The Lab</strong></a><span> </span>today.    </em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span> </span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-04-15T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"dust-planet-2204", "title"=>"In the Dust of This Planet", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast040822_dustofplanet.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1197327", "description"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Horror, fashion, and the end of the world … In this episode, first aired in 2014, but maybe even more relevant today, things get weird as we explore the undercurrents of thought that link nihilists, beard-stroking philosophers, Jay-Z, and True Detective.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Today on Radiolab, a puzzle. Jad’s brother-in-law <a href=\"https://www.johnhuntpublishing.com/zer0-books/our-books/in-the-dust-of-this-planet\">wrote a book called 'In The Dust of This Planet'.</a></p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/400/620/c/80/1/Cover2.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\n<p class=\"p1\">It’s an academic treatise about the horror humanity feels as we realize that we are nothing but a speck in the universe. For a few years nobody read it. But then …</p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/620/348/l/80/1/True_Detective.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\"><a href=\"http://blogs.wsj.com/speakeasy/2014/02/02/writer-nic-pizzolatto-on-thomas-ligotti-and-the-weird-secrets-of-true-detective/\">It seemed to show up on True Detective.</a></div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/594/867/l/80/1/Lilly_Collins.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Then in a fashion magazine.</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/620/348/l/80/1/JayZShot.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">And then on Jay-Z's back. How? </div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"p1\">We talk nihilism with Eugene Thacker &amp; Simon Critchley, leather jackets with June Ambrose, climate change with David Victor, and hope with the father of Transcendental Black Metal - Hunter Hunt Hendrix of the band<span> </span><a href=\"http://www.thrilljockey.com/thrill/Liturgy/Aesthethica#.VA9NM7ywK68\">Liturgy. </a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Also, check out WNYC Studio's <em>On the Media </em>episode <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/otm/segments/staring-abyss\">Staring into the Abyss</a>, in it Brooke Gladstone and Jad Abumrad continue their discussion of nihilism and its place in history.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><a href=\"http://www.zero-books.net/books/in-the-dust-of-this-planet\">You can find Eugene Thacker's 'In The Dust Of the Planet' at Zero Books<br></a></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong>The Lab</strong></a><span> </span>today.    </em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span> </span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>", "tease"=>"Horror, fashion, and the end of the world ... the undercurrents&nbsp;of thought that link nihilists, philosophers, Jay-Z and True Detective.", "publishTime"=>1649426400000, "publish-at"=>"2022-04-08T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1649426400, "estimated-duration"=>1739, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/04/RL_InTheDustOfThisPlanet_EpisodeImage-NASA_resize.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2022/04/RL_InTheDustOfThisPlanet_EpisodeImage-NASA_resize.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/04/RL_InTheDustOfThisPlanet_EpisodeImage-NASA_resize.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>199864, "credits-name"=>"NASA"}, "objectID"=>"1197327", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"In the Dust of This Planet", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p class=\"p1\">Horror, fashion, and the end of the world … In this episode, first aired in 2014, but maybe even more relevant today, things get weird as we explore the undercurrents of thought that link nihilists, beard-stroking philosophers, Jay-Z, and True Detective.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Today on Radiolab, a puzzle. Jad’s brother-in-law <a href=\"https://www.johnhuntpublishing.com/zer0-books/our-books/in-the-dust-of-this-planet\">wrote a book called 'In The Dust of This Planet'.</a></p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/400/620/c/80/1/Cover2.jpg\" alt=\"\"></div>\n<p class=\"p1\">It’s an academic treatise about the horror humanity feels as we realize that we are nothing but a speck in the universe. For a few years nobody read it. But then …</p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/620/348/l/80/1/True_Detective.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\"><a href=\"http://blogs.wsj.com/speakeasy/2014/02/02/writer-nic-pizzolatto-on-thomas-ligotti-and-the-weird-secrets-of-true-detective/\">It seemed to show up on True Detective.</a></div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/594/867/l/80/1/Lilly_Collins.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">Then in a fashion magazine.</div>\n</div>\n<p> </p>\n<div class=\"embedded-image\"><img class=\"mcePuppyImage\" src=\"https://www.wnyc.org/i/620/348/l/80/1/JayZShot.jpeg\" alt=\"\">\n<div class=\"image-caption\">And then on Jay-Z's back. How? </div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"p1\">We talk nihilism with Eugene Thacker &amp; Simon Critchley, leather jackets with June Ambrose, climate change with David Victor, and hope with the father of Transcendental Black Metal - Hunter Hunt Hendrix of the band<span> </span><a href=\"http://www.thrilljockey.com/thrill/Liturgy/Aesthethica#.VA9NM7ywK68\">Liturgy. </a></p>\n<p class=\"p1\">Also, check out WNYC Studio's <em>On the Media </em>episode <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/otm/segments/staring-abyss\">Staring into the Abyss</a>, in it Brooke Gladstone and Jad Abumrad continue their discussion of nihilism and its place in history.</p>\n<p class=\"p1\"><a href=\"http://www.zero-books.net/books/in-the-dust-of-this-planet\">You can find Eugene Thacker's 'In The Dust Of the Planet' at Zero Books<br></a></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong>The Lab</strong></a><span> </span>today.    </em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span> </span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-04-08T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"inheritance-2204", "title"=>"Inheritance", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast040122_inheritance.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1194963", "description"=>"<p><span>Once a kid is born, their genetic fate is pretty much sealed. Or is it? In this episode, originally aired in 2012, we put nature and nurture on a collision course and discover how outside forces can find a way inside us, and change not just our hearts and minds, but the basic biological blueprint that we pass on to future generations.<br><br></span><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong>The Lab</strong></a><span> </span>today.    </em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span> </span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>", "tease"=>"Is our genetic fate sealed the moment we're born? Or can we change the future spelled out by our DNA?", "publishTime"=>1648807200000, "publish-at"=>"2022-04-01T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1648821600, "estimated-duration"=>3874, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/03/RL_Inheritance_EpisodeImage.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2022/03/RL_Inheritance_EpisodeImage.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/03/RL_Inheritance_EpisodeImage.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>199766, "credits-name"=>"Pixabay.com"}, "objectID"=>"1194963", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Inheritance", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Once a kid is born, their genetic fate is pretty much sealed. Or is it? In this episode, originally aired in 2012, we put nature and nurture on a collision course and discover how outside forces can find a way inside us, and change not just our hearts and minds, but the basic biological blueprint that we pass on to future generations.<br><br></span><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong>The Lab</strong></a><span> </span>today.    </em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span> </span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-04-01T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"right-stuff", "title"=>"The Right Stuff", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast032522_rightstuff.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1192699", "description"=>"<p><span>Since the beginning of the space program, we’ve always expected astronauts to be fully abled athletic overachievers who are one-part science-geek, two-parts triathlete – a mix the writer Tom Wolfe famously called “the right stuff.”</span></p>\n<p><span>But what if, this whole time, we’ve had it all wrong?</span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode, reporter Andrew Leland joins a blind linguistics professor named Sheri Wells-Jensen and a crew of eleven other disabled people on a mission to prove that disabled people have what it takes to go to space. And not only that, but that they may have an edge over non-disabled people. We follow the Mission AstroAccess crew members to Long Beach, California, where they hop on an airplane to take an electrifying flight that simulates zero-gravity – a method used by NASA to train astronauts – and afterwards learn that the biggest challenges to a future where space is accessible to all people may not be where they expected to find them. And our reporter Andrew, who is legally blind himself, confronts some unexpected conclusions of his own.<br><br><em>This episode was reported by Andrew Leland and produced by Maria Paz Gutierrez, Matt Kielty and Pat Walters. Jeremy Bloom contributed music and sound design. Production sound recording by Dan McCoy.<br><br></em></span><em>Special thanks to William Pomerantz, Sheyna Gifford, Jim Vanderploeg, Tim Bailey, and Bill Barry</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong>The Lab</strong></a><span> </span>today.    </em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span> </span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1caY-zb5LZ0i6Pr95QTHgSfnGWFMCsmpk/view?usp=sharing\">DOWNLOAD BRAILLE READY FILE HERE</a><span> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/vWtJYGLn6UXm\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/vWtJYGLn6UXm\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/vWtJYGLn6UXm\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/vWtJYGLn6UXm</a></span></span><span>)</span></span></p>\n<p><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm140274514757904b531b6d2-aadc-456b-886e-12dee5667fac\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/vpe6azxvQpM?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a-425346793093022370\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://youtu.be/vpe6azxvQpM\"></iframe></div></div>  <span><span></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span><strong>Citations in this episode</strong><br></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span><strong>Multimedia:<br></strong>Sheri Wells-Jensen’s <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-iiC0jGgxH8\"><em>SETI Institute presentation<br></em></a>Learn more about <a href=\"https://astroaccess.org/\">Mission AstroAccess<br></a>Other work by <a href=\"https://www.andrewleland.org/\">Andrew Leland</a></span></span></p>\n<p><strong>Articles:<br></strong>Sheri Wells-Jensen’s, “<a href=\"https://blogs.scientificamerican.com/observations/the-case-for-disabled-astronauts/\">The Case for Disabled Astronauts</a>,” <em>Scientific American</em><span><br></span></p>", "tease"=>"We&rsquo;re sending more and more people to space every year. But are we sending the right ones?", "publishTime"=>1648216800000, "publish-at"=>"2022-03-25T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1648216800, "estimated-duration"=>2511, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"A woman with a look of surprised excitement floating, weightless in an airplane cabin. ", "name"=>"2022/03/RL_TheRightStuff_EpisodeImage_PowersAl.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2022/03/RL_TheRightStuff_EpisodeImage_PowersAl.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/03/RL_TheRightStuff_EpisodeImage_PowersAl.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>199683, "credits-name"=>"Al Powers for Zero Gravity Corporation"}, "objectID"=>"1192699", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Right Stuff", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Since the beginning of the space program, we’ve always expected astronauts to be fully abled athletic overachievers who are one-part science-geek, two-parts triathlete – a mix the writer Tom Wolfe famously called “the right stuff.”</span></p>\n<p><span>But what if, this whole time, we’ve had it all wrong?</span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode, reporter Andrew Leland joins a blind linguistics professor named Sheri Wells-Jensen and a crew of eleven other disabled people on a mission to prove that disabled people have what it takes to go to space. And not only that, but that they may have an edge over non-disabled people. We follow the Mission AstroAccess crew members to Long Beach, California, where they hop on an airplane to take an electrifying flight that simulates zero-gravity – a method used by NASA to train astronauts – and afterwards learn that the biggest challenges to a future where space is accessible to all people may not be where they expected to find them. And our reporter Andrew, who is legally blind himself, confronts some unexpected conclusions of his own.<br><br><em>This episode was reported by Andrew Leland and produced by Maria Paz Gutierrez, Matt Kielty and Pat Walters. Jeremy Bloom contributed music and sound design. Production sound recording by Dan McCoy.<br><br></em></span><em>Special thanks to William Pomerantz, Sheyna Gifford, Jim Vanderploeg, Tim Bailey, and Bill Barry</em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong>The Lab</strong></a><span> </span>today.    </em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span> </span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1caY-zb5LZ0i6Pr95QTHgSfnGWFMCsmpk/view?usp=sharing\">DOWNLOAD BRAILLE READY FILE HERE</a><span> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/vWtJYGLn6UXm\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/vWtJYGLn6UXm\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/vWtJYGLn6UXm\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/vWtJYGLn6UXm</a></span></span><span>)</span></span></p>\n<p><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm140274514757904b531b6d2-aadc-456b-886e-12dee5667fac\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/vpe6azxvQpM?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a-425346793093022370\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://youtu.be/vpe6azxvQpM\"></iframe></div></div>  <span><span></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span><strong>Citations in this episode</strong><br></span></span></p>\n<p><span><span><strong>Multimedia:<br></strong>Sheri Wells-Jensen’s <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-iiC0jGgxH8\"><em>SETI Institute presentation<br></em></a>Learn more about <a href=\"https://astroaccess.org/\">Mission AstroAccess<br></a>Other work by <a href=\"https://www.andrewleland.org/\">Andrew Leland</a></span></span></p>\n<p><strong>Articles:<br></strong>Sheri Wells-Jensen’s, “<a href=\"https://blogs.scientificamerican.com/observations/the-case-for-disabled-astronauts/\">The Case for Disabled Astronauts</a>,” <em>Scientific American</em><span><br></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-03-25T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"stress-2203", "title"=>"Stress", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast031822_stress.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1190312", "description"=>"<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1046\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1055\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1075\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1069\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1092\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<p>Stress can give your body a boost - raising adrenaline levels, pumping blood to the muscles, heightening our senses. And those sudden superpowers can be a boon when you’re running from a lion. But repeatedly dipping into that well can make you sick, even kill you. Since it feels like there’s been an extra bit of stress going around lately, we decided to replay this episode, originally aired back in 2005, which takes a long hard look at the body's system for getting out of trouble. And how in our modern, hyper-connected world, that system misfires and takes us from the frying pan, right into another, albeit entirely different, frying pan.</p>\n<p>Stanford University neurologist (and part-time \"baboonologist\") Dr. Robert Sapolsky takes us through what happens on our insides when we stand in the wrong line at the supermarket, and offers a few coping strategies: gnawing on wood, beating the crap out of somebody, and having friends. Plus: the story of a singer who lost her voice, and an author stuck in a body that never grew up.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1101\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong>The Lab</strong></a><span> </span>today.    </em></div>\n<div class=\"story-credits__producing-org-credits producing-org-credits\">\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span> </span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>", "tease"=>"When it comes to stress, a little bit goes a long way. We look at why this trick of our biology that saved us on the plains, might be killing us in the boardroom.", "publishTime"=>1647612000000, "publish-at"=>"2022-03-18T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1647612000, "estimated-duration"=>3434, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/03/RL_Stress_Episodeimage_tejedoraaronblanco_fdwFBfr.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1200/900/l/80/2022/03/RL_Stress_Episodeimage_tejedoraaronblanco_fdwFBfr.png", "h"=>900, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/03/RL_Stress_Episodeimage_tejedoraaronblanco_fdwFBfr.png", "w"=>1200, "id"=>199580, "credits-name"=>"Aarón Blanco Tejedor on Unsplash.com"}, "objectID"=>"1190312", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Stress", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<div class=\"story__details\">\n<div id=\"ember1046\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div id=\"ember1055\" class=\"article-tabs ivy-tabs nypr-tabs ember-view\">\n<div aria-hidden=\"false\" id=\"ember1075\" role=\"tabpanel\" class=\"ivy-tabs-tabpanel active ember-view\" aria-labelledby=\"ember1069\" tabindex=\"0\">\n<div class=\"story__body\">\n<div id=\"ember1092\" class=\"ember-view\">\n<div class=\"django-content\">\n<p>Stress can give your body a boost - raising adrenaline levels, pumping blood to the muscles, heightening our senses. And those sudden superpowers can be a boon when you’re running from a lion. But repeatedly dipping into that well can make you sick, even kill you. Since it feels like there’s been an extra bit of stress going around lately, we decided to replay this episode, originally aired back in 2005, which takes a long hard look at the body's system for getting out of trouble. And how in our modern, hyper-connected world, that system misfires and takes us from the frying pan, right into another, albeit entirely different, frying pan.</p>\n<p>Stanford University neurologist (and part-time \"baboonologist\") Dr. Robert Sapolsky takes us through what happens on our insides when we stand in the wrong line at the supermarket, and offers a few coping strategies: gnawing on wood, beating the crap out of somebody, and having friends. Plus: the story of a singer who lost her voice, and an author stuck in a body that never grew up.</p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id=\"ember1101\" class=\"story-credits ember-view\">\n<div class=\"story-credits__appearance-credits\"><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of<span> </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong>The Lab</strong></a><span> </span>today.    </em></div>\n<div class=\"story-credits__producing-org-credits producing-org-credits\">\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a><span> </span><span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n</div>\n</div>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-03-18T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"helen-keller-exorcism", "title"=>"The Helen Keller Exorcism", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast031122_hellenkellerexorcism.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1188188", "description"=>"<p>Fantasy writer Elsa Sjunneson has been haunted by Helen Keller for nearly her entire life. Like Helen, Elsa is Deafblind, and growing up she was constantly compared to her. But for a million different reasons she hated that, because she felt different from her in a million different ways. Then, a year ago, an online conspiracy theory claiming Helen was a fraud exploded on TikTok, and suddenly Elsa found herself drawing her sword and jumping to Helen’s defense, setting off a chain of events that would bring her closer to the disability icon than she ever dreamt. For over a year, Elsa, Lulu and the Radiolab team dug through primary sources, talked to experts, even visited Helen’s birthplace Ivy Green, and discovered the real story of Helen Keller is far more complicated, mysterious and confounding than the simple myth of a young Deafblind girl rescued by her teacher Annie Sullivan. It’s a story of ghosts, surprises, a few tears, a bit of romance, some hard conversations, and a possibly psychic dog.<strong><br></strong><strong><br></strong><em><span>This episode was reported by Elsa Sjunneson and Lulu Miller. It was produced by Sindhu Gnanasambandan and Rachel Cusick, with help from Sarah Qari, Tanya Chawla, and Carolyn McClusker. </span></em><em><span>Jeremy Bloom contributed music and sound design. Additional Mixing by Arianne Wack.<br></span></em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Georgina Kleege, Julia Bascom, Desiree Kocis, Peter C. Kunze, Andrew Leland, Sara Luterman, Alexander Richey, Will Healy, Nate Jones, Nate Peereboom, and Pamela Sabaugh (who was our voice of Helen Keller).<br><br><strong>ASL TRANSCRIPTION</strong><br><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm139682565697792df25521b-bef7-4689-982e-c2337e2b5167\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/P1L5Dn-LQ8c?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a9158495047396415481\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://youtu.be/P1L5Dn-LQ8c\"></iframe></div></div><br></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong>The Lab</strong></a> today.    </em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!<br><br><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Qw4-eXnmBDEEZeF2z5KTGioz5O40vqQj/view?usp=sharing\">DOWNLOAD BRAILLE READY FILE HERE</a> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/s23JtuYxyrNA\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/s23JtuYxyrNA\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/s23JtuYxyrNA\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/s23JtuYxyrNA</a></span>)<br><br></span><strong>Citations in this episode<br><br>Books:<br></strong><span>Elsa Sjunneson, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/56650715-being-seen?ref=nav_sb_ss_2_10\"><em><span>Being Seen<br></span></em></a><span>Kim Nielsen, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/74519.The_Radical_Lives_of_Helen_Keller?ref=nav_sb_ss_1_22\"><em><span>The Radical Lives of Helen Keller<br></span></em></a><span>Georgina Kleege, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/316581.Blind_Rage?ref=nav_sb_ss_3_10\"><em><span>Blind Rage: Letters to Helen Keller<br></span></em></a><span>Katie Booth, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/54737718-the-invention-of-miracles?ref=nav_sb_ss_1_21\"><em><span>The Invention of Miracles: language, power, and Alexander Graham Bell’s quest to end deafness<br></span></em></a><span>Haben Girma, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/43211952-haben?ref=nav_sb_ss_1_6\"><em><span>Haben: The Deafblind Woman Who Conquered Harvard Law<br></span></em></a><span><strong><br>Articles:</strong><br>Susan Crutchfield, “</span><a href=\"https://dsq-sds.org/article/view/577/754\"><span>Play[ing] her part correctly: Helen Keller as Vaudevillian Freak</span></a><span>,” </span><em><span>Disability Studies Quarterly</span></em><span>.<br></span><span>Desiree Kocis, “</span><a href=\"https://www.planeandpilotmag.com/news/pilot-talk/did-helen-keller-fly-plane/\"><span>Did Helen Keller Fly A Plane?</span></a><span>” (she did), </span><em><span>Plane &amp; Pilot Magazine.<br></span></em><span>Peter C. Kunze, “</span><a href=\"https://www.academia.edu/4493150/What_We_Talk_about_When_We_Talk_about_Helen_Keller_Disabilities_in_Children_s_Biographies\"><span>What We Talk about When We Talk about Helen Keller</span></a><span>,” </span><em><span>Children’s Literature Association Quarterly<br></span></em><span>The archives of the </span><a href=\"https://www.afb.org/\"><span>American Foundation for the Blind</span></a><span> (AFB)</span></p>", "tease"=>"Elsa Sjunneson, a fantasy writer and Deafblind woman, was haunted by Hellen Keller.&nbsp;Then, a TikTok conspiracy brought her closer to the disability icon than she ever dreamt.", "publishTime"=>1647010800000, "publish-at"=>"2022-03-11T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1647010800, "estimated-duration"=>3827, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"A cartoon illustration of Elsa facing Helen Keller.  And a quote, 'the industrial system under which we live is at the root of much of the physical deafness and blindness in the world,' in Braille. ", "name"=>"2022/03/RL_HelenKeller_EpisodeImage_RearSabine.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2022/03/RL_HelenKeller_EpisodeImage_RearSabine.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"http://www.michaelsabine.com/", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/03/RL_HelenKeller_EpisodeImage_RearSabine.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>199493, "credits-name"=>"Sabine Rear"}, "objectID"=>"1188188", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Helen Keller Exorcism", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>Fantasy writer Elsa Sjunneson has been haunted by Helen Keller for nearly her entire life. Like Helen, Elsa is Deafblind, and growing up she was constantly compared to her. But for a million different reasons she hated that, because she felt different from her in a million different ways. Then, a year ago, an online conspiracy theory claiming Helen was a fraud exploded on TikTok, and suddenly Elsa found herself drawing her sword and jumping to Helen’s defense, setting off a chain of events that would bring her closer to the disability icon than she ever dreamt. For over a year, Elsa, Lulu and the Radiolab team dug through primary sources, talked to experts, even visited Helen’s birthplace Ivy Green, and discovered the real story of Helen Keller is far more complicated, mysterious and confounding than the simple myth of a young Deafblind girl rescued by her teacher Annie Sullivan. It’s a story of ghosts, surprises, a few tears, a bit of romance, some hard conversations, and a possibly psychic dog.<strong><br></strong><strong><br></strong><em><span>This episode was reported by Elsa Sjunneson and Lulu Miller. It was produced by Sindhu Gnanasambandan and Rachel Cusick, with help from Sarah Qari, Tanya Chawla, and Carolyn McClusker. </span></em><em><span>Jeremy Bloom contributed music and sound design. Additional Mixing by Arianne Wack.<br></span></em></p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to Georgina Kleege, Julia Bascom, Desiree Kocis, Peter C. Kunze, Andrew Leland, Sara Luterman, Alexander Richey, Will Healy, Nate Jones, Nate Peereboom, and Pamela Sabaugh (who was our voice of Helen Keller).<br><br><strong>ASL TRANSCRIPTION</strong><br><div class=\"user-embedded-video\"><div id=\"videoplayer_idm139682565697792df25521b-bef7-4689-982e-c2337e2b5167\"><iframe width=\"620\" height=\"349\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/P1L5Dn-LQ8c?wmode=transparent&amp;autohide=1&amp;rel=0&amp;showinfo=0&amp;feature=oembed&amp;enablejsapi=1\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen=\"allowfullscreen\" id=\"a9158495047396415481\" class=\"youtube_video\" mozallowfullscreen=\"mozallowfullscreen\" webkitallowfullscreen=\"webkitallowfullscreen\" data-original-url=\"https://youtu.be/P1L5Dn-LQ8c\"></iframe></div></div><br></em></p>\n<p><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong>The Lab</strong></a> today.    </em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!<br><br><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Qw4-eXnmBDEEZeF2z5KTGioz5O40vqQj/view?usp=sharing\">DOWNLOAD BRAILLE READY FILE HERE</a> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/s23JtuYxyrNA\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/s23JtuYxyrNA\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/s23JtuYxyrNA\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/s23JtuYxyrNA</a></span>)<br><br></span><strong>Citations in this episode<br><br>Books:<br></strong><span>Elsa Sjunneson, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/56650715-being-seen?ref=nav_sb_ss_2_10\"><em><span>Being Seen<br></span></em></a><span>Kim Nielsen, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/74519.The_Radical_Lives_of_Helen_Keller?ref=nav_sb_ss_1_22\"><em><span>The Radical Lives of Helen Keller<br></span></em></a><span>Georgina Kleege, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/316581.Blind_Rage?ref=nav_sb_ss_3_10\"><em><span>Blind Rage: Letters to Helen Keller<br></span></em></a><span>Katie Booth, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/54737718-the-invention-of-miracles?ref=nav_sb_ss_1_21\"><em><span>The Invention of Miracles: language, power, and Alexander Graham Bell’s quest to end deafness<br></span></em></a><span>Haben Girma, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/43211952-haben?ref=nav_sb_ss_1_6\"><em><span>Haben: The Deafblind Woman Who Conquered Harvard Law<br></span></em></a><span><strong><br>Articles:</strong><br>Susan Crutchfield, “</span><a href=\"https://dsq-sds.org/article/view/577/754\"><span>Play[ing] her part correctly: Helen Keller as Vaudevillian Freak</span></a><span>,” </span><em><span>Disability Studies Quarterly</span></em><span>.<br></span><span>Desiree Kocis, “</span><a href=\"https://www.planeandpilotmag.com/news/pilot-talk/did-helen-keller-fly-plane/\"><span>Did Helen Keller Fly A Plane?</span></a><span>” (she did), </span><em><span>Plane &amp; Pilot Magazine.<br></span></em><span>Peter C. Kunze, “</span><a href=\"https://www.academia.edu/4493150/What_We_Talk_about_When_We_Talk_about_Helen_Keller_Disabilities_in_Children_s_Biographies\"><span>What We Talk about When We Talk about Helen Keller</span></a><span>,” </span><em><span>Children’s Literature Association Quarterly<br></span></em><span>The archives of the </span><a href=\"https://www.afb.org/\"><span>American Foundation for the Blind</span></a><span> (AFB)</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-03-11T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"life-barrel", "title"=>"Life in a Barrel", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast030422_lifebarrel.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1179880", "description"=>"<p>This week, we flip the Disney story of life on its head thanks to a barrel of seawater, a 1970s era computer, and underwater geysers. It’s the chaos of life.</p>\n<p>Latif, Lulu, and our Senior Producer Matt Kielty were all sitting on their own little stories until they got thrown into the studio, and had their cherished beliefs about the shape of life put on a collision course. From an accidental study of sea creatures, to the ambitions of Stephen J Gould, to an undercooked theory that captured the world’s imagination, we undo the seeming order of the living world and try to make some music out of the wreckage. (Bonus: Learn how Francis Crick really thought life got started on this planet).</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser, Matt Kielty, Heather Radke, Lulu Miller and Candice Wang. It was produced by Matt Kielty and Simon Adler. Sound and music from Matt Kielty, Simon Adler, and Jeremy Bloom, and dialogue mix by Arianne Wack.<br><br><span>Special thanks to Alan and Alida Goffinski for giving our chaos musical life in the song at the end of the episode.</span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span> </span></em></span></p>\n<p><strong>Citations in this episode<br><br>Scientific Papers:<br></strong>Elisa Beninca, Reinhard Heerkloss, et al, <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/nature06512\">“Chaos in a long-term experiment with a plankton community”</a> <em>Nature</em> (2008)<br><span>Hendrik Schubert, Reinhard Heerkloss, et al, </span><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6937249/pdf/41598_2019_Article_56851.pdf\"><span>“Chaos theory discloses triggers and drivers of plankton dynamics in a stable environment”</span></a> <em><span>Scientific Reports</span></em><span> (2019)</span></p>\n<p><strong>Books:<br></strong><span>Nick Lane, </span><a href=\"https://nick-lane.net/books/the-vital-question-why-is-life-the-way-it-is/\"><em><span>The Vital Question</span></em></a><em><span>: Energy, Evolution, and the Origins of Complex Life<br></span></em><span>Francis Crick, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/865615.Life_Itself\"><em><span>Life Itself</span></em></a><em><span>: Its Origin and Nature<br></span></em><span>Stephen Jay Gould: </span><a href=\"https://www.publishersweekly.com/978-0-517-70394-6\"><em><span>Full House</span></em></a><em><span>: The Spread of Excellence from Plato to Darwin, </span></em><span>and </span><a href=\"https://wwnorton.com/books/The-Mismeasure-of-Man/\"><em><span>The Mismeasure of Man<br></span></em></a><a href=\"https://wwnorton.com/books/The-Mismeasure-of-Man/\"><span></span></a><span>David M. Raup, </span><a href=\"https://www.abebooks.com/9780393309270/Extinction-Bad-Genes-Luck-Raup-0393309274/plp\"><em><span>Extinction: Bad Genes or Bad Luck?<br></span></em></a>David Sepkoski, <em><a href=\"https://chicago.universitypressscholarship.com/view/10.7208/chicago/9780226748580.001.0001/upso-9780226748559-chapter-1\">Rereading the Fossil Record</a>: The Growth of Paleobiology as an Evolutionary Discipline</em></p>\n<p><span><em><span> </span></em></span></p>", "tease"=>"This week, we flip the Disney story of life on it&rsquo;s head thanks to a barrel of seawater, a 1970s era computer, and underwater geyser. It&rsquo;s the chaos of life.", "publishTime"=>1646406000000, "publish-at"=>"2022-03-04T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1646406000, "estimated-duration"=>3203, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"An illustration with a deep blue background wherein sea life and creatures are spiraling up to a person looking over it all. ", "name"=>"2022/03/RL_LifeInABarrel_EpisodeImage.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2022/03/RL_LifeInABarrel_EpisodeImage.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/03/RL_LifeInABarrel_EpisodeImage.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>199378, "credits-name"=>"Kevin Whipple"}, "objectID"=>"1179880", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Life in a Barrel", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>This week, we flip the Disney story of life on its head thanks to a barrel of seawater, a 1970s era computer, and underwater geysers. It’s the chaos of life.</p>\n<p>Latif, Lulu, and our Senior Producer Matt Kielty were all sitting on their own little stories until they got thrown into the studio, and had their cherished beliefs about the shape of life put on a collision course. From an accidental study of sea creatures, to the ambitions of Stephen J Gould, to an undercooked theory that captured the world’s imagination, we undo the seeming order of the living world and try to make some music out of the wreckage. (Bonus: Learn how Francis Crick really thought life got started on this planet).</p>\n<p><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser, Matt Kielty, Heather Radke, Lulu Miller and Candice Wang. It was produced by Matt Kielty and Simon Adler. Sound and music from Matt Kielty, Simon Adler, and Jeremy Bloom, and dialogue mix by Arianne Wack.<br><br><span>Special thanks to Alan and Alida Goffinski for giving our chaos musical life in the song at the end of the episode.</span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span> </span></em></span></p>\n<p><strong>Citations in this episode<br><br>Scientific Papers:<br></strong>Elisa Beninca, Reinhard Heerkloss, et al, <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/nature06512\">“Chaos in a long-term experiment with a plankton community”</a> <em>Nature</em> (2008)<br><span>Hendrik Schubert, Reinhard Heerkloss, et al, </span><a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6937249/pdf/41598_2019_Article_56851.pdf\"><span>“Chaos theory discloses triggers and drivers of plankton dynamics in a stable environment”</span></a> <em><span>Scientific Reports</span></em><span> (2019)</span></p>\n<p><strong>Books:<br></strong><span>Nick Lane, </span><a href=\"https://nick-lane.net/books/the-vital-question-why-is-life-the-way-it-is/\"><em><span>The Vital Question</span></em></a><em><span>: Energy, Evolution, and the Origins of Complex Life<br></span></em><span>Francis Crick, </span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/865615.Life_Itself\"><em><span>Life Itself</span></em></a><em><span>: Its Origin and Nature<br></span></em><span>Stephen Jay Gould: </span><a href=\"https://www.publishersweekly.com/978-0-517-70394-6\"><em><span>Full House</span></em></a><em><span>: The Spread of Excellence from Plato to Darwin, </span></em><span>and </span><a href=\"https://wwnorton.com/books/The-Mismeasure-of-Man/\"><em><span>The Mismeasure of Man<br></span></em></a><a href=\"https://wwnorton.com/books/The-Mismeasure-of-Man/\"><span></span></a><span>David M. Raup, </span><a href=\"https://www.abebooks.com/9780393309270/Extinction-Bad-Genes-Luck-Raup-0393309274/plp\"><em><span>Extinction: Bad Genes or Bad Luck?<br></span></em></a>David Sepkoski, <em><a href=\"https://chicago.universitypressscholarship.com/view/10.7208/chicago/9780226748580.001.0001/upso-9780226748559-chapter-1\">Rereading the Fossil Record</a>: The Growth of Paleobiology as an Evolutionary Discipline</em></p>\n<p><span><em><span> </span></em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-03-04T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"speed-2202", "title"=>"Speed", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast022522_speed.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1183075", "description"=>"<p><span>We live our lives at human speed, we experience and interact with the world on a human time scale. In this episode, which first aired in its entirety in the winter of 2013, we put ourselves through the paces. We examine a material that exists between two states of matter, take a ride on the death-defying roller coaster that is the stock market, open up our internal clocks of thought, and achieve mastery over the fastest thing in the universe.<br></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"In this episode, we put ourselves through the paces, peek inside a microsecond, and master the fastest thing in the universe.&nbsp;", "publishTime"=>1645801200000, "publish-at"=>"2022-02-25T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1645801200, "estimated-duration"=>3371, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"Blurry, long exposure image of people moving across a subway platform.", "name"=>"2022/02/RL_Speed_EpisodeImage_PauwelsGeorgie.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2022/02/RL_Speed_EpisodeImage_PauwelsGeorgie.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/02/RL_Speed_EpisodeImage_PauwelsGeorgie.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>199294, "credits-name"=>"Georgie Pauwels"}, "objectID"=>"1183075", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Speed", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>We live our lives at human speed, we experience and interact with the world on a human time scale. In this episode, which first aired in its entirety in the winter of 2013, we put ourselves through the paces. We examine a material that exists between two states of matter, take a ride on the death-defying roller coaster that is the stock market, open up our internal clocks of thought, and achieve mastery over the fastest thing in the universe.<br></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today.</span></em><em><span>    </span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-02-25T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"wordless-place", "title"=>"The Wordless Place", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast021822_worldlessplace.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1180664", "description"=>"<p><span>This week, we turn to an expert who tromps the wilds of wordlessness. Lulu’s young son. In this essay, originally published for </span><em><span>The</span></em> <em><span>Paris Review</span></em><span> under the title “</span><a href=\"https://www.theparisreview.org/blog/2020/10/05/the-eleventh-word/\"><span>The Eleventh Word</span></a><span>,” Lulu explores what is lost with the gaining of language. And how, in a very odd way, a fear of confusion and the unknown may begin with the advent of words. The </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span> sound team brings this piece to life with original music, and at one point the words melt right out of the air.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of <strong><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\">The Lab</a> </strong>today.</span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>", "tease"=>"Lulu and her son explore what is lost with the gaining of language. And how, in a very odd way, a fear of confusion and the unknown may begin with the advent of words.", "publishTime"=>1645196400000, "publish-at"=>"2022-02-18T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1645196400, "estimated-duration"=>1596, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/02/RL_Lulu_EpisodeImage_4x3.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/l/80/2022/02/RL_Lulu_EpisodeImage_4x3.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"l", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/02/RL_Lulu_EpisodeImage_4x3.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>199199, "credits-name"=>"F.E. Clarke and rawpixel.com"}, "objectID"=>"1180664", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The Wordless Place", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>This week, we turn to an expert who tromps the wilds of wordlessness. Lulu’s young son. In this essay, originally published for </span><em><span>The</span></em> <em><span>Paris Review</span></em><span> under the title “</span><a href=\"https://www.theparisreview.org/blog/2020/10/05/the-eleventh-word/\"><span>The Eleventh Word</span></a><span>,” Lulu explores what is lost with the gaining of language. And how, in a very odd way, a fear of confusion and the unknown may begin with the advent of words. The </span><em><span>Radiolab</span></em><span> sound team brings this piece to life with original music, and at one point the words melt right out of the air.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of <strong><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\">The Lab</a> </strong>today.</span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-02-18T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"hello-2202", "title"=>"Hello", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast021122_hello.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1178438", "description"=>"<p><span>It's hard to start a conversation with a stranger—especially when that stranger is, well, different. He doesn't share your customs, celebrate your holidays, watch your TV shows, or even speak your language. Plus he has a blowhole.</span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode, which originally aired in the summer of 2014, we try to make contact with some of the strangest strangers on our little planet: dolphins. Producer Lynn Levy eavesdrops on some human-dolphin conversations, from a studio apartment in the Virgin Islands to a research vessel in the Bermuda Triangle.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today</span></em>.</span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>", "tease"=>"It's tough to make small talk with a stranger&mdash;especially when that stranger doesn't speak your language. (And he has a blowhole.)", "publishTime"=>1644591600000, "publish-at"=>"2022-02-11T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1644591600, "estimated-duration"=>2783, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/02/RL_Hello_EpisodeImage.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2022/02/RL_Hello_EpisodeImage.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/02/RL_Hello_EpisodeImage.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>199102, "credits-name"=>"きうこ"}, "objectID"=>"1178438", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Hello", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>It's hard to start a conversation with a stranger—especially when that stranger is, well, different. He doesn't share your customs, celebrate your holidays, watch your TV shows, or even speak your language. Plus he has a blowhole.</span></p>\n<p><span>In this episode, which originally aired in the summer of 2014, we try to make contact with some of the strangest strangers on our little planet: dolphins. Producer Lynn Levy eavesdrops on some human-dolphin conversations, from a studio apartment in the Virgin Islands to a research vessel in the Bermuda Triangle.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member of <a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/membership\"><strong><em>The Lab</em></strong></a> <em><span>today</span></em>.</span></em></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-02-11T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"forests-forests", "title"=>"Forests on Forests", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast020422_forestsonforests.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1176365", "description"=>"<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">For much of history, tree canopies were pretty much completely ignored by science. It was as if researchers said collectively, \"It's just going to be empty up there, and we've got our hands full studying the trees down here! So why bother?!\"</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">But then, around the mid-1980s, a few ecologists around the world got curious and started making their way up into the treetops using any means necessary (ropes, cranes, hot air dirigibles) to document all they could find. It didn't take long for them to realize not only was the forest canopy not empty, it was absolutely </span><em><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">filled to the brim</span></em><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> with life. You've heard of treehouses? How about tree </span><em><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">gardens</span></em><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">?! </span></p>\n<p><span>This week we journey up into the sky and discover Forests above the forest. We learn about the secret powers of these sky gardens from ecologist <a href=\"https://twitter.com/korenamafune\">Korena Mafune</a>, and we follow <a href=\"https://nalininadkarni.com/\">Nalini Nadkarni</a> as she makes a ground-breaking discovery that changes how we understand what trees are capable of. </span></p>\n<p><span>P.S. This episode is a layer cake of arboreal surprises (including the reappearance of a certain retired host). </span></p>\n<p><strong>A few visual tre(e)ats: </strong></p>\n<p><span>We first learned about the magical world of the canopy from </span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mRd8_Tu7YDs&amp;ab_channel=PBSTerra\"><span>this beautiful video</span></a><span> from Michael Werner, Joe Hanson, and the PBS Overview team. It features Korena Mafune’s research up in the treetops, as well as the people who have dedicated their lives to saving what’s left of the old growth forests. We highly recommend checking it out! <br></span><br><span>And, if you’re hankering to go climb a tree after this episode, you might enjoy browsing Hallie Bateman’s </span><a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2015/09/03/magazine/04mag-trees.html?_r=0\"><span>wonderfully illustrated guide</span></a><span> to the best climbing trees in NYC for a little inspiration.<br><br></span><strong><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </em></strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><strong><em>.<br><br></em></strong><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "tease"=>"Join us, along with a surprise guest, as we grab a branch, pull ourselves up off the forest floor, and climb through into a secret garden in the sky.", "publishTime"=>1643986800000, "publish-at"=>"2022-02-04T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1643986800, "estimated-duration"=>1446, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"A mossy, leafy, damp and lush green forest scene among the trunks. ", "name"=>"2022/02/Forests-on-Forests-RL_4x3-by-Malonecr7_WEB-READY_8QgUTtn.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1139/854/c/80/2022/02/Forests-on-Forests-RL_4x3-by-Malonecr7_WEB-READY_8QgUTtn.png", "h"=>854, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/02/Forests-on-Forests-RL_4x3-by-Malonecr7_WEB-READY_8QgUTtn.png", "w"=>1139, "id"=>199000, "credits-name"=>"Malonecr7"}, "objectID"=>"1176365", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Forests on Forests", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">For much of history, tree canopies were pretty much completely ignored by science. It was as if researchers said collectively, \"It's just going to be empty up there, and we've got our hands full studying the trees down here! So why bother?!\"</span></p>\n<p><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">But then, around the mid-1980s, a few ecologists around the world got curious and started making their way up into the treetops using any means necessary (ropes, cranes, hot air dirigibles) to document all they could find. It didn't take long for them to realize not only was the forest canopy not empty, it was absolutely </span><em><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">filled to the brim</span></em><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\"> with life. You've heard of treehouses? How about tree </span><em><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">gardens</span></em><span data-preserver-spaces=\"true\">?! </span></p>\n<p><span>This week we journey up into the sky and discover Forests above the forest. We learn about the secret powers of these sky gardens from ecologist <a href=\"https://twitter.com/korenamafune\">Korena Mafune</a>, and we follow <a href=\"https://nalininadkarni.com/\">Nalini Nadkarni</a> as she makes a ground-breaking discovery that changes how we understand what trees are capable of. </span></p>\n<p><span>P.S. This episode is a layer cake of arboreal surprises (including the reappearance of a certain retired host). </span></p>\n<p><strong>A few visual tre(e)ats: </strong></p>\n<p><span>We first learned about the magical world of the canopy from </span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mRd8_Tu7YDs&amp;ab_channel=PBSTerra\"><span>this beautiful video</span></a><span> from Michael Werner, Joe Hanson, and the PBS Overview team. It features Korena Mafune’s research up in the treetops, as well as the people who have dedicated their lives to saving what’s left of the old growth forests. We highly recommend checking it out! <br></span><br><span>And, if you’re hankering to go climb a tree after this episode, you might enjoy browsing Hallie Bateman’s </span><a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2015/09/03/magazine/04mag-trees.html?_r=0\"><span>wonderfully illustrated guide</span></a><span> to the best climbing trees in NYC for a little inspiration.<br><br></span><strong><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </em></strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><strong><em>.<br><br></em></strong><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span> </span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-02-04T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"first-radiolab", "title"=>"The First Radiolab", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast012822_firstradiolab.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1173761", "description"=>"<p><span>Jad started Radiolab roughly 20 years ago. And now he is stepping aside from hosting and producing the show to replenish, to think, to rock in his chair and be with his kids and wife, and maybe make some music. The news has been all over twitter and there’s a letter from Jad and our hosts Latif and Lulu on the website. But in this episode, Jad talks through his decision to leave and the future of the show with Lulu and Latif. And then, as a parting gift, we play him the very first episode of Radiolab (“The Radio Lab” as he called it then). He tells us about biking the CDs over the Brooklyn bridge just before the show was supposed to air, reading the news and weather between segments, and then we just sit back together and listen to where it all began.</span></p>\n<p><span>Jad, for those of us who have been radically changed by the thing you put out into the world, we are both sad to lose you in our ears and endlessly grateful for what you’ve given us</span><strong>.</strong></p>", "tease"=>"Jad Abumrad, our founder,&nbsp;is stepping aside from the show&nbsp;to focus on being. We look back at the very first Radiolab, and&nbsp;look forward to&nbsp;what is yet to come.", "publishTime"=>1643382000000, "publish-at"=>"2022-01-28T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1643382000, "estimated-duration"=>5066, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/01/Jad_magnifyingglass4x3.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2022/01/Jad_magnifyingglass4x3.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/01/Jad_magnifyingglass4x3.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>198900, "credits-name"=>"Matthew Septimus"}, "objectID"=>"1173761", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The First Radiolab", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Jad started Radiolab roughly 20 years ago. And now he is stepping aside from hosting and producing the show to replenish, to think, to rock in his chair and be with his kids and wife, and maybe make some music. The news has been all over twitter and there’s a letter from Jad and our hosts Latif and Lulu on the website. But in this episode, Jad talks through his decision to leave and the future of the show with Lulu and Latif. And then, as a parting gift, we play him the very first episode of Radiolab (“The Radio Lab” as he called it then). He tells us about biking the CDs over the Brooklyn bridge just before the show was supposed to air, reading the news and weather between segments, and then we just sit back together and listen to where it all began.</span></p>\n<p><span>Jad, for those of us who have been radically changed by the thing you put out into the world, we are both sad to lose you in our ears and endlessly grateful for what you’ve given us</span><strong>.</strong></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-01-28T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"11th-letter-george", "title"=>"The 11th: A Letter From George", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast012122_11thlettergeorge.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1171625", "description"=>"<p><span>Last week, Lulu heard an interview that trapped her in her car. She decided to play it for Latif.</span></p>\n<p><span>The interview – originally from a podcast called </span><a href=\"https://relentlesspicnic.com/\"><span>The Relentless Picnic</span></a><span>, but presented by one of Lulu’s current podcast faves, </span><a href=\"http://pineapple.fm/the-11th\"><span>The 11th</span></a><span> – is part of an episode of mini pep talks designed to help us all get through this cold, dark, second-pandemic-winter-in-a-row. </span><span>But the segment that Lulu brings Latif is about someone trying to get through something arguably much more difficult, something a pep talk can’t solve, </span><span>but that a couple friends — and one very generous stranger — might be able to help make a little more bearable.</span></p>\n<p><span>The episode of The 11th this comes from is </span><a href=\"https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/im-here-to-pep-you-up/id1566642706?i=1000547543765\"><span>“I’m Here to Pep You Up.”</span></a><span> The Relentless Picnic is currently running a series of episodes called CABIN, an audio exploration of isolation, which you can listen to </span><a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/playlist/3qvthPp5wUlGhJrWK4o9nI\"><span>here</span></a><span>. The organization where Matt volunteers as a counselor is called </span><a href=\"https://www.epilepsy.com/learn/early-death-and-sudep/sudep\"><span>SUDEP</span></a><span>. The Lu Olkowski story Lulu recommends at the end of the episode is </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/segments/91568-grandpa\"><span>“Grandpa,”</span></a><span> and the lobster story Latif recommends is </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/articles/91969-the-luckiest-lobster\"><span>“The Luckiest Lobster.”</span></a></p>\n<p><strong>Special Thanks:</strong></p>\n<p><em>Eric Mennel, senior producer at The 11th, and host of the podcast <a href=\"https://shows.cadence13.com/podcast/stay-away\">Stay Away from Matthew Magill.<br><br></a>Lu Olkowski, voracious listener, super reporter, and host of the podcast <a href=\"https://www.cbc.ca/radio/loveme\">Love Me</a><span><em>.</em><br></span></em><span><br></span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </em><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><em><span>.  </span></em></span></p>", "tease"=>"Two old friends and one new one show up to help a man get through something impossibly hard.", "publishTime"=>1642777200000, "publish-at"=>"2022-01-21T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1642777200, "estimated-duration"=>1384, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/01/11th_Logo_080921.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1860/c/80/2022/01/11th_Logo_080921.png", "h"=>1860, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/01/11th_Logo_080921.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>198769, "credits-name"=>"Pineapple Street Studios"}, "objectID"=>"1171625", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"The 11th: A Letter From George", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>Last week, Lulu heard an interview that trapped her in her car. She decided to play it for Latif.</span></p>\n<p><span>The interview – originally from a podcast called </span><a href=\"https://relentlesspicnic.com/\"><span>The Relentless Picnic</span></a><span>, but presented by one of Lulu’s current podcast faves, </span><a href=\"http://pineapple.fm/the-11th\"><span>The 11th</span></a><span> – is part of an episode of mini pep talks designed to help us all get through this cold, dark, second-pandemic-winter-in-a-row. </span><span>But the segment that Lulu brings Latif is about someone trying to get through something arguably much more difficult, something a pep talk can’t solve, </span><span>but that a couple friends — and one very generous stranger — might be able to help make a little more bearable.</span></p>\n<p><span>The episode of The 11th this comes from is </span><a href=\"https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/im-here-to-pep-you-up/id1566642706?i=1000547543765\"><span>“I’m Here to Pep You Up.”</span></a><span> The Relentless Picnic is currently running a series of episodes called CABIN, an audio exploration of isolation, which you can listen to </span><a href=\"https://open.spotify.com/playlist/3qvthPp5wUlGhJrWK4o9nI\"><span>here</span></a><span>. The organization where Matt volunteers as a counselor is called </span><a href=\"https://www.epilepsy.com/learn/early-death-and-sudep/sudep\"><span>SUDEP</span></a><span>. The Lu Olkowski story Lulu recommends at the end of the episode is </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/segments/91568-grandpa\"><span>“Grandpa,”</span></a><span> and the lobster story Latif recommends is </span><a href=\"https://www.wnycstudios.org/podcasts/radiolab/articles/91969-the-luckiest-lobster\"><span>“The Luckiest Lobster.”</span></a></p>\n<p><strong>Special Thanks:</strong></p>\n<p><em>Eric Mennel, senior producer at The 11th, and host of the podcast <a href=\"https://shows.cadence13.com/podcast/stay-away\">Stay Away from Matthew Magill.<br><br></a>Lu Olkowski, voracious listener, super reporter, and host of the podcast <a href=\"https://www.cbc.ca/radio/loveme\">Love Me</a><span><em>.</em><br></span></em><span><br></span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span></p>\n<p><span><em>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </em><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><em><span>.  </span></em></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-01-21T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"darkode-2201", "title"=>"Darkode", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast011422_darkode.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1169532", "description"=>"<p><span>It would seem that hackers today can do just about anything they want - from turning on the cellphone in your pocket to holding your life's work hostage. Cyber criminals today have more sophisticated tools, have learned to work collaboratively around the world and have found innovative ways to remain deep undercover in the internet's shadows. This episode, we shine a light into those shadows to see the world from the perspectives of both cybercrime victims and perpetrators.</span></p>\n<p><span>First we meet mother-daughter duo Alina and Inna Simone, who tell us about being held hostage by criminals who have burrowed into their lives from half a world away. Along the way we learn about the legally sticky spot that unwitting accomplices like Will Wheeler find themselves in.</span></p>\n<p><span>Then reporter and author Joseph Menn tells us about the surprisingly lucrative professional hacker structure in places throughout the former Soviet Union. Finally, the co-creator of one of the most notorious online marketplaces to ever exist speaks to us and NPR cyber-crime expert Dina Temple-Raston about how a young suburban Boy Scout can turn into a world renowned black hat hacker.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </span></em><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><em><span>.<br></span></em><span><br><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span><br></span></p>", "tease"=>"We shine a light into the shadows of ransomware and cybercrime, from the perspectives of the perpetrators and victims.", "publishTime"=>1642172400000, "publish-at"=>"2022-01-14T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1642172400, "estimated-duration"=>2314, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"A silhouette of a hand in front of a backdrop of multi-colored, cascading, binary code. ", "name"=>"2022/01/RL_Darkode_EpisodeImage_4x3.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1860/1395/c/80/2022/01/RL_Darkode_EpisodeImage_4x3.png", "h"=>1395, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/01/RL_Darkode_EpisodeImage_4x3.png", "w"=>1860, "id"=>198680, "credits-name"=>"Kai Stachowiak"}, "objectID"=>"1169532", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Darkode", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>It would seem that hackers today can do just about anything they want - from turning on the cellphone in your pocket to holding your life's work hostage. Cyber criminals today have more sophisticated tools, have learned to work collaboratively around the world and have found innovative ways to remain deep undercover in the internet's shadows. This episode, we shine a light into those shadows to see the world from the perspectives of both cybercrime victims and perpetrators.</span></p>\n<p><span>First we meet mother-daughter duo Alina and Inna Simone, who tell us about being held hostage by criminals who have burrowed into their lives from half a world away. Along the way we learn about the legally sticky spot that unwitting accomplices like Will Wheeler find themselves in.</span></p>\n<p><span>Then reporter and author Joseph Menn tells us about the surprisingly lucrative professional hacker structure in places throughout the former Soviet Union. Finally, the co-creator of one of the most notorious online marketplaces to ever exist speaks to us and NPR cyber-crime expert Dina Temple-Raston about how a young suburban Boy Scout can turn into a world renowned black hat hacker.</span></p>\n<p><em><span>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </span></em><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><em><span>.<br></span></em><span><br><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong>Radiolab is on YouTube!</strong></a> <span>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</span><br></span></p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-01-14T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}, {"slug"=>"worst-year-ever", "title"=>"Worst. Year. Ever.", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/audio.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast010722_worstyearever.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1167712", "description"=>"<p><span>What was the worst year to be alive on planet Earth?  </span></p>\n<p><span>We make the case for 536 AD, which set off a cascade of catastrophes that is almost too horrible to imagine. A supervolcano. The disappearance of shadows. A failure of bread. Plague rats. Using evidence painstakingly gathered around the world - from Mongolian tree rings to Greenlandic ice cores to Mayan artifacts - we paint a portrait of what scientists and historians think went wrong, and what we think it felt like to be there in real time. (Spoiler: not so hot.)  We hear a hymn for the dead from the ancient kingdom of Axum, the closest we can get to the sound of grief from a millennium and a half ago.</span></p>\n<p><span>The horrors of 536 make us wonder about the parallels and perpendiculars with our own time: does it make you feel any better knowing that your suffering is part of a global crisis? Or does it just make things worse?\"<br><br><em>Thanks to reporter Ann Gibbons whose Science article \"<a href=\"https://www.science.org/doi/abs/10.1126/science.362.6416.733\">Eruption made 536 ‘the worst year to be alive</a>\"<strong> </strong></em><em>got us interested in the first place. </em><br><br></span><span><strong>In case you want to learn more about 536, here are some other sources: <br></strong><br></span><span>Timothy P. Newfield, </span><strong><a href=\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1057/978-1-137-43020-5_32\">“The Climate Downturn of 536-50”</a></strong><span> in the </span><em><span>Palgrave Handbook on Climate History<br><br></span></em><span>Dallas Abbott et al., </span><a href=\"https://academiccommons.columbia.edu/doi/10.7916/D81V5DCR\"><span><strong>“What caused terrestrial dust loading and climate downturns between A.D. 533 and 540?”<br></strong><br></span></a><span>Joel Gunn and Alesio Ciarini (editors),</span><a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/348577900_THE_AD_536_CRISIS_A_21ST_CENTURY_PERSPECTIVE\"><span><strong> “The A.D. 536 Crisis: A 21st Century Perspective”<br></strong><br></span></a><span>Antti Arjava, </span><strong><a href=\"https://www.jstor.org/stable/4128751\">“The Mystery Cloud of 536 CE in the Mediterranean Sources”</a></strong><span><strong> <br></strong><br></span><span>And for more on the composer Yared, watch Meklit Hadero’s TED talk <strong>“</strong></span><strong><a href=\"https://www.ted.com/talks/meklit_hadero_the_unexpected_beauty_of_everyday_sounds/transcript?language=en\">The Unexpected Beauty of Everyday Sounds”</a></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Credits: </strong><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and Lulu Miller, and produced by Simon Adler.  With sound and music from Simon Adler and Jeremy Bloom.<br></em></p>\n<p><span><strong>Special Thanks:</strong> </span><em>Thanks to Joel Gunn, Dallas Abbott, Mathias Nordvig, Emma Rigby, Robert Dull, Daniel Yacob, Kay Shelemey, Jacke Phillips, Meklit Hadero, and Joan Aruz.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><strong><em>. </em></strong></em></p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Radiolab</strong><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> is on YouTube!</strong></a> </strong>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</p>", "tease"=>"If you thought the last few years were bad &hellip;", "publishTime"=>1641567600000, "publish-at"=>"2022-01-07T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1641567600, "estimated-duration"=>1478, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"", "name"=>"2022/01/WorstYearEver_4X3.jpg", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2022/01/WorstYearEver_4X3.jpg", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2022/01/WorstYearEver_4X3.jpg", "w"=>1600, "id"=>198585, "credits-name"=>"Liam Eisenberg"}, "objectID"=>"1167712", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Worst. Year. Ever.", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p><span>What was the worst year to be alive on planet Earth?  </span></p>\n<p><span>We make the case for 536 AD, which set off a cascade of catastrophes that is almost too horrible to imagine. A supervolcano. The disappearance of shadows. A failure of bread. Plague rats. Using evidence painstakingly gathered around the world - from Mongolian tree rings to Greenlandic ice cores to Mayan artifacts - we paint a portrait of what scientists and historians think went wrong, and what we think it felt like to be there in real time. (Spoiler: not so hot.)  We hear a hymn for the dead from the ancient kingdom of Axum, the closest we can get to the sound of grief from a millennium and a half ago.</span></p>\n<p><span>The horrors of 536 make us wonder about the parallels and perpendiculars with our own time: does it make you feel any better knowing that your suffering is part of a global crisis? Or does it just make things worse?\"<br><br><em>Thanks to reporter Ann Gibbons whose Science article \"<a href=\"https://www.science.org/doi/abs/10.1126/science.362.6416.733\">Eruption made 536 ‘the worst year to be alive</a>\"<strong> </strong></em><em>got us interested in the first place. </em><br><br></span><span><strong>In case you want to learn more about 536, here are some other sources: <br></strong><br></span><span>Timothy P. Newfield, </span><strong><a href=\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1057/978-1-137-43020-5_32\">“The Climate Downturn of 536-50”</a></strong><span> in the </span><em><span>Palgrave Handbook on Climate History<br><br></span></em><span>Dallas Abbott et al., </span><a href=\"https://academiccommons.columbia.edu/doi/10.7916/D81V5DCR\"><span><strong>“What caused terrestrial dust loading and climate downturns between A.D. 533 and 540?”<br></strong><br></span></a><span>Joel Gunn and Alesio Ciarini (editors),</span><a href=\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/348577900_THE_AD_536_CRISIS_A_21ST_CENTURY_PERSPECTIVE\"><span><strong> “The A.D. 536 Crisis: A 21st Century Perspective”<br></strong><br></span></a><span>Antti Arjava, </span><strong><a href=\"https://www.jstor.org/stable/4128751\">“The Mystery Cloud of 536 CE in the Mediterranean Sources”</a></strong><span><strong> <br></strong><br></span><span>And for more on the composer Yared, watch Meklit Hadero’s TED talk <strong>“</strong></span><strong><a href=\"https://www.ted.com/talks/meklit_hadero_the_unexpected_beauty_of_everyday_sounds/transcript?language=en\">The Unexpected Beauty of Everyday Sounds”</a></strong></p>\n<p><strong>Credits: </strong><em>This episode was reported by Latif Nasser and Lulu Miller, and produced by Simon Adler.  With sound and music from Simon Adler and Jeremy Bloom.<br></em></p>\n<p><span><strong>Special Thanks:</strong> </span><em>Thanks to Joel Gunn, Dallas Abbott, Mathias Nordvig, Emma Rigby, Robert Dull, Daniel Yacob, Kay Shelemey, Jacke Phillips, Meklit Hadero, and Joan Aruz.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Support Radiolab by becoming a member today at </strong><a href=\"https://pledge3.wnyc.org/donate/radiolab/onestep/\"><em><span>Radiolab.org/donate</span></em></a><strong><em>. </em></strong></em></p>\n<p><strong><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCaum_fMDGgFQCmKHUBPq_xg\"><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\">Radiolab</strong><strong data-stringify-type=\"bold\"> is on YouTube!</strong></a> </strong>Catch up with new episodes and hear classics from our archive. Plus, find other cool things we did in the past — like miniseries, music videos, short films and animations, behind-the-scenes features, Radiolab live shows, and more. Take a look, explore and subscribe!</p>", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "publish-at"=>{"value"=>"2022-01-07T10:00:00", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}}}], 2023=>[{"slug"=>"universe-verse", "title"=>"Universe In Verse", "audio"=>"https://chrt.fm/track/53A61E/pdst.fm/e/www.podtrac.com/pts/redirect.mp3/waaa.wnyc.org/radiolab_podcast/radiolab_podcast010623_inverse.mp3?awCollectionId=15957&awEpisodeId=1286860", "description"=>"<p>For a special New Year’s treat, we take a tour through the history of the universe with the help of… poets. Our guide is Maria Popova, who writes the popular blog The Marginalian (formerly Brain Pickings), and the poetry is from her project, “The Universe in Verse” — an annual event where poets read poems about science, space, and the natural world.</p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to all of our poets, musicians, and performers: Marie Howe, Tracy K. Smith, Rebecca Elson, Joan As Police Woman, Patti Smith, Gautam Srikishan, Zoe Keating, and Emily Dickinson.</em></p>\n<p><strong>EPISODE CREDITS:</strong></p>\n<p><span>Reported by - Lulu Miller<br></span><span>with help from - Maria Popova<br></span><span>Produced by - Sindhu Gnanasambandan<br></span><span>with mixing help from - Jeremy Bloom<br></span><span>Fact-checking by - Natalie A. Middleton<br></span><span>and Edited by  - Pat Walters</span></p>\n<p><strong>FURTHER READING AND RESEARCH:</strong><em><span><br></span></em><span>To dig deeper on this one, we recommend<br></span><span><br></span><strong>Books: </strong><strong><br></strong><span>- Tracy K Smith’s “</span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/9639765-life-on-mars\"><span>Life On Mars</span></a><span>” (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/weTzGTbZyVDT\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/weTzGTbZyVDT\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/weTzGTbZyVDT\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/weTzGTbZyVDT</a>)</span></span><span><br></span><span>- Marie Howe’s “</span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/1054593.The_Kingdom_of_Ordinary_Time\"><span>The Kingdom Of Ordinary Times</span></a><span>” (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/Tj9cWTsQxHG3\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/Tj9cWTsQxHG3\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/Tj9cWTsQxHG3\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/Tj9cWTsQxHG3</a>)</span></span><span><br></span><span>- Rebecca Elson’s “</span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/1912586.A_Responsibility_to_Awe\">A Responsiblity To Awe” (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/PLR3KL8SfuPR\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/PLR3KL8SfuPR\"></span></a><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/PLR3KL8SfuPR\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/PLR3KL8SfuPR</a>)<br><span>- Patti Smith’s “</span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/341879.Just_Kids?from_search=true&amp;from_srp=true&amp;qid=2Jb4h4SSWL&amp;rank=1\"><span>Just Kids</span></a><span>” (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/zM47P5KqqKZx\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/zM47P5KqqKZx\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/zM47P5KqqKZx\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/zM47P5KqqKZx</a>)</span></span><span></span><br><br><strong>Music:</strong><strong><br></strong><span>- </span><a href=\"https://joanaspolicewoman.com/\">Joan As Policewoman (https://joanaspolicewoman.com/)<br></a><span>- </span><a href=\"https://www.floatingfast.com/\">Gautam Srikishan (https://www.floatingfast.com/)<br></a><span>- </span><a href=\"https://www.zoekeating.com/\">Zoe Keating (https://www.zoekeating.com/)<br></a></p>\n<p><strong>Internet:</strong><strong><br></strong><span>- </span><a href=\"https://www.themarginalian.org/2022/02/24/dark-matter/\"><span>The Marginalian blog post</span></a> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/abTuDFH9pfwu\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/abTuDFH9pfwu\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/abTuDFH9pfwu\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/abTuDFH9pfwu</a>)</span><span> about Vera Rubin<br>- Check out photos of Emily Dickinson’s <a href=\"https://www.themarginalian.org/2017/05/23/emily-dickinson-herbarium/\"><span>Herbarium</span></a> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/XkgTscKBfem6\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/XkgTscKBfem6\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/XkgTscKBfem6\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/XkgTscKBfem6</a>)</span><span>, a book of 424 flowers she picked and pressed and identified while studying the wild botany of Massachusetts.</span></span><span><br></span><span><br></span><span>Tracy K. Smith, “My God, It’s Full of Stars” from Such Color: New and Selected Poems. Copyright © 2011 by Tracy K. Smith. Read by the author and used with the permission of The Permissions Company, LLC on behalf of Graywolf Press, Minneapolis, Minnesota,</span> <a href=\"https://protect-us.mimecast.com/s/WjXzCgJGwxHYAx2nHZcZqA?domain=graywolfpress.org\"><span>www.graywolfpress.org</span></a><span>.<br><br>Fun fact: This episode was inspired by the fact that many Navy ships record the first log entry of the New Year in verse! To see some of this year's poems and learn about the history of the tradition, check out this post by <a href=\"https://www.history.navy.mil/research/archives/resources-for-the-fleet/deck-logs/new-years-contest.html#history\">the Naval History and Heritage Command</a>. And, if you want to read a bit from Lulu's interview with sailor poet Lt. Ian McConnaughey, subscribe to our newsletter. <br></span></p>\n<p><em><span>Our newsletter comes out every Wednesday. It includes short essays, recommendations, and details about other ways to interact with the show. </span></em><a href=\"https://radiolab.org/newsletter\"><em><span>Sign up</span></em></a><em><span> (https://radiolab.org/newsletter)!</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Radiolab is supported by listeners like you. Support Radiolab by becoming a member of </span></em><a href=\"http://members.radiolab.org\"><em><span>The Lab</span></em></a><em><span> (https://members.radiolab.org/) today.</span></em></p>\n<p><em><span>Follow our show on </span></em><a href=\"http://instagram.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Instagram</span></em></a><em><span>, </span></em><a href=\"http://twitter.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Twitter</span></em></a><em><span> and </span></em><a href=\"http://facebook.com/radiolab\"><em><span>Facebook</span></em></a><em><span> @radiolab, and share your thoughts with us by emailing </span></em><a href=\"mailto:radiolab@wnyc.org\"><em><span>radiolab@wnyc.org</span></em></a><em><span>.<br><br></span></em></p>\n<hr>\n<p><em><span><br></span></em><em><span>Leadership support for Radiolab’s science programming is provided by the Gordon and Betty Moore Foundation, Science Sandbox, a Simons Foundation Initiative, and the John Templeton Foundation. Foundational support for Radiolab was provided by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation.</span></em></p>", "tease"=>"A poet-led tour of the universe.", "publishTime"=>1673017200000, "publish-at"=>"2023-01-06T10:00:00", "date-line-ts"=>1673017200, "estimated-duration"=>1924, "image-main"=>{"alt-text"=>"A series of expanding semi-circles highlighted in the foreground of a picture of a deep galaxy field of stars.", "name"=>"2023/01/Inverse_1600x1200.png", "source"=>nil, "url"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/1600/1200/c/80/2023/01/Inverse_1600x1200.png", "h"=>1200, "is-display"=>true, "crop"=>"c", "caption"=>"", "credits-url"=>"", "template"=>"https://media.wnyc.org/i/%s/%s/%s/%s/2023/01/Inverse_1600x1200.png", "w"=>1600, "id"=>203491, "credits-name"=>"Jared Bartman"}, "objectID"=>"1286860", "_highlightResult"=>{"title"=>{"value"=>"Universe In Verse", "matchLevel"=>"none", "matchedWords"=>[]}, "description"=>{"value"=>"<p>For a special New Year’s treat, we take a tour through the history of the universe with the help of… poets. Our guide is Maria Popova, who writes the popular blog The Marginalian (formerly Brain Pickings), and the poetry is from her project, “The Universe in Verse” — an annual event where poets read poems about science, space, and the natural world.</p>\n<p><em>Special thanks to all of our poets, musicians, and performers: Marie Howe, Tracy K. Smith, Rebecca Elson, Joan As Police Woman, Patti Smith, Gautam Srikishan, Zoe Keating, and Emily Dickinson.</em></p>\n<p><strong>EPISODE CREDITS:</strong></p>\n<p><span>Reported by - Lulu Miller<br></span><span>with help from - Maria Popova<br></span><span>Produced by - Sindhu Gnanasambandan<br></span><span>with mixing help from - Jeremy Bloom<br></span><span>Fact-checking by - Natalie A. Middleton<br></span><span>and Edited by  - Pat Walters</span></p>\n<p><strong>FURTHER READING AND RESEARCH:</strong><em><span><br></span></em><span>To dig deeper on this one, we recommend<br></span><span><br></span><strong>Books: </strong><strong><br></strong><span>- Tracy K Smith’s “</span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/9639765-life-on-mars\"><span>Life On Mars</span></a><span>” (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/weTzGTbZyVDT\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/weTzGTbZyVDT\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/weTzGTbZyVDT\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/weTzGTbZyVDT</a>)</span></span><span><br></span><span>- Marie Howe’s “</span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/1054593.The_Kingdom_of_Ordinary_Time\"><span>The Kingdom Of Ordinary Times</span></a><span>” (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/Tj9cWTsQxHG3\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/Tj9cWTsQxHG3\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/Tj9cWTsQxHG3\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/Tj9cWTsQxHG3</a>)</span></span><span><br></span><span>- Rebecca Elson’s “</span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/en/book/show/1912586.A_Responsibility_to_Awe\">A Responsiblity To Awe” (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/PLR3KL8SfuPR\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/PLR3KL8SfuPR\"></span></a><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/PLR3KL8SfuPR\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/PLR3KL8SfuPR</a>)<br><span>- Patti Smith’s “</span><a href=\"https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/341879.Just_Kids?from_search=true&amp;from_srp=true&amp;qid=2Jb4h4SSWL&amp;rank=1\"><span>Just Kids</span></a><span>” (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/zM47P5KqqKZx\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/zM47P5KqqKZx\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/zM47P5KqqKZx\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/zM47P5KqqKZx</a>)</span></span><span></span><br><br><strong>Music:</strong><strong><br></strong><span>- </span><a href=\"https://joanaspolicewoman.com/\">Joan As Policewoman (https://joanaspolicewoman.com/)<br></a><span>- </span><a href=\"https://www.floatingfast.com/\">Gautam Srikishan (https://www.floatingfast.com/)<br></a><span>- </span><a href=\"https://www.zoekeating.com/\">Zoe Keating (https://www.zoekeating.com/)<br></a></p>\n<p><strong>Internet:</strong><strong><br></strong><span>- </span><a href=\"https://www.themarginalian.org/2022/02/24/dark-matter/\"><span>The Marginalian blog post</span></a> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/abTuDFH9pfwu\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/abTuDFH9pfwu\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/abTuDFH9pfwu\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/abTuDFH9pfwu</a>)</span><span> about Vera Rubin<br>- Check out photos of Emily Dickinson’s <a href=\"https://www.themarginalian.org/2017/05/23/emily-dickinson-herbarium/\"><span>Herbarium</span></a> (<span data-sheets-value='{\"1\":2,\"2\":\"https://zpr.io/XkgTscKBfem6\"}' data-sheets-userformat='{\"2\":513,\"3\":{\"1\":0},\"12\":0}' data-sheets-hyperlink=\"https://zpr.io/XkgTscKBfem6\"><a class=\"in-cell-link\" href=\"https://zpr.io/XkgTscKBfem6\" target=\"_blank\">https://zpr.io/XkgTscKBfem6</a>)</span><span>, a book of 424 flowers she picked and pressed and identified while studying the wild botany of Massachusetts.</span></span><span><br></span><span><br></span><span>Tracy K. Smith, “My God, It’s Full of Stars” from Such Color: New and Selected Poems. Copyright © 2011 by Tracy K. Smith. Read by the author and used with the permission of The Permissions Company, LLC on behalf of Graywolf Press, Minneapolis, Minnesota,</span> <a href=\"https://protect-us.mimecast.com/s/WjXzCgJGwxHYAx2nHZcZqA?domain=graywolfpress.org\"><span>www.graywolfpress.org</span></a><span>.<br><br>Fun fact: This episode was inspired by the fact that many Navy ships record the first log entry of the New Year in verse! To see some of this year's poems and learn about the history of the tradition, check out this post by <a href=\"https://www.history.navy.mil/research/archives/resources-for-the-fleet/deck-logs/new-years-contest.html#history\">the Naval History and Heritage Command</a>. And, if you want to read 